// Scenario Lab asset library — Phase 7A: kahneman
const _AL_kahneman = {"id":"kahneman","title":"Phase 7A: kahneman","chapters":[{"id":"ch05","title":"Introduction","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Two Agents of the Mind: System 1 and System 2","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen diagram renders the mind as two animated agents. On the left, a blue figure labeled System 1 flickers at lightning speed over a lamp, a telephone, and a car, producing answers before they finish appearing; on the right, a slower amber figure labeled System 2 works through a spreadsheet with a furrowed brow. A progress bar shows the player sorting seven mental events into the two agents. The critical case, knowing there is a lamp on the desk, glows in the center of the screen.","player_mission":"Sort each mental event into the agent that produces it, then classify the glowing critical case. Correctly mapping all events to System 1 or System 2 reveals how much mental work runs silently beneath awareness.","key_concepts":["System 1 produces fast thinking that operates automatically and quickly, while System 2 produces slow, deliberate, and effortful thinking.","The mental work that produces impressions, intuitions, and many decisions goes on in silence in the mind.","You cannot trace how you came to the belief that there is a lamp on the desk, nor how you detected irritation in your spouse's voice on the telephone.","Fast thinking includes expert intuition, heuristic intuition, and the entirely automatic mental activities of perception and memory.","The intuitive System 1 is more influential than your experience tells you and is the secret author of many of the choices and judgments you make.","We normally allow ourselves to be guided by impressions and feelings, and the confidence in our intuitive beliefs is usually justified, but not always.","We are often confident even when we are wrong, and an objective observer is more likely to detect our errors than we are.","System 2 can perform vital tasks that require effort and self-control when System 1's intuitive solution is not available.","Most of our judgments and actions are appropriate most of the time, so the focus on error does not denigrate human intelligence.","The distinction between fast and slow thinking is described by the metaphor of two agents, System 1 and System 2."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the two-agent metaphor of mental life: System 1 silently generates impressions and many decisions without our awareness, while System 2 is the slow, deliberate system. The chapter claims System 1 is more influential than our experience tells us and is the secret author of many of the choices and judgments we make.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each mental event by the mind's agent that produces it. Then classify the critical case: how do you know there is a lamp on the desk in front of you?","column_a_label":"System 1 - fast, automatic, silent","column_b_label":"System 2 - slow, deliberate, effortful","column_c_label":"Both - automatic association feeding deliberate reasoning","rows":[{"label":"Detecting a hint of irritation in your spouse's voice on the telephone","col":"a"},{"label":"Knowing there is a lamp on the desk in front of you","col":"a"},{"label":"Avoiding a threat on the road before you become consciously aware of it","col":"a"},{"label":"Deliberately verifying the arithmetic of a budget spreadsheet line by line","col":"b"},{"label":"Combining the librarian resemblance intuition with base-rate knowledge to reach a corrected answer","col":"b"},{"label":"Automatically retrieving the name of the capital of Russia when asked","col":"a"},{"label":"Switching to slow, effortful thinking when no intuitive answer comes to mind","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - you consciously verified the lamp's presence before accepting it","c - the belief requires deliberate reasoning about the light source","b - remembering any object's location requires effortful search"],"explanation":"The text states that most impressions and thoughts arise in conscious experience without our knowing how they got there, and that the mental work producing impressions, intuitions, and many decisions goes on in silence. Knowing there is a lamp on the desk is an automatic perceptual operation of System 1, the fast thinking, exactly like detecting irritation in a spouse's voice or avoiding a road threat before awareness."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Three Sources of Intuitive and Deliberate Judgment","visual_frame_description":"A training dojo has three doorways labeled Expert Recognition, Heuristic Shortcut, and Deliberate Reasoning. Snapshots float past: a firefighter commander shouting in a burning kitchen, a chess master at a street game, Steve the shy and tidy librarian candidate, a doctor glancing at a patient, and an executive beaming at a car. The player drags each snapshot through the matching doorway.","player_mission":"Route each judgment snapshot through the correct door. Then identify which source produced the firefighter's sixth sense to unlock the exit and advance the battle.","key_concepts":["Expert intuition strikes us as magical but is not; it is best summarized by Herbert Simon: intuition is recognition.","Valid intuitions develop when experts have learned to recognize familiar elements in a new situation and act in a manner appropriate to it.","Gary Klein's firefighter commander sensed the fire was unusually quiet and his ears unusually hot, prompting him to shout, 'Let's get out of here!' just before the floor collapsed.","Skill and heuristics are alternative sources of intuitive judgments and choices.","The chess master who announces 'White mates in three' without stopping and the physician who diagnoses at a single glance are canonical examples of expert intuition.","Each of us performs feats of intuitive expertise many times each day, such as detecting anger in the first word of a telephone call.","The specific heuristics Amos and I studied provide little help in understanding the executive's Ford stock decision, which is better explained by the broader affect heuristic.","When the question is difficult and a skilled solution is not available, intuition still has a shot by answering an easier question.","Good intuitive judgments come to mind with the same immediacy as a two-year-old saying 'doggie!' at the sight of a dog.","The accurate intuitions of experts are better explained by the effects of prolonged practice than by heuristics."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between two variants of fast thinking: expert intuition, which is recognition built from prolonged practice, and heuristic intuition, which answers an easier question. The book argues that skill and heuristics are alternative sources of intuitive judgment, and that expert intuition is best understood through Herbert Simon's dictum that intuition is recognition.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each judgment by its source. Then classify the critical case: why did the firefighter commander shout, 'Let's get out of here!' before he knew why?","column_a_label":"Expert intuition - recognition built from prolonged practice","column_b_label":"Heuristic intuition - answering an easier question or using resemblance","column_c_label":"System 2 deliberation - conscious, effortful analysis","rows":[{"label":"The commander sensed the unusually quiet fire and unusually hot ears and ordered everyone out","col":"a"},{"label":"Steve the shy, tidy soul judged more likely a librarian than a farmer","col":"b"},{"label":"The chess master announcing 'White mates in three' without stopping","col":"a"},{"label":"The executive who bought Ford stock because he liked the cars","col":"b"},{"label":"The physician making a complex diagnosis after a single glance at a patient","col":"a"},{"label":"A researcher computing required sample size from a statistical formula","col":"c"},{"label":"Detecting anger in the first word of a telephone call","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - he answered the easier question of whether he felt afraid","c - he consciously deduced that the fire must be in the basement","b - he recalled a similar fire from a news report and matched it"],"explanation":"The commander's intuition is expert intuition: a situation cue, the quiet fire and the heat on his ears, gave him access to information stored in memory, and the information provided the answer. Herbert Simon's account holds that intuition is nothing more and nothing less than recognition, and valid intuitions develop when experts have learned to recognize familiar elements in a new situation."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Availability Heuristic and the Letter-K Study","visual_frame_description":"A word-laboratory shows giant alphabet tiles. A voice lists words beginning with K while a counter marks how fast each word comes to mind; a second, slower counter tallies words with K in the third position. News headlines about professors' divorces and politicians flash beside memory bubbles. The player assigns each example to the heuristic driving it.","player_mission":"Classify each example by the heuristic at work, then diagnose why people overestimate first-position letter frequency. Choosing correctly explains the availability bias behind the letter-K result.","key_concepts":["Kahneman and Tversky called reliance on the ease of memory search the availability heuristic.","People judged the size of categories by the ease with which instances came to mind, as in the question about the divorce rate among professors.","It is easier to come up with words beginning with a particular letter than to find words with that letter in the third position, for every letter of the alphabet.","Respondents exaggerated the frequency of letters in the first position, even for letters such as K, L, N, R, and V that in fact occur more frequently in the third position.","Reliance on a heuristic produces predictable biases, systematic errors, in judgments.","Kahneman's impression that adultery is more common among politicians than among physicians or lawyers could be due to journalists' choices of topics and to reliance on the availability heuristic.","People tend to assess the relative importance of issues by the ease with which they are retrieved from memory.","Frequently mentioned topics populate the mind even as others slip away from awareness.","Media feeding frenzies are common because public interest is most easily aroused by dramatic events and by celebrities.","The availability heuristic helps explain why some issues are highly salient in the public's mind while others are neglected."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability heuristic: people judge the frequency or importance of a category by the ease with which instances come to mind. The letter-K study shows this reliance on ease of retrieval produces a predictable bias, exaggerating first-position frequency even for letters that actually occur more often in the third position.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each example by the heuristic that best explains it. Then classify the critical case: why do people exaggerate the frequency of letters in the first position of words?","column_a_label":"Availability heuristic - judging frequency by ease of memory search","column_b_label":"Representativeness heuristic - judging by resemblance to a stereotype","column_c_label":"Affect heuristic - judging by feelings of liking or disliking","rows":[{"label":"Estimating the divorce rate among professors from how easily divorced professors come to mind","col":"a"},{"label":"Believing K appears more often as a first letter when it actually occurs more often in the third position","col":"a"},{"label":"Judging Steve a librarian because his personality resembles the librarian stereotype","col":"b"},{"label":"Suspecting adultery is more common among politicians because their transgressions are more likely to be reported","col":"a"},{"label":"Buying Ford stock because you like the cars the company makes","col":"c"},{"label":"Assessing the importance of issues by how frequently the media mentions them","col":"a"},{"label":"Judging a category's size by the ease with which instances come to mind","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - words beginning with a letter resemble a mental image of the letter","c - certain letters feel more pleasant than others","b - the first-position letter is more representative of typical words"],"explanation":"The letter-K study is a canonical availability demonstration: it is much easier to come up with words beginning with a letter than words with that letter in the third position, so respondents exaggerated first-position frequency even for letters like K, L, N, R, and V that truly occur more often in the third position. Reliance on the ease of memory search produces a predictable bias."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Halo Effect and Global Impressions","visual_frame_description":"An awards ceremony: a handsome, confident speaker bounds onto a stage while an audience of judges, including Dr. Priya Nair, nods and smiles. Her scoring tablet glows with high marks and no content notes. Side panels show three suspect mechanisms labeled for sorting.","player_mission":"Sort each perception by the bias it illustrates, then diagnose the halo effect inflating the speaker's ratings. Correctly naming the mechanism unlocks the label that makes the bias visible.","key_concepts":["When a handsome and confident speaker bounds onto the stage, you can anticipate that the audience will judge his comments more favorably than he deserves.","The availability of a diagnostic label for a bias, such as the halo effect, makes it easier to anticipate, recognize, and understand the error.","The halo effect is a common bias that plays a large role in shaping our view of people and situations.","Systematic errors are known as biases, and they recur predictably in particular circumstances.","A good diagnostician needs a large set of labels that bind an idea of an illness with its symptoms, antecedents, consequences, and interventions.","A deeper understanding of judgments and choices requires a richer vocabulary than is available in everyday language.","The expectation of intelligent gossip is a powerful motive for serious self-criticism.","It is much easier and more enjoyable to identify and label the mistakes of others than to recognize our own.","When the question is difficult and a skilled solution is not available, intuition still has a shot, but the answer is not an answer to the original question.","An accurate diagnosis may suggest an intervention to limit the damage that bad judgments and choices often cause."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the halo effect: a single salient, valued attribute of a person colors the overall evaluation of everything else about them. The chapter shows that once a diagnostic label such as the halo effect exists, it becomes easier to anticipate, recognize, and understand the systematic error.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each perception by the bias it best illustrates. Then classify the critical case: when a handsome, confident speaker bounds onto the stage, why will the audience judge his comments more favorably than he deserves?","column_a_label":"Halo effect - a salient trait colors the overall judgment of a person","column_b_label":"Availability heuristic - frequency or importance judged by ease of recall","column_c_label":"Substitution - answering an easier, related question instead of the hard one","rows":[{"label":"Judging the handsome, confident speaker's comments more favorably than he deserves","col":"a"},{"label":"Liking a politician's voice and appearance because you like his politics","col":"a"},{"label":"Answering 'do I like Ford cars?' instead of 'is Ford stock underpriced?'","col":"c"},{"label":"Estimating how common adultery is among politicians from how easily reported cases come to mind","col":"b"},{"label":"Rating a colleague's entire proposal highly because she is articulate and well-dressed","col":"a"},{"label":"Assessing a technology's danger from how readily dramatic incidents come to mind","col":"b"},{"label":"When asked about the risk of a plan, reporting how anxious the idea makes you feel","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - his talk was the most recent one the audience heard","c - the audience answered the easier question of whether they liked his delivery","b - the audience recalls a similar brilliant speaker from the past"],"explanation":"The halo effect is the tendency to like, or dislike, everything about a person, including their comments, on the basis of one salient trait. The chapter uses the handsome, confident speaker to show that a diagnostic label like the halo effect makes it easier to anticipate, recognize, and understand a systematic error."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Distinguishing Studies, Theories, and Anecdotes in the Text","visual_frame_description":"An evidence desk organizes the chapter's claims into three labeled drawers: Documented Study, Theory or Construct, and Personal Anecdote. Files include the mathematical-psychology survey, the letter-K study, prospect theory, the affect heuristic, the Ford investment, and Kahneman's musings about adultery among politicians. The player files each claim.","player_mission":"File each item into the correct epistemic drawer, then classify the Society of Mathematical Psychology survey. Sorting correctly demonstrates how the chapter distinguishes experimental evidence from theory and personal anecdote.","key_concepts":["In 1969 Kahneman invited Amos Tversky to speak to a seminar on intuitive statistics at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem.","The seminar concluded with a qualified no to the question of whether people are good intuitive statisticians.","Kahneman and Tversky planned a study of the statistical intuitions of sophisticated researchers after concluding their own intuitions were deficient.","The survey was collected at a meeting of the Society of Mathematical Psychology, including the authors of two statistical textbooks.","Expert colleagues greatly exaggerated the likelihood that the original result of an experiment would be successfully replicated even with a small sample.","The experts also gave very poor advice to a fictitious graduate student about the number of observations she needed to collect.","Even statisticians were not good intuitive statisticians.","The findings were published in Science in the article 'Judgment Under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases,' which described some 20 biases as manifestations of simplifying shortcuts.","The article challenged the 1970s assumptions that people are generally rational and that emotions explain departures from rationality.","The article is one of the most highly cited works in social science, with more than three hundred scholarly articles referring to it in 2010."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the epistemic grounding of the chapter's claims by sorting documented studies from proposed theories and from personal anecdotes. The Society of Mathematical Psychology survey is a documented study showing that even expert statisticians exaggerated the replicability of small-sample results, evidence that intuitive statistics is systematically biased.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each item by its epistemic status in the chapter. Then classify the critical case: the survey given to members of the Society of Mathematical Psychology.","column_a_label":"Documented study - an experiment or survey with reported findings","column_b_label":"Theory or construct - a proposed explanation or mechanism","column_c_label":"Personal anecdote or reflection - Kahneman's own experience, not a formal experiment","rows":[{"label":"The survey of expert researchers that found they exaggerated replication likelihood with small samples","col":"a"},{"label":"The letter-K study of first- and third-position frequencies in English text","col":"a"},{"label":"The Steve problem, in which participants ignored base rates and relied on resemblance","col":"a"},{"label":"Prospect theory, the 1979 model of choice under risk","col":"b"},{"label":"The affect heuristic, in which judgments are guided by feelings of liking and disliking","col":"b"},{"label":"The chief investment officer's investment of tens of millions in Ford Motor Company stock","col":"c"},{"label":"Kahneman's doubt that adultery is more common among politicians than physicians or lawyers","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - the replication claim was only a theory the authors proposed","c - it was a casual conversation among colleagues rather than data collection","b - the result illustrated the halo effect, which is a theory"],"explanation":"The survey was a documented study: Amos collected responses from expert participants at a meeting of the Society of Mathematical Psychology, including the authors of two statistical textbooks, and found that they greatly exaggerated the likelihood that the original result of an experiment would be successfully replicated even with a small sample, and gave poor advice about the number of observations a fictitious graduate student needed."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Investment Officer and the Affect Heuristic","visual_frame_description":"A conference-room interrogation plays as a recording: Chief Investment Officer Marcus Reid leans back, smiling, recounting an automobile show, then hesitates and fidgets when an auditor asks about valuation. A timestamped behavioral log and transcript scroll beside the video feed.","player_mission":"Audit Marcus's decision file, weigh the nonverbal log and transcript against his methodical baseline, and diagnose the cognitive mechanism behind his ten-million-dollar Ford investment.","key_concepts":["The executive's decision to invest in Ford would today be described as an example of the affect heuristic, where judgments and decisions are guided directly by feelings of liking and disliking, with little deliberation or reasoning.","The question the executive faced, should I invest in Ford stock, was difficult, but the answer to an easier and related question, do I like Ford cars, came readily to his mind and determined his choice.","This is the essence of intuitive heuristics: when faced with a difficult question, we often answer an easier one instead, usually without noticing the substitution.","The executive made it very clear that he trusted his gut feeling and was satisfied with himself and with his decision.","The executive apparently never considered the question an economist would call relevant: whether Ford stock was currently underpriced.","From what is known about the accuracy of stock picking, it is reasonable to believe the executive did not know what he was doing.","An important advance is that emotion now looms much larger in our understanding of intuitive judgments and choices than it did in the past.","The specific heuristics Amos and I studied provide little help in understanding the executive's decision, but a broader conception of heuristics offers a good account.","When the individual has relevant expertise, she will recognize the situation and the intuitive solution that comes to mind is likely to be correct.","An accurate diagnosis may suggest an intervention to limit the damage that bad judgments and choices often cause."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the affect heuristic and substitution: judgments and choices guided directly by feelings of liking, where a difficult target question is replaced by an easier related one. The chapter's Ford Motor example shows the executive answering 'do I like Ford cars?' in place of 'is Ford underpriced?' without noticing the substitution.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the cognitive mechanism driving Chief Investment Officer Marcus Reid's ten-million-dollar decision to buy Ford stock.","subject_baseline":"Marcus is the methodical chief investment officer of a large financial firm, known for rigorous discounted-cash-flow modeling and multi-week due diligence before committing capital. Colleagues describe him as numbers-driven and rarely impulsive.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:02 - Marcus leans back, relaxed, smiling broadly while recounting a recent automobile show.","09:04 - Gestures expand and his voice warms as he praises the cars; no notes or spreadsheets are in view.","09:06 - Asked about valuation metrics, he hesitates, gazes upward, and fidgets with his pen.","09:07 - Answers about the stock in short, vague sentences and speeds up his speech.","09:08 - Becomes mildly defensive and waves a hand when pressed on whether the stock is underpriced."],"interrogation_transcript":["Marcus: I recently attended an automobile show and I was impressed. Boy, do they know how to make a car!","Auditor: Did you consider whether Ford stock is currently underpriced?","Marcus: I like the cars. I like the company. I like the idea of owning the stock. It felt right.","Auditor: Is that how you normally evaluate a position of this size?","Marcus: I trust my gut feeling. It has served me well."],"correct_diagnosis":"The affect heuristic with substitution: Marcus's judgment was guided directly by feelings of liking for the cars and the company, with little deliberation, and he answered the easier question 'do I like Ford cars?' in place of the difficult target question 'is Ford stock underpriced?'","diagnostic_breakdown":"The warmth, broad gestures, and smile appear only when discussing the cars, evidence of affect, while the valuation question triggers hesitation, an upward gaze, and vague hurried answers, evidence of no actual financial analysis. His own words, 'I like the cars. I like the company. I like the idea of owning the stock,' are a textbook substitution, and this behavior deviates sharply from his numbers-driven baseline. The book states such a decision would today be described as an example of the affect heuristic.","distractors":["Anchoring effect: Marcus anchored his valuation on the automobile show's sticker prices.","Availability heuristic: Marcus over-weighted a recent news story about Ford's recovery.","Base-rate neglect: Marcus ignored the typical failure rate of auto-industry stocks."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Awards Panel and the Halo Effect","visual_frame_description":"An awards panel room: Dr. Priya Nair scores keynote speakers while her own behavioral log runs alongside, showing nods and smiles for the charismatic presenter and a still pen and furrowed brow for the quieter one. The transcript shows her citing delivery when asked about methodology.","player_mission":"Review the scored rubrics, the nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then render the diagnosis for Priya's inconsistent evaluations of the two keynote speakers.","key_concepts":["When the handsome and confident speaker bounds onto the stage, you can anticipate that the audience will judge his comments more favorably than he deserves.","The availability of a diagnostic label for this bias, the halo effect, makes it easier to anticipate, recognize, and understand it.","Systematic errors are known as biases, and they recur predictably in particular circumstances.","The halo effect is one of the ways the representation of the world that System 1 generates is simplified and made coherent.","We are often confident even when we are wrong, and an objective observer is more likely to detect our errors than we are.","A deeper understanding of judgments and choices requires a richer vocabulary than is available in everyday language.","The expectation of intelligent gossip is a powerful motive for serious self-criticism.","An accurate diagnosis may suggest an intervention to limit the damage that bad judgments and choices often cause.","Most impressions and thoughts arise in conscious experience without our knowing how they got there.","It is easier, and far more enjoyable, to identify and label the mistakes of others than to recognize our own."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the halo effect: a single salient positive trait inflates the overall evaluation of a person's work. The chapter predicts an audience will judge a handsome, confident speaker's comments more favorably than he deserves, and shows that a diagnostic label makes the bias easier to anticipate and recognize.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the bias affecting Dr. Priya Nair's scoring of the two keynote speakers on the research awards panel.","subject_baseline":"Priya is a rigorous peer reviewer who normally scores presentations against a detailed rubric, takes methodical content notes, and is known for separating style from substance in grant panels.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:15 - During the keynote by a tall, confident, impeccably dressed speaker, Priya's gaze stays fixed on him and she nods frequently.","09:18 - She circles high marks on the rubric without writing any content notes.","09:22 - She smiles and leans forward with open posture.","09:35 - A quieter, disheveled speaker covers similar technical depth; her pen stays still, her brow furrows, and she checks her phone."],"interrogation_transcript":["Colleague: What did you think of the keynote?","Priya: He was fantastic. Brilliant. Really impressive work.","Colleague: Anything specific about the methodology?","Priya: The delivery was just so compelling. You can tell he knows his stuff.","Colleague: How did the second talk compare?","Priya: Harder to follow. I wasn't really engaged."],"correct_diagnosis":"The halo effect: one salient positive attribute, the speaker's handsome, confident, well-dressed presentation, colored Priya's overall evaluation of the technical content, causing her to judge his work more favorably than the evidence warranted.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Priya's baseline is rubric-based scoring with content notes, yet she circled high marks while taking none, and her nonverbal affiliation, nodding, smiling, and open posture, was reserved for the attractive presenter. When pressed for specifics she cites delivery rather than methodology, and she rated the equal-depth but less polished talk lower. This matches the chapter's prediction that a handsome, confident speaker will be judged more favorably than he deserves.","distractors":["Anchoring effect: Priya anchored her score on the first speaker and could not adjust.","Availability heuristic: she recalled a famous researcher's earlier work and generalized.","Confirmation bias: she had already decided the speaker was good before the talk began."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Newsroom Salience and the Availability Heuristic","visual_frame_description":"A modern newsroom at 11:30 a.m.: editor-in-chief Yara Okonkwo's desk shows a celebrity death dominating the layout while a reporter gestures at declining-literacy charts. A behavioral log timestamps Yara's widening eyes, dismissive wave, and reallocated reporters.","player_mission":"Audit the editorial decision, connect the observed cues and transcript to the mechanism behind the coverage priorities, and deliver the correct diagnosis for why the literacy story was buried.","key_concepts":["People tend to assess the relative importance of issues by the ease with which they are retrieved from memory.","Salience is largely determined by the extent of coverage in the media; frequently mentioned topics populate the mind even as others slip away from awareness.","What the media choose to report corresponds to their view of what is currently on the public's mind.","Public interest is most easily aroused by dramatic events and by celebrities, so media feeding frenzies are common.","For several weeks after Michael Jackson's death, it was virtually impossible to find a television channel reporting on another topic.","There is little coverage of critical but unexciting issues that provide less drama, such as declining educational standards.","It is no accident that authoritarian regimes exert substantial pressure on independent media.","The availability heuristic helps explain why some issues are highly salient in the public's mind while others are neglected.","Kahneman noticed that his own choice of 'little-covered' examples was guided by availability.","Students of policy have noted that the availability heuristic helps explain which issues become salient in public attention."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability heuristic as it operates on public and editorial attention: issues are judged important by the ease with which they are retrieved, which is largely driven by media coverage. The chapter shows this produces media feeding frenzies around dramatic events while critical but unexciting issues are neglected.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose why editor-in-chief Yara Okonkwo's newsroom is burying a decade-long literacy story to lead with a celebrity death.","subject_baseline":"Yara is an experienced editor who publicly champions coverage of what matters, holds weekly data reviews, and has built a reputation for careful, deliberative editorial judgment rather than chasing ratings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["11:30 - At the pitch meeting, when a producer mentions a celebrity death, Yara's eyes widen, she leans forward, and starts typing immediately.","11:33 - A reporter pitches declining reading scores; Yara makes a dismissive hand wave and briefly rolls her eyes.","11:35 - She interrupts the data discussion to sketch a front-page layout of the celebrity story.","11:40 - She tells the team to move everything and allocates three reporters to the celebrity coverage."],"interrogation_transcript":["Reporter: The literacy data shows a decade-long decline. That is arguably the bigger story.","Yara: Nobody is going to read that. The audience cares about this right now.","Reporter: Is that a reporting decision or a viewership decision?","Yara: The public's mind is on this. We cover what is on the public's mind.","Reporter: Should we at least hold space for the education analysis?","Yara: Not this week. This is what everyone is talking about."],"correct_diagnosis":"The availability heuristic: Yara judged the importance and newsworthiness of stories by the ease with which they come to mind and by media salience, so the dramatic, heavily covered celebrity event crowded out the critical but unexciting education story, an availability-driven media feeding frenzy.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her baseline is data-driven editorial review, yet the celebrity mention triggered immediate attention and resources while the statistics-backed education story was dismissed with a wave and an eye roll. Her rationale, 'we cover what is on the public's mind,' inverts the mechanism: the text notes people assess issue importance by retrieval ease, which is largely determined by media coverage, so coverage itself makes topics salient while critical but unexciting issues are neglected.","distractors":["Affect heuristic: Yara personally dislikes education reporting and dislikes the reporter.","Halo effect: Yara overrated the celebrity story because the producer is charismatic.","Representativeness: Yara judged the literacy story not to resemble typical front-page news."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Intuitive Statistics and the Advice to a Graduate Student","visual_frame_description":"A professor's office: Dr. Alan Whitfield waves a hand dismissively at a graduate student's sample-size question while a ticking log records his fast, confident answers and watch-checking. Pilot data from 12 participants sits on the desk between them.","player_mission":"Review the meeting log and transcript, compare Alan's behavior to his rigorous baseline, and diagnose the statistical intuition error in his replication advice to the graduate student.","key_concepts":["Kahneman and Tversky planned a study of the statistical intuitions of sophisticated researchers after concluding their own intuitions were deficient.","In spite of years of teaching and using statistics, they had not developed an intuitive sense of the reliability of statistical results observed in small samples.","Expert colleagues at the Society of Mathematical Psychology greatly exaggerated the likelihood that the original result of an experiment would be successfully replicated even with a small sample.","The experts also gave very poor advice to a fictitious graduate student about the number of observations she needed to collect.","Even statisticians were not good intuitive statisticians.","The early research concluded that people are good intuitive grammarians but that a qualified no was the better answer to whether they are good intuitive statisticians.","We are far too willing to believe research findings based on inadequate evidence and prone to collect too few observations in our own research.","Statistics requires thinking about many things at once, which System 1 is not designed to do.","People easily think associatively, metaphorically, and causally, but statistical thinking is difficult for System 1.","Reliance on a heuristic causes predictable biases, systematic errors, in predictions."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the bias of intuitive statistics, sometimes called the law of small numbers: overconfidence in the reliability of results from small samples. The book documents that even expert statisticians exaggerated the likelihood of replication with small samples and gave poor advice about sample size.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the cognitive error in Dr. Alan Whitfield's advice about how many participants his graduate student needs for the real study.","subject_baseline":"Alan is a senior psychology professor who teaches a graduate statistics course and has published dozens of studies; he is normally rigorous about experimental design and routinely advises colleagues on power analyses.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:00 - Alan flips quickly through the student's pilot data, nodding firmly within seconds.","14:03 - When the student asks about sample size, he waves a hand dismissively.","14:06 - Pressed about replication, his answers become faster and vaguer and he checks his watch.","14:07 - He gives a reassuring pat on the desk and declares the effect real."],"interrogation_transcript":["Student: The pilot worked on 12 participants. How many do I need for the real study?","Alan: Twelve worked. The effect is real. Just run it again with the same number.","Student: Shouldn't I compute power or account for variance?","Alan: Trust me. If the effect showed up once, it will show up again.","Student: What if it was just noise?","Alan: You saw it with your own data. That is not noise."],"correct_diagnosis":"Bias of intuitive statistics, the tendency the book treats as the law of small numbers: Alan treats a single small-sample result as diagnostic of a reliable effect, overestimates the likelihood of replication, and fails to consider that the result could be sampling noise, the very pattern the book documents in even expert statisticians.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Alan's baseline is methodical power analysis, yet here he rendered judgment within seconds, dismissed the sample-size question with a hand wave, and grew vaguer under pressure. His claim that a 12-person pilot guarantees replication mirrors the survey in which expert researchers, including textbook authors, greatly exaggerated the likelihood that an original result would replicate with a small sample and gave poor advice about the number of observations a fictitious graduate student needed.","distractors":["Anchoring effect: Alan anchored on the number 12 and refused to adjust upward.","Availability heuristic: Alan recalled a successful replication from his own lab.","Halo effect: Alan overrated the student and therefore the student's pilot data."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Silent Mind and the Two Systems","visual_frame_description":"A frozen freeway scene inside a car: Ravi's hands are already on the corrected wheel and his foot on the brake, while his conscious attention is still on lunch plans with colleagues. A narrator prompt asks which mind agent acted. In stage two, the passenger asks how he did it so fast.","player_mission":"Identify which system produced Ravi's swerve, then intervene correctly when he offers a confident but reconstructed account of his decision.","key_concepts":["The mental work that produces impressions, intuitions, and many decisions goes on in silence in our mind.","You cannot trace how you came to the belief that there is a lamp on the desk, or how you managed to avoid a threat on the road before you became consciously aware of it.","Fast thinking includes expert and heuristic intuition as well as entirely automatic mental activities of perception and memory.","System 1 is the intuitive system that produces fast thinking; System 2 is the deliberate system that produces slow thinking.","The intuitive System 1 is more influential than your experience tells you and is the secret author of many of the choices and judgments you make.","We are often confident even when we are wrong, and an objective observer is more likely to detect our errors than we are.","Most of us are pitch-perfect in detecting anger in the first word of a telephone call and react quickly to subtle signs that a driver in the next lane is dangerous.","When the spontaneous search for an intuitive solution fails, we often switch to slower, more deliberate, effortful thinking.","When we think of ourselves, we identify with System 2, the conscious, reasoning self that believes it is in charge.","An accurate diagnosis of a bias may suggest an intervention to limit the damage that bad judgments and choices often cause."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the silence of the mind and the two-agent metaphor: System 1 produces fast, automatic responses without our awareness, while the conscious System 2 confidently narrates after the fact. The chapter claims System 1 is the secret author of many choices and that we are often confident even when wrong.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that mental work producing impressions, intuitions, and many decisions goes on in silence: System 1 generates fast, automatic outputs while System 2, the deliberate self, believes it is in charge and confidently narrates reasons that are often reconstructions. The player must learn to attribute mental events to the right system and distrust confident introspection.","stage_1_observation":"Ravi is driving his colleagues to lunch on a busy freeway when a car cuts sharply into his lane. The scene freezes mid-action: Ravi's hands have already corrected the wheel and his foot has hit the brake, but his conscious attention is still on the conversation about the restaurant.","stage_1_question":"Which system of the mind produced Ravi's evasive driving maneuver, and why can he not recall deciding to make it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"System 1 produced the maneuver: the text says we manage to avoid a threat on the road before we become consciously aware of it, and the mental work that produces impressions, intuitions, and many decisions goes on in silence in our mind.","stage_2_intervention":"The battle escalates: in the passenger seat, Ravi's colleague asks, 'How did you do that so fast?' Ravi replies with a confident, elaborate account of how he weighed the options and decided to brake. Choose the best intervention: (a) praise his rapid decision-making as a conscious choice, (b) gently note that his account is likely a confident reconstruction of System 1's silent work and that confidence does not prove the account is accurate, or (c) insist his System 2 must have made the decision because he can describe it.","stage_2_correct_answer":"(b) - the book states we cannot trace how most impressions and reactions arose, that we are often confident even when we are wrong, and that System 1 is the secret author of many choices; Ravi's story is a post-hoc reconstruction by the conscious self."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Catching the Substitution of an Easier Question","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom freeze: Chief Financial Officer David's 'Route Feasibility' slide shows only sun-drenched destination photos and a survey about liking the destination, while CEO Linda Harmon reaches for approval. The player must spot the substitution and redirect the room to the real analysis.","player_mission":"Detect which question David actually answered, then choose the intervention that forces the team back to the target question about whether the route will be profitable.","key_concepts":["When faced with a difficult question, we often answer an easier one instead, usually without noticing the substitution.","This is the essence of intuitive heuristics: an answer may come to mind quickly, but it is not an answer to the original question.","The executive faced the difficult question of whether to invest in Ford stock, but the answer to the easier question of whether he liked Ford cars came readily to mind and determined his choice.","When the individual has relevant expertise, she will recognize the situation and the intuitive solution that comes to mind is likely to be correct.","When the question is difficult and a skilled solution is not available, intuition still has a shot by answering an easier question.","The affect heuristic is a case in which judgments and decisions are guided directly by feelings of liking and disliking, with little deliberation or reasoning.","The specific heuristics studied early provide little help with the executive's decision, but a broader conception of heuristics offers a good account.","An accurate diagnosis may suggest an intervention to limit the damage that bad judgments and choices often cause.","The expectation of intelligent gossip is a powerful motive for serious self-criticism.","A deeper understanding of judgments and choices requires a richer vocabulary than is available in everyday language."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests substitution, the essence of intuitive heuristics: a difficult target question is silently replaced by an easier one. The chapter's Ford example shows an executive answering 'do I like Ford cars?' in place of 'is Ford underpriced?', and this battle asks the player to catch and correct that substitution in a business decision.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The essence of intuitive heuristics: when faced with a difficult question, we often answer an easier one instead, usually without noticing the substitution. This battle tests whether the player can spot a substitution in a business decision and force a return to the hard, target question.","stage_1_observation":"In a modern boardroom, CEO Linda Harmon asks the team whether to launch a new airline route between Pittsburgh and Lisbon. Chief Financial Officer David projects a single slide titled 'Route Feasibility' showing sun-drenched photos of the destination and a survey result that 92% of the panel love the idea of this destination. The rest of the deck is empty. The scene freezes with David pointing proudly at the slide.","stage_1_question":"What did David actually evaluate in place of the difficult target question of whether the route will be profitable?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He substituted an easier question, 'do people like this destination?', for the target question 'will this route be profitable?', answering the related easy question without noticing the substitution, just as the executive answered 'do I like Ford cars?' instead of 'is Ford underpriced?'","stage_2_intervention":"Linda, persuaded by the slide, is about to approve the launch. Choose the most effective move: (a) cheerfully second the motion so the meeting stays positive, (b) ask David to walk the team through the target analysis, forecast revenues, operating costs, load factors, and base demand, before any approval, explicitly naming the substitution that occurred, or (c) tell the team to trust their gut because they are experienced executives.","stage_2_correct_answer":"(b) - redirecting the room to the target question and naming the substitution forces System 2 deliberation on the hard analysis; the book's aim is exactly to enrich the vocabulary of judgment so that errors like substitution can be recognized and their damage limited."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Availability and Public Risk Perception","visual_frame_description":"A crowded coastal town hall: council member Nia points at dramatic shark-attack headlines while a quiet statistics table showing far higher drowning counts sits untouched on the projector. An outraged resident demands the beach be closed.","player_mission":"Identify the mechanism inflating the perceived danger, then pick the intervention that brings actual base-rate evidence into the decision.","key_concepts":["People tend to assess the relative importance of issues by the ease with which they are retrieved from memory.","The availability heuristic is reliance on the ease of memory search to judge the size of categories.","Respondents exaggerated the frequency of letters in the first position even for letters like K, L, N, R, and V that truly occur more often in the third position.","Reliance on a heuristic produces predictable biases in judgment.","Public interest is most easily aroused by dramatic events and by celebrities, so media feeding frenzies are common.","What the media choose to report corresponds to their view of what is currently on the public's mind.","There is little coverage of critical but unexciting issues that provide less drama.","Students of policy have noted that the availability heuristic helps explain which issues are salient and which are neglected.","Frequently mentioned topics populate the mind even as others slip away from awareness.","We are often confident even when we are wrong, and an objective observer is more likely to detect our errors than we are."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability heuristic in risk perception: dramatic, easily retrieved events inflate judged frequency and importance. The chapter shows availability drives public salience and media feeding frenzies, so the corrective is to consult actual base-rate statistics rather than the ease with which cases come to mind.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The availability heuristic causes people to judge the frequency and importance of events by the ease with which instances come to mind, which is driven by drama and media coverage. The battle tests whether the player can recognize availability-driven risk perception and counteract it with actual base-rate statistics.","stage_1_observation":"A coastal town hall is packed. Council member Nia proposes redirecting the entire public-safety budget to shark-mitigation measures because everyone is talking about the attacks. On the projector, three dramatic news headlines about recent shark encounters sit beside a quiet table showing years of data in which drownings outnumber shark encounters by a wide margin. The scene freezes as Nia gestures at the headlines.","stage_1_question":"What cognitive mechanism is driving the council's and the public's perception of how dangerous the water is?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The availability heuristic: dramatic, heavily covered attacks come to mind easily, so people overestimate their frequency and importance, just as the letter-K study showed that easy retrieval of first-letter words exaggerates their perceived frequency even when the truth favors the third position.","stage_2_intervention":"An outraged resident stands up and demands the beach be closed indefinitely. Choose the best intervention: (a) agree with the resident and close the beach to show responsiveness, (b) invite the group to compare the actual statistics, drowning counts against shark-attack counts, and use the base-rate evidence to set risk priorities while acknowledging the fear, or (c) declare the resident irrational and adjourn the meeting.","stage_2_correct_answer":"(b) - replacing ease of retrieval with actual evidence shifts the judgment to the statistics; the text notes availability produces predictable biases and that people assess importance by retrieval ease, so the remedy is to consult the true frequencies and to recognize that dramatic events drive salience while unexciting critical issues are neglected."}]},{"id":"ch06","title":"1. The Characters of the Story","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"System 2 self-control and the impulse to snap","visual_frame_description":"A lunchroom at a busy hospital. Two nurses, Dana and Marcus, argue over a patient chart. Dana's face flushes as Marcus loudly accuses her of falsifying records. A third nurse, Lena, watches Dana's jaw clench, then Dana turns and walks out without saying a word. A caption asks the player to classify the mental machinery behind each observed moment.","player_mission":"Sort each on-screen observation into the column that names the mental mechanism behind it. Then pick the column that explains why Dana stayed polite despite the provocation.","key_concepts":["System 1 operates automatically and quickly, with little or no effort and no sense of voluntary control.","System 2 allocates attention to the effortful mental activities that demand it, including complex computations.","One of the tasks of System 2 is to overcome the impulses of System 1, which makes System 2 responsible for self-control.","The ice-driving example, 'steer into the skid, and whatever you do, do not touch the brakes,' shows System 2 overriding a well-rehearsed natural response.","Every human being has had the experience of not telling someone to go to hell; restraint is an act of System 2 control.","The labels System 1 and System 2 come from psychologists Keith Stanovich and Richard West.","When we think of ourselves, we identify with System 2, the conscious, reasoning self that has beliefs, makes choices, and decides what to think about and what to do.","System 1 cannot be turned off: if you know a language, you will read a word shown on screen unless your attention is totally focused elsewhere.","System 2 is mobilized to increased effort when it detects an error about to be made."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the division of labor between System 1 and System 2, specifically System 2's role in self-control: it must override the automatic impulses System 1 generates, such as the urge to snap back under provocation. Kahneman argues that when all goes smoothly System 2 adopts System 1's suggestions, but when impulses must be restrained, System 2 does the effortful work of control.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dana was furious, yet she kept her composure and said nothing. Which column best explains the mental mechanism that let her do that?","column_a_label":"System 1 — automatic, fast, effort-free impulses","column_b_label":"System 2 — effortful computations that demand attention","column_c_label":"System 2 self-control — overruling the impulses of System 1","rows":[{"label":"Keeping yourself polite when you are angry","col":"c"},{"label":"Not telling someone to go to hell","col":"c"},{"label":"Steering into a skid instead of touching the brakes on ice","col":"c"},{"label":"Not staring at the oddly dressed couple at the next table","col":"c"},{"label":"Working hard to restore control before you blurt out an offensive remark","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["System 1 handled it automatically, because the urge to snap is fast and effortless","System 2 ran a routine computation like mental arithmetic","There was no mental mechanism at all; Dana simply felt nothing"],"explanation":"Dana's fury was a System 1 impulse that arose automatically and outside her control. Keeping it unspoken required System 2, which Kahneman says is in charge of self-control and exists to overcome the impulses of System 1."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The limited budget of attention","visual_frame_description":"A delivery driver's cab on a rain-slicked two-lane road. The driver, Omar, tightens his grip as he overtakes a semi truck; his passenger, Selma, stops mid-sentence and goes silent until the truck is passed. In the back, a dashboard navigation voice keeps talking, unheard. The screen prompts the player to sort the observations.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the column that names the mechanism it demonstrates. Then choose the column that explains why Selma stopped talking.","key_concepts":["Attention is a limited budget: if you try to go beyond it, you will fail.","It is the mark of effortful activities that they interfere with each other, so several cannot be conducted at once.","You can do several things at once only if they are easy and undemanding, such as talking while driving on an empty highway.","When the driver is overtaking on a narrow road, passengers stop talking because they know distracting the driver is unwise.","Social behavior makes allowances for the limited capacity of attention.","The phrase 'pay attention' is apt because attention is a resource you allocate to activities.","Orienting to a loud sound is an involuntary System 1 operation that immediately mobilizes the voluntary attention of System 2.","Intense focusing on a task can make people effectively blind to stimuli that normally attract attention.","Attention can be moved away from an unwanted focus by focusing intently on another target."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the limited-capacity model of attention: System 2's effortful operations draw on a finite budget, and two demanding tasks interfere with one another. Kahneman notes that social behavior even accommodates this, as passengers go quiet during a risky overtake because the driver is effectively deaf while focused.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Why do adult passengers sensibly stop talking while a driver overtakes a truck on a narrow road?","column_a_label":"Limited attention budget — effortful tasks interfere with each other","column_b_label":"Automatic System 1 routines that run unattended","column_c_label":"System 2 programming memory for a deliberate task set","rows":[{"label":"Passengers fall silent while the driver passes a truck on a narrow road","col":"a"},{"label":"You cannot compute 17 x 24 while making a left turn into dense traffic","col":"a"},{"label":"Effortful activities interfere with each other when attempted at once","col":"a"},{"label":"You can hold a conversation on an empty highway because the task is easy","col":"a"},{"label":"Trying to go beyond your budget of attention leads to failure","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Selma was bored and had nothing to say","The driver had programmed his memory to look for oncoming cars","Talking is a System 1 task that never interferes with driving"],"explanation":"Kahneman writes that you dispose of a limited budget of attention and that it is the mark of effortful activities that they interfere with each other. Overtaking on a narrow road is effortful, so the driver is temporarily deaf and passengers stop talking."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Müller-Lyer illusion and cognitive illusions","visual_frame_description":"A psychology classroom. On the projector screen two horizontal lines with fins appear, the bottom one looking clearly longer. A student, Mei, measures the printed handout with a ruler and frowns. Her classmate, Diego, still insists the bottom line looks longer. A side panel shows a small gray silhouette of the angry woman. The player is asked to classify the mental processes at work.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the column naming the mental mechanism it demonstrates. Then pick the column that explains why the lines still look unequal after measuring.","key_concepts":["The Müller-Lyer illusion shows that what you see and what you believe can come apart.","Even after measuring the lines, you still see the bottom line as longer because System 1 is autonomous.","To resist the illusion you must learn to mistrust your impression of line length when fins are attached.","There are illusions of thought as well as visual illusions; Kahneman calls them cognitive illusions.","A strong attraction to a patient with a repeated history of failed treatment is a danger sign, like the fins on the parallel lines.","System 1 operates automatically and cannot be turned off at will.","Errors of intuitive thought are often difficult to prevent because System 2 may have no clue to the error.","Errors can be prevented only by the enhanced monitoring and effortful activity of System 2.","Continuous vigilance is impractical; the compromise is to recognize situations in which mistakes are likely and try harder when the stakes are high.","The premise of the book is that it is easier to recognize other people's mistakes than our own."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the autonomy of System 1 and the distinction between impressions and beliefs: a System 1 impression (the lines look unequal) persists even after System 2 acquires a conflicting belief (the lines are equal). Kahneman extends the same logic to cognitive illusions, where a compelling feeling, such as being uniquely able to help a charming patient, can be a danger sign that must be recognized rather than believed.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After measuring the Müller-Lyer lines with a ruler and knowing they are equal, Mei still sees the bottom line as longer. Which column explains this?","column_a_label":"Autonomy of System 1 — impressions persist even when belief changes","column_b_label":"System 2 computing an answer through deliberate steps","column_c_label":"Two competing System 1 responses to the same stimulus","rows":[{"label":"You measure the lines and know they are equal, yet you still see one as longer","col":"a"},{"label":"You cannot decide to see the lines as equal, although you know they are","col":"a"},{"label":"The illusion continues because System 1 operates automatically and cannot be turned off","col":"a"},{"label":"Beliefs updated by measurement coexist with persistent visual impressions","col":"a"},{"label":"The only defense is to learn to mistrust impressions of line length when fins are attached","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The ruler measurement was performed by System 1 automatically","The fins are processed by System 2 as a deliberate computation","Seeing one line as longer is a voluntary choice the observer makes"],"explanation":"The Müller-Lyer illusion shows the autonomy of System 1: your belief, held by System 2, can change while your impression, produced by System 1, stays fixed. Kahneman stresses the distinction between impressions and beliefs, so you know the lines are equal but cannot make them look equal."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Psychopathic charm as a cognitive illusion","visual_frame_description":"A clinical supervision room. A supervising therapist reviews an intake file on a tablet while a transcript and a timed nonverbal log of a client interview scroll beside it. The client's face, shown in a still frame, is calm and smiling. A red flag icon pulses over a line reading 'You're different, I can tell.' The player must audit the file and issue a verdict.","player_mission":"Review the client's baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis for whether this client's narrative is a trustworthy signal.","key_concepts":["Kahneman's teacher taught that a patient who shares a disturbing tale of repeated failures in previous treatment and insists 'you are different' is most likely a psychopath.","The teacher assumed the sympathy felt for such a patient would not be under our control; it arises from System 1.","The warning was not to be suspicious of all feelings about patients, but to treat strong attraction to a patient with a history of failed treatment as a danger sign.","The analogy to the Müller-Lyer illusion is close: the compelling feeling is an illusion, and you must recognize the pattern and not act on it.","The leading authority in the study of psychopathy confirmed that the teacher's advice was sound.","System 1 generates impressions and feelings automatically, and these are the main sources of the explicit beliefs and deliberate choices of System 2.","Errors of intuitive thought are difficult to prevent because System 2 may have no clue to the error.","The best compromise is to learn to recognize situations in which mistakes are likely and to try harder when the stakes are high.","The premise of the book is that it is easier to recognize other people's mistakes than our own."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the cognitive-illusion mechanism: System 1 automatically produces a strong, compelling impression that the flattering client is uniquely helpable, but that impression is an illusion that must be recognized and refused, exactly as the Müller-Lyer fins must be recognized. Kahneman reports his clinical teacher's hard rule, a repeated history of failed treatment combined with claims that 'you are different' is a psychopathy danger sign, endorsed by the leading authority on the condition.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"You are a supervising therapist reviewing an intake file. Is the client's narrative and your strong pull to help him a trustworthy signal, or a cognitive illusion?","subject_baseline":"In the clinic's records, the client, Ray, presents with warm eye contact, steady speech, and a calm, self-assured tone. His file shows four prior therapists in three years, each ending treatment after Ray described them as having misunderstood him. His baseline manner is smooth, composed, and slightly flattering.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:14 — Ray recounts his failed treatments; voice stays level, no strain, no hesitation","09:17 — He says 'You're different, I can tell' and leans forward; pupils steady, posture relaxed","09:21 — Asked about the earliest therapist, he answers instantly with detail, showing no mental effort to recall","09:26 — He smiles when describing how each therapist gave up on him; no distress cues","09:31 — He compliments your insight three times within two minutes"],"interrogation_transcript":["Ray: 'The first four just never really listened. Dr. Kim said she understood, then she dropped me after two months. You already get me better than she did.'","Ray: 'I've read that sometimes a patient just needs one therapist who clicks. I feel that with you.'","Ray: 'My last therapist actually said I was the most honest client she'd ever had.'","Ray: 'So you'll take me on, right? You can see I'm different from what they said.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"High risk — deceptive. The polished narrative of repeated failed treatments combined with instant flattery is a cognitive illusion, the 'psychopathic charm' warning sign. Do not take on this patient.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Ray's tale of multiple failed therapists matches the exact profile Kahneman's clinical teacher warned about: the patient says everyone failed him and that you are different. The teacher's verdict was blunt, 'Throw him out of the office.' The absence of any strain or recall effort during emotionally loaded answers, while he works smoothly to flatter you, fits practiced psychopathic charm rather than genuine distress. Like the fins on the Müller-Lyer lines, the strong pull you feel is a danger sign your System 1 produces automatically and must not be trusted or acted on.","distractors":["Truthful and mistreated — the prior therapists genuinely failed him and he is simply hopeful","Borderline personality — the instability in his treatment history is the real red flag","Performance anxiety — his smoothness is overcompensation for nervousness about being judged"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Gut impressions that misread truthfulness","visual_frame_description":"A recorded procurement negotiation plays in a review suite. On the left, the supplier representative Elena re-checks her notes and asks for a calculator; on the right, the manager's typed objections appear with timestamps. A verdict panel waits for the player's diagnosis of whether Elena was deceptive or the manager's gut was an unreliable System 1 impression.","player_mission":"Audit the recording: compare the rep's baseline behavior to the timed log and transcript, then decide whether the manager's 'gut feeling' was evidence of deception or a System 1 impression that misread truthfulness.","key_concepts":["System 1 generates impressions and feelings automatically; System 2 generally believes its impressions and acts on them.","Fast thinking gives you a premonition or feeling with the sense that it just happened, without intention.","System 1 can detect hostility in a voice and make quick assessments of people without effort.","Impressions are not always beliefs: Kahneman stresses the distinction between impressions and beliefs.","Cognitive illusions show that a strong feeling can persist even when it is known to be wrong.","Errors of intuitive thought are difficult to prevent because System 2 may have no clue to the error.","The book's premise is that it is easier to recognize other people's mistakes than our own.","When all goes smoothly, System 2 adopts the suggestions of System 1 with little or no modification.","The best approach is a compromise: recognize situations in which mistakes are likely and try harder when the stakes are high.","The psychopathic-charm case illustrates that a compelling System 1 attraction is a danger sign, not evidence."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the reliability of System 1 impressions: the manager's gut feeling about deception is an automatic System 1 judgment that System 2 lazily endorses, even though impressions can be cognitive illusions. Kahneman emphasizes that fast, effortless impressions feel certain and are usually trusted, which is exactly why they can mislead when stakes are high.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A senior procurement manager insists a supplier's rep was lying because 'something felt off.' Audit the recording: was the rep deceptive, or was the manager's gut feeling an unreliable System 1 impression?","subject_baseline":"The rep, Elena, negotiates price sheets weekly. In routine contract talks she speaks at a steady pace, answers promptly, and occasionally re-checks her notes. Colleagues describe her as brisk and direct; she does not flatter or small-talk.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02 — Asked about the defect rate, Elena pauses 2 seconds and looks down at her notes; voice stays steady","14:06 — Manager says the offer 'feels wrong'; Elena's brows rise, she blinks rapidly for a moment","14:10 — Elena answers the exact figure question with clear numeric detail, no hesitation","14:15 — Elena asks for a calculator to recompute her own quote, showing visible care","14:22 — After the call, Elena rubs her eyes and exhales loudly"],"interrogation_transcript":["Manager: 'Your quoted rate can't be right. I have a feeling you're holding something back.'","Elena: 'I can give you the exact numbers from our line logs. They're in this folder.'","Manager: 'You hesitated when I asked about the defects. That's when people lie.'","Elena: 'I paused because I wanted to check the batch number before I said anything. That's all.'","Manager: 'Look, I've interviewed hundreds of vendors. I trust my gut. It says no.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"No deception. The 'off' feeling was a System 1 impression treated as evidence; Elena's behavior shows care and verification, and the manager mistook ordinary deliberation for deceit.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Every cue the manager cites, the pause, the downward glance, the blink, is ordinary effortful verification, and System 1's read of hostility and deception is quick but not authoritative. Kahneman notes that System 1 impressions feel certain and that System 2 generally believes them, yet impressions can be illusions. The manager anchored on his gut and let the feeling drive the verdict, converting a routine negotiation into a rejection. The audit shows the rep recomputing her own quote and offering line logs, behavior consistent with truthfulness, not the smooth practiced evasion of the psychopathic-charm pattern.","distractors":["Deceptive — the pause before answering the defect question is a classic concealment cue","Deceptive — rapid blinking under pressure indicates fabrication","Indeterminate — gaze and hesitation data are too weak to draw any conclusion about Elena"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Invisible Gorilla and inattentional blindness","visual_frame_description":"A training room where twelve new security officers watch a basketball-passing video on a large screen, each instructed to count the passes made by the white team and ignore the black team. A woman in a gorilla suit crosses the court, thumps her chest, and exits, while most eyes stay locked on the white players. A counter shows how many officers report seeing anything unusual.","player_mission":"Observe the training outcome, answer why so many officers missed the gorilla, then decide how to deploy attention in a real surveillance shift to avoid the same failure mode.","key_concepts":["In the gorilla study, viewers counting passes by the white team missed a woman in a gorilla suit that was in view for 9 seconds.","About half of the many thousands who have seen the video do not notice anything unusual.","It is the counting task, especially the instruction to ignore one team, that causes the blindness.","No one who watches the video without the task would miss the gorilla.","Seeing and orienting are automatic functions of System 1, but they depend on the allocation of some attention to the relevant stimulus.","The most remarkable observation of the study is that people find its results very surprising.","Viewers who fail to see the gorilla are initially sure that it was not there.","The gorilla study illustrates that we can be blind to the obvious and also blind to our blindness.","Surprise activates and orients your attention: you stare and search your memory for a story that makes sense of the surprising event."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests inattentional blindness: the gorilla study by Chabris and Simons shows that intense focusing on a counting task makes people blind even to striking stimuli that normally attract attention. Kahneman uses it to show that attention allocation gates even System 1's automatic seeing, and that we are blind to our own blindness.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that paying close, effortful attention can make people blind to the obvious: in the gorilla video, about half of viewers counting basketball passes entirely miss a woman in a gorilla suit who crosses the court, thumps her chest, and is in view for nine seconds. We can be blind to the obvious and blind to our blindness.","stage_1_observation":"A training room. Twelve new security officers watch the basketball-passing video on a large screen, each instructed to count the passes made by the white team and ignore the black team. On a side camera you see half the officers' eyes locked on the white players. A woman in a gorilla suit crosses the court, beats her chest, and exits. Afterward, the instructor asks how many officers saw the gorilla.","stage_1_question":"Roughly how many of the twelve officers report seeing the gorilla, and why?","stage_1_correct_answer":"About half, about six, report nothing unusual, because the absorbing counting task and the instruction to ignore one team caused the blindness; seeing and orienting depend on the allocation of attention, and the observers were also blind to their own blindness.","stage_2_intervention":"The same officers must now guard a busy plaza and are told to stay hyper-focused on the entrance. A decoy theft takes place at the opposite end of the plaza. The shift supervisor must decide how to deploy attention to avoid a repeat of the gorilla failure.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Do not rely on one officer's tunneled focus; the gorilla finding shows focused attention creates blindness, so the supervisor should assign a second observer or a rotation that periodically reallocates attention and scanning. Intense focusing makes people effectively blind to stimuli that normally attract attention, so a single hyper-focused watcher is exactly the failure mode the video demonstrates."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Recognizing cognitive illusions in the clinic","visual_frame_description":"A quiet office at a community clinic. Dr. Ngo, a junior therapist, reads an intake note describing a new client, Marcus, who reports three prior therapists who all failed him and insists Dr. Ngo is different and truly understands him. A warm, flattering pull flickers over Dr. Ngo's face as she begins planning a long-term treatment, while the supervising clinician watches from the doorway.","player_mission":"Recognize the cognitive-illusion pattern in the intake, decide how Dr. Ngo should respond, then advise the supervisor when the therapist hesitates.","key_concepts":["A patient who tells a disturbing tale of multiple mistakes in previous treatments and insists 'you are different' is most likely a psychopath.","Kahneman's teacher said of such a patient: do not even think of taking him on; throw him out of the office.","The sympathy felt for the patient is not under voluntary control; it arises from System 1.","The teacher was not teaching general suspicion of patients, but that strong attraction to a patient with repeated failed treatment is a danger sign.","The analogy to the Müller-Lyer illusion is close: the feeling is an illusion to be recognized and refused.","System 2 is in charge of self-control, and its task is to overcome the impulses of System 1.","The book's premise is that it is easier to recognize other people's mistakes than our own.","The best compromise is to learn to recognize situations where mistakes are likely and try harder when the stakes are high."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the cognitive-illusion concept: System 1 automatically generates a compelling, flattering impression that feels true, but the impression is an illusion that System 2 must recognize and refuse. Kahneman reports the clinical warning against 'psychopathic charm,' a patient with a string of failed treatments who insists you are different, as a textbook cognitive illusion akin to the Müller-Lyer illusion.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that a compelling System 1 feeling can be a cognitive illusion that must be recognized, not believed. A strong, instant attraction to a patient with a history of failed treatments is a danger sign, analogous to the fins on the Müller-Lyer lines.","stage_1_observation":"A quiet office at a community clinic. Dr. Ngo, a junior therapist, reads an intake note: the new client, Marcus, describes three prior therapists who all failed him and says he immediately feels Dr. Ngo is different and truly understands him. Dr. Ngo notices a warm, flattering pull toward Marcus and begins planning a long-term treatment plan. The supervising clinician is watching the file.","stage_1_question":"What is happening, and what should Dr. Ngo's first move be?","stage_1_correct_answer":"This is the cognitive-illusion pattern from Kahneman's clinical training: a patient with repeated failed treatments who insists 'you are different' is most likely a psychopath, and the therapist's strong attraction is the danger sign. Dr. Ngo should recognize the pattern and decline to take on the patient; the teacher's verdict was to throw him out of the office.","stage_2_intervention":"Dr. Ngo hesitates and tells the supervisor, 'But my gut says I can really help him.' The supervisor must decide how to respond to keep the clinic safe while respecting Dr. Ngo's feelings.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The supervisor should confirm the feeling is real but insist it is an illusion that must not be acted on: the sympathy is an automatic System 1 response that cannot be switched off, but recognizing the pattern, like recognizing the Müller-Lyer fins, means not believing or acting on it. The advice from the authority on psychopathy confirms the warning was sound."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"When System 2 takes over from System 1","visual_frame_description":"A kitchen at a dinner party with twelve guests around a table under warm lights and easy conversation. A small dog in the corner suddenly lets out a loud, cat-like meow. Every head snaps toward the dog, glasses rattle, and the host, Priya, stops mid-sentence and stares. A caption asks the player to explain the surge of attention and later to advise Priya on a high-stakes family decision.","player_mission":"Explain why the room's attention surged at the surprising sound, then decide how Priya should handle a warm, high-stakes financial decision where System 1 says it will be fine.","key_concepts":["System 1 runs automatically and continuously generates suggestions for System 2: impressions, intuitions, intentions, and feelings.","When all goes smoothly, System 2 adopts the suggestions of System 1 with little or no modification.","System 2 is mobilized when a question arises for which System 1 does not offer an answer, as with 17 x 24.","System 2 is activated when an event is detected that violates the model of the world that System 1 maintains.","In System 1's world, lamps do not jump, cats do not bark, and gorillas do not cross basketball courts.","Surprise activates and orients your attention: you stare and search your memory for a story that makes sense of the surprising event.","System 2 is credited with the continuous monitoring of your own behavior, keeping you polite when angry and alert when driving at night.","System 2 is mobilized to increased effort when it detects an error about to be made.","The best compromise is to learn to recognize situations in which mistakes are likely and to try harder when the stakes are high."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests when System 2 is mobilized: System 1 answers the easy, routine moments, but a surprise that violates System 1's model of the world, a meowing dog, triggers System 2's attention and memory search, and difficult or high-stakes judgments require its effortful, rule-following review. Kahneman's plot synopsis holds that System 1 originates most thought but System 2 takes over when things get difficult and normally has the last word.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the plot of the two systems: System 1 runs automatically and continuously generates impressions and impulses; System 2 is normally lazy and endorses them, but is mobilized when System 1 runs into difficulty, when a question has no ready answer, or when an event violates System 1's model of the world. System 2 normally has the last word, and self-control means knowing when to bring it online.","stage_1_observation":"A kitchen at a dinner party. Twelve guests sit around a table; the lights are warm and conversation is easy. Suddenly a small dog in the corner lets out a loud, cat-like meow. Every head snaps toward the dog, glasses rattle, and the host, Priya, stops mid-sentence and stares, searching her memory for a story that makes sense of the moment.","stage_1_question":"Why did the entire room's attention surge at the meowing dog?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the event violated the model of the world that System 1 maintains, cats meow and dogs bark, and System 2 is activated whenever such an event is detected, orienting attention and triggering a memory search for a story that makes sense of the surprise. The cat-barking world is exactly the set of violations that mobilizes System 2.","stage_2_intervention":"Later the same night, Priya must decide whether to lend her brother a large sum to start a business. Her System 1 says 'he's family, it'll be fine.' The stakes are high and she knows mistakes are likelier in exactly this kind of warm, trusting moment. She must decide how to handle the decision.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Priya should recognize that this is a high-stakes situation where System 1's fluent, optimistic suggestion needs deliberate System 2 review: the compromise Kahneman recommends is to learn to recognize situations in which mistakes are likely and to try harder when the stakes are high. System 2 is the agent that follows rules, compares options on several attributes, and can make the deliberate choice, and it normally has the last word."}]},{"id":"ch07","title":"2. Attention and Effort","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Pupils as a window to mental effort","visual_frame_description":"A dark psychology laboratory. A participant's head rests on a chin-and-forehead frame while an infrared flash pictures her eye each second. On the corridor wall, a projector shows the pupil blown up to a foot across, and two researchers watch it dilate and contract like a metronome-driven dial. The player must classify what the pupil reveals.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the column that names what the pupil data demonstrate. Then choose the column that best captures the pupillometry findings on mental effort.","key_concepts":["The pupil is a sensitive indicator of mental effort: it dilates substantially when people multiply two-digit numbers.","Hess observed that pupils dilate more for hard problems than for easy ones, and that this response is distinct from emotional arousal.","The shape of the pupil response over a paced task was an inverted V.","Longer digit strings reliably caused larger dilations, and the transformation task compounded the effort.","Add-1 with four digits caused a larger dilation than the task of holding seven digits for immediate recall.","Add-3, the most demanding task Kahneman ever observed, dilates the pupil by about 50% in the first 5 seconds and raises heart rate by about 7 beats per minute.","When subjects were given more digits than they could remember, their pupils stopped dilating or actually shrank.","Routine conversation demanded little effort, no more than retaining two or three digits.","The pupil offers an index of the current rate at which mental energy is used, like the electricity meter outside a house."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the pupillometry mechanism: the pupil dilates with mental effort, tracks demand second by second, and contracts when the effort ends, giving a visible 'window to the soul.' Kahneman and Jackson Beatty built the Add-1 and Add-3 paced tasks precisely to read this index, and found the pupil's inverted-V curve mirrors subjective mental strain.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which column best captures what Kahneman and Jackson Beatty found about the pupil during mental work?","column_a_label":"The pupil tracks mental effort second by second — dilating as effort builds and contracting when it ends","column_b_label":"The pupil is a fixed measure of intelligence that never varies during a task","column_c_label":"The pupil responds only to emotional arousal, not to effort","rows":[{"label":"Longer digit strings reliably caused larger pupil dilations","col":"a"},{"label":"The peak of pupil size coincided with maximum effort","col":"a"},{"label":"Add-1 with four digits caused a larger dilation than holding seven digits for recall","col":"a"},{"label":"The pupil contracted immediately when the participant found a solution or gave up","col":"a"},{"label":"Routine conversation left the pupil small, as undemanding as holding two or three digits","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The pupil constricts whenever a task gets harder","Pupil size only changes when someone is lying","The pupil dilates randomly and tells us nothing about effort"],"explanation":"Beatty and Kahneman's paced tasks showed the pupil is a faithful second-by-second index of mental effort: it dilates with every added digit, peaks with maximum effort, and relaxes as memory is unloaded, and it stays small during effortless conversation."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Selective allocation of attention under overload","visual_frame_description":"A testing booth in the 1970s University of Michigan lab. A subject on the chin rest performs the Add-1 digit task while a screen flashes letters; a researcher in the corridor watches the giant pupil image and notes that when the digit load peaks, the flashing letter K goes unseen even though the subject's open eye is staring straight at it. The player must classify the mechanism.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the column that names the mechanism at work. Then choose the column that explains why the subject missed the letter K at peak effort.","key_concepts":["In the Add-1 blindness study, subjects almost never missed a K shown at the beginning or near the end of the task.","The letter K was missed almost half the time when mental effort was at its peak, although the camera showed a wide-open eye staring straight at it.","Failures of detection followed the same inverted-V pattern as the dilating pupil.","The pupil proved a good measure of the physical arousal that accompanies mental effort.","System 2 protects the most important activity, so it receives the attention it needs.","Spare capacity is allocated second by second to other tasks.","The timing of the visual target had no effect on the main task, confirming the priority instruction was followed.","In emergencies, System 1 takes over and assigns total priority to self-protective actions.","The sophisticated allocation of attention has been honed by a long evolutionary history of responding to threats and opportunities."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests selective attention under overload: mental capacity is limited but does not trip like a breaker; System 2 shields the highest-priority activity and rations spare capacity, so peripheral stimuli are simply not seen at peak effort. Kahneman's Add-1 version of the gorilla study showed the letter K vanishing exactly when effort peaked, even with the subject's eye open on it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which column best explains why the Add-1 subjects missed the flashing letter K almost half the time at peak effort yet never faltered on the digit task?","column_a_label":"Selective allocation — System 2 protects the most important activity and doles out spare capacity","column_b_label":"Breaker-trip overload — the whole mind shuts down like a tripped circuit","column_c_label":"Motivational failure — the subjects secretly stopped paying attention","rows":[{"label":"Participants were told to give the digit task complete priority and they followed the instruction","col":"a"},{"label":"The timing of the visual target had no effect on the main digit task","col":"a"},{"label":"If the critical letter was presented at a time of high demand, the subjects simply did not see it","col":"a"},{"label":"When the transformation task was less demanding, detection performance was better","col":"a"},{"label":"Spare capacity is allocated second by second to other tasks","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Mental overload trips a breaker, knocking out all processing at once","The subjects chose to ignore the letters out of boredom","Effort has no effect on what a person can see"],"explanation":"Unlike a breaker that trips and kills all circuits, mental overload is selective: System 2 protects the most important activity so the digits always get processed, while spare capacity for the letter collapses, which is why the wide-open eye missed a K that was staring straight back at it."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Task sets, switching, and the cost of rapid switching","visual_frame_description":"A modern usability lab. A designer, Kojo, has just spent ten minutes counting the letter f on a page for a training exercise; the instructor then tells him to count commas on the next page. Kojo visibly slows, re-reads lines, and rubs his forehead. A brain-scan panel beside the screen glows in the prefrontal area. The player must classify what makes the switch hard.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the column naming the mechanism it demonstrates. Then choose the column that best explains why switching from f-counting to comma-counting is so hard.","key_concepts":["System 2 can program memory to obey an instruction that overrides habitual responses, an ability called adopting a task set.","Psychologists speak of 'executive control' for the adoption and termination of task sets.","Neuroscientists have identified brain regions serving the executive function, including the prefrontal area.","Switching from one task to another is effortful, especially under time pressure.","The need for rapid switching is one of the reasons Add-3 and mental multiplication are so difficult.","Modern tests of working memory require switching between two demanding tasks, retaining one result while performing the other.","People who do well on switching tests tend to do well on tests of general intelligence.","Measures of efficiency in the control of attention predict performance of air traffic controllers and of Israeli Air Force pilots beyond the effects of intelligence.","Counting the letter f is a task you have never performed before, so setting yourself up for it is effortful, though you improve with practice."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests executive control and task-switching: System 2 programs memory with a task set, and switching to a competing set is effortful, especially under time pressure. Kahneman reports that the prefrontal cortex serves this function and that rapid switching is a key reason Add-3 and mental multiplication demand so much effort.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which column best explains why switching from counting the letter f to counting commas on the next page is effortful?","column_a_label":"Adoption and switching of task sets under executive control","column_b_label":"System 1's automatic statistical inference","column_c_label":"The pupil's baseline resting state","rows":[{"label":"You must overcome the newly acquired tendency to focus attention on the letter f","col":"a"},{"label":"Switching from one task to another is effortful, especially under time pressure","col":"a"},{"label":"Rapid switching is one reason Add-3 and mental multiplication are so difficult","col":"a"},{"label":"Modern working-memory tests require switching between two demanding tasks","col":"a"},{"label":"The prefrontal area of the brain is involved in operations we associate with intelligence","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Counting letters draws on System 1's automatic pattern detection","The difficulty comes from the letters being emotional stimuli","The task is hard only because the page is printed in a small font"],"explanation":"Psychologists call the adoption and termination of task sets 'executive control,' and switching from one set to another is effortful, especially under time pressure. Counting commas means suppressing the freshly learned f-focus, and the prefrontal cortex serves this executive function."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Pupil dilation as an index of fabrication effort","visual_frame_description":"An internal investigator's review suite plays a videotaped interview with a finance clerk, Dev, while a live pupil-trace graph scrolls beneath the footage, spiking and then collapsing at specific timestamps. A verdict panel awaits the player's diagnosis of when and whether Dev fabricated his story.","player_mission":"Compare Dev's baseline pupil behavior to the timed log and transcript, then determine whether he was fabricating and at which point the pattern gives it away.","key_concepts":["The pupil is a sensitive indicator of mental effort and dilates substantially during mental work.","When subjects were given more digits than they could remember, their pupils stopped dilating or actually shrank.","The pupil contracted immediately when the participant found a solution or gave up.","The size of the pupil varied second by second, reflecting the changing demands of the task.","The pupil offers an index of the current rate at which mental energy is used, like an electricity meter.","Routine conversation demanded little effort, no more than holding two or three digits.","Add-3, the most demanding task, dilates the pupil by about 50% of its original area and raises heart rate by about 7 beats per minute.","Failures of detection followed the same inverted-V pattern as the dilating pupil.","Mental life is normally conducted at the pace of a comfortable walk, with occasional sprints."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the pupil as an effort index in a deception context: fabrication is a mental sprint that drives the pupil up, and the moment the story is abandoned the pupil contracts, the same signature Kahneman observed when subjects exceeded their memory and gave up. Because people decide what to do but have limited control over the effort of doing it, the pupil reveals effort that words can hide.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An internal investigator watches a videotaped interview in which a finance clerk, Dev, answers questions about a missing payment. Using the pupil index from the Add-1 research, is Dev fabricating, and at which point?","subject_baseline":"During the first five minutes of routine questions about his schedule, Dev's pupils stay small and steady, his voice is even, and he answers within a beat. His baseline reads as calm and cognitively unloaded, like a casual stroll.","observed_nonverbal_log":["11:00 — Routine questions; pupils small and stable","11:06 — Asked to recall where he was at 3 p.m. on Friday; pupils dilate sharply and heart rate ticks up","11:07 — He stalls, 'I was at the office, I think,' pupils at peak","11:09 — Asked to produce the receipt; pupils constrict suddenly as he says 'I don't have it'","11:11 — Asked again about the afternoon; pupils stay small, voice flat","11:14 — Asked whether anyone else touched the payment; pupils dilate again, then shrink as he gives up on an explanation"],"interrogation_transcript":["Investigator: 'Where were you Friday at 3 p.m.?'","Dev: 'I was at the office, I think. The back desk.'","Investigator: 'Can you show me the receipt?'","Dev: 'I don't have it. It must have been filed wrong.'","Investigator: 'Who else could have touched the payment?'","Dev: 'I really couldn't say. People come and go.'","Investigator: 'Did you move the money yourself?'","Dev: 'No. I told you, no.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Deceptive — Dev's pupil peaked exactly while he built a shaky alibi and shrank the moment he abandoned it, matching the pattern of a subject who has exceeded what memory can support and gives up.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The 11:06 dilation marks effort far above his baseline: inventing a plausible location is a mental sprint, not recall. The constriction at 11:09 is the giveaway Kahneman describes, the pupil contracts immediately when the person finds a solution or gives up; here Dev gave up the fabrication and settled for 'filed wrong.' His flat, small pupils on re-questions show no genuine memory search. When asked about an accomplice, effort again spikes and then collapses, the signature of a story abandoned because nothing further could be constructed. The pupil, like the electricity meter, indexes the rate of mental energy use.","distractors":["Truthful but nervous — the dilations are simple anxiety about being interviewed","Truthful — recollection of a routine Friday genuinely takes effort","Indeterminate — pupil size has no known connection to effort or deception"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The overloaded employee who didn't hear the instruction","visual_frame_description":"A floor supervisor's office. On one screen, meeting footage shows engineer Nora with her eyes locked on a live production dashboard while a safety instruction is delivered to the room; on the other screen, an incident report accuses her of ignoring it. A verdict panel asks the player whether Nora was defiant or cognitively overloaded.","player_mission":"Audit the incident: compare Nora's record and baseline to the timed observation log and transcript, then decide whether she was being defiant or genuinely did not hear the instruction.","key_concepts":["People engaged in a mental sprint may become effectively blind to surrounding stimuli.","In the Add-1 study, subjects missed the target letter almost half the time when mental effort was at its peak.","The subjects missed the K even though the camera showed a wide-open eye staring straight at it.","The timing of the visual target had no effect on the main task, confirming subjects followed the priority instruction.","Spare capacity is allocated second by second to other tasks.","System 2 protects the most important activity, so it receives the attention it needs.","We can be blind to the obvious and also blind to our blindness.","Mental life is normally a comfortable walk, sometimes jogging, and on rare occasions a frantic sprint.","Kahneman's own line: 'She did not forget about the meeting. She was completely focused on something else when the meeting was set and she just didn't hear you.'"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests attention blindness under overload: an employee at peak mental load can genuinely fail to hear a spoken instruction, exactly as Add-1 subjects missed a flashing letter while their eyes were open on it. Kahneman notes that attentional gaps are selective and precise, and that the conventional 'she just didn't hear you' explanation is exactly right when someone is deep in a mental sprint.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A floor supervisor accuses an engineer, Nora, of ignoring a safety instruction given in a meeting. Nora insists she never heard it. Audit whether Nora is being defiant, or was cognitively overloaded in a way that made her genuinely deaf.","subject_baseline":"Nora is a meticulous engineer who always acknowledges instructions, confirms deadlines in writing, and has never had a disciplinary issue in six years. She is typically composed and attentive during briefings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["16:02 — During the meeting, Nora is simultaneously reconciling a live production dashboard she is responsible for","16:05 — The supervisor states the safety instruction; Nora's eyes remain locked on the dashboard and she shows no head turn or acknowledgment","16:07 — Nora keeps entering figures and appears focused, breathing steady","16:10 — A colleague repeats the instruction directly to Nora; she looks up, surprised, and asks them to repeat it","16:12 — Nora writes the instruction down and confirms it aloud, then apologizes for missing it"],"interrogation_transcript":["Supervisor: 'You heard me say to halt the line until the bracket is replaced. You did not act. Explain.'","Nora: 'I genuinely did not hear the instruction. I was reconciling the live dashboard at that moment.'","Supervisor: 'The whole room heard it. You were standing right there.'","Nora: 'I'm sorry. My attention was fully on the dashboard figures. I missed it.'","Colleague: 'She really looked startled when I repeated it to her. I don't think she was faking.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"No defiance — Nora was effectively blind to the spoken instruction because she was engaged in a mental sprint, the same overload that made Add-1 subjects miss a flashing letter K that their open eyes were staring at.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The failure is selective and precise, not a lack of intent: Nora was performing a demanding working-memory task when the instruction was given, and System 2 protects the most important activity while spare capacity is rationed second by second. The supervisor's conclusion that the whole room heard it is the gorilla-blindness error: other people with spare attention heard it, but a person at peak load can be blind to the obvious and blind to their blindness. Nora's surprise when the instruction was repeated, and her immediate written confirmation, match an attentional gap rather than defiance.","distractors":["Defiant — Nora deliberately ignored the instruction to stall the line","Deceptive — she claims forgetfulness to avoid accountability for a shutdown","Unreliable — her memory is poor and she frequently forgets directives"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The mental sprint and the point of giving up","visual_frame_description":"A 1970s laboratory corridor. Through the glass, a participant on a chin rest works a paced mental-multiplication task while the projected image of her pupil fills the wall. The researchers watch the large pupil stay big for several seconds, then suddenly contract, and they lean toward the intercom to ask why she stopped. The player must interpret the give-up signal.","player_mission":"Interpret the sudden contraction of the projected pupil, then decide whether to keep pushing a participant on an impossible Add-3 trial or read the pupil signal and stop.","key_concepts":["Add-3 is the most demanding task Kahneman ever observed; few people can cope with more than four digits in Add-1.","In the first 5 seconds of Add-3, the pupil dilates by about 50% of its original area and heart rate increases by about 7 beats per minute.","During a mental multiplication, the pupil dilated to a large size within a few seconds and stayed large as long as the individual worked.","The pupil contracted immediately when she found a solution or gave up.","When subjects were exposed to more digits than they could remember, their pupils stopped dilating or actually shrank.","The researchers could divine when the participant gave up, and their favorite answer was: 'We have a window to your soul.'","Mental life is normally conducted at the pace of a comfortable walk, sometimes interrupted by jogging, and on rare occasions by a frantic sprint.","Add-1 and Add-3 exercises are sprints, while casual chatting is a stroll.","However much you want to live, you cannot exert as much effort as an Add-3 transformation demands; you have limited control over the effort of doing it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the ceiling on mental effort: the pupil dilates and stays large through a mental sprint and contracts the instant the person solves or gives up, marking the hard limit of working memory. Kahneman's Add-3 data show the pupil swelling about 50% in five seconds, and that beyond this ceiling the pupil stops dilating or shrinks, the unmistakable give-up signal.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that mental effort is a limited resource you can spend up to a hard ceiling: the Add-3 task is 'as hard as people can work,' dilating the pupil by about 50% and raising heart rate by about 7 beats per minute in the first 5 seconds, and when more is demanded than memory can hold, people give up and their pupils stop dilating or shrink.","stage_1_observation":"A 1970s laboratory corridor. Through the glass, a participant on a chin rest works a paced mental-multiplication task while the projected image of her pupil fills the wall. The researchers watch the large pupil stay big for several seconds, then suddenly contract. They lean in and ask over the intercom: 'Why did you stop working just now?'","stage_1_question":"Why did the researchers already know she had quit, and why did the pupil suddenly contract?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the pupil stays large as long as the person keeps working and contracts immediately when she finds a solution or gives up, so the sudden contraction was the visible signature of quitting. The participant, surprised, asked 'How did you know?' and the answer was: 'We have a window to your soul.'","stage_2_intervention":"The same participant is next handed Add-3 with six digits, far beyond what anyone can hold. The researchers must decide whether to keep pushing her to try harder or read the pupil signal and stop.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Read the pupil and stop: when subjects were exposed to more digits than they could remember, their pupils stopped dilating or actually shrank; this is as hard as people can work, and they give up if more is asked of them. Pushing further has no value; the pupil shows the ceiling has been reached."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The law of least effort at work","visual_frame_description":"A finance team's shared drive. Analyst Tasha must produce a quarterly reconciliation and hesitates between two shortcuts on her desktop: an old template and a blank data-linked workbook. She opens the old template while her teammate Ibrahim watches. A caption asks the player to name the principle behind her choice and then advise whether to invest the effort.","player_mission":"Identify the principle behind Tasha's choice of the easy template, then advise the team on whether building the robust workbook is worth the effort when errors are costly.","key_concepts":["A general 'law of least effort' applies to cognitive as well as physical exertion.","If there are several ways of achieving the same goal, people eventually gravitate to the least demanding course of action.","In the economy of action, effort is a cost, and the acquisition of skill is driven by the balance of benefits and costs.","Laziness is built deep into our nature.","The defining feature of System 2 is that its operations are effortful, and one of its main characteristics is laziness.","As you become skilled in a task, its demand for energy diminishes, with fewer brain regions involved.","Highly intelligent individuals need less effort to solve the same problems, as indicated by both pupil size and brain activity.","We normally avoid mental overload by dividing our tasks into multiple easy steps and committing intermediate results to memory or to paper."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the law of least effort: when several paths lead to the same goal, System 2 lazily takes the least demanding one, because effort is a cost in the economy of action. Kahneman argues this laziness is built into our nature, but also that skill and intelligence reduce effort, so the compromise is to mobilize effort deliberately when the stakes justify it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the law of least effort: if there are several ways of achieving the same goal, people gravitate to the least demanding course of action; effort is a cost in the economy of action, and laziness is built deep into our nature. It also drives skill acquisition, because as you become skilled, a task's demand for energy diminishes.","stage_1_observation":"A finance team's shared drive. Analyst Tasha must produce a quarterly reconciliation. She can copy the previous quarter's template and tweak the numbers, low effort with higher error risk, or build a fresh data-linked workbook, more effort but robust. She hesitates over the two shortcuts on her desktop, then opens the old template. Her teammate Ibrahim watches.","stage_1_question":"Which principle best explains Tasha's choice of the old template?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The law of least effort: given several routes to the same goal, people gravitate to the least demanding course. Effort is a cost, and System 2 is inherently lazy, reluctant to invest more effort than strictly necessary, so the familiar, easy template wins.","stage_2_intervention":"Ibrahim suggests they build the robust workbook together and split the effort. The team lead is skeptical and says the quick template has always been fine. The team must decide whether to invest the effort.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Invest the effort when the stakes justify it: the law of least effort explains the default pull toward the easy template, but it is exactly the lazy System 2 tendency that should be overcome when errors are costly. As you become skilled at a task its demand for energy diminishes, so the robust workbook gets easier with practice, and the compromise is to try harder when the stakes are high."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Protecting the main task under cognitive overload","visual_frame_description":"An operating theater between cases. Surgeon Priya is mentally rehearsing the next procedure and reconciling the day's surgical counts while a colleague, Dr. Ade, twice says her name about a scheduling change. Priya does not look up or respond, and Dr. Ade grows visibly irritated. The player must decide how Dr. Ade should deliver the change so it is actually received.","player_mission":"Determine whether Priya is ignoring Dr. Ade or is at peak mental load, then choose the delivery method that will actually get the message through.","key_concepts":["The response to mental overload is selective and precise: System 2 protects the most important activity, so it receives the attention it needs.","Spare capacity is allocated second by second to other tasks.","In the Add-1 study, the letter K was missed almost half the time at peak effort even with the subject's open eye staring straight at it.","The timing of the visual target had no effect on the main task, confirming that priority was assigned to the digits.","If the critical letter was presented at a time of high demand, the subjects simply did not see it.","When the transformation task was less demanding, detection performance was better.","In emergencies, System 1 takes over and assigns total priority to self-protective actions.","People engaged in a mental sprint may become effectively blind, as the gorilla was made invisible by the counting task.","'She did not forget about the meeting. She was completely focused on something else when the meeting was set and she just didn't hear you.'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the selective-allocation mechanism of attention: under overload System 2 protects the priority task and rations the rest, so a person can genuinely fail to register a message delivered at the moment of peak effort. Kahneman's letter-K result shows the effect is about timing and capacity; a message delivered when spare attention exists is heard, and one delivered at peak load is simply not processed.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is selective attention under overload: System 2 shields the highest-priority activity and rations spare capacity second by second, so a person at peak load can be genuinely unaware of what happens around them, even of a letter flashing at their open eye. This explains why someone 'didn't hear you' and why high-stakes operators guard their mental load.","stage_1_observation":"An operating theater, a clean-up between cases. Surgeon Priya is mentally rehearsing the next procedure and reconciling the day's surgical counts while a colleague, Dr. Ade, twice says her name about a scheduling change. Priya does not look up or respond. Dr. Ade is visibly irritated and starts to repeat herself more loudly, assuming Priya is ignoring her.","stage_1_question":"Is Priya ignoring Dr. Ade, and what should Dr. Ade infer from the non-response?","stage_1_correct_answer":"No; Priya is at peak mental load, and overload is selective: System 2 protects the most important activity, the surgical plan and counts, and rations spare capacity, so the spoken message falls outside what attention can capture. Like the Add-1 subjects who missed the letter K with their eyes open, Priya can be blind to the obvious and blind to her blindness.","stage_2_intervention":"Dr. Ade must decide how to deliver the scheduling change so it is actually received. She can keep repeating it louder, wait and confirm Priya is free then state the change and ask Priya to repeat it back, or file the change in the shared calendar and assume Priya will see it.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose to wait until Priya is out of the demanding task, state the change, and have her confirm it aloud. The research shows a person at peak effort simply does not process the input; the timing of the stimulus, not its loudness, determines detection, so the fix is to deliver the message when spare capacity exists, not to escalate volume."}]},{"id":"ch08","title":"3. The Lazy Controller","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Cognitive Load, Ego Depletion, and Flow","visual_frame_description":"The player faces a sorting board with three columns labeled Cognitive Load, Ego Depletion, and Flow. Video-clip cards float in a tray: a frazzled accountant juggling numbers, a drained manager reaching for a chocolate cake, and a painter absorbed in her canvas. The player drags each clip card into the column that names the mechanism at work.","player_mission":"Classify all seven real-life episodes into the mechanism they best demonstrate: cognitive busyness (System 2 overloaded right now), ego depletion (willpower spent earlier, motivation drained), or flow (effortless, absorbing concentration). Finish the board with every card in the correct column.","key_concepts":["Self-control and deliberate thought draw on the same limited budget of mental effort.","People who are cognitively busy are more likely to choose a tempting chocolate cake over a virtuous fruit salad.","Cognitive busyness loosens System 2's hold on behavior, making selfish choices, sexist language, and superficial judgments more likely.","Unlike ego depletion, cognitive load cannot be fixed by simply trying harder.","Ego depletion is at least in part a loss of motivation: after exerting self-control you feel unwilling, not unable, to make further effort.","Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi named the state of effortless attending 'flow,' describing it as a state of effortless concentration so deep that people lose their sense of time and self.","In flow, maintaining focused attention requires no exertion of self-control, freeing resources for the task at hand.","Activities that impose high demands on System 2 require self-control, and the exertion of self-control is depleting and unpleasant.","Baumeister's experiments show that all variants of voluntary effort draw at least partly on a shared pool of mental energy."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the effect of cognitive load on self-control: when System 2 is busy holding digits in short-term memory, System 1 has more influence over behavior, so people yield to temptations such as chocolate cake. The chapter presents the digit-load dessert finding as evidence that self-control and cognitive effort draw on the same limited budget of mental work.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Your colleague Nina is holding a seven-digit delivery code in working memory while the team orders dessert, and she picks the chocolate cake over the fruit salad. Into which column does her choice sort?","column_a_label":"Cognitive Load (System 2 busy right now)","column_b_label":"Ego Depletion (willpower spent earlier)","column_c_label":"Flow (effortless absorbed attention)","rows":[{"label":"Nina holds seven digits in short-term memory and chooses the chocolate cake over the fruit salad.","col":"a"},{"label":"Stifling your emotional reaction to a sad film, then quitting earlier on a painful handgrip task.","col":"b"},{"label":"Riding a motorcycle at 150 miles an hour, focused entirely on the road with no need for self-control.","col":"c"},{"label":"Repeating digits so that System 2 loosens its grip and you use sexist language in a meeting.","col":"a"},{"label":"Eating radishes and celery while resisting cookies, then giving up sooner on a hard logic problem.","col":"b"},{"label":"A chess player in a competitive match, losing all sense of time and self in the game.","col":"c"},{"label":"Trying to hold six digits in memory while doing arithmetic, with no option to compensate by trying harder.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Ego depletion: Nina exhausted her willpower on an earlier task today.","Flow: Nina is so absorbed in remembering the digits that she forgets her diet.","A deliberate System 2 choice: she evaluated the calories and decided the cake is worth it."],"explanation":"Nina's memory load is simultaneous with the choice, which is cognitive busyness, not the residual loss of motivation seen in ego depletion. The book's evidence shows that people who are cognitively busy are more likely to select a tempting dessert, make selfish choices, and judge superficially, because a loaded System 2 gives System 1 more influence over behavior."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Depleters of Self-Control and Signs of Depletion","visual_frame_description":"A diagnostic wall shows two stacks of cue cards. The left stack is titled What Drains Self-Control and shows clips of people suppressing urges: a man told not to think of white bears, a woman fighting tears during a film. The right stack is titled What Signals Depletion and shows a dieter eating chips, a shopper overspending, and a driver snapping at a barista. One card shows a painter lost in her canvas.","player_mission":"Sort each episode into one of three bins: a depleting situation (self-control being spent now), a sign of depletion (the costly result that shows up later), or a flow state (neither). Place all seven cards before the timer runs out.","key_concepts":["All depleting situations involve conflict and the need to suppress a natural tendency.","Avoiding the thought of white bears depletes self-control.","Inhibiting your emotional response to a stirring film depletes self-control.","Making a series of choices that involve conflict depletes self-control.","Trying to impress others depletes self-control.","Signs of depletion include deviating from one's diet, overspending on impulsive purchases, and reacting aggressively to provocation.","Depleted people persist less time in a handgrip task and perform poorly in cognitive tasks and logical decision making.","Flow separates concentration on the task from the deliberate control of attention.","The law of least effort describes people's tendency to avoid effortful mental work when possible."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix separates the situations that deplete self-control from the behaviors that signal depletion has already occurred. Baumeister's research shows that all these conflict-laden exertions draw on a shared pool of mental energy, so suppressing one tendency makes diet, spending, and temper harder to control afterward.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After a day of forcing himself to ignore his inbox, Joel snaps at a barista over a routine question and later admits he skipped the gym. Into which column does this episode belong?","column_a_label":"Depleting situation (self-control spent now)","column_b_label":"Sign of depletion (result of earlier self-control)","column_c_label":"Neither - a flow state","rows":[{"label":"Joel avoids the thought of white bears when told not to think about them.","col":"a"},{"label":"Inhibiting your emotional reaction to a stirring film.","col":"a"},{"label":"Joel deviates from his diet and eats the chips he planned to avoid.","col":"b"},{"label":"Overspending on impulsive purchases at the mall.","col":"b"},{"label":"Joel reacts aggressively to a mild provocation from a roommate.","col":"b"},{"label":"Trying to impress others at a networking event all evening.","col":"a"},{"label":"A painter lost in her canvas, unaware of the passing hours.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["All of these episodes are the same thing: cognitive busyness.","Aggression and overspending are caused by hunger alone.","Impressing others is a flow state because it feels rewarding."],"explanation":"The book's depleters all involve suppressing a natural tendency, such as resisting thoughts, stifling emotion, or performing to impress. Its indicators of depletion, such as breaking a diet, impulsive overspending, aggression, and quitting a grip task early, show up later, after self-control has already been spent. The painter is in flow, where absorbing attention needs no self-control at all."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Intelligence, Rationality, and Impulsive System 1","visual_frame_description":"A split screen shows two applicants in adjacent interview booths receiving the same rapid-fire puzzle. One blurts out an answer instantly; the other pauses, frowns, scribbles, and answers more slowly. Between the booths hangs a three-column scoring panel labeled Impulsive System 1, Intelligence/Algorithmic Mind, and Rationality/Engaged System 2.","player_mission":"Classify each behavior into the right column: impulsive System 1 thinking, algorithmic intelligence, or Stanovich's rationality. Read the evidence about puzzle performance, IQ, and delay of gratification, then place all seven cards.","key_concepts":["More than 50% of students at Harvard, MIT, and Princeton gave the intuitive, incorrect answer to the bat-and-ball puzzle.","At less selective universities, the rate of demonstrable failure to check the bat-and-ball answer was in excess of 80%.","The bat-and-ball puzzle costs little to check: a few seconds of mental work with slightly tensed muscles and dilated pupils.","Stanovich distinguishes algorithmic brain power (intelligence) from rationality, a reflective ability to override intuitions.","High intelligence does not make people immune to biases of judgment.","Bat-and-ball-type questions are somewhat better predictors of susceptibility to cognitive errors than conventional IQ tests.","Low scorers on Frederick's Cognitive Reflection Test answer with the first idea that comes to mind and are unwilling to check their intuitions.","Intuitive respondents prefer $3,400 this month over $3,800 next month; only 37% of those who solve all three puzzles do.","Intelligence is not only the ability to reason but also the ability to find relevant material in memory and deploy attention when needed."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests Stanovich's separation of intelligence from rationality: a smart person can still endorse a compelling wrong answer if their System 2 is lazy. The chapter argues that the failure to check intuitive answers is largely a matter of insufficient motivation rather than lacking ability, which is why puzzle-based measures predict biased judgment better than conventional IQ.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Two applicants score identically on an IQ test. In a quick-fire session, one immediately accepts the bat-and-ball answer of 10 cents; the other pauses, works it out, and says 5 cents. Where does the second applicant's behavior belong?","column_a_label":"Impulsive System 1 (lazy thinking)","column_b_label":"Intelligence / algorithmic mind","column_c_label":"Rationality / engaged System 2","rows":[{"label":"Answering with the first idea that comes to mind and never checking it.","col":"a"},{"label":"Excelling on intelligence tests and switching between tasks quickly.","col":"b"},{"label":"Refusing to be satisfied with a superficially attractive answer and checking the logic.","col":"c"},{"label":"Preferring $3,400 this month over $3,800 next month.","col":"a"},{"label":"Paying twice as much for overnight delivery of a book.","col":"a"},{"label":"The ability to find relevant material in memory and deploy attention when needed.","col":"b"},{"label":"Feeling the tug of 'it's true, it's true' on a flawed syllogism and overriding it anyway.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Rationality is identical to high intelligence.","Accepting the intuitive answer proves the person lacks intelligence.","Impatience is a sign of a strong, fast System 2."],"explanation":"Stanovich distinguishes algorithmic brain power (intelligence) from rationality, the reflective tendency to override plausible-but-wrong intuitions. High IQ does not make people immune to bias: bat-and-ball-type questions predict susceptibility to cognitive errors somewhat better than IQ tests do, and low scorers on the Cognitive Reflection Test are impulsive, impatient, and keen on immediate gratification."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Detecting a Lazy System 2 (Failure to Verify)","visual_frame_description":"The player is an internal auditor in a gray interview room with a two-way mirror. Across the table sits Marcus, a data analyst in shirtsleeves, and the screen overlays his answers in real time. On the desk are his report, a pen, and a notepad he never touches.","player_mission":"Audit whether Marcus actually verified the report numbers he claims to have double-checked, or whether he let a quick, appealing answer pass without checking. Weigh his baseline, his timed behavior, and the interrogation transcript, then file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["The bat-and-ball puzzle evokes an answer that is intuitive, appealing, and wrong: 10 cents.","People who answer 10 cents did not actively check the answer; their System 2 endorsed an intuition it could have rejected with a small investment of effort.","A failure to check is remarkable because the cost of checking is so low.","People who say 10 cents appear to be ardent followers of the law of least effort.","Failing these minitests is at least partly a matter of insufficient motivation, not trying hard enough.","Those who avoid the sin of intellectual sloth could be called 'engaged.'","The ease with which students are satisfied enough to stop thinking is rather troubling.","If System 1 is involved, the conclusion comes first and the arguments follow."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the lazy System 2: a person can accept a compelling, intuitive, wrong answer because the cost of checking is low but the motivation to check is missing. The book frames failing the bat-and-ball puzzle as insufficient motivation and weak self-monitoring, not a lack of intelligence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Did Marcus actually verify the report numbers he claims to have double-checked, or did he let an easy, appealing answer slip through unchecked?","subject_baseline":"Marcus, 31, a data analyst at a regional logistics firm, is usually diligent: he is the one who finds rounding errors in teammates' spreadsheets. He describes himself as a careful checker. Today is heavy, with three back-to-back review meetings and a skipped lunch.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:12 - Given the bat-and-ball-style check question ('Two numbers together cost $1.10; one is a dollar more; what is the smaller?'), Marcus answers '10 cents' within two seconds, before the interviewer finishes speaking.","09:13 - Asked to show his work, he shrugs, reaches for his coffee, and does not pick up the pen lying in front of him.","09:15 - He claims he 'verified' the freight report, but he cannot recall the exact total or the formula he says he used.","09:18 - When the correct answer (5 cents) is revealed, he frowns, leans back, and says he 'must have skimmed it.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Quick one, no calculators: a bat and a ball together cost $1.10. The bat costs a dollar more than the ball. What does the ball cost?'","Marcus: 'Ten cents. That's easy.'","Interviewer: 'And in the freight report, you wrote that the carrier's total came to $14,200. What line items make up that number?'","Marcus: 'Uh... I'd have to reopen the file. I trust the template did the math.'","Interviewer: 'You told Sofia you double-checked every cell.'","Marcus: 'I skimmed the columns. Close enough, right?'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Marcus is not deliberately fabricating a story, but he has not actually verified the report: his System 2 was lazy. He endorsed the intuitive '10 cents' answer without checking, exactly the failure the bat-and-ball puzzle is designed to expose.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His instant '10 cents' answer is the signature intuitive response the book documents: over 50% of students at Harvard, MIT, and Princeton give it, and more than 80% at less selective universities fail to check. His inability to reconstruct the report's total, combined with the admission of skimming, shows the same refusal to invest the small effort needed to verify. People who say 10 cents have a System 2 that endorsed an answer it could have rejected with a few seconds of work, so his pattern is lazy self-monitoring driven by motivation, not by ability.","distractors":["Marcus is deliberately fabricating the report to get Sofia in trouble.","Marcus is cognitively overloaded right now because of his three meetings.","Marcus has poor short-term memory and could not check even if he tried."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Decision Fatigue and the Default of Denial","visual_frame_description":"The player is an efficiency auditor watching a calendar view of a grants office. Elena, a grants director, reviews applications in six-minute blocks with timestamps. The screen plots her approval rate across the day, spiking after breaks and collapsing before the afternoon meal break.","player_mission":"Determine whether Elena's behavior matches her claim of giving every proposal a full, fair, case-by-case review, or whether she is silently defaulting to denial once depleted. Inspect the timing of her approvals and the transcript, then file the diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Israeli parole judges spend an average of 6 minutes per case; the default decision is denial of parole, with only 35% of requests approved.","Approval rates spike after each meal, to about 65%, and drop steadily toward zero before the next break.","Tired and hungry judges tend to fall back on the easier default position of denying requests for parole.","The exertion of self-control is depleting and unpleasant.","Ego-depleted people are less willing or less able to exert self-control when the next challenge comes around.","Signs of depletion include performing poorly in cognitive tasks and logical decision making.","The evidence supports the claim that self-control requires attention and effort.","Frequent switching of tasks and speeded-up mental work are not intrinsically pleasurable, and people avoid them when possible."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests ego depletion and decision fatigue in judgment: mental effort and self-control draw on a shared, depleting budget, so after hours of decisions people default to the easier option. The chapter cites the Israeli parole judges whose approval rate dropped toward zero as time since their last food break increased.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Elena claims she gives every funding proposal her full, fair review. Does her behavior across the day match that claim, or is she defaulting when depleted?","subject_baseline":"Elena, 44, a grants director at a state agency, approves funding requests after reviewing applications at an average of six minutes each. She prides herself on 'giving each proposal a fresh look' and insists decisions are made case by case, not by default.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:05, right after the morning break - Elena approves three of four proposals in a row, leaning forward and writing notes.","12:50, minutes after lunch - she approves two applications quickly, humming.","15:40, about two hours after lunch - she denies six consecutive requests, each review taking less than two minutes, with a blank stare and a hurried signature.","17:20 - she denies the final application without opening the executive summary, checking her watch."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'This afternoon you turned down six proposals in a row. Tell me what you saw in them.'","Elena: 'They were... they all had the same weaknesses. I'm consistent.'","Auditor: 'Three of them had identical risk scores to ones you approved this morning.'","Elena: 'Look, by late afternoon my head is fried. I just go with what's in front of me.'","Auditor: 'Go with what's in front of you - meaning the default?'","Elena: '...Meaning the default, yes.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Elena is experiencing decision fatigue: her approval behavior collapses after the last food break, and she falls back on the easier default of denial. Her claim of case-by-case fairness is contradicted by the timing of her denials.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The pattern mirrors the Israeli parole study: approval rates spike right after meals (about 65%) and slide toward zero as time since the last food break grows, because tired, hungry judges default to the easier decision of denial. Elena's denials cluster at the end of the day, her reviews shrink to two minutes, and she stops opening documents, the tell-tale signs of a depleted System 2 reaching for the path of least effort.","distractors":["Elena has a personal grudge against this morning's applicants.","The afternoon proposals were genuinely weaker by design.","Elena is deliberately trying to cut the agency's budget."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Bat-and-Ball Puzzle: Resisting a Tempting Wrong Answer","visual_frame_description":"A university lecture hall fills with students as Professor Ravi projects the bat-and-ball puzzle on a screen and gives them four seconds. Over half the room shouts '10 cents.' One student, Amina, hesitates, scribbles two equations, and quietly says '5 cents.' The frame freezes on her worksheet.","player_mission":"Defeat the lazy System 2 boss by explaining why the room was fooled and by arming Amina with a strategy that blocks the next tempting wrong answer. Answer the stage-one diagnosis, then choose the stage-two intervention that best builds her defense.","key_concepts":["The bat-and-ball puzzle is the first encounter with a recurrent theme: many people are overconfident and place too much faith in their intuitions.","The distinctive mark of the puzzle is that it evokes an answer that is intuitive, appealing, and wrong.","Those who end up with the correct answer somehow managed to resist the intuition.","A failure to check is remarkable because the cost of checking is so low.","People who avoid the intuitive answer appear to have more active minds.","Anyone admitted to a good university is able to reason through the puzzle when not tempted to accept a superficially plausible answer.","The extent of deliberate checking and search is a characteristic of System 2 that varies among individuals.","The 'lazy' judgment about the self-monitoring of students who fail these minitests does not seem to be unfair."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle trains the player to recognize the signature of the lazy System 2: endorsing an intuitive, appealing, wrong answer because checking is skipped. The chapter shows this failure is common, with the majority of students at elite universities giving the 10-cent answer, and that resisting it requires a small but genuine effort of engagement.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle is against your own lazy System 2: the supervisory function that is supposed to check System 1's suggestions but often fails to bother. The bat-and-ball puzzle is the boss's opening attack.","stage_1_observation":"A lecture hall fills with students. Professor Ravi projects the bat-and-ball puzzle and gives them four seconds. Hands go up; over half the room shouts '10 cents.' One student, Amina, hesitates, scribbles, and quietly says '5 cents.' The screen freezes on her worksheet showing the two equations.","stage_1_question":"Why did so many students answer 10 cents, and what did Amina do differently?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The 10-cent answer is the intuitive, appealing, wrong answer that System 1 offers; more than 50% at top universities and over 80% at less selective ones fail to check it. Amina resisted the intuition with a few seconds of System 2 work and got the correct 5 cents.","stage_2_intervention":"You are Amina's study partner preparing for an exam. She wants to know how to avoid being fooled by the next tempting wrong answer that appears in her head. Choose the intervention that best builds her defense against a lazy System 2.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Train her to treat every instant, plausible answer as a suspect: deliberately slow down, do the math or the logic, and ask what obvious cue would explain why this puzzle was included before she commits to an answer."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Ego Depletion and the Collapsing Approval Rate","visual_frame_description":"It is 4:15 pm on a Thursday in a district intake office. Judge Okafor has heard forty cases since the 1:00 lunch break. A clerk slides case number 41 across the desk: a parole request structurally identical to one granted at 1:05. The judge signs the denial form without reading page three, while the wall clock shows three hours since the last meal.","player_mission":"Diagnose the mechanism driving the judge's denial, then design an operating schedule for a call center that prevents the same depletion-default pattern. Beat the boss by applying the chapter's evidence on ego depletion to a real workplace.","key_concepts":["Ego depletion: an effort of will is tiring, so after forcing yourself to do something, you are less willing to exert self-control at the next challenge.","Parole approval rates spike after each meal and slide steadily toward zero before the next break.","Both fatigue and hunger probably play a role in the decline of approvals.","Depleted people default to the easier option, which for judges is denial.","People were able to resist the effects of ego depletion when given a strong incentive to do so.","Increasing effort is not an option when you must keep digits in short-term memory while performing a task.","The nervous system consumes more glucose than most other parts of the body, and effortful mental activity is especially expensive in glucose.","Baumeister and his colleagues confirmed that ingesting glucose can undo the effects of ego depletion."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests ego depletion as it appears in real judgment: exerting self-control drains a shared pool of mental energy, and depleted people default to the easier option. The chapter grounds this in Baumeister's depletion experiments and the Israeli parole judges whose approval rate fell to near zero before meals.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is a full workday that drains the shared budget of self-control. The battle is to recognize depletion in yourself and others and to intervene before a lazy default decides for you.","stage_1_observation":"It is 4:15 pm on a Thursday in the district court's intake office. Judge Okafor has heard forty cases since the 1:00 lunch break. His clerk hands him case number 41, a parole request identical in structure to one he granted at 1:05. He signs the denial form without reading page three. The clock shows the last meal was three hours ago.","stage_1_question":"What is the most likely mechanism behind Judge Okafor's denial, given the case timing?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Ego depletion and decision fatigue: the parole data show approval rates spiking after meals and sliding toward zero before the next break, because tired, hungry decision makers fall back on the easier default of denial.","stage_2_intervention":"You run a call center that must make fair approval decisions on customer refunds all day, and your team's denial rate creeps up every afternoon. Choose the intervention that best counteracts the depletion-default pattern shown in the research.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Schedule high-stakes discretionary approvals right after breaks, add a real food break before the late-afternoon slump, and require reviewers to state a written reason before selecting the default denial option."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Flow versus Willpower: Attention Control as the Shield","visual_frame_description":"A preschool waiting room appears on screen. Four-year-olds sit alone at a desk facing a single cookie and a bell. Half ring the bell within minutes; others last the full fifteen minutes. The camera lingers on Mira, who lasts by turning her chair away from the cookie and humming to herself while watched through a one-way mirror.","player_mission":"Explain why Mira outlasted the bell ringers, then redesign a draining weekly meeting so your team can work like her, preserving attention and willpower. Defeat the boss by using the chapter's evidence on flow and attention control.","key_concepts":["In Mischel's cookie experiment, about half of the four-year-olds waited the full 15 minutes, mainly by keeping their attention away from the tempting reward.","The resisters later had higher measures of executive control, especially the ability to reallocate attention effectively.","As young adults, the resisters were less likely to take drugs.","The children who showed more self-control as four-year-olds had substantially higher scores on tests of intelligence.","Training attention with computer games improved executive control and nonverbal intelligence scores, and the improvement lasted for several months.","Flow is 'a state of effortless concentration so deep that they lose their sense of time, of themselves, of their problems.'","In flow, maintaining focused attention requires no exertion of self-control, freeing resources for the task at hand.","Self-control and cognitive effort are both forms of mental work."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle contrasts fragile willpower with flow: self-control is depleting and must be budgeted, while flow provides deep, effortless attention that frees the limited budget for the task itself. The chapter connects Mischel's cookie-waiting children and Csikszentmihalyi's flow research to the idea that attention control is trainable and central to self-control.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle is between two ways of deploying System 2: grinding willpower, which depletes, and flow, which frees attention from self-control. The arena is a preschool waiting room.","stage_1_observation":"Four-year-olds sit alone at a desk facing a single cookie and a bell that calls the experimenter. The room has no toys or pictures. Half the children ring the bell within minutes; the others last the full fifteen minutes. The camera shows one girl, Mira, who lasts by turning her chair away from the cookie and humming to herself.","stage_1_question":"Why did Mira succeed where the other children rang the bell?","stage_1_correct_answer":"She kept her attention away from the tempting reward rather than staring it down. About half of the children managed the fifteen-minute wait mainly by reallocating attention, and those resisters later showed better executive control, less drug use, and higher intelligence-test scores.","stage_2_intervention":"You manage a research team whose members say the weekly metrics meeting drains them for the rest of the day. You want them to work like Mira, resilient under pressure, without burning willpower. Choose the strategy that fits the chapter's evidence about attention and flow.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Remove the distracting temptations from the room, such as phones and a snack table within reach, give each person a single absorbing task that structures attention, and let people enter a state of flow where concentration requires no self-control."}]},{"id":"ch09","title":"4. The Associative Machine","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Hume's Three Principles of Association","visual_frame_description":"A Victorian-style diagram shows the words BANANAS and VOMIT linked by branching threads that light up one by one: one thread to 'sick,' another to 'yellow,' another to 'fruit.' At the bottom of the board sit three labeled bins: Resemblance, Contiguity in Time and Place, and Causality.","player_mission":"Sort each association into the principle it best illustrates, using David Hume's three rules of association. Classify all six items correctly to show you understand how the associative machine links ideas.","key_concepts":["Associative activation means ideas that have been evoked trigger many other ideas in a spreading cascade of activity in the brain.","The word 'vomit' triggered disgust responses, pushing the book away, raising heart rate, and activating sweat glands, all automatically and beyond control.","Your mind automatically assumed a temporal sequence and a causal connection between the words 'bananas' and 'vomit.'","David Hume reduced the principles of association to three: resemblance, contiguity in time and place, and causality.","Ideas are nodes in a vast network called associative memory, each linked to many others.","Causes are linked to their effects (virus to cold), things to their properties (lime to green), and things to their categories (banana to fruit).","Only a few of the activated ideas register in consciousness; most of the work of associative thinking is silent.","Cognition is embodied: you think with your body, not only with your brain.","You know far less about yourself than you feel you do."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the associative machine's rules: ideas trigger other ideas along links of resemblance, contiguity, and causality, as David Hume first catalogued. The book shows System 1 builds an entire causal scenario from a mere juxtaposition of words, automatically and without will.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"You see the words 'bananas' and 'vomit' side by side, and your mind instantly builds a story in which the bananas made you sick. Which of Hume's principles is doing the heaviest lifting?","column_a_label":"Resemblance","column_b_label":"Contiguity in time and place","column_c_label":"Causality","rows":[{"label":"Reading 'lime' makes you think of the color green.","col":"a"},{"label":"The word 'virus' brings 'cold' to mind.","col":"c"},{"label":"The juxtaposition of 'bananas' and 'vomit' is read as a cause-and-effect sequence.","col":"c"},{"label":"The word 'banana' brings 'fruit' to mind.","col":"a"},{"label":"Sitting at a wobbly restaurant table primes the word 'wobbly' later.","col":"b"},{"label":"Passing the bakery you walk by every morning makes you think of its smell.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Association happens one idea at a time, like beads on a string.","The banana-vomit link is a conscious, deliberate inference.","Only causality matters; resemblance and contiguity are obsolete."],"explanation":"Hume reduced association to three principles: resemblance (lime to green), contiguity in time and place (the wobbly table and the morning bakery), and causality (virus to cold). The banana-vomit effect is a causal story: System 1 imposed a temporal sequence and a causal link onto two unrelated words. The modern view keeps Hume's start but notes that much association happens simultaneously and silently, outside awareness."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Word Priming, Behavioral Priming, and Embodied Priming","visual_frame_description":"Three laboratory clips play side by side: a screen flashing EAT followed by the fragment SO_P; a hallway where students who unscrambled elderly-themed sentences walk more slowly; and a student holding a pencil in her mouth while rating cartoons. Three columns labeled Word, Behavior, and Embodied wait below.","player_mission":"Sort each priming demonstration into the right column: conceptual word priming, behavioral (ideomotor) priming, or embodied and reciprocal priming. Classify all six demonstrations correctly.","key_concepts":["Exposure to the word EAT makes you more likely to complete SO_P as SOUP; WASH primes SOAP instead.","Priming is not restricted to concepts: your actions and emotions can be primed by events of which you are not even aware.","In Bargh's Florida effect, elderly-themed words made young students walk down a corridor significantly more slowly.","The Florida effect is two-stage: words prime thoughts of old age, and these thoughts prime the behavior of walking slowly.","None of the students noticed the common theme, and all insisted nothing they did afterward could have been influenced by the words.","The influencing of an action by an idea is known as the ideomotor effect.","Walking slowly for five minutes made participants quicker to recognize old-age words such as 'forgetful,' a reciprocal priming effect.","Students holding a pencil so their face formed a smile found cartoons funnier than those forced into a frown.","People told to nod while listening to editorials tended to accept them; those told to shake their heads tended to reject them."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix distinguishes the layers of priming in the associative machine: exposure to a word changes the ease of recognizing related words, primes behaviors through the ideomotor effect, and works reciprocally through the body. The chapter's demonstrations, Bargh's Florida effect, the pencil-in-mouth study, and the nod-versus-shake headphone study, all occur without the participant's awareness.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A job candidate has just unscrambled sentences full of 'Florida,' 'bald,' and 'wrinkle.' She walks to the next interview room noticeably more slowly than when she arrived. Which column explains her pace?","column_a_label":"Conceptual word priming","column_b_label":"Behavioral (ideomotor) priming","column_c_label":"Embodied / reciprocal priming","rows":[{"label":"Seeing EAT makes you complete SO_P as SOUP.","col":"a"},{"label":"Elderly-themed words make students walk more slowly down a corridor.","col":"b"},{"label":"Holding a pencil so your face forms a smile makes cartoons seem funnier.","col":"c"},{"label":"Walking at one-third your normal pace makes you quicker to recognize 'forgetful' and 'old.'","col":"c"},{"label":"Seeing WASH makes you complete SO_P as SOAP.","col":"a"},{"label":"Nodding while listening to an editorial makes you accept the message; shaking makes you reject it.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Walking slowly after elderly words is an act of conscious role-playing.","Priming only affects the recognition of words, never actions or emotions.","The smile effect works only when you know you are smiling."],"explanation":"Word fragments show conceptual priming (EAT primes SOUP). The Florida effect is ideomotor, or behavioral, priming: elderly words prime the idea of old age, which in turn primes the behavior of walking slowly, with no awareness. The pencil, slow-walk, and nod studies are embodied and reciprocal: a posture or gesture changes the emotion or attitude, and the state feeds back on itself."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Money, Mortality, and Moral-Cleansing Primes","visual_frame_description":"Three experimental rooms appear on screen. In one, a screen saver of dollar bills floats on a monitor while a participant drops a pencil; in another, a subject reads reminders of mortality before answering a questionnaire; in a third, a person who just lied in an email stands in a shop aisle holding soap. Three columns wait beneath.","player_mission":"Sort each finding into the prime family it demonstrates: money priming (individualism), mortality priming (authoritarian appeal), or moral-cleansing priming (the Lady Macbeth effect). Classify all seven findings correctly.","key_concepts":["Money-primed people persevered almost twice as long on a difficult problem before asking for help.","Money-primed participants picked up fewer pencils when an experimenter dropped a bunch on the floor.","Money-primed participants set chairs 118 centimeters apart for a chat, far from the 80 centimeters of nonprimed peers.","The general theme is that money primes individualism: a reluctance to be involved with, depend on, or accept demands from others.","Thinking of an action you are ashamed of makes you more likely to complete W_ _ H as WASH and S_ _ P as SOAP.","Thinking about stabbing a coworker in the back leaves people more inclined to buy soap, disinfectant, or detergent.","People who lied on the phone preferred mouthwash over soap; those who lied in email preferred soap over mouthwash.","Reminding people of their mortality increases the appeal of authoritarian ideas.","A culture saturated with reminders of money may shape behavior and attitudes in ways we do not know about and may not be proud of."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the reach of subtle primes into attitudes and purchases: money reminders shift people toward individualism, reminders of mortality toward authoritarian appeal, and moral stains toward physical cleansing (the Lady Macbeth effect). All operate silently in System 1, which is why the chapter says you know far less about yourself than you feel you do.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In a get-acquainted setup, an experimenter asks a participant to set up two chairs for a conversation with a stranger, and the participant places them 118 centimeters apart. What primed that choice?","column_a_label":"Money priming (individualism)","column_b_label":"Mortality priming (authoritarian appeal)","column_c_label":"Moral-cleansing priming (Lady Macbeth effect)","rows":[{"label":"Picking up fewer pencils for a stranger who drops them.","col":"a"},{"label":"After lying in an email, preferring soap over mouthwash.","col":"c"},{"label":"Thinking of an action you are ashamed of makes you complete W_ _ H as WASH.","col":"c"},{"label":"Reminders of death increasing the appeal of authoritarian ideas.","col":"b"},{"label":"Persevering almost twice as long on a hard problem before asking for help.","col":"a"},{"label":"After 'stabbing a coworker in the back' in a thought exercise, buying more detergent than juice.","col":"c"},{"label":"Choosing to sit much farther apart (118 versus 80 centimeters) before a conversation.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Money primes generosity and helpfulness toward strangers.","The 118-centimeter distance is a rational preference for personal space.","Buying soap after a lie is a hygienic habit unrelated to the lie."],"explanation":"Money cues prime individualism: independence, selfishness, and distance, seen in fewer pencils picked up, farther chairs, and longer self-reliance. Moral-cleansing studies show that a stained conscience triggers a desire to clean the relevant body part, such as washing after email lies and mouthwash after phone lies. The chapter also notes that mortality reminders increase the appeal of authoritarian ideas, as with the Dear Leader portraits that reduce spontaneous independent thought."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Honesty Box: Unaware Influence of Watching Eyes","visual_frame_description":"The player is an office auditor viewing a break-room camera feed above a tea price list. A poster changes each week from wide-open eyes to flowers and back. A contributor, Priya, drops coins into the honesty box, and the screen tallies her payments in pence against the poster type.","player_mission":"Audit whether Priya's paying behavior is under her conscious control. Compare her weekly payments with the posters, read the transcript, and decide whether her insistence that posters have no effect is credible.","key_concepts":["In a British university kitchen, an 'honesty box' collected payment for tea and coffee for many years.","When posters of watching eyes were displayed, contributions were almost three times higher than in flower weeks.","No one commented on the new decorations, yet the amounts paid changed significantly.","A purely symbolic reminder of being watched prodded people into improved behavior.","The effect occurs without any awareness.","Priming phenomena arise in System 1, and you have no conscious access to them.","Your subjective experience consists largely of the story that your System 2 tells itself about what is going on.","Among a hundred voters, only a few with uncertain preferences will vote differently because of a prime, but a few percent can tip an election."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the reach of primes without awareness: a purely symbolic reminder of being watched improves honest behavior, and people deny any influence while the effect is measurable. The chapter concludes that priming arises in System 1, to which you have no conscious access, so your sense of being in charge is partly a story System 2 tells.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya insists that posters above the office tea fund could never influence her, and that she always pays her fair share. Is her behavior actually under her conscious control, or is she being silently steered by the environment?","subject_baseline":"Priya, 29, a product manager, is widely seen as scrupulous: she pays for her tea, returns borrowed money on time, and lectures teammates about the 'honesty box.' She has never been caught cheating.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Week 1 - a poster of two wide-open eyes hangs above the price list. Priya puts 140 pence into the box for her week of tea and milk.","Week 2 - the poster changes to flowers. Priya drops in 30 pence, then refills her mug and leaves without looking at the price list.","Week 4 - flowers again. She adds 20 pence for two days of coffee.","Week 5 - the eyes return. Priya hesitates at the box, glances up at the poster, and adds 110 pence."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Did you notice the posters above the price list?'","Priya: 'Honestly? No. I never look up.'","Auditor: 'In flower weeks you paid about a fifth of what you paid in eye weeks.'","Priya: 'That's impossible. I pay what I owe, every time. The posters have nothing to do with me.'","Auditor: 'So the eye weeks and flower weeks differ by chance?'","Priya: 'They must. I'm not the kind of person who'd be swayed by a picture.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Priya is not deliberately cheating, but her paying is not under her conscious control: a symbolic image of watching eyes raises her contributions to roughly three times the flower-week level, and she is completely unaware of the influence.","diagnostic_breakdown":"In the British university study, contributions averaged almost three times more in 'eye weeks' than in 'flower weeks,' with nobody commenting on the posters or noticing the manipulation. Priya shows the same signature: she pays dramatically more when watched-eyes imagery is present, denies noticing the posters, and insists her behavior is unaffected. Her confident 'I'm not the kind of person' claim is exactly the System 2 story that priming research shows is unreliable.","distractors":["Priya is consciously cheating and the posters are irrelevant.","Priya's payments vary because she uses the kitchen more in some weeks.","Priya noticed the posters and is consciously imitating the study."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Lady Macbeth Effect: Moral Stains and Cleaning","visual_frame_description":"The player is an auditor reviewing a single day in the life of a sales manager through a timeline of stills. Omar sends an email, then appears in a shop aisle holding soap, washes his hands at a sink, and later asks where the mouthwash is kept. Each still is timestamped.","player_mission":"Audit whether Omar's behavior after sending a dishonest email reveals a hidden moral discomfort. Compare his baseline habits with the timed observations and the transcript, then file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Feeling that one's soul is stained appears to trigger a desire to cleanse one's body, an impulse dubbed the 'Lady Macbeth effect.'","People asked to think of an action they are ashamed of are more likely to complete W_ _ H as WASH.","Merely thinking about stabbing a coworker in the back leaves people more inclined to buy soap, disinfectant, or detergent than batteries, juice, or candy bars.","The cleansing is highly specific to the body parts involved in a sin.","People who lied on the phone preferred mouthwash over soap; people who lied in email preferred soap over mouthwash.","Priming effects threaten our self-image as conscious, autonomous authors of our judgments and choices.","The associations connecting guilt to cleansing are unconscious and automatic.","Associative activation produces a self-reinforcing pattern of cognitive, emotional, and physical responses."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the Lady Macbeth effect: moral contamination primes physical cleansing, specific to the body part used in the transgression. The chapter reports that merely thinking about a betrayal leaves people more inclined to buy soap and disinfectant, demonstrating how unconscious associations connect abstract guilt to concrete behavior.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Omar lied to a client in an email and now claims he 'feels fine about it.' Is his behavior consistent with feeling untroubled, or does his conduct betray a hidden moral stain?","subject_baseline":"Omar, 36, a sales account manager, is normally unfussy: he drinks black coffee, buys batteries and snacks, and never mentions hygiene products. He is proud of 'moving on quickly' after difficult calls.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02 - Omar sends the exaggerated email inflating the delivery timeline. He clears his throat twice and runs a hand over his face.","14:30 - In the company shop, he lingers in the soap and disinfectant aisle for over a minute, then buys a bottle of antibacterial hand wash.","15:10 - He washes his hands at the sink even though he touched nothing but his keyboard.","16:00 - Offered a free snack, he declines and instead asks where the mouthwash is kept."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'How do you feel about that email you sent the client?'","Omar: 'Totally fine. It's business. I don't carry that stuff around.'","Auditor: 'You bought hand wash this afternoon. Do you usually?'","Omar: 'I've been... thinking about hygiene, I guess. No reason.'","Auditor: 'And you asked for mouthwash?'","Omar: 'Just checking stock. I'm not stressed about anything, if that's what you're asking.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Omar is not consciously washing away guilt, but his behavior reveals an unconscious moral-cleansing drive: committing a deceitful act in email primed him to seek physical cleansing, especially for the body part, the hands, involved in the sin.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The Lady Macbeth studies show that a stained conscience triggers a desire to cleanse: people who lied in an email preferred soap to mouthwash, while those who lied on the phone preferred mouthwash to soap, and thinking about stabbing a coworker in the back increased purchases of soap and disinfectant. Omar's email lie, his hand-washing, his soap purchase, and his mouthwash question all cluster around the hands and mouth of the 'sin,' and he offers no conscious explanation, the tell-tale pattern of a priming effect operating below awareness.","distractors":["Omar is deliberately trying to conceal the lie by buying cleaning products.","Omar is naturally germ-averse and shops this way every week.","Omar is worried the client will sue him."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Nod-and-Shake Effect: Gestures Steering Judgment","visual_frame_description":"The player is an auditor reviewing a procurement evaluation session. Diego wears large headphones, and the screen shows a side view of him shaking his head side to side every few seconds while a recorded supplier pitch plays. He writes 'REJECT' on a score sheet.","player_mission":"Audit whether Diego's rejection of the supplier's proposal is a purely rational judgment of the content, or whether his repeated head-shaking gesture steered his verdict. Weigh the observation log and transcript, then file the diagnosis.","key_concepts":["People asked to listen to messages through new headphones were told to move their heads repeatedly to check for distortions of sound.","Those who were told to nod their head up and down tended to accept the radio editorials they heard.","Those who were told to shake their heads tended to reject the message.","There was no awareness, just a habitual connection between an attitude of rejection or acceptance and its common physical expression.","Reciprocal links in the associative network mean behavior feeds back into feeling.","Being amused tends to make you smile, and smiling tends to make you feel amused.","The admonition to 'act calm and kind regardless of how you feel' is good advice because you are likely to be rewarded by actually feeling calm and kind.","Simple, common gestures can unconsciously influence our thoughts and feelings."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests embodied priming of attitudes: a repeated yes or no gesture can push judgment toward acceptance or rejection without awareness. The chapter's nod-and-shake study shows the habitual link between a gesture and its meaning, and supports the advice to act calm and kind regardless of how you feel because behavior feeds back into feeling.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diego insists his rejection of the supplier's proposal is a purely rational judgment of the content. Was his verdict actually steered by something irrelevant?","subject_baseline":"Diego, 41, a procurement officer, was asked to 'test new headphones' by moving his head repeatedly to check for audio distortion while listening to a recorded supplier pitch. He is confident and says he does not do gut reactions.","observed_nonverbal_log":["12:01 - Diego is told to shake his head side to side every few seconds during the playback, allegedly to test the headphones.","12:02 - He shakes his head as instructed, even as the pitch's strongest argument plays.","12:05 - He removes the headphones, writes 'REJECT - weak overall' in two minutes, and pushes the score sheet away.","12:08 - Asked to recall one strong point from the pitch, he cannot name any."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'You rejected the proposal in under three minutes. Why?'","Diego: 'It was weak. I could just tell.'","Auditor: 'You were shaking your head the whole time you listened. Did that feel relevant?'","Diego: 'I was testing the audio. It has zero to do with my judgment.'","Auditor: 'But you rated it weak overall - the same verdict your body was rehearsing.'","Diego: 'Coincidence. I'd have said the same thing with headphones off.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Diego's rejection was steered by an embodied prime: the habitual no-gesture of shaking transferred its meaning onto the message he was evaluating, and he had no awareness of the influence.","diagnostic_breakdown":"In the headphone study, participants told to nod while listening to radio editorials tended to accept the message, while those told to shake their heads tended to reject it, with no awareness of the manipulation. Diego was assigned the shake condition, and his swift blanket rejection, combined with his inability to recall a single strong point, is exactly the pattern: a habitual connection between a rejection gesture and an attitude of rejection, operating entirely outside his System 2.","distractors":["Diego actually disliked the headphones and vented on the pitch.","Diego is lying and had already decided to reject the supplier before listening.","Diego's verdict is accurate because he is an experienced procurement officer."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Honesty Box: Using a Being-Watched Prime Responsibly","visual_frame_description":"The break-room wall of a London marketing agency. Above the price list for tea and milk hangs a poster of two wide-open eyes. For ten weeks the image alternates: eyes, flowers, eyes, flowers. The staff never comment on it, but the honesty box fills when the eyes are up and empties when flowers appear.","player_mission":"Explain why contributions jump sharply in eye weeks despite nobody mentioning the posters, then choose a low-cost policy for a shared snack pantry that applies the priming evidence responsibly. Defeat the boss by making the environment work without coercion.","key_concepts":["The honesty-box study is presented as a perfect demonstration of a priming effect.","A banner poster displayed above the price list was changed weekly for ten weeks, alternating flowers and watching eyes.","Contributions averaged almost three times more in eye weeks than in flower weeks.","The effect happens without any awareness and with nobody commenting on the decorations.","A purely symbolic reminder of being watched prodded people into improved behavior.","Priming phenomena arise in System 1, to which you have no conscious access.","The effects of the primes are robust but not necessarily large, yet a few percent can tip an election or a budget.","System 2 believes that it is in charge and that it knows the reasons for its choices."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the power of an ambient prime to change behavior silently: an image of eyes improves honesty-box payments almost threefold even though no one notices it. The chapter presents this as a perfect demonstration that System 1 is influenced by primes to which System 2 has no access.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the environment itself: an office where a symbolic image of watching eyes reliably lifts honesty-box contributions to roughly three times the level of flower weeks. The battle is to understand that prime and to use it responsibly.","stage_1_observation":"The break-room wall of a London marketing agency. Above the price list for tea and milk hangs a poster of two wide-open eyes. For ten weeks the image alternates: eyes, flowers, eyes, flowers. The staff never comment on it, but the honesty box fills when the eyes are up and empties when flowers appear.","stage_1_question":"Why do contributions jump so sharply in eye weeks despite nobody mentioning the posters?","stage_1_correct_answer":"A purely symbolic reminder of being watched primes prosocial behavior without awareness: in the British kitchen study, contributions averaged almost three times more in eye weeks than in flower weeks, with no one noticing the change.","stage_2_intervention":"You manage a shared snack pantry where losses run high, and you must choose a low-cost policy. Which approach best reflects what the priming evidence supports?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Add a discreet image of watching eyes above the price list rather than installing cameras, signs, or fines: the symbolic prime raises compliance without creating distrust or surveillance."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Money Screen Saver: Individualism by Ambience","visual_frame_description":"The sales floor of a software company. Two labs are set up identically except that one team's breakroom computer runs a screen saver of dollar bills floating in water. In a collaboration drill, the money-primed team persists twice as long before asking for help, picks up fewer pencils dropped by a clumsy colleague, and sets chairs far apart for a chat.","player_mission":"Identify the single theme that explains the whole cluster of money-primed behaviors, then advise the CEO on how to foster collaboration given that money reminders quietly shape behavior. Defeat the boss by acting on the research rather than on intuition.","key_concepts":["Money-primed people become more independent than they would be without the associative trigger.","They persevered almost twice as long on a very difficult problem before asking the experimenter for help.","Money-primed people are more selfish: much less willing to spend time helping a student who pretended to be confused.","When an experimenter dropped a bunch of pencils, the participants with money on their mind picked up fewer pencils.","Money-primed participants placed chairs 118 centimeters apart for a get-acquainted chat, versus 80 centimeters for nonprimed peers.","Money-primed undergraduates showed a greater preference for being alone.","The idea of money primes individualism: a reluctance to be involved with, depend on, or accept demands from others.","The psychologist Kathleen Vohs conducted this research and was laudably restrained in discussing its implications.","A culture that surrounds us with reminders of money may shape our behavior and attitudes in ways that we do not know about."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the money-priming effect: reminders of money push people toward individualism, greater self-reliance, less helpfulness, and more distance from others, all without awareness. The chapter, citing Kathleen Vohs's experiments, warns that a culture saturated with money reminders may quietly shape attitudes in ways people do not recognize.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is a screen saver of dollar bills: a workplace ambient prime that quietly shifts everyone toward individualism. The battle is to recognize how money reminders change behavior and decide what to do about them.","stage_1_observation":"The sales floor of a software company. Two labs are set up identically except that one team's breakroom computer runs a screen saver of dollar bills floating in water. In a collaboration drill, the money-primed team persists twice as long before asking for help, picks up fewer pencils dropped by a clumsy colleague, sets chairs 118 centimeters apart for a chat, and says it prefers working alone.","stage_1_question":"What single theme explains the whole cluster of money-primed behaviors?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Money primes individualism: a reluctance to depend on others, help others, or accept demands from others, shown in longer self-reliance, fewer pencils picked up, farther chair placement, and a preference for solitude.","stage_2_intervention":"Your CEO wants next quarter to be the most collaborative quarter ever, but the lobby TV loops stock tickers and the breakroom shows cash-counting footage. Choose the move that best applies the research.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Remove the money reminders from shared spaces and replace them with neutral or warm imagery, because the research shows money cues shift behavior toward independence and selfishness even when people deny any effect."}]},{"id":"ch10","title":"5. Cognitive Ease","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Sources of Cognitive Ease and Cognitive Strain","visual_frame_description":"A cockpit-style control panel fills the screen, dominated by a single large dial labeled Cognitive Ease that swings between Easy and Strained. Below the dial, a conveyor belt feeds in seven everyday stimuli, each represented by a small icon card: a crisp printed sentence, a faded gray poster, a cheerful radio voice, a repeated slogan, and an effortful worksheet. The player drags each card into one of three labeled bins beneath the dial.","player_mission":"Sort each incoming stimulus card into the bin it registers on the cognitive ease dial. Read each cue carefully and recall what the chapter says about which manipulations make associative processing flow smoothly and which engage effortful System 2.","key_concepts":["Cognitive ease is a single dial in System 1 whose range runs from Easy to Strained, and its setting tells System 1 whether extra System 2 effort is required.","Easy is a sign that things are going well, with no threats, no major news, and no need to redirect attention or mobilize effort.","Cognitive strain indicates that a problem exists and will require increased mobilization of System 2.","A sentence printed in a clear font is fluently processed with cognitive ease.","A word that has been repeated, or has been primed, will be processed with cognitive ease on later exposure.","Hearing a speaker while you are in a good mood, or even holding a pencil crosswise in your mouth to force a smile, induces cognitive ease.","The various causes of ease and strain have interchangeable effects on the same single dial.","When you feel strained you are more likely to be vigilant and suspicious, invest more effort, and make fewer errors.","When you are in a state of cognitive ease you are likely to like what you see, believe what you hear, trust your intuitions, and feel that the situation is comfortably familiar."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the cognitive-ease dial: System 1 continuously monitors ease versus strain to decide whether to mobilize effortful System 2. The chapter shows that fluency producers such as clear fonts, repetition, priming, good mood, and rhyme all register on the same single dial, so their effects are interchangeable.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"For each stimulus card, decide which setting it would produce on the cognitive ease dial.","column_a_label":"Induces Cognitive Ease","column_b_label":"Induces Cognitive Strain","column_c_label":"Induces Neither Ease Nor Strain","rows":[{"label":"A sentence printed in a clear, high-contrast font","col":"a"},{"label":"A slogan you have seen repeated dozens of times","col":"a"},{"label":"Reading text printed in small, washed-out gray type","col":"b"},{"label":"Listening to a speaker while you are in a cheerful mood","col":"a"},{"label":"A word primed for a few milliseconds just before you read it","col":"a"},{"label":"An effortful task that engages the operations of System 2","col":"b"},{"label":"An aphorism that rhymes, like Woes unite foes","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Induces neither ease nor strain because most stimuli have no effect on the dial","Only affects long-term memory storage rather than current processing","Only affects visual perception, never judgment or mood"],"explanation":"Clear fonts, repetition, priming, good mood, and rhyme all make associative processing run fluently and register on the Easy side of the dial, while effortful System 2 operations and illegible gray print register as Strained. A single dial receives all these diverse inputs, which is why their effects are interchangeable."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Familiarity, Truth, and Liking: Three Illusions of Ease","visual_frame_description":"An investigator's evidence wall in a bright lab-like setting displays three open case folders labeled Illusion of Remembering, Illusion of Truth, and Mere Exposure Effect. Seven evidence slips float over the folders, each describing a finding such as a made-up celebrity name, a repeated factoid, and a Turkish word shown many times in a newspaper. The player drags each slip into the folder that best explains it.","player_mission":"Classify each finding into the correct illusion by matching the psychological mechanism the chapter describes. Read the slip, recall who studied the effect, and decide whether it is about familiarity misread as fame, repetition misread as truth, or repetition creating liking.","key_concepts":["Larry Jacoby demonstrated the memory illusion in an article titled Becoming Famous Overnight.","A name you have seen before is more likely to be judged the name of a famous person because it is perceived with greater cognitive ease.","Jacoby described familiarity as having a simple but powerful quality of pastness that seems to indicate a direct reflection of prior experience, but the quality is an illusion.","Words that have been seen before become easier to see again, quicker to read, and better identified when masked by noise.","A new word is more likely to be recognized as familiar if it is unconsciously primed for a few milliseconds or shown in sharper contrast.","Frequent repetition is a reliable way to make people believe falsehoods, because familiarity is not easily distinguished from truth.","People repeatedly exposed to the phrase the body temperature of a chicken were more likely to accept the statement that the body temperature of a chicken is 144 degrees.","Robert Zajonc dedicated much of his career to showing that the repetition of an arbitrary stimulus creates mild affection for it, an effect he called the mere exposure effect.","In the Michigan and Michigan State student newspapers, Turkish words shown more often were rated as meaning something good far more than words shown only once.","The mere exposure effect occurs even when repeated stimuli are never consciously seen, and it is actually stronger for stimuli the individual never consciously sees."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how the sense of cognitive ease produces three distinct illusions: familiarity misattributed to fame or memory (Jacoby), repetition misattributed to truth, and repetition producing liking (Zajonc). The chapter shows that a single feeling of ease has many possible sources and that System 1 cannot easily tell them apart.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Drag each evidence slip into the folder that names the illusion it demonstrates.","column_a_label":"Illusion of Remembering (familiarity misread as memory or fame)","column_b_label":"Illusion of Truth (repetition misread as truth)","column_c_label":"Mere Exposure Effect (repetition creating liking)","rows":[{"label":"The made-up name David Stenbill is later judged to be a famous celebrity","col":"a"},{"label":"Hearing the phrase body temperature of a chicken makes you accept the number 144 as true","col":"b"},{"label":"The Turkish word saricik shown 25 times is rated as meaning something good","col":"c"},{"label":"A word shown in sharper contrast than others feels as if you have seen it before","col":"a"},{"label":"Chicks exposed to a tone inside the egg emit fewer distress calls to that tone after hatching","col":"c"},{"label":"An unconsciously primed word is more likely to be recognized as familiar","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Illusion of Truth, because all fluency effects are ultimately about belief","Halo Effect, in which one trait colors all judgments of a person","Anchoring, in which the first number considered biases later estimates"],"explanation":"The David Stenbill case is Jacoby's becoming-famous-overnight effect: ease of perception is misattributed to the person being a known celebrity. The illusion-of-truth and mere-exposure slips operate on the same ease signal but produce belief and liking, respectively, rather than memory."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Designing Persuasion: What Reduces Strain and What Backfires","visual_frame_description":"A graphic designer's studio desk holds two competing campaign boards side by side. The left board uses glossy paper, bright blue ink, a rhyming headline, plain words, and a short pronounceable source name. The right board uses gray matte stock, dense jargon, a tongue-twisting source name, and a claim that contradicts common knowledge. The player assigns each design element to a category on a sorting grid.","player_mission":"Sort each design element into the category that predicts its effect on readers' belief. Use the chapter's persuasive-message findings to separate elements that reduce cognitive strain from those that increase it, and recognize the limits of these tricks.","key_concepts":["Anything you can do to reduce cognitive strain will help your message be believed, so you should first maximize legibility.","Experiments show that a statement like Adolf Hitler was born in 1892 is more likely to be believed than the same claim printed in a less legible form.","Messages are more likely to be believed when printed in bright blue or red than in middling shades of green, yellow, or pale blue.","Danny Oppenheimer showed that couching familiar ideas in pretentious long words is taken as a sign of poor intelligence and low credibility.","Aphorisms such as Woes unite foes were judged more insightful when they rhymed than when they did not.","People gave much more weight to a brokerage report from an easily pronounced name like Artan than from a firm with an unfortunate name like Taahhut.","System 2 is lazy and mental effort is aversive, so recipients want to stay away from anything that reminds them of effort, including a source with a complicated name.","High-quality paper, bright colors, and rhyme will not help a message that is obviously nonsensical or contradicts facts the audience knows to be true.","When a statement is strongly linked by logic or association to beliefs you hold, or comes from a source you trust and like, you feel a sense of cognitive ease and accept it.","People can overcome some superficial factors that produce illusions of truth when strongly motivated to do so, but the lazy System 2 usually adopts the suggestions of System 1 and marches on."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the persuasive-message logic of the ease dial: legibility, vivid contrast, rhyme, and pronounceable names all reduce strain and inflate belief. The chapter adds the crucial limit that these fluency tricks cannot rescue a claim that clashes with facts the audience already knows.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Assign each design element to the category predicting its effect on belief.","column_a_label":"Reduces Strain and Boosts Belief","column_b_label":"Increases Strain and Hurts Belief","column_c_label":"Cannot Be Rescued by Fluency (ease tricks fail)","rows":[{"label":"Bright blue ink on glossy, high-contrast paper","col":"a"},{"label":"A rhyming slogan instead of a plain paraphrase","col":"a"},{"label":"Pretentious vocabulary when simple words would do","col":"b"},{"label":"Quoting a brokerage firm with the unfortunate name Taahhut","col":"b"},{"label":"Plain, simple language, as in Oppenheimer's study","col":"a"},{"label":"An obviously nonsensical claim even when printed in rhyme","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Increases strain and boosts belief, because effort makes a claim more credible","Only a mere exposure effect, having nothing to do with belief","Only a priming effect from muscle tension in the reader"],"explanation":"Bright color, rhyme, and simple language all ease the associative machine and inflate belief, while jargon and hard-to-pronounce sources force strain and undercut credibility. The obviously nonsensical element sorts to column c because the chapter states that these fluency tricks do not rescue a message that contradicts facts the audience knows to be true."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Confident Witness: Familiarity Misread as Memory","visual_frame_description":"A small interview room with a wall clock; Priya, a retail loss-prevention investigator in a navy blazer, sits across from Marco, a store clerk wearing a laminated badge. A transcript panel and a nonverbal log with timestamps scroll on the right side of the screen. Marco answers quickly and leans back in his chair, appearing relaxed and certain.","player_mission":"Audit Marco's account of which customer shared a leaked promo code. Weigh his fluent confidence against what the cognitive-ease research says about familiarity, and issue the correct diagnosis rather than jumping to a deception verdict.","key_concepts":["Familiarity has a simple but powerful quality of pastness that seems to indicate a direct reflection of prior experience, but the quality is an illusion.","Seeing a name before makes it easier to see again and gives the impression that you know it.","Jacoby's becoming-famous-overnight effect shows people judge a previously seen name as belonging to a famous person.","The sense of ease in perceiving a previously seen word is what gives you the impression of familiarity.","System 2 relies on System 1's impression of familiarity for its judgments, even when the source of the familiarity is unknown.","Fluent, relaxed testimony is not evidence of memory accuracy, because cognitive ease is produced by many causes besides genuine recollection.","When you cannot remember the source of a statement and cannot relate it to other things you know, you have no option but to go with the sense of cognitive ease.","The various causes of ease or strain are interchangeable, and you may not know precisely what makes things feel easy or strained."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the illusion of remembering: Marco's fluent confidence comes from cognitive ease, not from a reliable memory trace. The chapter shows that a sense of pastness is manufactured by ease of perception, so confident familiarity is a poor guide to whether a memory is genuine.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Marco's testimony about the customer's identity?","subject_baseline":"Marco is a diligent nine-year store clerk with no prior discipline record; in his monthly interviews he is typically reserved, pauses before answering, and double-checks dates before asserting facts.","observed_nonverbal_log":"10:02 — Marco answers the opening question within two seconds, palm flat on the table, with no hesitation. 10:04 — When asked how he knows the customer's name, he recites it fluently, smiling slightly, gaze steady. 10:07 — Asked for the date of the leak, he gives a confident answer instantly, then shrugs and says he cannot place exactly where he first saw the name. 10:09 — He corrects himself mildly about the day of the week but does not show distress; his posture stays relaxed throughout.","interrogation_transcript":"Priya: How do you know this customer was the one with the code? Marco: I just know the name. I have seen it, it was definitely in the store. Priya: Where exactly did you see it? Marco: I could not tell you where, but I know it. I saw it, that is enough. Priya: Could you have seen the name somewhere else, like on a poster or a report? Marco: No, I remember the person. I am sure of it.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deliberate deception — Marco is experiencing an illusion of familiarity, misreading the cognitive ease of recognizing a name as evidence of having personally seen the customer.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Marco shows classic ease cues, such as instant answers, relaxed posture, and no stress, but that fluency is exactly the problem: the chapter shows cognitive ease produces a feeling of pastness regardless of its source. His admission that he cannot say where he first saw the name is the tell that the familiarity signal is unattached to any memory file, precisely the pattern of Jacoby's becoming-famous-overnight illusion.","distractors":["Deliberate deception, because Marco is lying to protect the real customer","Accurate, detailed memory, because his confidence confirms recall","Cognitive impairment, because his recall failure indicates poor memory function"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Repeated Statistic: Repetition Masquerading as Verification","visual_frame_description":"A windowless compliance office; Dana, a quality-assurance lead, reviews a claim on her monitor while her colleague Tina, a sales director, sits opposite holding a printed spec sheet. On screen, a fact tracker shows the claim conversion rose 12 percent with a small tally of how often the figure has appeared in emails and slide decks this quarter.","player_mission":"Audit whether Tina's assertion of the 12 percent conversion figure is grounded in verified data or in sheer repetition. Diagnose the source of her confidence using the illusions-of-truth findings.","key_concepts":["A reliable way to make people believe in falsehoods is frequent repetition, because familiarity is not easily distinguished from truth.","Authoritarian institutions and marketers have always known that repetition breeds belief.","You do not have to repeat an entire statement to make it appear true; repeating one familiar phrase can make the whole statement feel familiar.","People repeatedly exposed to the phrase the body temperature of a chicken were more likely to accept as true the statement that the body temperature of a chicken is 144 degrees.","If you cannot remember the source of a statement and have no way to relate it to other things you know, you have no option but to go with the sense of cognitive ease.","System 2 relies on System 1's impression of familiarity when making a true-false judgment.","The feeling of ease has many causes, including repetition, and you have no simple way of tracing the feeling to its source.","People can overcome some superficial factors when strongly motivated to do so, but the lazy System 2 usually adopts the suggestions of System 1 and marches on."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the illusion of truth: Tina's confidence rests on how often the figure has been repeated, not on verification. The chapter shows that repetition manufactures a feeling of familiarity that System 2 misreads as the mark of a true statement.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Tina's insistence that the 12 percent figure is accurate?","subject_baseline":"Tina is an energetic, deadline-driven sales director who forwards the quarterly deck to the whole team every Monday; she is known to assert figures with total confidence and rarely re-reads the underlying analytics exports herself.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:41 — Tina quotes 12 percent within three seconds of the question, leaning forward with a bright tone. 09:43 — Asked for the source, she taps the printed deck confidently and says the number has been there all quarter. 09:46 — Asked when she last ran the query herself, she hesitates, glances at the ceiling, then answers cheerfully that the team has been quoting it since January. 09:50 — No classic deception cues; her posture is open, her speech steady, with occasional gestures to the deck.","interrogation_transcript":"Dana: Can you show me the query that produced the 12 percent? Tina: It is in the deck, Dana. It has been in every report since January. Dana: But where did the number itself come from? Tina: Honestly? It has been repeated so often that I would bet on it. Everybody says 12 percent. Dana: So you have never verified it yourself. Tina: I do not need to. It is obviously right, it is everywhere.","correct_diagnosis":"Not a deliberate lie — Tina is suffering the illusion of truth, in which the frequency of repetition creates a feeling of familiarity that is mistaken for verified accuracy.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Tina's answer that it has been repeated so often that she would bet on it is a direct description of the mechanism: familiarity produced by repetition is indistinguishable from truth. Her admission that she has never run the query mirrors the chapter's point that when you cannot trace a feeling to its source you have no option but to trust the ease. The absence of deception cues is consistent with the diagnosis, because the belief is genuinely held.","distractors":["Deliberate fraud, because Tina invented the 12 percent to impress her boss","Careful statistical verification from a fresh data export","A random guess with no underlying cause"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Primed Account: A Coerced-Sounding Story That Isn't a Lie","visual_frame_description":"A neutral meeting room in a bank; Victor, a compliance officer, reviews a recorded interview with Nadia, a junior analyst who reported a suspected policy breach. On screen, a transcript panel shows Nadia's first attempt, halting and vague, and a second account given a day later after she read a company-wide memo on the same incident; the second account is smooth and detailed.","player_mission":"Audit the two accounts and decide why Nadia's second story became so fluent. Determine whether the fluency reflects recovered truth, deliberate coaching, or the interchangeable effects of priming and pre-exposure on memory.","key_concepts":["A word that has been primed, or shown for a few milliseconds just before the test, is more likely to be recognized as familiar.","The various ways of inducing cognitive ease or strain are interchangeable, and you may not know precisely what makes things easy or strained.","The impression of familiarity is produced by System 1, and System 2 relies on that impression for a judgment.","Words that have been seen before become easier to see again and quicker to read.","Repeated exposure to a phrase can make the whole statement feel familiar and therefore true.","The feeling of pastness is an illusion; the sense of ease gives the impression of familiarity without telling you its source.","Manipulations that increase cognitive ease, including priming, a clear font, and pre-exposing words, all have interchangeable effects on judgments.","When you are in a state of cognitive ease you are more likely to trust your intuitions and believe what you hear."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests priming and pre-exposure as causes of cognitive ease: Nadia's second, fluid account was made fluent by reading the company memo, not by recovering a memory. The chapter shows ease has interchangeable causes, so a smoother story is not necessarily a truer one.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of the change between Nadia's two accounts of the same event?","subject_baseline":"Nadia is a junior analyst known for meticulous, slightly anxious reporting; in her first interview she speaks in short sentences, corrects herself often, and flags uncertainty about her own memory of the incident.","observed_nonverbal_log":"11:15 — First interview: Nadia pauses four to five seconds per answer, looks down, describes the meeting in fragmentary sentences, and twice says she is not sure. 11:18 — She checks a notebook repeatedly and gives conflicting orderings of events. 15:02 — Second interview, next day: her answers begin within one second, her sentences are complete and chronological, and she gestures smoothly to details that match the wording of the memo now in her folder. 15:05 — Her posture is relaxed, her gaze steady, with none of the previous hesitation.","interrogation_transcript":"Victor: Your first account was hesitant. What changed? Nadia: I read the company memo about the incident yesterday, and everything snapped into place. Now I remember it clearly. Victor: So the sequence you now give comes from your memory? Nadia: Yes, it all flows now, exactly how it happened. Victor: Did you see any of these details before the memo existed? Nadia: I do not know. But now it is perfectly clear.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deliberate deception — Nadia's new fluency is the product of pre-exposure: reading the memo primed the details, and the resulting cognitive ease is being misattributed to recovered memory.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter shows that pre-exposing material increases ease and that ease is misattributed to familiarity; Nadia even states that the memo snapped everything into place, and her story matches the memo's wording. Her transition from fragmented uncertainty to smooth chronological fluency is the signature of priming, not of a suddenly recovered memory trace. Because the ease is genuine, there are no stress cues, so the correct conclusion is that her confidence is contaminated rather than recovered.","distractors":["Deliberate deception, because Nadia fabricated the second story to cover her own error","Genuine recovered memory, because the delay allowed her to remember correctly","Mood effects, because Nadia simply felt better on the second day for unrelated reasons"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Defeating the Illusion of Truth","visual_frame_description":"A sleek campaign video fills the screen: a glossy, high-contrast ad repeats the claim Vitalight eliminates 99 percent of fatigue in a catchy rhyming jingle, flashes the company logo in bright red on white, and ends with a short, pronounceable brand name. An audience panel on the right shows rising trust meters as each repetition plays.","player_mission":"Stop the audience from accepting an unverified claim. Observe how the ad engineers cognitive ease, identify the mechanism being exploited, and then choose the intervention that breaks the illusion before the campaign goes nationwide.","key_concepts":["Frequent repetition is a reliable way to make people believe falsehoods, because familiarity is not easily distinguished from truth.","Anything that reduces cognitive strain will help make a message believed, so legibility and fluency are powerful levers.","Messages are more likely to be believed when printed in bright colors and on high-contrast, high-quality paper.","Aphorisms are judged more insightful when they rhyme than when they do not.","People give more weight to sources with easily pronounceable names than to those with complicated names.","System 2 relies on System 1's impression of familiarity when making a true-false judgment.","When you feel strained you are more likely to be vigilant and suspicious and to make fewer errors.","High-quality paper, bright colors, and rhyme will not help a message that is obviously nonsensical or contradicts facts the audience knows to be true.","People can overcome some superficial factors that produce illusions of truth when strongly motivated to do so."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The battle tests the illusion of truth: a message becomes more believable when repetition, clear fonts, bright colors, rhyme, and pronounceable sources manufacture cognitive ease, because familiarity is easily mistaken for truth. The chapter shows that introducing strain and confronting the claim with known facts are the ways to break the illusion.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the illusion of truth: a message becomes more believable when repetition, clear fonts, bright colors, rhyme, and pronounceable sources manufacture cognitive ease, because familiarity is easily mistaken for truth.","stage_1_observation":"A sleek campaign video fills the screen: a glossy, high-contrast ad repeats the claim Vitalight eliminates 99 percent of fatigue in a catchy rhyming jingle, flashes the company logo in bright red on white, and ends with a short, pronounceable brand name. The audience panel on the right shows rising trust meters as each repetition plays.","stage_1_question":"Which mechanism is primarily being exploited to make the audience accept this unverified claim?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The illusion of truth through repetition and fluency: frequent, easy-to-process repetition creates a feeling of familiarity that System 2 misreads as evidence that the claim is true.","stage_2_intervention":"You have 30 seconds before the ad runs again. Choose the intervention most likely to break the illusion for the audience.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Force the audience out of cognitive ease: present the claim in an unpolished, hard-to-read format, confront it with a known fact that contradicts it, and demand the source of the 99 percent figure, since the chapter shows cognitive strain mobilizes System 2 and that fluency tricks cannot rescue a claim that contradicts known facts."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Slick Pitch Versus the Lazy Controller","visual_frame_description":"In a boardroom, the founders of NovaGrid deliver a flawless pitch: a rhyming tagline, glossy slides in bright blue, the word breakthrough repeated four times, and a one-syllable company name. The CEO, Marcus, leans back smiling, and the audience's expressions relax with each smooth slide. The financials are never shown in raw form.","player_mission":"Prevent Marcus from approving the investment on the basis of polish alone. Identify how the presentation's fluency is distorting his judgment, then run an intervention that tests the plan's real worth rather than its ease of processing.","key_concepts":["When you are in a state of cognitive ease you are likely to like what you see, believe what you hear, and trust your intuitions.","The various causes of ease or strain have interchangeable effects, and you may not know precisely what makes things easy or strained.","System 2 is lazy, mental effort is aversive, and the lazy System 2 usually adopts the suggestions of System 1.","Cognitive strain, whatever its source, tends to mobilize System 2 and shift people from a casual intuitive mode to a more engaged analytic mode.","In the cognitive reflection test, students who saw puzzles in a small washed-out gray font made far fewer mistakes than those who saw the normal font.","Performance was better with the bad font because cognitive strain makes System 2 more likely to reject the intuitive answer suggested by System 1.","Repetition induces cognitive ease and a comforting feeling of familiarity.","Messages are more likely to be believed when printed in bright colors, and people trust sources with easy names.","When you are in a good mood you become more intuitive but less vigilant and more prone to logical errors."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The battle tests whether a decision maker can resist the allure of cognitive ease: a fluent, well-designed pitch is judged more credible and more sound than the same content delivered under strain because the lazy System 2 adopts System 1's ease signal. The chapter's font experiment shows that deliberately inducing strain mobilizes System 2 and improves the rejection of intuitive but wrong answers.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that fluency misleads judgment: a pitch that is fluent, well-designed, and pleasantly presented will be judged more credible and more sound than the same content delivered with strain, because the lazy System 2 adopts System 1's ease signal without checking the underlying content.","stage_1_observation":"In a boardroom, the founders of NovaGrid deliver a flawless pitch: a rhyming tagline, glossy slides in bright blue, the word breakthrough repeated four times, and a one-syllable company name. The CEO, Marcus, leans back smiling, and the audience's expressions relax with each smooth slide. The financials are never shown in raw form.","stage_1_question":"What is the most dangerous error Marcus is about to make as he feels ready to approve the investment?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He is misattributing cognitive ease to the soundness of the business plan: the fluency of the presentation, through repetition, rhyme, bright contrast, and an easy name, inflates his judgment, and his lazy System 2 is adopting the ease signal without checking the underlying numbers.","stage_2_intervention":"Marcus invites you to act as devil's advocate before he signs. Choose the intervention that most reliably tests the plan's real worth rather than its polish.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Deliberately introduce cognitive strain: print the deck in a hard-to-read font, make the founders answer detailed financial questions without their slides, and require a written source for every repeated claim, because the chapter shows cognitive strain mobilizes System 2, which is then more likely to reject the easy, intuitive verdict of System 1."}]},{"id":"ch11","title":"6. Norms, Surprises, and Causes","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Active Expectations, Passive Norms, and Normalized Coincidences","visual_frame_description":"A living-room calendar wall in a family home shows three magnetic zones: a red zone labeled Active Expectation, a blue zone labeled Passive Expectation, and a green zone labeled Made Normal by Recurrence. Seven day-planner cards float in, each describing an event such as a child returning from school or a repeated car fire on the same road. The player drags each card to the zone that best describes how it is expected.","player_mission":"Classify each event by the kind of expectation it creates, using the chapter's distinction between active conscious expectations, passive normal expectations, and events made to feel normal by a single prior incident.","key_concepts":["System 1's main function is to maintain and update a model of your personal world that represents what is normal in it.","Some expectations are active and conscious, meaning you know you are waiting for a particular event to happen.","You will be surprised if an actively expected event does not occur.","A much larger category of events are expected passively; you do not wait for them, but you are not surprised when they happen.","A single incident may make a recurrence less surprising.","Under some conditions, passive expectations quickly turn active, as when the same unusual event recurs in the same circumstances.","A capacity for surprise is the most sensitive indication of how we understand our world and what we expect from it.","Events appear normal not necessarily because they confirm advance expectations, but because they recruit the original episode from memory and are interpreted in conjunction with it."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between active and passive expectations that System 1 maintains: active expectations produce surprise when violated, while passive norms make events feel unsurprising, and a single recurrence can normalize a rare coincidence. The chapter uses the island-resort and burning-car examples to show how quickly experience redraws the boundaries of normal.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Drag each event card to the zone that best describes how it is expected.","column_a_label":"Active (Conscious) Expectation","column_b_label":"Passive Expectation (normal, not awaited)","column_c_label":"Made Normal by Recurrence","rows":[{"label":"At 4 p.m. you stand by the door awaiting your child returning from school","col":"a"},{"label":"You are not surprised when a stranger at the next table orders water","col":"b"},{"label":"Meeting the same acquaintance abroad twice makes a third meeting feel almost normal","col":"c"},{"label":"For months after two car fires on the same stretch of road, you are prepared to see another burning car there","col":"a"},{"label":"You do not wait for the mail, but you are not surprised when it arrives in the afternoon","col":"b"},{"label":"After a diner winces at the soup, a later startled reaction from the same diner seems unsurprising","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Statistical expectation based on base rates and sample sizes","Effortful deliberate reasoning performed only by System 2","Impossible events that violate physical laws"],"explanation":"Awaiting the child at the door is a textbook active expectation whose violation produces surprise. The mail and the water order are passive norms, while the travel coincidence and the car fires show how a single recurrence recruits the prior episode and makes the event feel normal, exactly as the chapter describes."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Associative Coherence Versus Abnormality Detection","visual_frame_description":"A stage-lit quiz set: a podium with a question card is flanked by three judge panels labeled Fits the Norm (Coherent), Abnormality Detected (Incongruent), and Active Expectation Met. A ticker streams sentences, including the Moses question, odd-sounding sentences, and a normal-size mouse and elephant, and the player assigns each to the panel that best describes System 1's reaction.","player_mission":"Sort each sentence by how System 1 treats it: whether it slots comfortably into associative coherence, triggers fast abnormality detection, or satisfies an active expectation.","key_concepts":["The Moses illusion occurs because the idea of animals going into the ark sets up a biblical context in which Moses is not abnormal.","Moses is not positively expected in the ark question, but his name is not surprising in that context.","Moses and Noah have the same vowel sound and number of syllables, which aids the associative coherence.","Replace Moses with George W. Bush in the ark sentence and you have a poor political joke but no illusion.","Violations of normality are detected with astonishing speed: an odd word in a sentence produced a distinctive brain response within two-tenths of a second.","The same fast brain response occurs when a male voice says I believe I am pregnant or an upper-class voice says I have a large tattoo on my back.","We have norms for a vast number of categories, and these norms provide the background for the immediate detection of anomalies.","When you mention a table without specifying further, people understand a normal table, with a surface that is approximately level and far fewer than 25 legs.","The sentence The large mouse climbed over the trunk of the very small elephant works because shared norms for mice and elephants specify typical sizes and ranges."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests norm theory: sentences that fit the activated context produce associative coherence and are accepted uncritically, while incongruent words trigger rapid abnormality detection. The chapter shows the Moses illusion and fast brain responses to odd sentences as two faces of the same System 1 norm machinery.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Assign each sentence to the panel describing System 1's immediate reaction to it.","column_a_label":"Fits the Norm (Associative Coherence)","column_b_label":"Abnormality Detected (Incongruent)","column_c_label":"Active Expectation Met","rows":[{"label":"How many animals of each kind did Moses take into the ark?","col":"a"},{"label":"Earth revolves around the trouble every year","col":"b"},{"label":"A male voice: I believe I am pregnant because I feel sick every morning","col":"b"},{"label":"An upper-class voice: I have a large tattoo on my back","col":"b"},{"label":"The large mouse climbed over the trunk of the very small elephant","col":"a"},{"label":"The ark question with the name George W. Bush in place of Moses","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Active expectation, because all expected sentences fit the norm","Physical causality perception, as in Michotte's launching","Halo effect from a trusted source"],"explanation":"The Moses question slides past because the ark context makes Moses coherent, supported by the shared sound of the two names; the incongruent sentences trip fast abnormality detection, and the mouse-and-elephant sentence works precisely because shared size norms make the normal image coherent."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Physical Causality, Intentional Causality, and Statistical Reasoning","visual_frame_description":"A psychology-demo theater shows three screens: Michotte's black squares colliding, Heider and Simmel's animated triangles and circle beside a schematic house, and a spreadsheet of county cancer rates with a margin-of-error note. A control desk lets the player route each observation to the mode of thinking it demonstrates.","player_mission":"Route each observation to the kind of causality or reasoning it embodies: Michotte's perceived physical launching, Heider and Simmel's perception of intentions, or genuine statistical reasoning about categories and ensembles.","key_concepts":["Albert Michotte argued that we see physical causality as directly as we see color, and his black-square episodes produce a powerful illusion of causality.","If a second object starts moving instantly after contact, observers describe it as having been launched by the first.","Six-month-old infants see Michotte's sequence as a cause-effect scenario and show surprise when the sequence is altered.","Fritz Heider and Mary-Ann Simmel's one-minute-forty-second film makes viewers see an aggressive large triangle bullying a smaller triangle and a terrified circle.","The perception of intention and emotion in the triangle film is irresistible; only people with autism do not experience it.","We are born ready to identify agents, assign them intentions, and view their actions as expressing individual propensities.","System 1 is adept at finding a coherent causal story that links the fragments of knowledge at its disposal.","The automatic search for causes led Bloomberg to run two contradictory headlines explaining bond prices through Saddam Hussein's capture.","Statistical thinking derives conclusions about individual cases from properties of categories and ensembles, a mode of reasoning System 1 cannot perform.","System 2 can learn to think statistically, but few people receive the necessary training."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario discriminates the chapter's three modes: Michotte's perceived physical launching, Heider and Simmel's irresistible intentional attributions, and statistical reasoning that System 1 lacks. The chapter argues that causal intuitions are prominent because people apply causal thinking even where statistical reasoning is required.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Route each observation to the mode of thinking it demonstrates.","column_a_label":"Physical Causality (launching perception)","column_b_label":"Intentional Causality (agents and intentions)","column_c_label":"Statistical Reasoning (categories and ensembles)","rows":[{"label":"A black square in motion contacts a stationary square, which instantly moves, and you see it as launched","col":"a"},{"label":"Watching Heider and Simmel's film, you see the large triangle bullying the small triangle","col":"b"},{"label":"A six-month-old shows surprise when the launch sequence is reversed","col":"a"},{"label":"Bond traders and editors explain the day's price moves with the capture of Saddam Hussein","col":"b"},{"label":"Jane's lost wallet in New York conjures pickpocket rather than sights","col":"b"},{"label":"Judging a county's kidney-cancer rate while accounting for its small population sample","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Physical causality, because all causality is ultimately physical","Mere exposure effect from repeated exposure","Anchoring and adjustment from an initial number"],"explanation":"Michotte's launching and the infant's surprise are physical causality; the Heider-Simmel film, the bond headlines, and the pickpocket association are all System 1's automatic search for intentional causes; only the sample-size-aware judgment qualifies as statistical reasoning, which System 1 cannot perform on its own."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Coincidence Audit: How Surprise Becomes a False Signal","visual_frame_description":"A travel-and-expense office in the evening; Omar, a procurement manager, reviews a claim from Priya, a sales lead, about randomly running into a major client twice in one week at different airports. On screen, a coincidence tracker shows the two meetings flagged in red as improbable, and a probability wheel spins to illustrate the odds.","player_mission":"Audit whether Priya's two chance meetings with the client are genuinely improbable evidence of a coordinated relationship, or a normal coincidence that surprise has dressed up as significant. Use the chapter's analysis of how a first meeting normalizes a second.","key_concepts":["A single incident may make a recurrence less surprising.","Kahneman describes meeting the same psychologist, Jon, twice on a trip and being distinctly less surprised the second time.","The first meeting changed the idea of Jon in their minds, so he became the psychologist who shows up when we travel abroad.","By any measure of probability, meeting Jon in the theater was no more likely than meeting any other acquaintance, yet it seemed more normal.","Surprise is the most sensitive indication of how we understand our world and what we expect from it, but it is not a measure of objective probability.","When an event recurs in the same circumstances, the second incident can create an active expectation that lasts for months.","Events appear normal because they recruit the original episode from memory and are interpreted in conjunction with it.","System 2 may know that a normalized coincidence is statistically unlikely, but System 1 has already made it feel almost normal."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests whether surprise can be trusted as a probability signal: Priya's second meeting feels improbably meaningful only because the first meeting normalized the idea of encountering this client. The chapter's Jon anecdote shows that recurrence, not objective odds, drives how surprising a coincidence feels.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of the remarkable coincidence in Priya's expense report?","subject_baseline":"Priya is a methodical, straight-talking sales lead who files meticulous trip reports and has never inflated an expense in six years; her diary is color-coded and her receipts are always numbered.","observed_nonverbal_log":"16:40 — Priya recounts the two airport meetings eagerly, smiling, with quick hand movements; she sounds delighted rather than evasive. 16:43 — When Omar asks about her flight schedule, she answers precisely from her diary without checking notes. 16:47 — Asked whether she arranged the second meeting, she pauses, blinks, and says honestly that she could not believe it either and that it just happened again. 16:50 — No signs of concealment: no gaze aversion, no long latencies, no over-explaining.","interrogation_transcript":"Omar: Two meetings with the same client in five days, that seems like a setup. Priya: It was pure chance. I was as surprised as you. Omar: You understand how unlikely that looks? Priya: Yes, but it felt normal. Honestly, after the first meeting, bumping into her again did not feel shocking at all. Omar: Statistically it is about as likely as meeting any other person in that terminal. Priya: I know. But my gut said it meant something.","correct_diagnosis":"No misconduct — Priya is reporting an honest coincidence whose felt significance is an illusion produced by norm theory: the first meeting normalized the second, so her surprise was no longer calibrated to the true odds.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Priya's own words, that after the first meeting it did not feel shocking at all, reproduce the chapter's Jon effect exactly: the recurrence recruited the first episode and made the second meeting feel almost normal. The absence of deception cues and her precise, non-defensive diary answers support honesty. The signal she felt is surprise, which the chapter shows reflects what our model of the world expects rather than the objective probability of the event.","distractors":["Deliberate fabrication to justify an expensive business trip","A coordinated client relationship that Priya is hiding","Cognitive impairment causing memory distortion about the meetings"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Sabotage Story: Causal Thinking After an Unlucky Accident","visual_frame_description":"A warehouse safety office; Ravi, a safety officer, interviews Delia, a forklift operator, after a pallet of ceramics toppled near the packing line. On the right, a root-cause board shows Delia's confident narrative that someone deliberately shifted the load. A dry-erase section labeled Possible causes lists both the sabotage story and mundane slip-ups.","player_mission":"Audit Delia's confident blame of a colleague. Decide whether her account is deception or an automatic causal story, using the chapter's evidence that System 1 hunts for agents and causes even when luck is the true explanation.","key_concepts":["Finding causal connections is part of understanding a story and is an automatic operation of System 1; System 2, the conscious self, accepts the interpretation it is offered.","In a story about a lost wallet in crowded New York, people recalled the word pickpocket more strongly than the word sights that actually appeared.","When the ideas of a lost wallet, New York, and crowds are juxtaposed, they jointly evoke the explanation that a pickpocket caused the loss.","System 1 is adept at finding a coherent causal story that links the fragments of knowledge at its disposal.","The automatic search for causes made the capture of Saddam Hussein the destined explanation of whatever happened in the bond market that day.","A statement that can explain two contradictory outcomes explains nothing at all; the headlines only satisfy our need for coherence.","A large event is supposed to have consequences, and consequences need causes to explain them.","People are prone to apply causal thinking inappropriately to situations that require statistical reasoning.","The chapter notes that someone who cannot accept being unlucky will end up thinking that someone intentionally sabotaged their work."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the automatic search for causality: Delia's confident sabotage claim is a System 1 causal story generated to explain an unlucky accident, not evidence of deception. The chapter shows that a need for coherent causes fills the gap left by statistical explanations such as chance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Delia's confident claim that a coworker deliberately shifted the pallet?","subject_baseline":"Delia is a careful, experienced forklift operator who has never filed a grievance or accused a coworker before; her incident reports are typically terse and factual, and she is respected for level-headedness on the floor.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:12 — Delia states the sabotage story immediately, before any questions, with a fixed, intense gaze and a slightly raised voice. 09:14 — Asked for evidence, she lists the pallet's tilt and the timing of the break, then adds that it has to be someone and that things do not just fall. 09:18 — She becomes agitated when Ravi mentions the forklift's vibration history and repeats the sabotage claim word for word. 09:21 — She shows anger, not fear or evasion, and repeatedly returns to the same conclusion rather than expanding the story.","interrogation_transcript":"Ravi: What exactly do you think happened? Delia: Someone moved that pallet. It was deliberate. Ravi: What makes you say that? Delia: A load that is stacked right does not just topple. There has to be a reason. Ravi: The maintenance log shows this forklift vibrates badly on that strip of floor. Delia: No. That is not it. Someone did this. There has to be a cause, it cannot just be nothing.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deception and not a reliable factual account — Delia is expressing an automatic causal story: her System 1 cannot accept an unlucky, chance event, so it generates an intentional agent as the cause, exactly as the chapter's pickpocket and sabotaged-work examples describe.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Delia's insistence that there has to be a cause is the chapter's point verbatim: consequences need causes, and System 1 fills the gap with an agent when no cause is visible. The signs of anger and fixation, with no classic deception cues, fit a genuinely held causal story rather than a manufactured alibi. Her refusal of the mundane vibration explanation mirrors how causal thinking crowds out statistical, chance-based explanations.","distractors":["Deliberate false accusation to cover her own driving error","Accurate eyewitness testimony based on direct observation","A delusional disorder requiring medical referral"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Two Wins Do Not Make a Law","visual_frame_description":"In a startup's war room, the founders show a dashboard: their new ad strategy has won big in two consecutive test markets. The whole team nods, saying twice in a row means it is clearly working. A ticker notes that the two markets were chosen for convenience and that hundreds of markets remain untested.","player_mission":"Stop the team from scaling a strategy on the strength of two convenient wins. Identify why the recurrence feels so convincing, then force the team to switch from causal storytelling to statistical reasoning before they commit the budget.","key_concepts":["A single incident may make a recurrence less surprising, and very little repetition is needed for a new experience to feel normal.","System 1's model of what is normal is built from associations of events that co-occur with some regularity.","When an event recurs in the same circumstances, it can quickly become an active expectation.","System 1 does not have the capability for statistical reasoning about categories and ensembles.","System 2 can learn to think statistically, but few people receive the necessary training.","People are prone to apply causal thinking inappropriately to situations that require statistical reasoning.","We should not focus exclusively on the average; we should consider the entire range of normal reactions.","A capacity for surprise is the most sensitive indication of how we understand our world and what we expect from it."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The battle tests the normalization of small-sample evidence: when an event recurs just a couple of times, System 1 quietly treats it as normal and meaningful, yet such recurrences prove nothing about the underlying rate. The chapter shows that causal thinking and the comfort of recurrence must be checked by statistical reasoning, which only a trained System 2 can supply.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the normalization of small-sample evidence: when an event recurs just a couple of times, System 1 quietly treats it as normal and meaningful, yet such recurrences prove nothing about the underlying rate, and only statistical reasoning about the whole ensemble can correct the impression.","stage_1_observation":"In a startup's war room, the founders show a dashboard: their new ad strategy has won big in two consecutive test markets. The whole team nods, saying twice in a row means it is clearly working. A ticker notes that the two markets were chosen for convenience and that hundreds of markets remain untested.","stage_1_question":"Why is the team's leap from two wins to the strategy works so seductive despite being statistically weak?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The recurrence normalizes the result and manufactures confidence, but two observations tell you almost nothing about the true rate; the chapter shows System 1 is comfortable with causal stories from tiny samples and is not built for statistical reasoning about ensembles.","stage_2_intervention":"The team wants to scale the strategy nationally tomorrow. Choose the intervention that best counters the normalcy trap.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Force a shift to statistical reasoning: demand the base rate of similar campaigns, the size of the samples, and the full range of plausible outcomes before scaling, because System 2 can learn to think statistically even though few people are trained to do so, and the ease-driven conclusion that it works must be checked against the ensemble."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Post-Hoc Headline Machine","visual_frame_description":"A newsroom screen shows the morning's bond desk: prices rose after Saddam Hussein's capture, and Bloomberg flashed the headline U.S. TREASURIES RISE; HUSSEIN CAPTURE MAY NOT CURB TERRORISM. Half an hour later prices fell, and the headline flipped to U.S. TREASURIES FALL; HUSSEIN CAPTURE BOOSTS ALLURE OF RISKY ASSETS. Editors nod approvingly at each framing.","player_mission":"Reform the newsroom's habit of forcing every market move into a causal story about the day's biggest event. Identify the flaw in the paired headlines, then choose the reporting move that resists the automatic search for causes.","key_concepts":["A story in Nassim Taleb's The Black Swan shows bond prices rising and then falling after the capture of Saddam Hussein, with a matching headline for each direction.","Because of the automatic search for causes, Hussein's capture was destined to be the explanation of whatever happened in the market that day.","A statement that can explain two contradictory outcomes explains nothing at all.","The two headlines only satisfy our need for coherence.","A large event is supposed to have consequences, and consequences need causes to explain them.","System 1 is adept at finding a coherent causal story that links the fragments of knowledge at its disposal.","Finding causal connections is part of understanding a story and is an automatic operation of System 1.","People are prone to apply causal thinking inappropriately to situations that require statistical reasoning."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"The battle tests the automatic search for causes: any notable event becomes the explanation for whatever happens next, even when the two explanations are contradictory and therefore explain nothing. The chapter shows these stories are not explanations but products of our need for coherence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the automatic search for causality: any large, salient event becomes the explanation for whatever happens next, even when the resulting statements are contradictory and therefore explain nothing at all.","stage_1_observation":"A newsroom screen shows the morning's bond desk: prices rose after Saddam Hussein's capture, and Bloomberg flashed the headline U.S. TREASURIES RISE; HUSSEIN CAPTURE MAY NOT CURB TERRORISM. Half an hour later prices fell, and the headline flipped to U.S. TREASURIES FALL; HUSSEIN CAPTURE BOOSTS ALLURE OF RISKY ASSETS. Editors nod approvingly at each framing.","stage_1_question":"What is the central flaw in the two headlines the editors are celebrating?","stage_1_correct_answer":"A statement that can explain two contradictory outcomes explains nothing at all; the headlines only satisfy the need for coherence, since a large event is supposed to have consequences and consequences need causes, a search performed automatically by System 1.","stage_2_intervention":"You have one minute to steer the next bulletin. Choose the move that best resists the causal-story machine.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Publish the price move without forcing a cause, label the explanation as speculation, and acknowledge that the day's fragments do not determine a single cause, because System 1 is adept at finding a coherent story but the honest report must refuse to manufacture one from insufficient information."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Slaying the Moses Illusion","visual_frame_description":"A live interview set: the host asks a bright guest, How many animals of each kind did Moses take into the ark? The guest smiles and starts counting, the audience murmurs approvingly, and a fact-check panel lights up with the warning PRESUPPOSITION ERROR, noting that Moses took no animals onto the ark. The guest still does not notice.","player_mission":"Use the Moses illusion to teach the audience how System 1 accepts a false presupposition. Explain why the error goes undetected, then choose an intervention that makes people actually catch it.","key_concepts":["The Moses illusion occurs because the ark sets up a biblical context in which Moses is not abnormal.","You did not positively expect Moses in the ark question, but the mention of his name is not surprising in that context.","It also helps that Moses and Noah have the same vowel sound and number of syllables.","As with the triads that produce cognitive ease, you unconsciously detect associative coherence between Moses and ark and so quickly accept the question.","Replace Moses with George W. Bush in the sentence and you have a poor political joke but no illusion.","When something does not fit into the current context of activated ideas, the system detects an abnormality.","Violations of normality are detected with astonishing speed, within about two-tenths of a second of an odd word.","We have norms for a vast number of categories, and these norms provide the background for the immediate detection of anomalies."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The battle tests the Moses illusion: a question whose false presupposition fits the activated context will be accepted and answered, because associative coherence plus phonological similarity suppress the abnormality that System 2 would otherwise detect. The chapter shows that swapping in an incongruent name triggers abnormality detection and that only deliberate System 2 checking catches the error.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the Moses illusion: a question whose false presupposition fits the activated context will be accepted and answered, because associative coherence plus phonological similarity suppress the abnormality that System 2 would otherwise detect.","stage_1_observation":"A live interview set: the host asks a bright guest, How many animals of each kind did Moses take into the ark? The guest smiles and starts counting, the audience murmurs approvingly, and a fact-check panel lights up with the warning PRESUPPOSITION ERROR, noting that Moses took no animals onto the ark. The guest still does not notice.","stage_1_question":"Why does nearly everyone fail to catch the error in this question?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The ark sets up a biblical context in which Moses is not abnormal, and the similarity of the names Moses and Noah in sound and syllable count strengthens the associative coherence, so System 1 accepts the question and jumps to answering rather than checking its presupposition.","stage_2_intervention":"The host wants to turn the moment into a lesson. Choose the intervention most likely to make the audience actually detect the error.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Replace Moses with a name that does not fit the context, such as George W. Bush, which triggers abnormality detection, and then deliberately check the presupposition of every question, because the chapter shows violations of normality are detected with astonishing speed when the context does not fit, and only deliberate System 2 checking is what catches the illusion."}]},{"id":"ch12","title":"7. A Machine for Jumping to Conclusions","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Halo Effect, Confirmation Bias, and WYSIATI as Coherence Mechanisms","visual_frame_description":"An evidence-sorting station shows three glowing bins labeled Halo Effect, Confirmation Bias, and WYSIATI. A conveyor belt feeds in six scenario cards, each showing a named person and a short workplace story: a manager sizing up a colleague, a professor grading essays, a memory question about a friend, a product lead running tests, a review board, and a leadership panel. The player drags each card into the bin whose mechanism best explains the outcome.","player_mission":"Read each scenario card and decide which of the three coherence mechanisms it demonstrates. Match the behavior to the mechanism the chapter names, and justify each placement from the evidence in the text.","key_concepts":["The halo effect is the tendency to like or dislike everything about a person, including things you have not observed, and it plays a large role in shaping our view of people and situations.","The halo effect is one of the ways the representation of the world that System 1 generates is simpler and more coherent than the real thing.","Real evidence of generosity is missing in the story of Joan, and the gap is filled by a guess that fits one's emotional response to her.","The sequence in which we observe characteristics of a person is often determined by chance, yet sequence matters because the halo effect increases the weight of first impressions.","The operations of associative memory contribute to a general confirmation bias: when asked whether Sam is friendly, different instances of Sam's behavior come to mind than if you had been asked whether he is unfriendly.","A deliberate search for confirming evidence, known as positive test strategy, is how System 2 tests a hypothesis, contrary to the philosophers' advice to try to refute it.","WYSIATI, which stands for what you see is all there is, describes how System 1 is radically insensitive to both the quality and the quantity of information behind its impressions and intuitions.","The measure of success for System 1 is the coherence of the story it manages to create; the amount and quality of the data on which the story is based are largely irrelevant.","The combination of a coherence-seeking System 1 with a lazy System 2 implies that System 2 will endorse many intuitive beliefs that closely reflect the impressions generated by System 1."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the three main coherence mechanisms of the chapter: the halo effect, which inflates the weight of first impressions of a person; confirmation bias, which biases memory and evidence search toward what is already believed; and WYSIATI, the radical insensitivity of System 1 to missing information. Kahneman shows all three make the world feel simpler and more coherent than it is, which is why a lazy System 2 so often endorses the resulting intuitions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"For each scenario card, decide which mechanism best explains the outcome.","column_a_label":"Halo Effect (emotional coherence about a person)","column_b_label":"Confirmation Bias / Positive Test Strategy","column_c_label":"WYSIATI (what you see is all there is)","rows":[{"label":"A manager who liked a colleague's confident presentation now assumes the same colleague is punctual, fair, and generous","col":"a"},{"label":"After a student's strong first essay, a professor gives the vague second essay the benefit of the doubt","col":"a"},{"label":"Asked whether Sam is friendly, you retrieve instances of friendliness rather than instances of unfriendliness","col":"b"},{"label":"A product lead designs tests that would confirm her new feature works rather than tests that could refute it","col":"b"},{"label":"A review board that saw only the prosecution's account reports more confidence than a board that heard both sides","col":"c"},{"label":"Hearing that Mindik is intelligent and strong, you answer that she will be a good leader without asking what you would need to know","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Anchoring, because the first piece of information biases later estimates","Availability heuristic, because vivid cases come to mind easily","Regression to the mean, because extreme first impressions average out"],"explanation":"The halo-effect rows spread one positive impression to unobserved traits and give later information the benefit of the doubt; the confirmation-bias rows show memory and test design slanted toward what is already believed; the WYSIATI rows show confident judgments formed from partial, one-sided evidence with no allowance for what is missing."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Suppression of Ambiguity, Framing, and Base-Rate Neglect","visual_frame_description":"A museum-style exhibit wall presents three panels labeled Suppression of Ambiguity, Framing Effects, and Base-Rate Neglect. Six evidence cards float in front of the wall, each illustrating a finding: the A B C and 12 13 14 display, the word bank, a surgeon's survival statistic, a fat-free label, and a meek tidy stranger. The player routes each card to the panel it best demonstrates.","player_mission":"Classify each exhibit into the bias it demonstrates. Read the card, recall how the chapter presents each example, and decide whether it is about resolving ambiguity, reacting to a single formulation, or letting vivid individual description override statistical facts.","key_concepts":["The middle shapes in the A B C and 12 13 14 displays are identical, but you read them as letters in a context of letters and as numbers in a context of numbers.","The word bank is ambiguous: without an explicit context, System 1 generated a likely context on its own, and you were not aware of the choice or of the possibility of another interpretation.","System 1 does not keep track of alternatives that it rejects, or even of the fact that there were alternatives; conscious doubt is not in its repertoire.","The statement that the odds of survival one month after surgery are 90 percent is more reassuring than the equivalent statement that mortality within one month is 10 percent.","Cold cuts described as 90 percent fat-free are more attractive than when they are described as 10 percent fat.","The equivalence of the alternative formulations is transparent, but an individual normally sees only one formulation, and what she sees is all there is.","Steve, the meek and tidy soul, is often believed to be a librarian even though you surely know that there are more male farmers than male librarians.","The personality description of Steve is salient and vivid, and the statistical fact about farmers almost certainly did not come to mind when you first considered the question.","Framing effects arise because different ways of presenting the same information often evoke different emotions."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests three biases that Kahneman traces to the same System 1 machinery: the suppression of ambiguity, framing effects, and base-rate neglect. Each demonstrates that what we see is all there is, because System 1 resolves ambiguity without awareness, reacts to the single formulation before it, and lets vivid individual descriptions override statistical facts.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Assign each exhibit card to the bias it best demonstrates.","column_a_label":"Suppression of Ambiguity","column_b_label":"Framing Effects","column_c_label":"Base-Rate Neglect","rows":[{"label":"The same middle shape is read as B among letters and as 13 among numbers, without awareness of the ambiguity","col":"a"},{"label":"Without context, the word bank conjures money and tellers, and you never notice that another interpretation was possible","col":"a"},{"label":"The odds of survival one month after surgery are 90 percent feels more reassuring than mortality within one month is 10 percent","col":"b"},{"label":"Cold cuts described as 90 percent fat-free are more attractive than the same product described as 10 percent fat","col":"b"},{"label":"Steve, meek and tidy, is judged likely to be a librarian even though there are far more male farmers than male librarians","col":"c"},{"label":"A politely dressed stranger carrying a hardcover book is assumed to be a writer, though writing is a far rarer occupation than the trades around him","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Halo effect, because one trait colors all judgments of a person","Anchoring, because the first number considered dominates the estimate","Mere exposure, because repeated exposure increases liking"],"explanation":"The ambiguity cards show System 1 settling on a single interpretation without registering that alternatives existed. The framing cards show equivalent descriptions evoking different emotions because only one formulation is seen. The base-rate cards show a vivid individual description crowding out the statistical fact that should govern the judgment."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Independent Judgments, Shared Bias, and First-Impression Contamination","visual_frame_description":"A meeting-room simulation shows three zones on a whiteboard: Independent Judgments, Correlated Judgments, and Halo-Effect Contamination. Six activity cards float in, depicting a penny-jar contest, two witnesses chatting before testifying, an open committee discussion, a professor grading booklets, and a row of scorecards. The player sorts each card into the zone that describes how the judgments behave.","player_mission":"Sort each activity by how its errors behave. Use the chapter's principle of decorrelated errors to tell apart independent judgments that cancel errors, shared biases that resist aggregation, and sequential judgments contaminated by a first impression.","key_concepts":["When many people estimate the pennies in a jar, individuals do very poorly, but pools of individual judgments do remarkably well.","The errors that individuals make are independent of the errors made by others and, in the absence of a systematic bias, they tend to average to zero.","If the observers share a bias, the aggregation of judgments will not reduce it.","Allowing the observers to influence each other effectively reduces the size of the sample, and with it the precision of the group estimate.","Good police procedure keeps multiple witnesses from discussing an event before testifying, so that unbiased witnesses do not influence each other.","A simple rule for meetings is that before an issue is discussed, all members of the committee should write a very brief summary of their position.","The standard practice of open discussion gives too much weight to the opinions of those who speak early and assertively, causing others to line up behind them.","Kahneman found that when he scored the first essay before the rest, that question had a disproportionate effect on the overall grade, a halo effect.","When he switched to scoring all answers to one question before the next, his grades for a single student often varied over a considerable range."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the principle of decorrelated errors: independent judgments with independent errors average to accurate estimates, while shared bias and mutual influence preserve or inflate error. Kahneman argues that the halo effect contaminates sequential judgments, so the remedy is to make observations independent before aggregating them.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each activity card into the zone that best describes how its judgments and errors behave.","column_a_label":"Independent Judgments (errors cancel)","column_b_label":"Correlated Judgments / Shared Bias (aggregation fails)","column_c_label":"Halo-Effect Contamination (first impression distorts later judgments)","rows":[{"label":"Many people privately estimate the pennies in a jar, and the averaged estimates are remarkably accurate","col":"a"},{"label":"Witnesses who discuss the event before testifying end up sharing the same errors, reducing the value of their testimony","col":"b"},{"label":"In an open committee, members line up behind the first person who speaks early and assertively","col":"b"},{"label":"Observers who share a common bias produce an average estimate that is just as biased as any single judgment","col":"b"},{"label":"A professor who scores a student's strong first essay then gives the same student's weak second essay the benefit of the doubt","col":"c"},{"label":"Scoring all students' answers to question one before reading any question two makes each student's grades vary across a wide range","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Only a laboratory curiosity with no real-world application","A proof that group judgment is always worse than individual judgment","A phenomenon caused exclusively by personality traits"],"explanation":"Private, independent estimates have uncorrelated errors that average toward accuracy, and scoring all answers to one question first removes contamination. The witness chat, the open committee, and the shared bias all make errors correlated, so aggregation cannot reduce them. The professor who lets the first essay color the second is the chapter's own halo-effect example."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The One-Sided Case: Confidence Without Evidence","visual_frame_description":"A corporate investigation room with a single wall clock; Theo, a regional operations manager in a gray suit, sits behind a desk with one thin report open. A colleague stands across from him holding a second folder. On the right, a confidence meter shows a high, steady reading, and a transcript panel scrolls Theo's answers.","player_mission":"Audit Theo's confident ruling in a workplace complaint case. Decide whether his certainty reflects deliberate deception, accurate expertise, or the one-sided-evidence effect, and issue the correct diagnosis using the chapter's legal-scenario findings.","key_concepts":["Participants who were exposed to one-sided evidence were more confident of their judgments than those who saw both sides.","The confidence that people experience is determined by the coherence of the story they manage to construct from available information.","It is the consistency of the information that matters for a good story, not its completeness.","Knowing little often makes it easier to fit everything you know into a coherent pattern.","The lawyers in the study added no useful information that you could not infer from the background story, yet one-sided presentation had a pronounced effect.","WYSIATI, what you see is all there is, explains why neither the quantity nor the quality of evidence counts for much in subjective confidence.","We often fail to allow for the possibility that evidence that should be critical to our judgment is missing.","The associative system tends to settle on a coherent pattern of activation and suppresses doubt and ambiguity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests WYSIATI and one-sided evidence: Theo's confidence comes from the coherence of a single consistent story, not from the completeness of the evidence. Kahneman's legal scenario showed that people given one-sided evidence are more confident than those who heard both sides, because confidence tracks story coherence, not data quality.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Theo's confident ruling in favor of the store manager?","subject_baseline":"Theo is a meticulous regional manager with twenty years of experience who prides himself on reading every document twice; in past reviews he has often changed his position after hearing the other side, and his colleagues call him even-handed.","observed_nonverbal_log":"14:05 — Theo states his conclusion within seconds of being asked, arms crossed, tone flat and certain. 14:08 — Asked whether he considered the union representative's account, he gestures to the single report on his desk and says the manager's version is consistent. 14:11 — When pressed to imagine the other side's argument, he shrugs, looks away briefly, then repeats his conclusion with unchanged confidence. 14:15 — No classic deception cues: no sweating, no long pauses, no contradictions; his confidence simply does not waver.","interrogation_transcript":"Colleague: What is your ruling? Theo: The manager acted properly. He gave the union man a lawful choice, and the arrest followed a refusal. Colleague: Did you read the union representative's statement? Theo: I do not need it. The manager's account hangs together; every detail supports every other. Colleague: Your colleague who read both accounts came to the opposite conclusion. Theo: She sat with too much information. The more I read, the less sure I felt, and that is not a good sign.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deliberate deception — Theo's high confidence is a product of one-sided evidence: he constructed a coherent story from a single account, and coherence, not completeness, drove his confidence (WYSIATI).","diagnostic_breakdown":"Theo exhibits the exact pattern from the chapter's legal study: participants who saw only one side were more confident than those who saw both, because confidence depends on the coherence of the story that can be constructed from available information. His remark that more reading made him less sure is the WYSIATI signature, and the absence of deception cues confirms that the belief is genuine rather than manufactured.","distractors":["Deliberate deception to protect a favored store manager","Accurate judgment from superior expertise in retail operations","Emotional bias from personal dislike of union representatives"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Depleted Believer: System 2 Too Busy to Unbelieve","visual_frame_description":"A dim compliance office late in the evening; Lena, a compliance analyst in a rumpled cardigan, slumps at her desk under a wall of sticky notes. A manager stands beside her holding a printed chain email. On the right, a fatigue tracker shows a full day of audit sessions, and a transcript panel records Lena's flat, certain answers.","player_mission":"Audit Lena's insistence that a vendor-tampering rumor is a verified fact. Determine whether she is lying, accurately recalling a document, or demonstrating the bias to believe under depletion, and issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Daniel Gilbert proposed that understanding a statement must begin with an attempt to believe it; only then can you decide whether or not to unbelieve it.","Gilbert sees unbelief as an operation of System 2, which is in charge of doubting and unbelieving but is sometimes busy and often lazy.","In Gilbert's experiment, participants who held digits in memory during the task had difficulty unbeliving false sentences.","In a later memory test, the depleted participants ended up thinking that many of the false sentences were true.","When System 2 is otherwise engaged, we will believe almost anything; System 1 is gullible and biased to believe.","Even a nonsensical statement will evoke initial belief, as when whitefish eat candy produced vague impressions of fish and candy.","People are more likely to be influenced by empty persuasive messages, such as commercials, when they are tired and depleted.","The initial attempt to believe is an automatic operation of System 1, which involves the construction of the best possible interpretation of the situation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the bias to believe described by Daniel Gilbert: understanding begins with an automatic attempt to believe, and unbelief is a System 2 operation. When System 2 is busy or depleted, we fail to unbelieve false statements and later treat them as true, which is why Lena remembers the rumor as fact.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Lena's insistence that the vendor-tampering claim is a verified fact?","subject_baseline":"Lena is a careful compliance analyst who normally double-checks source documents before asserting anything; in her first six months she was praised for flagging two genuine discrepancies and for refusing to speculate.","observed_nonverbal_log":"21:40 — Lena states the tampering claim as fact on the first mention, eyes unfocused, voice low and flat, shoulders slumped. 21:44 — Asked where she saw it, she hesitates, scrolls her inbox, and says she saw it somewhere, maybe in the afternoon meeting. 21:48 — Told that the claim came from a chain email, she frowns, appears genuinely puzzled, and says that cannot be because she remembers it being true. 21:52 — No evasion cues; her affect is exhaustion, not alertness, and she seems unable to trace the source of her own belief.","interrogation_transcript":"Manager: You stated the vendor tampered with the invoice as a confirmed fact. Lena: It is confirmed. I am sure I saw it. Manager: Where was that confirmed? Lena: I do not remember exactly, but I am sure it is true. Manager: You were in a three-hour audit session when that email went around, holding figures in your head the whole time. Lena: Maybe. I do not remember that part. But the claim is true; I remember it as true.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deliberate deception — Lena is experiencing the bias to believe: her System 2 was otherwise engaged during a demanding task, so she failed to unbelieve a false statement and now remembers it as true.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Gilbert's experiment showed that when System 2 is disrupted, people have difficulty unbeliving false sentences and later misremember them as true; Lena's story mirrors this exactly, since she was holding figures in memory at the very moment the claim reached her. Her inability to trace the source while insisting on truth is the signature of a depleted, gullible System 1, and her flat, exhausted affect shows no classic deception cues.","distractors":["Deliberate cover-up because Lena helped tamper with the invoice","Accurate recollection of a documented fraud she reviewed","A random guess made under time pressure"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Breaking the Halo Effect in Hiring","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit hiring suite at a design studio; three panelists interview a single candidate, Tomas, whose opening portfolio answer is brilliant. The panelists nod and smile, and from that moment every later answer is rated glowingly. A scoring sheet on the wall shows all six competencies marked at the same near-perfect level, and one panelist whispers that the candidate seems flawless.","player_mission":"Stop the panel from converting one strong first impression into a flawless scorecard. Identify the halo effect in action, then run the intervention that produces reliable, uncorrelated scores before the hiring decision is made.","key_concepts":["The halo effect is the tendency to like or dislike everything about a person, including things you have not observed.","The halo effect increases the weight of first impressions, sometimes to the point that subsequent information is mostly wasted.","Kahneman found that when he gave a high score to a student's first essay, he gave the student the benefit of the doubt whenever he encountered a vague or ambiguous statement later on.","The consistency that came from reading each booklet in sequence was spurious and produced a feeling of cognitive ease, which his System 2 lazily accepted.","Kahneman's new procedure was to read and score all the students' answers to the first question before going on to the next, writing scores where they could not bias later reading.","Under the new procedure his grades for a single student often varied over a considerable range, and his confidence in his grading was much lower.","The procedure Kahneman adopted conforms to a general principle: decorrelate error.","If the observers share a bias, the aggregation of judgments will not reduce it."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The battle tests the halo effect in evaluation: one strong first impression inflates every later judgment, and the resulting consistency is spurious, producing a feeling of cognitive ease that a lazy System 2 accepts. The chapter's remedy is to decorrelate errors by scoring each dimension across candidates before discussing, which is exactly the intervention that defeats the halo in this panel.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the halo effect in evaluation: once the interviewer is impressed by a strong first answer, the positive impression colors every later judgment, so the candidate gets the benefit of the doubt on everything else and the final rating becomes more coherent than the evidence justifies.","stage_1_observation":"In a sunlit hiring suite at a design studio, three panelists interview a single candidate, Tomas. His opening portfolio answer is brilliant, and the panelists nod and smile. From that moment each later answer is rated glowingly, and the final scoring sheet shows all six competencies at the same near-perfect level. One panelist whispers that the candidate seems flawless.","stage_1_question":"Why is the panel's near-uniform glowing scorecard so seductive, and what psychological mechanism is driving it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The halo effect: the strong first impression led the panel to like everything about Tomas, including competencies they did not actually observe, so later information was mostly wasted and the scores became artificially homogeneous; the spurious consistency also produces cognitive ease, so a lazy System 2 accepts the final scorecard without noticing the missing evidence.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the hiring lead and the panel is about to lock in final scores. Choose the intervention that most directly defeats the halo effect before the decision is made.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Decorrelation of errors: have each panelist score every candidate on a single competency before anyone moves to the next competency, with all scores written where they cannot contaminate later ratings and no discussion until every score is recorded, because Kahneman's grading change showed this procedure exposes real variability and removes first-impression contamination."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Mindik's Two Adjectives (WYSIATI in Leadership)","visual_frame_description":"In a boardroom, the succession committee reviews a one-line candidate summary that reads: Sofia is intelligent and strong. The chair asks for a show of hands on leadership readiness, and nearly everyone raises a hand within seconds. A junior analyst in the corner starts to ask what other information they would need before judging, but the vote is already underway.","player_mission":"Stop the committee from converting two adjectives into a leadership verdict. Explain the WYSIATI trap, then force the committee to seek the information the story does not contain before any decision is finalized.","key_concepts":["WYSIATI, which stands for what you see is all there is, describes how System 1 is radically insensitive to both the quality and the quantity of the information behind its impressions.","An essential design feature of the associative machine is that it represents only activated ideas; information that is not retrieved from memory might as well not exist.","The measure of success for System 1 is the coherence of the story it manages to create, while the amount and quality of the data on which the story is based are largely irrelevant.","When information is scarce, System 1 operates as a machine for jumping to conclusions.","On reading that Mindik is intelligent and strong, you answer that she will be a good leader without asking what you would need to know before forming an opinion about someone's leadership.","The best story that can be constructed from two adjectives is delivered with great cognitive ease, and there remains a bias favoring the first impression.","System 2 is capable of a more systematic approach to evidence, such as buying a home, when you deliberately seek information that you don't have.","Overconfidence follows from WYSIATI because neither the quantity nor the quality of evidence counts for much in subjective confidence."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"The battle tests WYSIATI: System 1 constructs the most coherent story from activated information and is radically insensitive to both the quality and quantity of the missing evidence. Kahneman shows that jumping to conclusions from scant data feels easy and confident, and the only correction is a deliberate System 2 search for the information that the story does not contain.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is WYSIATI, what you see is all there is: System 1 builds the best possible story from the ideas currently activated and cannot allow for information it does not have, so a two-adjective description like intelligent and strong produces an instant, overconfident leadership verdict.","stage_1_observation":"In a boardroom, the succession committee reviews a one-line candidate summary that reads: Sofia is intelligent and strong. The chair asks for a show of hands on leadership readiness, and nearly everyone raises a hand within seconds. A junior analyst in the corner starts to ask what other information they would need before judging, but the vote is already underway.","stage_1_question":"What does the chapter say the board members failed to do before forming their opinion about Sofia's leadership?","stage_1_correct_answer":"They never asked what they would need to know before forming an opinion: System 1 built the best possible story from the two adjectives and delivered it with cognitive ease, so the members jumped to a conclusion and retained a bias favoring that first impression, the pattern Kahneman describes with Mindik (WYSIATI).","stage_2_intervention":"The vote is about to be finalized. Choose the intervention that best forces the board to overcome WYSIATI before deciding.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Stop the vote and require the board to list the information they would need about Sofia's integrity, track record, and management results, then gather that evidence from independent sources before any judgment, because System 1 is radically insensitive to missing information and only a deliberate, checklist-driven System 2 can ask what the story leaves out."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: Killing the Rumor Machine (Confirmation Bias)","visual_frame_description":"In a crowded office kitchen, a rumor spreads that the company is being acquired and the local office will close. Within an hour, employees trade evidence: the CEO's calendar has a mystery meeting, a contractor asked about office space, two managers left early. Each new item is greeted as proof, and a whiteboard labeled Evidence fills up. An intern quietly asks what would disprove the rumor, and nobody can answer.","player_mission":"Defuse the rumor before morale collapses. Identify the confirmation machinery that keeps the evidence board growing, then run an intervention that forces the office to seek disconfirming evidence.","key_concepts":["The operations of associative memory contribute to a general confirmation bias.","When asked whether Sam is friendly, different instances of Sam's behavior come to mind than if you had been asked whether he is unfriendly.","A deliberate search for confirming evidence, known as positive test strategy, is how System 2 tests a hypothesis.","Contrary to the rules of philosophers of science, who advise testing hypotheses by trying to refute them, people seek data that are likely to be compatible with the beliefs they currently hold.","The confirmatory bias of System 1 favors uncritical acceptance of suggestions.","If you are asked about the probability of a tsunami hitting California within the next thirty years, the images that come to mind are images of tsunamis, and you will be prone to overestimate the probability of a disaster.","When System 2 is otherwise engaged, we will believe almost anything; System 1 is gullible and biased to believe.","The combination of a coherence-seeking System 1 with a lazy System 2 implies that System 2 will endorse many intuitive beliefs."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The battle tests confirmation bias: people retrieve and seek evidence that matches the belief they already hold, and the confirmatory bias of System 1 favors uncritical acceptance of suggestions. Kahneman's positive-test-strategy analysis shows that a deliberate refutation test is the countermeasure to the rumor machine.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is confirmation bias and the positive test strategy: once a belief takes hold, associative memory retrieves confirming instances and people deliberately seek evidence compatible with what they already hold, rarely attempting to refute it.","stage_1_observation":"In a crowded office kitchen, a rumor spreads that the company is being acquired and the local office will close. Within an hour, employees trade evidence: the CEO's calendar has a mystery meeting, a contractor asked about office space, two managers left early. Each new item is greeted as proof, and a whiteboard labeled Evidence fills up. An intern quietly asks what would disprove the rumor, and nobody can answer.","stage_1_question":"Which mechanism from the chapter best explains why the rumor keeps accumulating confirming evidence?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Confirmation bias: associative memory retrieves instances compatible with the belief that the office will close, and the employees are using a positive test strategy, seeking data likely to confirm the rumor rather than trying to refute it, exactly as the chapter describes for the friendly-or-unfriendly Sam question.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the office manager and need to calm the rumor before morale collapses. Choose the intervention most aligned with the chapter's analysis.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Force a search for disconfirming evidence: ask everyone to write down what would prove the rumor false, check each claim directly against its source, and obtain an independent, uncorrelated statement from the CEO, because the chapter shows people normally test hypotheses by seeking confirming data and a deliberate refutation test is the countermeasure."}]},{"id":"ch13","title":"8. How Judgments Happen","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Basic Assessments, Deliberate Computation, and Intensity Matching","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory console shows three compartments labeled Automatic Basic Assessment, Deliberate System 2 Computation, and Intensity Matching. Six task cards float in, each naming a mental act: evaluating a situation as good or bad, averaging a set of lines, counting capital letters, totaling a set of lines, and matching a sound's loudness to a crime's severity. The player routes each card to the compartment that describes how the mind performs it.","player_mission":"Classify each mental act by how the mind performs it. Use the chapter's distinction between the automatic basic assessments of System 1, the deliberate computations only System 2 can carry out, and the cross-dimensional matching that intensity scales make possible.","key_concepts":["System 1 continuously monitors what is going on outside and inside the mind and generates assessments of various aspects of the situation without specific intention and with little or no effort.","Situations are constantly evaluated as good or bad, requiring escape or permitting approach.","Good mood and cognitive ease are the human equivalents of assessments of safety and familiarity.","A fraction of a second is sufficient for people to register the average length of an array of lines with considerable precision.","A glance provides an immediate impression of many features of a display, but you do not immediately know how many blocks are in a tower; that requires counting.","The only way to answer the total-length question is by activating System 2, which will laboriously estimate the average, estimate or count the lines, and multiply average length by the number of lines.","An underlying scale of intensity permits matching across diverse dimensions, because an underlying dimension of intensity allows the use of the word more.","In classic experiments, people adjusted the loudness of a sound to the severity of crimes, and other people adjusted loudness to the severity of legal punishments.","System 2 receives questions or generates them; in either case it directs attention and searches memory to find the answers."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the difference between the automatic basic assessments of System 1, the deliberate computations that only System 2 can perform, and the cross-dimensional matching that intensity scales make possible. The chapter shows that averages and similarities are computed automatically, sums require effort, and intensity matching lets System 1 translate between seemingly unrelated scales.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"For each mental act, decide which compartment best describes how the mind performs it.","column_a_label":"Automatic Basic Assessment (System 1)","column_b_label":"Deliberate System 2 Computation","column_c_label":"Intensity Matching","rows":[{"label":"Continuously evaluating whether the current situation is good or bad, safe or threatening","col":"a"},{"label":"Registering the average length of an array of lines within a fraction of a second","col":"a"},{"label":"Counting the number of capital letters on a page","col":"b"},{"label":"Estimating the total length of a set of lines by multiplying the average length by the number of lines","col":"b"},{"label":"Matching the loudness of a sound to the severity of a crime so the punishment feels just","col":"c"},{"label":"Judging how tall a man must be to be as remarkable as Julie reading fluently at age four","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["A process that only works for numbers and never for visual scenes","A process that requires conscious awareness to occur at all","A process governed by social norms rather than the individual mind"],"explanation":"Evaluating situations as good or bad and registering the average of a set of lines are automatic System 1 assessments; counting letters and totaling lengths are deliberate, effortful System 2 computations; matching a crime's severity to a sound's loudness and Julie's precocity to a man's height are cross-dimensional intensity matches."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Facial Competence, Substitution, and the Mental Shotgun","visual_frame_description":"A research theater shows three stage lights labeled Facial Competence Assessment, Substitution, and Mental Shotgun. Six finding cards float across the stage: a set of campaign portraits, a competence rating, a voter's decision, a GPA prediction, a rhyme test, and a sentence judgment. The player assigns each finding to the mechanism that best explains it.","player_mission":"Classify each finding by the System 1 mechanism it demonstrates. Read the card, recall the chapter's studies, and decide whether it shows an automatic facial assessment, the substitution of an easier question, or unintended excess computation.","key_concepts":["Alex Todorov showed that a single glance at a stranger's face supports rapid judgments of how dominant and how trustworthy the person is.","The shape of the face provides the cues for assessing dominance, such as a strong square chin, and facial expression provides the cues for assessing the stranger's intentions.","In about 70 percent of the races for senator, congressman, and governor, the election winner was the candidate whose face had earned a higher rating of competence.","Todorov found that people judge competence by combining the two dimensions of strength and trustworthiness.","There is no evidence that these facial features actually predict how well politicians will perform in office.","The essential idea of the heuristics and biases approach is that basic assessments are easily substituted for more difficult questions.","System 1 can translate values across dimensions, as when you answer how tall Sam would be if he were as tall as he is intelligent.","The mental shotgun is the excess computation of System 1: an intention to answer a specific question automatically triggers other computations.","Listeners instructed to compare the sounds of VOTE and GOAT were slowed because they also compared the spelling, a superfluous computation that was detrimental to the main task."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario discriminates three features of System 1 in judgment: the automatic facial assessment of competence, the substitution of an easier question for a harder one, and the mental shotgun's excess computation. The chapter shows that all three operate without intention and influence judgments even when System 2 later makes the decision.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Assign each finding card to the System 1 mechanism that best explains it.","column_a_label":"Facial Competence Assessment","column_b_label":"Substitution (answering an easier question)","column_c_label":"Mental Shotgun (excess computation)","rows":[{"label":"Princeton students rated competence from candidate faces shown for as little as one-tenth of a second and agreed strongly with one another","col":"a"},{"label":"A face combining a strong chin and a slight confident smile is read as competent","col":"a"},{"label":"A voter asked how well a candidate would perform in office instead answers how competent the face looks","col":"b"},{"label":"Asked to predict Julie's college GPA from the age she learned to read, a predictor picks the GPA that matches her precocity","col":"b"},{"label":"Listeners instructed to compare the sounds of VOTE and GOAT are slowed because they also compare the spelling","col":"c"},{"label":"The sentence Some jobs are jails takes longer to reject because it is metaphorically true","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Deliberate reasoning that requires conscious effort in every case","A deliberate strategy that people consciously choose to adopt","A purely social process that requires other people to be present"],"explanation":"Reading competence from a chin and smile and the one-tenth-of-a-second ratings are automatic facial assessments. Answering how the face looks instead of how well the candidate would govern, and matching Julie's GPA to her precocity, are substitutions. The spelling interference in the rhyme test and the metaphor interference in the sentence test are mental-shotgun computations that fired beyond the intended task."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Face of Leadership: Reading Dominance in a Glance","visual_frame_description":"A bright interview suite at a law firm; Maria, a senior HR partner in a tailored blazer, sits opposite candidate Tomas. A colleague holds a file showing Tomas's team-turnover data. On the right, a facial-feature tracker highlights a strong chin and a slight smile, and a transcript panel records Maria's confident pronouncements.","player_mission":"Audit Maria's confident prediction that Tomas will be an outstanding leader. Decide whether her certainty reflects deliberate bias, expert judgment, or the automatic facial competence assessment described by Todorov, and issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Alex Todorov explored the biological roots of the rapid judgments of how safe it is to interact with a stranger.","We are endowed with an ability to evaluate, in a single glance at a stranger's face, how dominant and how trustworthy that person is.","The shape of the face provides the cues for assessing dominance, such as a strong square chin, and facial expression provides the cues for intentions.","Todorov found that people judge competence by combining the two dimensions of strength and trustworthiness.","The faces that exude competence combine a strong chin with a slight confident-appearing smile.","There is no evidence that these facial features actually predict how well politicians will perform in office.","Todorov showed that in about 70 percent of races, the election winner was the candidate whose face earned a higher competence rating.","The accuracy of face reading is far from perfect: round chins are not a reliable indicator of meekness, and smiles can to some extent be faked."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the automatic assessment of competence from faces: Maria reads dominance and trustworthiness in a glance and lets that quick impression override harder evidence. Todorov's research shows the mechanism shapes real decisions such as voting, even though facial features do not predict performance in office.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Maria's confident prediction that Tomas will be an outstanding leader?","subject_baseline":"Maria is a senior HR partner with eighteen years of experience who is known for structured interview rubrics and for demanding behavioral evidence; in previous selection panels she has twice vetoed candidates based on missing reference checks.","observed_nonverbal_log":"10:15 — Within two minutes of meeting Tomas, Maria describes him as a natural leader, leaning forward with a bright smile. 10:20 — Asked what specifically signals leadership, she names his strong jawline, upright posture, and confident slight smile, gesturing to his face. 10:24 — When asked about his management results, she waves dismissively and says you can tell with some people in one glance. 10:28 — No signs of concealment; her confidence is warm and consistent, and she shows genuine surprise that anyone would doubt the read.","interrogation_transcript":"Colleague: What is your assessment of Tomas? Maria: He is the obvious choice. He looks like a leader. Colleague: What in his track record supports that? Maria: You can tell in one glance. Strong features, a calm smile; he projects competence. Colleague: But the data on his prior team shows high turnover. Maria: That will not matter. Some people simply look the part. I have always been able to read faces.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deliberate deception — Maria is relying on an automatic facial competence assessment (Todorov): she is reading dominance and trustworthiness from his face and substituting that quick impression for the harder judgment of leadership performance.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Todorov's studies show people evaluate dominance and trustworthiness in a single glance and combine them into a competence judgment, and this ancient mechanism influences even elections, yet there is no evidence that the facial features predict how well anyone performs in office. Maria's flat dismissal of track-record evidence, her appeal to looking the part, and the complete absence of deception cues mark this as a genuinely held System 1 assessment rather than a lie.","distractors":["Deliberate bias to favor a candidate she already knows","Accurate expert judgment from two decades of leadership hiring","A moral judgment about Tomas's integrity"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Fixed Donation: A Prototype That Ignores Quantity","visual_frame_description":"A bright development office at a coastal wildlife nonprofit; Raymond, the development director, presents a bar chart to his colleagues. Three bars labeled 2,000 birds, 20,000 birds, and 200,000 birds all reach nearly the same height near eighty dollars. A video thumbnail of a struggling bird plays in a corner of the screen, and a transcript panel records Raymond's confident explanation.","player_mission":"Audit Raymond's claim that donor giving will not change when the number of birds saved changes. Decide whether he is manipulating numbers, describing a real marketing effect, or presenting the chapter's prototype effect on sum-like variables, and issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Participants asked their willingness to pay to save 2,000, 20,000, or 200,000 birds gave average contributions of 80, 78, and 88 dollars respectively.","If saving birds is an economic good it should be a sum-like variable, and saving 200,000 birds should be worth much more than saving 2,000.","The number of birds made very little difference in the Exxon Valdez studies.","What the participants reacted to, in all three groups, was a prototype: the awful image of a helpless bird drowning, its feathers soaked in thick oil.","Because System 1 represents categories by a prototype or a set of typical exemplars, it deals well with averages but poorly with sums.","The size of the category, the number of instances it contains, tends to be ignored in judgments of sum-like variables.","The almost complete neglect of quantity in such emotional contexts has been confirmed many times.","System 1 has no suggestions to offer for the total length of a set of lines, and only System 2 can laboriously compute a sum."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the neglect of quantity in sum-like variables: willingness to pay did not grow from 2,000 to 200,000 birds saved because System 1 represents the category by a prototype, and the awful image of one drowning bird dominates the math. The chapter reports that the average contributions were 80, 78, and 88 dollars in the three groups.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Raymond's confidence that donor giving will stay flat no matter how many birds are saved?","subject_baseline":"Raymond is a seasoned development director at a coastal wildlife nonprofit who runs quarterly giving analyses and is known for preaching that donors respond to impact numbers; his reports are normally full of precise figures.","observed_nonverbal_log":"13:05 — Raymond presents the mock giving data with pride, noting that 80, 78, and 88 dollars are nearly identical across the three scenarios. 13:09 — Asked whether a donor should pay more to save 200,000 birds than 2,000, he smiles and says people give to the picture, not the math. 13:12 — When pressed, he shrugs, cites the focus-group video of a struggling bird, and cannot articulate any mechanism besides the image. 13:15 — No deception cues; he is pleased, relaxed, and treats the result as a marketing insight rather than a problem.","interrogation_transcript":"Colleague: What did the giving test show? Raymond: We showed donors three versions: save 2,000, 20,000, or 200,000 birds. The pledges were basically the same, about 80 dollars either way. Colleague: Should not 200,000 birds be worth a hundred times more than 2,000? Raymond: In theory, but people respond to one image: a helpless bird, feathers soaked in oil. That image is the story. Colleague: Are you saying the count is irrelevant to giving? Raymond: Yes. The bigger the number, the more it is just a number. The bird is what moves people.","correct_diagnosis":"Not deception — Raymond's donors are showing neglect of quantity in a sum-like variable: their willingness to pay was driven by the prototype of a single suffering bird, not by the number of birds saved, exactly as in the Exxon Valdez studies.","diagnostic_breakdown":"In the chapter's example, contributions were 80, 78, and 88 dollars regardless of whether 2,000, 20,000, or 200,000 birds would be saved, because participants reacted to the prototype of a helpless drowning bird. Raymond's focus-group insight that the image is the story is a faithful restatement of that mechanism, and his open, pleased demeanor with no deception cues indicates genuine belief rather than manipulation.","distractors":["Deliberate fraud to inflate projected donations","A proven marketing strategy based on price anchoring","Donor apathy that has nothing to do with how the ask is framed"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Julie Problem (Prediction by Matching)","visual_frame_description":"In a university admissions office, three counselors review a file containing a single fact: Julie read fluently when she was four years old. Asked to predict her college GPA, they quickly agree on a near-perfect score because such early reading is so remarkable. A note on the wall reminds them that last year's early readers performed at or below average on the college measure.","player_mission":"Stop the office from converting a striking early achievement into an extreme prediction. Explain why prediction by matching is statistically wrong, then install a prediction process that replaces scale-matching with a sound statistical approach.","key_concepts":["System 1 continuously generates basic assessments without specific intention, and these assessments are easily substituted for more difficult questions.","An underlying scale of intensity permits matching across diverse dimensions, allowing the use of the word more, as in more happy or more severe.","If crimes were colors, murder would be a deeper shade of red than theft, and if crimes were music, mass murder would be played fortissimo while parking tickets would be pianissimo.","In classic experiments, people adjusted the loudness of a sound to the severity of crimes, and other people adjusted loudness to the severity of punishments.","Julie read fluently when she was four years old, and you can match her precocity to a height, an income, a crime, or a GPA.","When people are asked to predict Julie's GPA from the age at which she learned to read, they answer by translating from one scale to another and pick the matching GPA.","This mode of prediction by matching is statistically wrong, although it is perfectly natural to System 1.","For most people except statisticians, prediction by matching is also acceptable to System 2.","System 2 receives questions or generates them, and in either case it directs attention and searches memory to find the answers."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The battle tests prediction by intensity matching: people answer the hard question of Julie's future GPA by translating her precocity into a matching value on another scale. Kahneman shows this feels natural to System 1 yet is statistically wrong, because matching ignores the imperfect relationship between the predictor and the outcome, and only statistical training makes System 2 reject it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is prediction by matching: when asked to predict a quantity such as Julie's college GPA from an early achievement such as reading at age four, people translate across dimensions and pick the value that matches in intensity, a mode that is natural to System 1 but statistically wrong because it ignores the imperfect relationship between the two variables.","stage_1_observation":"In a university admissions office, three counselors review a file containing a single fact: Julie read fluently when she was four years old. Asked to predict her college GPA, they quickly agree on a near-perfect score because such early reading is so remarkable. A note on the wall reminds them that last year's early readers performed at or below average on the college measure.","stage_1_question":"Why is the counselors' confident matching prediction statistically wrong even though it feels perfectly natural?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The prediction was produced by translating across intensity scales, matching how remarkable the reading was to an equally remarkable GPA, but the chapter states that this mode of prediction by matching is statistically wrong: the translation ignores that the relationship between the two measures is imperfect, so an extreme value on one scale should not imply an equally extreme value on the other.","stage_2_intervention":"The admissions director asks you to fix the prediction process before the decisions are sent. Choose the intervention that replaces matching with a sound statistical approach.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use the actual historical relationship between early reading age and college GPA to produce the prediction, shrink extreme predictions toward the group average, and report a range rather than a single matched value, because the chapter shows matching is natural to System 1 but statistically wrong, and it is acceptable to System 2 only for people who are not trained to think statistically."}]},{"id":"ch14","title":"9. Answering an Easier Question","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Substitution in Charitable Giving Decisions","visual_frame_description":"Nina, a sustainability officer, watches a board member, Ray, fill out a pledge form for a wildlife campaign. On the left of the screen the target question is printed in bold: \"How much should the firm contribute to save an endangered species?\" On the right, a thought bubble shows Ray's face flicker with emotion as images of dying dolphins play, while a slider labeled \"dollars pledged\" moves under his hand.","player_mission":"Identify which substituted question Ray actually answers and sort each scenario into the correct component of the substitution mechanism: target question, heuristic question, or intensity matching. Assign every row to the column it illustrates before Ray finalizes the pledge.","key_concepts":["Substitution occurs when System 1 cannot quickly answer a hard target question, so it answers a related, easier heuristic question instead.","The target question is the assessment the person intends to produce, while the heuristic question is the simpler one actually answered.","The mental shotgun means System 1 computes more than the task intends, automatically generating related assessments without being asked.","Intensity matching maps the strength of a feeling onto another scale, such as converting felt concern into a dollar contribution.","In the book's table, \"How much would you contribute to save an endangered species?\" is paired with the heuristic \"How much emotion do I feel when I think of dying dolphins?\"","The heuristic answer must be fitted to the original question, and intensity matching supplies the mapping between the two scales.","Pólya's heuristics are deliberate System 2 strategies, but the heuristics in this chapter arise automatically from the mental shotgun.","A lazy System 2 often endorses the heuristic answer without much scrutiny of whether it truly answers the target question.","People can be influenced by the answer to a question they were not asked while remaining unaware that they substituted.","Substituting one question for another is a general account of how people generate intuitive opinions on complex matters."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests substitution: when System 1 cannot solve a hard target question, it answers an easier heuristic question and maps the answer onto the original scale via intensity matching. Kahneman reports that the heuristic questions paired with difficult targets are very likely to be evoked and easily answered, and a lazy System 2 typically endorses the substitute.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Ray is asked how much his firm should contribute to save an endangered species. According to the substitution account, which substituted question does he most likely answer instead?","column_a_label":"Target question (the intended assessment)","column_b_label":"Heuristic question (the easier substitute)","column_c_label":"Intensity matching (mapping feeling onto the target scale)","rows":[{"label":"How much emotion do I feel when I think of dying dolphins?","col":"b"},{"label":"How much should the firm contribute to save an endangered species?","col":"a"},{"label":"Converting a level of concern for seabirds into a dollar pledge amount","col":"c"},{"label":"Using how popular the president is right now to answer how popular he will be in six months","col":"b"},{"label":"Answering \"How much anger do I feel thinking of financial predators who prey on the elderly?\" in place of a sentence recommendation","col":"b"},{"label":"Feeling that a candidate looks like a winner and concluding she will go far in the primary","col":"b"},{"label":"Translating how impressed Ray is by a presentation into a budget number for next quarter","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Ray runs a full cost-benefit analysis, weighing every alternative before pledging","Ray waits to pledge until his colleagues reach a consensus on the donation amount","Ray refuses to give any amount until he receives complete data on the species' population"],"explanation":"Ray cannot answer the difficult target question about contribution size, so System 1 answers the readymade heuristic question of how much emotion the dolphins arouse, and intensity matching converts that feeling into dollars. Rows describing an intended assessment, an easier substitute, or a cross-scale mapping sort into the three components, while the distractors describe effortful or social reasoning the substitution account does not rely on."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Affect Heuristic, Mood Heuristic, and WYSIATI Compared","visual_frame_description":"Three panels hang on one wall. Panel one shows a voter, Dana, nodding at a health-policy poster she likes, with a risk-o-meter set to low risk and a benefit bar set to high. Panel two shows two student survey forms: one asks \"How many dates did you have last month?\" directly above \"How happy are you these days?\" Panel three shows a lone eye hovering over a newspaper headline with the letters WYSIATI and blank space where missing evidence would be.","player_mission":"Decide which mechanism each scenario illustrates by assigning every row to the affect heuristic, the mood heuristic for happiness, or WYSIATI. Then identify which mechanism best explains a voter's risk beliefs.","key_concepts":["The affect heuristic, proposed by Paul Slovic, lets people's likes and dislikes determine their beliefs about the world.","If you like the current health policy, you believe its benefits are substantial and its costs more manageable than those of alternatives.","A person who dislikes a technology such as nuclear power tends to believe its risks are high and its benefits negligible.","In the German student survey, asking about dates before happiness made reported happiness strongly correlate with dating, although the two were unrelated when happiness was asked first.","The mood heuristic substitutes the present emotional state for an assessment of global life satisfaction.","WYSIATI (what you see is all there is) means System 1 focuses on existing evidence and ignores absent evidence.","Any emotionally significant question that alters a person's mood will affect happiness reports; the present state of mind looms very large.","In the context of attitudes, System 2 acts more as an apologist for the emotions of System 1 than as a critic.","System 2's search for information is mostly constrained to information consistent with existing beliefs.","The dominance of conclusions over arguments is most pronounced where emotions are involved."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario contrasts three System 1 shortcuts: Slovic's affect heuristic, in which liking drives beliefs about benefits and risks; the mood heuristic, in which the present emotional state is substituted for global happiness; and WYSIATI, the focus on available evidence. Kahneman reports that in the German student survey the correlation between dating and happiness jumped to near its maximum only when the dating question came first.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dana likes a new health policy. Without seeing any data on its costs, she concludes it is low-risk and high-benefit. Which mechanism best explains her beliefs?","column_a_label":"Affect heuristic (likes and dislikes drive beliefs)","column_b_label":"Mood heuristic for happiness (present state stands in for a global assessment)","column_c_label":"WYSIATI (existing evidence in, absent evidence ignored)","rows":[{"label":"A dove in foreign policy believes other nations are strong and will not be easily coerced","col":"a"},{"label":"A student who just reported many dates rates general life happiness as high","col":"b"},{"label":"A person who dislikes irradiated food believes its risks are high and benefits negligible","col":"a"},{"label":"A student who just reported no dates rates general happiness low because loneliness is on her mind","col":"b"},{"label":"Judging a candidate's fitness from a confident face without seeking a voting record","col":"c"},{"label":"Believing the message of a poll while ignoring the sample size printed beneath it","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Dana is deliberately concealing her true opinion to please her party","Dana is judging her own life satisfaction by her current mood","Dana can only use the information in front of her and cannot imagine what is missing"],"explanation":"Dana's conclusion flows from her liking of the policy, the defining move of the affect heuristic; the book notes that information about lower risks would also improve her view of benefits. Rows about dating-primed happiness illustrate the mood heuristic, rows about judging from available cues illustrate WYSIATI, and the distractors describe deception, global mood, or pure availability that the scenario does not show."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The 3-D Size Illusion in a Packaging Review","visual_frame_description":"An auditor's desk is stacked with printed carton mockups. Priya, a senior packaging designer at Meridian Foods, points at a new label where two identical cartoon figures stand in a perspective corridor, while a ruler lies on the desk. A timestamped video log of the review meeting plays in a corner window.","player_mission":"Audit whether Priya's claim that the right-hand figure is larger is a deliberate deception, a misunderstanding, or an automatic perceptual illusion. Compare her baseline behavior against the observed nonverbal log and the interrogation transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["In the 3-D figure, the man on the right looks larger although both figures are exactly the same size.","The perspective corridor automatically triggers a three-dimensional interpretation of the flat picture.","In the 3-D interpretation the person on the right appears both farther away and larger, so the impression of 3-D size dictates the judgment of 2-D size.","The substitution is a true perceptual illusion, not a misunderstanding of the question being asked.","When asked to estimate the size of the figures, experimental participants answer in inches, not feet, showing they understood the question.","The essential step, substituting three-dimensional for two-dimensional size, occurs automatically and cannot be overridden.","Objects that appear more distant also appear larger on the page, a predictable bias of the 3-D heuristic.","Only visual artists and experienced photographers develop the skill of seeing the drawing as an object on the page.","A judgment that is based on substitution will inevitably be biased in predictable ways.","The 3-D heuristic is a clear illustration of being influenced by the answer to a question you were not asked."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the 3-D heuristic, in which the perceptual system automatically substitutes three-dimensional for two-dimensional size, making the farther figure appear larger even when both are equal. Kahneman shows the illusion is a true automatic perception, because participants who are asked to estimate size respond in inches rather than feet and still cannot correct their impression.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya insists the right-hand figure in the printed carton mockup is larger and objects even when the ruler proves otherwise. What is the correct diagnosis of her claim?","subject_baseline":"Priya has fifteen years of packaging design experience and is known in the studio for meticulous attention to line weight and spacing; she has never previously been accused of misstating a specification in a review.","observed_nonverbal_log":"13:02 Priya points at the mockup and answers immediately, before the ruler is mentioned, with a steady voice. 13:05 Asked to confirm, she answers without stammering and maintains eye contact, with no sign of pupil dilation. 13:11 She frowns with genuine surprise as the ruler is laid across both figures. 13:14 She leans in, rechecks the measurement, and says \"That can't be right\" with the calm bewilderment of someone shown a magic trick, not the tension of someone caught.","interrogation_transcript":"Auditor: \"Priya, can you confirm the right-hand figure is larger than the left?\" Priya: \"Yes, obviously. It's clearly bigger.\" Auditor: \"Even with a ruler across both of them, same size?\" Priya: \"It really looks bigger to me. I know the ruler says otherwise, but my eyes keep telling me it's larger.\"","correct_diagnosis":"Priya is telling the truth as she perceives it: she is experiencing a genuine automatic 3-D illusion, not lying about the mockup.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Priya's instant, unguarded answer and her sincere surprise at the ruler match the automatic, involuntary nature of the 3-D heuristic rather than the physiology of deliberate deception. Her comprehension is intact, because she answers about size on the page rather than about the people's height, and the perspective cue that misleads her is irrelevant to the task yet impossible to ignore, exactly as the book describes.","distractors":["Priya is deliberately hiding a production error she made in the artwork","Priya is anxious and guessing because she never checked the mockup","Priya misunderstood the question and answered about the height of the people instead of the size of the figures"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Affect Heuristic in Executive Risk Assessment","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom at Northbridge Capital. Marcus, the CFO, presents a risk-benefit chart for a bioreactor venture he championed: the benefit bar is tall and the risk bar is tiny. The chief auditor, Elena, watches a live feed with a risk matrix on the wall and timestamped footage of Marcus pitching the same venture to the investment committee.","player_mission":"Determine whether Marcus is deceiving the board about the venture's risk or sincerely believing his own optimistic assessment. Compare his baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["The affect heuristic, proposed by Paul Slovic, holds that people let their likes and dislikes determine their beliefs about the world.","If a person likes an activity, they tend to believe its benefits are high and its risks low.","If a person dislikes something such as irradiated food or nuclear power, they tend to believe its risks are high and benefits negligible.","Information that lowers perceived risk will also improve perceived benefits, even when nothing is said about benefits.","In the context of attitudes, System 2 is more of an apologist for the emotions of System 1 than a critic.","System 2's search for information and arguments is mostly constrained to information consistent with existing beliefs.","An active, coherence-seeking System 1 suggests solutions to an undemanding System 2.","The dominance of conclusions over arguments is most pronounced where emotions are involved.","Beliefs and even emotional attitudes may change, at least a little, when risk information improves.","The affect heuristic explains why people's estimates of benefits and risks for a technology are strongly correlated in the wrong direction."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests Slovic's affect heuristic: Marcus's liking of the venture drives his beliefs about its benefits and risks, so his estimates are emotionally coherent rather than evidence-based. Kahneman emphasizes that in the context of attitudes System 2 acts as an apologist for System 1's emotions, endorsing rather than criticizing the conclusions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus's risk rating for the bioreactor venture is nearly zero while his benefit rating is maximal, and he pushes the board to approve it. What is the correct diagnosis of his assessment?","subject_baseline":"Marcus has run four venture reviews this year with disciplined, numbers-first presentations; colleagues describe him as cautious with capital, and he routinely demands external audits before committing funds.","observed_nonverbal_log":"14:20 Marcus opens the risk slide and talks faster with rising energy; his pupils are slightly dilated, consistent with excitement rather than strain. 14:26 When Elena asks for the downside scenario, he waves it away and pivots to the upside case, showing no averted gaze or lip compression. 14:31 He cites the benefit data fluently but struggles to recall any cost figure he prepared. 14:37 Asked directly whether he has any doubts, he answers instantly, \"Not really,\" with relaxed posture.","interrogation_transcript":"Elena: \"What is your worst-case estimate for this venture?\" Marcus: \"Honestly, the downside here is negligible. The upside is what matters.\" Elena: \"Do you have the independent cost assessment we asked for?\" Marcus: \"It's on the server. I haven't looked at it closely. I know in my gut this one is right.\"","correct_diagnosis":"Marcus is not deliberately deceiving the board; his polarized risk and benefit judgments are genuine outputs of the affect heuristic, with System 2 endorsing rather than auditing his emotion-driven conclusions.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Marcus's sincerity is supported by relaxed, non-defensive body language and instant answers, while his pattern matches the affect heuristic exactly: liking the venture inflates benefits and collapses risks, information that would lower risk would also raise benefits, and his System 2 searches for confirmatory evidence rather than examining the belief. The verdict is emotion-driven judgment, not fraud.","distractors":["Marcus is lying to secure his own bonus","Marcus is anchoring on a figure from an earlier deal and adjusting insufficiently","Marcus holds full evidence and made a rational, well-calculated trade-off"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Mood Heuristic in Survey Design","visual_frame_description":"A policy research office. The research director's desk holds a printed draft survey in which the item \"How happy are you with your life these days?\" sits directly beneath \"How many dates did you have last month?\" On a monitor, two pilot respondents complete the form; both pause at the dating item, smile or frown, and then answer the happiness item within seconds.","player_mission":"Catch the survey-ordering flaw before the client ships the report, explain what it will do to the data, and force the correct redesign when the client resists.","key_concepts":["In the German student survey, the correlation between dating and happiness was about zero when happiness was asked first.","When the dating question preceded the happiness question, the correlation was about as high as correlations between psychological measures can get.","The explanation is substitution: the dating question arouses an emotional reaction that is still active when the happiness question is asked.","Students with many dates were reminded of a happy aspect of life, while those with none were reminded of loneliness and rejection.","Respondents substituted the question they had a readymade answer to, happiness with their love life, for the question they were asked.","The students were not confused about the distinction between romantic life and life as a whole.","The same pattern appears if a question about relations with parents or about finances precedes the happiness question.","Any emotionally significant question that alters a person's mood will affect happiness reports.","The present state of mind looms very large when people evaluate their happiness.","WYSIATI applies: the present state of mind is the evidence that dominates the assessment."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the mood heuristic: a preceding, emotionally significant question alters mood and is substituted for the target assessment of global happiness, so question order manufactures a correlation. Kahneman reports the correlation between dating and happiness was near zero when happiness was asked first but near the maximum possible when the dating question preceded it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The mood heuristic for happiness: when people are asked to assess global life happiness, an emotionally significant preceding question can substitute a mood-derived answer for a considered one. In the German student survey, happiness and dating were uncorrelated when happiness came first, but the correlation jumped dramatically when the dating question preceded it.","stage_1_observation":"You are the research director at a policy firm. A junior analyst, Priyanka, has drafted a citizen survey for a quality-of-life report. Question 4 asks \"How happy are you with your life these days?\" and Question 5, printed directly above it, asks \"How many dates did you have last month?\" On screen, you watch two pilot respondents complete the form; both pause at the dating item, smile or frown, and then answer the happiness item in seconds.","stage_1_question":"What is the flaw in this questionnaire order, and what outcome will it produce in the data?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The order invites substitution: the dating item arouses an emotional reaction that is still active when the happiness question is asked, so respondents answer about their romantic life rather than their life as a whole. The result is a spurious positive correlation between dating frequency and reported happiness that would not appear if happiness were asked first.","stage_2_intervention":"The client has already collected 2,000 responses using this ordering and insists the finding that \"romance drives happiness\" is real and should headline the report. You must decide what to do before the report ships.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Push back on the headline and demand a re-run: move the happiness item to the start of the survey, or separate the domain questions with neutral filler items, so the mood from the dating question does not color global happiness reports. Explain that the observed correlation is an artifact of question order, not evidence about happiness, citing the German student survey in which the same two items produced opposite results depending on order."}]},{"id":"ch15","title":"10. The Law of Small Numbers","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Statistical Artifacts vs. Causal Stories in County Data","visual_frame_description":"A wall map of the 3,141 U.S. counties glows with kidney-cancer rates: the highest and lowest counties are both rural and sparsely populated. Beside the map, an urn demonstration shows Jack drawing 4 marbles while Jill draws 7, and a statistician's notepad contrasts \"clean living\" explanations with a sample-size explanation.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence row into the correct column, distinguishing sampling artifacts from causal stories and from sample-size neglect. Then answer which account correctly explains why rural counties appear at both extremes of the cancer map.","key_concepts":["Both the highest- and the lowest-kidney-cancer counties in the United States are mostly rural, sparsely populated, and in traditionally Republican states.","The rural lifestyle cannot explain both very high and very low cancer incidence in the same kind of county.","The key factor is population size: rural counties have small populations, and extreme outcomes are more likely in small samples.","A random event does not lend itself to explanation, but collections of random events behave in a highly regular fashion.","From an urn of half red and half white marbles, the outcome 2 red and 2 white occurs about 6 times as often as 4 red or 4 white.","Samples of 4 marbles yield extreme homogeneous outcomes about 8 times more often than samples of 7 marbles (12.5% versus 1.56%).","The apparent county differences are artifacts produced by differences in sample size, not real differences in cancer rates.","If the analysis is repeated next year, the counties with high incidence last year will not necessarily be high again.","Large samples are more precise than small samples, which is the same statement as small samples yielding extreme results more often.","Even sophisticated researchers have poor intuitions and a wobbly understanding of sampling effects."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the law of small numbers: extreme outcomes appear more often in small samples, so sparsely populated counties dominate both the highest and lowest cancer rates with no causal meaning. Kahneman, citing Wainer and Zwerling, notes the rural lifestyle cannot explain both very high and very low incidence, so the differences are sampling artifacts.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"The counties with the lowest kidney-cancer rates and the counties with the highest rates are both mostly rural, sparsely populated, and in Republican states. What is the correct account of this pattern?","column_a_label":"Statistical artifact (sampling variation)","column_b_label":"Causal story (attributing a mechanism)","column_c_label":"Sample-size neglect (acting as if small samples are representative)","rows":[{"label":"Concluding that rural clean living causes low kidney-cancer rates","col":"b"},{"label":"Concluding that rural poverty and poor medical care cause high kidney-cancer rates","col":"b"},{"label":"Extreme outcomes appearing more often in sparsely populated counties than in dense ones","col":"a"},{"label":"Jack's 4-marble samples showing all-red or all-white outcomes 8 times more often than Jill's 7-marble samples","col":"a"},{"label":"A researcher running an experiment on 20 subjects by tradition instead of computing the needed sample","col":"c"},{"label":"Treating a telephone poll of 300 as if it were as precise as a poll of 3,000","col":"c"},{"label":"Concluding small schools are better because 6 of the top 50 schools in a 1,662-school survey were small","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Rural lifestyle is genuinely protective and rural poverty genuinely harmful, both at once","Republican governance directly reduces cancer incidence in small counties","Small counties report health data more accurately than dense urban counties"],"explanation":"The identical pattern at both extremes cannot be produced by any single causal factor; it is the sampling artifact that small samples yield extreme results more often, as the Jack-and-Jill marble demonstration shows. Rows that attach a mechanism to the rates illustrate causal stories, rows that ignore the trustworthiness of small samples illustrate sample-size neglect, and the distractors offer causal or data-quality explanations the statistical account rejects."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Seeing Order in Randomness: Hot Hands and Clustered Hits","visual_frame_description":"A basketball arena scoreboard shows a player's shot sequence with a run of makes, while a shot-chart analyst pulls up thousands of logged sequences that all pass randomness tests. Alongside it, a map of World War II London shows V-2 rocket hits with conspicuous gaps that turned out to be typical of a random distribution.","player_mission":"Sort each row into the correct column, separating genuine statistical processes from misperceived patterns and from the law of large numbers. Then answer what the shot-sequence analysis reveals about the hot hand.","key_concepts":["Gilovich, Vallone, and Tversky analyzed thousands of shot sequences and found no such thing as a hot hand in professional basketball.","Shot sequences from the field and the foul line satisfy all tests of randomness.","Some players are more accurate than others, but success does not cluster the way intuition suggests.","The inference that a player sinking three or four baskets in a row is hot is described as irresistible.","Teammates adapt by passing more to the hot scorer and the defense adapts by double-teaming.","The hot hand is entirely in the eye of the beholders, who are consistently too quick to perceive order and causality in randomness.","During the intensive rocket bombing of London, map gaps led some to suspect German spies, but the distribution was typical of a random process.","Random processes produce many sequences that convince people the process is not random after all.","We are pattern seekers who do not expect to see regularity produced by a random process.","If you follow your intuition, you will more often than not misclassify a random event as systematic."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the misperception of randomness: people impose causal stories on random sequences, from basketball hot hands to clustered wartime bomb hits. Kahneman reports that thousands of analyzed shot sequences satisfy all tests of randomness and that the London bombing map was typical of a random process despite looking clustered.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After a player sinks four shots in a row, teammates pass to him more and the defense double-teams him. What does the Gilovich, Vallone, and Tversky analysis of thousands of shot sequences show?","column_a_label":"Random statistical process (no cause to find)","column_b_label":"Pattern misperception (seeing order where none exists)","column_c_label":"Law of large numbers (precision grows with sample size)","rows":[{"label":"Judging the sequence BGBBGB more likely than BBBGGG or GGGGGG even though all six-birth sequences are equally probable","col":"b"},{"label":"Suspected German spies in unharmed districts because gaps in the London bombing map looked nonrandom","col":"b"},{"label":"The conclusion that professional basketball shooting sequences satisfy all tests of randomness","col":"a"},{"label":"The Israeli Air Force investigating why one squadron lost four planes while another from the same base lost none","col":"b"},{"label":"Repeated sampling from an urn producing a predictable 6-to-1 ratio of balanced to extreme outcomes","col":"a"},{"label":"A large-sample study reliably confirming that six-year-old girls have larger vocabularies than boys","col":"c"},{"label":"Expecting a fair coin's sample proportion to settle near 50% as trials accumulate","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The shooter genuinely becomes more accurate after making baskets","The defense's double-teaming is proven to cause the next miss","Hot streaks can be confirmed by watching a single game closely"],"explanation":"The analysis found no such thing as a hot hand: shot sequences from the field and the foul line satisfy all tests of randomness, so the hot shooter is a pattern misperception by players, coaches, and fans. Rows about births, bomb maps, and squadron losses are further causal attributions to random variation, while rows about urn sampling and large samples illustrate statistical regularity and the precision of large samples."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Certainty Over Doubt, WYSIATI, and the Halo Effect","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom slide shows a new CEO next to a column of three successful acquisitions, with a question mark where the sample size should be. Beside it, a news ticker reads \"Telephone poll of 300 seniors: 60% support the president\" and a colleague's face is overlaid with the word \"librarian\" after a one-minute introduction.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence row into the correct column, distinguishing the bias toward certainty over doubt, WYSIATI, and the halo effect. Then identify which mechanism most directly drives the board's conclusion about the CEO.","key_concepts":["The law of small numbers is a manifestation of a general bias that favors certainty over doubt.","System 1 is not prone to doubt; it suppresses ambiguity and spontaneously constructs stories that are as coherent as possible.","System 2 is capable of doubt because it can maintain incompatible possibilities at the same time.","Sustaining doubt is harder work than sliding into certainty.","Unless a message is immediately negated, the associations it evokes spread as if the message were true.","People pay more attention to the content of messages than to information about their reliability.","Can your System 1 distinguish degrees of belief? The principle of WYSIATI suggests that it cannot.","The exaggerated faith in what can be learned from a few observations is closely related to the halo effect.","The halo effect is the sense we often get that we know and understand a person about whom we actually know very little.","System 1 runs ahead of the facts in constructing a rich image on the basis of scraps of evidence."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the general bias favoring certainty over doubt, of which the law of small numbers is one manifestation: people treat a few observations as representative and slide into certainty because sustaining doubt is harder work. Kahneman links this to WYSIATI, which suggests System 1 cannot distinguish degrees of belief, and to the halo effect, the sense of knowing a person from very little evidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A board concludes a new CEO has extraordinary acquisition flair after three successful deals. Which mechanism is most directly responsible for this conclusion?","column_a_label":"Confidence over doubt (believing small samples resemble the population)","column_b_label":"WYSIATI (ignoring reliability information in messages)","column_c_label":"Halo effect (knowing a person from a few observations)","rows":[{"label":"Research psychologists choosing samples that expose them to a 50% risk of failing to confirm true hypotheses","col":"a"},{"label":"Summarizing \"a telephone poll of 300 seniors, 60% support the president\" as \"elderly support president\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Feeling you know and understand a person about whom you actually know very little","col":"c"},{"label":"System 1 suppressing ambiguity and constructing the most coherent story while sustaining doubt remains harder work","col":"a"},{"label":"Treating a watercooler claim with the same belief as a report in The New York Times","col":"b"},{"label":"Judging a new manager's competence after watching a single meeting","col":"c"},{"label":"Believing the law of large numbers applies to small numbers as well","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The CEO's success was guaranteed by favorable market conditions","Three deals are a statistically sufficient sample to establish skill","The board weighed the base rate of acquisition success correctly"],"explanation":"The conclusion rests on the law of small numbers, a manifestation of the general bias that favors certainty over doubt: three successes are treated as if they represented the population, and the board never asks how many deals would be needed to distinguish skill from luck. Rows about ignoring sample-size information in polls illustrate WYSIATI, rows about knowing people from scraps illustrate the halo effect, and the distractors assert the statistical rigor the scenario lacks."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Scout's Hot-Hand Claim","visual_frame_description":"A gymnasium press box. Marcus, a veteran NBA scout for the Valley Hawks, points at a player who has just made four straight three-pointers. On the wall, a play-by-play chart shows the shooter's full sequence of makes and misses, and a timestamped audio log of the scouting call plays in the corner.","player_mission":"Determine whether Marcus is deliberately inflating the player's value, sincerely misreading randomness, or correctly identifying a real streak. Compare his baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Gilovich, Vallone, and Tversky analyzed thousands of shot sequences and found no such thing as a hot hand in professional basketball.","Shot sequences from the field and the foul line satisfy all tests of randomness.","Some players are more accurate than others, but success does not cluster the way intuition suggests.","The inference that a player sinking three or four baskets in a row is hot is described as irresistible.","Teammates adapt by passing more to the hot scorer and the defense adapts by double-teaming.","The hot hand is entirely in the eye of the beholders, who are consistently too quick to perceive order and causality in randomness.","The public response to the finding was disbelief; the celebrated coach Red Auerbach dismissed the study.","Random processes produce many sequences that convince people the process is not random.","We are pattern seekers, believers in a coherent world, who do not expect regularity to be produced by chance.","If you follow your intuition, you will more often than not misclassify a random event as systematic."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the hot-hand illusion, the systematic misperception that random shot sequences reveal a causal streak. Kahneman reports that thousands of analyzed sequences satisfy all tests of randomness, making the hot hand a massive and widespread cognitive illusion in the eye of the beholder.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus recommends giving the shooter every shot because \"he's hot.\" What is the correct diagnosis of his recommendation?","subject_baseline":"Marcus has scouted for twelve years, keeps detailed shot charts, and has a reputation for honest, unglamorous reporting; he has never been suspected of favoring a client or inflating a player's stock.","observed_nonverbal_log":"18:05 Marcus sees the fourth make and immediately straightens in his seat, his voice rising as he points with excitement. 18:11 He recounts the streak accurately from memory, checking his chart, and lists each shot location without hesitation. 18:18 When reminded that the same player missed five in a row last quarter, he waves dismissively and says that was before he got hot. 18:24 Asked whether he has any doubt, he answers firmly with no averted gaze or defensive tension.","interrogation_transcript":"Auditor: \"Do you believe this player is genuinely more likely to score right now?\" Marcus: \"Absolutely. Four in a row, he's locked in. You ride the hot hand.\" Auditor: \"But he missed five straight earlier.\" Marcus: \"Different story. That was before the switch flipped.\"","correct_diagnosis":"Marcus is sincere but wrong: he is a victim of the hot-hand illusion, misperceiving a random sequence as a causal streak rather than deliberately inflating the player's value.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Marcus's fluent recall, consistent enthusiasm, and quick, non-defensive answers indicate genuine belief, while his dismissal of the earlier misses reveals the pattern-seeking behavior the chapter describes: he reads order and causation into a sequence that satisfies all tests of randomness. The accurate shot chart he keeps rules out fabrication, so the verdict is a cognitive illusion, not deception.","distractors":["Marcus is lying to boost the player's trade value","Marcus has correctly detected a real improvement in shooting mechanics","Marcus is under pressure from ownership to produce a star"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing the Small-Schools Finding","visual_frame_description":"A foundation boardroom at the Crestwood Education Trust. Lena, the education program officer, presents a slide titled \"The Most Successful Schools Are Small,\" citing 6 of the top 50 schools in a 1,662-school Pennsylvania survey. In the corner, a data window shows the same survey's bottom 50 schools, which are also mostly small, and a timestamped video of the board presentation plays.","player_mission":"Determine whether Lena is concealing data that contradicts the small-schools conclusion, made an honest statistical error, or found a real effect. Audit her baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["The Gates Foundation made a substantial investment in the creation of small schools after researchers found that the most successful schools tend to be small.","In a survey of 1,662 schools in Pennsylvania, 6 of the top 50 were small, an overrepresentation by a factor of 4.","If the statisticians had asked about the characteristics of the worst schools, they would have found that bad schools also tend to be smaller than average.","Small schools are not better on average; they are simply more variable.","If anything, large schools tend to produce better results, especially in higher grades where a variety of curricular options is valuable.","A causal story that small schools give students more personal attention is easy to construct but pointless because the facts are wrong.","The Annenberg Foundation, the Pew Charitable Trust, and the U.S. Department of Education also joined the small-schools effort.","The finding is an artifact: extreme outcomes appear more often in small samples.","We are far too willing to reject the belief that much of what we see in life is random.","Causal explanations of chance events are inevitably wrong."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the sample-size artifact underlying the small-schools finding: the most successful schools and the worst schools are both smaller than average because small samples produce extreme outcomes more often. Kahneman reports that asking about the worst schools would have exposed the same small pattern, showing the effect is variability, not quality.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Lena recommends splitting large schools into small units because \"the best schools are small.\" What is the correct diagnosis of her recommendation?","subject_baseline":"Lena is a former teacher with a doctorate in education policy; she is known for reading every brief and has twice returned grants because the evidence was thin.","observed_nonverbal_log":"10:12 Lena opens with the top-50 slide and speaks fluently with open gestures. 10:19 When a trustee asks whether the bottom schools were also checked, she hesitates briefly, then says that analysis was not part of the report, her brow furrowing. 10:26 She defends the funding decision with rising conviction, citing the personal-attention story. 10:31 Asked directly whether she saw the bottom-50 data, she admits it was never pulled, with a genuine look of surprise rather than evasion.","interrogation_transcript":"Trustee: \"Did anyone check whether the worst schools are also small?\" Lena: \"No, that wasn't in the scope of the report.\" Trustee: \"If they were, what would that do to the conclusion?\" Lena: \"I honestly hadn't considered it. The story made so much sense to me.\"","correct_diagnosis":"Lena is not hiding evidence; she is sincerely endorsing a statistical artifact. The small-schools pattern reflects the variability of small samples, and neither she nor the researchers checked the bottom of the distribution.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Lena's honest admission that the bottom-50 analysis was never run, combined with her genuine surprise, rules out deliberate concealment. Her confident causal narrative about personal attention is exactly the story Kahneman warns against: easy to construct, pointless because the facts are wrong, since the worst schools are small too. The verdict is sample-size neglect, not fraud.","distractors":["Lena suppressed a report showing large schools outperformed small ones","Lena fabricated the 6-of-50 statistic to win the grant","Lena correctly identified that small schools cause better outcomes"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Loss-Pattern Investigation","visual_frame_description":"A briefing room at an air-force base. Major Cohen, a wing commander, argues that the squadron which lost four planes to missile attacks must have done something wrong and wants every surviving pilot interrogated. On the wall, a mission board shows identical sortie counts for both squadrons flying from the same base, and a timestamped audio log of the command meeting plays.","player_mission":"Determine whether the proposed investigation into the squadron's losses is justified, masks a political motive, or misreads blind luck as a cause. Audit the commander's baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["In the 1973 Yom Kippur War, one Israeli squadron lost four planes while another squadron from the same base lost none.","There was no prior reason to believe one squadron was more effective than the other, and no operational differences were found.","Kahneman advised the Israeli Air Force to stop the investigation and accept that the different outcomes were due to blind luck.","Sampling variation makes extreme outcomes such as four losses concentrated in one unit appear even when there is no cause.","A random search for a nonobvious cause was judged hopeless.","Kahneman reasoned that the pilots in the squadron that had sustained losses did not need the extra burden of being made to feel that they and their dead friends were at fault.","Causal attributions to random events are the subject of the chapter's warning about cause and chance.","System 1 automatically and effortlessly identifies causal connections between events, sometimes even when the connection is spurious.","Statistical facts change the probability of outcomes but do not cause them to happen.","We are pattern seekers who quickly reject the idea that a process is truly random."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the confusion of cause with chance: the unequal squadron losses are what sampling variation looks like, not evidence of error. Kahneman reports advising the Israeli Air Force that the outcomes were due to blind luck and that the investigation should stop because a random search for a nonobvious cause was hopeless.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Major Cohen wants every surviving pilot interrogated to find why one squadron lost four planes. What is the correct diagnosis of the proposed investigation?","subject_baseline":"Major Cohen is a decorated pilot known for meticulous debriefings and a strong sense of duty; he is not known for grandstanding, but he visibly struggles when a unit under his command takes losses.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:40 Cohen sets down the loss chart and speaks with controlled anger, his jaw tight. 09:47 He insists the four-plane squadron must differ in procedure, citing debriefing differences while flipping through mission logs. 09:55 When the adjutant notes that the other squadron flew identical sorties, Cohen waves the point away and repeats that it cannot just be luck. 10:02 Asked what he would do if the inquiry finds nothing, he says he would keep looking, his voice rising, with no sign of evasion or personal gain.","interrogation_transcript":"Adjutant: \"The sortie counts are identical. What makes you certain there is an error to find?\" Cohen: \"Four planes is four planes. That doesn't happen by accident. There is always a cause.\" Adjutant: \"And if there isn't one?\" Cohen: \"Then we haven't looked hard enough.\"","correct_diagnosis":"The investigation is a misreading of random variation as a cause: the unequal losses are a sampling outcome, and continuing the inquiry would wrongly burden the surviving pilots, exactly as Kahneman advised against.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Cohen's certainty, emotional charge, and refusal to entertain chance match the chapter's account of causal thinking: System 1 automatically invents causes for statistical facts. Because no operational difference existed and both squadrons flew identical mission patterns, the four-versus-none split is the extreme outcome small groups produce, so the correct verdict is blind luck rather than error, and the honest recommendation is to stop the investigation.","distractors":["Cohen is launching the inquiry to protect a favored pilot from blame","The squadron that lost planes was demonstrably flying more dangerous missions","Four losses in one squadron proves that squadron trained incorrectly"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Kidney-Cancer County Mystery","visual_frame_description":"A state health department war room. A data advisor studies a map of 3,141 counties where both the highest- and lowest-cancer counties are rural and sparsely populated. A colleague, Dr. Osei, types a press release crediting rural clean living for the low-rate counties, while a senator's funding proposal and a pollution investigation sit in separate inboxes.","player_mission":"Stop the causal misinterpretation of the county data, explain why both extremes are sampling artifacts, and block policy proposals that would spend public money chasing random variation.","key_concepts":["The counties with the lowest kidney-cancer incidence are mostly rural, sparsely populated, and in traditionally Republican states.","The counties with the highest incidence have the very same profile.","A single causal factor cannot explain both extremes, so the differences are artifacts of sample size.","Extreme outcomes are most likely to be found in sparsely populated counties because rural samples are smaller.","The small population of a county neither causes nor prevents cancer; it merely allows incidence to be much higher or lower.","If the analysis is repeated next year, the counties where cancer was common last year will not necessarily have a high incidence this year.","The differences between dense and rural counties do not really count as facts; they are artifacts produced by differences in sample size.","System 1 is inept when faced with merely statistical facts, which change the probability of outcomes but do not cause them to happen.","Sampling variation is a nuisance that turns every research project into a gamble.","Causal explanations of chance events are inevitably wrong."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the law of small numbers: extreme outcomes are more likely in small samples, so sparsely populated counties dominate both ends of the kidney-cancer distribution with no causal meaning. Kahneman, citing Wainer and Zwerling, notes that the rural lifestyle cannot explain both very high and very low incidence of kidney cancer.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The law of small numbers: extreme outcomes appear more often in small samples, so sparsely populated counties dominate both the highest and lowest kidney-cancer rates. The pattern is a sampling artifact, not a causal finding about rural life, and policy built on it misreads chance as cause.","stage_1_observation":"You are a data advisor at a state health department. A colleague, Dr. Osei, has mapped the 3,141 counties and found that both the highest- and lowest-cancer counties are mostly rural, sparsely populated, and in traditionally Republican states. He is drafting a press release crediting rural clean living for the low-rate counties.","stage_1_question":"Why do sparsely populated counties appear at both extremes of the cancer map, and what should Dr. Osei understand before he writes the release?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Both extremes are sampling artifacts: small populations yield extreme outcomes more often, so the low rates are no more real a fact than the high rates, and the pattern would shift if the analysis were repeated next year. Dr. Osei should not attribute the low counties to clean living, because the same kind of county can produce the highest rates for no causal reason.","stage_2_intervention":"A state senator has read Dr. Osei's draft and now proposes redirecting millions into rural wellness grants targeting the low-cancer counties, while the health director wants to investigate the high-cancer counties for industrial pollution. Both want your signature on their proposals by Friday.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Refuse to sign either causal proposal: the county differences do not count as facts but as artifacts of sample size, so targeting the low-rate counties would reinforce noise and the pollution investigation would chase a random outcome. Instead, recommend rate estimates adjusted for population size, aggregate small counties into larger regions for analysis, and repeat the analysis over multiple years before drawing any conclusion."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Sample-Size Decision","visual_frame_description":"A graduate research lab. A senior advisor stands beside a whiteboard where a doctoral student, Maya, has written her hypothesis that six-year-old girls have larger vocabularies than boys, with a plan to test 15 children per group and no power calculation. A line in the notes reads \"what the lab has always done.\"","player_mission":"Prevent Maya from running an undersized study, quantify the risk her sample size creates, and stop the department chair from treating a null result as proof that the hypothesis is false.","key_concepts":["Psychologists commonly chose samples so small that they exposed themselves to a 50% risk of failing to confirm their true hypotheses.","Sample-size decisions traditionally reflect prevalent intuitive misconceptions of the extent of sampling variation rather than computation.","Kahneman found that even authors of statistical textbooks made the same sample-size mistakes as everyone else.","The hypothesis that six-year-old girls have larger vocabularies than boys is true in the population but can be missed by a bad sample.","Using a sufficiently large sample is the only way to reduce the risk of failing to confirm a true hypothesis.","Intuitions about random sampling appear to satisfy the law of small numbers, which asserts that the law of large numbers applies to small numbers as well.","Kahneman and Tversky recommended regarding statistical intuitions with proper suspicion and replacing impression formation by computation whenever possible.","Sampling variation turns the undertaking of every research project into a gamble.","Large samples are more precise than small samples.","Researchers who pick too small a sample leave themselves at the mercy of sampling luck."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the belief in the law of small numbers, the intuition that the law of large numbers applies to small numbers as well. Kahneman reports that psychologists' sample-size decisions exposed them to about a 50% risk of failing to confirm true hypotheses, and that even sophisticated researchers made the same errors.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Researchers' intuitions about sample size are poor: psychologists commonly chose samples so small they exposed themselves to a 50% risk of failing to confirm true hypotheses, and even experts paid insufficient attention to sample size. Kahneman and Tversky called the faith that small samples mirror the population belief in the law of small numbers.","stage_1_observation":"You are a senior advisor to a graduate lab. A doctoral student, Maya, shows you her study plan for testing whether six-year-old girls have larger vocabularies than boys: she plans to test 15 children per group, following what the lab has always done. A whiteboard in the scene shows her hypothesis, a total sample of 30 subjects, and no power calculation.","stage_1_question":"What risk does Maya's chosen sample size expose her to, and why does her reasoning fail?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Her small sample leaves her at the mercy of sampling luck, exposing her to as much as a 50% risk of failing to confirm a true hypothesis, because extreme results are more common in small samples. Her reasoning fails because she is following tradition and intuition rather than computing the sample size that would reduce the risk of failure to an acceptable level.","stage_2_intervention":"Maya runs the study with 30 children, the difference is in the predicted direction but not significant, and the department chair now wants to abandon the hypothesis as probably false. Maya asks you what to do next.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Stop treating the null result as evidence against the hypothesis: it is likely an artifact of an undersized sample. Compute the sample size required for an acceptable risk of failure, run a sufficiently large replication, and replace impression formation by computation, because the vocabulary hypothesis was true in the population and the small sample simply failed to detect it."}]},{"id":"ch16","title":"11. Anchors","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Two Mechanisms of Anchoring: Adjustment vs. Priming","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen diagnostic lab. On the left, Marco, a freelance industrial designer in Portland, stares at a client email that mentions an $80,000 project budget. On the right, two glowing panels are labeled 'System 2 Adjustment' and 'System 1 Priming,' and case cards slide between them. A needle hovering at $78,500 drifts toward whichever panel the player selects as the source of Marco's estimate.","player_mission":"Classify each case card into the anchoring mechanism that produced it, then decide which mechanism produced Marco's quote. Use the chapter's evidence about adjustment and priming to sort every case correctly before locking in your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["An anchoring effect occurs when people consider a particular value for an unknown quantity before estimating that quantity, and their estimate stays close to that number.","In Kahneman and Tversky's wheel-of-fortune experiment, students who spun 10 estimated that about 25% of African nations were UN members, while those who spun 65 estimated about 45%.","Anchoring has two distinct mechanisms: a deliberate System 2 adjustment process and an automatic System 1 priming effect.","Insufficient adjustment means people stop moving away from an anchor as soon as they are no longer certain they should move farther, at the near edge of their region of uncertainty.","Epley and Gilovich found that people who shook their heads as if rejecting an anchor moved farther from it, while those who nodded showed enhanced anchoring.","Adjustment is an effortful operation: people adjust less when their mental resources are depleted, such as when their memory is loaded with digits or they are slightly drunk.","Kahneman proposed that anchoring is a case of suggestion, now understood as a priming effect: System 1 tries to construct a world in which the anchor is true.","Mussweiler and Strack showed that a 68-degree anchor made summer words like 'sun' easier to recognize, while a 40-degree anchor facilitated winter words like 'frost.'","A high anchor about German car prices selectively primed luxury brands like Mercedes and Audi, while a low anchor primed mass-market brands like Volkswagen.","People who are exposed to random or absurd anchors confidently deny being influenced, but they are wrong: System 2 has no control over the effect and no knowledge of it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the two-mechanism account of anchoring: a deliberate System 2 adjustment that typically stops too early, and an automatic System 1 priming effect in which the anchor selectively activates compatible memories. Kahneman and Tversky demonstrated that even obviously uninformative numbers, such as a rigged wheel of fortune, shift estimates, and later research confirmed that both mechanisms are real.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marco, a freelance designer, is asked by a new client to quote a project. The client mentions a budget of $80,000, and Marco's quote comes out at $78,500, near that number. Yet when he reviews his reasoning he finds no moment of deliberate calculation; the figure simply felt right. Which anchoring mechanism best explains his estimate?","column_a_label":"System 2 Adjustment: deliberate, effortful movement away from the anchor that stops too early","column_b_label":"System 1 Priming: automatic associative activation that makes anchor-compatible values feel true","column_c_label":"No Anchoring: the prior number was correctly ignored","rows":[{"label":"A teenager turns down loud music only slightly to reach a parent's 'reasonable' volume, still leaving it too loud","col":"a"},{"label":"Mussweiler and Strack's 68-degree temperature anchor speeds recognition of summer words like 'sun' and 'beach'","col":"b"},{"label":"Exploratorium visitors asked to donate to save seabirds without any suggested amount gave $64 on average","col":"c"},{"label":"A driver leaving the highway keeps going 45 mph on a 25 mph city street, adjusting down only a little","col":"a"},{"label":"A judge rolls loaded dice showing 9 and sentences a shoplifter to 8 months","col":"b"},{"label":"A student estimating the share of African nations in the UN writes down a wheel-of-fortune spin of 65 and estimates 45%","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Adjustment, because Marco consciously considered the budget number before quoting","No anchoring, because the client's number was a real, informative hint about the project","System 2 adjustment, because producing a professional quote always requires deliberate reasoning"],"explanation":"Marco experienced no deliberate walking away from the anchor, so adjustment is not the driver. The budget figure selectively activated compatible values, quote amounts near $80,000, which is exactly the automatic System 1 priming effect Kahneman describes: the estimate is biased even though the person cannot recall being influenced. Random or absurd anchors, such as dice or wheel-of-fortune spins, produce the same priming-based pull, which is why the wheel-of-fortune experiment yielded estimates of 25% and 45% from spins of 10 and 65."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Resisting Anchors in Negotiation and Judgment","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom at a software company, Priya, a vice president of talent, sits across a table from a candidate while a whiteboard lists counter-strategies: think the opposite, costs of no deal, equal counteroffer, nod or shake. Tactic cards slide onto the board, and the player must drag each one into the column it belongs to: effective resistance, no effect, or backfire.","player_mission":"Classify each anchoring defense or aggravator into the correct column, then judge whether Priya's planned preparation for her salary negotiation will actually neutralize the anchor.","key_concepts":["Galinsky and Mussweiler found that instructing negotiators to focus on the minimal offer the opponent would accept reduces or eliminates anchoring.","Deliberately thinking the opposite is a good defense against anchoring because it negates the biased recruitment of thoughts that produces the effect.","In single-issue negotiations, moving first is an advantage because the initial anchor has a powerful pull on the final price.","Kahneman's advice was to make a scene or threaten to walk out when the other side makes an outrageous proposal, rather than replying with an equally extreme counteroffer.","Epley and Gilovich found that nodding when hearing an anchor enhances anchoring, while shaking the head moves estimates farther from the anchor.","Insufficient adjustment is a failure of a weak or lazy System 2: depleted people adjust less and stay closer to the anchor.","Anchoring effects in real-estate pricing were 41% for experienced agents, who denied the influence, and 48% for business-school students, who admitted it.","Anchors do not have their effects because people believe they are informative; random anchors are just as effective as plausible hints.","You should assume that any number on the table has had an anchoring effect on you, and if the stakes are high, mobilize System 2 to combat it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the countermeasures to anchoring in negotiation: the pull of the first number on the table can be weakened by deliberately mobilizing System 2, for example by focusing on the opponent's minimal acceptable offer, the cost of a failed deal, or by thinking the opposite. Kahneman and colleagues showed these tactics reduce or eliminate anchoring, while nodding, countering with an extreme offer, or depleted mental resources strengthen it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya is negotiating salary with a candidate who opened at $185,000. Before responding, she writes down every reason the opening number is too high, names the maximum she is willing to pay, and estimates the cost to the candidate of walking away. Does this preparation neutralize the anchor?","column_a_label":"Effective Resistance: activates System 2 and weakens the anchor","column_b_label":"No Effect: judgment remains anchored","column_c_label":"Backfires: strengthens the anchor","rows":[{"label":"Focusing attention on the minimal offer the opponent would accept (Galinsky and Mussweiler)","col":"a"},{"label":"Deliberately thinking the opposite and generating arguments against the anchor","col":"a"},{"label":"Returning an equally outrageous counteroffer, widening the gap in negotiations","col":"c"},{"label":"Nodding your head when you hear the anchor, signaling acceptance","col":"c"},{"label":"Making a scene or declaring the negotiations over when the other side names an extreme number","col":"a"},{"label":"Estimating while your working memory is loaded with digits or you are slightly drunk","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Her preparation is futile because anchors act only when the stakes are low","She will be more anchored because she paid close attention to the $185,000 number","She should instead counter immediately with an equally extreme figure to reset the negotiation"],"explanation":"Galinsky and Mussweiler showed that deliberately activating System 2, by focusing on the opponent's minimal acceptable offer, the costs of failing to reach an agreement, or generally thinking the opposite, reduces or eliminates anchoring. Priya's search for arguments against the anchor negates the biased recruitment of thoughts that produces the effect. By contrast, nodding enhances anchoring, an equally extreme counteroffer leaves a gap that is hard to bridge, and depleted mental resources leave people staying closer to the anchor."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Anchors in Real-World Money and Pricing Decisions","visual_frame_description":"A scrolling gallery of everyday money scenes: a supermarket shelf with a Campbell's soup 'limit of 12' sign, an online trading app showing two buy-now prices for the same gadget, a fine-art auctioneer gesturing at a printed estimate, and a donation kiosk at a science museum. Each scene is tagged with a number the player must sort into the correct mechanism column.","player_mission":"Decide which mechanism produced each anchoring effect in real-world decisions, automatic priming, deliberate adjustment, or a deliberate debiasing move, and then judge what is driving the change in donations at Mara's charity.","key_concepts":["In the Exploratorium seabird study, visitors with no anchor gave $64 on average, those considering $5 gave $20, and those considering $400 gave $143.","Increasing the initial request by $100 brought a return of about $30 in average willingness to pay, an anchoring effect above 30%.","French residents of the polluted Marseilles region showed an anchoring effect over 50% when asked what living-cost increase they would accept to live in a less polluted region.","Supermarket shoppers bought an average of 7 cans of soup under a 'limit of 12' sign, twice as many as when the sign was absent.","The estimate in fine-art auctions is an anchor that influences the first bid.","Online trading shows anchoring in everyday life, where the same item is offered at different buy-now prices.","Random anchors are as effective as potentially informative ones: a wheel-of-fortune spin produced an anchoring index of 44%.","Anchors do not have their effects because people believe they are informative.","Arbitrary rationing works as a marketing ploy in part because mentioning 12 cans as a possible purchase produces anchoring."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests anchoring in real-world money decisions: the amount a person first considers automatically pulls estimates and willingness to pay toward it. Kahneman's willingness-to-pay studies showed average donations rising from $64 with no anchor to $143 with a $400 anchor, an effect above 30%, and similar anchoring appears in auctions, online trading, and store rationing signs.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Mara runs a nonprofit and is choosing the wording of her online donation page. In the Exploratorium seabird study, visitors who first considered a $5 anchor gave about $20, those who considered $400 gave about $143, and visitors asked with no anchor gave $64. Which mechanism is producing the change in giving?","column_a_label":"System 1 Priming Anchoring: the considered number automatically pulls the estimate or decision toward it","column_b_label":"System 2 Adjustment Anchoring: deliberate movement from the anchor that stops too early","column_c_label":"Debiasing: an explicit System 2 move to neutralize the anchor","rows":[{"label":"Shoppers seeing a 'limit of 12 per person' sign on 10%-off Campbell's soup bought 7 cans on average, twice the no-limit amount","col":"a"},{"label":"The printed estimate in a fine-art auction influencing the first bid","col":"a"},{"label":"The same item offered at different buy-now prices in online trading shifting what buyers expect to pay","col":"a"},{"label":"A negotiator who read the other side's business plan and then forecast revenue close to the plan's figures","col":"a"},{"label":"A judge mentally walking down from a plaintiff's demand to a lower damages number but not far enough","col":"b"},{"label":"A company setting aside the seller's revenue plan and explicitly forecasting ways the plan could go wrong","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["System 2 adjustment, because donors reason carefully about how much to give","Debiasing, because the anchor amounts are so different that donors notice them","No mechanism: $64, $20, and $143 are just random variation in generosity"],"explanation":"The donation results are a classic priming-based anchoring effect: the considered amount, $5 or $400, automatically recruits compatible thinking about what is reasonable to give, so willingness to pay rises from $64 with no anchor to $143 with the $400 anchor, an effect above 30%. The rationing, auction-estimate, and buy-now price examples all show the same automatic pull of a considered number, whereas a truly deliberate countermeasure, like setting the business plan aside and imagining ways it could go wrong, is a debiasing move."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Denied Anchor: A Real-Estate Agent's Valuation","visual_frame_description":"A bright condo living room where Nina, a fifteen-year real-estate agent in her fifties, studies a thick booklet of comparable sales. A small clock and an observational camera log appear in the corner, timestamping every glance she steals at the asking-price line while she circles numbers on a valuation worksheet.","player_mission":"Review Nina's behavior log and interview transcript, then decide whether her confident denial of the listing price's influence is trustworthy or whether she is anchored despite herself.","key_concepts":["Real-estate agents given an inflated asking price produced valuations 41% higher than those given a deflated price.","Business-school students with no real-estate experience showed an anchoring index of 48% and conceded the anchor had influenced them.","The only difference between the agents and the students was that the agents denied the influence of the asking price while the students admitted it.","System 2 has no control over anchoring and no knowledge of it, so participants confidently deny that useless anchors influenced their estimates.","Anchoring works whether or not the anchor is informative; random numbers are as effective as plausible hints.","Any number considered as a possible answer to an estimation problem will induce an anchoring effect.","People often clutch at straws on difficult questions, and the anchor is a plausible straw, but random anchors work just as well as informative ones.","Anchoring effects can be just as strong in the real world as in the laboratory."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the claim that anchoring influences professional judgments even when experts deny it: System 2 has no knowledge of the effect and no control over it. Kahneman's real-estate-agent study showed a 41% anchoring effect on valuations alongside confident denial, with professionals almost as susceptible as novices.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Nina, a 15-year residential real-estate agent, toured a three-bedroom condo and studied a comprehensive booklet of comparable sales. Unbeknownst to her, the booklet showed an asking price $120,000 higher than the true listing. When asked what affected her opinion, Nina insisted the asking price had no influence on her valuation. Is her self-report trustworthy?","subject_baseline":"Nina is a meticulous, veteran agent known for studying comp sheets line by line and lecturing junior agents about pricing discipline. In past listings she has prided herself on ignoring sellers' inflated asks and pricing purely from data. She keeps a calm, confident posture when discussing numbers and treats her pricing method as entirely analytic.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:03 - Nina opens the booklet; her gaze lingers on the asking-price line for roughly eight seconds before she flips on.","10:14 - While computing her reasonable buying price, her eyes repeatedly return to the price field at the top of the page.","10:21 - She writes $512,000, then underlines it without hesitation; her speech speeds up when she explains her method.","10:26 - Asked whether the listing price mattered, she smiles, uncrosses her arms, and leans forward to answer quickly.","10:29 - She checks the asking price one final time before giving the lowest price she would sell for figure of $498,000."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: What did the asking price tell you about this property?","Nina: Honestly, I barely register it. I price from comps, solds within the last six months, same block. The ask is just a seller's fantasy.","Auditor: You've listed your reasonable buying price at $512,000. The comparable solds you cited average $470,000. What accounts for the gap?","Nina: This unit has better light and a renovated kitchen. That's worth money.","Auditor: And your lowest acceptable selling price of $498,000, if you owned it?","Nina: That's my floor. I'd never go below it, regardless of the ask.","Auditor: Do you think the number you first saw could have nudged you at all?","Nina: Absolutely not. I've done this for fifteen years. I don't get anchored."],"correct_diagnosis":"Anchored. Nina's valuations are pulled upward by the inflated asking price she saw first, consistent with the 41% anchoring effect Kahneman measured in real-estate agents, even though she sincerely denies being influenced and can cite plausible reasons for the gap.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The behavioral log shows Nina repeatedly returning to the price field before producing each figure, the hallmark of a considered number doing quiet work. Her $512,000 and $498,000 estimates both sit well above the $470,000 comparable sales she herself cited, which is hard to explain by condition alone. The decisive evidence is the chapter's experimental finding: when half the agents saw an inflated listing price and half a deflated one, valuations moved by 41%, and the agents, like Nina, insisted the ask had no effect while the inexperienced students conceded that it did. Nina's fluent denial and confident body language are exactly what the study predicts, not a sign that she is lying; her self-report is simply inaccurate.","distractors":["Not anchored: Nina's fifteen years of experience let her genuinely ignore the listing price","Deliberate deception: Nina is inflating her valuation on purpose to earn a higher commission","Halo effect: Nina is overvaluing the renovated kitchen rather than reacting to the asking price"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Judge and the Loaded Dice","visual_frame_description":"A wood-paneled judge's chambers. Judge Keller, a gray-haired jurist in his sixties, reads a shoplifting case file, then rolls a pair of dice that stop on 9. A timestamped log in the corner shows him writing an 8-month sentence immediately afterward while a clerk watches, and the number 3 hovers ghosted beside the dice to remind the player of the rigged alternative.","player_mission":"Study the judge's behavior during the sentencing session and his explanation afterward, then determine whether his stated reasoning, offense severity, actually explains the sentence he imposed.","key_concepts":["German judges with over fifteen years of experience rolled dice rigged to show only 3 or 9 before sentencing a shoplifter.","Judges who rolled a 9 sentenced the shoplifter to 8 months on average; those who rolled a 3 gave 5 months, a 50% anchoring effect.","The anchoring effect appeared even though the dice obviously carried no information about the crime.","Participants exposed to random or absurd anchors confidently deny that the useless information influenced their estimate, and they are wrong.","System 2 works on data retrieved automatically from memory by System 1 and is therefore susceptible to anchors that make some information easier to retrieve.","Random anchors are just as effective as potentially informative ones; the wheel-of-fortune experiment produced an anchoring index of 44%.","The last digits of a Social Security number used as an anchor produced anchoring effects comparable to plausible hints.","Anchors do not have their effects because people believe they are informative.","The anchoring effect is one of the most reliable and robust results of experimental psychology."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the power of random anchors on expert judgment: even when a number obviously carries no information, it pulls System 2's final answer toward it while remaining invisible to the decision maker. Kahneman's loaded-dice study found a 50% anchoring effect on experienced judges' sentencing, with the judges sincerely denying any influence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Judge Keller, who has averaged over fifteen years on the bench, reads a case file describing a woman caught shoplifting a small amount. Before sentencing, he rolls a pair of dice that have been rigged to land only on 3 or 9; the roll shows 9. He sentences the woman to 8 months, explaining that the term fits the theft's seriousness. Does his stated reasoning explain the sentence?","subject_baseline":"Judge Keller is a deliberative jurist who favors written sentencing guidelines, runs long hearings, and frequently lectures clerks that sentences should track offense severity, not whim. He is proud of his even-handedness and keeps a running personal record of his sentencing consistency across similar cases.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:05 - Keller skims the shoplifting file, then picks up the dice as part of the study procedure; his eyes flick to the number as they stop.","14:07 - The dice show 9. He holds the position for two seconds before writing the comparative-sentence check mark on the form.","14:12 - Asked to specify the sentence, he writes '8 months' quickly, without a pause to consult the file again.","14:14 - Explaining his reasoning, he taps the file: theft is theft, and the guidelines place this squarely at eight months.","14:18 - He pushes back when a clerk suggests the sentence might be lenient: he weighs the crime, not his mood, and certainly not a pair of dice."],"interrogation_transcript":["Clerk: Judge, why eight months rather than five?","Keller: The file. Repeat offender history, taken goods valued over the threshold, no remorse in the arrest report. Eight months is consistent with what I'd hand down on any Tuesday.","Clerk: If the dice had shown a 3, do you think your sentence would have changed?","Keller: Not in the slightest. That's the point of the exercise, showing that extraneous numbers don't leak into my rulings.","Clerk: Five months versus eight months, both could be defensible under the guidelines. What made you settle?","Keller: I settled on the seriousness of the offense. The number I rolled is irrelevant."],"correct_diagnosis":"Anchored. Keller's 8-month sentence is pulled up by the rolled 9, Kahneman reports that judges who rolled 9 averaged 8 months while those who rolled 3 averaged 5 months, a 50% anchoring effect, despite his sincere insistence that the dice were irrelevant to his reasoning.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Keller's file itself supports sentences in the 5-to-8-month range, so his stated rationale, offense severity, cannot discriminate between the two outcomes, which is exactly why the dice matter. The 14:12 time-stamp shows he wrote 8 months immediately after the roll without re-consulting the file, and his protestations to the clerk match the chapter's core finding: experienced judges were strongly influenced by random anchors while firmly believing they were not. A 50% anchoring effect with loaded dice that show only 3 or 9 means the average difference between the two conditions was half the distance between the anchors, evidence of a genuine priming pull rather than a reasoned judgment.","distractors":["Not anchored: fifteen-plus years of judicial experience insulate sentencing from random numbers","Self-serving bias: Keller is deliberately harsher because the defendant is a repeat offender","Priming from the case file: the crime details, not the dice, drove the sentence"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Lowball Offer: A Negotiation Battle","visual_frame_description":"A conference room in Columbus, Ohio, where Marta, the seller of a family warehouse, sits beside you across from three buyers. The buyer's negotiator has just slid a term sheet showing $2.1 million across the table. The word ANCHOR flickers above the number as the tension mounts, and a sidebar tracks your response strategy options.","player_mission":"Run the negotiation as Marta's representative: choose a first move that defeats the lowball anchor rather than matching it, then handle the buyer's second offer by keeping System 2 engaged.","key_concepts":["Moving first is an advantage in single-issue negotiations, because the initial anchor has a powerful effect on the final price.","Galinsky and Mussweiler found that focusing on the minimal offer the opponent would accept reduces or eliminates anchoring.","Focusing on the costs to the opponent of failing to reach an agreement also weakens the anchor's pull.","Deliberately thinking the opposite is a good defense against anchoring because it negates the biased recruitment of thoughts that produces the effect.","Kahneman advised that when the other side makes an outrageous proposal, you should make a scene or threaten to walk away rather than respond with an equally outrageous counteroffer.","An equally extreme counteroffer creates a gap that will be difficult to bridge in further negotiations.","You are always aware of the anchor and even pay attention to it, but you do not know how it guides your thinking.","If the stakes are high, you should mobilize your System 2 to combat the anchoring effect of any number on the table.","Anchoring effects in negotiations over a single issue such as price are powerful and reliably observed.","Insufficient adjustment, stopping at the near edge of the region of uncertainty, leaves negotiators closer to the anchor than they realize."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests anchoring in single-issue negotiation: the first number considered pulls the final price toward it, and matching a lowball with an equal counteroffer widens an unbridgeable gap. Kahneman and colleagues showed the anchor's pull can be reduced by deliberately focusing on the opponent's minimal acceptable offer, the cost of no deal, and arguments against the anchor, that is, by mobilizing System 2.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is anchoring in negotiation: the first number on the table exerts a powerful pull on the final price, so a lowball offer from the buyer becomes an anchor you must actively defeat rather than match. The chapter's tested defenses are moving to reject the anchor outright and deliberately thinking the opposite by focusing on the other side's minimal acceptable offer and the cost of no deal.","stage_1_observation":"You represent Marta, who is selling her family's warehouse in Columbus. The buyer's team opens by offering $2.1 million, far below the $3.2 million that two independent appraisers support. Across the table, the buyer's negotiator slides a printed term sheet toward you and leans back with a satisfied look, waiting. The room is quiet; every eye is on how you handle the number now on the table.","stage_1_question":"The buyer's lowball is now the number on the table. What is the most effective first move, based on the chapter's negotiation evidence?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Refuse to negotiate from that number: make it clear, to yourself and the other side, that negotiations cannot continue while $2.1 million is on the table, rather than countering with an equally extreme figure.","stage_2_intervention":"The buyer withdraws the $2.1 million offer and returns the next day with $2.4 million, still below appraised value. Marta is tired and anxious to close, and she suggests you just meet in the middle at $2.8 million, which is close to her goal but still short. The buyer's negotiator, sensing weakness, begins talking about the warehouse needing a new roof to push the price down further. You have one move before the meeting escalates.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep Marta's System 2 engaged: explicitly search for arguments against the $2.4 million anchor, the cost to the buyer of failing to reach a deal, the buyer's own minimum acceptable price, and evidence contradicting the roof story, before naming any counterfigure, instead of anchoring on meeting in the middle."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Damages Cap: Anchoring in Public Policy","visual_frame_description":"A state capitol briefing room where you, chief policy adviser to the hospital association, stand before a table of three hospital executives. A projector shows a bar chart of personal-injury awards from $200,000 to $9 million, with a red line drawn at $1 million labeled CAP and small upward arrows sprouting from the smaller bars below it.","player_mission":"Advise the board on what a $1 million damages cap will actually do to the distribution of awards, then pick a lobbying strategy that accounts for the cap's hidden anchoring effect rather than assuming a lower cap is pure upside.","key_concepts":["A cap on damages eliminates all larger awards but also acts as an anchor that pulls up the size of many awards that would otherwise be much smaller.","Capping personal-injury awards would almost certainly benefit serious offenders and large firms much more than small ones.","Anchoring effects are so common in everyday decision making that any number considered as a possible solution will induce an anchoring effect.","Anchors do not have their effects because people believe they are informative; random anchors work as well as plausible hints.","System 2 has no control over anchoring and no knowledge of it, so policy makers cannot rely on deliberation to neutralize the effect.","Powerful anchoring effects are found in decisions people make about money, including contributions to causes and estimates of damages.","The anchoring index in the redwood experiment was 55%, a typical value across many problems.","If the stakes are high, you should assume that any number on the table has anchored you and mobilize System 2 to combat the effect.","People are far more suggestible than most of us would want to be, and there are people willing and able to exploit this gullibility."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests anchoring as a hidden force in public policy: a damages cap is simultaneously a ceiling and a psychological anchor that drags smaller awards upward. Kahneman's analysis shows the cap would eliminate the largest verdicts while inflating many modest ones, making the policy's real winners serious offenders and large firms.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that anchors operate even in policy design: a legislated cap on damages is not only a ceiling but an anchor that pulls smaller awards upward. Kahneman argues that capping personal-injury awards at $1 million would eliminate larger awards but also raise many awards that would otherwise be far smaller, benefiting serious offenders and large firms more than small ones.","stage_1_observation":"You are chief policy adviser to the state hospital association, which has lobbied for years to cap personal-injury damages at $1 million. A plaintiff's advocacy group just released a report showing several recent awards of $3 to $9 million. The hospital board, including three executives who have never faced a lawsuit personally, expects you to brief them on why the cap is a win. You must decide what the cap will actually do to the distribution of awards.","stage_1_question":"Based on the chapter's analysis of anchoring, what is the most accurate prediction for a $1 million cap on damages?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The cap will eliminate awards above $1 million, but it will also serve as an anchor that pulls up many awards that would otherwise be much smaller, so the rule would likely benefit serious offenders and large firms more than small ones, and the net effect is not a straightforward win for defendants.","stage_2_intervention":"After your brief, the board splits. The general counsel insists the cap is pure upside and wants to lobby for an even lower cap of $500,000. A junior analyst objects that a lower cap anchors claims even harder upward, while the plaintiff's group now offers to support a $2 million cap in exchange for procedural concessions. The board looks to you to choose the strategy with the least anchoring downside.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reject the framing that lower caps are pure upside: recognize that any cap, $500,000 or $2 million, becomes an anchor pulling smaller awards up toward it, and press the board to weigh the anchoring effect, not just the ceiling, when choosing a figure, rather than assuming a lower cap always helps defendants."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Donation Campaign: Setting the Anchor","visual_frame_description":"A bright nonprofit office where you, fundraising manager for CoastCare, face a large monitor showing three donation-page mockups: a bare donate button, a suggested $5 amount, and a suggested $400 amount. Below them, a small banner displays last year's average donation of $64, while a progress bar tracks how each anchor moves actual average gifts.","player_mission":"Choose the donation-page design that will maximize average giving based on the chapter's willingness-to-pay evidence, then rule on the executive director's proposal to escalate the anchor to $2,000.","key_concepts":["Exploratorium visitors asked to contribute to save seabirds with no anchor gave $64 on average.","Visitors anchored to $5 gave an average of $20, while those anchored to $400 gave an average of $143.","The difference between the high-anchor and low-anchor groups was $123, an anchoring effect above 30%.","Increasing the initial request by $100 brought a return of about $30 in average willingness to pay.","Anchoring effects in decisions about money, such as contributions to a cause, are powerful.","Random anchors are just as effective as potentially informative ones.","Anchors do not have their effects because people believe they are informative.","Any number considered as a possible answer to an estimation problem will induce an anchoring effect.","People are far more suggestible than most of us would want to be.","If the stakes are high, assume any number on the table has anchored you and mobilize System 2."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests anchoring in prosocial giving: the first dollar amount a donor considers becomes an anchor that shifts actual contributions. Kahneman's Exploratorium willingness-to-pay study showed average gifts of $64 with no anchor rising to $143 with a $400 anchor and falling to $20 with a $5 anchor, an effect above 30%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is anchoring in willingness to pay: the amount a donor is first asked to consider shifts the amount they actually give, even when the anchor is arbitrary. In the Exploratorium seabird study, visitors with no anchor offered $64 on average, a $5 anchor pulled giving down to $20, and a $400 anchor raised it to $143.","stage_1_observation":"You manage fundraising for CoastCare, a seabird rescue nonprofit. You are redesigning the online donation page, which currently has no suggested amount. Your colleagues argue over three mockups: a bare donate button with no figure, a suggested amount of $5, and a suggested amount of $400. The screen in front of you shows the three options side by side, with last year's average donation of $64 displayed underneath.","stage_1_question":"Which mockup will produce the highest average donation, based on the chapter's willingness-to-pay evidence?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The $400 suggested amount: the Exploratorium study showed that anchoring with $400 raised average willingness to pay to $143, versus $64 with no anchor and $20 with a $5 anchor.","stage_2_intervention":"Your mockup with the $400 anchor wins, and first-week data shows average gifts of about $138, matching the study. Now the executive director wants to push further: she proposes a $2,000 hero-donor anchor on the same page, reasoning that if $400 worked, $2,000 will work even better. A skeptical donor-relations lead warns the number looks absurd and might backfire, and the board asks you to rule on the proposal.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep a high-but-plausible anchor on the $400 scale rather than escalating to an extravagant $2,000: the chapter's evidence supports strong effects from plausible high anchors, and while even absurd anchors still exert a priming pull, the $2,000 figure introduces reactions the study did not test, so you should monitor the data rather than assume a larger number always yields proportionally larger gifts."}]},{"id":"ch17","title":"12. The Science of Availability","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Sources of Availability Bias in Everyday Judgment","visual_frame_description":"A newsroom wall of memory clips: a burning car on a highway shoulder, a celebrity divorce headline, a plane crash story, a family courtroom scene, and a couple arguing over chores. Each clip shows a person about to estimate a frequency, and the player must sort each scene into the availability source that is skewing the estimate.","player_mission":"Classify each scenario by the source of its availability bias, dramatic or salient events, personal or vivid experience, or fluency of retrieval, and then diagnose what is skewing Dana's fear of the ocean.","key_concepts":["The availability heuristic judges the frequency of a category by the ease with which instances come to mind.","Salient events that attract attention are easily retrieved, so people exaggerate the frequency of celebrity divorces and political sex scandals.","A dramatic event temporarily increases the availability of its category: a plane crash alters feelings about the safety of flying for a while.","Personal experiences, pictures, and vivid examples are more available than incidents that happened to others, mere words, or statistics.","A judicial error that affects you undermines your faith in the justice system more than a similar incident read about in a newspaper.","Substitution of questions, estimating frequency while reporting ease of retrieval, inevitably produces systematic errors.","You can resist availability biases by asking whether a belief is due to a few recent instances rather than real frequency.","People estimate the frequency of a category such as people who divorce after age 60 from how easily instances come to mind.","The availability heuristic, like other heuristics, substitutes one question for another."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability heuristic, in which people judge frequency by the ease with which instances come to mind rather than by actual counts. Kahneman and Tversky showed that vivid, personal, and dramatic instances are more available than statistics, so a single vivid story can outweigh accurate base rates.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dana read one vivid feature story about a shark bite off the Florida coast. Now, at a lifeguard briefing that cites statistics showing shark bites are extremely rare, she still tells friends the ocean is far more dangerous than the lifeguards' numbers suggest. Which availability mechanism is driving her judgment?","column_a_label":"Dramatic or salient event: attention-grabbing news temporarily inflates the category's judged frequency","column_b_label":"Personal or vivid experience: a personal, pictured, or story-based instance is more available than statistics","column_c_label":"Fluency of retrieval: the ease of calling instances to mind, not their number, drives the estimate","rows":[{"label":"After passing a car burning on the highway shoulder, Priya feels the roads are dangerous for weeks","col":"a"},{"label":"A media-covered plane crash makes Lena overestimate the risk of flying for a while","col":"a"},{"label":"Hollywood celebrity divorces make Leo think half of all marriages end in divorce","col":"a"},{"label":"A judicial error that struck your own family undermines your faith in the courts more than a newspaper story would","col":"b"},{"label":"A couple each remember their own household chores vividly and both estimate they do more than their share","col":"b"},{"label":"People asked for twelve instances of assertiveness rate themselves as less assertive than people asked for six","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["She is rationally updating her risk estimate from the shark-bite report","A dramatic event: the shark story was attention-grabbing news","Anchoring on the lifeguards' statistic they just quoted"],"explanation":"Dana is responding to a vivid, story-based instance, the single shark-bite feature, which is far more available in memory than the lifeguards' dry statistics. Kahneman notes that personal experiences, pictures, and vivid examples are more available than incidents that happened to others, mere words, or statistics, and that a dramatic event temporarily raises the availability of its category. Because the shark story is a vivid example rather than a frequency-driving salience event like the burning car or a plane crash, it sorts to column B."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Ease of Retrieval Versus Content in Self-Judgment","visual_frame_description":"A split laboratory: on one side, a person struggling to list twelve assertive moments, brow furrowed; on the other, a person breezing through six. Between them a whiteboard tallies amount retrieved against ease of retrieval. Cards showing Schwarz's paradoxical results and the heart-disease study slide past, and the player must sort which factor drove each judgment.","player_mission":"Determine, for each experiment card, whether the judgment followed content, ease of retrieval, or a disrupted and explained fluency, then diagnose the recruiter's counterintuitive impression of a candidate.","key_concepts":["People who listed twelve instances of assertiveness rated themselves as less assertive than people who listed six.","Participants asked to list twelve cases of non-assertive behavior ended up thinking of themselves as quite assertive.","Self-ratings were dominated by the ease with which examples came to mind; fluent retrieval trumped the number retrieved.","Frowning accompanies cognitive strain, and frowners who struggled to retrieve examples rated themselves as less assertive.","People believe they use their bicycles less after recalling many rather than few instances.","People are less confident in a choice when asked to produce more arguments to support it.","UCLA students who listed more ways to improve a course rated the course higher.","When fluency is given a spurious explanation, background music, straight text boxes, or screen color, it no longer influences judgment.","People who are personally involved in a judgment consider the number of instances retrieved rather than fluency.","The availability heuristic is better described as an unexplained unavailability heuristic."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests Schwarz's fluency discovery: judgments of traits and events are driven by the ease with which instances come to mind, not by the number retrieved, so demanding twelve examples paradoxically lowers self-rated assertiveness. The effect reverses when fluency is explained away, and people with personal stakes shift to evaluating content.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A recruiter at a talent agency is asked to list 10 weaknesses of a candidate before rating her. She struggles through the task, producing only awkward, minor items. Afterward she rates the candidate MORE impressive than a colleague who was asked for just 3 weaknesses. Which factor is driving the recruiter's judgment?","column_a_label":"Content: the judgment follows the number and quality of instances retrieved","column_b_label":"Ease of retrieval: fluency, how hard the instances were to produce, drives the judgment","column_c_label":"Fluency explained or disrupted: a spurious cause is provided, so the heuristic stops operating","rows":[{"label":"Participants who listed twelve assertive instances rated themselves as less assertive than those who listed six","col":"b"},{"label":"UCLA students who listed more ways to improve the course rated the course higher","col":"b"},{"label":"Subjects told that background music would make recall harder rated themselves equally assertive at six and twelve instances","col":"c"},{"label":"Cardiac-risk students with a family history of heart disease felt safer after recalling many safe behaviors","col":"a"},{"label":"Frowners, who experienced greater retrieval strain, rated themselves as less assertive","col":"b"},{"label":"Subjects whose difficulty was explained by straight text boxes stopped using fluency in their judgment","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Content: she genuinely retrieved many weaknesses, so the rating is evidence-based","Halo effect: she liked the candidate before the task began","Anchoring: the requested number 10 set a high standard for the rating"],"explanation":"The recruiter's rating follows the difficulty of retrieval, not what she retrieved: struggling to produce ten weaknesses felt like unexplained unavailability, so she inferred the candidate must have few real weaknesses. Schwarz and colleagues showed self-ratings are dominated by fluency, people who listed twelve hard-to-produce instances rated themselves less assertive than those who listed six, and the paradox disappears when the fluency is given a spurious explanation. The heart-disease participants with personal stakes are the exception who used content instead, which is why they sort to column A."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Chore Audit: When Both Partners Claim More Than Half","visual_frame_description":"A softly lit couples-counseling office with a beige couch. Marcus and Elena sit at opposite ends, each holding a worksheet. A timestamped observational log in the corner shows how fluently each describes their own chores and how vaguely they describe the other's, while a tally at the top reads 70% plus 65%.","player_mission":"Review how each partner describes their own and the other's contributions, then determine whether either account of the household split is accurate or whether both are distorted by an availability bias.","key_concepts":["In a famous study, spouses' self-assessed contributions to household tasks added up to more than 100%.","Both spouses remember their own individual efforts much more clearly than those of the other, and the difference in availability leads to a difference in judged frequency.","The bias is not necessarily self-serving: spouses also overestimated their contribution to causing quarrels, though less than for desirable outcomes.","Many members of a collaborative team feel they have done more than their share and feel that the others are not adequately grateful.","There is usually more than 100% of credit to go around, and the mere observation of this is sometimes enough to defuse a situation.","The availability heuristic judges frequency by the ease with which instances come to mind.","Personal experiences are more available than incidents that happened to others.","Vigilance against availability biases is a chore, but the chance to avoid a costly mistake is sometimes worth the effort.","You will occasionally do more than your share, but it is useful to know you are likely to have that feeling even when everyone feels the same."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the availability bias in self-assessment: because people recall their own actions far more vividly than others', both partners' estimates of contribution sum past 100%. Kahneman's marriage study showed the summed estimates exceed 100% and that acknowledging there is usually more than 100% of credit available can defuse the conflict.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus and Elena have been married nine years and are in couples counseling over household duties. When asked to estimate their own contribution to keeping the apartment tidy, each claims to do well over half the work, and each is certain the other underestimates them. Whose account of the household should the counselor treat as accurate?","subject_baseline":"Marcus keeps a running mental tally of chores and quotes specific evenings he scrubbed the kitchen; Elena maintains a shared calendar of her errands and resents having to reassign tasks. Both are ordinarily candid people who dislike conflict and genuinely believe they pull their weight; neither has any history of deceptive behavior.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:02 - Marcus answers about 70 percent instantly, ticking off three specific chores he performed this week without hesitation.","15:06 - Asked to name Elena's contributions, Marcus pauses, looks at the ceiling, and produces two generic examples, she does some laundry.","15:11 - Elena answers 65 percent with detailed dates and times; her voice rises slightly as she itemizes.","15:16 - Elena, when listing Marcus's chores, gives a vague he takes out the garbage sometimes, after a two-second hesitation.","15:24 - Both lean forward when recounting their own work and lean back, relaxed, when describing the other's."],"interrogation_transcript":["Counselor: Marcus, how much of the tidy-keeping would you say is yours?","Marcus: Honestly? Seventy percent. I did the kitchen after dinner Tuesday and Thursday, the bathroom Saturday, and I sweep every other day.","Counselor: And Elena's share?","Marcus: She handles some laundry, I guess. I don't keep track of her stuff the way I keep track of mine.","Counselor: Elena, your estimate?","Elena: Sixty-five percent, at least. I plan the meals, I book the cleaners, I do the groceries, I have the calendar to prove it.","Counselor: And what would Marcus's day look like, from your view?","Elena: He does the trash run and the occasional sweep. Beyond that, honestly, I couldn't say.","Counselor: So between you, that's 135% of the tidy-keeping. Does that strike either of you as possible?","Marcus: It must be more than 100% then, because neither of us is lying."],"correct_diagnosis":"No deception. Both partners are honestly misestimating due to an availability bias: each remembers their own contributions far more vividly than the other's, so the judged contributions exceed the total. The counselor should treat neither estimate as an accurate allocation and should recognize that there is usually more than 100% of credit to go around.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The fluency markers are decisive: both partners produce their own contributions instantly, concretely, and in volume, but hesitate and go vague when asked about the other's, vivid personal recall is far more available. Kahneman's marriage study showed that self-assessed contributions add up to more than 100% for exactly this reason, and that people also overestimate their own contribution to causing quarrels. Neither partner is lying; their self-reports are both fluent and wrong. The constructive move is to defuse the conflict by observing that more than 100% credit is typically available for distribution.","distractors":["Marcus is lying: his concrete, itemized account is rehearsed to win the counseling session","Elena is accurate: her shared calendar proves her 65% is factual","One partner must be right because household work is a zero-sum allocation"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Performance Review: Twelve Hard-Won Examples","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled review room at Meridian Consulting. Raj, a senior consultant, sits across from his manager, a notebook of twelve bullet points between them. A timestamped behavioral log shows him writing the first three examples quickly, then hesitating with a furrowed brow on the later ones before marking himself a 3 out of 10 on assertiveness.","player_mission":"Audit Raj's self-rating: weigh his visible retrieval effort, his interview answers, and his documented assertive record, then decide whether the low score reflects his actual behavior or an artifact of the task.","key_concepts":["People who listed twelve instances of assertive behavior rated themselves as less assertive than people who listed six.","The experience of fluent retrieval trumped the number of instances retrieved.","Participants asked for twelve cases of non-assertive behavior ended up thinking of themselves as quite assertive.","The availability heuristic is better described as an unexplained unavailability heuristic: people infer from difficulty that the trait must be absent.","The inference rests on surprise: retrieval fluency dropped more steeply between six and twelve instances than participants expected.","When fluency was given a spurious explanation, background music, straight text boxes, or screen color, it no longer influenced judgment.","Frowning accompanies cognitive strain, and instructed frowners rated themselves as less assertive.","People are less confident in a choice when asked to produce more arguments to support it.","The difficulty of coming up with new instances increases more rapidly than people expect."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the fluency paradox at the heart of Schwarz's research: asking for twelve examples makes retrieval hard, and the experienced difficulty is misread as evidence that the trait is absent. People who listed twelve instances rated themselves less assertive than those who listed six, so Raj's low self-rating reflects retrieval strain, not his actual behavior.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"At Meridian Consulting's annual review, Raj, asked by his manager to prepare twelve examples of his assertiveness, produces them with visible effort and then rates himself as not very assertive. His colleague Mei, asked for six examples, breezes through and rates herself highly assertive. Should Raj's low self-rating be taken at face value as an accurate measure of his assertiveness?","subject_baseline":"Raj is a senior consultant who volunteers first in meetings, has led two client pitches this year, and was nominated for a leadership track. He is not given to self-deprecation; in informal settings he describes himself as direct. He had a good, rested morning before the review.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:12 - Raj picks up his pen; the first three assertiveness examples come quickly, written in under a minute.","09:19 - For examples seven through twelve, he pauses repeatedly, looks up, and taps the table; his brow furrows on examples nine and eleven.","09:24 - He finishes the list with a sigh, shaking his head slightly at the last two entries.","09:31 - Asked to rate his assertiveness, he marks 3 out of 10 without hesitation, citing his struggles during the task.","09:35 - His voice is flat as he explains that if he has to dig this hard, he must not really be like that.","09:40 - He recalls the twelve examples again with visible discomfort before closing his notebook."],"interrogation_transcript":["Manager: Raj, that was a long list. How did the exercise feel?","Raj: The first few were easy, the client pitch, the team stand-up. After that it got hard. I was reaching for the last ones.","Manager: You've given me examples of leading two pitches and pushing back on scope creep. Those sound assertive.","Raj: But if being assertive were really me, the examples would have come easily. Twelve was nearly impossible. That tells me something.","Manager: You rate yourself three out of ten. Does that match how you behave in the room?","Raj: Apparently not. The task showed me the truth."],"correct_diagnosis":"Not a valid self-assessment. Raj's low rating is produced by the difficulty of retrieving twelve instances, low fluency read as unexplained unavailability, not by his actual behavior. His earlier successes and his own account of leading pitches contradict the rating, and the chapter shows people who list twelve instances rate themselves less assertive than those who list six purely because retrieval got hard.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The behavioral log shows the telltale signature of the fluency effect: examples one through three flowed, then retrieval stalled, culminating in the brow-furrowing and sighs at the end, the exact pattern Schwarz and colleagues documented. Raj's own inference, that if it is hard to recall, he must not be that way, is the availability heuristic misreading retrieval difficulty as diagnostic content. Because he was asked for twelve rather than six instances, his low self-rating is an artifact of the task's difficulty, and his genuine assertive record, the pitches, stand-up, and scope-pushback, is the better evidence. The correct verdict is that the self-rating is biased, not that Raj is unassertive or that he is lying.","distractors":["Valid: the twelve hard-to-produce examples prove Raj truly lacks assertiveness","Deception: Raj is underrating himself strategically to lower next year's targets","Halo effect: Raj is flattering Mei by comparison because he likes her"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The CEO's Winning Streak: Confronting Availability-Driven Overconfidence","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom at Meridian Logistics where CEO Carol stands at a large screen showing a pipeline dashboard with zero contingency scenarios. Below, the board table is lined with water glasses and folders. A small confidence meter at the side of the screen reads high while a faint image of a failed acquisition fades in and out of the display.","player_mission":"Diagnose the source of the CEO's confidence in the sixth acquisition, then take the deciding vote to ratify or require a debiasing review before the deal is signed.","key_concepts":["After a series of successes, failure does not come easily to mind, and the availability bias makes the CEO overconfident.","People who go with the flow are more strongly affected by ease of retrieval than by the content they retrieved.","Reliance on ease of retrieval is amplified when people are engaged in another effortful task, in a good mood, low on depression, knowledgeable novices, high in faith in intuition, or feeling powerful.","Merely reminding people of a time when they had power increases their trust in their own intuition.","Faith in intuition is only in part a personality trait; situational power inflates it.","The availability heuristic judges frequency and risk by the ease with which instances come to mind.","A dramatic event temporarily increases the availability of its category, and personal experiences are more available than statistics.","Resisting availability biases requires making the effort to reconsider impressions and asking whether a belief is due to a few recent instances.","People who let themselves be guided by System 1 are more susceptible to availability biases than those in a state of higher vigilance.","Substitution of questions, reporting ease of retrieval instead of actual frequency, inevitably produces systematic errors."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests availability-driven overconfidence after a winning streak: because failure no longer comes easily to mind, the CEO's risk estimates rest on fluency rather than evidence. Kahneman notes the bias is amplified in people who feel powerful and trust their intuition, and the corrective is deliberate System 2 engagement, generating failure scenarios and consulting base rates.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is availability-driven overconfidence: after a run of successes, failure becomes hard to retrieve, so the CEO underestimates risk. The chapter shows that ease of retrieval dominates when people go with the flow, under cognitive load, good mood, high faith in intuition, or a feeling of power, and that the corrective is to force System 2 engagement and look at statistics rather than fluency.","stage_1_observation":"You are chair of the board of Meridian Logistics. CEO Carol has closed five acquisitions in a row, and her quarterly letter to shareholders glows. In the board meeting she waves off risk analysis for deal number six, saying failure does not come easily to her mind right now, and the room nods. On the screen behind her, the pipeline dashboard shows zero contingency scenarios for the deal she is about to sign.","stage_1_question":"Carol says failure does not come easily to mind and feels the sixth deal is near-riskless. What is driving her optimism, and how should you interpret it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Her optimism is driven by an availability bias: a sequence of recent successes makes failure instances hard to retrieve, so she judges risk by ease of recall rather than by statistics, and she is especially prone because she is powerful and trusts her intuition, conditions that amplify reliance on ease of retrieval.","stage_2_intervention":"Carol has signed off on the $90 million acquisition with no hedging and, when a director raises integration risk, replies that she does not spend a lot of time taking polls to tell her what is right, she just has to know how she feels. She asks the board to ratify the deal today. You hold the deciding vote. How do you respond?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Vote to force a debiasing review rather than ratify on intuition: require the team to deliberately generate failure scenarios and disconfirming evidence, ask whether the confidence is driven by a few recent wins, and compare the deal against base-rate statistics on similar acquisitions, mobilizing System 2 instead of deferring to Carol's fluent confidence."}]},{"id":"ch18","title":"13. Availability, Emotion, and Risk","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Affect Heuristic and the Benefit-Risk Inversion","visual_frame_description":"A product-safety boardroom. On screen, a marketing team presents a new food preservative to a skeptical compliance officer; above the table, three labeled columns float like inboxes. Cards showing risk judgments hover in the center, waiting to be sorted into the column that best explains them.","player_mission":"Sort each risk judgment into the column that best explains it. Read each card carefully and drag it to the affect heuristic, availability bias, or rational cost-benefit column based on the psychological mechanism the book describes.","key_concepts":["Slovic and his colleagues observed an implausibly high negative correlation between the benefits and the risks respondents attributed to technologies.","When people were favorably disposed toward a technology, they rated it as offering large benefits and imposing little risk.","When people disliked a technology, they could think only of its disadvantages, and few advantages came to mind.","Members of the British Toxicology Society responded similarly to laypeople, finding little benefit in substances they thought risky.","Respondents who received a message extolling a technology's benefits also lowered their estimates of its risks, although no risk evidence was given.","Ratings of risk and benefit corresponded even more closely when people rated them under time pressure.","The affect heuristic answers the easy question 'How do I feel about it?' in place of the harder question 'What do I think about it?'","Slovic related his views to Antonio Damasio's finding that people who lack the appropriate emotional signals, sometimes due to brain damage, make impaired decisions.","Jonathan Haidt's phrase 'The emotional tail wags the rational dog' captures how feelings steer evaluations of risk.","The affect heuristic simplifies life by creating a world in which good technologies have few costs and bad technologies have no benefits.","The affect heuristic is an instance of substitution, replacing a difficult judgment with an easier emotional one."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the affect heuristic: judgments about benefit and risk are made by consulting emotions such as 'Do I like it?' rather than by analyzing evidence, producing a strong negative correlation between perceived benefit and perceived risk. Slovic's research team documented this inversion across technologies, in laypeople and toxicologists alike, and under time pressure.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each risk judgment into the mechanism that best explains it. The focal card reads: 'A marketing team likes a new food preservative, so they rate its benefits high and its risks negligible.'","column_a_label":"Affect heuristic (emotion substitutes for analysis)","column_b_label":"Availability bias (ease of recall drives frequency estimates)","column_c_label":"Rational cost-benefit reasoning","rows":[{"label":"A marketing team likes a new food preservative, so they rate its benefits high and its risks negligible.","col":"a"},{"label":"After televised shark-attack coverage, a beach town overestimates shark-related deaths relative to drowning.","col":"b"},{"label":"A utility regulator compares the cost per life saved across safety programs before choosing one.","col":"c"},{"label":"Survey respondents who disliked water fluoridation rated it as risky with few benefits.","col":"a"},{"label":"A homeowner judged tornadoes a more frequent killer than asthma, although asthma causes 20 times more deaths.","col":"b"},{"label":"An insurance actuary prices flood policies from historical loss data rather than from recent headlines.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The team reasoned from formal statistical analysis of the preservative's safety data","The team judged risk by the ease of recalling recent food recalls, an availability effect","The team balanced the preservative's risk against its benefit in a deliberate tradeoff"],"explanation":"Liking a technology makes it feel high in benefit and low in risk even without supporting evidence, because the emotional evaluation substitutes for analysis. This mirrors Slovic's finding that benefit and risk ratings were implausibly negatively correlated, with toxicologists showing the same pattern as the public."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Availability Bias in Estimates of Causes of Death","visual_frame_description":"A public-health studio in a city health department. A data analyst stands before a wall of charts comparing the public's guesses about causes of death with official mortality statistics. Floating cards list survey findings, and three columns offer the candidate explanations.","player_mission":"Sort each statement into the mechanism it illustrates, using the findings of the Slovic, Lichtenstein, and Fischhoff surveys described in the chapter. Identify which judgments reflect media-warped availability, which reflect probability neglect, and which reflect sound statistical reasoning.","key_concepts":["Slovic, Lichtenstein, and Fischhoff asked people to compare pairs of causes of death, such as diabetes and asthma or stroke and accidents.","Strokes cause almost twice as many deaths as all accidents combined, yet 80% of respondents judged accidental death to be more likely.","Tornadoes were seen as more frequent killers than asthma, although asthma causes 20 times more deaths.","Death by lightning was judged less likely than death from botulism even though it is 52 times more frequent.","Death by disease is 18 times as likely as accidental death, but the two were judged about equally likely.","Accidental death was judged to be more than 300 times more likely than death from diabetes, but the true ratio is 1 to 4.","Estimates of causes of death are warped by media coverage, which is itself biased toward novelty and poignancy.","Unusual events such as botulism attract disproportionate attention and are consequently perceived as less unusual than they really are.","The estimates of causes of death are an almost direct representation of the activation of ideas in associative memory.","Frightening thoughts and images occur with particular ease, and fluent, vivid thoughts of danger exacerbate fear."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability bias in risk perception: people estimate the frequency of causes of death by the ease with which examples come to mind, so dramatic and media-covered causes are overestimated relative to health statistics. Slovic, Lichtenstein, and Fischhoff's surveys produced large systematic errors, such as judging accidents more likely killers than strokes.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each statement into the mechanism it illustrates. The focal card reads: 'Respondents judged accidental death more likely than stroke, although strokes kill almost twice as many people as all accidents combined.'","column_a_label":"Availability bias (media-warped frequency estimates)","column_b_label":"Probability neglect (concern insensitive to likelihood)","column_c_label":"Accurate statistical reasoning","rows":[{"label":"Respondents judged accidental death more likely than stroke, although strokes kill almost twice as many people as all accidents combined.","col":"a"},{"label":"Tornadoes were rated more frequent killers than asthma, which causes 20 times more deaths.","col":"a"},{"label":"Death by lightning was judged less likely than death from botulism, although lightning deaths are 52 times more frequent.","col":"a"},{"label":"A worried parent cannot sleep while a teenager is late, even while knowing the true risk is tiny.","col":"b"},{"label":"A regulator authorizes a ban because an image of harm is vivid, even though the probability of harm is negligibly small.","col":"b"},{"label":"A public-health analyst cites annual mortality tables when ranking prevention programs.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The stroke-versus-accident error reflects an accurate memory of news reports about car crashes","The pattern reflects rational weighting of each cause by its true death toll","The errors arise from a deliberate rejection of base-rate statistics"],"explanation":"The death-estimate errors track the availability of dramatic, emotionally vivid media coverage: unusual causes such as accidents, tornadoes, and botulism dominate memory, so their frequency is overestimated relative to the official statistics. The correct response for the focal card is the availability bias, since the error stems from ease of recall rather than from indifference to probability or accurate calculation."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Availability Cascade from News to Policy","visual_frame_description":"A newsroom map of a town. A small chemical-residue story on a corner screen is growing into headlines, tickers, and a crowd outside a courthouse. Three columns label the stages of an availability cascade, and cards describing events float between them.","player_mission":"Sort each event into the cascade stage it represents: media amplification, public emotional response, or political and policy response. Trace the self-sustaining chain from a minor report to large-scale government action.","key_concepts":["Kuran and Sunstein invented the term availability cascade for the mechanism through which biases flow into policy.","An availability cascade is a self-sustaining chain of events that may start from media reports of a relatively minor event and lead up to public panic and large-scale government action.","The importance of an idea is often judged by the fluency and emotional charge with which it comes to mind.","'Availability entrepreneurs' are individuals or organizations who work to ensure a continuous flow of worrying news.","The danger is increasingly exaggerated as the media compete for attention-grabbing headlines.","Scientists and others who try to dampen the increasing fear attract little attention, most of it hostile.","Anyone who claims that the danger is overstated is suspected of association with a 'heinous cover-up.'","The issue becomes politically important because it is on everyone's mind, and the response of the political system is guided by the intensity of public sentiment.","The availability cascade resets priorities, pushing other risks and other uses of resources into the background.","At Love Canal, daily stories, the ABC program The Killing Ground, and empty baby-size coffins paraded before the legislature sustained the cascade.","The Love Canal affair helped produce CERCLA, which established a Superfund for the cleanup of toxic sites.","In the Alar scare of 1989, the apple industry sustained large losses and the manufacturer withdrew the product after the FDA banned it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability cascade: a self-sustaining cycle in which media coverage of a minor threat inflames public worry, the worry generates more coverage, and policy responds to the intensity of sentiment rather than to the objective risk. Kuran and Sunstein coined the term to explain events such as Love Canal and the Alar scare, where regulation followed public emotion rather than careful cost-benefit analysis.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A minor chemical-residue story is snowballing into public panic. Sort each event into the cascade stage it represents. The focal card reads: 'A relatively minor event is reported, and the emotional reaction becomes a story in itself, prompting additional coverage.'","column_a_label":"Media amplification","column_b_label":"Public emotional response","column_c_label":"Political and policy response","rows":[{"label":"A relatively minor event is reported, and the emotional reaction becomes a story in itself, prompting additional coverage.","col":"a"},{"label":"'Availability entrepreneurs' work to ensure a continuous flow of worrying news.","col":"a"},{"label":"Scientists who say the danger is overstated are ignored or suspected of a cover-up.","col":"b"},{"label":"The issue becomes politically important because it is on everyone's mind.","col":"c"},{"label":"The political system responds to the intensity of public sentiment rather than to careful risk analysis.","col":"c"},{"label":"The panic results in large-scale government action, such as the Superfund legislation after Love Canal.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The cascade is best understood as rational Bayesian updating on new evidence","The media story reflects the objective magnitude of the danger","Public officials respond to the statistical base rate of harm"],"explanation":"In an availability cascade, a media report of a minor event arouses public worry, the worry becomes a story that prompts more coverage, and politicians eventually respond to the intensity of sentiment. The focal card is the amplification stage, because the emotional reaction being turned into further coverage is precisely the self-sustaining media loop Kuran and Sunstein described."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Probability Neglect and the Post-Disaster Insurance Cycle","visual_frame_description":"A bright living room after a river flood. An auditor sits across from Priya Sharma, a homeowner, with an official flood-probability chart on the table between them. A timed transcript panel records her words and gestures as she describes the disaster.","player_mission":"Audit Priya's reasoning about flood insurance and decide which mechanism explains her judgment. Weigh the vividness of her memories, her reaction to the probability figure, and her own forecast of future complacency.","key_concepts":["Howard Kunreuther noticed that availability effects help explain the pattern of insurance purchase and protective action after disasters.","After each significant earthquake, Californians are for a while diligent in purchasing insurance and adopting measures of protection and mitigation.","The memories of a disaster dim over time, and so do worry and diligence.","The dynamics of memory help explain the recurrent cycles of disaster, concern, and growing complacency.","Protective actions, whether by individuals or governments, are usually designed to be adequate to the worst disaster actually experienced.","Societies as long ago as pharaonic Egypt tracked the high-water mark of rivers and always prepared as if floods would not rise higher.","Images of a worse disaster than the one experienced do not come easily to mind.","The amount of concern is not adequately sensitive to the probability of harm; people imagine the numerator and not the denominator.","Sunstein coined the phrase 'probability neglect' to describe the pattern of ignoring probability in the face of vivid harm.","Concern is focused on the tragic story seen on the news rather than on the full set of cases the probability describes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests probability neglect and the availability-driven insurance cycle: concern about a hazard is driven by how vividly it comes to mind and is nearly insensitive to its true probability, and that concern fades as memories of the disaster decay. Kunreuther showed this dynamic explains why insurance purchases spike after earthquakes and decline until the next disaster.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After last winter's river flood, homeowner Priya Sharma must decide whether to renew her flood insurance. Audit her reasoning and identify the mechanism driving her judgment.","subject_baseline":"Priya is a careful civil engineer who tracks her budget, reads policy documents line by line, and in normal years decides insurance renewals on cost grounds alone.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-1:30: Priya describes the flood vividly, recounting water stains and the sound of the sirens; her voice speeds up and her hands gesture broadly.","1:30-4:00: Asked for the probability of another flood this decade, she pauses, looks away, and answers, 'Hard to imagine... but after last time, I don't want to risk it.'","4:00-6:00: Shown the official 1% annual flood-probability chart, she nods but immediately returns to describing the basement damage.","6:00-8:00: Asked about earthquake risk in her area, which is very low, she shrugs and says she never thinks about it."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'What is the chance of another serious flood in the next ten years?'","Priya: 'I honestly can't picture it happening again. But I also can't picture my basement like that again.'","Auditor: 'The chart says roughly a one-in-one-hundred annual chance. Does that change anything?'","Priya: 'Numbers like that don't feel real. What feels real is the smell and the cleanup.'","Auditor: 'Would you still buy insurance three years from now?'","Priya: 'Probably not. By then I'll have forgotten how bad it was.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Priya's insurance decision is driven by availability and probability neglect rather than by statistical risk: a vivid recent flood dominates her concern now, her concern is insensitive to the actual low probability, and her own forecast of forgetting matches the documented cycle of disaster, concern, and growing complacency.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The vivid narration and bodily animation show the recent disaster is maximally available in memory, inflating present concern; her dismissal of the 1% figure illustrates probability neglect, where concern fails to track likelihood; and her prediction that she will forget reflects the memory-decay mechanism Kunreuther described, in which worry and diligence fade as memories dim, recreating the complacency that precedes the next disaster.","distractors":["Priya is deliberately misrepresenting her risk to obtain a subsidy on her policy premium","Priya is a rational Bayesian who has correctly weighted the 1% base rate against her exposure","Priya's anxiety reflects an availability cascade driven by media coverage rather than by her own memory"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Affect Heuristic in Expert Risk Assessment","visual_frame_description":"A regulatory briefing room. Dr. Nadia Fontaine, a senior toxicologist, sits across from a regulator reviewing a food preservative. A live transcript panel and a body-language feed capture her posture, expression, and words during the session.","player_mission":"Audit whether Dr. Fontaine's risk assessment reflects evidence or the affect heuristic. Compare her emotional reactions, her benefit and risk ratings, and her response to contrary dose-response data.","key_concepts":["Slovic developed the notion of an affect heuristic, in which people make judgments and decisions by consulting their emotions.","In many domains people form opinions and make choices that directly express their feelings and their tendency to approach or avoid, often without knowing it.","The affect heuristic is an instance of substitution, in which the answer to an easy question serves as the answer to a harder one.","Slovic's team observed an implausibly high negative correlation between the benefits and risks people attributed to technologies.","When people disliked a technology, they could think only of its disadvantages, and few advantages came to mind.","Members of the British Toxicology Society responded similarly to laypeople, finding little benefit in substances they thought risky.","Because the technologies lined up neatly from good to bad, no painful tradeoffs needed to be faced.","The emotional tail wags the rational dog, in Jonathan Haidt's phrase, and the affect heuristic creates a world much tidier than reality.","Experts show many of the same biases as the rest of us in attenuated form.","People who received a message extolling the benefits of a technology also changed their beliefs about its risks, with no relevant evidence provided."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the affect heuristic in expert judgment: a feeling of dislike substitutes for analysis, so a toxicologist rates a disliked substance as high-risk and low-benefit and holds the verdict even when shown contrary evidence. The book reports that even members of the British Toxicology Society showed the same benefit-risk inversion as the general public.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dr. Nadia Fontaine, a senior toxicologist, is briefing a regulator on a food preservative. Audit whether her risk assessment reflects evidence or the affect heuristic.","subject_baseline":"Nadia is a rigorous scientist who publishes peer-reviewed toxicology studies and always demands dose-response data before opining on a chemical.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-2:00: When the preservative is named, Nadia's face tightens; she frowns, leans back, and crosses her arms before any data is shown.","2:00-5:00: Asked for the benefits, she lists none and changes the subject; asked for the risks, she speaks fluently about 'unnecessary additives.'","5:00-8:00: Presented a dose-response study showing negligible risk at realistic intake, she nods but repeats, 'I just don't like the idea of it.'","8:00-10:00: Asked to rate benefit on a scale of 1 to 10, she says '1'; asked for risk, she says '9', with no reference to any figure."],"interrogation_transcript":["Regulator: 'Can you quantify the risk at the approved concentration?'","Nadia: 'Risk at that level is negligible by the numbers, but I would not put it in my family's food.'","Regulator: 'So is the risk low or high?'","Nadia: 'Statistically low. I still think of it as dangerous. It feels wrong.'","Regulator: 'Is there any evidence of harm at realistic intake?'","Nadia: 'No. But I cannot find a benefit either, and that settles it for me.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Nadia's assessment is an affect heuristic rather than an evidence-based one: a feeling of dislike substitutes for analysis, so she rates the preservative as high-risk and low-benefit, and her verdict does not change when she is shown contrary dose-response data.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The immediate defensive posture and fluent litany of risks with no benefits mirror Slovic's finding that disliked technologies are judged high-risk and low-benefit with no painful tradeoffs; the book notes the parallel case of the British Toxicology Society responding like laypeople; and her admission that the statistics are negligible while her verdict stays dangerous shows the emotional tail wagging the rational dog.","distractors":["Nadia is deliberately concealing evidence of real harm to protect the chemical industry","Nadia's judgment reflects accurate Bayesian updating on the dose-response data","Nadia's response is driven by an availability cascade from media stories about additives"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Availability Cascade and Political Responsiveness","visual_frame_description":"A city council chamber mid-debate. Council member Leo Ortiz faces a staffer across a table covered with news clips, a toxicology report, and polling numbers. A transcript feed and a gesture tracker capture his reasoning in real time.","player_mission":"Audit whether council member Leo Ortiz is reasoning on the evidence or being carried along by an availability cascade. Examine what he cites as his basis for a ban and how he responds to probabilistic risk data.","key_concepts":["An availability cascade is a self-sustaining chain that may start from media reports of a relatively minor event and lead up to public panic and large-scale government action.","The importance of an idea is often judged by the fluency and emotional charge with which it comes to mind.","Lawmakers and regulators may be overly responsive to the irrational concerns of citizens, both because of political sensitivity and because they are prone to the same biases.","The danger is increasingly exaggerated as the media compete for attention-grabbing headlines.","The issue becomes politically important because it is on everyone's mind.","The response of the political system is guided by the intensity of public sentiment.","The availability cascade resets priorities, and other risks fade into the background.","In the Alar scare of 1989, the topic dominated the news and produced dramatic media events such as the testimony of the actress Meryl Streep before Congress.","In the Alar incident, the manufacturer withdrew the product and the FDA banned it, despite research showing only a possible very small risk.","Concern is not adequately sensitive to the probability of harm; people imagine the numerator, the tragic story seen on the news, and not the denominator."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests how an availability cascade reaches into policy: media coverage and public arousal set the agenda, and the political response is guided by the intensity of sentiment rather than by probabilistic analysis. The chapter's account of the Alar scare shows a minor possible risk producing a ban, large industry losses, and a broad overreaction.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Council member Leo Ortiz must decide whether to support a ban on a common growth-regulating spray after local news coverage. Audit his reasoning and identify the mechanism driving his decision.","subject_baseline":"Leo is a pragmatic politician who usually demands cost estimates and staff analysis before voting, and who previously opposed a similar ban for lack of evidence.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-2:00: Leo opens by replaying the news clip and calling the spray 'apples that poison kids'; his voice rises and he taps the table.","2:00-5:00: When a state toxicologist reports that only gigantic doses caused tumors in rats, Leo interrupts to ask about the actress who testified before Congress.","5:00-8:00: Shown a low probabilistic risk estimate, he waves it away: 'The public is scared. That is the reality I answer to.'","8:00-10:00: He recalls that after the last scare the product was withdrawn and the FDA banned it, and he offers that as proof of danger."],"interrogation_transcript":["Staffer: 'The evidence suggests the risk is minimal at realistic doses.'","Leo: 'My constituents saw the coverage. They called my office all week.'","Staffer: 'How many lives would the ban realistically protect?'","Leo: 'Doesn't matter. When everyone is talking about it, I have to act. Silence is political suicide.'","Staffer: 'Other risks with far higher death tolls receive no funding.'","Leo: 'They aren't on the news. Apples are.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Leo is being pulled along by an availability cascade: media coverage and public arousal set the political agenda, the intensity of public sentiment overrides probabilistic analysis, and his justification by recall of a prior scare repeats the cascade's logic rather than evaluating the evidence.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The cascade mechanics are on display: his decision cites public fear, news coverage, and the memory of a prior scare rather than the dose-response data; the book notes lawmakers and regulators may be overly responsive to irrational citizen concerns, both from political sensitivity and shared bias; and his 'silence is political suicide' line shows the political system responding to the intensity of sentiment, which is exactly how an availability cascade resets priorities.","distractors":["Leo is rationally weighting the expected number of lives saved by the ban","Leo is exhibiting the affect heuristic in his own liking for the spray's manufacturer","Leo is applying Bayes's rule to the toxicology evidence"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Expert versus Public: Risk Policy Priorities","visual_frame_description":"A federal agency briefing room. A senior EPA advisor stands before a wall screen showing viral clips of a chemical scare, celebrity testimony, and a proposed ban. Two advisors on either side gesture with contrasting briefing binders, one marked Sunstein and one marked Slovic.","player_mission":"Guide the agency administrator through the experts-versus-public debate. Recognize the availability cascade and the affect heuristic at work, then craft a policy position that addresses both the legitimate pain of public fear and the need for broad, evidence-based priorities.","key_concepts":["Sunstein defends the role of experts as a bulwark against populist excesses in risk regulation.","Biased reactions to risks are an important source of erratic and misplaced priorities in public policy.","Slovic argues that there is no such thing as 'real risk' or 'objective risk' waiting to be measured.","The public draws finer distinctions than experts, such as between 'good deaths' and 'bad deaths.'","Widespread fears, even if unreasonable, should not be ignored by policy makers, because fear is painful and debilitating.","Availability cascades may have a long-term benefit by calling attention to classes of risks and increasing the risk-reduction budget.","Psychology should inform the design of risk policies that combine the experts' knowledge with the public's emotions and intuitions.","Lawmakers and regulators may be overly responsive to the irrational concerns of citizens, both because of political sensitivity and because they share the same biases."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the expert-versus-public debate over risk policy: Sunstein defends expert insulation against availability-driven populism, while Slovic insists that risk is not objective and that experts must respect the public's richer conception of risk and its real fears. Kahneman sides with both, concluding that policy should combine expert knowledge with the public's emotions and intuitions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests whether a risk regulator can resist the affect heuristic and availability cascade while respecting legitimate public fears, the core tension between Sunstein's expert-centered view and Slovic's public-centered view.","stage_1_observation":"You are a senior advisor to the Environmental Protection Agency. A media-driven scare over a widely used growth-regulating chemical has erupted: viral clips, a celebrity testifying before Congress, and empty baby-size coffins at a hearing. You must brief the administrator on how to set the agency's priorities.","stage_1_question":"What should your opening recommendation be about how the agency should weight the public's fear?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Acknowledge the fear as real and painful while insisting that resource allocation be guided by a broad comparison of risks, because policy must both protect the public from fear and avoid erratic priorities: the book's lesson is that fear, rational or not, is painful and must not be ignored, but availability cascades distort the allocation of public resources.","stage_2_intervention":"The administrator asks you to draft the decision memo. Two factions press you: one quotes Sunstein and demands the decision be made by impartial experts insulated from public pressure; the other quotes Slovic and warns that expert-imposed priorities the public rejects are untenable in a democracy. A third group suggests quietly feeding the public reassuring statistics to calm the panic.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Draft a memo that combines the experts' broad risk assessment with explicit respect for the public's emotions and intuitions, and decline both pure insulation and fear-based capitulation, because the book concludes that psychology should inform risk policies that combine expert knowledge with public emotions, and that insulating experts entirely produces policies the public will reject."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Defusing an Availability Cascade","visual_frame_description":"A small-town communications office in crisis. A wall of monitors shows a single food-safety incident exploding into national headlines, with a red 'panic meter' climbing. The mayor's phone glows with angry callers, and a reporter with a camera presses at the door.","player_mission":"Diagnose the situation as an availability cascade and then choose a response that breaks the self-sustaining loop. Recognize probability neglect in the public's demands and craft communication that addresses the fear without sensationalizing the risk.","key_concepts":["An availability cascade is a self-sustaining chain that may start from media reports of a relatively minor event and lead to public panic.","The emotional reaction becomes a story in itself, prompting additional coverage and greater concern.","The danger is increasingly exaggerated as the media compete for attention-grabbing headlines.","Anyone who claims the danger is overstated is suspected of association with a 'heinous cover-up.'","The amount of concern is not adequately sensitive to the probability of harm; people imagine the numerator and not the denominator.","Sunstein coined the phrase 'probability neglect' to describe the pattern.","The Alar incident was an enormous overreaction to a minor problem, and its net effect on public health was probably detrimental.","The combination of probability neglect with availability cascades leads to gross exaggeration of minor threats."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the player's ability to recognize and interrupt an availability cascade, in which media amplification and public arousal inflate a minor risk into a policy panic. The chapter documents how such cascades, as at Love Canal and with Alar, reset public priorities, and how probability neglect makes concern insensitive to the true likelihood of harm.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests whether the player can diagnose and interrupt an availability cascade, a self-sustaining media-fear-policy loop, by applying probability neglect and base-rate awareness.","stage_1_observation":"You are the communications director for a small town after a single dramatic food-safety incident made the national news. The story is dominating coverage, angry callers flood the mayor's office, and rival reporters compete for ever-more-alarming angles. Local officials are about to ban a harmless ingredient.","stage_1_question":"What is the defining feature of the situation you should first identify?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It is an availability cascade: a minor event has been inflated by media competition and public arousal into a self-sustaining loop in which the intensity of sentiment, not the probability of harm, is driving decisions.","stage_2_intervention":"The mayor asks for your recommendation. A reporter offers to run a 'balance' story claiming the danger is vastly overstated, but the FDA warns that public reassurance may backfire. Meanwhile, an 'availability entrepreneur' group is organizing a rally. What should you do?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Provide accurate, low-anxiety information that addresses the probability directly and refuses to sensationalize, while acknowledging the fear, because the book shows that scientists who try to dampen the fear attract little attention and are suspected of cover-ups, so reassurance must be persistent, honest, and grounded in the denominator rather than the vivid numerator."}]},{"id":"ch19","title":"14. Tom W's Specialty","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Representativeness versus Base Rates in Prediction","visual_frame_description":"A university career-counseling office. A stack of Tom W-style personality sketches sits on the desk, and three columns float above it: prediction by representativeness, pure base-rate reasoning, and Bayesian combination. Cards describing judgments drift between the columns.","player_mission":"Sort each judgment into the mechanism it reflects. Distinguish similarity-based prediction from base-rate reasoning and from a Bayesian combination of the two, using the Tom W experiments as your guide.","key_concepts":["In the Tom W problem, participants ranked nine fields by how similar the description was to the stereotype of each field.","The participants' rankings of the nine fields by probability did not differ from their ratings by similarity to the stereotype.","Substitution was perfect: there was no indication that the participants did anything but judge representativeness.","Tom W was designed as an 'anti-base-rate' character, a good fit to small fields and a poor fit to the most populated specialties.","The correct answer is to stay very close to your prior beliefs, slightly reducing the high probabilities of well-populated fields and slightly raising the low probabilities of rare specialties.","The representativeness heuristic is involved when someone says 'She will win the election; you can see she is a winner.'","A question about probability or likelihood activates a mental shotgun, evoking easier questions, including an automatic assessment of representativeness.","Prediction by representativeness is not statistically optimal.","In the absence of specific information, using base rates is the obvious move when no other information is provided.","Base rates matter even in the presence of evidence about the case at hand.","When the evidence is weak, one should stick with the base rates."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests prediction by representativeness, in which the similarity of a description to a stereotype substitutes for a probability judgment. Kahneman and Tversky's Tom W studies showed that even statistically trained participants ranked outcomes by similarity, perfectly substituting representativeness for likelihood and neglecting base rates.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A hiring committee debates a candidate. Sort each judgment into the mechanism it reflects. The focal card reads: 'The committee predicts the quiet, tidy applicant will be a great librarian even though the city employs almost no librarians.'","column_a_label":"Prediction by representativeness","column_b_label":"Pure base-rate reasoning","column_c_label":"Bayesian combination of base rate and evidence","rows":[{"label":"The committee predicts the quiet, tidy applicant will be a great librarian even though the city employs almost no librarians.","col":"a"},{"label":"A recruiter says, 'She will win the election; you can see she is a winner.'","col":"a"},{"label":"With no information about an applicant, HR guesses the field with the largest enrollment.","col":"b"},{"label":"An analyst estimates that, given a 3% base rate and evidence four times more likely under one hypothesis, the posterior belief is 11%.","col":"c"},{"label":"A scout rejects a promising batter because he does not look the part.","col":"a"},{"label":"A judge sets the probability that an event occurred equal to the base rate because the evidence is worthless.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The librarian prediction is a rational use of the candidate's job-market statistics","The prediction reflects WYSIATI only, with no stereotype involved","The committee combined the candidate's similarity with the relevant base rates"],"explanation":"Predicting the probability that Tom W studies a field by how similar his description is to the field's stereotype is prediction by representativeness. The Tom W studies showed participants' probability rankings did not differ from their similarity ratings, so the focal card sorts to the representativeness column: the description fits a stereotype, while the tiny base rate is ignored."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"WYSIATI and the Quality of Evidence","visual_frame_description":"A residency-program admissions office. A director holds a glowing but unverified letter of recommendation under a desk lamp, while three columns show how the evidence can be handled: as all there is, with appropriate skepticism, or by anchoring on base rates. Cards describing reactions hover above.","player_mission":"Sort each handling of the evidence into the correct column. Determine which reactions reflect WYSIATI, which reflect appropriate skepticism about evidence quality, and which anchor on base rates.","key_concepts":["WYSIATI is insensitivity to the quality of evidence.","Unless you decide immediately to reject evidence, your System 1 will automatically process the information available as if it were true.","Worthless information should not be treated differently from a complete lack of information, but WYSIATI makes that principle very difficult to apply.","The Tom W description was written from psychological tests of uncertain validity, yet readers treated its claims as reliable.","When you have doubts about the quality of the evidence, you should let your judgments of probability stay close to the base rate.","Staying close to the base rate requires a significant effort of self-monitoring and self-control.","The statement that Tom W has little feel for people was probably enough to convince most readers that he is unlikely to study social science.","The combination of WYSIATI and associative coherence tends to make us believe in the stories we spin for ourselves.","One thing you can do with dubious evidence is reject it immediately, for example by determining that you received it from a liar."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests insensitivity to the quality of evidence, the WYSIATI principle: System 1 treats whatever information is present as if it were true and complete, so unreliable evidence carries the same weight as reliable evidence. In the Tom W problem, readers trusted a description explicitly drawn from tests of uncertain validity, and the corrective is to anchor on base rates and question the diagnosticity of evidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A residency director weighs a letter of recommendation. Sort each handling of the evidence into the correct column. The focal card reads: 'The director treats a glowing letter from an unknown mentor as strong proof the candidate will succeed, ignoring how rarely such letters are reliable.'","column_a_label":"WYSIATI (treating available evidence as all there is)","column_b_label":"Appropriate skepticism about evidence quality","column_c_label":"Base-rate anchoring","rows":[{"label":"The director treats a glowing, low-reliability letter as strong proof the candidate will succeed.","col":"a"},{"label":"Because the description came from tests of uncertain validity, the reader discounts it and stays near the base rates.","col":"b"},{"label":"A recruiter who knows most applicants are rejected still lets one strong interview swing the decision to accept.","col":"a"},{"label":"An admissions dean first asks, 'What share of applicants is accepted here?' before judging any file.","col":"c"},{"label":"A manager immediately discards an anonymous complaint because it cannot be verified, treating it as no information.","col":"b"},{"label":"A voter assumes a candidate's confident speech reveals competence without checking any record.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Discarding a letter because it cannot be trusted is the same as ignoring the base rate","WYSIATI means people always verify the quality of their evidence","Treating a glowing letter as strong proof is a Bayesian combination of base rate and evidence"],"explanation":"Under WYSIATI, System 1 processes the available description as true unless it is immediately rejected as a lie, so the director treats the glowing letter as decisive despite its low reliability. The focal card sorts to the WYSIATI column because the available evidence is treated as if it were all there is, exactly the pattern that led readers to trust Tom W's untrustworthy sketch."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"System 2 Vigilance: Ignorance versus Laziness","visual_frame_description":"A psychology laboratory classroom. Half the students are puffing out their cheeks and half are frowning while ranking a Tom W-style description. A column chart beside them divides predictive errors into ignorance, laziness, and successful System 2 vigilance.","player_mission":"Sort each case into the correct column, using the frowning experiment and the Schwarz findings to decide whether an error reflects ignorance of base rates, laziness in applying them, or successful System 2 vigilance.","key_concepts":["When an incorrect intuitive judgment is made, System 1 suggested the incorrect intuition and System 2 endorsed it and expressed it in a judgment.","There are two possible reasons for the failure of System 2: ignorance or laziness.","Some people ignore base rates because they believe them to be irrelevant in the presence of individual information.","Others make the same mistake because they are not focused on the task.","If frowning makes a difference, laziness seems to be the proper explanation of base-rate neglect, at least among Harvard undergrads.","The students who puffed out their cheeks replicated the original results, relying exclusively on representativeness and ignoring the base rates.","The frowners did show some sensitivity to the base rates.","Frowning generally increases the vigilance of System 2 and reduces both overconfidence and the reliance on intuition.","Their System 2 knows that base rates are relevant even when they are not explicitly mentioned, but applies that knowledge only when it invests special effort in the task.","Instructing people to 'think like a statistician' enhanced the use of base-rate information, while 'think like a clinician' had the opposite effect."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the laziness-versus-ignorance account of base-rate neglect: when an intuitive error occurs, a vigilant System 2 can correct it, and the failure reflects laziness when people know the base rate but do not apply it. The Harvard frowning experiment showed that raising System 2 vigilance restored sensitivity to base rates, implicating laziness rather than ignorance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Predictive errors can reflect ignorance or laziness in System 2, or can be prevented by vigilance. Sort each case into the correct column. The focal card reads: 'A Harvard student told to puff out his cheeks relies exclusively on representativeness.'","column_a_label":"Ignorance (does not know base rates apply)","column_b_label":"Laziness (knows but does not apply the base rate)","column_c_label":"System 2 vigilance (resisting the intuition)","rows":[{"label":"A Harvard student told to puff out his cheeks relies exclusively on representativeness.","col":"b"},{"label":"A student told to frown shows some sensitivity to the base rates.","col":"c"},{"label":"A novice who has never heard of base rates predicts from the vivid description.","col":"a"},{"label":"Instructed to 'think like a statistician,' a person makes more use of base-rate information.","col":"c"},{"label":"A person convinced the base rate is irrelevant whenever individual information exists.","col":"a"},{"label":"A manager who knows most start-ups fail but is too distracted to apply that fact when funding a charismatic founder.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Cheek-puffing students ignore the base rate because they have never heard of it","Frowning reduces vigilance and increases reliance on intuition","The instruction to think like a clinician increases the use of base rates"],"explanation":"The cheek-puffing group reproduced the original Tom W results, relying exclusively on representativeness, while frowning restored some sensitivity to base rates by raising System 2 vigilance. Because these students demonstrably know base rates are relevant, the focal case reflects laziness rather than ignorance: System 2 endorses the intuition unless it makes a special effort."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Prediction by Representativeness in Talent Evaluation","visual_frame_description":"A baseball front office. Veteran scout Marcus Reed sits across from the general manager, a clipboard of performance statistics between them and a highlight reel on the wall. A transcript feed captures their exchange about an 'unathletic-looking' prospect.","player_mission":"Audit scout Marcus Reed's evaluation of a prospect and identify the mechanism driving his forecast. Compare his reliance on appearance, his dismissal of performance statistics, and his confidence in the prediction.","key_concepts":["Moneyball is a story about the inefficiency of prediction by representativeness in baseball scouting.","Professional baseball scouts traditionally forecast the success of players in part by their build and look.","Billy Beane, the manager of the Oakland A's, overruled his scouts and selected players by the statistics of past performance.","The players the A's picked were inexpensive because other teams had rejected them for not looking the part.","We rely on representativeness when we judge potential leadership by the shape of a chin or the forcefulness of a speech.","Judging probability by representativeness has important virtues, but exclusive reliance on it is associated with grave sins against statistical logic.","Prediction by representativeness is not statistically optimal.","The representativeness heuristic substitutes a judgment of similarity for a probability assessment.","Even when the heuristic has some validity, it misleads when it causes people to neglect base-rate information that points in another direction."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests prediction by representativeness in talent evaluation: a scout forecasts success from how well a player's build and manner match a stereotype rather than from the statistical record. The Moneyball example shows that players who do not 'look the part' are systematically undervalued, and that selecting on past performance yields excellent results at low cost.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"General manager Andre Silva is building a team on a small budget. Audit scout Marcus Reed's player evaluation and identify the mechanism driving his forecast.","subject_baseline":"Marcus is a veteran scout with twenty years of amateur baseball experience who values intangibles and the look of a player; he has never read a sabermetrics report.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-2:00: Marcus watches a compact, deliberate prospect and shakes his head; he says nothing about statistics.","2:00-5:00: Asked about the player's on-base percentage, Marcus shrugs and gestures at his build: 'He doesn't look like a major leaguer.'","5:00-8:00: Shown that similar 'unathletic-looking' players have outperformed flashier ones, he nods politely but returns to the player's swing mechanics.","8:00-10:00: He confidently predicts the prospect will not reach the majors, citing the player's lack of size."],"interrogation_transcript":["GM: 'His numbers are excellent. What's the problem?'","Marcus: 'He doesn't look the part. I've seen a thousand hitters. He won't go far.'","GM: 'The data says players with his profile succeed at twice the average rate.'","Marcus: 'Data can't watch him run. I trust what I see.'","GM: 'Last year you said the same about three players who made the majors.'","Marcus: 'Those were flukes. This guy is not a big leaguer.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Marcus is predicting by representativeness: he forecasts success from how well the player's build and manner match his stereotype of a major leaguer, ignoring the statistical record, the very inefficiency the Moneyball approach was built to exploit.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His evaluation relies entirely on physical appearance and the look, the trait-based forecasting that led scouts to reject undervalued players in Moneyball; his dismissal of the statistical profile despite its strong record shows base-rate neglect, and his overconfidence despite past misses reflects representativeness' disregard for evidence quality and base rates.","distractors":["Marcus is rationally weighting the player's limited power and swing mechanics","Marcus is displaying the affect heuristic, judging the player by liking rather than evidence","Marcus is applying Bayes's rule to the player's performance statistics"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Trusting Unreliable Evidence","visual_frame_description":"A corporate HR office. Hiring director Celeste Nguyen holds a personality sketch written from tests of uncertain validity, nodding as she reads. A transcript feed captures her conversation with a hiring coach about how much the sketch should count.","player_mission":"Audit whether Celeste's use of the personality sketch reflects WYSIATI-based trust in unreliable evidence or appropriate skepticism. Test her reaction to the sketch, to the warning about its validity, and to the correct discipline of staying near the base rates.","key_concepts":["In the Tom W example, what activates the associative machinery is a description of Tom, which may or may not be an accurate portrayal.","The statement that Tom W has little feel and little sympathy for people was probably enough to convince most readers that he is very unlikely to study social science.","You were explicitly told that the description of Tom W should not be trusted.","Unless you decide immediately to reject evidence, your System 1 will automatically process the information available as if it were true.","Worthless information should not be treated differently from a complete lack of information, but WYSIATI makes it very difficult to apply that principle.","There is one thing you can do when you have doubts about the quality of the evidence: let your judgments of probability stay close to the base rate.","Staying close to the base rate requires a significant effort of self-monitoring and self-control.","The combination of WYSIATI and associative coherence tends to make us believe in the stories we spin for ourselves.","The description of Tom W was written from psychological tests of uncertain validity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests WYSIATI-based insensitivity to the quality of evidence: a description drawn from tests of uncertain validity is processed as true unless immediately rejected. In the Tom W problem, readers trusted exactly such a sketch, and the correct discipline is to stay close to the base rate and question the diagnosticity of the evidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Hiring director Celeste Nguyen is selecting a project manager using a personality sketch written by a former manager from tests of uncertain validity. Audit her use of the evidence.","subject_baseline":"Celeste is a disciplined hiring manager who runs structured interviews and checks references before offers; she keeps a rubric and dislikes snap judgments.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-2:00: She reads the sketch aloud, noting 'needs order and clarity... little sympathy for others,' and nods; her expression relaxes into certainty.","2:00-5:00: Asked how reliable the sketch is, she says, 'It was written by a manager, so fairly reliable,' without checking.","5:00-8:00: Told the tests behind the sketch are of uncertain validity, she pauses briefly, then continues treating the traits as established facts.","8:00-10:00: She predicts the candidate will be difficult in team settings, citing only the sketch."],"interrogation_transcript":["Coach: 'How sure are you the sketch describes him accurately?'","Celeste: 'It says what he's like. I'm going to run with it.'","Coach: 'It was derived from psychological tests of uncertain validity.'","Celeste: 'Still, it's the only real information I have about his personality.'","Coach: 'Should a possibly worthless description change your hiring decision?'","Celeste: 'I can't just ignore it. It's in front of me, so it counts.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Celeste is committing WYSIATI-based insensitivity to the quality of evidence: she processes a sketch of uncertain validity as if it were true, cannot discount it even after being told it is untrustworthy, and lets it dominate her prediction, exactly the pattern shown in the Tom W problem.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her certainty is built entirely from a description she was warned is unreliable; the book's WYSIATI rule says that unless evidence is immediately rejected as a lie, System 1 processes it as true; her statement that 'it's the only real information' reveals the failure to discount worthless evidence, and the correct discipline is to stay close to the base rate and question diagnosticity.","distractors":["Celeste is correctly weighting the sketch as Bayesian evidence for the candidate's fit","Celeste's prediction is driven by an availability cascade from recent team conflicts","Celeste is rationally combining the sketch with structured interview data"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Predicting Rare Events from Weak Evidence","visual_frame_description":"A venture capital meeting room. Partner Jonah Weiss leans in over a founder's profile, a market-data deck beside him showing a brutal industry success rate. A transcript feed captures his pitch to the rest of the partnership.","player_mission":"Audit Jonah's prediction about the founder and identify the mechanism driving it. Compare his vivid impression of the founder, the industry base rate he admits to, and his confidence despite past failures.","key_concepts":["One sin of representativeness is an excessive willingness to predict the occurrence of unlikely, low-base-rate events.","If you see a person reading The New York Times on the New York subway, you should seriously consider that she does not have a college degree, because many more nongraduates than PhDs ride the subways.","Even if every female student of Chinese literature is shy and loves poetry, there are more bashful poetry lovers in the much larger population of business students.","The correct answer to the Tom W puzzle is to stay very close to your prior beliefs.","Prediction by representativeness is not statistically optimal.","Intuitive impressions of the diagnosticity of evidence are often exaggerated.","Base rates matter, even in the presence of evidence about the case at hand.","When the evidence is weak, one should stick with the base rates.","The combination of WYSIATI and associative coherence tends to make us believe in the stories we spin for ourselves."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the sin of predicting rare events from weak evidence: a vivid description that fits a stereotype is treated as strong grounds for betting on a low-base-rate outcome. The book's subway example shows that because the base rate of PhDs among subway readers is tiny, the safer bet is often the statistically common alternative.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Venture partner Jonah Weiss must decide whether to fund a founder described as 'brilliant, obsessive, a born leader.' Audit his prediction.","subject_baseline":"Jonah is a savvy investor who usually demands market data and traction metrics, and he has lost money on two previous 'obvious winner' founders.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-2:00: Reading the founder's profile, Jonah smiles and leans in; he already sounds convinced.","2:00-5:00: Asked about the industry's success rate, he says 'low, like 5%,' but immediately adds, 'this one is different.'","5:00-8:00: Shown that 95% of similar start-ups fail, he nods and still proposes a large check, citing the founder's forcefulness.","8:00-10:00: He describes his gut feeling as 'I just know this guy.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Partner: 'The base rate here is brutal. What changes the odds?'","Jonah: 'Look at him. He's a natural leader. You can see it.'","Partner: 'Every founder we passed on looked like a leader too.'","Jonah: 'But this one fits the profile of a winner.'","Partner: 'The profile you used for the last two losers?'","Jonah: 'Those were flukes. This is the real one.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Jonah is committing the sin of predicting a rare, low-base-rate event from representativeness: he bets on a description that fits his stereotype of a winner while neglecting that almost all ventures in the class fail, and he stays overconfident despite his own track record.","diagnostic_breakdown":"This mirrors the book's subway example, where a person reading The New York Times is more likely to lack a college degree than to hold a PhD because of the base rates; Jonah acknowledges the low base rate but lets the vivid profile override it, exaggerating the diagnosticity of his evidence and forecasting a low-base-rate event from weak evidence, the error the book's 'stick with the base rates' advice targets.","distractors":["Jonah is rationally adjusting the base rate upward for this exceptional founder","Jonah's confidence reflects a well-calibrated expert intuition in venture investing","Jonah is using the outside view to anchor his prediction on industry statistics"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Bayesian Discipline: Anchoring and Diagnosticity","visual_frame_description":"A university career-counseling office. A counselor faces a Tom W-style personality sketch on a screen, with a base-rate bar chart of graduate enrollments on one side and a Bayes's-rule equation on the other. A dean presses for a confident headline answer.","player_mission":"Apply disciplined Bayesian reasoning to predict a student's most likely field. Anchor on the base rates, question the diagnosticity of the unreliable sketch, and resist the pressure to predict by representativeness.","key_concepts":["The correct answer to the Tom W puzzle is to stay very close to your prior beliefs.","Bayes's rule combines prior beliefs, the base rates, with the diagnosticity of the evidence.","Base rates matter even in the presence of evidence about the case at hand.","Intuitive impressions of the diagnosticity of evidence are often exaggerated.","Anchor your judgment on a plausible base rate and question the diagnosticity of your evidence.","With a 3% base rate and evidence 4 times more likely under the hypothesis, the posterior belief is 11%.","The combination of WYSIATI and associative coherence makes us believe the stories we spin for ourselves.","Staying close to the base rate requires a significant effort of self-monitoring and self-control."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the two keys to disciplined Bayesian reasoning the chapter sets out: anchor your judgment on a plausible base rate and question the diagnosticity of your evidence. Kahneman shows that base-rate neglect and exaggerated diagnosticity combine under WYSIATI to make people over-trust vivid descriptions, and that correcting them requires effortful System 2 self-monitoring.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests disciplined Bayesian prediction: anchor on a plausible base rate and question the diagnosticity of the evidence instead of predicting purely by representativeness.","stage_1_observation":"A university career counselor, you must predict the most likely field for a new graduate student based only on a personality sketch written from tests of uncertain validity. The student's description fits a small, prestigious specialty's stereotype, but enrollment in that specialty is tiny compared with large fields such as humanities and education.","stage_1_question":"How should you set your initial probability estimates for the candidate's field?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Anchor on the base rates, keeping the large fields most likely and the small specialties unlikely, because with no trustworthy information your estimate should stay close to prior enrollment proportions, slightly adjusting them for whatever modest evidence exists.","stage_2_intervention":"A colleague insists the candidate is 'obviously' the rare specialty because the description matches the stereotype perfectly, and the dean wants a confident headline prediction. The description came from tests of uncertain validity and includes stereotype-planted hints. What do you do?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Resist prediction by representativeness: keep the posterior close to the base rates, explicitly downgrade the weight of the unreliable description, and present a hedged, base-rate-anchored estimate, since the book shows even statistically trained judges substitute similarity for probability and must exert deliberate self-monitoring to correct it."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Sins of Representativeness in Hiring","visual_frame_description":"A corporate hiring-committee room. Chair Dana Whitfield faces two candidate folders, a delivery-history spreadsheet for Samuel and a glowing interview note for Priya. Two senior engineers argue across the table as a base-rate chart of the applicant pool glows on the wall.","player_mission":"Steer the committee away from representativeness in a consequential hiring decision. Anchor on the base rates, separate reliable from unreliable evidence, and resist predictions of a rare successful hire from a charismatic impression.","key_concepts":["One sin of representativeness is an excessive willingness to predict unlikely, low-base-rate events.","The second sin of representativeness is insensitivity to the quality of evidence.","Base rates matter even in the presence of evidence about the case at hand.","Intuitive impressions of the diagnosticity of evidence are often exaggerated.","When the evidence is weak, one should stick with the base rates.","The representativeness heuristic is involved when judging a candidate by the forcefulness of speeches or the shape of a chin.","An anchor on a plausible base rate and questioning the diagnosticity of evidence are the essential keys to disciplined reasoning.","System 2 knows base rates are relevant but applies that knowledge only when it invests special effort."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests applying the chapter's corrections in a hiring context: predictions should be anchored on base rates and driven by the diagnosticity of reliable evidence, not by representativeness. The book's guidance that base rates matter even with individual evidence, and that impressions of diagnosticity are exaggerated, is the weapon the player must wield against committee intuition.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the player's ability to avoid the two sins of representativeness in a consequential hiring decision: predicting a rare event from weak evidence and being insensitive to the quality of evidence.","stage_1_observation":"You chair a hiring committee for a single software role. The finalist, Priya Rao, has an average portfolio but a charismatic interview; a rival candidate, Samuel, has a mediocre interview but strong, verifiable delivery history. The hiring pool is about 3% experienced developers and 97% junior applicants. Committee members say Priya 'looks like a lead.'","stage_1_question":"What should your first move as chair be?","stage_1_correct_answer":"State the base rates explicitly and separate the reliability of each piece of evidence, because committee judgments by 'look' are representativeness, the verifiable delivery history has genuine diagnosticity, and the charismatic impression is weak evidence that should be heavily discounted.","stage_2_intervention":"A senior engineer argues Priya's strong interview 'proves' she is the leader they need, and two members echo that a candidate who looks the part rarely fails. The deliverable-based assessment says Samuel outperforms on the exact skills the role requires, but the committee leans toward Priya. What do you do?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Override the representativeness-based intuition and choose on the base rate and the stronger evidence: anchor on the low experienced-developer base rate, weight the verifiable delivery history, and warn the committee that predicting a rare successful hire from a charismatic impression is the classic sin of predicting low-base-rate events from weak evidence."}]},{"id":"ch20","title":"15. Linda: Less is More","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Representativeness, Logic, and Plausibility in Probability Judgments","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen evidence board in a bright data-analysis office. On the left, three labeled columns await sorted cards: 'Representativeness', 'Logical Inference', and 'Plausibility Substitution'. In the center, Priya, a 34-year-old senior risk analyst in a navy blazer, circles two forecast scenarios on a whiteboard, a broad 'North America flood' and a detailed 'California earthquake causing a flood'. On the right, a stack of gray evidence cards shows judgment snippets from the Linda studies and the forecast experiments.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column it best illustrates, then classify Priya's forecast judgment. Read the label on every card and decide whether it reflects a similarity judgment, a logical-inclusion inference, or a substitution of plausibility for probability.","key_concepts":["The Linda problem pits the intuition of representativeness against the logic of probability, because a conjunction can never be more probable than one of its components.","Every feminist bank teller is a bank teller, so the probability that Linda is a feminist bank teller must be lower than the probability that she is a bank teller.","Specifying a possible event in greater detail can only lower its probability, because a more specific event is a subset of a broader one.","Judgments of probability in the Linda problem corresponded precisely to judgments of representativeness, that is, to similarity to a stereotype.","The notions of coherence, plausibility, and probability are easily confused, and the uncritical substitution of plausibility for probability corrupts scenario-based forecasting.","A detailed scenario (an earthquake in California causing a flood) was judged more probable than a broad one (a massive flood in North America), although the detailed event is strictly less probable.","The outcomes 'Mark has blond hair' and 'Jane is a teacher and walks to work' produce no fallacy, because the added detail is only detail, not extra plausibility.","System 2 is not impressively alert: respondents who knew the logic of Venn diagrams still failed to apply it when the conjunction simply appeared plausible.","Representativeness belongs to a cluster of closely related basic assessments that are generated together, so resemblance, coherence, and likelihood judgments tend to move as one."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the conjunction fallacy and the substitution of plausibility for probability: System 1 endorses the story that feels most coherent, so a detailed conjunction is treated as more likely than the broader event that contains it. Kahneman and Tversky showed that 85% to 90% of undergraduates violated the logic of the Linda problem, and that richer forecast scenarios were rated more probable despite being strictly less likely.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya, a senior risk analyst, is evaluating two scenarios for next year: 'a massive flood somewhere in North America in which more than 1,000 people drown' and 'an earthquake in California causing a flood in which more than 1,000 people drown.' She rates the California earthquake scenario as more probable. Study the sorted rows, then classify which column her judgment belongs in.","column_a_label":"Representativeness judgment (similarity to a stereotype)","column_b_label":"Logical inference (inclusion and sum-like variables)","column_c_label":"Plausibility substitution (detail taken for probability)","rows":[{"label":"Audience members judge that Linda, the philosophy-majoring antinuclear activist, is a very good fit for an 'active feminist'.","col":"a"},{"label":"Every feminist bank teller is a bank teller, so the probability of 'feminist bank teller' cannot exceed the probability of 'bank teller'.","col":"b"},{"label":"Forecasters rate the detailed California earthquake flood scenario as more probable than the broader North America flood, though detail can only lower probability.","col":"c"},{"label":"The detailed outcome 'Jane is a teacher and walks to work' is judged no more probable than 'Jane is a teacher,' because the extra detail adds no plausibility.","col":"b"},{"label":"Linda looks more like a 'feminist bank teller' than like the stereotypical bank teller, a purely resemblance-based assessment.","col":"a"},{"label":"A committee prizes a richly detailed disaster scenario above a sparser one without checking the subset relation, confusing a good story with a high probability.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Priya is correctly applying the inclusion rule because California is a part of North America.","Priya is using a causal base rate about earthquakes in California to update her estimate.","Priya is anchoring her probability estimate on the number 1,000 drownings."],"explanation":"Priya's rating is the forecasting trap the book describes: adding detail (an earthquake in California) makes a scenario more coherent and persuasive, but the richer scenario is a subset of the broader one and is strictly less probable. Her judgment sorts to column c because she substituted plausibility for probability; a logician applying the inclusion rule would assign it to column b."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Single versus Joint Evaluation: When Intuition Beats Logic","visual_frame_description":"A museum-style comparison lab. On a long table sit two clearanced dinnerware displays and two trays of baseball cards. A left monitor shows one shopper pricing a single set (single evaluation) while a right monitor shows a shopper comparing both sets side by side. A small progress bar tracks the player's classifications, and a card deck labeled 'Linda', 'Wimbledon', and 'Die sequences' waits at the edge of the table.","player_mission":"Classify each experiment result as an instance of the less-is-more pattern, of logic prevailing in joint evaluation, or of a conjunction error surviving a direct comparison. Then classify the target row for the dinnerware joint-evaluation finding.","key_concepts":["In Hsee's dinnerware experiment, Set A (40 pieces, including broken cups and saucers) was priced below Set B (24 intact pieces) in single evaluation: about $23 versus $33.","In joint evaluation, participants were willing to pay a little more for Set A than for Set B, about $32 versus $30, because more dishes can only add value.","Sets, including dinnerware sets, are represented by norms and prototypes, so the average value of the dishes dominates single evaluation.","By removing 16 items from Set A, 7 of them intact, its judged value improved, a pattern Hsee called 'less is more.'","John List replicated the less-is-more pattern in a real market for baseball cards: larger sets sold for less in single evaluation but for more in joint evaluation.","Probability and economic value are sum-like variables, so adding a positively valued event or item to a set can only increase its true value.","In the within-subject Linda study, 89% of undergraduates still ranked 'feminist bank teller' above 'bank teller,' a conjunction error that survived direct comparison.","In the Wimbledon problem, 72% assigned 'Borg will lose the first set' a lower probability than 'Borg will lose the first set but win the match.'","The die problem was a nonverbal version of Linda: almost two-thirds of respondents preferred to bet on a longer, more representative sequence that was strictly less likely."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the contrast between single and joint evaluation: in single evaluation, System 1 averages instead of adding, producing the less-is-more illusion, while joint evaluation makes sum-like logic salient and lets System 2 rule. Kahneman shows the same structure governs probability and value, and that unlike the dinnerware case, the Linda conjunction error often survived joint evaluation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Mia, a pricing analyst, is auditing how customers valued dinnerware and baseball-card sets in Hsee's and List's experiments. The target row: shoppers who saw both dinnerware sets together were willing to pay a little more for Set A (40 pieces) than for Set B (24 pieces). Which column best describes that result?","column_a_label":"Less is more: intuition governs in single evaluation","column_b_label":"Logic rules: joint evaluation makes the sum-like value obvious","column_c_label":"Conjunction fallacy persists even in a direct comparison","rows":[{"label":"A shopper shown only the 40-piece Set A with broken cups prices it at about $23, well below the price of the smaller intact Set B.","col":"a"},{"label":"Shoppers who saw both sets together priced Set A (40 pieces) slightly above Set B (24 pieces), about $32 versus $30.","col":"b"},{"label":"In John List's market for baseball cards, an identical set with three added cards of modest value sold for less than the smaller set when each was priced alone.","col":"a"},{"label":"In List's market, the larger sets were valued more than the smaller ones when bidders could compare them.","col":"b"},{"label":"In the within-subject Linda study, 89% of undergraduates ranked 'feminist bank teller' as more probable than 'bank teller' even though both items sat on the same page.","col":"c"},{"label":"In the Wimbledon study, 72% judged 'Borg will lose the first set but win the match' more probable than the more inclusive 'Borg will lose the first set.'","col":"c"},{"label":"Shoppers in joint evaluation paid a little more for the 40-piece Set A than for the 24-piece Set B.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The shoppers were applying the norm of the broken-dishes average and penalizing Set A for its damaged pieces.","The shoppers fell for the conjunction fallacy in a direct comparison.","The shoppers anchored their price on the regular dinnerware range of $30 to $60."],"explanation":"Joint evaluation lets shoppers compare the sets at once, and then they apply the sum-like rule that more dishes can only add value, valuing Set A a little above Set B ($32 versus $30). That is why the target row sorts to column b: logic rules when the comparison is present, whereas intuition governs single evaluation."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Question Formats That Mobilize or Defeat System 2","visual_frame_description":"A trainer's whiteboard in a bright data-literacy workshop. Niko, a 47-year-old data-literacy trainer, has pinned three question formats: the eight-outcome Linda ranking, the stark two-item question, and a 'how many of the 100 participants' version. Colored magnets label each card, and a small histogram above shows the error rates 89%, 65%, and 25%.","player_mission":"Sort each presentation format into the correct column: formats that breed the conjunction fallacy, formats that make the inclusion relation obvious and reduce the error, and formats that cause no fallacy because added detail adds no plausibility. Then classify the target 'how many of the 100 participants' frequency format.","key_concepts":["In the original eight-outcome Linda problem, only 15% of a group of graduate students correctly ranked 'bank teller' above 'feminist bank teller.'","In the stark two-item version, about 85% to 90% of undergraduates chose the conjunction, contrary to logic.","In one study, 64% of graduate students in the social sciences at Stanford and Berkeley answered the short version correctly.","The shorter version required an explicit comparison that mobilized System 2 and allowed most statistically sophisticated students to avoid the fallacy.","A version asking 'How many of the 100 participants...' reduced conjunction errors to 25%, while the equivalent 'What percentage...' version kept errors at 65%.","The frequency representation, phrased as 'how many?', makes individuals concrete and makes the relation of inclusion obvious, whereas 'what percentage?' does not.","The problems 'Mark has hair versus Mark has blond hair' and 'Jane is a teacher versus Jane is a teacher and walks to work' cause no fallacy because the added detail adds no plausibility.","In the absence of a competing intuition, logic prevails.","System 2 is lazy: respondents spent very little time on the problem and were content to answer as if they had only been asked for their opinion."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how question format recruits System 2: a direct comparison or a 'how many' frequency frame makes the subset relation vivid and collapses the conjunction fallacy, while word-based probability wording leaves plausibility in charge. Kahneman reports the frequency version cut the error rate from 65% to 25%, a likely explanation being the spatial imagery of people sorting themselves into groups.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Niko, a data-literacy trainer, wants to know which presentation of the Linda problem will reduce the conjunction fallacy. Sort each format into the correct column, then classify the target: the version that asks 'How many of the 100 participants...'","column_a_label":"Format that produces the conjunction fallacy","column_b_label":"Format that makes inclusion obvious and sharply reduces the error","column_c_label":"No fallacy arises because added detail adds no plausibility","rows":[{"label":"The original eight-outcome Linda ranking, in which 'feminist bank teller' appears as the last item.","col":"a"},{"label":"The stark two-item question 'Which alternative is more probable?' with only 'bank teller' and 'feminist bank teller.'","col":"a"},{"label":"The frequency version asking how many of 100 participants fit each description, which cuts errors from 65% to 25%.","col":"b"},{"label":"The percentage version of the same problem, in which 65% of respondents still committed the fallacy.","col":"a"},{"label":"Which is more probable: Mark has hair, or Mark has blond hair? Answered correctly, since the detailed option adds no plausibility.","col":"c"},{"label":"Which is more probable: Jane is a teacher, or Jane is a teacher and walks to work? Answered correctly for the same reason.","col":"c"},{"label":"The frequency format asking 'How many of the 100 participants...'","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The frequency format increases the error because counting individuals makes the story more vivid.","The frequency format eliminates the error because it removes the word 'probability' and therefore removes all logical constraints.","The frequency format works only for statistically sophisticated respondents, not for undergraduates."],"explanation":"The 'how many of the 100 participants' format brings a spatial representation to mind, as if people were sorting themselves into groups in a room, so the inclusion relation becomes obvious and System 2 can apply it, cutting errors to 25%. That is why the target row sorts to column b."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Forecast Built on Plausibility","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled conference room at Apex Home Appliances. Dana stands at a projector, her quarterly forecast deck visible behind her, and the finance director Marcus sits opposite with a notepad. A timestamped observation feed runs along the bottom of the screen showing Dana's posture, gestures, and speech tempo during the meeting.","player_mission":"Audit Dana's probability claim during the quarterly risk review. Read her baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue the verdict: is her 'highly probable' detailed scenario a sound judgment or a substitution of plausibility for probability?","key_concepts":["Specifying a possible event in greater detail can only lower its probability, because a more detailed event is a subset of a broader one.","Adding detail to a scenario makes it more persuasive but less likely to come true, a trap for forecasters and their clients.","Probability is a sum-like variable: the probability of the broad scenario equals the probability of the detailed scenario plus the probability of all other ways the broad scenario could occur.","The uncritical substitution of plausibility for probability has pernicious effects on judgments when scenarios are used as tools of forecasting.","In the book's example, the California earthquake flood scenario was judged more probable than the North America flood scenario, contrary to logic.","The most coherent stories are not necessarily the most probable, but they are plausible, and the two are easily confused.","System 2 is not impressively alert: it often endorses a conclusion because the conjunction appeared plausible.","Joint comparison of a detailed scenario with its broader alternative mobilizes System 2 and reveals the inclusion relation."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the substitution of plausibility for probability in forecasting: a scenario gains persuasive power from added detail while its true probability falls, because a more detailed event is a subset of the broader one. Kahneman warns that forecasters and their clients systematically rate richer scenarios as more probable even though such scenarios are less likely to come true.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Dana's 'highly probable' detailed launch forecast a sound probability judgment, or is she substituting plausibility for probability?","subject_baseline":"Dana, 41, is the senior product forecast lead at Apex Home Appliances. She is admired for crisp timelines and energetic slide narratives, and she habitually delivers probability claims with flat, confident authority. She rarely pauses to decompose a forecast into its parts.","observed_nonverbal_log":"10:02 Dana opens her forecast deck, standing tall at the conference table, and gestures broadly toward the detailed scenario slide. 10:04 When Marcus, the finance director, asks how the detailed scenario relates to the broad market-entry scenario, Dana waves the question off and quickens her speech. 10:07 She reads the scenario aloud rapidly, a confident half-smile on her face, and taps the 'highly probable' label twice. 10:09 Asked to state the probability of the broader scenario alone, Dana frowns, glances at the ceiling, and gives a vague 'pretty high' while drumming her fingers. 10:11 When Marcus proposes writing the two scenarios side by side, Dana's shoulders drop slightly and she reaches for her coffee.","interrogation_transcript":"Marcus: 'So you're telling me the detailed launch scenario is more likely than simply entering the European market next year?' Dana: 'Yes, the full story, entry plus a 9% share in Germany within 18 months, that's the one we can plan on.' Marcus: 'What's the probability of the broad scenario, just entry, no share target?' Dana: 'I don't think we should waste time splitting hairs. The detailed picture is the real one.' Marcus: 'But isn't the detailed case contained inside the broad one?' Dana: 'Contained, sure, but it's the only version that makes sense as a plan.'","correct_diagnosis":"Dana is committing the conjunction fallacy in reverse form: she treats a detailed conjunction scenario as more probable than the broader scenario that contains it, substituting plausibility for probability. Her verdict reflects System 1's coherence-seeking, not a sum-like probability judgment.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The detailed scenario (enter Europe AND capture 9% share in Germany within 18 months) is a subset of the broad scenario (enter the European market next year), so it can only be less probable. Dana's confident delivery, her dismissal of the decomposition question, her vagueness about the broad scenario alone, and her discomfort when the two scenarios were placed side by side all point to the forecast trap the book describes: adding detail makes a scenario more persuasive but less likely to come true. The fix Marcus suggests, explicit joint comparison, is exactly the move that mobilizes System 2.","distractors":["Dana is anchoring on the 9% market-share figure she set earlier and insufficiently adjusting from it.","Dana is overconfident because of the availability heuristic: recent press coverage of appliance exports came easily to mind.","Dana is using a causal base rate about German market growth, which legitimately raises the probability of the specific scenario."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Sports Analyst's Conjunction Error","visual_frame_description":"A sports-network studio at 2 PM. Raj, a 29-year-old analyst, stands at a glass board with four tennis match outcomes written on it while the host sits opposite. A timestamped observation feed tracks Raj's posture, gestures, and speech tempo as he discusses Björn Borg's chances in the Wimbledon tournament.","player_mission":"Audit Raj's ranking of the Borg match outcomes during the broadcast review. Read his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript, then determine whether his ranking is logically sound or an instance of the conjunction fallacy in a direct comparison.","key_concepts":["The critical comparison in the Wimbledon problem is between 'Borg will lose the first set' and 'Borg will lose the first set but win the match.'","The event 'Borg will lose the first set but win the match' is wholly included in the event 'Borg will lose the first set,' so it cannot be more probable.","Contrary to logic, but not to representativeness or plausibility, 72% of respondents assigned the more inclusive event a lower probability.","The scenario that was judged more probable was also more plausible, a more coherent fit with what was known about the best tennis player in the world.","A conjunction fallacy is committed when people judge a conjunction of two events to be more probable than one of the events in a direct comparison.","As in the Müller-Lyer illusion, the fallacy remains attractive even when you recognize it for what it is.","Kahneman and Tversky found similar violations of logic in many other judgments, not only in the Linda problem.","Representativeness can block the application of an obvious logical rule."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the conjunction fallacy in a direct comparison: the more inclusive event (losing the first set) is always at least as probable as the event it contains (losing the first set but winning the match), yet representativeness made 72% of respondents reverse the order. Kahneman reports the more probable scenario was also the more plausible, a coherent fit with what was known about the best tennis player in the world.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Raj's ranking of the Borg match outcomes logically sound, or does it commit a conjunction fallacy in a direct comparison?","subject_baseline":"Raj, 29, is a tennis analyst for a regional sports network. He is quick, personable, and proud of his story-first approach to match analysis. He reads matches narratively and visibly enjoys retelling dramatic comeback arcs.","observed_nonverbal_log":"14:03 During the broadcast review, Raj leans forward and tells the story of Borg's fighting spirit, shaping a comeback in the air with his hands. 14:05 He writes the four match outcomes on the glass and circles 'lose the first set but win the match,' nodding. 14:07 Asked to compare it with 'lose the first set' on its own, Raj tilts his head, raises his eyebrows, and answers with a dismissive shrug. 14:09 When the host says the two outcomes must be ranked against each other, Raj's speech slows and he taps his pen on 'lose the first set' without conviction. 14:11 He finally laughs, 'The comeback is just a better story,' and looks relieved.","interrogation_transcript":"Host: 'Which is more probable, Borg loses the first set, or Borg loses the first set but wins the match?' Raj: 'Oh, the comeback, no question. He's the best in the world; he drops a set, then takes over.' Host: 'But if he loses the first set and wins the match, he has still lost the first set.' Raj: 'Sure, technically, but that's not how the match plays out in anyone's mind.' Host: 'So you're ranking the comeback scenario above the plain fact it contains?' Raj: 'I'm ranking the story that fits him.'","correct_diagnosis":"Raj is committing the conjunction fallacy in a direct comparison: he judges 'Borg loses the first set but wins the match' more probable than 'Borg loses the first set,' even though the former is wholly included in the latter. Representativeness, not probability, drives his ranking.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The critical comparison in the Wimbledon study is between 'Borg will lose the first set' and 'Borg will lose the first set but win the match.' The second event is a subset of the first and cannot be more probable, yet 72% of respondents judged it more likely, a less-is-more error in a direct comparison. Raj's eager narrative, his refusal to engage the inclusion relation, and his closing admission that the comeback is 'a better story' are the signature tells of representativeness overriding logic.","distractors":["Raj is suffering from a hot-hand illusion, believing Borg is 'in the zone' during comebacks.","Raj is anchoring on Borg's world ranking and adjusting his probabilities from there.","Raj is applying a correct causal base rate: champions who drop the first set historically still win the match."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Defeating the Intuition of Representativeness","visual_frame_description":"A crowded university lecture hall. Yousef, a teaching assistant, stands at the front handing out the eight-item Linda questionnaire while students skim the description of the thirty-one-year-old philosophy major and begin ranking scenarios. A thought bubble shows the 'little homunculus' shouting, 'she can't just be a bank teller, read the description.'","player_mission":"Defeat the boss of representativeness in two stages. First diagnose the student responses that reveal System 1 winning the conflict, then choose the intervention that forces System 2 to apply the inclusion rule.","key_concepts":["In the within-subject Linda ranking, 89% of undergraduates ranked 'feminist bank teller' as more probable than 'bank teller,' a failure of System 2 despite a fair opportunity to detect the logical rule.","The stark two-item version, which requires an explicit comparison, allows most statistically sophisticated students to avoid the fallacy.","In one study, 64% of graduate students in the social sciences at Stanford and Berkeley answered the short version correctly.","A 'how many of the 100 participants' frequency version reduced conjunction errors to 25%, while a 'what percentage' version kept them at 65%.","The frequency representation makes the inclusion relation obvious by bringing a spatial image of people sorting into groups to mind.","Stephen Jay Gould's 'little homunculus' is System 1 speaking in insistent tones, overriding his knowledge of the correct answer.","The fallacy remains attractive even when recognized for what it is, like the Müller-Lyer illusion.","System 2 is lazy: respondents spent very little time and endorsed the plausible conjunction without effort.","The conjunction fallacy is a case of conflict between intuition and logic, and representativeness won in the original studies."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the conjunction fallacy as a conflict between intuition and logic: representativeness wins unless System 2 is actively recruited. Kahneman's evidence shows that an explicit two-item comparison and especially a 'how many' frequency frame collapse the fallacy, while vague probability wording leaves the plausible story in charge.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the insistent intuition of representativeness, the 'little homunculus' that shouts 'she can't just be a bank teller, read the description!' The battle tests whether you can recognize System 1 winning the conflict and then use format to mobilize System 2 against it.","stage_1_observation":"You are Yousef, a teaching assistant in a crowded lecture hall at a large university. You hand out the eight-outcome Linda questionnaire: two of the items are 'Linda is a bank teller' (sixth position) and 'Linda is a bank teller and is active in the feminist movement' (last position). You watch as students skim the description of the thirty-one-year-old philosophy major who was active in antinuclear demonstrations, smile knowingly, and rank the scenarios. In the first row of responses you see 'feminist bank teller' ranked above 'bank teller,' and the pattern spreads across the room.","stage_1_question":"Which response shows System 1 winning the conflict between representativeness and logic?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Students rank 'Linda is a bank teller and is active in the feminist movement' as more probable than 'Linda is a bank teller,' even though both items appear on the same page. Kahneman found 89% of undergraduates violated the logic this way, a failure of System 2 despite a fair opportunity to detect the logical rule.","stage_2_intervention":"The department now asks Yousef to brief a room full of statistically trained analysts, all of whom know the logic of Venn diagrams, so the error is harder to trigger. You must choose the move most likely to make them apply the inclusion rule rather than the intuitive story.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Present the stark two-item question ('Which alternative is more probable? Linda is a bank teller. Linda is a bank teller and is active in the feminist movement.') and, for stubborn cases, switch to the frequency frame 'How many of the 100 participants...?' The explicit comparison mobilizes System 2 (64% of Stanford and Berkeley social-science graduate students answered the short version correctly), and the 'how many' frequency representation reduced errors from 65% to 25% by making the inclusion relation spatial and obvious."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Breaking the Plausibility Trap in Forecasting","visual_frame_description":"A national emergency-preparedness committee in a wood-paneled briefing room. Two scenarios are projected on the wall, the broad 'North America flood' and the detailed 'California earthquake causing a flood'. Members lean forward when the detailed scenario is discussed, pointing at specific fault lines on a map, while the broad scenario receives dismissive waves.","player_mission":"Diagnose why the committee rates the detailed California earthquake scenario as more probable, then intervene to make the panel apply the logic of inclusion before budget is allocated.","key_concepts":["Specifying an event in greater detail can only lower its probability, because the specified event is a subset of the broader event.","In Kahneman's example, probability judgments were higher for the richer and more detailed scenario, contrary to logic.","Adding detail to scenarios makes them more persuasive, but less likely to come true, a trap for forecasters and their clients.","The California earthquake flood scenario is more plausible than the North America scenario, although its probability is certainly smaller.","The uncritical substitution of plausibility for probability corrupts scenario-based forecasting.","Coherence, plausibility, and probability are easily confused by the unwary.","Joint comparison of the two scenarios mobilizes System 2 and reveals the inclusion relation.","The 'how many' frequency representation makes the inclusion relation obvious and reduces reliance on the plausible story."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the forecasting trap of plausibility substitution: adding detail makes a scenario more persuasive but strictly less probable, because a specified event is a subset of the broader event. Kahneman's flood scenarios showed judged probability rising with detail, and he warned that rich scenarios are a trap for forecasters and their clients.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is plausibility substitution: a richly detailed scenario is rated as more probable than the sparser scenario that contains it. The battle tests whether you can diagnose why detail inflates judged probability and then force the committee to apply the logic of inclusion.","stage_1_observation":"You are seated in a national emergency-preparedness committee. The panel is asked to rate two scenarios for next year: 'A massive flood somewhere in North America in which more than 1,000 people drown' and 'An earthquake in California sometime next year, causing a flood in which more than 1,000 people drown.' The room buzzes as the California scenario is discussed; members cite recent seismic risk, name specific fault lines, and several call the detailed scenario 'obviously the real risk.' The broad North America scenario is waved off as vague.","stage_1_question":"Why is the detailed California earthquake scenario rated more probable than the broad North America flood scenario, and what is wrong with that judgment?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The detailed scenario is more plausible and more coherent, so plausibility is substituted for probability, but an earthquake in California that causes a flood is wholly contained in a massive flood somewhere in North America, and specifying an event in greater detail can only lower its probability. Kahneman reports probability judgments were higher for the richer, more detailed scenario, contrary to logic.","stage_2_intervention":"The committee is about to allocate budget toward the 'likely' California scenario. You must intervene to make the panel see the inclusion relation and correct its probability ranking. Which action is most likely to work?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Place the two scenarios side by side and mark that the California event is a subset of the North America event, then reframe the question in frequency terms, asking how many of 100 such years would produce a flood with more than 1,000 drownings in North America and, of those, how many involve an earthquake in California. Joint comparison and a frequency frame make the subset relation obvious and mobilize System 2, exactly the conditions under which logic prevailed in the studies."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Defeating the Less-is-More Average","visual_frame_description":"A home-goods store during a clearance sale. On a shelf sit two dinnerware sets: Set A, 40 pieces with visibly chipped cups and saucers, and Set B, 24 intact pieces. Shoppers inspect each set one at a time and glance at price tags reading about $23 for Set A and about $33 for Set B. A small diagram overlay shows a scale of 'average dish value' tipping toward Set B.","player_mission":"Explain why the 40-piece set with broken pieces is priced below the intact 24-piece set, then choose the display change that makes shoppers value the larger set correctly.","key_concepts":["Sets, including dinnerware sets, are represented by norms and prototypes, so the average value of the dishes dominates single evaluation.","In Hsee's single evaluation, Set B (24 intact pieces) was priced much higher than Set A (40 pieces with broken cups and saucers): about $33 versus $23.","By removing 16 items from Set A, 7 of them intact, its judged value improved, a pattern Hsee called 'less is more.'","In joint evaluation, participants were willing to pay a little more for Set A than for Set B, about $32 versus $30.","People who see only the set that includes broken dishes price it very low, a rule of intuition.","People who see both sets at once apply the logical rule that more dishes can only add value.","John List replicated the pattern in a real market for baseball cards: larger sets valued less in single evaluation, more in joint evaluation.","Economic value is a sum-like variable: adding a positively valued item to the set can only increase its value.","Intuition governs judgments in between-subjects conditions; logic rules in joint evaluation."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the less-is-more illusion: System 1 evaluates sets by their norm and average, so broken or cheap items depress value in single evaluation even when removing them is irrational. Kahneman, drawing on Hsee's dinnerware study and List's baseball-card auction, shows joint evaluation restores the sum-like logic that more of a positively valued good can only add value.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the norm-and-prototype representation of sets: in single evaluation, System 1 prices by the average value of the parts, so removing broken or cheap items can make a set look more valuable. The battle tests whether you can spot the averaging error and then force the sum-like logic of value into play.","stage_1_observation":"You are a store manager overseeing a clearance sale of dinnerware that regularly runs between $30 and $60. On the shelf sits Set A: 40 pieces, with 2 of 8 cups broken and 7 of 8 saucers broken. Next to it, Set B: 24 pieces, all in good condition. A stream of shoppers is shown only one set at a time, and you watch them inspect the broken cups and saucers of Set A, grimace, and set it back. Your clerk reports that shoppers price Set A at about $23 while pricing the smaller, intact Set B at about $33.","stage_1_question":"Why does the 40-piece Set A with broken pieces fetch less than the 24-piece intact Set B, when Set A contains every dish in Set B plus seven intact extras?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Sets are represented by norms and prototypes, and the average value of the dishes in Set A is much lower because no one wants to pay for broken pieces, so the average dominates single evaluation. This is Hsee's less-is-more pattern: by removing 16 items from Set A, 7 of them intact, its judged value actually improved.","stage_2_intervention":"You decide you want shoppers to value Set A correctly, recognizing that adding dishes can only add value. Which change to the display is the most effective intervention?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Show both sets side by side so shoppers make a joint evaluation, the within-subject comparison Hsee used, which produced the logical result of valuing Set A a little above Set B ($32 versus $30). In joint evaluation people apply the sum-like rule that more dishes can only add value, whereas intuition governs the between-subjects single evaluation."}]},{"id":"ch21","title":"16. Causes Trump Statistics","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Statistical versus Causal Base Rates","visual_frame_description":"An evidence board in a police training room. Three columns are labeled 'Statistical Base Rate', 'Causal Base Rate', and 'Recited But Not Applied'. Cards show the cab problem, Ajzen's exam experiment, the helping experiment, and statements about inner-city school graduates. A detective's hand places a green card marked 'Green cabs cause 85% of accidents' above the board.","player_mission":"Sort each base-rate statement into the correct column, then classify the target base rate from version two of the cab problem. Decide whether each base rate is a neglected population fact, a causal fact used as information about the individual case, or a rate people accept but refuse to apply.","key_concepts":["Statistical base rates are facts about a population to which a case belongs, but they are not relevant to the individual case.","Causal base rates change your view of how the individual case came to be.","Statistical base rates are generally underweighted, and sometimes neglected altogether, when specific information about the case at hand is available.","Causal base rates are treated as information about the individual case and are easily combined with other case-specific information.","In the cab problem, 'Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents' reads as a story of reckless Green drivers and pulls judgments toward the Bayesian estimate.","In Ajzen's experiment, a class in which 75% passed suggests the test was easy, a causal fact about every student's outcome.","Ajzen's constructed sample, in which 75% had failed, was a purely statistical fact about the ensemble and had much less impact.","Students in the helping-experiment study could recite the base rate but still predicted that two specific individuals would rush to help.","System 1 can deal with stories in which the elements are causally linked, but it is weak in statistical reasoning."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between statistical and causal base rates: inert population facts are underweighted or neglected, while causal base rates are treated as information about the individual case and combined with other evidence. Kahneman, borrowing the notion from Icek Ajzen, shows the two types of base-rate information are processed very differently by System 1.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In the cab problem, version two presents this base rate: 'The two companies operate the same number of cabs, but Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents.' How do people treat this base rate? Sort the rows, then classify the target.","column_a_label":"Statistical base rate (a population fact that is underweighted or neglected)","column_b_label":"Causal base rate (treated as information about the individual case and used)","column_c_label":"Base rate that conflicts with personal beliefs and is recited but not applied","rows":[{"label":"85% of the cabs in the city are Green and 15% are Blue, an inert statistical fact that most respondents ignore once a witness says 'Blue.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents, immediately read as 'the Green drivers must be reckless madmen,' a stereotype applied to individual drivers.","col":"b"},{"label":"Ajzen told one group that the students were drawn from a class in which 75% passed, read as evidence that the test was easy, a causal fact about each student's outcome.","col":"b"},{"label":"Ajzen told another group that the sample had been constructed so that 75% failed, a purely statistical fact about the ensemble that had much less impact.","col":"a"},{"label":"Only 4 of the 15 participants in the helping experiment rushed to help; students could recite this rate yet still predicted that the two interviewees would rush to help.","col":"c"},{"label":"Most graduates of this inner-city school go to college, interpreted as a propensity in individual members and fit into a causal story.","col":"b"},{"label":"The base rate that Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The base rate is neglected because people anchor on the number 85.","The base rate is treated as a statistical fact about the population that does not apply to any individual driver.","The base rate has no effect on judgment because it conflicts with the witness's testimony."],"explanation":"Causal base rates change your view of how the individual case came to be, so System 1 readily uses them, while statistical base rates are usually underweighted or neglected. The 'Green cabs cause 85% of accidents' phrasing invites the causal story of reckless drivers and pulls judgments toward the Bayesian estimate."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Cab Problem: Neglect, Causal Stories, and Bayes","visual_frame_description":"A night city street rendered on a decision-science dashboard. Two columns of cards show the cab problem in its two versions, a witness box with an 80% reliability badge, and a Bayes calculator displaying the 41% estimate. A poll meter shows the most common answer, 80%, ticking upward as new cards load.","player_mission":"Classify each response pattern in the cab problem as base-rate neglect, use of the causal base rate, or a correct Bayesian combination. Then classify the target row, the most common answer to the first version of the problem.","key_concepts":["In the first cab problem, 85% of cabs are Green and 15% are Blue, and a witness who is 80% reliable identifies the cab as Blue.","The correct Bayesian estimate for the first cab problem is about 41%.","The most common answer people give to the first cab problem is about 80%, driven by the witness's reliability.","In the absence of a witness, the probability of the guilty cab being Blue is 15%, the base rate.","If the two cab companies had been equally large, the base rate would be uninformative and the reliability of the witness, 80%, would be the answer.","In the causal version, people give considerable weight to the base rate, and their average judgment is not too far from the Bayesian solution.","In the causal version, the two causal stories (reckless Green driver, Blue-identifying witness) are contradictory and approximately cancel each other.","The two versions of the cab problem are mathematically indistinguishable but psychologically quite different.","A mind that is hungry for causal stories finds nothing to chew on in the first version's inert population count."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests base-rate neglect and the power of causal base rates: given a statistical base rate and a specific witness, System 1 ignores the base rate and answers with witness reliability, while a causal restatement of the base rate moves judgments toward Bayes. Kahneman reports the most common answer to the first cab problem is 80% rather than the correct 41%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In the first version of the cab problem, a witness identified the hit-and-run cab as Blue, 85% of cabs are Green, and the witness is 80% reliable. The most common answer people give is about 80%. Which column best describes that response?","column_a_label":"Neglect of the statistical base rate (going with the witness)","column_b_label":"Use of the causal base rate (judgments move toward the Bayesian solution)","column_c_label":"Correct Bayesian combination of base rate and witness reliability","rows":[{"label":"The most common answer to the first cab problem is about 80%, driven almost entirely by the witness's reliability.","col":"a"},{"label":"The correct estimate in the first cab problem, combining the 15% Blue base rate with the 80%-reliable witness, is about 41%.","col":"c"},{"label":"In the causal version, average judgments land not too far from the Bayesian solution, because the two causal stories roughly cancel each other.","col":"b"},{"label":"If the two companies were equally large, the base rate would be uninformative and the witness's 80% reliability would be the answer.","col":"c"},{"label":"A respondent answers 'Blue, because the witness saw it' without ever considering that only 15% of cabs are Blue.","col":"a"},{"label":"Readers of the causal version give considerable weight to the base rate of accidents rather than only to the witness.","col":"b"},{"label":"The most common answer to the first cab problem, about 80%.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The answer reflects a correct Bayesian update because the witness is highly reliable.","The answer reflects the affect heuristic, since Blue cabs feel safer at night.","The answer is produced by the law of small numbers applied to the night's traffic."],"explanation":"The dominant 80% response ignores the statistical base rate entirely and goes with the witness, the pattern the book describes as base-rate neglect. Version two changes only the presentation, making the base rate causal, and average judgments then move close to the Bayesian 41%."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Causal Stereotypes, Affect, and Norms of Categories","visual_frame_description":"A mind-map display over a debate stage. Three columns show 'Causal Stereotype in Use', 'Affect Heuristic in Argument', and 'Norms and Prototypes of Categories'. Cards drift in showing reckless Green drivers, inner-city school graduates, the no-cost claim of political arguments, and images of horses, refrigerators, and New York police officers.","player_mission":"Sort each claim into the column it best illustrates, then classify the target claim about how System 1 represents categories. Distinguish judgment-improving causal stereotypes from emotion-driven affect arguments and from the structural fact of norm-based categorization.","key_concepts":["One of the basic characteristics of System 1 is that it represents categories as norms and prototypical exemplars.","We hold in memory a representation of one or more 'normal' members of each category, such as horses, refrigerators, and New York police officers.","When the categories are social, these representations are called stereotypes, and stereotyping is how we think of categories.","Stereotypes, both correct and false, are statements about the group accepted as facts about every member, and they fit in a causal story.","In the cab problem, stereotyping the Green drivers improves the accuracy of judgment.","Reliance on the affect heuristic is common in politically charged arguments, where the positions we favor have no cost and those we oppose have no benefits.","Resistance to stereotyping is a laudable moral position, but the idea that it is costless is not scientifically defensible.","In sensitive social contexts such as hiring and profiling, the law and social norms reject causal base rates, treating them as statistical facts about the group.","Neglecting valid stereotypes inevitably results in suboptimal judgments."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests three distinct ideas about categories: causal stereotypes are used as information and can improve judgment, the affect heuristic powers politically charged claims that favored positions have no cost, and System 1 structurally represents categories as norms and prototypes. Kahneman stresses that stereotyping is neutral in his usage even though it is a bad word in our culture.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"System 1 represents categories as norms and prototypical exemplars; when the categories are social, these representations are called stereotypes. The target claim is: 'We hold in memory a representation of one or more normal members of each category, such as horses, refrigerators, and New York police officers.' Which column describes it?","column_a_label":"Causal stereotype used to improve judgment","column_b_label":"Affect heuristic in a politically charged argument","column_c_label":"Representation of categories as norms and prototypical exemplars","rows":[{"label":"Green drivers are dangerous, a stereotype of Green recklessness applied to unknown drivers, which improved accuracy in the cab problem.","col":"a"},{"label":"Many graduates of this inner-city school go to college, a propensity read into individual members and fit into a causal story.","col":"a"},{"label":"The positions we favor have no cost and those we oppose have no benefits, a hallmark of reliance on the affect heuristic.","col":"b"},{"label":"The idea that resistance to stereotyping is costless, though satisfying to the soul and politically correct, is not scientifically defensible.","col":"b"},{"label":"We hold in memory a representation of one or more 'normal' members of each category, which is how we think of horses, refrigerators, and New York police officers.","col":"c"},{"label":"When the categories are social, these representations are called stereotypes, and they are a basic characteristic of System 1.","col":"c"},{"label":"We hold in memory a representation of one or more 'normal' members of each category.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["The claim is an example of the halo effect spreading one good trait to the whole category.","The claim recommends using stereotypes in hiring, which the book endorses.","The claim is an affect heuristic because it treats the category with emotional warmth."],"explanation":"Kahneman's neutral usage holds that stereotypes are simply how System 1 represents social categories, as norms and prototypical exemplars, no more and no less. That is a structural claim about the mind, distinct from the judgment-improving causal stereotypes of the cab problem and from the emotion-driven affect heuristic of political debate."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing an Investigator's Base-Rate Neglect","visual_frame_description":"A police precinct briefing room at 9 AM. Detective Lena Chen stands at a whiteboard with '80%' written in large letters, while the deputy chief sits with a folder that shows the cab data. A timestamped observation feed tracks Chen's posture, gestures, and attention as the base rate is introduced.","player_mission":"Audit Detective Chen's probability estimate for the hit-and-run case. Read her baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then decide whether her 80% verdict correctly combines the base rate and the witness or is an instance of base-rate neglect.","key_concepts":["In the cab problem, 85% of cabs are Green and 15% are Blue, and a witness who is 80% reliable identifies the cab as Blue.","The correct Bayesian estimate is about 41%, not 80%.","The most common answer people give is about 80% because they go with the witness and neglect the base rate.","Statistical base rates are generally underweighted, and sometimes neglected altogether, when specific information about the case at hand is available.","A mind that is hungry for causal stories finds nothing to chew on in the inert count of 85% Green cabs.","In the absence of a witness, the probability of the guilty cab being Blue is 15%, the base rate of that outcome.","Bayes's rule combines the base rate and the reliability of the witness into a single probability.","The two versions of the cab problem are mathematically indistinguishable but psychologically quite different."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests base-rate neglect: statistical base rates are generally underweighted and sometimes neglected altogether when specific information about the case is available. Kahneman reports the most common answer to the cab problem is 80%, the witness's reliability, rather than the Bayesian 41%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Did Detective Chen's 80% probability estimate correctly combine the base rate of Blue cabs with the witness's reliability, or is it an instance of base-rate neglect?","subject_baseline":"Detective Lena Chen, 38, is a veteran investigator with twelve years on the force. She is methodical with evidence collection, decisive in briefings, and tends to commit to a single number early, defending it firmly once stated.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:15 In the morning briefing, Chen writes '80%' on the whiteboard without hesitation and squares off her shoulders. 09:18 When the deputy chief reads out that only 15% of cabs are Blue, Chen's eyes stay fixed on her own number and she gives a small shake of her head. 09:22 Asked to explain the role of the 15% figure, Chen pauses, rubs her chin, and answers about the witness instead. 09:26 The deputy chief writes '41%' on the board; Chen crosses her arms and glances at the door. 09:30 When asked to re-derive the estimate from scratch, she picks up the marker slowly and draws a blank.","interrogation_transcript":"Deputy Chief: 'The witness is 80% reliable, but Blue cabs are only 15% of the fleet. What's the probability the car was Blue?' Chen: 'Eighty percent. The witness saw a Blue cab and the court tested her; that's the evidence.' Deputy Chief: 'What does the 15% base rate add?' Chen: 'It tells us Blue is the smaller company, but the witness actually saw the car.' Deputy Chief: 'So you weight the witness alone?' Chen: 'In a hit-and-run you trust the eyes that were there.'","correct_diagnosis":"Chen is committing base-rate neglect: she anchors on the 80%-reliable witness and ignores the 15% statistical base rate, when the correct Bayesian combination yields about 41%. Her confidence in the single number and her refusal to integrate the base rate are the diagnostic pattern.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The book's cab problem has exactly these inputs, and the most common answer is 80% because people go with the witness and drop the statistical base rate, whereas Bayes's rule gives 41%. Chen's instant commitment to 80%, her dismissal of the 15% figure, and her inability to re-derive the estimate all match the profile of a respondent who treats specific, vivid information as sufficient and the population fact as irrelevant.","distractors":["Chen is overestimating because she is anchoring on the number 80 from the witness's reliability and adjusting from there.","Chen is using a causal base rate: she reasons that reckless drivers are likelier to be involved in accidents.","Chen is committing the conjunction fallacy by treating 'Blue cab seen by witness' as a subset event."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Student Who Quietly Exempts Herself","visual_frame_description":"A psychology seminar room. On the screen play the two bland interview videos from the helping experiment, showing nice, normal people describing their hobbies and future plans. Gemma, a 22-year-old senior, watches with a relaxed smile while the instructor holds a card reading 'Base rate: 4 of 15 helped.' A timestamped observation feed tracks her reactions.","player_mission":"Audit whether Gemma's predictions for the two interviewees reflect real learning from the helping experiment's statistics. Read her baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript, then decide whether her judgments changed or she quietly exempts herself and her friends from the statistical conclusion.","key_concepts":["In the New York University helping experiment, only four of the fifteen participants responded immediately to the appeal for help.","Six participants never got out of their booth, and five others came out only well after the seizure victim apparently choked.","Individuals feel relieved of responsibility when they know that others have heard the same request for help, an instance of diffusion of responsibility.","The base rate of immediate help was 4 of 15, about 27%, so the prior belief about an unspecified participant should be that he did not rush to help.","Students who knew both the procedure and the results of the helping experiment made predictions indistinguishable from students who knew only the procedure.","Nisbett and Borgida found that students quietly exempt themselves and their friends from the conclusions of experiments that surprise them.","A new group told only that the two individuals had not helped made extremely accurate guesses about the global results.","People infer the general from the particular, not the particular from the general.","Changing one's mind about human nature is hard work, and changing one's mind for the worse about oneself is even harder."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the gap between knowing statistics and changing one's view of human nature: students who learned the helping experiment's results made predictions indistinguishable from those who had not. Kahneman's conclusion is that surprising individual cases change thinking, while surprising statistical facts are readily recited but not absorbed.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After learning the helping experiment's results, does Gemma's prediction for the two interviewees show genuine learning, or does she quietly exempt herself and her friends from the statistical conclusion?","subject_baseline":"Gemma, 22, is a senior psychology major with a 3.9 GPA. In class she recites the helping experiment enthusiastically and can quote the 'diffusion of responsibility' interpretation word for word. She is warm, socially confident, and sure that she would step up in an emergency.","observed_nonverbal_log":"11:00 In the seminar, Gemma watches the two bland interview videos with a relaxed, open posture, nodding at the hobbies and future plans. 11:04 The instructor asks how quickly each person helped the stricken stranger; Gemma smiles and answers 'immediately' without a pause. 11:06 When the instructor reminds her that only 4 of the 15 participants responded right away, Gemma tilts her head and continues to smile, shrugging off the number. 11:09 Asked whether the base rate should shape her guess, Gemma's smile tightens and she looks at the ceiling. 11:12 She adds, 'But those two people seem really nice, they'd absolutely help.'","interrogation_transcript":"Instructor: 'The base rate is that 4 of 15 rushed to help, about 27%. What's your guess for this person?' Gemma: 'Oh, definitely quick to help. She seems lovely.' Instructor: 'So the base rate doesn't move your prediction?' Gemma: 'I understand the statistics, but I'm judging her as a person.' Instructor: 'What would you do if you were in that situation yourself?' Gemma: 'I'd run to help, no question. I'm not most people.'","correct_diagnosis":"Gemma knows the base rate but does not apply it to the individuals; her beliefs about human nature did not change. This is the pattern Nisbett and Borgida found, in which students quietly exempt themselves from the conclusions of experiments that surprise them.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The Bayesian task requires staying with the base rate, about 27% immediate help, because the videos were designed to be uninformative, but Gemma predicts immediate help for both, exactly like students who never saw the results. Her cheerful recitation of the statistics, her refusal to let them touch her predictions, and her appeal to 'I'm not most people' match the study's finding that surprising statistical facts are recited but do not change thinking, while surprising individual cases do.","distractors":["Gemma is committing the conjunction fallacy by judging 'nice person and quick helper' as more probable than 'nice person.'","Gemma is anchoring her prediction on the number 4 from the base rate and adjusting upward.","Gemma is using the availability heuristic because she recently heard a news story about a good Samaritan."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Defeating Base-Rate Neglect in the Cab Problem","visual_frame_description":"A focus-group room behind one-way glass. Twenty citizens fill out the cab problem sheets; a poll meter above the room climbs toward 80%. A court-appointed analyst at a desk holds two versions of the base-rate statement, one reading '85% of cabs are Green' and one reading 'Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents.'","player_mission":"Defeat the boss of base-rate neglect in two stages. First identify the error the focus group commits, then choose the presentation of the base rate that pulls their average judgments back toward the Bayesian estimate.","key_concepts":["In the first cab problem, 85% of cabs are Green and 15% are Blue, and a witness who is 80% reliable identifies the cab as Blue.","The correct Bayesian estimate is about 41%, while the most common answer is about 80%.","Statistical base rates are generally underweighted, and sometimes neglected altogether, when specific information about the case at hand is available.","Causal base rates are treated as information about the individual case and are easily combined with other case-specific information.","In the causal version, the base rate reads as a story of reckless Green drivers, and judgments move close to the Bayesian solution.","A mind that is hungry for causal stories finds nothing to chew on in the inert count of 85% Green cabs.","In the causal version, the two causal stories (reckless Green driver, Blue-identifying witness) are contradictory and approximately cancel each other.","The two versions of the cab problem are mathematically indistinguishable but psychologically quite different."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests base-rate neglect and the power of causal base rates: statistical base rates are ignored, but causal base rates are treated as information about the individual case and combined with case-specific evidence. Kahneman shows the two mathematically identical versions of the cab problem produce dramatically different judgments, with the causal version moving people close to Bayes's 41%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is base-rate neglect: given a statistical base rate and vivid case-specific evidence, System 1 ignores the base rate and goes with the witness. The battle tests whether you can identify the error and then use a causal base rate to pull judgments back toward the Bayesian solution.","stage_1_observation":"You are a court-appointed analyst observing a focus group of 20 citizens who are each given the standard cab problem: 85% of cabs are Green and 15% Blue, a witness identified the cab as Blue and is 80% reliable, and the question is the probability the cab was Blue. You watch as the group reads the numbers, then nearly everyone scribbles '80%.' One participant mutters, 'The witness saw it, that's what matters.'","stage_1_question":"What error is the focus group committing, and what is the correct probability?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The group is neglecting the statistical base rate and going with the witness, giving 80% when Bayes's rule yields about 41%. The 15% base rate of Blue cabs and the 80%-reliable witness must be combined; statistical base rates are underweighted or neglected whenever specific information about the case is available.","stage_2_intervention":"The judge asks you to redesign the problem so the group's judgments improve. You may alter only the presentation of the base rate. Which presentation is most likely to move average judgments close to the Bayesian answer?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Present the causal version: 'The two companies operate the same number of cabs, but Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents.' This turns the base rate into a causal story (reckless Green drivers) that combines with the witness's testimony, and people give considerable weight to it, with average judgments landing near the Bayesian solution. A mind hungry for causal stories finds nothing to chew on in the first version's inert population count."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Diffusion of Responsibility and How to Actually Teach It","visual_frame_description":"A research-lab schematic shows six booths with one active microphone. The stooge's booth is highlighted as his distress call plays, ending in choking sounds, while a side panel shows the results: 4 of 15 responded immediately, 6 never left their booth, and 5 left late. A panel of executives watches the footage with confident expressions.","player_mission":"Diagnose why so few participants helped in the classic New York University helping experiment, then choose the teaching intervention that Nisbett and Borgida found actually changes students' beliefs about human nature.","key_concepts":["In the helping experiment, six participants spoke in turn over an intercom while one, a stooge, described a seizure-prone condition.","The stooge's last words were 'C-could somebody-er-er-help-er-uh-uh-uh [choking sounds],' and then nothing more was heard from him.","Only four of the fifteen participants responded immediately to the appeal for help.","Six participants never got out of their booth, and five others came out only well after the seizure victim apparently choked.","Individuals feel relieved of responsibility when they know that others have heard the same request for help, an instance of diffusion of responsibility.","Most of us think of ourselves as decent people who would rush to help, and that expectation is wrong.","Students who learned the results of the helping experiment made predictions indistinguishable from students who had not.","Students who were told only that two individual interviewees had not helped then made extremely accurate guesses about the global results.","People are unwilling to deduce the particular from the general but very willing to infer the general from the particular."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests diffusion of responsibility and the teaching of psychology: a potent feature of the situation, not bad character, makes people unhelpful, yet surprising statistical facts do not change beliefs. Kahneman reports that Nisbett and Borgida's students learned nothing from the group results but immediately generalized from two surprising individual cases.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the diffusion of responsibility: when others have heard the same request for help, normal, decent people stay in their booths, and teaching this surprising result with statistics alone changes nothing. The battle tests whether you can recognize the effect and then choose the intervention that genuinely changes beliefs.","stage_1_observation":"You are reviewing the classic New York University helping experiment. Six participants sit in individual booths; one is a stooge who, after describing his seizure-prone condition, becomes agitated and pleads for help, ending with choking sounds: 'C-could somebody-er-er-help-er-uh-uh-uh...' You watch the participants' behavior: only four of the fifteen respond immediately, six never leave their booth, and five come out only after the victim apparently choked. A panel of executives watching this footage predict they themselves would have rushed to help at once.","stage_1_question":"Why did so few participants help, and why are the executives confident they would have acted differently?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Individuals feel relieved of responsibility when they know others have heard the same request; this diffusion of responsibility induced normal, decent people to behave in a surprisingly unhelpful way. The executives' confidence is the same error the participants themselves would make: most of us think of ourselves as the kind of person who would rush to help, and that expectation is wrong.","stage_2_intervention":"You are training a new class of managers and want their beliefs about human nature to actually change. You have two candidate approaches. Which is the one Nisbett and Borgida found worked?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Surprise them with individual cases rather than statistics: show the two bland interview videos and tell them that these two particular people did not help, then ask them to guess the global results, and their guesses were extremely accurate. Students who were told only the group statistics learned nothing at all and kept predicting immediate help; people infer the general from the particular, not the particular from the general."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Causal Stereotypes Versus the Norm Against Stereotyping","visual_frame_description":"A hiring-committee simulation room for a transit-safety analyst role. Two cab-problem versions flash on a screen while a committee member argues that ignoring all base rates is costless. A debate meter tracks whether the panel's reasoning relies on causal stereotypes, affect heuristics, or statistical norms.","player_mission":"Defeat the boss of the stereotype double standard in two stages. First explain why the causal version of the base rate improves the analyst's judgment, then respond to the committee member's claim that resisting stereotypes is costless.","key_concepts":["Causal base rates are treated as information about the individual case and are easily combined with other case-specific information.","In the cab problem, stereotyping the Green drivers improves the accuracy of judgment.","Stereotyping is a neutral term in Kahneman's usage because representing categories as norms and prototypes is a basic characteristic of System 1.","In sensitive social contexts such as hiring and profiling, there is a strong social norm against stereotyping that is also embedded in the law.","We reject causal base rates in sensitive contexts because we do not want to draw possibly erroneous conclusions about individuals from the statistics of the group.","The social norm against stereotyping has been highly beneficial in creating a more civilized and more equal society.","Resistance to stereotyping is a laudable moral position, but the idea that the resistance is costless is not scientifically defensible.","Reliance on the affect heuristic is common in politically charged arguments, where the positions we favor have no cost and those we oppose have no benefits.","Neglecting valid stereotypes inevitably results in suboptimal judgments, and the costs of resistance are worth paying."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the double standard of causal base rates: stereotypes that fit a causal story improve statistical judgment, yet in hiring and profiling we deliberately treat base rates as statistical facts about the group. Kahneman warns that claiming resistance to stereotyping is costless is an affect heuristic, not a scientifically defensible position.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the tension between causal stereotypes, which improve judgment, and the social norm against stereotyping, which is morally desirable but not costless. The battle tests whether you can recognize when a causal base rate legitimately improves a prediction and whether you can resist the affect heuristic's claim that favored positions have no costs.","stage_1_observation":"You sit on a hiring-committee simulation for a transit-safety analyst role. The panel is given the two cab-problem versions. In version one, the base rate is '85% of cabs are Green,' and the committee's analyst shrugs and says the base rate is irrelevant to the accident at hand. In version two, the base rate is 'Green cabs are involved in 85% of accidents,' and the same analyst immediately says, 'Then the Green drivers are the reckless ones, and that changes how I read the case.' The committee asks you which version is 'cheating' by relying on a stereotype.","stage_1_question":"Why does the causal version improve the analyst's judgment, and is that reliance on a stereotype a cognitive flaw in this context?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The causal base rate changes how the individual case came to be, so the stereotype of Green recklessness is used as information about the driver and improves accuracy, moving judgments near the Bayesian estimate. In the cab context, neglecting base-rate information is the cognitive flaw, and relying on the causal base rate is desirable; Kahneman calls stereotyping neutral in this usage because it is how System 1 represents categories.","stage_2_intervention":"A committee member now argues that since stereotyping is socially harmful, the panel should simply ignore all base rates, adding: 'Our position has no cost and the alternative has no benefits.' You must respond with the scientifically defensible position. What do you say?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reject the affect heuristic in that argument: the social norm against stereotyping and profiling is highly beneficial and the costs of resistance are worth paying, but the claim that resistance is costless is not scientifically defensible. Neglecting valid stereotypes inevitably results in suboptimal judgments; the defensible stance is that we accept the costs of resisting stereotypes in sensitive contexts because a better society is worth them."}]},{"id":"ch22","title":"17. Regression to the Mean","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Causal Stories versus Regression to the Mean","visual_frame_description":"A training hangar in the Israeli Air Force. A chalk target on the floor and a blackboard listing two coin-toss distances per officer. Beside it, a split-screen shows a flight cadet being praised after a clean aerobatic loop and then wobbling on the next attempt, while a veteran instructor watches and nods. The player reads six real-world observations and must sort each into its correct explanatory category.","player_mission":"Classify each observation as explained by regression to the mean, a genuine causal effect supported by controlled evidence, or an unfounded causal story about regression data. Get the classification right to prove you see past the 'perverse feedback' that fooled the flight instructors.","key_concepts":["When performance is far above average it is probably a lucky draw, so the next attempt is likely to be worse regardless of praise.","When performance is far below average the next attempt is likely to improve regardless of punishment.","The flight instructor praised only cadets whose execution was far better than average and punished only those far worse, so he attached a causal interpretation to the inevitable fluctuations of a random process.","Depressed children are an extreme group, and extreme groups regress to the mean over time even with no treatment.","A treatment can be judged effective only by comparing a treated group to an untreated or placebo control group that is expected to improve by regression alone.","Regression to the mean has an explanation but does not have a cause.","The feedback life exposes us to is perverse: we are statistically punished for being nice and rewarded for being nasty.","Success equals talent plus luck, and great success equals a little more talent plus a lot of luck.","Our mind is strongly biased toward causal explanations and does not deal well with mere statistics."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the causal-misattribution error: when extreme performances are followed by change, System 1 automatically invents a causal story even though the change is the mathematically inevitable regression of a random process. Kahneman's flight-instructor episode shows that reward and punishment are wrongly credited for changes that regression alone explains.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each observed pattern into the column that best explains it, using what the chapter establishes about regression to the mean.","column_a_label":"Regression to the mean (statistical explanation, no causal mechanism needed)","column_b_label":"A genuine causal effect supported by controlled comparison","column_c_label":"An unfounded causal claim about regression data","rows":[{"label":"A flight cadet praised for a clean aerobatic maneuver does worse on the next attempt.","col":"a"},{"label":"A cadet screamed at for bad execution does better on his next try.","col":"a"},{"label":"In a randomized placebo-controlled trial, treated patients improve significantly more than the control group over three months.","col":"b"},{"label":"Depressed children given an energy drink show clinically significant improvement over three months.","col":"a"},{"label":"A golfer who shoots 66 on day 1 of a tournament scores higher on day 2.","col":"a"},{"label":"An athlete on the cover of Sports Illustrated performs worse the following season.","col":"a"},{"label":"A business did better this year than last, so a commentator announces the improvement was caused by last year's poor performance.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Praise genuinely impairs learning, so praised maneuvers deteriorate for that reason.","Punishment is proven more effective than reward for skill training in humans.","The energy drink was tested against a proper placebo control group."],"explanation":"The modal category is regression to the mean: every extreme observation here selects an extreme group (praised successes, punished failures, depressed children, day-1 leaders, cover athletes), and because the relevant correlation is imperfect, the next measurement pulls back toward the average without any causal mechanism. Only the randomized controlled trial (b) warrants a causal claim, and only the commentator's 'better because it was worse' claim is an unfounded causal story (c), the kind Kahneman notes would end a business commentator's television career."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"When Imperfect Correlation Guarantees Regression","visual_frame_description":"A Victorian study recreated as a museum diorama. Sir Francis Galton stands at a lectern before the Royal Institution, holding a paper titled 'Regression towards Mediocrity in Hereditary Stature' beside a table of parent and child seed sizes. Floating over the scene are seven statistical facts, each a card the player must drag to the correct column about correlation, perfect correlation, or non-correlation.","player_mission":"Sort each statistical fact into the right column: imperfect correlation (so regression follows), perfect correlation (no regression), or no correlation at all. Show you understand Galton's discovery that correlation and regression are different perspectives on the same concept.","key_concepts":["Whenever the correlation between two scores is less than perfect, there will be regression to the mean.","Correlation and regression are not two concepts but different perspectives on the same concept.","The correlation between parent and child height is not far from .50 because environmental factors have little influence on that trait.","The correlation between object sizes measured in English and in metric units is 1, meaning 100% of determinants are shared.","The correlation between self-reported height and weight among adult American males is .41.","The correlation between SAT scores and college GPA is approximately .60.","The correlation between income and education level in the United States is approximately .40.","The correlation between family income and the last four digits of the family phone number is 0.","The correlation coefficient is a measure of the relative weight of the factors two measures share."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the statistical core of the chapter: regression to the mean is an inevitable consequence of imperfect correlation between two measures. Galton needed several years and the help of the best statisticians of his time to see that filial regression in seed and stature data was just the shadow of imperfect correlation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each fact about pairs of measures into the column that describes the correlation, using the coefficients and rules the chapter presents.","column_a_label":"Imperfect correlation, so regression to the mean follows","column_b_label":"Perfect correlation, no regression","column_c_label":"No meaningful correlation at all","rows":[{"label":"Height of parents and height of their adult children (coefficient near .50).","col":"a"},{"label":"The size of objects measured precisely in inches and the size of the same objects in centimeters.","col":"b"},{"label":"Self-reported height and weight among adult American males (coefficient .41).","col":"a"},{"label":"Family income and the last four digits of the family's phone number.","col":"c"},{"label":"SAT scores and college GPA (coefficient approximately .60).","col":"a"},{"label":"Intelligence scores of spouses (less than perfect correlation).","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Regression happens only when the two measures are causally connected.","A correlation of .41 means both measures regress to different means.","The phone-number example shows correlation and regression are unrelated concepts."],"explanation":"Every genuine pair of human measures in the chapter has imperfect correlation, so each belongs in column a and each guarantees regression to the mean. Only physical measurements in two units have perfect correlation (column b, regression coefficient of 1), and only the deliberately absurd family-income-versus-phone-digits example has no meaningful correlation (column c). This is exactly the insight Galton reached only with great difficulty."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Regressive versus Nonregressive Forecasting","visual_frame_description":"A department store executive's office. A whiteboard shows the Bazerman forecasting table: four stores with 2011 sales of $11M, $23M, $18M, and $29M, a total of $61M, and an empty 2012 column that must sum to $67.1M. Around the board float six candidate forecast actions the player must sort into regressive, nonregressive, or baseline-only predictions.","player_mission":"Classify each forecasting action as regressive (correct), nonregressive (biased), or a pure baseline. Then you will be ready to fill the 2012 column the way the chapter's lesson demands.","key_concepts":["The obvious solution of adding 10% to the sales of every store is wrong because it ignores regression to the mean.","Correct forecasts are regressive: add more than 10% to the low-performing branches and less (or even subtract) from the high performers.","Stores differ in sales because of location, competition, and random factors, and random factors are a source of regression.","The more extreme the original score, the more regression we expect, because an extreme score suggests a very lucky (or unlucky) day.","The best predicted performance on day 2 is more moderate, closer to the average, than the evidence on which it is based.","Nonregressive predictions are biased: overly optimistic for the best performers and overly pessimistic for the worst.","Regression does not have a causal explanation; it is a mathematically inevitable consequence of luck's role in the first observation.","Galton painfully discovered that the concept of regression is far from obvious, even to intelligent audiences."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the discipline of regressive prediction: when forecasting an outcome that is imperfectly correlated with past performance, you must pull each forecast toward the mean, adding more growth to weak branches and less to strong ones. Kahneman uses the department-store forecasting task, adapted from Bazerman, to show that even statistically literate readers find the regressive answer puzzling.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each forecasting action into the category that describes how it treats regression to the mean.","column_a_label":"Regressive (correct) prediction","column_b_label":"Nonregressive (biased) prediction","column_c_label":"Baseline-only prediction","rows":[{"label":"Adding exactly 10% to every store's 2011 sales to forecast 2012 sales.","col":"b"},{"label":"Adding more than 10% to the weakest stores and less than 10% to the strongest stores so the total rises 10%.","col":"a"},{"label":"Predicting the golfer who shot 66 on day 1 will shoot 66 again on day 2.","col":"b"},{"label":"Predicting the golfer who shot 66 on day 1 will finish day 2 closer to the tournament average.","col":"a"},{"label":"Forecasting the average GPA for all graduating seniors when told nothing about a specific student.","col":"c"},{"label":"Forecasting each candidate's future performance will exactly match his first-round screening score.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The most extreme performers keep performing at the same extreme level because talent is stable.","Regressive forecasts must add the same percentage to every unit so the total adds up.","A baseline prediction is the same thing as a nonregressive prediction."],"explanation":"The modal correct behavior is the regressive prediction (column a): pull extreme forecasts toward the average, because extreme first observations were probably inflated by luck. Flat 10% additions and exact repeat predictions are nonregressive (column b) and systematically biased in favor of the best performers and against the worst. The pure baseline (column c) applies only when you have no useful information at all, as when predicting GPA for an unnamed senior."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Flight Instructor's Training Theory","visual_frame_description":"A classroom at an Israeli Air Force base, mid-lecture. Major Avner, a gray-haired flight instructor, stands with a raised hand while other officers lean in, nodding. A case file on screen displays a timeline of his praise-punishment observations next to a red banner reading 'DECEPTION AUDIT: CAUSAL INFERENCE CHECK.'","player_mission":"Audit Major Avner's claim that punishment works and reward does not. Review his baseline behavior, his timed observations, and his interview transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis of what his data really show.","key_concepts":["The instructor's observations were astute and correct but his inference about reward and punishment was completely off the mark.","He praised only performances far better than average, which were probably lucky and likely to deteriorate regardless of praise.","He shouted only at performances far worse than average, which were likely to improve regardless of punishment.","Poor performance is typically followed by improvement and good performance by deterioration without any help from praise or punishment.","The flight instructors were trapped in an unfortunate contingency: because they punished poor performance, they were mostly rewarded by subsequent improvement even if punishment was ineffective.","Because we tend to be nice when people please us and nasty when they do not, we are statistically punished for being nice and rewarded for being nasty.","Regression to the mean has an explanation but does not have a cause.","System 1's demand for causal interpretations is a main reason regression stays obscure."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests causal misattribution of regression to the mean: the instructor's record of praise-then-worse and punishment-then-better is real, but it is a statistical artifact, not evidence about reinforcement. Kahneman's own eureka moment came from seeing that the instructor was 'right, but also completely wrong.'","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Major Avner's evidence about praise, punishment, and flight performance?","subject_baseline":"Major Avner is a seasoned flight instructor with over twenty years of training time, respected by his peers, who speaks with calm confidence and rarely interrupts. He claims to base his views strictly on what he has personally observed in the cockpit.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:42 - Raises hand immediately after the lecturer finishes speaking on reward versus punishment.","09:43 - Stands, squares his shoulders, and counts examples on his fingers as he speaks.","09:44 - Two junior officers nod emphatically as he describes cadets improving after being shouted at.","09:46 - Shrugs dismissively toward the 'reward works' principle, saying it may be fine 'for the birds.'","09:47 - Sits down, arms crossed, clearly satisfied with his refutation."],"interrogation_transcript":["Lecturer: 'Rewards for improved performance work better than punishment of mistakes.'","Avner: 'On many occasions I have praised flight cadets for clean execution of some aerobatic maneuver. The next time they try the same maneuver they usually do worse.'","Avner: 'On the other hand, I have often screamed into a cadet's earphone for bad execution, and in general he does better on his next try.'","Avner: 'So please don't tell us that reward works and punishment does not, because the opposite is the case.'","Lecturer: 'When you praised a cadet, how far above his usual level was that particular maneuver?'","Avner: 'Well, far above average, that's why I praised him.'","Lecturer: 'And when you screamed at a cadet, how bad was that attempt?'","Avner: 'Unusually bad, of course. That's why I screamed.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Major Avner is not lying, but he is wrong: he has observed regression to the mean and interpreted it as evidence that punishment works. His inference about the efficacy of reward and punishment is completely off the mark.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Evidence: He praised only performances far above the cadet's average, and shouted only at performances far below it. Conclusion: an extreme first attempt is likely to have been lucky (or unlucky), so the next attempt naturally regresses toward the cadet's typical level with no help from feedback. Therefore praise-then-worse and punishment-then-better are exactly what random fluctuation predicts, which is why his confident causal theory is unsupported. The pattern Kahneman demonstrated with the coin toss on the classroom floor confirms that the change requires no causal explanation.","distractors":["Punishment genuinely improves flight skill faster than reward, as the instructor's logs prove.","Praise actively degrades motor performance by making cadets overconfident.","The instructor is correct, and Kahneman was wrong to doubt his cockpit data."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Sportscaster's Causal Story","visual_frame_description":"A Winter Olympics broadcast booth overlooking the ski jump hill. A commentator leans into the microphone as jumpers prepare for their second jump; a live graphics feed shows his remarks as captions. A case file beside the screen labels his analysis 'CAUSAL STORY AUDIT' while the two-jump scoreboard updates behind him.","player_mission":"Audit the commentator's on-air explanations of why second jumps change. Compare his baseline style, his timed comments, and the transcript, then deliver the correct diagnosis of whether his causal stories are warranted.","key_concepts":["The ski jump event gives each athlete two jumps whose results are combined for the final score.","The sportscaster detected regression to the mean and invented causal stories for which there was no evidence.","The change from the first to the second jump does not need a causal explanation.","The change is a mathematically inevitable consequence of the fact that luck played a role in the outcome of the first jump.","It is possible the athlete is genuinely tense after a good jump or relaxed after a bad one, but the change itself requires no such account.","A player who did very well on day 1 probably enjoyed above-average luck on that day, so the best guess is that day 2 will be good but less extreme.","A player who scored 5 over par on day 1 is likely both weaker than average and unlucky, so day 2 will be below average but improved.","Regression is strange to the human mind and was only understood two hundred years after the theory of gravitation and differential calculus.","Our minds strongly prefer a causal account even when 'all there is' is the mathematics of regression."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the automatic invention of causal explanations for regression effects: the commentator noticed real regression but wrapped it in fabricated stories about tension and relaxation. Kahneman notes the stories could even be true, but they are unnecessary because the change is mathematically inevitable once luck influenced the first jump.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of the commentator's explanations for the athletes' second-jump performance?","subject_baseline":"The commentator is a seasoned Olympic broadcaster known for energetic, pattern-rich narration. He fills every pause with an explanation of form, weather, and psychology, and treats 'story' as his job even when the underlying data are thin.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:12 - After Norway's excellent first jump, the commentator's brow furrows and he lowers his voice conspiratorially.","14:13 - He gestures toward the scoreboard and predicts the athlete will 'be tense, hoping to protect his lead.'","14:18 - After Sweden's poor first jump, he brightens and smiles, describing the athlete as 'relaxed' with 'nothing to lose.'","14:19 - He taps the desk emphatically while asserting relaxation 'should help him do better.'","14:26 - He offers the same template again for a third jumper, adjusting only the names."],"interrogation_transcript":["Analyst: 'Norway had a great first jump. How do you see his second?'","Commentator: 'He will be tense, hoping to protect his lead and will probably do worse.'","Analyst: 'And Sweden, who struggled badly on his first jump?'","Commentator: 'Now he knows he has nothing to lose and will be relaxed, which should help him do better.'","Analyst: 'But is there any evidence about their heart rates or tension levels?'","Commentator: 'Well, no, but you can see it in their body language.'","Analyst: 'If we measured pulse before each jump, do you think it would back up your story?'","Commentator: 'Maybe, and maybe not. But someone has to explain why they keep flipping like this.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The commentator has correctly noticed the statistical pattern of regression to the mean but has fabricated causal stories (tension after good jumps, relaxation after bad ones) for which there is no evidence. The second-jump change needs no causal explanation.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Evidence: The analyst's final exchange shows the commentator himself admits his pulse story 'maybe' holds and 'maybe not.' Conclusion: the only robust facts are that the first jump included luck, the second jump will regress toward the athlete's mean, and the pattern holds equally when you reverse the time arrow. Because the commentator supplied cause where the data support only statistics, his diagnosis is unsound; a better analyst would describe regression directly and avoid inventing motives for the athletes.","distractors":["The commentator correctly identified nerves as the genuine cause of second-jump decline.","Relaxed athletes reliably jump farther, so his psychological account is empirically sound.","The first jump is a near-perfect predictor of the second jump, so the change is surprising."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Energy Drink Headline","visual_frame_description":"A newsroom at a health magazine. A young editor named Priya pitches a headline card reading 'Depressed Children Treated with an Energy Drink Improve Significantly over Three Months' while a fact-checker's file tab blinks 'PLACEBO WARNING.' A stack of cat-hugging and head-standing photos sits on the desk, and a control-group ledger is conspicuously empty.","player_mission":"Audit the pitch behind the energy-drink headline. Review Priya's baseline claims, her timed notes from the clinic, and her interview answers, then issue the correct diagnosis of whether the drink's improvement can be credited to the product.","key_concepts":["Depressed children are an extreme group, and extreme groups regress to the mean over time.","A group of depressed children would show a clinically significant improvement even if they did nothing.","Depressed children who stand on their head or hug a cat for twenty minutes a day would also show improvement.","The correlation between depression scores on successive occasions of testing is less than perfect, so regression to the mean applies.","To conclude a treatment is effective you must compare treated patients to an untreated or placebo control group.","The control group is expected to improve by regression alone, and the aim of the experiment is to see whether treated patients improve more than regression can explain.","Most readers of such headlines will automatically infer a cause, but the conclusion is completely unjustified.","Incorrect causal interpretations of regression effects are made even by eminent researchers, not just readers of the popular press.","The statistician Howard Wainer drew up a long list of researchers who confused mere correlation with causation."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the need for a control group when evaluating any treatment aimed at an extreme group: improvement alone cannot prove efficacy because regression to the mean guarantees change. Kahneman's fabricated headline is true in fact yet its causal conclusion is unjustified without a comparison group.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of the claim that the energy drink caused the children's improvement?","subject_baseline":"Priya is an enthusiastic health editor who genuinely believes the product line she covers. She is earnest rather than deceptive, but she consistently presents before-after improvement as proof of efficacy and rarely asks for control groups.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:02 - Slides the headline card across the desk with both hands, smiling as she reads it aloud.","10:05 - Points at the 'clinically significant' figure in the clinic report with visible pride.","10:07 - When asked about a control group, her smile flickers and she checks her notes, then moves on quickly.","10:09 - Shrugs off the cat-hugging example as 'ridiculous' without addressing the underlying logic.","10:11 - Concludes by arguing the improvement proves the drink works because the numbers are significant."],"interrogation_transcript":["Fact-checker: 'So the children were depressed, you gave them the drink, and they improved. What else was going on in those three months?'","Priya: 'Nothing systematic. Just the drink.'","Fact-checker: 'Was there a control group of depressed children who got no drink?'","Priya: 'No, the clinic did not want to deny any child the treatment.'","Fact-checker: 'Would depressed children improve on their own over three months?'","Priya: 'I don't think so, that's why the improvement is news.'","Fact-checker: 'What if I told you children who hug a cat for twenty minutes a day show the same improvement?'","Priya: 'That's ridiculous. Cats cannot cure depression.'","Fact-checker: 'Then how do you know the drink can?'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The causal claim is unjustified: the improvement is exactly what regression to the mean predicts for an extreme group over time, and without a control group there is no way to attribute the change to the drink.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Evidence: The children were selected because they were more depressed than most, placing them at an extreme that regresses toward the mean; there was no control group; and Priya had no answer when shown that cat hugging or head standing would produce the same 'clinically significant' pattern. Conclusion: the correct design compares the treated group to an untreated or placebo control group expected to improve by regression alone, then asks whether the drink adds improvement beyond that. Because that comparison is missing, the headline's causal conclusion fails; the drink could be effective, but this evidence cannot show it.","distractors":["The drink is proven effective because the improvement reached clinical significance.","The lack of a control group strengthens the result by letting every child receive treatment.","Priya's honest belief in the product is sufficient evidence of its efficacy."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Department Store Sales Forecast","visual_frame_description":"The boardroom of a department store chain at the end of 2011. A projector shows a whiteboard table: four stores with 2011 sales of $11M, $23M, $18M, and $29M, totaling $61M, and an empty 2012 column that must total $67.1M. The CEO leans forward expectantly while a forecast officer, Mira, faces the skeptical finance director.","player_mission":"Defeat the 'obvious' but wrong forecast. First study the 2011 sales extremes and decide how to distribute the economists' 10% overall growth across the stores; then defend your regressive allocation to an executive who insists each store should simply grow by 10%.","key_concepts":["All stores are similar in size and merchandise selection, but sales differ because of location, competition, and random factors.","The obvious solution of adding 10% to each store's sales is wrong.","Correct forecasts are regressive: add more than 10% to the low-performing branches and add less (or even subtract) from the best performers.","The more extreme a store's 2011 sales, the more of its performance was probably luck, so the more it should regress.","Regression to the mean inevitably occurs when the correlation between two measures is less than perfect.","The overall forecast of a 10% sales increase is the baseline total that all four individual forecasts must respect.","Nonregressive predictions are overly optimistic for the best performers and overly pessimistic for the worst.","Galton's discovery was that the concept of regression is far from obvious, even when the arithmetic is simple.","Causal stories (location advantages, management talent) will be offered for store differences, but random factors matter too."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests regressive forecasting against the intuitive equal-percentage method: because each store's 2011 result mixes stable factors with random luck, the best stores should grow less than 10% and the weakest more than 10%. Kahneman adapted this task from Bazerman's textbook to show that even the obvious arithmetic answer is statistically wrong.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Regression to the mean: forecast each store's 2012 sales by pulling the extreme 2011 performers toward the average, so the total still rises by 10% but individual growth rates differ.","stage_1_observation":"The board studies the table. Store 1 sold $11M in 2011, Store 2 $23M, Store 3 $18M, Store 4 $29M. The economists' forecast for the chain is a 10% overall increase, making the 2012 total $67.1M. Everyone in the room instinctively reaches for the same answer: add 10% to each store.","stage_1_question":"How should you allocate the 10% growth across the four stores to make the forecast regressive?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Add more than 10% to the weakest stores and less than 10% to the strongest, so the total rises by 10%: Store 1's $11M should jump well above 10%, Stores 2 and 3 around the average, and Store 4's $29M should rise by less than 10% (possibly even fall slightly). The extreme 2011 performances were partly luck, so the best store must be pulled back toward the mean while the worst store is pulled up.","stage_2_intervention":"The finance director slams the table: 'This is nonsense. Every store is the same size and carries the same merchandise. If the chain grows 10%, every store grows 10%. You're inventing winners and losers.' Mira must respond to save the regressive forecast in front of the CEO.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Mira should hold firm and explain that 2011 sales differences are partly location, competition, and random factors, and that an extreme 2011 result was probably inflated or depressed by luck. Because that luck will not repeat, the best store is likely to underperform its 2011 share next year and the weakest to improve, so the regressive allocation is the unbiased prediction. She can concede the chain-wide 10% total but refuse to apply it uniformly, framing it as anticipating regression rather than inventing winners."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Star Performer Hiring Trap","visual_frame_description":"The talent office of a management consultancy. A wall of name tags displays the screening scores of this year's new hires, with the top five circled in gold marker. The director, Carla, gestures toward the gold circles while a senior partner frowns at the first-year review rankings, which do not match the screening order.","player_mission":"Beat the star-performer trap. Study the screening scores and their first-year outcomes, decide what the screening really predicts, then intervene to stop the firm from fast-tracking the top scorers as if the screening were a perfect measure.","key_concepts":["Our screening procedure is good but not perfect, so we should anticipate regression.","We should not be surprised that the very best candidates often fail to meet our expectations.","Whenever the correlation between two scores is less than perfect, there will be regression to the mean.","The top candidates scored at an extreme, and extreme scores are likely to have been inflated by luck.","The more extreme the original score, the more regression we expect, because an extreme score suggests a very lucky performance.","Regression effects are ubiquitous, and so are misguided causal stories to explain them.","Causal explanations will be evoked when regression is detected, but they will be wrong because regression has no cause.","The event that attracts attention (top performers fading) is best explained by luck, but that explanation lacks the causal force our minds prefer.","Being 'nice when people please us and nasty when they do not' produces perverse statistical rewards and punishments in management."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the operational lesson of the chapter's closing example: an imperfect but good screening procedure guarantees that top-scoring candidates regress toward the mean, and managers who ignore this will be puzzled and unfair. Kahneman's advice is to anticipate regression and not be surprised when the very best candidates fail to meet expectations.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Screening-induced regression to the mean: the top-scoring hires earned extreme scores that were partly luck, so their first-year performance will be above average but less extreme than their screening score suggested.","stage_1_observation":"Carla's team reviews the annual intake. The five candidates with the highest screening scores were given the most coveted projects and fast-track review meetings. Their first-year performance ratings, however, are good but not spectacular, and several trail peers who scored below them. The partner suspects the screening tool is broken or that the fast-tracking somehow demotivated the stars.","stage_1_question":"What is the correct interpretation of why the top screening scorers underperformed their screening rank?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The screening is good but imperfect, so regression to the mean guarantees that the most extreme scorers were partly lucky and will perform less extremely on the next measurement. Their ratings were not 'underperforming' relative to their true ability; the screening overrated the true average standing of the extremes. No causal story about demotivation or a broken tool is needed.","stage_2_intervention":"Carla proposes fast-tracking next year's top five scorers straight into the partner track, arguing the screening found rare talent and the first-year dip was just bad luck in project assignments. You must decide how to intervene in the promotion decision.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Block the fast-track decision and instead adjust expectations: treat screening rank as an imperfect predictor, expect top scorers to regress toward the mean, and base fast-track decisions on a longer, more reliable performance sample. Communicate to Carla and the partner that the pattern is statistical and inevitable, and that rewarding the extremes on the basis of a single good-but-imperfect measurement will produce systematically disappointed managers."}]},{"id":"ch23","title":"18. Taming Intuitive Predictions","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Skilled Intuition versus Heuristic Substitution","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen montage. On the left, a chess master plays a rapid blitz move and a fireground commander points at a burning building; on the right, a graduate advisor reads a description of Julie and scribbles a GPA. Between the panels floats a card-sorting board with seven prediction situations the player must classify.","player_mission":"Sort each intuitive judgment into skilled intuition from repeated experience, heuristic substitution of an easier question, or deliberate System 2 analysis. Distinguish the two very different varieties of intuition the chapter describes.","key_concepts":["Some intuitions draw primarily on skill and expertise acquired by repeated experience.","Chess masters, fireground commanders, and physicians produce rapid automatic judgments because familiar cues are recognized.","Gary Klein described these skilled intuitions in Sources of Power.","Other intuitions arise from heuristics that substitute an easy question for the harder one that was asked.","Intuitive judgments can be made with high confidence even when based on nonregressive assessments of weak evidence.","Many professional judgments combine analysis and intuition.","Engineers' predictions rely on look-up tables, precise calculations, and explicit analyses of outcomes observed on similar occasions.","The two varieties of intuition can be subjectively indistinguishable to the person experiencing them.","Heuristic intuition substitutes an easier question without the person noticing the substitution."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the chapter's core distinction between two kinds of intuition: genuine skilled intuition built from repeated experience, and heuristic intuition that answers an easier question by substitution. Kahneman notes the two are subjectively indistinguishable, which is why heuristic predictions are delivered with such unwarranted confidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each intuitive judgment into the category that best describes how it was produced.","column_a_label":"Skilled intuition (repeated experience and recognized cues)","column_b_label":"Heuristic intuition (substituting an easier question)","column_c_label":"Deliberate System 2 analysis","rows":[{"label":"A chess master instantly sees the right move because familiar patterns are recognized.","col":"a"},{"label":"A fireground commander immediately assesses the trucks needed because the scene matches past fires.","col":"a"},{"label":"A physician instantly recognizes a familiar pattern of symptoms.","col":"a"},{"label":"Predicting Julie's GPA from the fact that she read fluently at age four.","col":"b"},{"label":"A venture capitalist judging a start-up's prospects by how impressive the founder's pitch feels.","col":"b"},{"label":"An engineer estimating concrete for a building from a look-up table and explicit calculation.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["All fast judgments are skilled intuition and equally trustworthy.","Heuristic intuition only occurs when people have no expertise at all.","Engineers' look-up table predictions are a form of skilled intuition because they are quick."],"explanation":"The modal category is skilled intuition (column a): the chess master, commander, and physician benefit from repeated experience in which familiar cues were recognized, exactly the intuitions Klein documented. Julie's GPA and the start-up pitch are heuristic (column b) because an easy question about the quality of evidence substitutes for the hard question about an uncertain future. The engineer's calculation is deliberate System 2 analysis (column c). The two intuition varieties are subjectively similar, which is why the heuristic ones feel so confident."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Baseline, Intuition, and the Corrected Prediction","visual_frame_description":"A whiteboard in a statistics lecture hall. The board shows four numbered steps under the title 'A Correction for Intuitive Predictions': start with average GPA, find the GPA that matches the evidence, estimate the correlation, and move that fraction of the distance. Floating cards present seven actual forecast moves the player must classify as baseline, intuitive, or corrected.","player_mission":"Classify each forecasting move as a baseline prediction, an intuitive nonregressive prediction, or the corrected regressive prediction from the four-step procedure. Prove you can separate the pieces of the correction method.","key_concepts":["Step 1 is the baseline: the GPA you would predict if you knew nothing beyond that Julie is a graduating senior.","Step 2 is your intuitive prediction, which matches your evaluation of the evidence.","Step 3 asks you to estimate the correlation between your evidence and the predicted variable.","Step 4: if the correlation is .30, move 30% of the distance from the average to the matching GPA.","The correlation between two measures equals the proportion of shared factors among their determinants.","Kahneman's most optimistic guess about the correlation between reading age and GPA is about 30%.","Intuitive predictions are not regressive and therefore biased.","Nonregressive predictions are overly optimistic for those who did best and overly pessimistic for those who had a bad start.","Unbiased predictions are about equally likely to overestimate and to underestimate the true value.","Correcting intuitive predictions is a task for System 2 and requires significant effort."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the four-step correction formula that tames intuitive predictions: anchor at the baseline, register the intuitive number, estimate the evidence's correlation with the outcome, and move only that fraction of the distance. Kahneman's method converts an extreme intuitive guess into an unbiased, regressed prediction.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each forecasting move into the stage of the correction procedure it represents.","column_a_label":"Baseline prediction (no useful information)","column_b_label":"Intuitive nonregressive prediction (matches the evidence)","column_c_label":"Corrected regressive prediction (moved toward the baseline)","rows":[{"label":"Predicting the average GPA for all seniors because you were told nothing about Julie.","col":"a"},{"label":"Predicting Julie's GPA is around 3.7 to 3.8 because early reading seems that impressive.","col":"b"},{"label":"An IDF officer predicts a cadet will earn an A in officer school because he earned an A in the unit's rating.","col":"b"},{"label":"Predicting each golfer's day-2 score will repeat his day-1 score exactly.","col":"b"},{"label":"With an estimated correlation of .30, moving 30% of the distance from the average GPA toward the matching GPA.","col":"c"},{"label":"Predicting the golfer who shot 66 on day 1 will finish day 2 closer to the tournament average.","col":"c"},{"label":"Assigning Julie the same percentile for GPA as for reading precocity.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The corrected prediction keeps the extreme number because intuition is the best evidence available.","The baseline should always be the intuitive prediction because it uses more information.","The correlation estimate determines which stores, not how far to move."],"explanation":"The modal category is the intuitive nonregressive prediction (column b): people match the extremeness of the prediction to the extremeness of the evidence, whether they are predicting Julie's GPA, repeating a golfer's day-1 score, or translating a unit grade into a school grade. The baseline (column a) applies only when no information exists, and the corrected prediction (column c) is what the four-step procedure produces by moving only the estimated correlation fraction of the distance from the baseline toward the intuition."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"When Extreme Predictions Are Justified","visual_frame_description":"A venture capital boardroom overlooking a city skyline. Across the table sit a conservative banker in a gray suit and a venture capitalist in a hoodie, each with a different set of decision cards. A flowchart on the wall asks: 'Are all errors equal?' and the player sorts seven decision rules into columns about unbiased prediction versus justified extremism.","player_mission":"Classify each decision rule as an unbiased moderate prediction, an extreme prediction justified by asymmetric costs, or an extreme prediction that is never justified. Master the chapter's argument that the choice of bias depends on how much worse one kind of error is than another.","key_concepts":["A preference for unbiased predictions is justified if all errors of prediction are treated alike regardless of direction.","There are situations in which one type of error is much worse than another.","When a venture capitalist looks for the next big thing, the risk of missing the next Google or Facebook matters more than small losses on failed start-ups.","The goal of venture capitalists is to call the extreme cases correctly, even at the cost of overestimating many other ventures.","For a conservative banker making large loans, the risk of a single borrower's bankruptcy may outweigh the risk of turning down several good clients.","In such cases extreme language ('very good prospect', 'serious risk of default') may be justified even if the underlying information is of only modest validity.","A rational person can invest a large sum in an enterprise most likely to fail if the rewards of success are large enough.","Rational individuals predicting a firm's revenue should consider a range of uncertainty, not a single number.","Extreme predictions and a willingness to predict rare events from weak evidence are both manifestations of System 1.","Unbiased predictions never produce the satisfying 'I thought so!' experience of correctly calling an extreme case."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the chapter's nuanced defense of extreme predictions: moderate unbiased predictions are the default ideal, but when the costs of errors are strongly asymmetric, extreme language can be justified even on weak evidence. Kahneman insists, however, that those who choose extreme predictions should remain aware they are indulging themselves.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each decision rule into the category that best describes its justification under the chapter's logic.","column_a_label":"Unbiased moderate prediction appropriate","column_b_label":"Extreme prediction justified by asymmetric error costs","column_c_label":"Extreme prediction always unjustified","rows":[{"label":"A venture capitalist aims to call the extreme cases correctly, accepting that most of her bets will fail.","col":"b"},{"label":"A conservative banker treats one borrower's bankruptcy as worse than declining several good clients, and uses strong risk language.","col":"b"},{"label":"A search committee forecasting a candidate's productivity regresses the prediction because the evidence from one talk is modest.","col":"a"},{"label":"A rational investor invests large sums in risky ventures only when the rewards of success are large enough, without deluding herself.","col":"a"},{"label":"A rational person predicting a firm's revenue reports a range of uncertainty instead of one dramatic number.","col":"a"},{"label":"A banker labels a borrower 'serious risk of default' even though the information is of modest validity, because default is catastrophic for him.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Extreme predictions are always the rational choice because they are more decisive.","Unbiased predictions are worthless because they never call extreme events correctly.","Asymmetric error costs have no bearing on whether extreme language is justified."],"explanation":"The modal category is the unbiased moderate prediction (column a): for rational decision makers with balanced error costs, moderate predictions and uncertainty ranges are the correct default. The venture capitalist and the conservative banker belong in column b because their error costs are asymmetric, making extreme bets or extreme risk language rational; nobody belongs in column c, because the chapter never endorses extremism as always wrong, only as a self-indulgence to be kept in view."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Officer School Forecasts","visual_frame_description":"An Israeli Defense Forces selection unit. Captain Mordechai, a senior interviewer, fills out a forecast card for a cadet while Kahneman's research assistant collects a stack of similar cards. A split screen compares the predicted grade distribution with the school's final grade distribution, showing nearly identical letter frequencies.","player_mission":"Audit the officer forecasts. Review Captain Mordechai's baseline grading habits, his timed forecasting behavior, and his interview answers, then diagnose why his predictions so perfectly mirror the school's grade frequencies instead of forecasting real outcomes.","key_concepts":["The unit selected candidates for officer training on the basis of a series of interviews and field tests.","The designated criterion for successful prediction was a cadet's final grade in officer school.","The validity of the ratings was known to be rather poor.","The officers were asked to give their best guess of the grade each future cadet would obtain in officer school.","The relative frequency of A's and B's in the predictions was almost identical to the frequencies in the final grades of the school.","The officers completely failed to discriminate between evaluating candidates at the unit and predicting a future grade.","They translated their own grades onto the scale used in officer school, applying intensity matching.","The failure to address the considerable uncertainty of their predictions led to predictions that were completely nonregressive.","When a link is found, WYSIATI applies: associative memory constructs the best possible story from the information available.","Intuitive predictions are almost completely insensitive to the actual predictive quality of the evidence."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests substitution and intensity matching in a real forecasting organization: officers answered 'what grade would he get here' instead of the harder question 'what grade will he earn next year,' so their predictions echoed the school's grade frequencies and ignored uncertainty. Kahneman collected a few hundred such forecasts and found them completely nonregressive.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of why the officers' forecast grades so closely matched the school's final grade distribution?","subject_baseline":"Captain Mordechai has spent years interviewing candidates and issuing letter grades that describe performance during the unit's observation period. He is skilled at evaluating candidates at the unit and proud of his judgment, but he has never tracked how his ratings relate to later officer school outcomes.","observed_nonverbal_log":["11:03 - Receives the forecast forms and reads the instructions aloud once, nodding.","11:05 - Pauses briefly, then writes a grade for the first cadet without consulting any prior notes.","11:06 - Works through the stack quickly, roughly one card every twenty seconds.","11:08 - Pauses at a borderline cadet, frowns, then assigns the same letter he gave in the unit rating.","11:12 - Hands back the finished stack, visibly satisfied, and remarks that the task 'was easy.'","11:13 - Does not ask how well such forecasts have historically tracked officer school grades."],"interrogation_transcript":["Researcher: 'This is not your usual rating. I am asking for the grade this cadet will obtain in officer school.'","Mordechai: 'Right. And he earned an A here, so he'll get an A there.'","Researcher: 'Officer school is a different environment, with different instructors and a different grading curve. Could that matter?'","Mordechai: 'A good cadet is a good cadet. Same scale, same letter.'","Researcher: 'How confident are you in each forecast?'","Mordechai: 'Very. I have met all of them.'","Researcher: 'If you compared these to the actual school grades later, would the match be perfect?'","Mordechai: 'It should be. That's the point of a forecast.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The officers committed substitution and intensity matching: they answered the easy question 'what grade did this candidate earn at the unit' instead of the hard question 'what grade will he earn in officer school,' translating their unit grades onto the school's scale and producing nonregressive predictions that ignored uncertainty.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Evidence: the predicted distribution of A's and B's matched the school's final grade frequencies almost exactly, and Mordechai's own answers reveal he saw no difference between the unit rating and the forecast. Conclusion: he evaluated current performance rather than predicting a future outcome, and because intensity matching holds the percentile constant, the prediction is as extreme as the evidence and ignores the school's uncertainty and the ratings' known poor validity. The correct approach would regress the forecasts toward the school's average grade in proportion to the (low) correlation between the ratings and final grades.","distractors":["The officers' forecasts were accurate, which is why the grade frequencies matched.","The officers deliberately copied the school's curve to please the researcher.","The matching grade frequencies prove the interviews were highly valid predictors."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Next Google Pitch","visual_frame_description":"A venture capital pitch room. Sofia, a founding partner, leans over the table as a founder finishes a demo, and she scrawls 'VERY HIGH probability of success' on a term sheet. Beside her, an analyst's laptop shows the start-up's scant early data. A banner reads 'NONREGRESSIVE PREDICTION AUDIT.'","player_mission":"Audit Sofia's forecast that this early-stage start-up is 'the next Google.' Review her baseline deal-making style, her timed reactions during the pitch, and her interview answers, then diagnose whether her extreme confidence is justified.","key_concepts":["Intuitive predictions tend to be overconfident and overly extreme.","Intensity matching yields predictions that are as extreme as the evidence on which they are based.","Confidence is determined by the coherence of the best story you can tell from the evidence at hand.","WYSIATI applies: associative memory quickly constructs the best possible story from available information.","Because of the limitations of the evidence, a venture capitalist will never be told that the probability of success for a start-up in its early stages is 'very high.'","If your predictions are unbiased, you will never have the satisfying experience of correctly calling an extreme case.","Unbiased predictions permit predicting rare or extreme events only when the information is very good.","A rational investor can invest in a venture most likely to fail if the rewards of success are large enough, without deluding herself.","Extreme predictions are a manifestation of System 1, which naturally matches the extremeness of the prediction to the extremeness of the evidence.","Correcting intuitive predictions is a task for System 2 and requires significant effort."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests overconfident nonregressive prediction: a compelling narrative makes confidence high while the actual evidence is tiny, so the 'very high probability' label is unjustified by the data. Kahneman states directly that an unbiased forecaster will never attach 'very high' probability to an early-stage start-up.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is the correct diagnosis of Sofia's 'very high probability of success' forecast for the early-stage start-up?","subject_baseline":"Sofia is a charismatic founding partner known for betting on narrative, not numbers. She routinely speaks in superlatives, prides herself on gut instinct, and has a track record of championing one dramatic success while quietly losing money on many smaller bets she barely remembers.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:02 - Leans forward the moment the founder says the product will 'change how people think about work.'","15:04 - Interrupts the demo to ask about the founder's background rather than unit economics.","15:07 - Writes 'next Google' on a napkin and slides it toward her analyst, grinning.","15:09 - When the analyst mentions that only 20 pilot users exist, Sofia waves dismissively.","15:11 - Scrawls 'VERY HIGH probability of success' on the term sheet without running any scenario analysis."],"interrogation_transcript":["Analyst: 'Sofia, you just marked the probability of success as very high. What data support that?'","Sofia: 'Did you watch that demo? The founder is exceptional, and the story is obvious.'","Analyst: 'Twenty pilot users and no revenue is a very small sample.'","Sofia: 'Google started in a garage. Small samples are exactly where the next big thing hides.'","Analyst: 'How many early-stage start-ups that you rated very high have failed?'","Sofia: 'I don't keep a count. I keep the ones I believed in.'","Analyst: 'Would you bet your bonus on this probability being accurate?'","Sofia: 'I would bet my reputation on the founder. That is what the number means.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The forecast is unjustified and nonregressive: Sofia substituted a compelling evaluation of the story for a probability judgment about an uncertain future, produced an extreme confidence number with no data behind it, and ignored the tiny sample and the base rate of start-up failure.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Evidence: twenty pilot users, no revenue, no scenario analysis, and Sofia's own refusal to track how her 'very high' ratings have fared. Conclusion: her confidence comes from the coherence of the story, not the quality of the evidence, which is exactly how System 1 generates overconfidence. The chapter states that an unbiased predictor would never call an early-stage start-up's success probability 'very high'; the rational move is to invest only if the potential payoff justifies the odds, while keeping the probability estimate moderate and honest.","distractors":["Sofia's track record of believing in founders proves her extreme probability estimate is accurate.","The small pilot sample is actually a strength because big ideas always start small.","An honest probability estimate for a start-up in its early stages should be 'very high' if the founder is exceptional."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Julie's GPA Correction","visual_frame_description":"A university advising office. On one screen, a file for Julie: 'read fluently at age four,' with a sticky note reading 'GPA ~3.7.' Beside it, a four-step whiteboard shows baseline, matching GPA, correlation estimate of .30, and the final corrected number. An advising dean, Rosa, stares at the corrected figure with her arms crossed.","player_mission":"Win the battle against the intuitive but biased GPA prediction. First apply the four-step correction to Julie's case and name the correct predicted GPA; then defend your regressive number against Dean Rosa, who prefers the exciting 3.7.","key_concepts":["Julie read fluently when she was four years old, and early reading and high GPA are both indications of academic talent.","People familiar with the American educational scene quickly estimate her GPA in the vicinity of 3.7 or 3.8.","The first step of the correction is the baseline: the average GPA for graduating seniors.","The second step is the GPA that matches your impression of the evidence.","The third step is estimating the correlation between the evidence (reading age) and GPA.","Kahneman's most optimistic estimate of that correlation is about 30%.","The final step moves 30% of the distance from the average to the matching GPA.","WYSIATI applies: associative memory constructs the best possible story from the available information.","Intuitive predictions are nonregressive and therefore systematically biased.","The corrected prediction is influenced by intuition but far more moderate."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the four-step correction formula using the chapter's central example: Julie's GPA should be predicted by moving only the estimated correlation fraction of the distance from the baseline toward the intuitive 3.7-3.8, yielding a much more moderate number. Kahneman's method makes predictions unbiased so errors do not systematically favor high or low outcomes.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The correction for intuitive predictions: start from the baseline, register the intuitive matching GPA, estimate the correlation between evidence and outcome, and move only that fraction of the distance from the baseline toward the intuition.","stage_1_observation":"Rosa reviews the file: Julie is a graduating senior who read fluently at age four. Everyone in the office immediately feels the GPA should be around 3.7 or 3.8. The four-step board shows the baseline average GPA for seniors (about 3.0), the matching GPA for early reading (about 3.75), and the estimated correlation between reading precocity and GPA of .30.","stage_1_question":"Applying the four-step correction with a .30 correlation, what GPA should you actually predict for Julie?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Move 30% of the distance from the baseline (about 3.0) toward the matching GPA (about 3.75), which lands around 3.2. You should not report 3.7-3.8: the intuitive number matches the extremeness of the evidence but ignores uncertainty, and with only a .30 correlation you are allowed to move only 30% of the distance, producing a moderate, regressive prediction.","stage_2_intervention":"Dean Rosa pushes back: 'This is ridiculous. A student who read fluently at four is clearly a 3.7 student. If we record 3.2 on her academic profile, we undersell a brilliant young woman. Change the number.'","stage_2_correct_answer":"Hold the regressive prediction and explain that 3.2 is the unbiased estimate because early reading and GPA share only about 30% of their determinants; the rest is specific factors and chance. Remind Rosa that predicting 3.7 would, on average, be overly optimistic for early readers, and that unbiased predictions are about equally likely to over- and underestimate. She may still invest emotionally in the student, but the forecast should remain moderate, with the acknowledgment that unbiased predictions never produce the thrilling 'I thought so' outcome."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Kim versus Jane","visual_frame_description":"A university search-committee room. On the screen, two candidate files: Kim, with spectacular recommendations and a brilliant talk but no substantial track record, and Jane, with three productive postdoctoral years but a less sparkling interview. Committee members lean toward Kim's file, which glows with the halo of a great impression.","player_mission":"Beat the WYSIATI-driven impulse to pick Kim. First decide which candidate's future productivity regresses more toward the mean and why; then guide the committee to the defensible hiring choice under the law of small numbers.","key_concepts":["Kim has spectacular recommendations, gave a brilliant talk, and impressed everyone in her interviews.","Kim has no substantial track record of scientific productivity.","Jane held a postdoctoral position for three years, is very productive, and has an excellent research record.","Jane's talk and interviews were less sparkling than Kim's.","The intuitive choice favors Kim because she left a stronger impression, and because of WYSIATI.","There is much less information about Kim than about Jane: a smaller sample, so extreme outcomes are much more likely.","More luck is in the outcomes of small samples, so you should regress your prediction more deeply toward the mean for Kim.","When you allow for the fact that Kim is likely to regress more than Jane, you might end up selecting Jane.","In the context of academic choices, Kahneman would vote for Jane, but it would be a struggle to overcome the impression that Kim is more promising.","Following our intuitions is more natural, and somehow more pleasant, than acting against them."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the law of small numbers applied to personnel prediction: Kim's brilliance rests on a small sample of impressive observations full of luck, so her future productivity should be regressed more deeply toward the mean than Jane's well-documented record. Kahneman says he would vote for Jane, but only by struggling against his intuition.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The law of small numbers in prediction: with less information about a candidate, extreme outcomes are more likely to be luck, so the future should be regressed more deeply toward the mean; the candidate with the longer record deserves more weight.","stage_1_observation":"The committee narrows the search to Kim and Jane. Kim's file glitters: spectacular recommendations, a brilliant talk, rave interviews, but no substantial publication record. Jane's file is solid: three years as a productive postdoc with an excellent research record, but her talk and interviews were less sparkling. The room's energy visibly tilts toward Kim.","stage_1_question":"Which candidate's future scientific productivity should be regressed more deeply toward the mean, and why?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Kim's. The evidence about her is a much smaller sample: spectacular impressions from a short contact window with no track record to back them. Small samples produce more extreme and luckier outcomes, so her observed brilliance is more likely to regress toward the mean than Jane's documented three-year record. The correct forecast pulls Kim's prediction down farther than Jane's.","stage_2_intervention":"The committee chair announces they will offer the position to Kim: 'Her talk was the best we have ever seen. This is the obvious hire.' You must decide how to steer the vote before the offer is made.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Argue for weighting the evidence correctly: Jane's larger sample of actual productivity is more reliable, and once Kim's prediction is regressed deeply toward the mean, Jane becomes the better bet. Recommend Jane, exactly as Kahneman says he would vote, while acknowledging that overriding the intuitive pull toward Kim is a real struggle; you can also suggest deferring judgment on Kim's fast-track potential until more of her track record accumulates."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The One-Review Trap","visual_frame_description":"A procurement desk at a retail buyer's office. Mateo, the procurement director, stares at two browser tabs: one brand with three five-star reviews, another with hundreds of reviews averaging 4.4. A graph shows the first brand's average trending downward as review count grows. His assistant points triumphantly at the five stars.","player_mission":"Defeat the one-review trap. First decide what the three five-star reviews actually predict about the brand's quality; then stop your assistant from ordering based on the small sample and choose the defensible procurement strategy.","key_concepts":["I read one review of that brand and it was excellent; still, that could have been a fluke.","Consider only the brands that have a large number of reviews and pick the one that looks best.","The law of small numbers: extreme outcomes are much more likely to be observed in small samples.","There is more luck in the outcomes of small samples.","When a link is found, WYSIATI applies: associative memory constructs the best possible story from the available information.","Intuitive predictions are almost completely insensitive to the actual predictive quality of the evidence.","A single excellent observation does not justify an extreme prediction of the true average.","Regression to the mean explains why the three-review brand's rating will likely drop as more reviews accumulate.","Unbiased predictions permit predicting extreme outcomes only when the information is very good.","Correcting intuitive predictions is a task for System 2 and requires significant effort."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the law of small numbers in everyday purchasing judgment: a handful of perfect reviews is a tiny sample whose extreme average is mostly luck, so it should regress toward the true quality as more data arrive. Kahneman's own advice is to consider only brands with a large number of reviews and pick the best among those.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The law of small numbers in data interpretation: small samples produce extreme and unreliable outcomes, so a brand's perfect-but-tiny review average is more likely to regress toward its true quality than a large sample's rating is to move.","stage_1_observation":"Mateo's assistant found a new supplier brand with only three reviews, all five stars, displayed prominently in the market. An established brand has hundreds of reviews averaging 4.4. The assistant argues the five-star brand is obviously superior because its average is higher.","stage_1_question":"What does the three-review brand's perfect average actually predict about the quality of its product?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Almost nothing reliable: three five-star ratings are a tiny sample, so the perfect average is very likely to be a fluke of luck and will regress toward the brand's true quality as more reviews accumulate. The correct expectation is a rating substantially below 5.0, while the 4.4 average based on hundreds of reviews is a far better estimate of actual quality.","stage_2_intervention":"The assistant has already prepared a purchase order for a large batch of the three-review brand, arguing that a 5.0 rating with rising demand makes it a bargain. Mateo must decide what to do before the order is submitted.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Cancel the small-sample order and switch procurement to the brand with a large number of reviews and the best stable rating, exactly as the chapter advises. Explain to the assistant that a perfect score on three reviews is expected to regress toward the mean, that extreme outcomes are much more likely in small samples, and that an honest estimate of the brand's quality must discount the perfect average until a large sample confirms it."}]},{"id":"ch24","title":"19. The Illusion of Understanding","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Halo, Outcome, and Hindsight Bias","visual_frame_description":"A warm living room in the evening. Dana and her brother Marcus argue about her failed startup while her friend Priya watches from the couch. Three labeled trays sit on the coffee table: Halo Effect, Outcome Bias, and Hindsight Bias. Statement cards float above the table, each describing a snippet of everyday judgment, and the player drags each card to the tray it best fits.","player_mission":"Sort each statement card into the mechanism it best illustrates. Read the situation behind each card and decide whether it shows the halo effect warping trait judgments, a decision judged by its outcome, or a memory rewritten after the fact.","key_concepts":["The halo effect inclines us to match our view of all of a person's qualities to our judgment of one attribute that is particularly significant.","If we think a baseball pitcher is handsome and athletic, we are likely to rate him better at throwing the ball, too.","Halos can be negative: if we think a player is ugly, we will probably underrate his athletic ability.","Hindsight bias is the I-knew-it-all-along effect, first demonstrated by Baruch Fischhoff when he was a student in Jerusalem.","Fischhoff and Ruth Beyth asked people to recall the probabilities they had assigned to Nixon's 1972 diplomatic outcomes; events that occurred had their earlier probability exaggerated.","Outcome bias makes it almost impossible to evaluate a decision properly in terms of the beliefs that were reasonable when the decision was made.","People are prone to blame decision makers for good decisions that worked out badly and to give them too little credit for successful moves that appear obvious only after the fact.","Actions that seemed prudent in foresight can look irresponsibly negligent in hindsight.","The worse the consequence, the greater the hindsight bias.","Hindsight and the outcome bias also bring undeserved rewards to irresponsible risk seekers, such as a general or an entrepreneur who took a crazy gamble and won."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the hindsight bias, the I-knew-it-all-along effect in which people who cannot reconstruct their past beliefs retrieve their current ones instead, an instance of substitution. Fischhoff and Beyth demonstrated it in 1972 when participants exaggerated the probabilities they had originally assigned to the Nixon trip outcomes that actually occurred.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus insists he warned Dana months ago that her startup would fail. Which mechanism best explains his confident claim that he knew all along?","column_a_label":"Halo Effect","column_b_label":"Outcome Bias","column_c_label":"Hindsight Bias","rows":[{"label":"A recruiter hears that a candidate's former company went bankrupt and concludes the candidate must be careless.","col":"b"},{"label":"An athlete considered unattractive is judged a weaker thrower despite strong performance statistics.","col":"a"},{"label":"After a merger collapses, an analyst recalls assigning it a high chance of failure all along.","col":"c"},{"label":"A successful CEO is described as flexible, methodical, and decisive.","col":"a"},{"label":"A jury concludes a standard low-risk surgery was actually risky because an unpredictable accident killed the patient.","col":"b"},{"label":"Students who learn that debris blocked a river and caused flood damage are far more likely to say Duluth should have hired a bridge monitor.","col":"b"},{"label":"Years after the fact, people say they knew the 2008 financial crisis was inevitable.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Marcus is deliberately lying to look smart in front of Dana.","Marcus wrote down his warning and can produce a dated, verified record of prescience.","Marcus is committing the outcome bias by judging Dana's decision harshly."],"explanation":"Marcus's claim is best explained by hindsight bias: asked to reconstruct his former belief, he retrieves his current one instead, an instance of substitution. The matrix also shows the halo effect (one salient attribute bending all trait judgments) and the outcome bias (a decision judged by its outcome rather than by the beliefs that were reasonable at the time)."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Deconstructing the Google Success Story","visual_frame_description":"A startup accelerator conference room. Consultant Marco projects a slide titled How Google Won with four tidy bullets: two clever Stanford graduate students, a superior search idea, a series of decisions that worked out, and the buyer who once refused their offer to sell for under a million dollars. Founders take notes while a counter on the wall ticks up the number of failure stories the slide omits.","player_mission":"Classify each statement from the chapter into the mechanism that produces it: the narrative fallacy, the WYSIATI rule, or regression to the mean. The goal is to see why Marco's inspiring lesson about business success is largely illusory.","key_concepts":["Taleb's narrative fallacy describes how flawed stories of the past shape our view of the world and our expectations for the future.","The explanatory stories people find compelling are simple, concrete, and assign a larger role to talent, stupidity, and intentions than to luck.","Stories focus on a few striking events that happened rather than on the countless events that failed to happen.","The human mind does not deal well with nonevents.","The ultimate test of an explanation is whether it would have made the event predictable in advance; no story of Google's success meets that test.","The more luck was involved in an event, the less there is to be learned from it.","WYSIATI: you cannot help dealing with the limited information you have as if it were all there is to know.","It is paradoxically easier to construct a coherent story when you know little, because there are fewer pieces to fit into the puzzle.","Our conviction that the world makes sense rests on our almost unlimited ability to ignore our ignorance.","Regression to the mean explains why gaps between successful and less successful firms tend to shrink over time."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the narrative fallacy in combination with the WYSIATI rule: System 1 builds the simplest coherent story from the information at hand and treats it as complete, ignoring the counterfactual events that never happened. Because luck is underweighted in such stories, the sense of understanding they produce is largely illusory, and regression to the mean quietly erases the apparent patterns.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marco tells the founders that the Google story proves they can predict which startups will succeed. Which mechanism best explains why his lesson is largely illusory?","column_a_label":"Narrative Fallacy","column_b_label":"WYSIATI","column_c_label":"Regression to the Mean","rows":[{"label":"The story is told as a simple, concrete sequence of good choices with no mention of the many paths that failed.","col":"a"},{"label":"One lucky incident, the buyer refusing the founders' offer to sell for less than one million dollars, makes the story even more compelling.","col":"a"},{"label":"The mind focuses on the striking events that happened and ignores the countless events that failed to happen.","col":"a"},{"label":"You build the best possible story from the limited information you have, as if it were all there is to know.","col":"b"},{"label":"It is easier to construct a coherent story when you know little, because there are fewer pieces to fit into the puzzle.","col":"b"},{"label":"The gap in profitability between Built to Last's outstanding firms and its laggards shrank to almost nothing in the period following the study.","col":"c"},{"label":"Fortune's Most Admired companies later earned much lower stock returns over twenty years than the least admired firms.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The lesson fails because the Google founders were unusually skilled and their skill cannot be replicated.","The lesson fails because the historical details about Stanford are inaccurate.","The lesson fails because regression to the mean guarantees that most startups will fail."],"explanation":"Marco's lesson is best explained by the narrative fallacy: the Google story is a tidy causal account that hides luck and nonevents, so it cannot make the outcome predictable in advance. The matrix also sorts WYSIATI claims, which explain why the coherent story feels complete, and regression to the mean, which explains why celebrated patterns of firm success shrink over time."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why Success-Book Lessons Fail","visual_frame_description":"A business book fair inside a bright airport bookstore. A display table features Built to Last, In Search of Excellence, and a biography of a gambler-general crowned with a laurel of prescience. Shoppers nod at the glowing cover quotes while a projection behind the counter shows stock return charts in which the stars' advantage shrinks to a flat line over time.","player_mission":"Classify each claim into the mechanism driving it: the halo effect, regression to the mean, or the risk aversion that hindsight fosters. Determine which bias manufactures the illusion that business success books sell.","key_concepts":["The halo effect inclines us to match our view of all the qualities of a person to our judgment of one attribute that is particularly significant.","The CEO of a successful company is likely to be called flexible, methodical, and decisive; the same executive in a failing company is described as confused, rigid, and authoritarian.","Because of the halo effect, we get the causal relationship backward: we believe the firm fails because its CEO is rigid, when the CEO appears rigid because the firm is failing.","A few lucky gambles can crown a reckless leader with a halo of prescience and boldness.","Although hindsight and the outcome bias generally foster risk aversion, they also bring undeserved rewards to irresponsible risk seekers who won.","Decision makers who expect to have their decisions scrutinized with hindsight are driven to bureaucratic solutions and an extreme reluctance to take risks.","The average gap between outstanding and less successful firms must shrink, because the original gap was due in good part to luck, which is regression to the mean.","In the presence of randomness, regular patterns can only be mirages.","The average profitability of the companies identified in In Search of Excellence dropped sharply within a short time.","A study of Fortune's Most Admired Companies found that over twenty years the worst-rated firms went on to earn much higher stock returns than the most admired firms."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how the halo effect and outcome bias combine to manufacture illusory insight about leaders, while regression to the mean erases the very patterns that success books celebrate. The chapter's evidence includes Rosenzweig's critique of Built to Last and the sharp post-publication decline of its stars' advantage.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A general who gambled recklessly and won is celebrated as bold and prescient. Which mechanism best explains his new reputation for insight?","column_a_label":"Halo Effect","column_b_label":"Regression to the Mean","column_c_label":"Risk Aversion from Hindsight","rows":[{"label":"A lucky gamble crowns a reckless leader with a halo of prescience and boldness.","col":"a"},{"label":"The same executive is called confused, rigid, and authoritarian a year after the firm's results sour.","col":"a"},{"label":"The CEO appears rigid because the firm is failing, even though we believe the firm fails because its CEO is rigid.","col":"a"},{"label":"The average profitability of the companies featured in In Search of Excellence dropped sharply within a short time.","col":"b"},{"label":"The gap in stock returns between Built to Last's stars and its laggards shrank to almost nothing after the study.","col":"b"},{"label":"Hindsight pushes physicians to order more tests and refer more cases to specialists, protecting themselves more than their patients.","col":"c"},{"label":"Accountable decision makers who expect hindsight scrutiny become extremely reluctant to take risks and retreat to standard operating procedures.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The general's victory proves that his strategy was rational and worth copying.","The celebration reflects regression to the mean in military outcomes.","The general is rewarded because the outcome bias systematically punishes cautious rivals."],"explanation":"The general's new reputation is best explained by the halo effect: a single salient success, the lucky win, is extended to his character, making him look prescient. The matrix also shows regression to the mean erasing the apparent advantage of celebrated firms and hindsight-driven risk aversion distorting the behavior of accountable decision makers."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing an I-Knew-It-All-Along Claim","visual_frame_description":"A quietly lit conference room at a wealth management firm. Priya, a financial adviser in a gray blazer, sits across from an internal auditor holding a file of client notes. A wall clock and a recorded video feed are visible. The audit is examining her claim that she warned clients about the 2008 crisis months in advance.","player_mission":"Audit Priya's claim of prescience. Compare her baseline behavior, the timed observations, and her answers during the interview, then decide whether her warning was genuine foresight or a hindsight-biased reconstruction.","key_concepts":["Hindsight bias is the I-knew-it-all-along effect, first demonstrated by Baruch Fischhoff when he was a student in Jerusalem.","Fischhoff and Ruth Beyth surveyed people before Nixon's 1972 trips to China and Russia and later asked them to recall the probabilities they had assigned; events that occurred had their earlier probability exaggerated.","Asked to reconstruct their former beliefs, people retrieve their current ones instead, an instance of substitution, and many cannot believe that they ever felt differently.","Your inability to reconstruct past beliefs will inevitably cause you to underestimate the extent to which you were surprised by past events.","Once you adopt a new view of the world, you immediately lose much of your ability to recall what you used to believe before your mind changed.","The tendency to revise the history of one's beliefs in light of what actually happened produces a robust cognitive illusion.","People were driven to overstate the accuracy not only of their own original predictions but also of those made by others.","The people who thought there would be a financial crisis could not conclusively show it at the time, so the crisis was not knowable.","The words know, intuition, and premonition are reserved for past thoughts that turned out to be true, which fosters the illusion that the world is more knowable than it is.","Many intelligent and well-informed people were keenly interested in the future of the economy and did not believe a catastrophe was imminent, implying the crisis was not knowable."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the hindsight bias: because people cannot reconstruct past beliefs, they substitute their current ones, so I-knew-it-all-along claims are usually self-deception rather than prescience. Fischhoff and Beyth's Nixon study and the many people who claimed to have known the 2008 crisis were inevitable are the chapter's evidence for the bias.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya claims she warned her clients about the 2008 financial crisis months before it hit. Is her claim genuine prescience, or a hindsight-biased reconstruction?","subject_baseline":"Priya is a cautious, detail-oriented financial adviser who almost never makes emphatic market calls. In eleven years of client notes, her strongest written statements are hedged, such as risks are elevated but the timing is unpredictable. She is modest in meetings and frequently corrects her own overstatements.","observed_nonverbal_log":["00:00-00:45: Priya sits relaxed, makes steady eye contact, and smiles; her posture is open and she answers the opening questions fluidly.","01:30: Asked for the exact date or wording of her warning, she pauses, looks at the ceiling, and rubs her neck; her speech slows and she starts with Well, it was somewhere in.","02:40: Asked to produce the written note she claims to have sent, she shuffles papers and says it was not in writing, it was at a lunch.","03:15: When pressed for the reasoning she used at the time, she gives a confident, tidy account: everyone knew leverage was out of control.","04:00: Asked whether she made the same call in 2005, when markets were still rising, she laughs and says no one could have called it that early."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: When exactly did you tell clients to get out of the market?","Priya: Months before the crash. I remember the lunch at the bistro, I told Marcus it was a house of cards.","Interviewer: Can you show me the note or the email?","Priya: It wasn't written down. I said it at a lunch.","Interviewer: What was your reasoning back then?","Priya: Everyone knew leverage was out of control.","Interviewer: Did you make the same warning in 2005, before the market peaked?","Priya: No one could have called it that early."],"correct_diagnosis":"Hindsight bias, not genuine prescience. Priya has substituted her current belief for her actual past belief and has no recoverable contemporaneous record of warning anyone.","diagnostic_breakdown":["No written record exists, yet her confidence is high and her story is a tidy causal narrative, everyone knew, the same flavor of after-the-fact coherence Fischhoff and Beyth measured when people exaggerated the probabilities they had assigned to events that actually occurred.","Her stated reasoning is only describable in hindsight terms; she articulates no distinctive belief she held at the time that other well-informed people did not also hold.","Her own admission that no one could have called it that early in 2005 undercuts the claim of knowability, matching the chapter's point that many people who claimed to know the 2008 crisis was inevitable could not conclusively show it at the time.","The nonverbal cues, pausing, neck-rubbing, and vagueness about specifics, appear precisely when she is asked to reconstruct her past beliefs, the task people fail because they retrieve current beliefs instead, an instance of substitution."],"distractors":["Priya is deliberately lying to attract new clients.","Priya showed genuine prescience and should be promoted for her market timing.","Priya's clients are committing the outcome bias against her."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing an Outcome-Biased Review","visual_frame_description":"A hospital review board hearing in a windowless conference room. Dr. Okafor, a general surgeon, sits at the end of a long table facing five reviewers who hold a patient's death certificate. Board chair Maria Rojas leads the review of a standard low-risk surgery that ended in an unpredictable complication and the patient's death.","player_mission":"Audit the review board's reasoning. Determine whether their verdict that Dr. Okafor was negligent reflects a fair evaluation of the decision or the outcome bias, in which a decision is judged by its result rather than by the beliefs that were reasonable at the time.","key_concepts":["Outcome bias makes it almost impossible to evaluate a decision properly in terms of the beliefs that were reasonable when the decision was made.","Consider a low-risk surgical intervention in which an unpredictable accident occurred that caused the patient's death: the jury will be prone to believe after the fact that the operation was actually risky and that the doctor who ordered it should have known better.","Actions that seemed prudent in foresight can look irresponsibly negligent in hindsight.","We are prone to blame decision makers for good decisions that worked out badly and to give them too little credit for successful moves that appear obvious only after the fact.","When outcomes are bad, clients often blame their agents for not seeing the handwriting on the wall, forgetting that it was written in invisible ink that became legible only afterward.","Hindsight is especially unkind to decision makers who act as agents for others, such as physicians, financial advisers, and CEOs.","Hindsight bias leads observers to assess the quality of a decision not by whether the process was sound but by whether its outcome was good or bad.","The worse the consequence, the greater the hindsight bias.","In the Duluth bridge-monitor study, 56% of participants told that debris had blocked the river and caused major flood damage said the city should have hired the monitor, versus 24% who saw only the evidence available at the time.","Because adherence to standard operating procedures is difficult to second-guess, decision makers who expect hindsight scrutiny are driven to bureaucratic solutions and an extreme reluctance to take risks."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the outcome bias: when outcomes are bad, observers judge a decision by its result rather than by the beliefs that were reasonable when it was made. The chapter's Duluth and malpractice examples show that hindsight knowledge routinely rewrites what a good decision was, punishing agents who made sound choices that turned out badly.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After a patient died from an unpredictable complication following standard low-risk surgery, the hospital review board concluded Dr. Okafor was negligent. Is the reviewers' verdict sound, or does outcome bias explain it?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Okafor is a careful general surgeon with twelve years of clean reviews, a protocol-adherent record, and complication rates at the hospital average. The review board members are experienced physicians and administrators who normally weigh evidence and cite guidelines in their written reports.","observed_nonverbal_log":["00:00-00:30: Chair Maria Rojas opens the review with a heavy sigh and glances at the patient's death certificate before reading the surgical notes.","01:10: When the anesthesia record is shown to be standard, three members exchange raised eyebrows and lean back in their chairs.","02:00: Board member Tom Ibarra repeatedly underlines the word death in the chart margin while Dr. Okafor testifies that every protocol was followed.","03:20: Asked whether they would have approved the same surgery before the complication, the members fall silent; no one answers for several seconds.","03:50: Chair Rojas says that given the outcome, the procedure was clearly too risky to order, and the board votes to censure Dr. Okafor."],"interrogation_transcript":["Dr. Okafor: The consent form listed this complication. It is a known, rare event after this procedure.","Tom Ibarra: But the patient is dead. That changes everything.","Maria Rojas: Given the outcome, the procedure was clearly too risky to order.","Dr. Okafor: You reviewed this exact protocol last year and called it standard of care.","Maria Rojas: Last year nothing went wrong.","Tom Ibarra: The handwriting was on the wall. A good surgeon would have seen it."],"correct_diagnosis":"The reviewers are committing outcome bias. The decision to operate was sound under the beliefs and evidence available at the time, and the unpredictable accident turned a good decision into a fatal one, so the board judged the decision by its outcome.","diagnostic_breakdown":["The surgical protocol matched hospital standards and the complication was a known rare risk, exactly the low-risk surgical intervention in which an unpredictable accident caused the patient's death that the chapter says will make a jury prone to believe after the fact that the operation was actually risky.","Board members' confidence rises only after the outcome is known: they reviewed the same protocol a year earlier and changed their verdict solely because the patient died.","Tom Ibarra's the handwriting was on the wall is the chapter's own image of hindsight: the ink was invisible until after the event, so the good decision can look irresponsibly negligent in hindsight.","The board failed the test of judging the decision by the beliefs that were reasonable when it was made, which the outcome bias makes almost impossible.","The nonverbal log shows outcome knowledge driving the verdict: sighs and underlining track the death certificate and the word death, not the procedural evidence, and no one answers when asked what they would have approved before the complication."],"distractors":["Dr. Okafor was negligent because the complication occurred.","Dr. Okafor is suffering from hindsight bias about his own skills.","The board was correctly applying a zero-tolerance patient safety policy."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Fighting the Narrative Fallacy","visual_frame_description":"A conference room at a startup accelerator. Consultant Marco projects a slide titled How Google Won with four bullet points: two clever Stanford graduate students, superior search, a series of decisions that worked out, and the buyer who once refused their offer to sell for less than a million dollars. The founders take notes while no slide mentions competitors, failed paths, or market conditions.","player_mission":"Defeat the boss, the narrative fallacy. In stage one, identify the feature that makes Marco's story feel coherent but uninformative. In stage two, choose the intervention that stops the founders from adopting his four-step playbook as a reliable lesson.","key_concepts":["Taleb's narrative fallacy describes how flawed stories of the past shape our view of the world and our expectations for the future.","The explanatory stories that people find compelling are simple, concrete, and assign a larger role to talent, stupidity, and intentions than to luck.","Stories focus on a few striking events that happened rather than on the countless events that failed to happen.","The ultimate test of an explanation is whether it would have made the event predictable in advance; no story of Google's unlikely success will meet that test.","The human mind does not deal well with nonevents.","Because every critical decision turned out well, the record suggests almost flawless prescience, though bad luck could have disrupted any one of the successful steps.","The more luck was involved in the actual event, the less there is to be learned from it.","WYSIATI: you build the best possible story from the information available to you, and if it is a good story, you believe it.","The halo effect adds the final touches, lending an aura of invincibility to the heroes of the story.","Our comforting conviction that the world makes sense rests on our almost unlimited ability to ignore our ignorance."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the narrative fallacy: System 1's sense-making machinery turns a lucky sequence of choices into a tidy causal story that hides luck and nonevents. Because no story can include all the events that would have caused a different outcome, the sense of understanding such stories produce is largely illusory.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the narrative fallacy, Taleb's term for how flawed stories of the past shape our view of the world and our expectations for the future. Compelling stories are simple, concrete, weight talent and intentions over luck, and ignore the countless events that did not happen, so the sense of understanding they produce is largely illusory.","stage_1_observation":"Marco's slide ends with a flourish: the buyer who once refused the founders' offer to sell the company for less than one million dollars. The founders lean forward, visibly impressed, and one writes down the four steps. The counter on the wall showing omitted failure paths keeps climbing.","stage_1_question":"Which single feature should make the founders treat the Google story as an unreliable source of business lessons?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The story omits the counterfactual events, the myriad ways the outcome could have differed, so it can never meet the test of making the event predictable in advance.","stage_2_intervention":"Marco now proposes that the founders adopt his four-step playbook for guaranteed startup success. You are advising the founders. Which action best counters the narrative fallacy before they commit?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Run a counterfactual and base-rate review: ask what percentage of similar startups fail despite equally good decisions, and price in the fact that the more luck was involved, the less there is to be learned."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Beating the Outcome Bias Tribunal","visual_frame_description":"A municipal hearing in Duluth, Minnesota, years after a spring flood. Debris blocked the river and floodwater damaged the town. A city council panel reopens the decision not to hire a full-time bridge monitor. The room is split into two groups: one reads the damage report, the other sees only the evidence available at decision time.","player_mission":"Defeat the boss, the outcome bias in institutional review. In stage one, predict what the two groups will conclude about the city's decision. In stage two, choose the intervention that protects the original decision maker from being judged by the flood that followed.","key_concepts":["In the Duluth bridge-monitor study, 24% of participants shown only the evidence available at the time of the city's decision felt that Duluth should take on the expense of hiring a flood monitor.","The second group, told that debris had blocked the river and caused major flood damage, produced 56% who said the city should have hired the monitor.","The second group produced this result even though they had been explicitly instructed not to let hindsight distort their judgment.","Outcome bias makes it almost impossible to evaluate a decision properly in terms of the beliefs that were reasonable when the decision was made.","Actions that seemed prudent in foresight can look irresponsibly negligent in hindsight.","Hindsight is especially unkind to decision makers who act as agents for others, such as physicians, financial advisers, and city officials.","The worse the consequence, the greater the hindsight bias.","People are prone to blame decision makers for good decisions that worked out badly.","Because adherence to standard operating procedures is difficult to second-guess, decision makers who expect their decisions to be scrutinized with hindsight are driven to bureaucratic solutions.","Increased accountability is a mixed blessing, fostering risk aversion and an extreme reluctance to take risks."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the outcome bias and its resistance to instruction: even explicitly warned, people judge the Duluth decision by the flood that followed, with 56% versus 24% finding the city negligent. Hindsight and outcome bias foster risk aversion in accountable decision makers, pushing them toward bureaucratic, self-protective choices.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is outcome bias in institutional review: evaluating a decision by its outcome rather than by the beliefs that were reasonable at the time. The chapter's Duluth study shows that people who know a flood occurred are far more likely to call the city's decision negligent, even when told not to let hindsight distort their judgment.","stage_1_observation":"The informed group reads the damage report and exchanges looks of certainty, while the uninformed group studies the original flood-risk memos from before the decision. A staffer distributes identical ballots asking each person whether the city should have hired a full-time bridge monitor.","stage_1_question":"Based on the chapter's bridge-monitor study, which outcome should you predict for the two groups?","stage_1_correct_answer":"About 56% of the informed group will say the city should have hired the monitor, versus about 24% of the group shown only the evidence available at the time, even though both were warned not to let hindsight distort their judgment.","stage_2_intervention":"The panel asks you to advise the official who made the original decision and now faces censure. Which intervention best protects against outcome bias in the panel's review?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Force the review to reconstruct the decision from the evidence available at the time and score decision quality before the outcome is revealed, evaluating the process rather than the result."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Breaking the Halo Effect","visual_frame_description":"In a boardroom, analyst Lena presents a performance review of CEO Amanda Chen. The firm's stock has doubled in two years, and the slide is filled with glowing trait labels: decisive, flexible, methodical, visionary. In gray at the bottom are two labels HR flagged as not fitting the narrative: rigid, cautious.","player_mission":"Defeat the boss, the halo effect. In stage one, explain why the team rejects rigid and cautious as descriptions of Amanda. In stage two, choose the intervention that prevents the board from overreacting when her firm's results turn sour.","key_concepts":["The halo effect inclines us to match our view of all the qualities of a person to our judgment of one attribute that is particularly significant.","The CEO of a successful company is likely to be called flexible, methodical, and decisive.","Imagine that a year has passed and things have gone sour: the same executive is now described as confused, rigid, and authoritarian.","Because of the halo effect, we get the causal relationship backward: we believe that the firm fails because its CEO is rigid, when the truth is that the CEO appears to be rigid because the firm is failing.","Halos can also be negative: if we think a player is ugly, we will probably underrate his athletic ability.","The halo effect helps keep explanatory narratives simple and coherent by exaggerating the consistency of evaluations.","A very generous estimate of the correlation between the success of a firm and the quality of its CEO might be as high as .30, indicating 30% overlap.","A correlation of .30 implies that the stronger CEO would lead the more successful firm in about 60% of pairs, a mere 10 percentage points over random guessing.","If you expected this value to be higher, that indicates you are prone to overestimate the predictability of the world you live in.","CEOs do influence performance, but the effects are much smaller than a reading of the business press suggests."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the halo effect: a salient success or failure colors every trait judgment, and the causal direction gets inverted, success seems to cause the halo rather than the halo the success. The chapter grounds this in CEO reputation studies and the modest .30 correlation between leadership quality and firm performance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the halo effect: one salient attribute of a person or firm colors every other judgment, exaggerating consistency so that good people do only good things. The chapter shows the halo flipping with fortune, the same CEO is flexible and methodical when the firm thrives and confused and rigid when it fails.","stage_1_observation":"Lena's team rejects the gray labels as absurd, noting that Amanda has made three bold, successful pivots. Below the slide, an analyst mutters that calling such a leader rigid would be ridiculous.","stage_1_question":"Why does Lena's team so confidently reject rigid and cautious as descriptions of Amanda?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the halo effect makes trait judgments match the salient success: a thriving firm's CEO seems flexible and methodical, while the same behaviors in a struggling firm would be read as rigid and confused.","stage_2_intervention":"A year later, Amanda's firm misses three quarters and the board now describes her as rigid and authoritarian. You are asked to advise the board on whether to replace her. Which intervention best counters the halo effect?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Separate the firm's current performance from an independent evaluation of Amanda's actual decisions and traits, and remember that the correlation between CEO quality and firm success is only about .30, so the stronger CEO leads the more successful firm in only about 60% of pairs."}]},{"id":"ch25","title":"20. The Illusion of Validity","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Engines Behind Confident Judgments: WYSIATI, Coherence, and Substitution","visual_frame_description":"An obstacle field on a military base: eight sweat-soaked soldiers in numbered tags haul a heavy log toward a six-foot wall while two evaluators in fatigues take notes from the side. An overlay of three color-coded cards floats above the scene, labeled WYSIATI, Confidence by Coherence, and Substitution, and the player drags each italicized judgment card onto the mechanism it best explains.","player_mission":"Sort each judgment card into the mechanism that best explains it, then confirm which single mechanism dominated Kahneman's army officer evaluations. Drag the cards into the correct columns before the hour-long session timer runs out.","key_concepts":["In the leaderless group challenge, eight candidates lifted a log over a six-foot wall, and one hour of watching produced impressions as direct and compelling as the color of the sky.","The officers' forecasts were a clear instance of substitution and of the representativeness heuristic: observed behavior stood in for future performance.","Their predictions were completely nonregressive, with no reservations about predicting failure or outstanding success from weak evidence.","WYSIATI (what you see is all there is) means only the evidence at hand counts, and System 1 is not designed to know the size of its jumps.","Confidence is a feeling that reflects the coherence of the information and the cognitive ease of processing it, not a reasoned probability of being correct.","Poor evidence can make a very good story, and the amount of evidence does not count for much in the feeling of confidence.","Nisbett and Borgida's students believed that most people did not help a stranger having a seizure, yet the base rates did not influence their judgment of the individual on the video.","People are often reluctant to infer the particular from the general, accepting base-rate information without applying it to individuals.","Knowledge of the general rule that the predictions were useless had no effect on the officers' confidence in individual cases.","Errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests substitution by the representativeness heuristic: evaluators answered the hard question of a soldier's future performance with the easy impression of one hour's behavior, and WYSIATI left them no representation of their ignorance. Kahneman & Tversky's research shows such judgments are made with high confidence even when their validity is barely above chance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Kahneman and a colleague watched eight soldiers haul a log over a six-foot wall for about an hour and felt they knew exactly who would make a strong officer. Which of the three mechanisms best explains each confidence-producing judgment below, and which mechanism dominated the army officer evaluations?","column_a_label":"WYSIATI (only the evidence at hand counts)","column_b_label":"Confidence by coherence (a coherent story creates the feeling of confidence)","column_c_label":"Substitution via representativeness (answering a hard question with a representative impression)","rows":[{"label":"Having observed one hour of a soldier's behavior in an artificial situation, the evaluators felt they knew how he would face officer training and combat.","col":"c"},{"label":"The evaluators had no good way to represent what they did not know about the factors that would eventually determine the candidate's performance.","col":"a"},{"label":"A doctor feels sure of a prognosis because every symptom fits neatly into one story, despite a medical record showing such cases are unpredictable.","col":"b"},{"label":"The soldier who took over when the exhausted group had to start over was a leader at that moment, so he must lead just as well in officer training and combat.","col":"c"},{"label":"Buyers and sellers trade billions of shares because each side attends only to the information it personally holds about the price.","col":"a"},{"label":"Nisbett and Borgida's students believed the statistic that most people did not help a seizure victim, yet still judged the man on the video as likely to help.","col":"c"},{"label":"Poor evidence can make a very good story, and the amount and quality of the evidence count for little in the feeling of confidence.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["The anchoring effect, because the first soldier evaluated sets the standard for all the rest","Loss aversion, because the evaluators feared being wrong about a candidate","The availability heuristic, because recent feedback sessions made past failures easy to recall"],"explanation":"The army forecast was a clear instance of substitution: instead of estimating the candidate's future performance, the evaluators answered with the representativeness of the behavior they had just watched, and WYSIATI left them no representation of what they did not know. Confidence by coherence then dressed the impression in the feeling of certainty, which is why the general knowledge of past failure did nothing to their confidence in individual cases."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Diagnostic Test of Skill: Persistent Achievement vs. Luck","visual_frame_description":"A research desk piled with ranked leaderboards: one board shows golfers and toll collectors whose names repeat at the top season after season, while another board shows fund names reshuffling randomly every year. A spreadsheet titled '25 wealth advisers, 8 years' sits in the corner with a large .01 highlighted in red.","player_mission":"Classify each result as evidence of skill, evidence of luck, or ambiguous, then determine what the wealth-adviser data actually demonstrated. Drag each row into the correct column before analyzing the correlation coefficient.","key_concepts":["Terry Odean's study of 10,000 brokerage accounts over seven years found that the stocks individual investors sold did better than those they bought by 3.2 percentage points per year, before trading costs.","For the large majority of individual investors, taking a shower and doing nothing would have been a better policy than implementing the ideas that came to their minds.","The diagnostic for the existence of skill is the consistency of individual differences: if performance in any year were entirely due to luck, year-to-year correlations would be zero.","Skill is confirmed by persistence, with stable rankings over time as with golfers, car salespeople, orthodontists, and speedy toll collectors on the turnpike.","Over fifty years of research show that at least two out of every three mutual funds underperform the overall market in any given year.","The year-to-year correlation of mutual fund outcomes is barely higher than zero, so the successful funds in any given year are mostly lucky.","For most stock pickers, the selection of stocks is more like rolling dice than like playing poker.","In highly efficient markets, educated guesses are no more accurate than blind guesses.","The average of 28 year-to-year correlations in the rankings of 25 wealth advisers over 8 years was .01, indistinguishable from zero.","The firm was rewarding luck as if it were skill, because the rankings varied erratically from year to year."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the diagnostic logic of skill: persistence of individual differences. Kahneman shows that when year-to-year rankings correlate at about zero, individual outcomes are chance, and he demonstrated this with 25 wealth advisers whose 28 correlations averaged .01.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Kahneman writes that the persistence of individual differences is the measure by which we confirm the existence of skill. Classify each result as evidence of skill, evidence of luck, or ambiguous, then answer what the wealth-adviser data showed.","column_a_label":"Evidence of skill (rankings are stable across time)","column_b_label":"Evidence of luck (rankings vary erratically; year-to-year correlation near zero)","column_c_label":"Ambiguous or mixed (depends on other factors)","rows":[{"label":"The average of 28 year-to-year correlations among 25 wealth advisers over 8 years was .01.","col":"b"},{"label":"Golfers and speedy toll collectors who rank high one season tend to rank high again next season.","col":"a"},{"label":"At least two out of every three mutual funds underperform the overall market in any given year.","col":"b"},{"label":"A car salesperson outsells every colleague in the dealership for five consecutive years.","col":"a"},{"label":"The most active individual traders get the poorest results while the least active earn the highest returns.","col":"c"},{"label":"The successful funds in any given year are mostly different funds from the successful ones last year.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Evidence of skill, because the advisers are experienced professionals","Evidence of skill, because a large number of correlations was computed","Ambiguous, because bonuses depend on a single year's return"],"explanation":"With a year-to-year correlation of .01, the advisers' rankings varied almost randomly from year to year, which is exactly the signature of luck rather than skill. Where skill exists, individual differences persist and rankings remain stable, as with golfers, car salespeople, and toll collectors."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Hedgehogs and Foxes in Expert Forecasting","visual_frame_description":"A television debate studio: on one side a hedgehog-style commentator gestures at a single chart, certain one force drives history; on the other side a fox-style analyst lists interacting factors and luck. A research overlay shows a twenty-year grid of 284 experts and more than 80,000 probability forecasts scored against what actually happened.","player_mission":"Classify each statement as typical of a hedgehog, a fox, or neither, then identify which type scored best in Tetlock's landmark study. Sort the statements before the debate segment ends.","key_concepts":["Tetlock's twenty-year study, published as Expert Political Judgment in 2005, gathered more than 80,000 predictions from 284 experts who commented on political and economic trends.","The experts performed worse than if they had simply assigned equal probabilities to the three potential outcomes, worse than dart-throwing monkeys distributing their choices evenly.","Even in the region they knew best, experts were not significantly better than nonspecialists.","Those who know more forecast very slightly better, but those with the most knowledge are often less reliable because they develop an enhanced illusion of their skill.","We reach the point of diminishing marginal predictive returns for knowledge disconcertingly quickly.","The more famous the forecaster, the more flamboyant the forecasts; experts in demand were more overconfident than colleagues far from the limelight.","Experts resisted admitting that they had been wrong and produced excuses: wrong only in timing, an unforeseeable event intervened, or wrong but for the right reasons.","Experts are led astray not by what they believe but by how they think, says Tetlock.","Hedgehogs know one big thing, explain events within a single coherent framework, and are confident and reluctant to admit error.","Foxes recognize that reality emerges from the interactions of many agents and forces, including blind luck, and they scored best in Tetlock's study, though their performance was still very poor."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction Tetlock drew, using Isaiah Berlin's terms, between hedgehogs who know one big thing and foxes who weigh many interacting forces. In Tetlock's twenty-year study the foxes scored best, yet both types performed poorly in absolute terms.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In Tetlock's twenty-year study of expert political judgment, one type of thinker consistently did better. Classify each statement as hedgehog, fox, or neither, then identify the type that scored best.","column_a_label":"Hedgehog (knows one big thing; one theory explains everything)","column_b_label":"Fox (recognizes many interacting forces, including blind luck)","column_c_label":"Neither (a general forecasting regularity, not a thinker type)","rows":[{"label":"Unlikely to accept the view that Ronald Reagan single-handedly ended the Cold War by standing tall against the Soviet Union.","col":"b"},{"label":"After a failed prediction, insists he was off only on timing or very nearly right.","col":"a"},{"label":"Accounted for events within a single coherent framework and bristled with impatience at those who did not see things his way.","col":"a"},{"label":"Recognized that reality emerges from the interactions of many agents and forces, including blind luck, producing large and unpredictable outcomes.","col":"b"},{"label":"The type that scored best in Tetlock's study, although its performance was still very poor.","col":"b"},{"label":"Short-term trends can be forecast, and behavior and achievements can be predicted with fair accuracy from previous behavior and achievements.","col":"c"},{"label":"Opinionated and clear, exactly what television producers love to see on programs.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The hedgehogs, because they are more confident","Nonspecialists, because they have less at stake","The most famous forecasters, because they have the most information"],"explanation":"Foxes scored best in Tetlock's study because their complex, multifactor view of the world made them less prone to confident error; even so, their performance was still poor. Hedgehogs were more confident, more flamboyant, and more reluctant to admit error, which is exactly why television producers favor them."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"What Sustains the Illusions of Skill and Validity","visual_frame_description":"A Wall Street trading floor at dusk: analysts pore over balance sheets and income statements, while a projector behind them shows the 25-adviser spreadsheet with a red .01. In the corner, an executive shakes hands at a bonus dinner, and a memo titled 'The Findings' slides into a drawer labeled 'Do Not Digest.'","player_mission":"Sort each sustaining factor into the mechanism that keeps the illusions alive, then identify the most potent psychological cause of the illusion of financial skill named by Kahneman. Sort the factors before the board meeting adjourns.","key_concepts":["One executive's defense, I have done very well for the firm and no one can take that away from me, shows personal experience outweighing base rates.","The most potent psychological cause of the illusion of financial skill is that stock pickers are exercising high-level skills, which gives them a valid experience of skilled work.","Skill in evaluating the business prospects of a firm is not sufficient for successful trading, because the key question is whether the information is already incorporated in the price of the stock.","Traders apparently lack the skill to answer that crucial question, but they appear to be ignorant of their ignorance.","Subjective confidence of traders is a feeling, not a judgment, and cognitive ease and associative coherence locate it firmly in System 1.","Professional culture sustains the illusions: people can maintain unshakable faith in any proposition when sustained by a community of like-minded believers.","The illusion of skill is not only an individual aberration but is deeply ingrained in the culture of the industry.","Facts that challenge basic assumptions, and thereby threaten people's livelihood and self-esteem, are simply not absorbed; the mind does not digest them.","Cognitive illusions can be more stubborn than visual illusions: learning about the Muller-Lyer illusion changed behavior, but knowledge of low validity changed neither feelings nor subsequent action.","Errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the three supports Kahneman identifies for the illusions of validity and skill: the genuine experience of exercising high-level skills, ignorance of ignorance in which confidence is a feeling rather than a judgment, and a professional culture of like-minded believers. He argues the most potent cause is the valid experience of skilled work, which masks the fact that the crucial information is already priced in.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Why does the illusion of financial skill persist even when the data show the results are luck? Sort each sustaining factor into its mechanism, then identify the most potent psychological cause named by Kahneman.","column_a_label":"Exercising high-level skills (valid experience of skilled work)","column_b_label":"Ignorance of ignorance (confidence is a feeling, not a judgment)","column_c_label":"Professional culture (a community of like-minded believers)","rows":[{"label":"Stock pickers consult economic data and forecasts, examine income statements and balance sheets, evaluate top management, and assess the competition.","col":"a"},{"label":"The crucial question, whether the information is already incorporated in the price, is the one traders lack the skill to answer.","col":"b"},{"label":"Cognitive ease and associative coherence locate subjective confidence firmly in System 1.","col":"b"},{"label":"People can maintain unshakable faith in any proposition, however absurd, when they are sustained by a community of like-minded believers.","col":"c"},{"label":"Statistical studies of performance provide base-rate information that people generally ignore when it clashes with their personal impressions from experience.","col":"c"},{"label":"A portfolio manager reads detailed company research and feels the immediate, valid experience of using professional skills.","col":"a"},{"label":"After hearing the .01 correlation, an executive says, I have done very well for the firm and no one can take that away from me.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The desire to impress clients with bold forecasts","Sheer greed on the part of individual investors","A conspiracy by fund managers to hide poor returns"],"explanation":"Kahneman identifies the most potent psychological cause as the fact that stock pickers are exercising high-level skills: they genuinely use expertise in evaluating businesses, and that valid experience of skilled work masks the fact that the key judgment, whether information is already priced in, is beyond them. Ignorance of ignorance and the industry culture then cement the illusion, so statistical base rates are pushed aside."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Three Illusions: Validity, Skill, and Understanding the Past","visual_frame_description":"A split triptych montage: on the left, army officers declare 'He will be a star' as a soldier finishes the obstacle wall; in the center, a bonus spreadsheet shows the same advisers reshuffling rank each year; on the right, a pundit narrates the day's news with complete hindsight certainty. A caption bar reads 'Which illusion dominates?'","player_mission":"Classify each episode by the illusion that dominates it, then decide which illusion best captures the officers' predictions on the obstacle field. Sort the episodes before the montage loop completes.","key_concepts":["Kahneman coined the term 'illusion of validity' after the Israeli Army's officer-selection tests showed his leadership forecasts were barely better than blind guesses.","In the leaderless group challenge, eight candidates lifted a log over a six-foot wall, and one hour of watching produced impressions as direct and compelling as the color of the sky.","Their predictions were completely nonregressive, with no reservations about predicting failure or outstanding success from weak evidence.","WYSIATI (what you see is all there is) means only the evidence at hand counts, and System 1 is not designed to know the size of its jumps.","Knowledge of the general rule that the predictions were useless had no effect on the officers' confidence in individual cases.","Nassim Taleb's The Black Swan argues that the ease of constructing coherent narratives of the past makes it difficult to accept the limits of forecasting ability.","Everything makes sense in hindsight, and we cannot suppress the powerful intuition that what made sense today was predictable yesterday.","The illusion that we understand the past fosters overconfidence in our ability to predict the future.","The image of the 'march of history' implies order and direction, yet the book argues that large historical events are determined by luck.","The book notes a fifty-fifty chance that the embryo that became Hitler could have been female, and one chance in eight of a twentieth century without Hitler, Stalin, and Mao."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario distinguishes three closely related illusions from the chapter: the illusion of validity in confident prediction from thin evidence, the illusion of skill when luck is rewarded as skill, and the illusion of understanding the past fostered by hindsight. Kahneman shows all three share a root: confidence by coherence and the ease of constructing stories.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"The chapter names three related illusions. Classify each episode by which illusion dominates, then decide which illusion best captures the officers' predictions on the obstacle field.","column_a_label":"Illusion of validity (confident prediction from thin, unrepresentative evidence)","column_b_label":"Illusion of skill (personal results treated as skill when they are luck)","column_c_label":"Illusion of understanding the past (hindsight makes the past look predictable)","rows":[{"label":"This one will never make it, said with certainty after one hour of watching a soldier on an obstacle field.","col":"a"},{"label":"An adviser's year-end bonus is based on returns that are statistically indistinguishable from chance.","col":"b"},{"label":"Financial pundits offer convincing accounts of the day's events every evening.","col":"c"},{"label":"Traders believe they know more about what a price should be than the market does.","col":"b"},{"label":"Everything that happened makes sense in hindsight, so it must have been predictable yesterday.","col":"c"},{"label":"Kahneman's forecasts for combat performance were better than blind guesses, but not by much.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Illusion of skill, because the evaluators were trained professionals","Illusion of understanding the past, because they reviewed past feedback","Anchoring, because the first soldier they saw set the standard"],"explanation":"The officers' predictions were nonregressive forecasts of success or failure from a single hour of behavior, which is exactly the illusion of validity Kahneman coined on the obstacle field. The illusion of skill applies to the financial world, and the illusion of understanding the past applies to pundits explaining history."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Confident Prognosis","visual_frame_description":"A hospital consult room with a family seated across from an oncologist. The doctor, Dr. Priya Raman, delivers a prognosis with a steady, rehearsed certainty while a floating audit log timestamps her gestures, pauses, and word choices. A sidebar shows the five-year record for this illness subtype with the label 'Mostly Unpredictable.'","player_mission":"Audit Dr. Priya Raman's confident prognosis: study her baseline, review the timed nonverbal log and the interrogation transcript, then file the correct diagnosis. Choose the verdict that best explains her 90 percent certainty.","key_concepts":["Kahneman coined the term 'illusion of validity' after the Israeli Army's officer-selection tests showed his leadership forecasts were barely better than blind guesses.","WYSIATI (what you see is all there is) means only the evidence at hand counts, and System 1 is not designed to know the size of its jumps.","Confidence is a feeling that reflects the coherence of the information and the cognitive ease of processing it, not a reasoned probability of being correct.","Poor evidence can make a very good story, and the amount of evidence does not count for much in the feeling of confidence.","People are often reluctant to infer the particular from the general, accepting base-rate information without applying it to individuals.","Knowledge of the general rule that predictions were useless had no effect on the officers' confidence in individual cases.","Errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable.","High subjective confidence is not to be trusted as an indicator of accuracy; low confidence could be more informative.","Short-term trends can be forecast, and behavior and achievements can be predicted with fair accuracy from previous behaviors and achievements.","Declarations of high confidence mainly tell you that an individual has constructed a coherent story, not necessarily that the story is true."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the illusion of validity as it appears in medicine: a knowledgeable professional's confidence reflects the coherence of the story she has built, not the predictability of the underlying condition. The chapter's claim is that high subjective confidence is a feeling produced by cognitive ease, so knowing that a record is mostly unpredictable rarely dampens certainty about a specific case.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Dr. Priya Raman's rock-solid confidence in her long-term prognosis evidence of genuine insight, or is it a self-deception that hides the record's unpredictability?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Priya Raman is a careful, widely respected oncologist who usually speaks in precise, hedged language and quotes study numbers from memory. In weekly tumor boards she is known as the colleague who flags uncertainty rather than glosses over it, which makes her unhesitating delivery today a departure from her norm.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02 - During the family meeting, Priya states the prognosis without pausing; her tone is steady and her posture relaxed, with no gaze shifts toward her notes.","14:04 - Asked about the range of possible outcomes, she waves a hand and says 'the pattern is clear,' smiling slightly.","14:07 - She speaks faster than is typical for her, listing vivid anecdotal cases rather than base rates.","14:09 - She leans forward and repeats the central claim word for word, as if rehearsed.","14:11 - Asked how much the literature supports certainty, she glances away briefly, then returns to the confident summary."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Priya, the five-year records on this subtype show outcomes are mostly unpredictable. How can you be so certain about this case?'","Priya: 'I have seen this pattern many times. The story fits together: the staging, the markers, the response so far.'","Interviewer: 'But the base rates suggest you cannot know.'","Priya: 'Base rates are averages. This patient is a person, and I know this person.'","Interviewer: 'If you had to put a number on your confidence, what would it be?'","Priya: 'Ninety percent. The evidence all points the same way.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Illusion of validity: Priya's high confidence reflects the coherence of the story she has constructed, not the predictability of the underlying disease. The record indicates the illness's course is mostly unpredictable, so her 90 percent certainty is a feeling, not a reasoned probability.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The timed log shows classic markers of a coherent narrative doing the work: rapid, unhesitating delivery; reliance on vivid anecdotes over base rates; and tightening of the same repeated claim under pressure. Her own words name the mechanism, 'the story fits together' and 'the evidence all points the same way,' which is coherence, not evidence quality. The chapter's lesson applies directly: declarations of high confidence mainly tell you that a person has constructed a coherent story, and knowing the general record is unpredictable does little to dampen confidence in a specific case.","distractors":["Illusion of skill, because Priya is exercising her medical expertise","The anchoring effect, because the first lab value she saw set her estimate","WYSIATI alone, because she ignored all evidence about the disease"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Adviser Who Beat the Market","visual_frame_description":"A private wealth firm's conference room at 15:00. Marcus Webb, 41, sits across from an interviewer with an 8-year spreadsheet of 25 advisers on the wall, where a red .01 is circled. A camera feed in the corner timestamps his expressions as he is shown that his own ranking is statistically indistinguishable from a dice roll.","player_mission":"Audit wealth adviser Marcus Webb's claim that his returns prove skill: review his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then file the correct diagnosis. Decide whether his belief is justified or whether he is rewarding luck as if it were skill.","key_concepts":["Terry Odean's study of 10,000 brokerage accounts over seven years found that the stocks individual investors sold did better than those they bought by 3.2 percentage points per year, before trading costs.","For the large majority of individual investors, taking a shower and doing nothing would have been a better policy than implementing the ideas that came to their minds.","The average of 28 year-to-year correlations in the rankings of 25 wealth advisers over 8 years was .01, indistinguishable from zero.","The executives expected the correlation to be 'not very high' but never expected it to be zero.","The firm was rewarding luck as if it were skill, because the rankings varied erratically from year to year.","The illusion of skill is not only an individual aberration but is deeply ingrained in the culture of the industry.","Facts that challenge basic assumptions, and thereby threaten people's livelihood and self-esteem, are simply not absorbed; the mind does not digest them.","One executive's defense, I have done very well for the firm and no one can take that away from me, shows personal experience outweighing base rates.","For most stock pickers, the selection of stocks is more like rolling dice than like playing poker.","The year-to-year correlation of mutual fund outcomes is barely higher than zero, so the successful funds in any given year are mostly lucky."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the illusion of stock-picking skill: Marcus's personal experience of careful, skilled work is far more compelling to him than the statistical fact that his ranking is essentially random year to year. Kahneman showed the same with 25 wealth advisers whose 28 correlations averaged .01, and observed that such facts are 'swept under the rug' because they threaten livelihood and self-esteem.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus Webb manages a portfolio for a family office and insists his returns prove skill. Is his belief justified, or is he rewarding luck as if it were skill?","subject_baseline":"Marcus Webb, 41, is a disciplined, detail-oriented wealth adviser who prides himself on reading every quarterly report and attending every company earnings call. He keeps a personal journal of his 'calls' and re-reads it before client meetings, and he has won the firm's top-producer award twice in eight years.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:20 - When the 8-year spreadsheet of all 25 advisers is shown, Marcus tilts his head, scans it quickly, then sits back.","15:23 - Asked to guess the average year-to-year correlation of rankings, he smiles and says 'performance certainly fluctuates.'","15:26 - On hearing the average correlation is .01, his smile freezes; he blinks twice and looks at the screen again.","15:28 - He recites his best single year at length, with hand gestures, but omits his worst years.","15:31 - He concludes that the clients are happy and he is paid to make them happy.","15:33 - His voice tightens as he says, 'No one can take my record away from me.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Marcus, if the year-to-year correlation in advisers' rankings is .01, what does that tell you?'","Marcus: 'That performance fluctuates. It always has. But that's the market, not me.'","Interviewer: 'Across eight years, is there any evidence your results reflect persistent skill?'","Marcus: 'Look at my year-three run. I called the rotation before anyone. That is not luck.'","Interviewer: 'Statistically, one good year is what chance looks like.'","Marcus: 'I have done very well for the firm and no one can take that away from me.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Illusion of skill: Marcus's personal experience of careful, skilled work is far more compelling to him than the statistical fact that his ranking is essentially random year to year. The .01 correlation means his results resemble a dice-rolling contest, so he is attributing to skill what is mostly luck.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The log shows the classic sequence from the wealth-adviser episode: he expected the correlation to be 'not very high' but not zero, his confidence hinged on vivid single-year successes while ignoring the base rate, and his defensiveness peaked when the numbers threatened his livelihood and self-esteem. The chapter notes that facts that challenge basic assumptions are simply not absorbed, and this is exactly the pattern in Marcus's final, self-protective line.","distractors":["Illusion of validity, because his judgments are based on thorough research","Hindsight bias, because he is explaining the past after the fact","The hot-hand fallacy, because he had two good years in a row"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Pundit's Certainty","visual_frame_description":"A financial news studio at 19:00. Anchor Camille Osei, behind a glass desk, narrates the day's market rout with confident hand chops while a teleprompter counter shows last week's headline predicting the opposite direction. A live audit sidebar timestamps her claims and the single story she never mentions: her own failed forecast.","player_mission":"Audit evening commentator Camille Osei's confident explanation of today's market rout and her prediction for tomorrow: review her baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then file the correct diagnosis. Decide whether her certainty reflects real forecasting ability or the illusion of understanding the past.","key_concepts":["Nassim Taleb's The Black Swan argues that the ease of constructing coherent narratives of the past makes it difficult to accept the limits of forecasting ability.","Everything makes sense in hindsight, and we cannot suppress the powerful intuition that what made sense today was predictable yesterday.","The illusion that we understand the past fosters overconfidence in our ability to predict the future.","The image of the 'march of history' implies order and direction, yet the book argues that large historical events are determined by luck.","Tetlock's twenty-year study, published as Expert Political Judgment in 2005, gathered more than 80,000 predictions from 284 experts.","The experts performed worse than if they had simply assigned equal probabilities to the three potential outcomes, worse than dart-throwing monkeys.","The more famous the forecaster, the more flamboyant the forecasts; experts in demand were more overconfident than colleagues far from the limelight.","Errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable.","High subjective confidence is not to be trusted as an indicator of accuracy; low confidence could be more informative.","Short-term trends can be forecast, and behavior and achievements can be predicted with fair accuracy from previous behaviors and achievements."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the illusion of understanding the past, which fosters overconfidence in predicting the future: the ease of explaining today's events in hindsight makes Camille believe they were predictable yesterday. Kahneman cites Taleb's point that coherent narratives of the past blind us to the limits of forecasting, and Tetlock's data showing even famous forecasters predict worse than chance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Evening financial commentator Camille Osei delivers a confident explanation of today's market rout and predicts tomorrow's rebound. Is her certainty a sign of real forecasting ability, or the illusion of understanding the past?","subject_baseline":"Camille Osei is a seasoned anchor for a financial news network, known for rapid-fire analysis and a large social media following. Colleagues describe her as never wrong twice on the same show. She prepares daily with a thick file of analyst notes but rarely quotes base rates or her own prediction track record on air.","observed_nonverbal_log":["19:00 - On air, Camille narrates the day's sell-off in one continuous, polished narrative complete with causes: the rate decision, the earnings miss, the overseas sell-off.","19:03 - She punctuates each cause with a decisive downward chop of her hand; the story sounds inevitable.","19:05 - Asked why no one predicted it, she waves and says 'the signs were there for anyone paying attention.'","19:07 - She forecasts a rebound tomorrow with the same flat certainty she used for the day's recap.","19:09 - She never mentions that her own segments last week predicted the opposite direction.","19:12 - She smiles at the camera: 'History will show this was the turning point.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Camille, your explanation of today's drop is very neat. Was it predictable yesterday?'","Camille: 'Of course. The signs were there for anyone paying attention.'","Interviewer: 'Last week you told viewers the rally would continue.'","Camille: 'That was before the data. Timing aside, the drivers of this move were clear.'","Interviewer: 'How does that square with evidence that long-term forecasts are barely better than chance?'","Camille: 'People on the ground, people who watch the markets daily, know things the studies don't capture.'","Interviewer: 'So you stand by a specific call for tomorrow?'","Camille: 'I stand by it. The story is coherent, and coherent stories are right.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Illusion of understanding the past, which fosters overconfidence in predicting the future: the ease of explaining today's events in hindsight makes Camille believe they were predictable, so her forecast for tomorrow carries the false weight of a 'march of history' rather than the uncertainty it deserves.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The log shows the pundit's signature: a fluid, causal narrative of the recent past delivered with the same certainty as the forward forecast, and a deflection of her own failed predictions with 'timing aside.' The chapter warns that everything makes sense in hindsight and that we cannot suppress the intuition that what made sense today was predictable yesterday; that very intuition is what makes punditry feel like privileged information.","distractors":["Illusion of skill, because she has years of on-air experience","The anchoring effect, because the day's opening price set her narrative","Base-rate neglect in isolation, because she ignored the market's typical volatility"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Hedgehog's Excuse","visual_frame_description":"A cable news panel at 10:00. Geopolitical analyst Geoffrey Hale, 58, sits behind a name card with his bestselling book visible on the desk. A research sidebar displays three of his failed predictions from the past year, each marked with a red X, while a counter in the corner shows what random assignment would have scored. The moderator reads the counter-evidence aloud.","player_mission":"Audit analyst Geoffrey Hale's response to three straight failed predictions: review his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then file the correct diagnosis. Decide whether his insistence that he was right 'in substance' is honest self-correction or the hedgehog's refusal to absorb evidence.","key_concepts":["Tetlock's twenty-year study, published as Expert Political Judgment in 2005, gathered more than 80,000 predictions from 284 experts.","Even in the region they knew best, experts were not significantly better than nonspecialists.","Those who know more forecast very slightly better, but those with the most knowledge are often less reliable because they develop an enhanced illusion of their skill.","We reach the point of diminishing marginal predictive returns for knowledge disconcertingly quickly.","Experts resisted admitting that they had been wrong and produced excuses: wrong only in timing, an unforeseeable event intervened, or wrong but for the right reasons.","Experts are led astray not by what they believe but by how they think, says Tetlock.","Hedgehogs know one big thing, explain events within a single coherent framework, and are confident and reluctant to admit error.","Foxes recognize that reality emerges from the interactions of many agents and forces, including blind luck.","High subjective confidence is not to be trusted as an indicator of accuracy; low confidence could be more informative."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests hedgehog cognition: Geoffrey explains everything through one big idea, treats failed forecasts as 'off only on timing' or 'wrong but for the right reasons,' and is especially reluctant to admit error. Tetlock found that exactly these thinkers were the most overconfident and the most resistant to absorbing disconfirming evidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"After three straight failed predictions, geopolitical analyst Geoffrey Hale insists he was right 'in substance.' Is this honest self-correction, or the hedgehog's refusal to absorb evidence?","subject_baseline":"Geoffrey Hale, 58, is a distinguished foreign-policy professor and author of a bestselling book with one grand thesis: great powers rise and fall on the strength of their leaders. He is a fixture of cable news panels and delivers sweeping forecasts with great flair, and he has never published an explicit scoring of his own predictions.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:05 - On a panel, Geoffrey is asked about his prediction last year that the regional alliance would collapse within months.","10:06 - He leans forward, brow furrowed, and begins: 'The mechanism was right.'","10:09 - He re-states his original thesis at length, connecting unrelated events to it, speaking faster as he goes.","10:11 - He does not look at the moderator when the counter-evidence is read aloud; he looks down at his notes.","10:13 - He concedes only the date: 'I was wrong on timing.'","10:15 - He adds, 'An unforeseeable event intervened, a once-in-a-generation rupture, so the substance stands.'","10:17 - When pressed, he smiles: 'Even the best scholars are wrong for the right reasons.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Moderator: 'Geoffrey, your forecast was that the alliance would dissolve within six months. It did not.'","Geoffrey: 'The mechanism was right; only the timing was wrong. This is common in history.'","Moderator: 'Could the theory itself be wrong?'","Geoffrey: 'No theory accounts for everything, but mine accounts for the pattern. That is why it was a good call.'","Moderator: 'Tetlock's data suggest experts with a single big theory predict worse than chance.'","Geoffrey: 'Those studies don't measure judgment the way we practice it in the field.'","Moderator: 'So what do you take from three failed predictions in a row?'","Geoffrey: 'That the world is harder than the textbooks. The framework stands.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Hedgehog cognition: Geoffrey explains everything through one big idea, treats failed forecasts as off only on timing or wrong but for the right reasons, and is especially reluctant to admit error. His confidence reflects the coherence of his framework, not its track record.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Every element of the log matches Tetlock's description of hedgehogs: a single coherent framework applied to everything, impatience with counter-evidence, deflections of failure into 'timing' and 'unforeseeable events,' and a final appeal to being 'wrong for the right reasons.' Tetlock found hedgehogs were exactly the experts who resisted admitting they were wrong and who maintained the most flamboyant confidence in their forecasts.","distractors":["Fox thinking, because he weighs many alternative explanations","Illusion of skill, because his book sold widely","The halo effect, because he is a distinguished professor"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Obstacle Field","visual_frame_description":"Under a scorching sun on an Israeli Army obstacle field, eight candidates in numbered tags, stripped of rank insignia, haul a heavy log toward a six-foot wall while a colleague and the player watch from the side. Sweat, shouts, a collapsed attempt, and a soldier berating a comrade fill the frame, and a thought bubble above the evaluators reads 'His true nature is revealed.'","player_mission":"Defeat the illusion of validity on the obstacle field. Study the scene and explain why one hour of observation produces total confidence in forecasts that the record shows are barely better than chance, then decide how the evaluation team should actually proceed with the next batch.","key_concepts":["Kahneman coined the term 'illusion of validity' after the Israeli Army's officer-selection tests showed his leadership forecasts were barely better than blind guesses.","In the leaderless group challenge, eight candidates lifted a log over a six-foot wall, and one hour of watching produced impressions as direct and compelling as the color of the sky.","The officers' forecasts were a clear instance of substitution and of the representativeness heuristic: observed behavior stood in for future performance.","Their predictions were completely nonregressive, with no reservations about predicting failure or outstanding success from weak evidence.","WYSIATI (what you see is all there is) means only the evidence at hand counts, and System 1 is not designed to know the size of its jumps.","Confidence is a feeling that reflects the coherence of the information and the cognitive ease of processing it, not a reasoned probability of being correct.","Nisbett and Borgida's students believed that most people did not help a stranger having a seizure, yet the base rates did not influence their judgment of the individual on the video.","Knowledge of the general rule that the predictions were useless had no effect on the officers' confidence in individual cases.","The illusion of validity resembles the Muller-Lyer illusion: knowing the lines are equal does not stop you from seeing them as different.","Cognitive illusions can be more stubborn than visual illusions: learning about the Muller-Lyer illusion changed behavior, but knowledge of low validity changed neither feelings nor subsequent action.","You should not expect performance in officer training and in combat to be predictable from behavior on an obstacle field.","If a test predicts an important outcome with a validity of .20 or .30, the test should be used, but you should not expect more."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the illusion of validity: System 1 jumps to conclusions from little evidence and is not designed to know the size of its jumps, so a vivid hour of behavior feels like a valid forecast of the future. Kahneman showed that knowledge of the general failure of his forecasts had no effect on his confidence in individual cases, the defining stubbornness of the illusion.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The illusion of validity is the chapter's central concept: System 1 is designed to jump to conclusions from little evidence and not designed to know the size of its jumps, so an hour of vivid behavior produces confident forecasts whose validity is barely above chance. The battle asks the player to see through that confidence by understanding substitution, WYSIATI, and confidence by coherence.","stage_1_observation":"Under a scorching sun on an Israeli Army obstacle field, Kahneman and a colleague watch eight unnamed, rank-stripped candidates lift a heavy log to a six-foot wall and try to get everyone over it without the log touching the ground or the wall. The evaluators note who takes charge, who is rebuffed, who berates a comrade whose mistake fails the group, and who steps forward to lead the exhausted team's restart. The scene is intense: sweat, repeated collapses, competitive spite, and a final leap along a pole held by the others, and every impression feels as direct and compelling as the color of the sky.","stage_1_question":"Why do Kahneman and his colleague feel completely confident in predicting each soldier's performance in officer training and combat after just one hour of observation, even though every feedback session shows their forecasts are only slightly better than blind guesses?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because their judgment is a substitution: they answer the hard question about future performance with the easy, vivid impression of representativeness from the obstacle field, and WYSIATI gives them no representation of the many unseen factors that will actually determine performance. Confidence then arrives from the coherence of the story, not from the validity of the evidence, so the general knowledge of past failure does nothing to the confidence in individual cases.","stage_2_intervention":"The next morning a fresh batch of candidates arrives and the routine is to begin again. Rather than repeat the same ritual, the evaluation team must decide how to change. What is the corrective response the chapter actually supports?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep watching, because the behavior is genuinely informative, but stop treating subjective confidence as a measure of accuracy: acknowledge that predictions from the obstacle field have validity barely above chance, use base rates about selection outcomes where available, moderate the forecasts, and treat the high confidence as a feeling to discount rather than evidence. The chapter's lesson is that errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable, and that declarations of high confidence mainly signal a coherent story, not a true one."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Dice-Rolling Fund","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom in a private wealth firm where a spreadsheet of 25 anonymous advisers' returns across eight years fills the wall. Sorting by any year reshuffles the rankings like a shuffled deck: the year-one star is mid-pack in year two and near the bottom in year three. Kahneman and Richard Thaler sit across from executives, and a highlighted box reads 'average correlation .01.'","player_mission":"Defeat the illusion of stock-picking skill. Interpret what the .01 average year-to-year correlation demonstrates about the advisers' results, then decide how the firm should actually change its bonus practice in light of the finding.","key_concepts":["Terry Odean's study of 10,000 brokerage accounts over seven years found that the stocks individual investors sold did better than those they bought by 3.2 percentage points per year, before trading costs.","For the large majority of individual investors, taking a shower and doing nothing would have been a better policy than implementing the ideas that came to their minds.","The diagnostic for the existence of skill is the consistency of individual differences: if performance in any year were entirely due to luck, year-to-year correlations would be zero.","Over fifty years of research show that at least two out of every three mutual funds underperform the overall market in any given year.","The year-to-year correlation of mutual fund outcomes is barely higher than zero, so the successful funds in any given year are mostly lucky.","The average of 28 year-to-year correlations in the rankings of 25 wealth advisers over 8 years was .01, indistinguishable from zero.","The executives expected the correlation to be 'not very high' but never expected it to be zero, and the firm was rewarding luck as if it were skill.","The illusion of skill is not only an individual aberration but is deeply ingrained in the culture of the industry, and facts that threaten livelihood and self-esteem are simply not absorbed.","The most potent psychological cause of the illusion of financial skill is that stock pickers are exercising high-level skills, which gives them a valid experience of skilled work.","Skill in evaluating the business prospects of a firm is not sufficient for successful trading, because the key question is whether the information is already incorporated in the price of the stock.","Traders apparently lack the skill to answer that crucial question, but they appear to be ignorant of their ignorance."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the illusion of skill in finance: because the year-to-year correlation of adviser and fund rankings is barely above zero, rewarding individual returns is rewarding luck as if it were skill. Kahneman showed this with 25 wealth advisers whose 28 correlations averaged .01, and noted that the disconfirming message was instantly swept under the rug.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The illusion of stock-picking skill is the chapter's second major concept: because the year-to-year correlation of fund and adviser rankings is barely above zero, individual returns are effectively chance, so rewarding them is rewarding luck as if it were skill. The battle asks the player to apply the persistence-of-achievement diagnostic and to confront how professional culture keeps the illusion alive.","stage_1_observation":"A boardroom in a private wealth firm. Kahneman and Richard Thaler are shown a spreadsheet with the year-end returns of twenty-five anonymous wealth advisers across eight consecutive years; each adviser's score determines his annual bonus. On the screen the rankings can be sorted by any year, and they reshuffle like a shuffled deck: the top performer of year one is mid-pack in year two and near the bottom in year three, and no one stays put.","stage_1_question":"Kahneman computed the 28 year-to-year correlations of these rankings and got an average of .01. What does that number demonstrate about the advisers' 'skill'?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It demonstrates that there is no persistence of individual differences, which is the signature of luck, not skill. If rankings were due to skill they would be stable; instead they vary erratically, exactly like a dice-rolling contest, so the returns being rewarded are effectively chance outcomes.","stage_2_intervention":"That evening Kahneman and Thaler dine with the executives who set bonuses and tell them the firm is rewarding luck as if it were skill. The executives nod and the conversation moves on. The player must choose how the firm should actually change its practice in light of the finding.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The firm should stop tying bonuses to short-run year-to-year returns and instead apply the diagnostic for skill, persistence of achievement over many periods and out-of-sample outcomes, while accepting that for most portfolios low-cost passive strategies will beat most active managers. The chapter warns the firm will likely do none of this: facts that threaten livelihood and self-esteem are simply not absorbed, and the message was instantly put away in a dark corner of memory."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION: The Expert's Forecast","visual_frame_description":"A scrolling wall display in a research archive shows a grid of 284 experts' probability assessments across three possible outcomes for each question about coups, wars, and emerging markets. A ticker under the grid counts hits and misses, and an overlay shows what dart-throwing random assignment would have scored. The expert line consistently trails the random line, even inside each expert's own specialty region.","player_mission":"Defeat the illusion of expert forecasting. Interpret why the most knowledgeable experts forecast least reliably in Tetlock's study, then steer a famous hedgehog expert toward the response that Tetlock's data actually support on a live television panel.","key_concepts":["Tetlock's twenty-year study, published as Expert Political Judgment in 2005, gathered more than 80,000 predictions from 284 experts.","The experts performed worse than if they had simply assigned equal probabilities to the three potential outcomes, worse than dart-throwing monkeys.","Even in the region they knew best, experts were not significantly better than nonspecialists.","Those who know more forecast very slightly better, but those with the most knowledge are often less reliable because they develop an enhanced illusion of their skill.","We reach the point of diminishing marginal predictive returns for knowledge disconcertingly quickly.","The more famous the forecaster, the more flamboyant the forecasts; experts in demand were more overconfident than colleagues far from the limelight.","Experts resisted admitting that they had been wrong and produced excuses: wrong only in timing, an unforeseeable event intervened, or wrong but for the right reasons.","Hedgehogs know one big thing, explain events within a single coherent framework, and are confident and reluctant to admit error.","Foxes recognize that reality emerges from the interactions of many agents and forces, including blind luck, and they scored best in Tetlock's study, though still poorly.","Errors of prediction are inevitable because the world is unpredictable.","High subjective confidence is not to be trusted as an indicator of accuracy; low confidence could be more informative."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests Tetlock's finding on expert judgment: 284 professional forecasters produced more than 80,000 predictions that were worse than assigning equal probabilities, and those who knew more often developed an enhanced illusion of their own skill. The battle asks the player to recognize that overconfidence grows with expertise and to prefer fox-style thinking, which scored best though still poorly.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Tetlock's study of expert political judgment is the chapter's third major concept: 284 professionals produced more than 80,000 predictions that were worse than assigning equal probabilities to the outcomes, and more knowledge built an enhanced illusion of skill rather than accuracy. The battle asks the player to apply the hedgehog-fox distinction to real forecasting behavior.","stage_1_observation":"A scrolling wall display in a research archive shows a grid of 284 experts' probability assessments, questions about coups, wars, and emerging markets, each answered across three possible outcomes. A ticker under the grid counts the hits and misses, and an overlay shows what 'dart-throwing' random assignment would have scored. As the player watches, the expert line consistently trails the random line, even inside each expert's own specialty region.","stage_1_question":"The experts scored worse than if they had simply assigned equal probabilities to each of the three outcomes. Why did those with the most knowledge often forecast least reliably?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because more knowledge produces an enhanced illusion of skill and unrealistic overconfidence: we reach the point of diminishing marginal predictive returns for knowledge disconcertingly quickly. The more famous and 'in demand' the forecaster, the more flamboyant the forecasts, so overconfidence grows with expertise and accuracy does not.","stage_2_intervention":"One of the study's best-known experts, a hedgehog who 'knows one big thing,' has just been shown three failed predictions and must now decide how to respond on a nationally televised panel. The player must steer him toward the response that Tetlock's data actually support.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Adopt fox-style cognition: acknowledge that outcomes emerge from many interacting forces including blind luck, state uncertainty honestly and in probability ranges, drop excuses like 'off only on timing' and 'an unforeseeable event intervened,' and treat long-term prediction as inherently unreliable. Tetlock found foxes scored best, though still poorly, and were less confident, and the chapter adds that low confidence could be more informative than high."}]},{"id":"ch26","title":"21. Intuitions Vs. Formulas","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Clinical vs. Statistical Prediction (the Meehl pattern)","visual_frame_description":"A sorting station shaped like a courtroom evidence board fills the screen. On the left, a stack of index cards carries one-line claims drawn from Paul Meehl's research review, and three labeled columns await them on the board. A faded portrait of Meehl at a Minnesota seminar overlooks the scene, and a tally counter records how many cards the player has placed correctly.","player_mission":"Read each evidence card and drag it into the column it best supports: formulas beating expert judgment, intuition adding genuine value, or moral resistance to algorithms. Complete the board so every card sits in the column the chapter's evidence supports.","key_concepts":["Meehl's review of 20 studies found that statistical formulas matched or beat trained clinical judgment in nearly every comparison.","Roughly two hundred follow-up studies have since been conducted, and about 60% show significantly better accuracy for algorithms, with the rest tying and no convincing exception documented.","In low-validity environments, where outcomes are hard to predict, the accuracy of experts was matched or exceeded by a simple algorithm in every domain examined.","When trained counselors interviewed freshmen for forty-five minutes, a formula using only high-school grades and one aptitude test beat 11 of the 14 counselors.","People given the score suggested by a formula still overrule it on the strength of additional information about the case, and they are wrong more often than not.","The broken-leg rule states it is proper to disregard a formula only when rare, decisive information, such as a broken leg, arrives.","Experienced radiologists contradict themselves about 20% of the time when they see the same chest X-ray on separate occasions.","Unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors of anything, which is why expert inconsistency destroys predictive validity.","Long-term predictions about a patient's future are where clinicians fail, whereas short-term in-session hunches are a genuine skill built on years of practice.","Many clinicians treat the idea that a mechanical combination of a few variables could outperform subtle human judgment as obviously wrong, an emotional reaction with a moral dimension."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the Meehl pattern: in low-validity environments a simple statistical combination of a few scores matches or beats the subjective judgment of trained professionals, with about 60% of roughly two hundred comparison studies favoring the algorithm. The book presents this as one of the most uniformly replicated findings in social science, tempered by the broken-leg rule for rare, decisive information and by genuine short-term clinical skill.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Drag each evidence card into the column it best supports: the Meehl pattern (formulas win), genuine clinical skill (intuition wins), or the moral resistance to algorithms.","column_a_label":"Formula beats expert judgment (Meehl pattern)","column_b_label":"Intuition adds genuine value","column_c_label":"Moral and aesthetic resistance to algorithms","rows":[{"label":"A counselor who interviewed a freshman for 45 minutes is outscored by a formula using only high-school grades and one aptitude test.","col":"a"},{"label":"A therapist correctly anticipates that a patient will resist a particular intervention during the session.","col":"b"},{"label":"Disregarding the movie-going prediction formula because the person broke a leg today.","col":"b"},{"label":"A clinician combines subtle cues about a patient's dynamics to predict outcomes five years in the future.","col":"a"},{"label":"An expert overrules a formula's score because she has extra information about the case, and she is wrong more often than not.","col":"a"},{"label":"Wine professionals call Ashenfelter's weather-based price formula ludicrous and absurd.","col":"c"},{"label":"A reviewer calls the statistical method mechanical, atomistic, cut and dried, dead and pedantic.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["That the 45-minute counselor study belongs in column b because a human gathered the grades used by the formula.","That the broken-leg example belongs in column a because it disproves the formula.","That the wine professionals' insults are evidence that the formula failed in practice."],"explanation":"The chapter's central Meehl pattern is that simple statistical rules match or beat expert judgment in low-validity environments: the 45-minute counselor study, long-term clinical predictions, and overruled formulas all land in column A. Genuine clinical skill survives only in short-term, practiced settings such as in-session hunches, and the broken-leg rule is the rare, decisive case where overriding a formula is legitimate. The wine professionals' insults and the critics' adjectives are not evidence about accuracy at all; they are the documented hostility to algorithms, rooted in a preference for the natural over the synthetic."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why Expert Judgment Fails: Complexity and Inconsistency","visual_frame_description":"A forensic analysis wall in a strategy war room displays two blurred photo panels: one of an expert hunched over a chessboard of complex feature combinations, the other of a judge at a bench where a food-break clock ticks. A third panel shows an interviewer checking off six trait boxes in a fixed order. The player drags colored observation chips onto the wall to sort them.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the reason Meehl believed experts lose to formulas: complexity-seeking, context-dependent inconsistency, or the disciplined structured procedure that fixes the problem.","key_concepts":["Meehl suspected experts are inferior because they try to be clever, think outside the box, and consider complex combinations of features.","Complexity may work in the odd case, but more often than not it reduces validity; simple combinations of features are better.","The widespread inconsistency of expert judgment is probably due to the extreme context dependency of System 1.","Experienced radiologists contradict themselves 20% of the time when they see the same chest X-ray on separate occasions.","A study of 101 independent auditors who evaluated the reliability of internal corporate audits revealed a similar degree of inconsistency.","A review of 41 studies of auditors, pathologists, psychologists, and managers found such inconsistency is typical, even when a case is reevaluated within minutes.","The prospects of a convict being granted parole can change significantly during the time between successive food breaks in the parole judges' schedule.","The brief pleasure of a cool breeze on a hot day may make a person slightly more positive and optimistic about whatever they are evaluating at the time.","Because people have little direct knowledge of what goes on in their mind, they never know they might have made a different judgment under slightly different circumstances.","Given the same input, a formula always returns the same answer, so when predictability is poor, inconsistency destroys any predictive validity."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the two reasons experts are inferior to formulas: complexity-seeking and the extreme context dependency of System 1. Because unnoticed stimuli such as a food break or a cool breeze shift judgments from moment to moment, experts are inconsistent, and the book notes that unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors of anything.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Meehl suspected two main reasons experts lose to formulas. Sort each observation into the reason it illustrates, or into the disciplined remedy.","column_a_label":"Experts try to be clever (complexity)","column_b_label":"Context-dependent inconsistency (System 1)","column_c_label":"Disciplined structured procedure","rows":[{"label":"Radiologists who read the same chest film twice on separate occasions contradict themselves 20% of the time.","col":"b"},{"label":"A parole decision becomes more favorable right after a judge's food break than just before it.","col":"b"},{"label":"Experts think outside the box and consider complex combinations of features.","col":"a"},{"label":"Simple combinations of features are better, because complexity reduces validity more often than not.","col":"a"},{"label":"The interviewer rates six traits in a fixed sequence on a five-point scale, scoring each before moving on.","col":"c"},{"label":"101 independent auditors gave inconsistent reliability ratings of the same internal audit.","col":"b"},{"label":"The same formula given the same input always returns the same answer.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["That radiologists' self-contradiction is a sign of careful, complex reasoning.","That scoring one trait at a time adds cleverness and thereby reduces validity.","That food-break timing is a deliberate policy choice made by the judge."],"explanation":"The chapter attributes expert inferiority to two causes: experts trying to be clever with complex combinations of features, and the extreme context dependency of System 1, which makes the same judge or radiologist give different answers on different occasions. Consistency matters because unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors, and the remedy is the disciplined procedure of rating each trait separately in a fixed order before summing, the same approach used in Kahneman's army interviews."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Equal-Weight Formulas and the Apgar Score","visual_frame_description":"A bright delivery-room tableau with a checklist overlay: a doctor checks heart rate, respiration, reflex, muscle tone, and color, each on a 0-2 scale, next to a scroll showing Dawes's marital stability formula. A regression equation and a midwife's folded arms frame the two other sorting bins. The player assigns each card to one of three labeled columns.","player_mission":"Sort each approach into equal-weight simplicity, regression-style optimization, or intuitive clinical judgment, then confirm which column the chapter credits with the most wins.","key_concepts":["Dawes's article 'The Robust Beauty of Improper Linear Models in Decision Making' showed that equal-weight formulas match optimally weighted regression in predicting new cases.","Equal-weight formulas are often superior because they are not affected by accidents of sampling that distort weights fit to one sample.","It is possible to develop useful algorithms without any prior statistical research using simple equally weighted formulas based on existing statistics or common sense.","Dawes showed marital stability is well predicted by the formula: frequency of lovemaking minus frequency of quarrels.","Apgar's score rates five variables (heart rate, respiration, reflex, muscle tone, and color) each on a 0, 1, or 2 scale, summed into a total.","A baby scoring 8 or above is likely to be pink, squirming, crying, and grimacing with a pulse of 100 or more; a score of 4 or below signals a need for immediate intervention.","Before Apgar intervened in 1953, physicians and midwives used inconsistent clinical judgment to decide whether a newborn was in distress.","The Apgar score is credited with helping reduce infant mortality and is still used every day in every delivery room.","Ashenfelter's formula predicts Bordeaux wine prices from three weather features: summer average temperature, harvest-time rain, and previous-winter rainfall.","Ashenfelter's predictions correlate above .90 with actual wine prices, outdoing the opinions of expert tasters."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests Dawes's finding of the robust beauty of improper linear models: equal-weight formulas are often as accurate as optimally weighted regression because they avoid accidents of sampling. The book applies this to Apgar's five-variable newborn score, Ashenfelter's three-weather-variable wine formula, and a marital stability rule that subtracts quarrels from lovemaking.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each approach into equal-weight simplicity, regression-style optimization, or intuitive clinical judgment.","column_a_label":"Simple or equal-weight formula","column_b_label":"Optimally weighted statistical model","column_c_label":"Intuitive clinical judgment","rows":[{"label":"Apgar's score sums five variables (heart rate, respiration, reflex, muscle tone, color) each scored 0-2.","col":"a"},{"label":"Multiple regression assigns each predictor the weight that was optimal in the original sample.","col":"b"},{"label":"Dawes's formula for marital stability: frequency of lovemaking minus frequency of quarrels.","col":"a"},{"label":"Ashenfelter predicts Bordeaux prices from summer temperature, harvest rain, and winter rain.","col":"a"},{"label":"A midwife watches whether the baby cries soon after birth and relies on her gut to decide if it is in distress.","col":"c"},{"label":"Equal-weight formulas are not affected by accidents of sampling and are often superior on new cases.","col":"a"},{"label":"A hiring manager selects the candidate who made the strongest first impression in the interview.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["That Apgar's score required years of statistical research to construct.","That Dawes's marital stability formula was computed by multiple regression.","That Ashenfelter's wine formula is an example of intuitive judgment."],"explanation":"The chapter's theme is that useful algorithms can be built on the back of an envelope: equal-weight formulas like the Apgar score, Dawes's lovemaking-minus-quarrels rule, and Ashenfelter's three-weather-variable wine formula routinely match optimized regression and beat intuition. The Apgar score, still used in every delivery room, is credited with saving hundreds of thousands of infants because it replaced inconsistent clinical judgment with a consistent, standardized procedure."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing an Expert's Consistency","visual_frame_description":"A hospital review desk with two lightboxes side by side displaying the same chest X-ray. Dr. Elena Marsh's file is open on the table, with a timeline strip marking the three times she dictated a verdict on the identical film. A recorder runs a timestamped audio log while the auditor ticks boxes on a consistency checklist.","player_mission":"Audit Dr. Marsh's reading record across a single day. Decide whether her confident intuition is reliable or whether she is exhibiting the inconsistency that makes expert judgment lose to formulas, then deliver your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Experienced radiologists who evaluate chest X-rays as normal or abnormal contradict themselves 20% of the time when they see the same picture on separate occasions.","A study of 101 independent auditors evaluating the reliability of internal corporate audits revealed a similar degree of inconsistency.","A review of 41 studies of auditors, pathologists, psychologists, and managers found such inconsistency is typical even when a case is reevaluated within a few minutes.","Unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors of anything.","The widespread inconsistency is probably due to the extreme context dependency of System 1.","Because people have little direct knowledge of what goes on in their mind, they never know they might have made a different judgment under very slightly different circumstances.","Given the same input, a formula always returns the same answer.","When predictability is poor, which it is in most of the studies Meehl reviewed, inconsistency is destructive of any predictive validity.","Experts feel they can overrule a formula because they have additional information about the case, but they are wrong more often than not.","People are incorrigibly inconsistent in making summary judgments of complex information."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests expert inconsistency: radiologists and other professionals contradict themselves at high rates when reevaluating the same case, because System 1's judgments are extremely context-dependent. The chapter concludes that unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors, which is why formulas, which always return the same answer for the same input, outpredict confident experts.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dr. Elena Marsh is a respected radiologist whose confident readings carry weight in her hospital. Is her impressive-sounding intuition reliable, or is she exhibiting the inconsistency that makes expert judgment lose to formulas?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Marsh is a 52-year-old senior radiologist at St. Andrew's Medical Center, known for crisp, confident dictations and a no-nonsense manner. She prides herself on reading films by the gestalt, rarely changes a first impression, and has never tracked her own error rate. Colleagues defer to her certainty.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:02 - Reads a chest film after a quick coffee and dictates 'normal'; leans back, appears relaxed and certain.","11:47 - Reviews the identical chest film during a busy pre-lunch stretch; pauses 40 seconds longer, frowns, then dictates 'subtle opacity in the right base, recommend follow-up.'","14:15 - Rereads the same film after returning from a walk; gestures dismissively, states 'definitely normal,' and closes the case.","16:40 - Asked whether she ever rereads her own films, she laughs and says 'Once I've seen it, I know it. I don't second-guess.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You dictated 'normal' at 9:02, 'subtle opacity, follow-up' at 11:47, and 'definitely normal' at 14:15 - the identical film, same patient.","Marsh: The light was different in the morning, the monitor was recalibrated. That happens.","Auditor: But the film is the same file on the same monitor.","Marsh: Look, I've read a quarter million films. My gut is calibrated. You can't reduce radiology to a checklist.","Auditor: How often do you check your own accuracy against biopsy outcomes?","Marsh: I don't need a spreadsheet to tell me what I saw."],"correct_diagnosis":"Illusion of skill through inconsistency: Dr. Marsh's confident intuitions are context-dependent and unreliable. Her readings of the same film changed three times in a single day, and she has never tracked her accuracy against outcomes.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter reports that experienced radiologists contradict themselves 20% of the time on the same X-ray seen on separate occasions, and that this level of inconsistency is typical across auditors, pathologists, psychologists, and managers. Marsh's three different verdicts on one film in a single day, together with her refusal to check readings against biopsy outcomes, fits that pattern exactly. Her confidence is not evidence of validity: unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors, and the correct response is to prefer a consistent scoring rule for screening, while letting intuition add value only after disciplined, standardized information collection.","distractors":["Deliberate deception: she is consciously hiding errors to protect her malpractice record.","Malicious carelessness: she is rushing on purpose to punish the hospital administration.","Superior skill: she can detect subtleties that simple formulas and other radiologists miss."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Food-Break Effect on a Parole Judge","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom bench in Austin at dusk, with a case file for Judge Rafael Ortiz open on the desk. A timeline chart plots his parole grants and denials against the clock, with lunch and snack breaks marked in green. A recorder plays his confident testimony while the audit screen tracks the hour-by-hour rulings.","player_mission":"Audit Judge Ortiz's ruling pattern against the timing of his food breaks. Determine whether he is being influenced by an unnoticed, context-dependent factor and deliver your verdict on what his data actually show.","key_concepts":["The prospects of a convict being granted parole may change significantly during the time that elapses between successive food breaks in the parole judges' schedule.","The brief pleasure of a cool breeze on a hot day may make a person slightly more positive and optimistic about whatever they are evaluating at the time.","Unnoticed stimuli in the environment have a substantial influence on thoughts and actions, and these influences fluctuate from moment to moment.","Because people have little direct knowledge of what goes on in their mind, they never know they might have made a different judgment or reached a different decision under very slightly different circumstances.","The widespread inconsistency of expert judgment is probably due to the extreme context dependency of System 1.","Unreliable judgments cannot be valid predictors of anything.","Given the same input, a formula always returns the same answer, while a judge's answer shifts with context.","The default decision in parole is denial, with only about 35% of requests approved.","People are incorrigibly inconsistent in making summary judgments of complex information."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the extreme context dependency of System 1: unnoticed stimuli, including the timing of food breaks, shift a judge's rulings from moment to moment without his awareness. The chapter uses the parole-judge example to show why experts are inconsistent and why a formula, which is immune to such fluctuations, can be more reliable.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Judge Rafael Ortiz grants or denies parole based on his experience and gut feel for each defendant. The audit file shows a suspicious pattern in his ruling times. Is he being influenced by something he does not notice?","subject_baseline":"Judge Ortiz is a veteran parole judge in Austin, Texas, with 18 years on the bench. He describes his method as weighing the whole person, insists every case is decided on its own merits, and denies that mood, fatigue, or timing plays any role in his rulings. His grant rate hovers near the state average of roughly 35%.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:55 - Second hearing of the morning block; the judge rubs his eyes, checks the clock, and denies parole to a candidate similar to cases he granted an hour earlier.","12:20 - Immediately after the lunch break; he returns to the bench briskly, grants parole twice in quick succession, looks engaged and cheerful.","13:05 - Mid-afternoon slump; he grants one of six petitions, sighs, and denies a borderline case he describes as a tough one.","15:10 - Near the end of a long block; he denies parole three times in a row, rubs his temples, and his verdicts grow terse."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: Your grant rate right after food breaks is nearly double your rate just before them.","Ortiz: That's coincidence. I decide each case on the record and the man in front of me. I don't check my watch.","Auditor: But the same profile of offender was granted at 12:20 and denied at 10:55.","Ortiz: Every case is different, counselor. You can't mechanize justice.","Auditor: If we plotted your rulings against time since the last meal, the line would be very clean.","Ortiz: Then your line is wrong, because my judgment is mine, not the cafeteria's."],"correct_diagnosis":"Unnoticed context dependency: the judge's rulings shift systematically with the timing of food breaks, an influence he is unaware of and denies. His pattern matches the chapter's account of parole decisions changing between successive food breaks.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter states that the prospects of a convict being granted parole can change significantly during the time between successive food breaks in the judges' schedule, and that a pleasant state such as the brief pleasure of a positive break makes a person slightly more optimistic about whatever they are evaluating. Judge Ortiz's grant pattern after breaks versus before them is that documented effect, and his insistence that timing plays no role is not deception but the fact that people have little direct knowledge of what goes on in their mind: he will never know he might have ruled differently under slightly different circumstances.","distractors":["Deliberate bias: he grants parole only to defendants who share his political affiliations.","Legal merit: the afternoon defendants genuinely committed more serious crimes.","Fatigue-induced corruption: he takes bribes during lunch and honors them in the afternoon."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Bordeaux Wine: Formula vs. Expert Palate","visual_frame_description":"A candle-lit Bordeaux tasting room in 1990. On one screen, economist Orley Ashenfelter's formula ticks through three inputs (summer temperature, harvest-time rain, and previous-winter rainfall) and prints price forecasts. Across the room, Master Sommelier Étienne Durand swirls a young wine, closes his eyes, and declares the vintage legendary purely from taste, while French wine professionals jeer at the formula as ludicrous and absurd.","player_mission":"Advise an investor on which forecast to trust for the future value of a young vintage, then survive the hostile pushback from the wine establishment when your recommendation follows the formula instead of the palate.","key_concepts":["Ashenfelter converted conventional knowledge into a formula that predicts wine price from three weather features: average summer temperature, harvest-time rain, and previous-winter rainfall.","The correlation between Ashenfelter's predictions and actual wine prices is above .90.","Ashenfelter's formula forecasts future prices much more accurately than the current prices of young wines do.","The fine-wine price question matters because bottles filled only twelve months apart can differ in value by a factor of 10 or more.","Experts who evaluate the quality of immature wine have a source of information that almost certainly makes things worse rather than better: they can taste the wine.","The response in French wine circles to Ashenfelter's formula ranged somewhere between violent and hysterical.","One oenophile called Ashenfelter's findings ludicrous and absurd; another scoffed that it is like judging movies without actually seeing them.","The aversion to algorithms making decisions is rooted in a strong preference for the natural over the synthetic or artificial.","Even after being told organic and commercial apples are identical in taste, nutrition, and healthfulness, a majority still prefer the organic fruit.","Experts are overconfident in their intuitions and assign too much weight to their personal impressions."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the Meehl pattern applied to fine wine: a formula built from three weather variables forecasts Bordeaux prices more accurately than expert tasters, with a correlation above .90. The chapter uses the wine community's hostile reaction to illustrate that the aversion to algorithms is an emotional preference for the natural over the synthetic, not a response to evidence.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests whether a simple statistical formula can beat world-renowned expert intuition: Ashenfelter's three-weather-variable formula predicted future Bordeaux prices with a correlation above .90, whereas the expert opinions that shape early prices were less accurate, and tasting the immature wine gave experts information that made things worse rather than better.","stage_1_observation":"You are an investor's analyst in a candle-lit Bordeaux tasting room in 1990. On one screen, economist Orley Ashenfelter's formula ticks through three inputs (average summer temperature, harvest-time rain, and prior-winter rainfall) and prints price forecasts for each vintage. Across the room, Master Sommelier Étienne Durand swirls a young wine, closes his eyes, and declares 'this vintage will be legendary' purely from taste, while French wine professionals call Ashenfelter's approach 'ludicrous and absurd.'","stage_1_question":"Which forecast should the investor trust for the future value of a young vintage: the three-weather-variable formula, the sommelier's blind tasting, or the consensus of the wine professionals?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The three-weather-variable formula. Ashenfelter's predictions correlated above .90 with actual prices, whereas the experts' opinions that shape early prices were less accurate, and tasting the immature wine gives experts information that the chapter says almost certainly makes their forecasts worse rather than better.","stage_2_intervention":"Word spreads that you are advising the investor from the formula rather than the palate. Étienne confronts you in the cellar: 'This is like judging movies without actually seeing them. You would let a blind, mechanical equation decide what we drink for the next decade?' The room turns hostile. How do you respond to keep the decision rational without losing the room?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Stand by the formula while acknowledging that the resistance is emotional, not technical. The chapter explains that the 'violent and hysterical' response of French wine circles is prejudice against algorithms rooted in a preference for the natural over the synthetic, and that for most people the cause of a mistake matters morally even when a formula is more accurate."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Recruitment Interview: Halo Effect vs. Discipline","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit briefing room at an Israeli Defense Forces base in 1955. Nineteen-year-old interviewers sit behind a long table with a list of six personality traits, each to be rated on a five-point scale in a fixed order. One young interviewer slams the paper down and protests that they are being turned into robots, while the others murmur in support.","player_mission":"Defend the structured interview procedure against the mutiny of bright young interviewers who prefer open-ended probing of the recruit's inner life. Then interpret the surprising 'close your eyes' result so the team does not throw out the disciplined method.","key_concepts":["The existing IDF interview procedure was almost useless for predicting the future success of recruits.","Meehl's book argued that statistical summaries of separately evaluated attributes achieve higher validity than global intuitive evaluations.","Kahneman's interviewers evaluated six traits including responsibility, sociability, and masculine pride, each scored on a five-point scale.","For each trait the interviewers asked factual questions about the individual's life before enlistment, such as the number of jobs held, punctuality, and interest in sports.","Focusing on standardized factual questions was designed to combat the halo effect, where favorable first impressions influence later judgments.","As a precaution against halos, interviewers rated the six traits in a fixed sequence, scoring each before going on to the next.","The sum of the six ratings predicted soldiers' performance much more accurately than the global evaluations of the previous method, progressing from completely useless to moderately useful.","The 'close your eyes' intuitive score, in which interviewers imagined the recruit as a soldier and assigned a score of 1 to 5, did just as well as the sum of the six trait ratings.","Intuition adds value even in the justly derided selection interview, but only after a disciplined collection of objective information and disciplined scoring of separate traits.","The general lesson is: do not simply trust intuitive judgment, but do not dismiss it either."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the halo effect and structured-interview design: by having interviewers rate six traits separately with factual questions in a fixed order, Kahneman's army interview progressed from completely useless to moderately useful in predicting soldier performance. The surprise finding that a final intuitive 'close your eyes' score matched the trait sum showed intuition adds value only on top of disciplined measurement.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the halo effect and the design of structured interviews: in Kahneman's Israeli army assignment, six separately scored factual traits predicted recruits' performance far better than free-form global interviews, and intuition added value only after disciplined scoring of separate traits.","stage_1_observation":"You are Lieutenant Kahneman at an IDF base in 1955, briefing a room of nineteen-year-old interviewers, mostly women exempt from combat duty. They are used to exploring a recruit's inner mental life in fifteen-minute conversations. You hand them a list of six traits (responsibility, sociability, masculine pride, and others), each to be rated on a five-point scale in a fixed order using factual questions about the recruit's pre-enlistment life. A young interviewer slams the paper down: 'You are turning us into robots!' The rest murmur in support.","stage_1_question":"The interviewers are mutinying because you want facts instead of their favorite open-ended probing. What do you do to preserve predictive validity without losing the team?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Hold the line on the structured procedure: standardized factual questions scored one trait at a time in a fixed sequence. The open-ended global interview had proven almost useless, and scoring separate attributes avoids the halo effect where a favorable first impression colors every later rating.","stage_2_intervention":"A month later the results arrive: the sum of the six ratings predicts commanders' performance evaluations far better than the old method. But the 'close your eyes' compromise, where interviewers imagined the recruit as a soldier and assigned a 1-5 score, did just as well as the sum of the six ratings. Your team now wants to scrap the trait grid entirely and go back to pure intuition. What lesson do you apply?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep the trait grid and give the intuitive 'close your eyes' score equal weight, not more. The chapter's lesson is that intuition adds value only after disciplined collection of objective information and disciplined scoring of separate traits; the compromise worked precisely because the intuition came last, on top of the structured ratings, not instead of them."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Hiring Sales Reps: The DIY Formula","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled conference room at a medical-device firm. CEO Marcus Bell is grinning at Priya, a warm and funny candidate, while the flatly spoken engineer candidate sits at the far end of the table. A whiteboard in the corner shows an empty six-trait grid with a 1-5 scale, and a sticky note reads 'resist inventing broken legs.'","player_mission":"Walk Marcus through the chapter's do-it-yourself hiring procedure instead of his charm-driven gut call, and then stop him from overturning the formula when his personal favorite loses on the numbers.","key_concepts":["The chapter's do-it-yourself hiring procedure selects a few prerequisite traits such as technical proficiency, engaging personality, and reliability; six dimensions is a good number.","The chosen traits should be as independent as possible from each other and reliably assessable by asking a few factual questions.","To avoid halo effects, information on one trait must be collected and scored before moving on to the next; the interviewer should not skip around.","The candidate's score is the sum of the six individual ratings, and the interviewer should firmly resolve to hire the highest scorer.","The procedure takes about half an hour of preparation and can make a significant difference in the quality of people hired.","People normally go into interviews unprepared and choose by an overall intuitive judgment such as 'I looked into his eyes and liked what I saw.'","The broken-leg rule: it is proper to disregard a formula only if rare, decisive information arrives, such as the individual breaking a leg today.","The temptation to invent broken legs to change the ranking should be resisted, because people who overrule formulas are wrong more often than not.","Interviewers are overconfident in their intuitions and assign too much weight to their personal impressions.","A simple equally weighted formula based on existing statistics or common sense is often good enough to outdo expert judgment."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the chapter's do-it-yourself hiring procedure: six independent traits, factual questions scored one at a time to block the halo effect, and a sum that decides the hire. The book warns against 'inventing broken legs' - the temptation to invent an excuse to overrule the formula for a candidate you prefer, which research shows is usually a mistake.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests disciplined hiring: selecting a few independent traits, scoring each with factual questions one at a time, summing the scores, and resolving in advance to take the highest score rather than inventing broken legs to overrule the formula.","stage_1_observation":"You are a consultant watching Marcus Bell, CEO of a medical-device firm, interview four candidates for a sales role. Marcus has no questions written down. Candidate Two, Priya, gives warm, funny answers and great handshake eye contact; Marcus is visibly charmed. Candidate Three, a former engineer, speaks flatly but answers every factual question about quotas and territory history precisely. By the time the lobby door closes, Marcus is already telling his CFO, 'I looked her in the eyes and liked what I saw.'","stage_1_question":"Before the interviews, Marcus skipped preparation. He is about to hire Priya on global charm. What should he do instead?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Stop, pick about six independent traits relevant to the job such as technical proficiency, engaging personality, and reliability, draft a few factual questions per trait with a 1-5 scoring rule, and re-interview the candidates scoring one trait at a time before moving on. Then hire the highest total, not the candidate he liked best.","stage_2_intervention":"Two weeks later Marcus ran the procedure. Candidate Three scored highest, but Marcus keeps staring at Priya's résumé photo. 'I know the numbers say Three,' he tells you, 'but I have a feeling about Priya. She'll fit our culture. Can't we make an exception, maybe weight the interview impression a bit more?' The chapter's broken-leg rule is on your mind. What is the right move?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Refuse the exception. The broken-leg rule permits overriding a formula only for rare, decisive information, and 'I like her better' is neither rare nor decisive; it is the halo effect trying to redraw the ranking. The chapter instructs you to firmly resolve to hire the highest score even if you like another candidate more, because people who overrule formulas are wrong more often than not."}]},{"id":"ch27","title":"22. Expert Intuition: When Can We Trust It?","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Recognition-Primed Decision vs. Question Substitution","visual_frame_description":"Split-screen hospital scene. Left: Dr. Ana Mora, a 14-year trauma veteran, stands in a trauma bay while a crash victim is wheeled in on a gurney and monitors beep; a resident holds a tablet. Right: a boardroom where a woman in a navy suit stares at a slide that pairs the CEO's portrait with the label 'patient volume forecast.' A sorting board with three labeled columns spans the bottom of the screen.","player_mission":"Read each evidence card and drag it into the mechanism column it best supports. Then select the single mechanism that best explains Ana's rapid 'torn aorta' call before the next shift begins.","key_concepts":["Intuition is recognition: the situation provides a cue that gives the expert access to information stored in memory, and the information provides the answer.","The recognition-primed decision (RPD) model describes expertise in firefighting, chess, and other domains.","RPD has two phases: a tentative plan comes to mind automatically from associative memory (System 1), then is mentally simulated to check if it will work (System 2).","Fireground commanders usually generate only a single plausible option, not a pair of options to compare.","If a course of action has shortcomings, the expert modifies it; if it cannot be easily modified, she turns to the next most plausible option.","System 1 is often able to produce quick answers to difficult questions by substitution, creating coherence where there is none.","The question that is answered by substitution is not the one that was intended, but the answer is quick and plausible enough to pass the lenient review of System 2.","Voters elected Harding because they substituted an intuitive judgment of his strong, decisive appearance for a forecast of his presidential performance.","A reader of the book should expect a substituted intuition to be held with confidence.","The mystery of knowing without knowing is not a distinctive feature of intuition; it is the norm of mental life."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the contrast between recognition-primed intuition and question substitution. Klein's RPD model shows skilled intuition as System 1 pattern recognition checked by a System 2 mental simulation, whereas Kahneman shows that System 1 can substitute an easy question for a hard one, producing confident but misdirected judgments.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"At Memorial General's trauma bay, Dr. Ana Mora takes one look at a crash victim and tells the resident to prep for a torn aorta. Across town, a hospital board member predicts next quarter's patient volume based on how impressed she is by the CEO's presentation. Both judgments were fast and confident. Sort the evidence below into the mechanism that best explains it, then answer: which mechanism explains Ana's rapid judgment?","column_a_label":"Recognition-primed intuition (System 1 pattern recognition plus System 2 simulation)","column_b_label":"Question substitution (System 1 answers an easier question than the one asked)","column_c_label":"Deliberate statistical algorithm (System 2 computes from base rates)","rows":[{"label":"Ana's single plan to prep for the aorta comes to mind automatically and she mentally simulates it before acting.","col":"a"},{"label":"The board member believes she is forecasting patient volume, but her judgment tracks the CEO's perceived energy and competence.","col":"b"},{"label":"Fireground commanders usually generate only one option and simulate whether it will work in the situation they are facing.","col":"a"},{"label":"Harding was elected because voters substituted a judgment of his strong, decisive appearance for a forecast of presidential performance.","col":"b"},{"label":"The situation provides a cue; the cue gives the expert access to information stored in memory, and the information provides the answer.","col":"a"},{"label":"The question that is answered is not the one that was intended, yet the quick answer passes the lax and lenient review of System 2.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The call was a lucky guess because Ana had no cues, just a hunch.","Ana answered a different, easier question than the one posed to her.","Ana ran a formal probabilistic model on the patient's charts before responding."],"explanation":"The rows split between two System 1 operations. Ana's judgment fits recognition-primed decision making: a cue in the situation gives access to stored patterns, a single tentative plan comes to mind automatically (System 1), and she checks it by mental simulation (System 2), exactly the two-phase process Klein described. The board member and the Harding voters instead exhibit substitution: System 1 answers an easy question about impressions (competence, appearance) while the judge believes she is answering the hard forecast question. Because the substituted answer is quick and plausible, System 2 endorses it, which is why both kinds of judgment are held with high confidence. The correct overall answer is 'a': Ana's rapid call is genuine recognition."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Conditions for Trustworthy Intuition","visual_frame_description":"A hospital-network operations conference room, with a mentor standing at a whiteboard divided into three columns. Small vignette cards float across the screen: an operating room with an anesthesiologist, a stock ticker wall, a nineteenth-century clinic with a doctor in a frock coat, a burning building with a chief in turnout gear, a parliament building with a pundit's face, and a surgeon's operating table.","player_mission":"Drag each professional-setting card into the column that describes its environment. Then select the single column where you can legitimately trust an expert's intuition.","key_concepts":["The two conditions for acquiring a skill are a sufficiently regular environment and an opportunity to learn the regularities through prolonged practice.","When both conditions are satisfied, intuitions are likely to be skilled.","Chess is an extreme example of a regular environment; bridge and poker also provide robust statistical regularities that can support skill.","Stock pickers and political scientists who make long-term forecasts operate in a zero-validity environment.","Robin Hogarth described 'wicked' environments in which professionals are likely to learn the wrong lessons from experience.","The typhoid physician developed a sense of clinical infallibility because his own unwashed hands spread the disease.","Anesthesiologists benefit from good feedback because the effects of their actions are quickly evident.","Radiologists obtain little information about the accuracy of their diagnoses, so their feedback is sparse or absent.","If a strong predictive cue exists, human observers will find it, given a decent opportunity to do so.","Intuition cannot be trusted in the absence of stable regularities in the environment."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the boundary condition for skilled intuition: a regular, predictable environment plus an opportunity to learn its regularities through practice. Kahneman and Klein agreed that when both conditions hold, intuitions are likely to be valid, while in zero-validity or wicked environments confidence is not diagnostic of accuracy.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Your mentor at a hospital network is deciding whose gut judgment to trust in an operations meeting, and the candidates disagree. Sort the professional settings below by whether their environment can support skilled intuition, then answer: which column describes the environment where you can legitimately trust an expert's intuition?","column_a_label":"Regular environment with opportunity to learn (skilled intuition possible)","column_b_label":"Low-validity or zero-validity environment (no reliable cues to learn)","column_c_label":"Wicked environment (feedback teaches the wrong lesson)","rows":[{"label":"Anesthesiologists, because the effects of their actions are quickly evident in the operating room.","col":"a"},{"label":"Stock pickers making long-term forecasts of company value.","col":"b"},{"label":"A physician in the early twentieth century who palpated patients' tongues without washing his hands, then watched patient after patient become ill.","col":"c"},{"label":"Fireground commanders who accumulate a decade of real and virtual experience fighting fires.","col":"a"},{"label":"Political scientists making forecasts a decade ahead.","col":"b"},{"label":"Surgeons, who can be highly proficient in some operations while remaining novices in others.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Trust the most self-confident professional, because subjective confidence reliably tracks accuracy.","Low-validity environments still allow skilled intuition as long as the expert has practiced for many years.","Any environment produces skilled intuition equally, because expertise is a single transferable skill."],"explanation":"The two conditions for acquiring a skill are an environment sufficiently regular to be predictable and an opportunity to learn the regularities through prolonged practice. Anesthesiologists, firefighters, and surgeons face complex but fundamentally orderly situations, so their intuitions can be skilled. Stock pickers and long-term political forecasters operate in a zero-validity environment where the events they forecast are fundamentally unpredictable, so their failures reflect the environment rather than a lack of talent. The typhoid physician is the classic wicked environment: he learned infallibility from outcomes that his own unwashed hands had caused, the wrong lesson from experience. The correct answer is 'a': regularity plus a learning opportunity is the boundary condition for trusting intuition."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"WYSIATI and Subjective Confidence","visual_frame_description":"A coaching studio with a long glass table. Six evidence cards float over the table: a chessboard in a mid-game tactical position, a dark radiology reading room, a glowing narrative labeled 'the story,' a silent web of associative nodes, a statistician's spreadsheet, and a quote of Kahneman's words in quotation marks. Three labeled columns line the back wall.","player_mission":"Classify each source of confidence into the correct mechanism column. Then select the mechanism that best explains why unfounded intuitions are held with such high confidence.","key_concepts":["Confidence in a belief traces to two related impressions: cognitive ease and coherence.","We are confident when the story we tell ourselves comes easily to mind, with no contradiction and no competing scenario.","Ease and coherence do not guarantee that a belief held with confidence is true.","The associative machine is set to suppress doubt and to evoke ideas and information that are compatible with the currently dominant story.","A mind that follows WYSIATI will achieve high confidence much too easily by ignoring what it does not know.","Subjective confidence is not a reliable guide to the validity of an intuition.","True experts know the limits of their knowledge, while pseudo-experts have no idea that they do not know what they are doing.","Statistical algorithms greatly outdo humans in noisy environments because they are more likely to detect weakly valid cues and to use them consistently.","Judgments that answer the wrong question can also be made with high confidence.","At least 10,000 hours of dedicated practice are required to attain the highest levels of chess performance."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the WYSIATI account of overconfidence: cognitive ease and coherence generate subjective confidence without regard to whether valid cues exist. Kahneman argues that because the mind ignores what it does not know and suppresses doubt, high confidence accompanies unfounded intuitions as easily as genuine ones, making subjective confidence a poor index of accuracy.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A leadership coach is teaching executives how to weigh confidence. The claim on the table is 'I trust my gut, and I am rarely wrong.' Sort the evidence below by the source of the confidence, then answer: which mechanism best explains why high confidence so often accompanies unfounded intuitions?","column_a_label":"WYSIATI-driven confidence (the mind ignores what it does not know)","column_b_label":"Confidence earned from a valid learning history (skilled intuition)","column_c_label":"Algorithmic consistency (weak cues applied reliably by a model)","rows":[{"label":"A mind that follows WYSIATI achieves high confidence much too easily by ignoring what it does not know.","col":"a"},{"label":"A chess master's certainty about a tactical sequence after ten thousand hours of dedicated practice.","col":"b"},{"label":"A radiologist who is confident about diagnoses whose accuracy she never learns about.","col":"a"},{"label":"Statistical algorithms are more likely than human judges to detect weakly valid cues and to maintain modest accuracy by using them consistently.","col":"c"},{"label":"The associative machine suppresses doubt and evokes ideas that are compatible with the currently dominant story.","col":"a"},{"label":"Subjective confidence is not a good diagnostic of accuracy because judgments that answer the wrong question can be made with high confidence.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Confidence is produced only by explicit, deliberate reasoning about the evidence.","WYSIATI applies only to perception and never to judgments about what one knows.","Algorithms are always worse than human experts at detecting weakly valid cues."],"explanation":"Confidence in a belief traces to two impressions: cognitive ease and coherence. The story comes easily to mind with no contradiction, so the mind feels sure. Because the associative machine suppresses doubt and WYSIATI makes the mind ignore what it does not know, high confidence is reached much too easily and does not distinguish a substituted, unfounded judgment from a genuinely skilled one. The chess master's confidence, by contrast, is grounded in a decade of practice in a regular environment, and the algorithm's modest, consistent accuracy in noisy environments is exactly what Meehl's research documents. The overall answer is 'a': WYSIATI is why confidence so often overreaches the evidence."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Anesthesiologist's Sixth Sense (Genuine Skilled Intuition)","visual_frame_description":"A hospital case-conference room with a long oak table. Dr. Yuki Tanaka sits across from a quality-review board, an anesthesia logbook open in front of her. A timeline of timed observations plays along the bottom of the screen with timestamps, and a transcript window shows the Q&A scrolling beside the speakers.","player_mission":"Review Dr. Tanaka's baseline, watch the timed nonverbal log, and read the interrogation transcript. Decide whether her claim is genuine skilled intuition, an illusion of validity, or deliberate deception, and justify the verdict with evidence.","key_concepts":["Anesthesiologists benefit from good feedback because the effects of their actions are likely to be quickly evident.","Radiologists obtain little information about the accuracy of the diagnoses they make, which is why their intuitive skills develop less.","When an anesthesiologist says 'I have a feeling something is wrong,' everyone in the operating room should prepare for an emergency.","Intuition is recognition: the situation provides a cue that gives the expert access to information stored in memory.","Subjective confidence is not a reliable guide to the validity of an intuition.","Klein's observation is that true experts know the limits of their knowledge.","The accurate intuitions Klein described are due to highly valid cues that System 1 has learned to use, even if System 2 has not learned to name them.","Expertise is not a single skill; it is a collection of skills, and the same professional may be expert in some tasks while a novice in others.","Whether professionals develop intuitive expertise depends on the quality and speed of feedback and on sufficient opportunity to practice.","Emotional learning can be quick, but expertise in complex tasks is slow because it is a large collection of miniskills."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests whether a confident claim reflects skilled intuition or an illusion of validity. Because Dr. Tanaka works in a regular environment with rapid feedback and can name a valid cue and a past failure, her 'feeling something is wrong' is recognition-based skill rather than overconfidence or deception.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dr. Yuki Tanaka, a 58-year-old anesthesiologist at St. Cecilia's Hospital, tells a quality-review board that when she gets 'a feeling something is wrong' in the operating room, the team should prepare for an emergency. Is her claim genuine skilled intuition, an illusion of validity, or deliberate self-promotion?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Yuki Tanaka is a 58-year-old anesthesiologist at St. Cecilia's Hospital with 30 years of operating-room experience. Colleagues describe her as calm and understated; she rarely makes dramatic claims and is known for catching subtle trends in heart rate and blood pressure that others miss. She keeps meticulous anesthesia records and reviews them monthly.","observed_nonverbal_log":"14:02 - Case conference begins; Dr. Tanaka is asked whether her intraoperative warnings are 'just intuition.' She nods slowly, with no defensiveness and steady eye contact. 14:05 - She opens her logbook and points to two specific cases from the past month; her voice is even and her pacing deliberate. 14:09 - Asked whether she can name the cue she uses, she pauses briefly, then says she tracks small, consistent drifts in vital signs that the monitor alarms are too slow to flag. 14:12 - Asked whether she has ever been wrong when she felt something was wrong, she answers immediately and without hedging that yes, she has been wrong, and describes a near-miss she misread three years ago. 14:15 - She does not boast, does not raise her voice, and does not dismiss the question as naive.","interrogation_transcript":"Q: Dr. Tanaka, when you get that feeling in the OR, what exactly are you responding to?\nTanaka: The numbers tell a story before the alarms do. A slow drift in saturation, a heart rate that climbs a little and doesn't come back. I've seen those patterns end badly, so I say something.\nQ: How do you know the warning is worth acting on?\nTanaka: Because I've been in that room a few thousand times, and when I've acted on it, the team catches it early. When I didn't act once, three years ago, I was wrong to stay quiet.\nQ: Aren't you just being dramatic? Confident people say this all the time.\nTanaka: I'm not trying to be confident. I'm telling you the trend is there. Check the chart.\nQ: If you can name the cue, is it really intuition?\nTanaka: The recognition is automatic. Naming it afterwards is the part that takes a second.","correct_diagnosis":"Genuine skilled intuition, not deception and not an illusion of validity. Dr. Tanaka operates in a regular environment where the effects of her actions are quickly evident, has a long and documented learning history, can articulate the cue, and reports a concrete past failure, which is the signature of an expert who knows the limits of her knowledge.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her baseline shows no pattern of boasting or overclaiming, so the confident tone is not deception. The key evidence is environmental: anesthesiologists receive immediate feedback because their interventions change vital signs within seconds, which the book identifies as a condition for developing skilled intuition. Her ability to name the cue (slow drifts in saturation and heart rate) and her willingness to cite a past misreading are signs that she has learned the regularities rather than mistaking luck for skill. The chapter's rule applies directly: when an anesthesiologist says 'I have a feeling something is wrong,' everyone in the operating room should prepare for an emergency.","distractors":["She is suffering from the illusion of validity, because her confidence reflects cognitive ease rather than skill.","She is deliberately exaggerating her warnings to raise her standing at the hospital.","Her intuition is bogus because she can name the cue, and true intuition is supposed to be ineffable."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Self-Confident Stock Picker (Illusion of Validity)","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled broadcast studio for an investor panel. Marcus Delgado sits in a leather chair, hands clasped, facing a moderator and a bank of cameras. A thin timeline of timed observations with timestamps scrolls across the bottom, and a transcript panel slides up beside the speakers as the interview plays.","player_mission":"Audit Marcus Delgado's claim: study his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript. Determine whether his 'sixth sense' is genuine skill, an illusion of validity, or outright fabrication, and mark the verdict with supporting evidence.","key_concepts":["Stock pickers making long-term forecasts operate in a zero-validity environment.","The failures of stock pickers reflect the basic unpredictability of the events they try to forecast.","As a general proposition, subjective confidence is commonly too high and often uninformative.","There are many pseudo-experts who have no idea that they do not know what they are doing.","In the absence of valid cues, intuitive 'hits' are due either to luck or to lies.","It seems fair to blame professionals for believing they can succeed in an impossible task.","Substituted judgments, which answer the wrong question, can be made with high confidence.","Whether professionals develop intuitive expertise depends on the quality and speed of feedback.","Klein and Kahneman agreed that confidence in intuitions is not a reliable guide to their validity.","The unrecognized limits of professional skill help explain why experts are often overconfident."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the illusion of validity in a zero-validity environment. Marcus's unverifiable confidence and refusal to confront a track record fit Kahneman's finding that subjective confidence is a poor index of accuracy, and that in the absence of stable regularities intuitive hits are luck or self-deception.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus Delgado, a 55-year-old equity strategist at Meridian Capital, tells an investor panel that he has a 'sixth sense' for which stocks will triple over the next five years. Should the panel trust his intuition, or is this an illusion of validity or even fabrication?","subject_baseline":"Marcus spent 22 years as a senior analyst and is famous for his rapid-fire confidence: he answers questions instantly, never second-guesses himself, and has built a public brand on decisive calls. He keeps no personal track record of his long-term predictions, and colleagues note that he celebrates his hits and rarely revisits his misses.","observed_nonverbal_log":"10:03 - Asked how often his five-year stock calls are correct, Marcus smiles, answers immediately ('more often than not'), and does not cite a number. 10:06 - Asked how he would verify that claim, he leans forward, speaks faster, and pivots to a story about a biotech stock he recommended in 2019 that 'everyone said would fail.' 10:09 - Pressed for his record, he looks away from the camera toward the window, crosses his arms, and says tracking accuracy 'isn't how the business works.' 10:11 - Asked about a high-profile call that lost 60% of its value, he laughs and says 'You win some, you lose some' before moving to the next question. 10:14 - He returns to describing his gut with visible enthusiasm, straightening in his chair and raising his voice.","interrogation_transcript":"Moderator: Mr. Delgado, you've said you have a sixth sense for stocks. What's your long-term accuracy rate?\nMarcus: More often than not. I've been doing this longer than most people in this room have been investing.\nModerator: Can you show us the numbers?\nMarcus: Nobody keeps those kinds of scorecards. This business is about judgment, not bookkeeping.\nModerator: In 2021 you called NexWave a ten-bagger. It is down 60%.\nMarcus: The thesis was right, the timing was wrong. The market doesn't always agree with you.\nModerator: Why should a family trust its savings to your intuition?\nMarcus: Because I feel it. When I'm right, I'm very right.","correct_diagnosis":"This is an illusion of validity, not skilled intuition. Marcus's environment for five-year stock forecasting is essentially zero-validity, he has no learning history with feedback (no scorecard), and his confidence is the sign of a pseudo-expert who does not know that he does not know. His intuition is not trustable.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter's test is not confidence but the environment and learning history. Long-term stock picking is the book's example of a zero-validity environment, where there are no stable regularities to learn. Marcus's own behavior confirms the absence of a learning history: he cannot produce a track record, deflects every request for verification, and treats a 60% loss as a timing issue, so there is no evidence of feedback that could have taught him valid cues. His smooth, unhedged delivery is the chapter's warning that judgments which answer the wrong question can be made with high confidence, and that subjective confidence is a poor diagnostic of accuracy. In the absence of valid cues, intuitive hits are due either to luck or to lies.","distractors":["He is genuinely skilled, because decades of experience always produce reliable intuition.","He is deliberately lying about a specific, verifiable fact.","He should be trusted because his confidence shows his System 2 has approved his judgment."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Wicked-Environment Clinician (Illusory Infallibility)","visual_frame_description":"A conference room at a bustling urgent-care chain. Dr. Ian Becker in a white coat sits opposite an interviewer, with a tablet reading 'Discharge outcomes: not tracked' faintly visible in a corner. A two-column comparison on screen shows his vivid recalled rescues versus an empty 'misses' ledger.","player_mission":"Audit Dr. Becker's intuition: study his baseline, timed nonverbal log, and transcript. Decide whether his 'feeling' is genuine skilled intuition, an illusion of validity from a wicked environment, or deliberate deception, then lock in the verdict with evidence.","key_concepts":["Robin Hogarth described 'wicked' environments in which professionals are likely to learn the wrong lessons from experience.","A physician who palpated patients' tongues without washing his hands developed a sense of clinical infallibility as patient after patient became ill.","Whether professionals develop intuitive expertise depends on the quality and speed of feedback.","The feedback that therapists receive from patients' long-term outcomes is sparse, delayed, or nonexistent.","Subjective confidence is not a reliable guide to the validity of an intuition.","Many pseudo-experts have no idea that they do not know what they are doing (the illusion of validity).","If a strong predictive cue exists, human observers will find it, given a decent opportunity to do so.","Statistical algorithms are more accurate than human judges, although they were never very accurate.","The unrecognized limits of professional skill help explain why experts are often overconfident.","Intuition cannot be trusted in the absence of stable regularities in the environment."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests intuitive claims made in a 'wicked' environment, where feedback is absent or teaches the wrong lesson. Dr. Becker's confident, dramatic recall of rescues without any outcome record reproduces the typhoid-physician pattern: an illusion of validity built on biased feedback rather than learned cues.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dr. Ian Becker, medical director of a fast-growing chain of urgent-care clinics, claims he can 'feel' which patients are about to worsen before any test turns positive. Is his intuition genuine skilled intuition, an illusion of validity from a wicked environment, or deliberate fabrication?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Becker is 61, board-certified in internal medicine, and charismatic; nurses describe him as a natural diagnostician. He has never kept outcome records, rejects clinical dashboards as 'cookbook medicine,' and relies on what he calls clinical gestalt. The chain gives clinicians no feedback on what happens to patients after discharge.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:40 - Asked how he knows a patient will crash, Dr. Becker taps his temple and says 'Twenty years at the bedside. You develop a feel.' His delivery is smooth and immediate. 09:44 - Asked which patients he has correctly predicted, he names two dramatic resuscitations from memory, each with vivid detail. 09:47 - Asked whether he tracks his false alarms, he waves a hand and says those don't stay with you. 09:50 - Asked whether the chain reviews discharge outcomes, he looks mildly annoyed and answers that review is for academics, not for people who save lives. 09:53 - When the interviewer reads a chart note where his gut feeling preceded a normal workup, he shrugs and calls it defensive medicine. 09:56 - He never requests to see the data the interviewer references and ends the interview early.","interrogation_transcript":"Interviewer: Dr. Becker, what makes your gut reliable when the tests are normal?\nBecker: Years of watching patients die when I trusted numbers over my instincts. The body sends signals before the labs catch up.\nInterviewer: Can you show me a record of your predictions, hits and misses together?\nBecker: That's not how real medicine works. You don't keep a scorecard on a life.\nInterviewer: The clinic system doesn't give you feedback on post-discharge outcomes, does it?\nBecker: I don't need a committee to tell me I was right.\nInterviewer: If you can't see your misses, how do you know the feeling isn't just confidence?\nBecker: Because I'm the one who's been in the room.","correct_diagnosis":"Illusory validity born of a wicked environment. Dr. Becker's setting gives him no reliable feedback because patients' post-discharge outcomes are never tracked, and his memory-based hits are selected for drama while misses are discarded, so the regularities he feels may be artifacts of biased recall rather than learned cues. His confidence cannot be validated.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter's two conditions are missing: the environment is not demonstrably regular, and there is no opportunity to learn through accurate feedback because the chain never closes the loop on outcomes. Recall the book's warning example: the typhoid physician who felt infallible while his own unwashed hands caused the illnesses, a wicked environment teaching the wrong lesson. Dr. Becker's refusal to confront misses and his reliance on vivid, memorable resuscitations are exactly how an illusory sense of infallibility is built. His confidence is high but nondiagnostic, matching the principle that subjective confidence is not a reliable guide to validity.","distractors":["His intuition is genuine because his rescues are dramatic and he remembers them clearly.","Board certification and decades at the bedside are sufficient proof of valid intuition.","He is lying to the interviewer to protect his reputation."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Fireground Commander's Recognition-Primed Decision","visual_frame_description":"A night street scene at a burning two-story apartment building. Chief Dana Whitfield in turnout gear studies the smoke while her crew waits at the curb, hose lines in hand. A HUD overlay tracks her decision process: pattern retrieval (System 1), then a simulated run of the stairwell plan (System 2), then a branching path when the plan fails.","player_mission":"Guide Chief Whitfield through the recognition-primed decision process: watch how she generates and simulates a single plan, then decide how she adapts when the plan shows shortcomings before conditions worsen.","key_concepts":["Fireground commanders usually generate only a single option, and that is all they need.","Commanders draw on a repertoire of patterns compiled during more than a decade of real and virtual experience.","The commander evaluates a candidate plan by mentally simulating it to see if it will work in the situation she is facing.","If the course of action has shortcomings, the expert modifies it; if it cannot be easily modified, she turns to the next most plausible option.","The first phase of the recognition-primed decision is an automatic function of associative memory, which is System 1.","The second phase is a deliberate mental simulation that checks the plan, an operation of System 2.","Herbert Simon's definition: intuition is nothing more and nothing less than recognition.","Accurate intuitions are due to highly valid cues that the expert's System 1 has learned to use.","Fireground commanders and clinical nurses are among the professionals who have real expertise.","The situation provides a cue; the cue gives the expert access to information stored in memory."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the recognition-primed decision model of expert intuition: System 1 automatically recognizes a pattern and proposes a single plan, which System 2 then checks by mental simulation. Klein's studies of fireground commanders show that skilled intuition is pattern recognition, not magic or luck.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the recognition-primed decision (RPD) model: a fireground commander draws on a decade of stored fire patterns, generates a single plausible plan automatically (System 1), and evaluates it by mental simulation (System 2), modifying or discarding it before acting.","stage_1_observation":"A two-story apartment building in a working-class district has smoke pouring from second-floor windows. Chief Dana Whitfield, an 18-year veteran of the fire service, stands at the curb with her hands on her hips, studying the smoke's color, the window frames, and the fire's trajectory. Her crew waits, radios crackling. She does not run a formal options analysis; a single course of action has already come to her.","stage_1_question":"According to Klein's studies of fireground commanders, what does Chief Whitfield generate and do first?","stage_1_correct_answer":"She generates a single plausible option from the repertoire of patterns she has compiled over a decade of real and virtual experience, and she evaluates it by mentally simulating whether it will work in this situation.","stage_2_intervention":"Chief Whitfield orders the initial attack through the front stairwell, but as her crew advances, smoke banks down and the plan begins to show shortcomings: the stairwell is a dead end under a collapsing roof truss. She has seconds to adapt before conditions worsen.","stage_2_correct_answer":"She modifies the plan to overcome the shortcoming; if it cannot be easily modified, she turns to the next most plausible option and runs the same mental simulation again until she finds an acceptable course of action."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Boundary: When to Trust a Claimed Intuition","visual_frame_description":"A hospital strategy retreat with floor-to-ceiling windows. Priya Shah sits at the head of a conference table; to her left, Ray Odum holds an evacuation diagram, and to her right, Todd Hsu gestures at a stock chart. A confidence meter on screen shows both men pegging at maximum while a second meter labeled 'valid cues' sits near zero for Todd and high for Ray.","player_mission":"Identify the question that separates the two experts' claims, then resist the pressure when the untrustworthy expert doubles down on confidence and vivid anecdotes, and commit to the correct verdict.","key_concepts":["The two basic conditions for acquiring a skill are a regular environment and an opportunity to learn its regularities through prolonged practice.","The confidence that people have in their intuitions is not a reliable guide to their validity.","Do not trust anyone, including yourself, to tell you how much you should trust their judgment.","Stock pickers operate in a zero-validity environment; their failures reflect the unpredictability of the events they forecast.","Intuition cannot be trusted in the absence of stable regularities in the environment.","Subjective confidence is commonly too high and often uninformative.","In a less regular, low-validity environment, the heuristics of judgment are invoked.","When evaluating expert intuition, consider whether there was an adequate opportunity to learn the cues, even in a regular environment.","Fireground commanders and clinical nurses have real expertise, while stock pickers and pundits do not.","Judgments that answer the wrong question can also be made with high confidence."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the chapter's central boundary question: when can you trust a confident professional's intuition? The answer is to assess the regularity of the environment and the expert's learning history, setting confidence aside, because subjective confidence is a poor diagnostic of accuracy.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the boundary Kahneman and Klein mapped for when an expert's claimed intuition deserves trust: the two conditions of a regular environment and a genuine learning opportunity, with subjective confidence set aside as a diagnostic.","stage_1_observation":"A hospital strategy retreat. Two experts make confident claims to the CEO, Priya Shah. Fire-safety consultant Ray Odum, who has led 300 building evacuations, insists a new office layout's exit paths are dangerously slow in a smoke event. Financier Todd Hsu, whose fund calls are famously bullish, insists a regional bank stock will triple within three years. Both speak with identical unshakable confidence.","stage_1_question":"Priya can only trust one of these intuitions. Which question separates them, according to the chapter's framework?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Ask about the environment and learning history, not confidence: does the domain have stable regularities, and did the expert get prolonged practice with clear, timely feedback? Ray's evacuation judgments meet both conditions; Todd's three-year stock forecast is a zero-validity environment.","stage_2_intervention":"Todd pushes back, arguing that his confidence and twenty years of experience prove the call. He offers a vivid story of a past winner and warns Priya she will miss the boat. Priya must decide whether to revise her assessment.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Hold the line: subjective confidence is not a reliable guide to validity, vivid winning stories do not establish a learning history, and experience in a zero-validity environment cannot produce skill. Priya should trust Ray and treat Todd's forecast as ungrounded."}]},{"id":"ch28","title":"23. The Outside View","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Inside View, Outside View, and Baseline Adjustment","visual_frame_description":"A project-management classroom with three tall sorting columns. Draggable cards float across the screen: a chapter outline being written, Seymour at a blackboard with '40% failed, 7-10 yrs,' a silhouette of a tall woman beside a basketball center, and a database of construction projects. A scoring meter at the top tracks correct sorts.","player_mission":"Sort each forecasting behavior into inside view, outside view, or baseline adjustment, then select which approach generated the startlingly accurate forecast in the curriculum story.","key_concepts":["The inside view is the forecast we spontaneously adopt: we focus on our specific circumstances and search for evidence in our own experiences.","The outside view directs attention to a class of similar cases and their statistics.","Seymour estimated the base rate of the reference class: 40% of comparable curriculum teams failed and the rest took seven to ten years.","A baseline prediction is the prediction you make about a case if you know nothing except the category to which it belongs.","The baseline prediction should be the anchor for further adjustments.","Extrapolating from the plan in front of you is a mistake: we were forecasting based on the information in front of us, which is WYSIATI.","The spectacular accuracy of the outside-view forecast was surely a fluke and should not count as evidence for the validity of the outside view.","The argument for the outside view should be made on general grounds: if the reference class is properly chosen, it indicates where the ballpark is.","Seymour knew the relevant base rate but did not think of applying it, like the participants in the Tom W experiment.","People who have information about an individual case rarely feel the need to know the statistics of the class to which it belongs."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the distinction between the inside view, the outside view, and baseline adjustment. Kahneman's curriculum story shows that the inside view produces confidently optimistic forecasts, while the outside view, built on reference-class statistics, anchors a realistic baseline.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A project-management office is teaching teams how to forecast completion times. Sort the following forecasting behaviors into inside view, outside view, or baseline adjustment, then answer: which approach produced the stunningly accurate forecast in the curriculum story?","column_a_label":"Inside view (forecast from the specifics of your own plan and experience)","column_b_label":"Outside view (forecast from the statistics of a reference class)","column_c_label":"Baseline adjustment (start from the category average, then adjust for case specifics)","rows":[{"label":"The curriculum team extrapolates from the two chapters already written to estimate the whole book.","col":"a"},{"label":"Seymour recalls that 40% of comparable curriculum teams never finished and the rest took seven to ten years.","col":"b"},{"label":"Guessing a New York woman's height as the city average, then adjusting upward because her son is a starting basketball center.","col":"c"},{"label":"A team forecasts from its own detailed chapter list without considering how other teams fared.","col":"a"},{"label":"Bent Flyvbjerg's database of hundreds of similar projects provides statistics of overruns.","col":"b"},{"label":"The baseline prediction is used as the anchor for further adjustments.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The inside view is the more accurate approach because it uses case-specific detail.","Baseline adjustment means ignoring all case information and using only the average.","The outside view requires access to a crystal ball about your own future."],"explanation":"The rows separate three ways of forecasting. The inside view focuses on the specific circumstances and the plan in front of you, which is how the curriculum team extrapolated from chapters already written, an approach driven by WYSIATI. The outside view asks what happened to similar cases: Seymour's 40% failure rate and seven-to-ten-year timeline for comparable curriculum teams. Baseline adjustment is the recommended structure: make the reference-class average the anchor, then adjust for case-specific information, exactly as you would for the height of a New Yorker whose son plays basketball. The spectacularly accurate forecast in the story came from the outside view, although the book cautions that its accuracy was a fluke and that the argument for the outside view rests on general grounds."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Planning Fallacy and Its Cure (Reference Class Forecasting)","visual_frame_description":"A transit authority boardroom with a light-rail map on the wall beside a screen of budget charts. Three sorting columns fill the center: a chandeliered kitchen-renovation photo, a database of railway projects, and a balanced scale labeled 'gains, losses, probabilities.'","player_mission":"Sort each statement into planning-fallacy symptom, reference-class step, or rational-economic assumption, then select the correct definition of the planning fallacy.","key_concepts":["Amos and Kahneman coined the term planning fallacy for plans and forecasts that are unrealistically close to best-case scenarios.","A planning-fallacy forecast could be improved by consulting the statistics of similar cases.","A 2005 study found that in more than 90% of rail projects, the passenger forecasts were overestimated.","Planners overestimated rail ridership by 106% on average, and the average cost overrun was 45%.","American homeowners expected kitchen remodels to cost $18,658 but paid an average of $38,769.","Reference class forecasting is the technical name for the cure to the planning fallacy.","Flyvbjerg concluded that the tendency to underweight or ignore distributional information is perhaps the major source of error in forecasting.","Using distributional information from similar ventures is called taking the outside view.","In the standard rational model of economics, people take risks because the odds are favorable.","Contractors readily admit that they routinely make most of their profit on additions to the original plan."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix contrasts the planning fallacy, plans unrealistically close to best-case scenarios, with its cure, reference class forecasting. Kahneman and Flyvbjerg show that ignoring distributional information is the major source of forecasting error.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A city transit authority is commissioning a new light-rail line. Sort the statements below into planning-fallacy symptoms, reference class forecasting steps, or the rational-economic assumption they replace, then answer: which description defines the planning fallacy?","column_a_label":"Planning fallacy symptom (plan unrealistically close to best-case)","column_b_label":"Reference class forecasting step (the cure)","column_c_label":"Rational-economic assumption (risk taken because odds are favorable)","rows":[{"label":"Kitchen renovators expected the job to cost $18,658 and ended up paying an average of $38,769.","col":"a"},{"label":"Identify an appropriate reference class, such as kitchen renovations or large railway projects.","col":"b"},{"label":"Obtain the statistics of the class and use them to generate a baseline prediction.","col":"b"},{"label":"In the standard rational model, people take risks because the odds are favorable.","col":"c"},{"label":"Plans and forecasts that are unrealistically close to best-case scenarios.","col":"a"},{"label":"Use specific information about the case to adjust the baseline prediction when warranted.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The planning fallacy is cured by building a bigger budget reserve.","The planning fallacy means planners are too pessimistic about costs.","Reference class forecasting means ignoring the specific case entirely."],"explanation":"The planning fallacy is defined by Amos and Kahneman as plans and forecasts that are unrealistically close to best-case scenarios and could be improved by consulting the statistics of similar cases. The kitchen-renovation survey is a textbook symptom: an expected $18,658 and a realized $38,769. The remedy, per Flyvbjerg, is reference class forecasting: identify a reference class, obtain its statistics, generate a baseline prediction, and adjust only for specific reasons. The rational-economic assumption differs because it describes risk-taking based on favorable odds, whereas the optimistic bias leads people to take risks they misjudge. The correct answer is 'a': the definition centers on best-case planning."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why the Outside View Is Ignored","visual_frame_description":"A behavioral-consulting studio with three tall columns on a dark wall. Cards drift across the screen: a classroom of students ignoring a chart of global results, a Friday meeting with paper slips reading '2 years,' Seymour at the blackboard, a drop of ink over '40% failed,' and a boardroom of executives refusing to end a project.","player_mission":"Sort each neglect mechanism into its correct column, then select the single mechanism that best explains why the curriculum team carried on after hearing the base-rate statistics.","key_concepts":["We were forecasting based on the information in front of us, which is WYSIATI, and our commitment to the project was at its peak.","The main problem was that we failed to allow for the unknown unknowns.","Seymour's forecast from his inside view was not an adjustment from a baseline prediction that had not come to his mind.","Like the participants in the Tom W experiment, Seymour knew the relevant base rate but did not think of applying it.","Nisbett and Borgida's students completely neglected the global results they had just learned when making predictions about an individual case.","Pallid statistical information is routinely discarded when it is incompatible with one's personal impressions of a case.","In the competition with the inside view, the outside view doesn't stand a chance.","Kahneman calls the failure to abandon the project irrational perseverance: we gave up rationality rather than give up the enterprise.","The effort already invested is an instance of the sunk-cost fallacy.","Kahneman described the team's state as a form of lethargy, an unwillingness to think about what had happened."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix examines why the outside view is routinely ignored: WYSIATI-driven inside forecasts, pallid base-rate neglect, and sunk-cost-driven irrational perseverance. In the curriculum story, the decisive mechanism was irrational perseverance, because the team heard the grim statistics and carried on rather than quit.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A behavioral consulting firm wants to know why teams keep ignoring base-rate information. Sort the following mechanisms by how each explains the neglect, then answer: which mechanism best explains why the curriculum team, having heard Seymour's statistics, carried on as if nothing had happened?","column_a_label":"WYSIATI and the pull of the inside view (the plan in front of you dominates)","column_b_label":"Pallid base-rate neglect (statistical information loses to vivid impressions)","column_c_label":"Sunk-cost / irrational perseverance (already invested, unwilling to quit)","rows":[{"label":"Nisbett and Borgida's students, having just learned global results, completely neglected them when predicting an individual case.","col":"b"},{"label":"The curriculum team had a plausible plan before them and did not feel the need for statistics about other teams.","col":"a"},{"label":"Seymour knew the base rate but his own estimate was never an adjustment from a baseline that had not come to mind.","col":"a"},{"label":"The team had already invested a year of Friday meetings and could not imagine abandoning the project.","col":"c"},{"label":"A lawyer answers 'every case is unique' when asked about a reference class.","col":"a"},{"label":"We gave up rationality rather than give up the enterprise.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["They lacked the statistical information entirely, so they had nothing to use.","The base rate never entered anyone's awareness, including Seymour's, so it could not have been applied.","The outside view is more persuasive than the inside view, but the meeting simply ended too early to decide."],"explanation":"The three columns capture distinct reasons base rates lose. WYSIATI and the inside view dominate because the plan in front of you is all there is: the curriculum team did not feel the need for other teams' statistics until asked, and Seymour's own estimate was never an adjustment from a baseline that had not come to mind. Pallid base-rate neglect appears in Nisbett and Borgida's students, who discarded global results they had just learned when facing an individual case. But the decisive mechanism for the curriculum team's carrying on is sunk cost and irrational perseverance: they had already invested a year of effort, gave up rationality rather than give up the enterprise, and slipped into lethargy. The correct answer is 'c': the team acted on irrational perseverance, not ignorance."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Optimistic Project Sponsor (Planning Fallacy)","visual_frame_description":"A wood-paneled executive boardroom. Priya Raman stands at a screen showing an eleven-month Gantt chart and a $4.2M budget; the CFO sits opposite with a laptop. A timed observation strip runs along the bottom with timestamps, and an interrogation transcript scrolls beside the speakers.","player_mission":"Audit Priya's plan: study her baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript. Determine whether the proposal is genuine planning, the planning fallacy, or deliberate fabrication, and record the verdict with supporting evidence.","key_concepts":["The planning fallacy describes plans and forecasts that are unrealistically close to best-case scenarios.","A planning-fallacy forecast could be improved by consulting the statistics of similar cases.","Errors in the initial budget are not always innocent; the authors of unrealistic plans are often driven by the desire to get the plan approved.","The greatest responsibility for avoiding the planning fallacy lies with the decision makers who approve the plan.","The prevalent tendency to underweight or ignore distributional information is perhaps the major source of error in forecasting.","Planners should frame the forecasting problem so as to facilitate utilizing all the distributional information that is available.","The outside view is the cure to the planning fallacy.","In the competition with the inside view, the outside view doesn't stand a chance.","American homeowners expected kitchen remodels to cost $18,658 but paid an average of $38,769.","A well-run organization will reward planners for precise execution and penalize them for failing to anticipate difficulties."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests whether a confident project plan reflects genuine forecasting or the planning fallacy. Priya's best-case schedule, unquantified buffer, and dismissal of reference-class statistics match Kahneman's definition of the planning fallacy, exacerbated by the incentive to win approval.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya Raman, a marketing VP, presents a 'confident, deliverable' rollout of a new customer platform with an eleven-month schedule and a $4.2 million budget. The audit question: is this genuine planning, the planning fallacy, or deliberate fabrication to win approval?","subject_baseline":"Priya is a seasoned marketer with a strong record of on-time campaign launches. She is detail-oriented, keeps clean project logs, and has no history of deception. She is, however, competing with another VP for the same budget, and the CEO has publicly favored confident, can-do proposals.","observed_nonverbal_log":"11:02 - Priya opens with her eleven-month schedule, reciting it without notes. Her posture is confident; she speaks quickly and smiles at the CEO. 11:06 - Asked how the schedule was derived, she cites the team's prior launch ('nine months last time') without mentioning that the project had different scope and doubled its budget. 11:09 - The CFO asks what comparable platform rollouts in the industry took. Priya's smile tightens; she looks down at the table and says 'we're not like other companies,' then moves on. 11:12 - Asked about contingency for delays, she says 'we've built in buffer' but cannot say how much when pressed. 11:15 - She becomes visibly more animated when describing the upside (new customers, revenue) and visibly less so when the CFO asks about cost overruns in similar projects. 11:18 - She closes by asking for approval 'this month so we don't lose the window,' with a mild urgency in her voice.","interrogation_transcript":"CFO: How confident are you in the eleven-month date?\nPriya: Very. This team is the best I've run.\nCFO: What did the last comparable rollout in the industry take?\nPriya: We're not like other companies. We have the talent and the process.\nCFO: How much buffer is in the budget?\nPriya: Enough. We manage it tightly.\nCFO: What would a baseline prediction from similar platforms look like?\nPriya: I don't think you can run a plan like this from other people's failures.","correct_diagnosis":"Priya is committing the planning fallacy, not fabricating: her plan is unrealistically close to a best-case scenario and is not anchored to any reference-class statistics. Her confidence reflects optimism and the incentive to win approval, and her refusal to consult distributional information is the classic inside view.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The baseline shows Priya is honest and capable, so deliberate fabrication is not supported. But her own prior project doubled its budget, and she cites it as evidence of speed, selecting data that flatters the inside view. Her answers to the CFO ('we're not like other companies,' an unquantified 'buffer') are the signature of the planning fallacy: forecasts that could be improved by consulting the statistics of similar cases. The book notes that errors in initial budgets are not always innocent, because the authors of unrealistic plans are often driven by the desire to get the plan approved, and the greatest responsibility then lies with the decision makers who approve it. The verdict is the planning fallacy.","distractors":["She is lying outright to secure the budget.","Her plan is rational because her team is objectively more capable than industry baselines.","The plan is solid because it includes a buffer, which is the same as the outside view."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Contractor and the Budget Reserve (Self-Fulfilling Prophecy)","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit bridge-construction site with an agency director in a hard hat and Bruno Ferris in worn work boots standing over a site drawing. An email window pops up showing the change-order packet, and a small speech bubble shows Bruno's 'red meat' remark to his estimator. A transcript panel scrolls beside the pair.","player_mission":"Audit Bruno's behavior: review his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript. Decide whether he is exploiting the reserve, responding reasonably, or fabricating defects, then lock in the verdict with evidence.","key_concepts":["Decision makers may wish to estimate the budget reserve they need in anticipation of overruns, but such precautions often become self-fulfilling prophecies.","As one official told Flyvbjerg, a budget reserve is to contractors as red meat is to lions, and they will devour it.","Contractors of kitchen renovations and weapon systems readily admit that they routinely make most of their profit on additions to the original plan.","The failures of forecasting reflect the customers' inability to imagine how much their wishes will escalate over time.","Customers end up paying much more than they would if they had made a realistic plan and stuck to it.","Reference class forecasting is the cure to the planning fallacy.","Errors in the initial budget are not always innocent; the authors of unrealistic plans are driven by the desire to get the plan approved.","The greatest responsibility for avoiding the planning fallacy lies with the decision makers who approve the plan.","Planners should make every effort to utilize all the distributional information that is available.","A well-run organization will penalize planners for failing to allow for difficulties they could not have anticipated, the unknown unknowns."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the self-fulfilling prophecy of budget reserves. Kahneman and Flyvbjerg note that reserves added in anticipation of overruns invite contractors to devour them, converting a safety cushion into spent money rather than a realistic forecast.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A city infrastructure agency adds a 15% contingency reserve to a bridge-repair contract and hires Ferris & Sons. Is contractor Bruno Ferris's behavior a reasonable response to the reserve, or does it show the reserve becoming a self-fulfilling prophecy?","subject_baseline":"Bruno Ferris is a 52-year-old general contractor with a reputation for solid, honest work on fixed-price jobs; inspectors praise his crews. His company's margins are thin, and he has told friends that change orders are where the money is in public work.","observed_nonverbal_log":"09:00 - At the kickoff meeting, the agency director announces the 15% contingency for surprises. Bruno nods and writes a note; his expression is flat. 09:05 - Asked about the reserve, Bruno says 'reserves exist to be used' and smiles briefly, glancing at his estimator. 09:20 - During a site walk, Bruno points out two conditions he calls beyond the drawings that he says warrant change orders, though his own surveyor earlier logged them as minor. 09:30 - When the director suggests finishing under the original price, Bruno's tone turns cautious: 'That would mean cutting corners; you don't want that.' 09:41 - He emails a change-order packet to the agency 'before the budget gets clawed back,' copying the finance director. 09:55 - Over coffee, Bruno tells his estimator, quietly, that the reserve is red meat and they will be lions.","interrogation_transcript":"Director: Bruno, the reserve is only for genuine surprises.\nBruno: These are genuine. Nobody builds a bridge without surprises.\nDirector: The site walk showed minor conditions. Why a change order?\nBruno: You want the drawings interpreted generously? That's how you get a lawsuit.\nDirector: If you come in under budget, it looks great for your company.\nBruno: Coming in under budget means my crew works free. You don't want that either.\nDirector: What happens to the reserve if we don't spend it?\nBruno: It gets clawed back by the city. Reserves exist to be used.","correct_diagnosis":"The reserve is becoming a self-fulfilling prophecy. Bruno's baseline is honest work at fair margins, so he is not fabricating defects, but he treats the contingency as money to be captured, exactly the pattern the book describes: a budget reserve is to contractors as red meat is to lions, and they will devour it.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter warns that budget reserves set up in anticipation of overruns often become self-fulfilling prophecies: the customer's inability to imagine how their wishes will escalate, combined with contractors who make most of their profit on additions, converts the cushion into spent money. Bruno's baseline (honest on fixed-price work) rules out outright fabrication, but his behavior, declaring minor conditions beyond the drawings, warning that thrift means cutting corners, and quietly framing the reserve as meat, shows the reserve being devoured. The agency's remedy is not a larger reserve but reference-class forecasting and disciplined scope control, because in the competition between the inside view and the outside view, the inside view wins when money is on the table.","distractors":["Bruno is lying about the site conditions to steal from the agency.","The reserve is a sound planning practice with no downside.","Bruno's behavior is unrelated to the reserve; he would seek change orders anyway."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Friday Afternoon Forecast (Inside vs. Outside View)","visual_frame_description":"A university seminar room with worn wood floors and a blackboard covered in a syllabus outline. Around a table sit six adults; paper slips showing '2 years' estimates sit in a neat stack. Seymour hesitates at the blackboard, visibly embarrassed. A clock shows Friday afternoon.","player_mission":"Guide Daniel through the forecasting decision: first direct the conversation toward the outside view, then decide how to respond when the baseline statistics conflict with the team's comfortable plan.","key_concepts":["The initial estimates of about two years exhibited a planning fallacy, being closer to a best-case scenario than to a realistic assessment.","The inside view focuses on specific circumstances and searches for evidence in one's own experiences.","Extrapolating from the plan in front of us was a mistake: we were forecasting based on the information in front of us, which is WYSIATI.","The main problem was failing to allow for the unknown unknowns.","Seymour's estimate of the reference class: 40% of comparable teams failed and the rest took seven to ten years.","The baseline prediction is the prediction you make if you know nothing except the category to which a case belongs.","In the competition with the inside view, the outside view doesn't stand a chance.","Kahneman calls the failure to abandon the project irrational perseverance.","The book was eventually completed eight years later and was never used.","The outside view is the cure to the planning fallacy."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the inside view versus the outside view in forecasting. The curriculum team's two-year estimate is a planning fallacy, a best-case extrapolation that ignores reference-class statistics showing 40% failure and seven-to-ten-year timelines.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the inside view versus the outside view in project forecasting. A team that extrapolates from its own progress commits the planning fallacy, while a baseline drawn from similar cases gives a realistic anchor.","stage_1_observation":"It is a Friday afternoon in a university seminar room. A curriculum-design team has met weekly for a year, written two chapters, and run sample lessons. The team leader, Daniel, collects confidential estimates of the time to finish the textbook: the numbers cluster around two years, from one and a half to two and a half. Everyone nods, because the plan looks very manageable.","stage_1_question":"Daniel turns to Seymour, a curriculum expert, and asks about the history of other teams that built curricula from scratch. What is the right way to use what Seymour knows?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Ask Seymour to identify similar teams and estimate the base rate of the reference class, how many finished and how long they took, to build a baseline prediction, rather than relying on the team's extrapolation from its own two chapters.","stage_2_intervention":"Seymour reports that 40% of comparable teams never finished and the rest took seven to ten years. The team is shocked but, after a brief desultory debate, starts gathering its notebooks to continue. The book is not yet written; the only sunk cost is a year of meetings. Daniel must decide how to respond.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Treat the baseline as the anchor: acknowledge the planning fallacy and irrational perseverance, recognize that the new forecast conflicts with the comfortable plan, and explicitly weigh whether continuing is rational. The chapter's lesson is that the team should have quit that day."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Scottish Parliament Budget Battle (Megaproject Overrun)","visual_frame_description":"A Treasury committee room in Edinburgh with tall windows. A document titled 'Estimated Cost 40M pounds' lies on a mahogany table beside a second, later sheet reading '109M pounds' and a third with a handwritten 'cap 195M pounds.' A line graph on the wall traces the cost climbing toward 431M pounds over time.","player_mission":"Guide the committee: diagnose why the 40 million pound estimate failed, then choose the method that produces a realistic budget as costs escalate past successive caps.","key_concepts":["In July 1997 the Scottish Parliament building was estimated to cost up to 40 million pounds.","By June 1999 the budget was 109 million pounds; legislators then imposed a 195 million pound cap.","The building was finally completed in 2004 at an ultimate cost of roughly 431 million pounds.","The planning fallacy describes plans unrealistically close to best-case scenarios that could be improved by consulting statistics of similar cases.","A 2005 study of rail projects found average cost overruns of 45% and passenger forecasts overestimated by 106%.","As more evidence accumulated, the experts did not become more reliant on it.","Flyvbjerg concluded that the tendency to underweight or ignore distributional information is perhaps the major source of error in forecasting.","Reference class forecasting uses a large database providing information on plans and outcomes for hundreds of projects.","The outside view is the cure to the planning fallacy.","Decision makers need a realistic assessment of the costs and benefits of a proposal before making the final decision to approve it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the planning fallacy in a public megaproject. The Scottish Parliament building's climb from 40 million to 431 million pounds illustrates a best-case initial estimate never anchored to reference-class statistics, which reference class forecasting is designed to cure.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the planning fallacy in public projects: an initial best-case estimate that escalates repeatedly because it was never anchored to reference-class statistics.","stage_1_observation":"Edinburgh, July 1997. The proposed Scottish Parliament building is projected to cost up to 40 million pounds. The documents on the desk show a bold new design, an optimistic timetable, and no comparison to the cost distribution of comparable legislative or landmark building projects.","stage_1_question":"The Treasury team must decide whether to trust the 40 million pound estimate. What is the likeliest cause of the coming gap between estimate and reality?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The planning fallacy: the estimate is close to a best-case scenario and ignores distributional information about similar projects, which the book shows routinely run far over initial budgets.","stage_2_intervention":"By June 1999 the budget has climbed to 109 million pounds; legislators impose a 195 million pound cap, then demand a final cost estimate of 241 million pounds, and costs keep climbing. The committee must now set a realistic budget. What move follows the chapter's method?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Apply reference class forecasting before approving any number: identify comparable building projects, obtain the statistics of their cost overruns, generate a baseline prediction, and adjust only for specific reasons, instead of layering ad hoc caps that keep escalating."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The CEO's Delusional Optimism (Optimistic Risk Taking)","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled boardroom at dusk. Amara Osei stands at a screen full of green upward arrows and synergy diagrams; Ravi sits at the far end with a folder marked 'Reference-class data: 40 deals.' A risk register glows on a side screen, almost empty. The mood is eager as the vote is called.","player_mission":"Identify the optimistic-bias error shaping the acquisition decision, then decide how to handle the vote when the CEO dismisses baseline data as 'different market conditions.'","key_concepts":["Executives too easily fall victim to the planning fallacy when forecasting the outcomes of risky projects.","In its grip, they make decisions based on delusional optimism rather than on a rational weighting of gains, losses, and probabilities.","They overestimate benefits and underestimate costs.","They spin scenarios of success while overlooking the potential for mistakes and miscalculations.","As a result, they pursue initiatives that are unlikely to come in on budget or on time or to deliver the expected returns.","People often take on risky projects because they are overly optimistic about the odds they face.","In the standard rational model, people take risks because the odds are favorable.","Kahneman and Lovallo sketched a theory in which the optimistic bias is a significant source of risk taking.","The prevalent tendency to underweight or ignore distributional information is perhaps the major source of error in forecasting.","The outside view is the cure to the planning fallacy."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the optimistic-bias account of risk taking. Kahneman and Lovallo argue that executives pursue risky projects on delusional optimism, overestimating benefits and underestimating costs, rather than on a rational weighting of odds, which the outside view corrects.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is Kahneman and Lovallo's claim that the optimistic bias is a significant source of risk taking: executives make decisions based on delusional optimism rather than a rational weighting of gains, losses, and probabilities.","stage_1_observation":"In the boardroom of a mid-size pharmaceutical company, the CEO, Amara Osei, is selling a 900 million dollar acquisition. The deck she presents spins an unbroken scenario of success, with blockbuster synergies and aggressive growth curves, while the risk register is a single slide of green checkmarks. A junior analyst, Ravi, has quietly prepared the outside-view statistics of similar acquisitions but has not been invited to speak.","stage_1_question":"What error is shaping the board's decision as the deck is presented?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The planning fallacy and optimistic bias: the board is overestimating benefits and underestimating costs, deciding on delusional optimism rather than a rational weighting of gains, losses, and probabilities.","stage_2_intervention":"The CFO asks for a show of hands. Before anyone votes, Ravi raises a hand and offers the baseline: of 40 similar acquisitions in the last decade, 31 destroyed value, and only 3 delivered the promised synergies. Amara dismisses the data as different market conditions and asks again for the vote. The board must decide how to proceed.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Insist on the outside view before voting: anchor the decision on the reference-class baseline (31 of 40 similar deals destroyed value), treat the CEO's optimism as a risk to be tested rather than evidence, and require a plan that accounts for the distributional information instead of dismissing it as different market conditions."}]},{"id":"ch29","title":"24. The Engine of Capitalism","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Three System 1 biases behind overconfidence: above-average effect, competition neglect, and illusion of control","visual_frame_description":"A co-working space at dusk. Carmen Reyes, a start-up founder in a worn hoodie, stands at a whiteboard covered in roadmap arrows and sprint charts, gesturing at a growth curve she drew herself. Her co-founder, seated with a notebook, is asking about competitors. A sticky note on the wall reads 'We control our destiny.'","player_mission":"Classify each evidence card into the correct column of the matrix, then decide which bias Carmen's claim about self-control best illustrates. Select the column label that fits every row before answering the matrix question.","key_concepts":["Start-up founders asked how much their outcome depends on what they do in the firm answer at least 80% almost without exception, even when they doubt they will succeed.","The outcome of a start-up depends as much on the achievements of competitors and changes in the market as on its own efforts.","The observation that 90% of drivers believe they are better than average has changed in interpretation from self-aggrandizement to a cognitive bias.","People respond to the hard question 'Are you better than average as a driver?' by answering the easy question 'Are you a good driver?' without ever thinking about the average.","When asked about a task they find difficult, such as starting conversations with strangers, people readily rate themselves below average.","Competition neglect, coined by Camerer and Lovallo, arises when planners consider only their own plans and skills and leave rivals out of the decision.","The consequence of competition neglect is excess entry: more competitors enter the market than it can profitably sustain, so their average outcome is a loss.","The System 1 processes of WYSIATI and substitution produce both competition neglect and the above-average effect.","Entrepreneurs naturally focus on what they know best, their own plans and immediate threats such as funding, and find it natural to imagine a future in which competition plays little part."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the illusion of control and its System 1 siblings: WYSIATI and substitution make people overestimate how much their own actions determine outcomes while they neglect what they do not know. Kahneman reports that start-up founders estimate their fate is at least 80% in their own hands, even though competitors and market shifts matter as much.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A start-up founder, Carmen Reyes, tells her co-founder that her venture's fate depends at least 80% on what the team does itself. Which bias does this statement best illustrate?","column_a_label":"Above-average effect (substitution)","column_b_label":"Competition neglect (WYSIATI)","column_c_label":"Illusion of control","rows":[{"label":"Start-up founders routinely answer 'How much depends on your own actions?' with answers of 80% or more, even when they are not sure they will succeed.","col":"c"},{"label":"90% of drivers rate themselves above average because they answer the easy question 'Am I a good driver?' instead of comparing themselves to the true average.","col":"a"},{"label":"The Disney chairman released blockbusters on the same crowded weekend because each studio considered only its own film, story department, and marketing team.","col":"b"},{"label":"People asked about a genuinely hard skill, like starting conversations with strangers, readily rate themselves below average.","col":"a"},{"label":"More entrants enter a market than the market can profitably sustain, so the average entrant loses money: this is excess entry.","col":"b"},{"label":"Founders think their fate is almost entirely in their own hands, though competitors and market changes matter just as much.","col":"c"},{"label":"A restaurateur plans her menu and ad spend without counting how many rivals are opening on the same street.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Competition neglect arises from fear of rival studios.","The above-average effect is limited to driving ability.","The illusion of control strengthens as stakes rise and information grows."],"explanation":"Carmen's claim matches the illusion of control: WYSIATI makes founders focus on their own plans and actions, so they attribute most of the outcome to themselves. The above-average effect answers an easy question in place of a hard one, and competition neglect leaves rivals out of the mental picture; all three flow from the same System 1 machinery."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Overconfidence in forecasting: the CFO's worthless confidence intervals","visual_frame_description":"A mahogany boardroom at a Cleveland manufacturer at dusk. Nathan Adeyemi, a silver-haired CFO, stands at the head of a long table pointing at a slide of quarterly S&P 500 projections. Directors lean in; a projector throws his forecast curve onto the wall behind him.","player_mission":"Audit whether Nathan is deliberately lying to the board or is simply overconfident about his forecasts. Read his baseline behavior, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then issue a correct diagnosis with evidence.","key_concepts":["Professors at Duke University collected 11,600 forecasts from the chief financial officers of large corporations and examined their accuracy.","The correlation between the CFOs' estimates of the S&P index and the true value was slightly less than zero; when they said the market would go down, it was slightly more likely than not to go up.","The truly bad news was that the CFOs did not appear to know that their forecasts were worthless.","Participants gave a value they were 90% sure would be too high and one they were 90% sure would be too low; the range is called an 80% confidence interval.","An individual who sets confidence intervals on multiple occasions expects about 20% of the outcomes to be surprises, but the actual incidence in the Duke study was 67%, more than three times higher than expected.","To maintain the desired rate of surprises, the CFOs should have said year after year that there is an 80% chance the S&P return next year will be between -10% and +30%.","The confidence interval that properly reflects the CFOs' knowledge is more than four times wider than the intervals they actually stated.","Overconfidence is another manifestation of WYSIATI: when we estimate a quantity we rely on information that comes to mind and construct a coherent story in which the estimate makes sense.","A CFO who informs colleagues that there is a good chance the S&P returns will be between -10% and +30% can expect to be laughed out of the room, because the wide interval is a confession of ignorance."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests overconfidence as a manifestation of WYSIATI: when estimating a quantity, people rely on what comes to mind and build a coherent story, and they cannot allow for information that never came to mind. The Duke CFO study found 11,600 forecasts whose correlation with the true S&P value was slightly less than zero, with surprises occurring 67% of the time instead of the expected 20%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Nathan Adeyemi, a veteran CFO, lying to the board when he insists he can predict next year's S&P 500 return within a narrow band?","subject_baseline":"Nathan is a meticulous, soft-spoken finance chief with twenty years at a Cleveland manufacturing firm. He prepares detailed models, cites precise percentages from memory, and almost never raises his voice in meetings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-0:45 min: Nathan stands at the head of the boardroom table, posture upright, steady eye contact, a faint confident smile as he reveals his forecast of +9% for the S&P 500.","0:45-2:00 min: Asked about a downside scenario, he shakes his head briskly, waves a hand dismissively, and says a decline is 'not a real scenario.'","2:00-2:50 min: He states an 80% confidence interval of +6% to +12%; his voice stays flat and rehearsed, with no hesitation or self-interruption.","2:50-3:30 min: When the CEO presses him, he repeats the interval word-for-word and adds, 'I have been doing this for twenty years,' before crossing his arms."],"interrogation_transcript":["Board member: 'How confident are you in that range, Nathan?'","Nathan: 'I am 90% confident it falls inside it. That is what my models say.'","Board member: 'But last year your interval missed the actual return entirely.'","Nathan: 'Last year was an anomaly. This year the fundamentals are clear.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Nathan is not lying; he is genuinely overconfident. In the Duke CFO survey, the correlation between CFOs' forecasts of the S&P index and the true value was slightly less than zero, and the CFOs did not appear to know their forecasts were worthless.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His calm confidence reflects social display and story coherence, not accuracy: subjective confidence is set by the coherence of the story one constructs, not by the quality of the information supporting it. To bring surprises down to the expected 20%, CFOs would need an interval about four times wider, roughly -10% to +30%, which Nathan would never dare state because the wide interval is a socially unacceptable confession of ignorance.","distractors":["Nathan is fabricating his forecasts to impress the board.","Nathan is unusually accurate and the board should trust his range.","Nathan secretly holds better information that he refuses to share."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The premortem: legitimizing doubt against groupthink","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled conference room in a Chicago pharmaceutical company. The leadership team has nearly approved a $2 billion acquisition; the CEO, Dr. Helen Osei, has just tipped her hand in favor of the deal. The finance VP nods energetically, the operations head praises the synergies, and Priya, a junior analyst who earlier voiced concerns, stares at her notes in silence. The room feels unanimous.","player_mission":"Identify the psychological process silencing Priya's doubts, then choose the correct organizational procedure for the final session before sign-off. Answer the observation question and select the best intervention.","key_concepts":["Gary Klein, Kahneman's adversarial collaborator, contributed the premortem procedure to tame overconfidence in organizations.","The premortem premise is a short speech: 'Imagine that we are a year into the future. We implemented the plan as it now exists. The outcome was a disaster. Please take 5 to 10 minutes to write a brief history of that disaster.'","The premortem has two main advantages: it overcomes the groupthink that affects many teams once a decision appears to have been made, and it unleashes the imagination of knowledgeable individuals in a much-needed direction.","As a team converges on a decision, and especially when the leader tips her hand, public doubts about the wisdom of the planned move are gradually suppressed.","Suppressed doubts eventually come to be treated as evidence of flawed loyalty to the team and its leaders, contributing to overconfidence in a group where only supporters of the decision have a voice.","The main virtue of the premortem is that it legitimizes doubts.","The premortem encourages even supporters of the decision to search for possible threats that they had not considered earlier.","The premortem is not a panacea and does not provide complete protection against nasty surprises, but it reduces the damage of plans subject to WYSIATI and uncritical optimism.","After Kahneman described the premortem casually at a session in Davos, a listener who turned out to be the CEO of a major international corporation muttered that it was worth coming to Davos just for this."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the premortem, a procedure that counteracts groupthink and uncritical optimism by legitimizing doubt before a decision is committed. Gary Klein's two-minute disaster-imagination speech overcomes the suppression of public doubt that makes groups more overconfident than their individual members.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The premortem, contributed by Gary Klein, attacks the optimistic bias at the group level: before a decision is formally committed, team members imagine that the plan failed a year later and write a brief history of that disaster, which legitimizes doubt and overcomes groupthink.","stage_1_observation":"In a glass-walled conference room of a Chicago pharma company, the leadership team has nearly approved a $2 billion acquisition. The CEO, Dr. Helen Osei, has just tipped her hand in favor of the deal. The finance VP nods energetically; the operations head praises the synergies; Priya, a junior analyst who earlier voiced concerns, looks down at her notes and says nothing. The room feels unanimous.","stage_1_question":"What psychological process is silencing Priya's doubts, and why does it raise the risk of the decision?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Groupthink: once a team converges and the leader tips her hand, public doubts are gradually suppressed and come to be treated as evidence of flawed loyalty, leaving only supporters of the decision with a voice and increasing overconfidence.","stage_2_intervention":"Helen must decide how to run the final session before sign-off. Her options: ask for a show of hands supporting the deal; schedule a 20-minute session that begins 'Imagine that we are a year into the future. We implemented the plan as it now exists. The outcome was a disaster. Please take 5 to 10 minutes to write a brief history of that disaster'; invite only the CEO's allies; or circulate a risk memo written by the CFO alone. Which action should Helen take?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Run the premortem as described: it overcomes the groupthink that affects teams once a decision appears to have been made, and it unleashes the imagination of knowledgeable individuals, including supporters, to search for threats they had not considered, although it is not a panacea."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Sources of overconfidence: temperament, hubris, and social reward","visual_frame_description":"A business magazine cover lies on a boardroom table next to a live stock ticker. Marcus Okafor, the celebrated CEO on the cover, gestures grandly at an acquisition map while his CFO frowns at a tablet showing the company's share price sliding. Analysts murmur in the background.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column that best explains it, then determine which mechanism accounts for the market downgrading the award-winning CEO's stock after his acquisition announcement. Select a column for every row before answering.","key_concepts":["A survey of founders of small businesses concluded that entrepreneurs are more sanguine than midlevel managers about life in general.","An optimistic attitude is largely inherited and is part of a general disposition for well-being.","The hypothesis that people who have the greatest influence on the lives of others are likely to be optimistic and overconfident, and to take more risks than they realize, is supported by the evidence.","The misguided acquisitions by executives have been explained by a hubris hypothesis: the executives of the acquiring firm are simply less competent than they think they are.","The economists Ulrike Malmendier and Geoffrey Tate identified optimistic CEOs by the amount of company stock they owned personally.","Highly optimistic leaders assumed debt rather than issue equity and were more likely than others to overpay for target companies and undertake value-destroying mergers.","The stock of the acquiring company suffered substantially more in mergers if the CEO was overly optimistic by the authors' measure, so the market can identify overconfident CEOs.","Prestigious press awards to the CEO are costly to stockholders; firms with award-winning CEOs subsequently underperform in terms of both stock and operating performance.","An expert worthy of the name is expected to display high confidence, and the most overconfident experts were the most likely to be invited to appear on news shows."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the hubris hypothesis: the executives of the acquiring firm are simply less competent than they think they are. Malmendier and Tate found that optimistic CEOs, identified by the company stock they personally owned, took greater risks and overpaid for target companies, and the market downgraded their stock accordingly.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A CEO who just won a business-press award announces a large acquisition, and the market immediately downgrades his company's stock. Which mechanism best explains the market's reaction?","column_a_label":"Optimism as inherited temperament","column_b_label":"Hubris hypothesis (overconfident executives)","column_c_label":"Social reward for confidence","rows":[{"label":"A survey of founders of small businesses found entrepreneurs more sanguine than midlevel managers about life in general.","col":"a"},{"label":"Malmendier and Tate found that CEOs who owned large personal stakes in their company overpaid for targets and undertook value-destroying mergers.","col":"b"},{"label":"Prestigious press awards to CEOs are costly to stockholders; award-winning CEOs subsequently underperform.","col":"b"},{"label":"An optimistic attitude is largely inherited and is part of a general disposition for well-being.","col":"a"},{"label":"Experts who acknowledge the full extent of their ignorance may expect to be replaced by more confident competitors who better gain the trust of clients.","col":"c"},{"label":"Clinicians who were 'completely certain' of a diagnosis antemortem were wrong 40% of the time.","col":"c"},{"label":"The stock market is apparently able to identify overconfident CEOs, whose firms' stock suffers more in mergers.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The market reacts because the CEO is known to be risk averse.","Press awards cause executives to become more cautious.","Social reward for confidence lowers CEO compensation."],"explanation":"The market's downgrade reflects the hubris hypothesis: acquiring executives are less competent than they think, and optimistic CEOs identified by personal stock ownership overpay for targets and destroy value. Press awards and social admiration do not cause the loss, they compound it, while temperament is a background disposition rather than the direct cause of the downgrade."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Optimistic persistence: the inventor who ignores a failure forecast","visual_frame_description":"A cluttered Toronto workshop lit by a single bench lamp. Priya Natarajan, a retired electrical engineer, holds a letter with the grade 'D' from the Inventor's Assistance Program while prototype circuit boards surround her. A prototype fan spins beside her notebooks.","player_mission":"Audit whether Priya is deceiving her investors or deceiving herself about the project's prospects. Review her baseline, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis with evidence from the chapter.","key_concepts":["The Canadian Inventor's Assistance Program collects a small fee to provide inventors with an objective assessment of the commercial prospects of their idea.","The evaluations rely on careful ratings of each invention on 37 criteria, including need for the product, cost of production, and estimated trend of demand.","The analysts summarize their ratings by a letter grade, where D and E predict failure, a prediction made for over 70% of the inventions they review.","The forecasts of failure are remarkably accurate: only 5 of 411 projects given the lowest grade reached commercialization, and none was successful.","Discouraging news led about half of the inventors to quit after receiving a grade that unequivocally predicted failure.","47% of the inventors continued development efforts even after being told that their project was hopeless, and on average these persistent individuals doubled their initial losses before giving up.","Persistence after discouraging advice was relatively common among inventors who had a high score on a personality measure of optimism, on which inventors generally scored higher than the general population.","Overall, the return on private invention was small, lower than the return on private equity and on high-risk securities.","The evidence suggests that optimism is widespread, stubborn, and costly."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests optimistic persistence: optimists maintain their effort after discouraging evidence because their optimism is widespread, stubborn, and costly. Ã…stebro's data showed 47% of inventors kept developing after an objective failure forecast and doubled their losses on average, and such persistence was most common among those high in optimism.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Priya Natarajan, an inventor, lying to her investors when she keeps spending money on a project that an objective program has graded D for failure?","subject_baseline":"Priya is a retired electrical engineer in Toronto who keeps meticulous lab notebooks, gives blunt technical feedback, and scores unusually high on a standard optimism questionnaire, higher than the general population, as most inventors do.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-1:00 min: Priya receives the letter-grade evaluation (D, predicted failure) and reads it twice; she pauses, then shrugs and smiles faintly.","1:00-2:30 min: She gestures quickly and speaks rapidly, listing reasons the analysts 'did not understand the real demand,' flipping through her notebook pages.","2:30-3:15 min: She announces she will spend another $40,000 on prototypes; her tone is bright and her posture relaxed, with no acknowledgment of the program's 37 evaluation criteria."],"interrogation_transcript":["Investor: 'The program says fewer than one in eighty of these lowest-grade projects ever reaches commercialization. Why keep going?'","Priya: 'Because my case is different. The criteria miss the niche I know exists.'","Investor: 'What would make you stop?'","Priya: 'I have not seen a reason yet.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Priya is not deceiving her investors; she is deceiving herself through optimism. In Thomas Ã…stebro's study of the Inventor's Assistance Program, 47% of inventors continued development even after receiving a grade that unequivocally predicted failure, and these persistent inventors doubled their initial losses on average before quitting.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Persistence after discouraging advice was common among inventors who scored high on optimism, and the financial returns on private invention were small, lower than returns on private equity and on high-risk securities. Her relaxed confidence is a display of an optimistic explanation style, not evidence that the evaluation is wrong.","distractors":["Priya is deliberately hiding the D grade from her investors.","Priya rationally re-analyzed the data and found the program's forecast inaccurate.","Priya has no emotional stake in the project's outcome."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Competition neglect: opening a blockbuster on a crowded weekend","visual_frame_description":"A dim screening room at a Los Angeles studio. Marketing director Leo Brandt gestures at a screen showing the trailer for 'Iron Skyfall,' then a chart of ad buys and a star's talk-show tour. A junior strategist slides up a chart showing three rival blockbusters opening the same Memorial Day weekend. Leo waves a hand dismissively.","player_mission":"Identify the two System 1 features driving Leo's dismissal of the competition, then choose the intervention that counters competition neglect before the release date is committed. Answer the observation question, then select the best course of action.","key_concepts":["Colin Camerer and Dan Lovallo coined the concept of competition neglect.","Asked why so many expensive big-budget movies are released on the same days, the then chairman of Disney Studios replied 'Hubris. Hubris. If you only think about your own business, you think I've got a good story department, I've got a good marketing department, we're going to go out and do this. And you don't think that everybody else is thinking the same way.'","The chairman's answer displayed no arrogance; the competition was simply not part of the decision, in which a difficult question was replaced by an easier one.","The question that needs an answer is, considering what others will do, how many people will see our film; the simpler question the studio considered is whether it has a good film and a good organization to market it.","The familiar System 1 processes of WYSIATI and substitution produce both competition neglect and the above-average effect.","The consequence of competition neglect is excess entry: more competitors enter the market than the market can profitably sustain, so their average outcome is a loss.","In a given weekend in a year there can be five movies opening, and there is certainly not enough audience to go around.","Giovanni Dosi and Dan Lovallo call entrepreneurial firms that fail but signal new markets to more qualified competitors 'optimistic martyrs', good for the economy but bad for their investors.","Entrepreneurs know less about their competitors and therefore find it natural to imagine a future in which competition plays little part."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests competition neglect, coined by Camerer and Lovallo: planners focus on what they know, their own plans and skills, and leave rivals out of the decision, producing excess entry. The consequence is that more competitors enter than the market can profitably sustain, so the average entrant's outcome is a loss even when failures signal new markets to others.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Competition neglect is the failure to include rivals in the decision picture, produced by WYSIATI and substitution: planners ask whether they have a good product and organization instead of whether the market can sustain everyone's launches.","stage_1_observation":"In a Los Angeles studio screening room, marketing director Leo Brandt presents the launch plan for 'Iron Skyfall' on Memorial Day weekend. He shows the trailer, the ad-buy schedule, and the star's talk-show tour. A junior strategist slides up a chart showing three rival blockbusters opening the same weekend and notes the weekend's total audience is finite. Leo waves a hand: 'Ours is better. That is all that matters.'","stage_1_question":"Which two System 1 features are driving Leo's dismissal, according to the chapter's account of the Disney chairman's answer?","stage_1_correct_answer":"WYSIATI and substitution: the studio answers the easy question 'Do we have a good film and a good organization to market it?' instead of the hard question 'Considering what others will do, how many people will see our film?' The competition simply is not part of the decision.","stage_2_intervention":"The strategist asks Leo to choose the approach for the final go/no-go memo: release on Memorial Day anyway, citing the film's quality; formally estimate the total audience across all five releases and the rivals' marketing firepower before committing the date; tell the rivals to move; or split the marketing budget evenly across the whole summer. Which approach counters competition neglect?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Formally estimate the total audience across all five releases and the rivals' plans: treating competitors as part of the decision avoids the excess entry in which more competitors enter than the market can profitably sustain, so the average entrant loses money."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Emotional, cognitive, and social roots of overconfidence","visual_frame_description":"A bright kitchen in an Italian restaurant under construction. Rosa Bianchi, the new owner, flips through paint swatches and a menu while a real-estate agent waves at the empty dining room. On the counter sits a trade magazine showing that 60% of new restaurants are out of business after three years, face down.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column that names the correct engine of the optimistic bias, then decide which engine is most directly at work in Rosa's decision to open without researching the base rate. Select a column for every row before answering.","key_concepts":["The planning fallacy is only one of the manifestations of a pervasive optimistic bias.","Most of us view the world as more benign than it really is, our own attributes as more favorable than they truly are, and the goals we adopt as more achievable than they are likely to be.","We focus on our goal, anchor on our plan, and neglect relevant base rates, exposing ourselves to the planning fallacy.","The chances that a small business will survive for five years in the United States are about 35%.","A survey found that American entrepreneurs' average estimate of the chances of success for any business like their own was 60%, almost double the true value.","Fully 81% of the entrepreneurs put their personal odds of success at 7 out of 10 or higher, and 33% said their chance of failing was zero.","60% of new restaurants are out of business after three years.","People who exaggerate their expected life span beyond actuarial predictions work longer hours, are more optimistic about their future income, and are more prone to bet on individual stocks.","A wide confidence interval is a confession of ignorance, which is not socially acceptable for someone who is paid to be knowledgeable, so the answer that a truthful CFO would offer is plainly ridiculous."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the three engines of the optimistic bias that Kahneman distinguishes: emotional optimism (temperament), cognitive bias (WYSIATI, planning fallacy, base-rate neglect), and social pressure favoring confident experts. Base-rate neglect arises because System 1 anchors on the plan and cannot allow for information that never comes to mind.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An entrepreneur opens an Italian restaurant without researching that 60% of new restaurants close within three years. Which engine of optimistic bias is most directly at work?","column_a_label":"Emotional optimism (temperament)","column_b_label":"Cognitive bias (WYSIATI and the planning fallacy)","column_c_label":"Social pressure for confidence","rows":[{"label":"We focus on our goal, anchor on our plan, and neglect relevant base rates, exposing ourselves to the planning fallacy.","col":"b"},{"label":"Most of us view the world as more benign than it is and our goals as more achievable than they are likely to be.","col":"a"},{"label":"A CFO who admitted the S&P return might fall between -10% and +30% would be laughed out of the room, because the wide interval is a confession of ignorance.","col":"c"},{"label":"People who exaggerate their expected life span work longer hours and are more prone to bet on individual stocks.","col":"a"},{"label":"Subjective confidence is determined by the coherence of the story one has constructed, not by the quality and amount of information that supports it.","col":"b"},{"label":"An expert worthy of the name is expected to display high confidence, and the most overconfident experts are the most likely to be invited on news shows.","col":"c"},{"label":"The optimist's explanation style takes credit for successes but little blame for failures, defending the self-image.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The restaurant owner is defying social norms that favor caution.","Base-rate neglect requires formal statistical training to overcome.","Emotional optimism has no measurable effects on lifespan."],"explanation":"Ignoring the base rate of restaurant failure is the cognitive engine: WYSIATI makes the entrepreneur anchor on her plan and neglect the 60% three-year failure rate. Emotional optimism shapes her overall outlook and social pressure discourages her from admitting ignorance, but the specific failure to weigh the known base rate is the cognitive planning-fallacy bias."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Overconfident forecasting: widening the CFO's 80% interval","visual_frame_description":"In the monthly risk committee of a pension fund, CFO Renata Silva stands beside a projector screen showing her 80% confidence interval for next year's S&P 500 return: +5% to +13%. A junior risk analyst, TomÃ¡s, points to a chart of the last four years in which her realized returns fell outside her stated intervals in about 65% of quarters. Renata gazes at the screen, arms crossed.","player_mission":"Explain why Renata keeps issuing intervals that are far too narrow, then select the interval that honestly reflects her knowledge. Answer the observation question, then choose the best course of action for next quarter's report.","key_concepts":["Professors at Duke University conducted a survey in which the chief financial officers of large corporations estimated the returns of the Standard & Poor's index over the following year, collecting 11,600 forecasts.","The correlation between the CFOs' estimates and the true value was slightly less than zero; financial officers of large corporations had no clue about the short-term future of the stock market.","The truly bad news is that the CFOs did not appear to know that their forecasts were worthless.","An individual who sets confidence intervals on multiple occasions expects about 20% of the outcomes to be surprises, but the incidence in the Duke study was 67%, more than three times higher than expected.","To maintain the rate of surprises at the desired level, the CFOs should have said year after year that there is an 80% chance the S&P return next year will be between -10% and +30%.","The confidence interval that properly reflects the CFOs' knowledge is more than four times wider than the intervals they actually stated.","Overconfidence is a direct consequence of features of System 1 that can be tamed, but not vanquished; the main obstacle is that subjective confidence is determined by the coherence of the story one has constructed.","The wide confidence interval is a confession of ignorance, which is not socially acceptable for someone who is paid to be knowledgeable in financial matters.","The most confident and optimistic CFOs about the S&P index were also overconfident and optimistic about the prospects of their own firm, which went on to take more risk than others."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests overconfidence in interval estimation: people rely on the information that comes to mind and construct a coherent story, so stated confidence intervals are far too narrow. The Duke CFO survey found surprises occurred 67% of the time instead of the expected 20%, and an honest interval for the S&P return would be about -10% to +30%, more than four times wider than the CFOs' stated ranges.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Overconfidence in judgment tasks such as forecasting: people set tight confidence intervals because subjective confidence is driven by the coherence of the story they construct, and the honest interval is far wider than the stated one.","stage_1_observation":"In the monthly risk committee of a pension fund, CFO Renata Silva presents her 80% confidence interval for next year's S&P 500 return: +5% to +13%. A junior risk analyst, TomÃ¡s, pulls up the last four years of her intervals on a projector: her realized returns fell outside her stated intervals in about 65% of quarters. Renata looks at the screen, then says, 'The model has improved since then.'","stage_1_question":"Why does Renata keep issuing intervals that are far too narrow even after repeated misses, and what mechanism underlies this?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Overconfidence is a direct consequence of System 1 features that can be tamed but not vanquished: subjective confidence is determined by the coherence of the story one has constructed, not by the quality and amount of information that supports it. Allowing for the information that does not come to mind is impossible.","stage_2_intervention":"The committee asks Renata to propose next quarter's interval. Her options: keep the tight +5% to +13% range to preserve credibility; present the interval that would actually reduce surprises to 20%, roughly -10% to +30%, more than four times wider; refuse to give any interval; or quietly widen the range by one point. Which choice best reflects an unbiased appreciation of uncertainty?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Present the roughly -10% to +30% interval: the Duke CFO study showed the incidence of surprises would fall from 67% to the expected 20% only with an interval about four times wider than those stated, because that is what honestly reflects the CFOs' knowledge, their ignorance. The truthful answer will be socially penalized, but it is the rational one."}]},{"id":"ch30","title":"25. Bernoulli's Errors","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Econs and Humans: the two agents of choice","visual_frame_description":"A university seminar room in Jerusalem in the early 1970s. Two professors face each other across a table covered in mimeographed papers, one with a dark red cover. Kahneman holds up the red essay while Tversky sketches a decision tree on a blackboard. A single student watches from the back.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column that correctly names the agent or law it describes, then determine which label fits the economics lecturer's claim about the rational agent. Select a column for every row before answering.","key_concepts":["Bruno Frey's essay began with the sentence: 'The agent of economic theory is rational, selfish, and his tastes do not change.'","To a psychologist it is self-evident that people are neither fully rational nor completely selfish, and that their tastes are anything but stable.","The behavioral economist Richard Thaler later dubbed the two kinds of agents Econs and Humans.","Unlike Econs, the Humans that psychologists know have a System 1, and their view of the world is limited by the information available at a given moment (WYSIATI).","Because of WYSIATI, Humans cannot be as consistent and logical as Econs.","Humans are sometimes generous and often willing to contribute to the group to which they are attached.","Humans often have little idea of what they will like next year or even tomorrow.","Gustav Fechner founded and named the field of psychophysics, which relates the subjective experience in the observer's mind to the objective quantity in the material world.","Fechner proposed that for many dimensions the psychophysical function is logarithmic: an increase of stimulus intensity by a given factor always yields the same increment on the psychological scale."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the Econs-versus-Humans distinction drawn from Bruno Frey's essay and named by Richard Thaler: the rational, selfish, stable-taste agent of economics versus the Humans psychologists know, who have a System 1, see the world through WYSIATI, are sometimes generous, and have unstable tastes.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An economics lecturer claims that the agent of economic theory is 'rational, selfish, and his tastes do not change.' Which label does this describe?","column_a_label":"Econs (rational-agent assumption)","column_b_label":"Humans (System 1, WYSIATI)","column_c_label":"Psychophysics (Fechner's logarithmic law)","rows":[{"label":"The agent of economic theory is rational, selfish, and his tastes do not change.","col":"a"},{"label":"Humans cannot be as consistent and logical as Econs because their view of the world is limited by the information available at a given moment (WYSIATI).","col":"b"},{"label":"An increase of stimulus intensity by a given factor always yields the same increment on the psychological scale.","col":"c"},{"label":"Humans are sometimes generous and often willing to contribute to the group to which they are attached.","col":"b"},{"label":"Humans often have little idea of what they will like next year or even tomorrow.","col":"b"},{"label":"The psychological response to a change of wealth is inversely proportional to the initial amount of wealth.","col":"c"},{"label":"Expected utility theory serves as a logic that prescribes how decisions should be made and as a description of how Econs choose.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Humans are always irrational and never generous.","Econs possess a System 1.","Fechner founded prospect theory."],"explanation":"The lecturer is describing the Econs of economic theory: rational, selfish, and stable in taste. Humans differ because they have a System 1 and see the world through WYSIATI, while psychophysics, Fechner's logarithmic law, describes how subjective response scales with physical stimulus rather than the rationality of the chooser."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reference points: the $20,000 bonus that stopped feeling large","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled office at a Boston software firm. Malik Reed, a senior engineer in a checked shirt, sits across from his manager as she slides an envelope with a bonus letter across the desk. A window behind them shows the city skyline at evening.","player_mission":"Audit whether Malik is lying about his lack of enthusiasm or genuinely reporting a change in how the bonus feels. Review his baseline, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["In Bernoulli's account, the gift of 10 ducats to someone with 100 ducats has the same utility as a gift of 20 ducats to someone with 200 ducats.","We normally speak of changes of income in terms of percentages, as when we say 'she got a 30% raise.'","A 30% raise may evoke a fairly similar psychological response for the rich and for the poor, which an increase of $100 will not do.","As in Fechner's law, the psychological response to a change of wealth is inversely proportional to the initial amount of wealth, leading to the conclusion that utility is a logarithmic function of wealth.","The happiness people experience is determined by the recent change in their wealth relative to the states of wealth that define their reference points.","Reference dependence is ubiquitous in sensation and perception: the same sound is experienced as very loud or quite faint depending on whether it was preceded by a whisper or a roar.","To predict the utility of an amount of wealth, you need to know the reference to which it is compared.","The book's example notes that a person was very happy with a $20,000 bonus three years ago, but his salary has gone up by 20% since, so he will need a higher bonus to get the same utility."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference dependence: the utility of a given amount of money depends on the reference point from which it is judged, not on the absolute amount. Kahneman's example, a person very happy with a $20,000 bonus whose salary has since risen 20% needs a higher bonus for the same utility, is exactly Malik's case.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Malik Reed, a senior engineer, lying when he says his $20,000 bonus no longer feels big, even though the same amount once made him 'very happy' three years ago?","subject_baseline":"Malik is a steady, honest engineer at a Boston software firm who keeps a gratitude journal, gives frank performance feedback, and has never complained about pay before this conversation.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-1:00 min: When told his bonus is $20,000, Malik nods slowly with no smile and glances at his phone; his tone is flat.","1:00-2:00 min: He recalls, 'Three years ago this felt enormous,' with a slight shrug and a long pause.","2:00-2:40 min: Asked whether he is satisfied, he says 'It is fine,' then adds that his salary is up 20% since then and the same number 'buys less in context.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Manager: 'You used to be thrilled with exactly this amount.'","Malik: 'And I would be thrilled again if my situation had not changed. But my reference point moved.'","Manager: 'So is it a good bonus or not?'","Malik: 'Objectively fine. Subjectively, it is not what it once was.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Malik is not lying; his satisfaction is reference-dependent. The book notes that a person who was 'very happy with a $20,000 bonus three years ago' will need a higher bonus once his salary has risen by 20%, because the psychological value of money is relative to a reference point, not absolute.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His flat affect matches a genuine change in utility: as in Fechner's law and Bernoulli's logarithmic function, the same absolute amount of money evokes a smaller subjective response at a higher baseline. There is no evidence of ingratitude, negotiation strategy, or misremembering, only a shifted reference point.","distractors":["Malik is ungrateful and entitled.","Malik is feigning disappointment to force a bigger raise.","Malik misremembers how happy he was three years ago."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Bernoulli's expected utility: pricing the infinite gamble and the spice shipment","visual_frame_description":"A decision-theory seminar at a London university. Professor Ada Mensah stands at a chalkboard covered in coin-toss payout trees and utility tables. A student near the window has just shouted, 'But the expected value is infinite!' Ada smiles and gestures toward the merchant's ledger on her desk.","player_mission":"Explain why people pay only a few pounds for a gamble with infinite expected value, then select the correct principle for pricing the merchant's insurance. Answer the observation question, then choose the best course of action.","key_concepts":["Simple gambles such as '40% chance to win $300' are to students of decision making what the fruit fly is to geneticists.","Expected value is a weighted average of the possible outcomes, where each outcome is weighted by its probability, as in the $82 expected value of an 80% chance to win $100 and a 20% chance to win $10.","Bernoulli proposed that people's choices are based not on dollar values but on the psychological values of outcomes, their utilities.","Bernoulli drew on the diminishing marginal value of wealth to explain risk aversion, the common preference for a sure thing over a favorable gamble of equal or slightly higher expected value.","The utility of a gamble is not the weighted average of its dollar outcomes but the average of the utilities of those outcomes, each weighted by its probability.","Bernoulli offered a solution to the famous St. Petersburg paradox, in which people offered a gamble that has infinite expected value are willing to spend only a few ducats for it.","Bernoulli computed how much a merchant in St. Petersburg would be willing to pay to insure a shipment of spice from Amsterdam when 5 of the 100 ships that sail are usually lost.","The utility function explains why poor people buy insurance and why richer people sell it to them.","The loss of 1 million causes a loss of 4 points of utility to someone who has 10 million and a much larger loss of 18 points to someone who starts off with 3 million."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests Bernoulli's expected-utility framework: diminishing marginal utility of wealth makes people risk averse, resolving the St. Petersburg paradox (a gamble with infinite expected value is priced at a few ducats) and explaining insurance. Bernoulli computed how much a St. Petersburg merchant would pay to insure a spice shipment when 5 of 100 ships are usually lost.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Bernoulli's expected utility (his 'moral expectation'), built on diminishing marginal utility of wealth, explains why people prefer sure things to favorable gambles, why the St. Petersburg gamble with infinite expected value is worth only a few ducats, and why poor people buy insurance from richer people.","stage_1_observation":"In a decision-theory seminar at a London university, Professor Ada Mensah presents the St. Petersburg game: a fair coin is tossed until it comes up tails, and the payout doubles with every heads (2, 4, 8, 16...), giving the gamble infinite expected value. She asks the class how much they would pay to play. The highest bid among the students is Â£12. One student shouts, 'But the expected value is infinite!'","stage_1_question":"Why would a person willingly pay only a few pounds for a gamble whose expected value is infinite?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because people evaluate gambles by expected utility, not expected value: Bernoulli's logarithmic utility of wealth assigns finite utility to the infinite gamble, which resolves the St. Petersburg paradox and explains why people are willing to spend only a few ducats on it.","stage_2_intervention":"Ada now presents a merchant's problem: a spice shipment worth 4 million ducats sailing from Amsterdam to St. Petersburg, where 5 of 100 ships are usually lost. She asks the class to decide how insurance should be priced. The options: charge the premium that equals the expected loss; set the premium by comparing utility losses, so the poorer merchant pays a premium to the richer insurer to transfer the risk; refuse to insure because the risk is too small; or charge both parties an identical flat premium regardless of wealth. Which pricing is grounded in Bernoulli's framework?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Set the premium by comparing utility losses: the loss of 4 utility points to the man with 10 million versus 18 points to the man with 3 million means the poorer merchant will happily pay a premium to transfer risk, which is why poor people buy insurance and richer people sell it to them."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reference points: why identical wealth brings different happiness","visual_frame_description":"Two portraits side by side in a clean editorial layout: Jack, a smiling man in a bright shirt with 'today $5M, yesterday $1M' beneath him, and Jill, a downcast woman in muted clothes with 'today $5M, yesterday $9M.' A financial adviser in the foreground points at a balance sheet that lists the same current wealth for both.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column that names the framework it supports, then determine which framework the adviser's claim relies on. Select a column for every row before answering.","key_concepts":["Jack and Jill each have a wealth of 5 million today; yesterday Jack had 1 million and Jill had 9 million.","Bernoulli's theory assumes that the utility of wealth is what makes people more or less happy, so with equal current wealth it asserts Jack and Jill should be equally happy.","In fact today Jack is elated and Jill despondent; Jack would be a great deal happier than Jill even if he had only 2 million today while she has 5.","The happiness that Jack and Jill experience is determined by the recent change in their wealth relative to the different states of wealth that define their reference points.","Reference dependence is ubiquitous in sensation and perception: the same sound is experienced as loud or faint depending on whether it was preceded by a whisper or by a roar.","A gray patch on a page will appear dark or light depending on the background to which it is compared.","You need to know the reference before you can predict the utility of an amount of wealth.","The error survived for so long because of theory-induced blindness: once you accept a theory and use it as a tool in your thinking, it is extraordinarily difficult to notice its flaws.","Many scholars thought of stories such as those of Anthony and Betty and casually noted that they did not jibe with utility theory, but they did not pursue the idea to the point of saying the theory is seriously wrong."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference dependence: utility depends on changes relative to a reference point, not on absolute wealth, so Jack and Jill with equal current wealth feel very different. Kahneman argues Bernoulli's model is seriously wrong because it ignores the fact that utility depends on the history of one's wealth, and the error persisted through theory-induced blindness.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Jack and Jill each hold $5 million today; yesterday Jack had $1 million and Jill had $9 million. A financial adviser insists they must be equally happy because their wealth is equal. Which framework does the adviser's claim rely on?","column_a_label":"State-of-wealth view (Bernoulli)","column_b_label":"Reference-dependence view (prospect theory)","column_c_label":"Theory-induced blindness (why the error survived)","rows":[{"label":"Bernoulli's theory asserts that Jack and Jill, with the same current wealth, should have the same utility and be equally happy.","col":"a"},{"label":"Jack is elated and Jill despondent because their happiness is determined by recent change relative to their reference points of $1 million and $9 million.","col":"b"},{"label":"The same sound is experienced as loud or faint depending on whether it was preceded by a whisper or a roar.","col":"b"},{"label":"Once you accept a theory and use it as a tool in your thinking, it is extraordinarily difficult to notice its flaws.","col":"c"},{"label":"Expected utility theory does not represent the obvious fact that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty.","col":"a"},{"label":"Scholars who noticed that such stories did not jibe with utility theory assumed there must be a perfectly good explanation they were somehow missing.","col":"c"},{"label":"You need to know the reference before you can predict the utility of an amount of wealth.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Reference dependence applies only to money, not to perception.","Theory-induced blindness is a deliberate, conscious choice to ignore evidence.","Jack's elation is caused by today's wealth alone."],"explanation":"The adviser is applying Bernoulli's state-of-wealth view, which judges utility from current wealth alone and therefore predicts equal happiness for equal wealth. The chapter rejects this view: reference dependence in perception and value means happiness tracks changes relative to each person's reference point, and the error survived so long because of theory-induced blindness."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk attitudes from reference points: why she settles and he goes to court","visual_frame_description":"A neutral mediation room in a suburban family-court annex. Elena, a pediatrician in a cardigan, sits beside a pad of settlement notes and nods at the mediator. Across the table, Tom, a restaurant owner, sits with arms crossed while his lawyer whispers to him. A court calendar hangs on the wall.","player_mission":"Audit whether either spouse is lying about wanting to settle or go to trial. Review the baselines, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis about their risk attitudes.","key_concepts":["The book's alimony example explains: 'She can only gain, so she's risk averse. He, on the other hand, faces options that are all bad, so he'd rather take the risk.'","Bernoulli's model could explain Anthony's risk aversion, but it cannot explain Betty's risk-seeking preference for the gamble.","Risk-seeking behavior in the face of bad options is often observed in entrepreneurs and in generals when all their options are bad.","Because Bernoulli's model lacks the idea of a reference point, expected utility theory does not represent the obvious fact that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty.","The psychological outcomes that Anthony and Betty assess are entirely different, although the possible states of wealth they face are the same.","Anthony, who owns 1 million, thinks of the sure thing as doubling his wealth; Betty, who owns 4 million, thinks of it as losing half of hers.","Betty is much more likely to take her chances, as others do when faced with very bad options.","The happiness people experience is determined by the recent change in their wealth relative to the states that define their reference points."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference-dependent risk attitudes: the same objective options are evaluated as gains or losses relative to each party's reference point. Kahneman's alimony example shows the spouse who can only gain is risk averse while the spouse facing all-bad options prefers the gamble, a pattern Bernoulli's model cannot explain.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In a divorce mediation, Elena wants to settle while her ex-husband Tom insists on going to court. Is one of them lying about their preferences?","subject_baseline":"Elena is a cautious, detail-oriented pediatrician who has never litigated anything; Tom is a proud restaurant owner who hates losing negotiations and keeps his own books.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-1:00 min: Elena reads the proposed settlement, exhales slowly, and nods twice at the mediator; her shoulders visibly drop as she relaxes.","1:00-2:30 min: Tom crosses his arms, shakes his head, and taps the table; when his lawyer whispers about trial costs, his jaw tightens but he keeps shaking his head.","2:30-3:30 min: The mediator notes that a trial could cost more than the settlement; Tom looks at Elena, then says, 'I will take my chances,' and leans back with a fixed stare."],"interrogation_transcript":["Mediator: 'Tom, the settlement guarantees you something. Why go to court?'","Tom: 'Because this settlement gives me nothing either way. In court, at least I have a shot.'","Mediator: 'You understand a trial is risky for you?'","Tom: 'So is settling. Every option I have right now is bad.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Neither party is lying; their risk attitudes follow from their reference points. Elena can only gain from a settlement, so she is risk averse; Tom faces options that are all bad, so he prefers the gamble, exactly the book's example: 'She can only gain, so she's risk averse. He, on the other hand, faces options that are all bad, so he'd rather take the risk.'","diagnostic_breakdown":"Bernoulli's state-of-wealth model cannot explain Tom's risk seeking, but reference dependence can: outcomes are coded as gains or losses from each party's reference point, and risk seeking appears when all options look like losses. Tom's tense body language shows genuine discomfort, not deception; his stated preference matches the pattern observed in entrepreneurs and generals with only bad options.","distractors":["Tom is hiding assets and wants court to delay discovery.","Elena is secretly wealthy and bluffing about wanting to settle.","The mediator is biased toward one spouse."]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Expected value, expected utility, and reference-dependent choice","visual_frame_description":"A tidy diagram on a screen shows three columns of math: a coin flip with dollar payoffs, a logarithmic curve labeled utility of wealth, and two figures labeled Anthony and Betty standing at different starting points on a wealth axis. A merchant's ship and a ledger sit in the corner of the frame.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column that names the framework it belongs to, then determine which framework best explains the merchant buying insurance at a premium. Select a column for every row before answering.","key_concepts":["Prior to Bernoulli, mathematicians assumed gambles are assessed by their expected value: a weighted average of outcomes, as in the $82 expected value of an 80% chance to win $100 and a 20% chance to win $10.","Bernoulli pointed out that people do not in fact evaluate gambles by expected value, since almost everyone prefers a sure $80 to a gamble with an expected value of $82.","Bernoulli observed that most people dislike risk and will choose a sure thing that is less than expected value, in effect paying a premium to avoid the uncertainty.","Adding 1 million to a wealth of 1 million yields 20 utility points, but adding 1 million to a wealth of 9 million adds only 4 points.","The utility of a gamble is the average of the utilities of its outcomes, each weighted by its probability; the utility of the 50/50 gamble for 1 or 4 million is 47, less than the utility of 60 for owning 4 million for sure.","Bernoulli's utility function explains why poor people buy insurance and why richer people sell it to them.","Anthony, who owns 1 million, thinks of the sure 2 million as doubling his wealth; Betty, who owns 4 million, thinks of it as losing half of hers.","Betty's risk-seeking preference for the gamble is often observed in entrepreneurs and in generals when all their options are bad.","Because Bernoulli's model lacks the idea of a reference point, it does not represent the fact that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the difference between expected value, expected utility, and reference-dependent choice. Bernoulli's logarithmic utility of wealth explains risk aversion and insurance (poor people buy it, richer people sell it), while the reference point absent from his model is what prospect theory adds to explain choices like Betty's.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A merchant in St. Petersburg happily pays an insurance premium to protect a spice shipment. Which framework best explains this willingness to pay more than the expected loss?","column_a_label":"Expected value (weighted average of dollars)","column_b_label":"Expected utility (Bernoulli's diminishing marginal utility)","column_c_label":"Reference-dependent choice (prospect theory)","rows":[{"label":"Mathematicians before Bernoulli assessed gambles by expected value: 0.8 x $100 + 0.2 x $10 = $82.","col":"a"},{"label":"Adding 1 million to a wealth of 1 million yields 20 utility points, but adding 1 million to a wealth of 9 million adds only 4.","col":"b"},{"label":"The utility of the 50/50 gamble for 1 or 4 million is 47, which is less than the utility 60 of owning 4 million for sure.","col":"b"},{"label":"Anthony codes the sure $2 million as doubling his wealth; Betty codes it as losing half of hers.","col":"c"},{"label":"A risk-averse decision maker will choose a sure thing that is less than the expected value, paying a premium to avoid uncertainty.","col":"b"},{"label":"Betty prefers the gamble because the sure option is a loss from her reference point.","col":"c"},{"label":"The St. Petersburg gamble has infinite expected value yet people spend only a few ducats for it.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Insurance pricing is explained by expected value, because the premium equals the expected loss.","Prospect theory predicts that merchants refuse to buy insurance.","The St. Petersburg paradox was never resolved by any theory."],"explanation":"The merchant's premium is explained by expected utility: because utility is a logarithmic function of wealth, the utility loss from a wreck is far larger for the poorer man, so he pays a premium to transfer the risk, which is what insurance is about. Expected value cannot explain a premium above expected loss, and reference dependence concerns gains and losses around a reference point rather than the wealth-based utility that grounds insurance."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk seeking in the loss domain: the manager who prefers the gamble","visual_frame_description":"A regional manager's office at a retail chain. Dana Kowalski sits opposite a CFO who has placed two documents on the desk: one labeled 'Guaranteed 15% cut' and one labeled 'Restructuring gamble: 10% or 30%.' Dana leans forward, tapping a pen, with a store-sales chart visible on the wall behind her.","player_mission":"Audit whether Dana is being honest when she says she prefers the gamble to the guaranteed cut. Review her baseline, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis about her risk attitude.","key_concepts":["Betty, who currently owns 4 million, sees the sure thing of owning 2 million as a certain loss of half her wealth, which is awful.","Betty is much more likely to take her chances with a gamble, as others do when faced with very bad options.","The behavior of preferring a gamble when all options are bad is often observed in entrepreneurs and in generals when all their options are bad.","Bernoulli's model could explain Anthony's risk aversion, but it cannot explain Betty's risk-seeking preference for the gamble.","Because Bernoulli's model lacks the idea of a reference point, it does not represent that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty.","The psychological outcomes that Anthony and Betty assess are entirely different, although the possible states of wealth they face are the same.","Anthony thinks of the sure thing as a gain (doubling his wealth) while Betty thinks of it as a loss (halving hers), so they choose differently.","The happiness people experience is determined by the recent change in their wealth relative to the states of wealth that define their reference points."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests risk seeking in the loss domain: when all options are bad, people choose the gamble over a certain loss. Kahneman notes this behavior is often observed in entrepreneurs and generals, and it is exactly what Bernoulli's state-of-wealth model cannot explain but reference dependence can.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dana Kowalski, a regional retail manager, must accept either a guaranteed 15% budget cut or gamble on a restructuring that could cut her budget by 10% or by 30%. Is she being dishonest when she says she prefers the gamble?","subject_baseline":"Dana is a conservative, detail-driven manager who has turned down risky store expansions for years and double-checks every budget line before signing it.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-1:00 min: When the CFO slides over the guaranteed 15% cut, Dana frowns, pushes the paper slightly away, and shakes her head slowly.","1:00-2:30 min: When the CFO describes the restructuring gamble (10% or 30%), Dana leans forward, asks three sharp questions about the upside, and taps her pen rhythmically.","2:30-3:15 min: She says 'I will take the chance' quickly, with her jaw set and eyes fixed on the CFO, then relaxes into her chair."],"interrogation_transcript":["CFO: 'The sure path is a 15% cut. The gamble could cost you 10% or 30%.'","Dana: 'I will take the gamble. The sure cut is a loss either way.'","CFO: 'A 30% cut could close one of your stores.'","Dana: 'And the 15% cut could close two. From where I sit, every option is a loss.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Dana is being honest: she is risk seeking in the loss domain because all her options are bad relative to her reference point. The book's Betty prefers the gamble over a sure loss of half her wealth, and this risk-seeking behavior is often observed in entrepreneurs and in generals when all their options are bad.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Bernoulli's model cannot explain Dana's choice, but reference dependence can: the sure 15% cut is coded as a certain loss, while the gamble offers a chance of a smaller loss, so it feels less bad. Her forward lean and quick decision indicate genuine engagement with the gamble, not concealment of information.","distractors":["Dana is trying to hide a worse problem in her own division.","Dana has secret information the CFO lacks.","Dana has a gambling problem unrelated to the budget."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reframing the buyout: Anthony and Betty","visual_frame_description":"A mediation suite overlooking a city. Two founders, Anthony and Betty, face a mediator across a table covered with offer sheets. Both sheets list the same pair of options: a sure $2 million or a 50/50 gamble ending at $1 million or $4 million. Anthony scribbles 'doubles everything' while Betty has written 'cuts my position in half.'","player_mission":"Explain why Anthony and Betty, facing identical final wealth states, want different options, then choose the correct framing for the closing memo. Answer the observation question, then select the best course of action.","key_concepts":["Anthony's current wealth is 1 million and Betty's is 4 million; both are offered equal chances to end up owning 1 million or 4 million, or the sure thing of owning 2 million.","In Bernoulli's account, Anthony and Betty face the same choice because their expected wealth will be 2.5 million with the gamble and 2 million with the sure thing, so he would expect the same choice.","The theory fails because it does not allow for the different reference points from which Anthony and Betty consider their options.","Anthony thinks: 'If I choose the sure thing, my wealth will double with certainty, which is very attractive; alternatively I can take a gamble with equal chances to quadruple my wealth or to gain nothing.'","Betty thinks: 'If I choose the sure thing, I lose half of my wealth with certainty, which is awful; alternatively I can take a gamble with equal chances to lose three-quarters of my wealth or to lose nothing.'","Neither Anthony nor Betty thinks in terms of states of wealth: Anthony thinks of gains and Betty thinks of losses, so the psychological outcomes they assess are entirely different.","Because Bernoulli's model lacks the idea of a reference point, expected utility theory does not represent the obvious fact that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty.","His model could explain Anthony's risk aversion, but it cannot explain Betty's risk-seeking preference for the gamble."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference dependence in choice: Anthony evaluates the sure option as a gain and Betty as a loss, so they choose differently despite identical final wealth states. Kahneman argues Bernoulli's expected utility theory does not represent the obvious fact that the outcome that is good for Anthony is bad for Betty.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Reference dependence in negotiation: the same objective outcomes are gains for one party and losses for another, because each evaluates options from a different reference point.","stage_1_observation":"Two founders, Anthony and Betty, meet with a mediator to divide a buyout offer. Both are offered the same pair of options: a sure $2 million in cash, or a gamble that ends at $1 million or $4 million with equal chances. Anthony, who currently has about $1 million in personal assets, keeps saying the sure thing 'doubles everything for me.' Betty, with about $4 million in assets, mutters that the sure thing 'cuts my position in half.' Both turn to the mediator, confused that they cannot agree.","stage_1_question":"Why do Anthony and Betty, facing identical final wealth states, want different options?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Their reference points differ: the sure $2 million doubles Anthony's wealth (a gain) but halves Betty's (a loss), so Anthony is risk averse and Betty is risk seeking. The psychological outcomes they assess are entirely different even though the possible states of wealth are the same.","stage_2_intervention":"The mediator must write the closing memo framing the offer. Options: present both options purely as final wealth amounts ($1M, $2M, or $4M); describe each option relative to each party's current wealth and acknowledge that the outcome 'good for Anthony is bad for Betty'; advise both to take the sure thing for safety; or warn both that they are being irrational. Which framing is most accurate?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Describe each option relative to each party's reference point and acknowledge that the outcome good for Anthony is bad for Betty: Bernoulli's model fails because it lacks a reference point, and reference dependence explains why risk aversion in gains and risk seeking in losses coexist in the same negotiation."}]},{"id":"ch31","title":"26. Prospect Theory","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reference Point and the Counterexample to Bernoulli","visual_frame_description":"Sofia, a junior analyst in a glass-walled finance office, stares at two side-by-side decision screens. On the left, the problem gives her $1,000 and asks whether she prefers a 50% chance to win $1,000 or $500 for sure. On the right, the problem gives her $2,000 and asks whether she prefers a 50% chance to lose $1,000 or losing $500 for sure. A subtle overlay highlights that both screens reduce to the same final wealth, while a small panel tracks her eye movements and reaction times.","player_mission":"Classify the evidence rows into the column that best explains why Sofia reverses her choice between the two problems. Your goal is to identify the single prospect-theory mechanism that accounts for the reversal, using each evidence item to confirm it.","key_concepts":["Prospect theory defines outcomes as gains and losses relative to a reference point, not as states of wealth.","Bernoulli's expected utility theory treats only final wealth, which is why it predicted identical choices in problems 3 and 4.","The reference point is the earlier state relative to which gains and losses are evaluated.","Gifts and bonuses already included in the reference point are generally ignored when people choose.","Identical final outcomes produce risk-averse choices when framed as gains and risk-seeking choices when framed as losses.","The reference point can be the status quo, an expected outcome, or an outcome to which the person feels entitled.","Kahneman and Tversky called the reference point the moving part missing from Bernoulli's model.","Theory-induced blindness kept scholars from noticing the missing reference point for more than 250 years.","The demonstration uses an adaptation level: the same stimulus can be a gain or a loss depending on context.","The rejection of a sure loss in favor of a gamble is driven by diminishing sensitivity in the loss domain."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference-point dependence: outcomes are evaluated as gains and losses relative to a reference point, and identical final states of wealth produce opposite choices when the reference point shifts. Kahneman and Tversky's problems 3 and 4 showed that a decisive counterexample to Bernoulli's theory arises purely from reframing the same outcomes relative to a different reference state.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sofia is given $2,000 before choosing between a 50% chance to lose $1,000 and a sure loss of $500; given $1,000 instead, she faces a 50% chance to win $1,000 versus $500 for sure and picks the sure thing. The two problems produce identical final states of wealth. Which mechanism in prospect theory explains why she reverses her choice?","column_a_label":"Evaluation relative to a reference point (gains and losses, not states of wealth)","column_b_label":"Loss aversion in mixed gambles (losses loom about twice as large as gains)","column_c_label":"Diminishing sensitivity (the S-shaped value function)","rows":[{"label":"With the $2,000 head start, the certain $1,500 outcome is experienced as a $500 loss, which makes the gamble look better.","col":"a"},{"label":"With the $1,000 head start, the same certain $1,500 outcome is experienced as a $500 gain, which makes the sure thing look better.","col":"a"},{"label":"The $1,000 or $2,000 gift is absorbed into the reference point, so Sofia barely notices it when she chooses.","col":"a"},{"label":"Bernoulli's utility theory predicted identical choices because only final states of wealth mattered, yet large majorities reversed.","col":"a"},{"label":"The reference point is the earlier state relative to which every option is coded as a gain or a loss.","col":"a"},{"label":"When the same pair of options is framed once as gains and once as losses, risk attitude flips from risk aversion to risk seeking.","col":"a"},{"label":"Kahneman and Tversky found this reversal to be a decisive counterexample to expected utility theory.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Loss aversion in mixed gambles: rejecting 50/50 bets with a positive expected value because the possible loss feels twice as large as the gain.","Diminishing sensitivity: the subjective difference between $1,000 and $2,000 is smaller than between $100 and $200.","Adaptation level: evaluations are relative to a neutral reference point, such as the temperature of three bowls of water."],"explanation":"All seven evidence items point to the reference point, the missing moving part in Bernoulli's model. In problems 3 and 4 the final states of wealth are identical, so utility theory demanded the same preference in both. The reversal happened because the $2,000 version coded the certain option as a $500 loss (aversion to a sure loss pushes people to gamble) while the $1,000 version coded it as a $500 gain (people prefer the sure gain). The gift itself vanishes into the reference point and is ignored. This is why prospect theory evaluates gains and losses relative to a reference state rather than evaluating states of wealth."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk Aversion, Risk Seeking, and Mixed-Prospect Rejection","visual_frame_description":"Tom, a portfolio manager in a trading floor, faces a large wall of seven decision cards. Each card shows a gamble from Kahneman and Tversky's problems, and three colored sorting bays glow beneath: green for the gain domain, red for the loss domain, and gold for mixed prospects. Tom must drag each card into the correct bay as a live preference ticker shows how real participants answered.","player_mission":"Sort each decision problem into the column whose prospect-theory mechanism dominates most people's choice. Focus first on the focal problem in the question, then use the remaining rows to confirm that the same mechanism explains the whole set.","key_concepts":["Risk aversion is the norm for gains: the subjective value of $900 is more than 90% of the value of $1,000.","Risk aversion is replaced by risk seeking when the focus switches to losses.","The sure loss is very aversive and drives people to take a risk.","The negative value of losing $900 is much more than 90% of the negative value of losing $1,000.","In problem 3 most people prefer the sure $500 gain over the gamble.","In problem 4 most people prefer the gamble over the sure $500 loss.","A coin toss that loses $100 and wins $150 has a positive expected value, yet most people reject it.","For most people, the fear of losing $100 is more intense than the hope of gaining $150.","In mixed gambles, loss aversion causes extremely risk-averse choices.","In bad choices, diminishing sensitivity causes risk seeking."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This asset contrasts the three operating characteristics of System 1 that anchor prospect theory: risk aversion for gains, risk seeking for losses, and loss aversion that makes mixed prospects unattractive. Kahneman and Tversky found the same value function explains all three: diminishing sensitivity bends the curve in both domains, while the steeper slope for losses produces the roughly two-to-one asymmetry.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya's team faces problem 2 from the book: lose $900 for sure OR a 90% chance to lose $1,000. If she is like most people, she will take the gamble. Which prospect-theory mechanism drives that choice?","column_a_label":"Risk aversion in the gain domain (sure gain preferred)","column_b_label":"Risk seeking in the loss domain (gamble preferred over a sure loss)","column_c_label":"Loss aversion rejects favorable mixed gambles (possible loss looms larger than the gain)","rows":[{"label":"Get $900 for sure versus a 90% chance to get $1,000: the great majority choose the sure $900.","col":"a"},{"label":"The sure loss of $900 is very aversive, which drives people to accept the risk of the gamble.","col":"b"},{"label":"In problem 3 (after a $1,000 gift) most choose the sure $500 over a 50% chance to win $1,000.","col":"a"},{"label":"In problem 4 (after a $2,000 gift) most choose the gamble over losing $500 for sure.","col":"b"},{"label":"A coin toss where tails loses $100 and heads wins $150 has a positive expected value, yet most people reject it.","col":"c"},{"label":"The fear of losing $100 is more intense than the hope of gaining $150 in the coin-toss prospect.","col":"c"},{"label":"The pain of losing $900 is more than 90% of the pain of losing $1,000, so the sure loss is unbearable.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Loss aversion: the possible loss of $900 looms larger than the possible gain of $1,000.","Expected value maximization: the gamble's expected value exceeds the sure outcome.","A preference for certainty: people always avoid risk regardless of the domain."],"explanation":"The focal problem sits in the loss domain, where the bending of the value curve makes losing $900 feel almost as bad as losing $1,000, so the 10% chance to escape the loss is worth taking. In the gain domain the same curvature makes the sure gain feel closer to the full gain, producing risk aversion. Mixed prospects are a different case: the possible loss looms about twice as large as the gain, so even favorable gambles are rejected. These two insights, loss aversion in mixed gambles and diminishing sensitivity driving risk seeking in losses, are the essence of prospect theory."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Expected Utility, Prospect Theory, and Regret-Based Models","visual_frame_description":"In a university seminar room, an economics PhD student stands beside a whiteboard divided into three columns labeled with the three theories. Underneath, a stack of evidence cards from the chapter lies scattered on the table, each printed with a finding. The student argues all three theories fit the data equally well, and the player must sort the evidence cards into the correct columns.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card from the chapter into the theory column it actually supports. Start with the focal finding in the question, then place the remaining findings to reveal which theory is supported, which is refuted, and which is overshadowed.","key_concepts":["Prospect theory assigns a value of zero to the reference outcome, so it cannot represent the disappointment of failing to win when a big win was very likely.","A high probability of winning sets up a tentative new reference point, so winning nothing is experienced as a large loss.","Regret makes the experience of an outcome depend on an option you could have adopted but did not.","In problem 7, choosing the gamble and losing compounds disappointment with regret over spurning a sure $150,000.","Regret and disappointment are real and decision makers anticipate them, yet regret theories had less influence than prospect theory.","Regret theories made few striking predictions that distinguished them from the simpler prospect theory.","Theory-induced blindness contributed to the acceptance of prospect theory, which has flaws of its own.","Expected utility theory treats gains and losses as differing only in sign, so it cannot represent loss aversion.","Kahneman and Tversky saw their move to gains and losses as overcoming a serious case of theory-induced blindness.","Rabin and Thaler concluded that it was time for economists to recognize that expected utility is an ex-hypothesis."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests prospect theory's blind spot: it cannot represent disappointment and regret because it assigns a fixed value of zero to the reference outcome, regardless of how likely or how valuable the alternative was. Kahneman notes that regret- and disappointment-based models gained less traction than prospect theory because they made few predictions that distinguished them from the simpler and otherwise successful theory.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Problem 7 offers a 90% chance to win $1 million OR $150,000 with certainty. Kahneman says the pain of choosing the gamble and losing is compounded by knowing you spurned a sure gift, an emotion prospect theory cannot represent. Which column does this finding belong to?","column_a_label":"Expected utility theory (Bernoulli): utility of states of wealth","column_b_label":"Prospect theory: reference point, loss aversion, diminishing sensitivity","column_c_label":"Regret and disappointment-based models: counterfactual emotion","rows":[{"label":"Failing to win in problem 7 (90% chance of $1 million versus $150,000 sure) compounds disappointment with regret over spurning the sure gift.","col":"c"},{"label":"In problem 6 (90% chance of $1 million versus $50 sure) failing to win is only a disappointment, without the added sting of regret.","col":"c"},{"label":"Regret makes the experience of an outcome depend on an option you could have chosen but did not.","col":"c"},{"label":"Problems 3 and 4 produce different preferences for identical final states of wealth, a counterexample to Bernoulli.","col":"b"},{"label":"The value of winning nothing is fixed at zero by prospect theory, yet failing to win when the prize was 90% likely feels like a large loss.","col":"c"},{"label":"Expected utility theory cannot represent the fact that the disutility of losing $500 exceeds the utility of winning $500.","col":"a"},{"label":"Regret- and disappointment-based models made few striking predictions that distinguished them from the simpler prospect theory.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Loss aversion: the possible loss looms twice as large as the gain in mixed prospects.","Diminishing sensitivity: the value curve bends in both domains, causing risk seeking in losses.","Theory-induced blindness: scholars accepted expected utility because it predicted most market outcomes."],"explanation":"The focal finding is the classic regret setup: the outcome is experienced relative to the option the chooser could have had. Because prospect theory fixes the value of the reference outcome at zero regardless of probability or the alternative's value, it cannot represent disappointment and regret. These emotions are real and anticipated, but regret models remained less influential because they made few predictions that the simpler prospect theory could not already make."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Detecting Reference-Point Dependence Behind a Wealth-Based Story","visual_frame_description":"In a quiet interview room, Devon, a 34-year-old accountant in a cardigan, sits across from an auditor. A video feed on the side of the screen shows time-stamped clips of his reactions, and a behavioral log streams each hesitation, facial expression, and answer to a set of gamble problems. The auditor's notes fill the right panel.","player_mission":"Review Devon's baseline, the timed observation log, and the interrogation transcript, then diagnose the real mechanism behind his choices. Choose the correct diagnosis and justify it with the evidence before the audit is closed.","key_concepts":["People evaluate options as gains and losses relative to a reference point, not as states of wealth.","The same final outcome is experienced as a gain in one frame and a loss in another.","Gifts already included in the reference point are generally ignored when people choose.","Risk aversion is the norm for gains; risk seeking appears when all options are losses.","The sure loss is very aversive and drives people to accept a gamble.","The rejection of the mixed coin-toss prospect shows that losses loom larger than gains.","Evaluation relative to a reference point is an operating characteristic of System 1.","Physiological arousal and hesitation can betray conflict between stated beliefs and emotional valuation.","Kahneman and Tversky called the reference point the missing moving part in Bernoulli's theory.","People generally dislike losing more than they like winning, regardless of their wealth."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests reference-point dependence and the gain/loss asymmetry: the same final outcomes reverse preferences when the reference point moves, and risk seeking appears in the loss domain. Kahneman and Tversky showed that people code options as gains and losses relative to a reference state, ignoring amounts already absorbed into it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Devon claims he evaluates every financial decision by its effect on his total wealth. His observed choices suggest otherwise. Diagnose the real mechanism behind his behavior.","subject_baseline":"Devon, a 34-year-old staff accountant, describes himself as completely rational and says he never lets feelings color money decisions. In routine chat he frequently cites his net worth and says a gain or loss of a few hundred dollars barely registers against his savings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["12:00 - Presented with Get $900 for sure versus a 90% chance to get $1,000. Devon answers quickly, Sure thing, and relaxes, leaning back.","12:04 - Presented with Lose $900 for sure versus a 90% chance to lose $1,000. He pauses 14 seconds, rubs his temple, and says he would roll the dice.","12:09 - Told he has been given $2,000, then offered a 50% chance to lose $1,000 or losing $500 for sure. He frowns at the framing, re-reads twice, and picks the gamble.","12:13 - Asked how the $2,000 gift affected his thinking, he says he ignored it because it is already his money. Pupils dilated during the 50/50 loss item."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You said a $500 move barely registers against your savings. Then why does losing $500 for sure feel worse than a 50% chance to lose $1,000?","Devon: It just does. If I am going to lose either way, I want a shot at escaping.","Auditor: But you called yourself wealth-based. Same final wealth either way.","Devon: I know the math. I just do not feel it that way. The $2,000 was already mine."],"correct_diagnosis":"Devon is not evaluating states of wealth; his choices are coded as gains and losses relative to a reference point, producing risk aversion for gains and risk seeking in the loss domain.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His claim of wealth-based evaluation predicts indifference to a $500 swing against his stated net worth, yet the sure-loss item produced a 14-second hesitation, temple rubbing, dilated pupils, and risk seeking. In the $2,000-framed problem the certain outcome is coded as a $500 loss, so he gambles; the same final outcome coded as a gain in the other frame produces a sure-thing choice. His own words confirm that the reference point absorbed the gift and that evaluations are relative, exactly the pattern prospect theory predicts and Bernoulli's theory cannot.","distractors":["He is deliberately lying about his net worth to appear rational in front of the auditor.","He has a stable, well-defined utility for total wealth that explains all seven responses.","His hesitation reflects simple fatigue or boredom, not a systematic preference structure."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Loss Aversion Disguised as Market Composure","visual_frame_description":"Renata, a 27-year-old day trader in a studio-lit mock trading desk, wears a discreet heart-rate monitor. The screen shows a split view: on one side, her terse answers to gamble questions; on the other, a live physiological trace that spikes at a simulated $3,000 loss. The auditor annotates each timestamp in the right rail.","player_mission":"Analyze the observation log and transcript to determine whether Renata's stated trader composure matches her actual loss responses. Render the correct diagnosis and cite the decisive evidence.","key_concepts":["In a mixed gamble the possible loss looms larger than a possible gain of the same size.","Most people reject a coin toss that loses $100 and wins $150 despite its positive expected value.","The loss aversion ratio is usually between 1.5 and 2.5, with many people needing about $200 to balance a $100 loss.","The rejection of a mixed gamble is an act of System 2, but the critical inputs are emotional responses generated by System 1.","Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","Professional risk takers are more tolerant of losses, probably because they do not respond emotionally to every fluctuation.","Participants instructed to think like a trader became less loss averse and their physiological reaction to losses dropped.","Loss aversion coefficients increase when stakes rise, and may be infinite when a loss threatens one's lifestyle.","Diminishing sensitivity causes risk seeking in the loss domain, while loss aversion causes extreme risk aversion in mixed gambles.","There is no contradiction between the two claims because they involve different slopes of the value function."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests loss aversion and its malleability: the fear of losing $100 exceeds the hope of gaining $150, so most people reject a positive-expected-value coin toss, and the loss aversion ratio typically falls between 1.5 and 2.5. Kahneman reports that instructing people to think like a trader reduced loss aversion and the physiological response to losses.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Renata insists she thinks like a trader and feels nothing at losses. Her behavior during a simulation suggests loss aversion is still driving her. Diagnose the mechanism.","subject_baseline":"Renata, 27, a day trader with two years of experience, prides herself on emotional composure and says she runs the numbers, not the feelings. Colleagues describe her as stoic and unfazed by market swings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:30 - Rejects a 50/50 coin toss that loses $100 or wins $150. Expected value +$25. She answers in under two seconds and shows no visible reaction.","09:34 - Asked for the smallest gain that would make that gamble attractive, she needs 24 seconds before saying $200.","09:38 - Presented with a 50/50 gamble losing $10 or winning $20, she accepts immediately and smiles.","09:42 - The heart-rate monitor shows a spike when a simulated position moves $3,000 into loss, though her face shows nothing.","09:47 - Asked to think like a trader explicitly, she re-evaluates the $150/$100 gamble and reluctantly says she would take it."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You said a $100 loss is noise. Then why reject a coin toss that wins $150?","Renata: The downside still bugs me. In real markets I have a book, I am hedging. This feels like a pure bet on my own skin.","Auditor: But the expected value is positive.","Renata: I know. It is just that when it is mine, the loss hurts differently."],"correct_diagnosis":"Renata shows classic loss aversion: losses loom about twice as large as gains, and her rejection of the positive-expected-value gamble is driven by System 1 emotional responses even though her System 2 knows the math.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her loss aversion ratio of about $200 to balance a $100 loss matches the 1.5 to 2.5 range and the roughly two-to-one ratio reported from many experiments. The physiological spike at a simulated loss contradicts her stated composure and reflects the automatic emotional valuation of System 1. Crucially, the explicit instruction to think like a trader, the manipulation the book reports reduces loss aversion and physiological arousal, changed her answer, showing the response is emotional and malleable rather than a fixed wealth-utility calculation.","distractors":["She is rationally maximizing expected utility and her rejection reflects a subtle tax or fee.","She is lying about her trading experience to impress the auditor.","Her hesitations are random noise with no stable preference structure."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Defeating Expected Utility Theory with the Reference Point","visual_frame_description":"A holographic courtroom in the learning environment pits the player against a towering avatar labeled Expected Utility Theory, dressed in a Bernoulli-era coat. In front of the player float two decision problems side by side, each showing identical final-wealth outcomes wrapped in different framing. A verdict ribbon awaits the player's judgment.","player_mission":"Study the two problems, predict what the boss theory demands, then land the knock-out move by identifying the variable that changed between them. Defeat the boss by naming the mechanism that explains the reversal.","key_concepts":["Prospect theory defines outcomes as gains and losses relative to a reference point.","Bernoulli's theory requires only the state of wealth to determine utility.","Problems 3 and 4 are identical in final states of wealth but elicit opposite preferences.","The reference point was $1,000 above current wealth in problem 3 and $2,000 above in problem 4.","Being richer by $1,500 is a $500 gain in problem 3 and a $500 loss in problem 4.","The gifts of $1,000 or $2,000 were absorbed into the reference point and ignored.","This finding was a decisive counterexample to the key idea of Bernoulli's theory.","Theory-induced blindness kept the flaw unquestioned for more than 250 years.","People like winning and dislike losing, and they dislike losing more than they like winning.","The rejection of the mixed coin toss shows that losses loom larger than gains."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the decisive counterexample to Bernoulli's utility theory: identical final states of wealth produced opposite preferences when the reference point moved. The missing variable was the reference point, the earlier state relative to which gains and losses are evaluated, which prospect theory adds as the moving part absent from expected utility theory.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the central counterexample to Bernoulli's theory: choices that depend on a reference point. The boss is expected utility theory, which insists that only final states of wealth matter, so transparently equivalent statements of the same problem must yield identical choices.","stage_1_observation":"Two decision problems appear on screen. Problem 3: In addition to whatever you own, you have been given $1,000. Choose: 50% chance to win $1,000 OR $500 for sure. Problem 4: In addition to whatever you own, you have been given $2,000. Choose: 50% chance to lose $1,000 OR lose $500 for sure. An overlay notes that both problems reduce to the same choice between final wealth of $1,500 for sure and a 50/50 shot at $1,000 or $2,000.","stage_1_question":"In both problems the final states of wealth are identical. What does Bernoulli's expected utility theory predict about the preferences people will express?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It predicts that the two problems will elicit the same preferences, because utility depends only on the state of wealth, so equivalent statements of the same choice must produce identical decisions.","stage_2_intervention":"The results are in: a large majority choose the sure thing in problem 3, and a large majority choose the gamble in problem 4. The boss is reeling. You must now deliver the knock-out move by identifying what changed between the two problems.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The reference point changed: it is $1,000 above current wealth in problem 3, so the certain $1,500 is a $500 gain, and $2,000 above in problem 4, so it is a $500 loss. Because outcomes are evaluated as gains and losses relative to a reference point, the same final outcome is a gain in one frame and a loss in the other, reversing preferences."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk Seeking When All Options Are Bad","visual_frame_description":"A medical-device startup's glass conference room hosts a decision war-game. Two problems project onto the wall while a tally board records how the founders, engineers, and an HR manager answered each. A stubborn co-founder stands between the two screens, insisting that risk aversion must be consistent.","player_mission":"Predict the team's preference in the loss-domain problem, then reconcile the two observations by identifying the single mechanism behind both. Name the mechanism to advance past the boss.","key_concepts":["Risk aversion in the gain domain: the subjective value of $900 is more than 90% of the value of $1,000.","In the loss domain, the negative value of losing $900 is much more than 90% of the value of losing $1,000.","The sure loss is very aversive and drives people to take the risk.","People become risk seeking when all their options are bad.","The two insights are not contradictory; they involve different parts of the value function.","Diminishing sensitivity applies to both gains and losses, making the value function S-shaped.","The evaluation of probabilities also contributes to both risk aversion and the preference for the gamble.","Theory-induced blindness had made economists ignore the different attitudes to risk for gains and losses.","The observation of contrasting risk attitudes across domains was a significant advance.","The value function's slope changes abruptly at the reference point due to loss aversion."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the gain/loss asymmetry in risk attitude: diminishing sensitivity makes people risk averse for gains but risk seeking when all options are bad. Kahneman and Tversky found that the familiar risk aversion was replaced by risk seeking when the focus switched to losses, driven by the mirror-image bending of the value function.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the mirror-image principle of prospect theory: when all options are losses, diminishing sensitivity makes people risk seeking. The boss is the intuition that risk aversion is a fixed personality trait that should hold in every domain.","stage_1_observation":"The startup faces two problems. Problem 1: Get $900 for sure OR a 90% chance to get $1,000. Problem 2: Lose $900 for sure OR a 90% chance to lose $1,000. A tally shows nearly everyone takes the sure gain in problem 1, while the tally for problem 2 is still blank.","stage_1_question":"Given that risk aversion dominates problem 1, what does the book predict for problem 2, where a sure loss is compared to a larger loss that is merely probable?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Risk aversion is replaced by risk seeking: most people choose the gamble in problem 2, because the sure loss is very aversive and the negative value of losing $900 is much more than 90% of the value of losing $1,000.","stage_2_intervention":"A skeptical co-founder insists the team's risk-seeking answer in problem 2 must be an error, because risk aversion should be consistent. The escalation: you must reconcile the two observations by naming the single mechanism behind both.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Diminishing sensitivity in the S-shaped value function: the pain of losing $900 is more than 90% of the pain of losing $1,000, just as the value of $900 is more than 90% of the value of $1,000. The same bending of the value curve produces risk aversion for gains and risk seeking for losses, so the two observations are not contradictory."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Loss Aversion and the Two-to-One Ratio","visual_frame_description":"A rooftop angel-investing round with Dana, a sharp-eyed investor, faces a floating coin-toss display: heads wins $150, tails loses $100. A live readout shows the +$25 expected value alongside her delay, pupil dilation, and a slight frown as she declines. Beside her, fellow investor Leo shrugs and accepts the same bet.","player_mission":"Explain why Dana rejects a gamble with positive expected value, then give her the empirically grounded gain that would make the gamble feel fair. Complete both stages to defeat the boss of rational-agent assumptions.","key_concepts":["In a mixed gamble the loss looms larger than a gain of equal size.","Most people reject a coin toss that loses $100 and wins $150 despite a positive expected value.","The rejection is an act of System 2, but its critical inputs are emotional responses from System 1.","The loss aversion ratio is usually between 1.5 and 2.5, with a typical need for about $200 to offset a $100 loss.","The value function is about twice as steep for losses as for gains.","Loss aversion coefficients increase with stakes but not dramatically, unless the loss is ruinous.","Professional risk takers are more tolerant of losses, probably because they do not respond emotionally to every fluctuation.","Think like a trader instructions reduced loss aversion and physiological arousal.","Rabin's theorem shows that rejecting small favorable gambles commits a person to absurd risk aversion at larger stakes.","An individual who rejects the $100/$200 gamble would, by utility theory, also reject a 50/50 gamble of losing $200 and winning $20,000."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests loss aversion in mixed prospects: the possible loss looms about twice as large as the possible gain, so favorable gambles are rejected. Kahneman and Tversky estimated the loss aversion ratio at roughly 1.5 to 2.5 and showed that instructing participants to think like a trader reduces the aversion and its physiological signature.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests loss aversion in mixed prospects: when a gamble contains both a possible gain and a possible loss, losses loom roughly twice as large as gains, so people reject favorable gambles. The boss is the rational-agent assumption that any positive expected value should be accepted.","stage_1_observation":"An angel investor, Dana, is offered a coin toss: heads wins $150, tails loses $100. The screen shows the expected value calculation of +$25 floating above her, while a live readout displays her delay, pupil dilation, and a slight frown before she declines. A second investor, Leo, accepts the same bet with a shrug.","stage_1_question":"Dana rejects a gamble with positive expected value. What is the psychological reason most people, like Dana, reject it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Loss aversion: the fear of losing $100 is more intense than the hope of gaining $150, because losses loom larger than gains, so the possible loss outweighs the possible gain in subjective value.","stage_2_intervention":"Dana asks how much the win would need to be for this to feel fair. The escalation: you must give her the empirically grounded answer and explain why it holds.","stage_2_correct_answer":"About $200, roughly twice the loss. The loss aversion ratio has been estimated in several experiments and is usually in the range of 1.5 to 2.5, consistent with a value function about twice as steep for losses as for gains. Telling Dana to think like a trader would reduce her aversion and blunt the physiological response to losses."}]},{"id":"ch32","title":"27. The Endowment Effect","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why Sellers Demand More Than Buyers Will Pay","visual_frame_description":"At a startup retreat, free university-branded mugs are handed to half the team. Marisol, holding her mug, chats with teammates while a price-bidding board on the wall shows the mug experiment's numbers. A comparison panel overlays Seller, Chooser, and Buyer values on a small value-function graph.","player_mission":"Classify the evidence rows into the mechanism that explains the gap between Marisol's selling price and her buying price. Use the experimental results on screen to confirm the correct column.","key_concepts":["The endowment effect is the tendency to value goods more once they are owned.","In the mugs experiment, Sellers set an average price of $7.12 while Buyers set $2.87.","Sellers and Choosers faced identical options yet set different values ($7.12 versus $3.12).","The gap between selling and buying prices is inconsistent with a single stable utility.","Thaler's Professor R would neither sell his wine at $100 nor buy the same quality above $35.","Owning a good appears to increase its value because giving it up is painful.","The estimated number of mug trades was less than half of the number predicted by standard theory.","Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","The loss-averse value function of prospect theory explained the endowment effect.","The endowment effect is not universal: goods held for exchange show little or no effect."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the endowment effect: people demand much more to give up a good they own than they would pay to acquire the same good. Thaler, Knetsch, and Kahneman showed that a loss-averse value function explains the seller-buyer gap, with selling prices roughly double buying prices.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"At a startup retreat, free university-branded mugs are handed to half the team. Marisol, who got a mug, refuses to sell hers for $12 even though she told everyone she would never pay more than $5 for such a mug. Which mechanism explains the gap between her selling price and buying price?","column_a_label":"Endowment effect (loss aversion for owned goods)","column_b_label":"Stable rational valuation (a single utility for the good)","column_c_label":"Strategic bargaining (sellers bluff high prices)","rows":[{"label":"In the mugs experiment, the average Sellers' price of $7.12 was more than double the average Buyers' price of $2.87.","col":"a"},{"label":"Sellers and Choosers faced identical options, yet Sellers set a much higher value ($7.12 versus $3.12).","col":"a"},{"label":"Economic theory expects a single value: if a bottle is worth $50 to you, sell at anything above $50 and buy at anything below $50.","col":"b"},{"label":"Professor R would neither sell his wine at $100 nor buy the same wine above $35, a gap inconsistent with a single value.","col":"a"},{"label":"Owning the good appears to increase its value because giving it up is painful.","col":"a"},{"label":"The estimated number of mug trades was less than half of the number predicted by standard theory.","col":"a"},{"label":"Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Marisol is trying to profit from a sucker by bargaining aggressively.","The mug's market value genuinely rose once she owned it.","Marisol and the buyers have different tastes for university-branded mugs."],"explanation":"The mug is a good held for use, so giving it up is coded as a loss, and the pain of the loss outweighs the pleasure of the equivalent gain. This loss-averse value function explains why Sellers set values about double those of Buyers and why the trades in the market were less than half the predicted number. Standard theory, which assumes a single stable utility for the good, cannot explain the divergence, and neither strategic bargaining nor differing tastes predicts a selling price that exceeds the buying price for the same person."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Held for Use, Held for Exchange, and Labile Reference Points","visual_frame_description":"A baseball card convention hall bustles with tables of cards, mugs, and chocolate bars. A consultant with a clipboard observes novice and veteran traders, a shoe merchant, and a condo owner's listing photos on a laptop. Each person floats above a colored sorting bay as the player classifies them.","player_mission":"Classify each person or group into the column that predicts whether they will show the endowment effect. Start with the experienced traders from List's replication, then place the remaining cases.","key_concepts":["The endowment effect appears for goods held for use but not for goods held for exchange.","Routine commercial exchanges, like a $5 bill for five singles, show no loss aversion.","The merchant selling shoes and the shopper paying for them both treat the goods as proxies for exchange.","List found that only 18% of inexperienced traders would exchange their gift at a card convention.","48% of experienced traders exchanged the same gift, showing no trace of an endowment effect.","Veteran traders ask how much they want the good compared with other things they could have instead.","Knetsch found only about 10% of participants would exchange a pen or a bar of Swiss chocolate for the alternative.","The endowment effect appears when the owner has physical possession of the good before trading is mentioned.","Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","Reference points are labile and the endowment effect can be eliminated by changing them."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests when the endowment effect appears: goods held for use trigger loss aversion, while goods held for exchange do not, and trading experience eliminates the effect. List's replication found 18% of inexperienced traders exchanged their gift while 48% of experienced traders did, and Knetsch's pen-and-chocolate study found only about 10% exchanged.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"List's replication at a card convention gave volunteers a random gift, a mug or an equal-value chocolate bar, then offered a trade. Inexperienced traders mostly refused to exchange; experienced traders traded freely. Which column describes the experienced traders?","column_a_label":"Endowment effect present (good held for use)","column_b_label":"No endowment effect (good held for exchange, or trading experience)","column_c_label":"Reference point shifts, changing the effect","rows":[{"label":"A novice collector at a card convention who refuses to part with the card he was just handed.","col":"a"},{"label":"A veteran dealer at the same convention who asks what else he could have instead, and trades readily.","col":"b"},{"label":"A merchant who sells shoes for money, treating the shoes as a proxy for cash.","col":"b"},{"label":"A shopper who pays the merchant, treating her money as a proxy for the shoes she intends to buy.","col":"b"},{"label":"An owner of a Boston condo who bought at the market peak and now prices the unit far above a rational estimate.","col":"c"},{"label":"A person asked to exchange a $5 bill for five singles, who does so without any sense of loss.","col":"b"},{"label":"A homeowner in a declining market who faces large losses and waits longer to sell, receiving more in the end.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Experienced traders experience stronger loss aversion because they own more valuable goods.","The experienced traders secretly value the mug more than the chocolate.","Trading experience increases emotional attachment to owned goods."],"explanation":"The endowment effect depends on whether a good is held for exchange and on trading experience. Veteran traders have learned to ask how much they want the good compared with other things they could have instead, the question Econs ask, which eliminates the asymmetry between the pleasure of getting and the pain of giving up. Novices, like babies clutching toys, show the automatic loss aversion of System 1; the contrast between the 18% of novices and 48% of experienced traders who traded is the cleanest demonstration that the effect is not fixed."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Status Quo and the Hedonic Twins","visual_frame_description":"Two identical-looking men, Albert and Ben, sit in matching cubicles on a large indifference-map projection. Albert's screen shows a raise of $10,000 and Ben's shows an extra vacation day each month. A coin flips in the center as the company's switch offer appears, and both twins frown at the proposal.","player_mission":"Classify the evidence rows to identify the mechanism that makes both twins prefer to stay in their current positions. Confirm the mechanism with the chapter's account of how reference points reshape the switch offer.","key_concepts":["Tastes are not fixed; they vary with the reference point.","The disadvantages of a change loom larger than its advantages.","Loss aversion induces a bias that favors the status quo.","After owning position A, Albert evaluates a switch as a $10,000 salary cut plus 12 vacation days.","Ben evaluates the same switch as a loss of now-precious leisure.","The twins were originally indifferent between the two positions.","Standard theory predicts stable preferences and little incentive to switch.","Loss aversion does not imply you never change; it biases choices toward the reference situation and small changes.","In labor negotiations the reference point is the existing contract.","Making concessions hurts, which is why both sides find bargaining difficult."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests status quo bias as a consequence of reference-point-dependent preferences: the same options the twins were originally indifferent between become unattractive once they are endowed with one option. Kahneman shows that loss aversion makes the disadvantage of a change loom larger than its advantage, biasing choices toward the status quo.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Albert and Ben are hedonic twins with identical tastes and identical starting jobs. Albert gets a $10,000 raise (position A) and Ben an extra vacation day each month (position B), options they were originally indifferent between. Now the company invites them to switch. Prospect theory says both will refuse. Which mechanism explains this?","column_a_label":"Status quo bias from loss aversion","column_b_label":"Stable preferences (standard indifference-curve theory)","column_c_label":"Diminishing marginal utility","rows":[{"label":"After taking position A, Albert codes a move to B as a $10,000 salary cut plus 12 vacation days, and the cut dominates.","col":"a"},{"label":"After taking position B, Ben codes a move to A as losing now-precious leisure, and that loss dominates.","col":"a"},{"label":"Standard theory assumes tastes are fixed, so the twins should need little or no incentive to switch.","col":"b"},{"label":"The disadvantages of a change loom larger than its advantages, biasing choices toward the status quo.","col":"a"},{"label":"Tastes are not fixed; they vary with the reference point.","col":"a"},{"label":"The more leisure you have, the less you care for an extra day of it.","col":"c"},{"label":"In labor negotiations both sides anchor on the existing contract, and making concessions hurts.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The twins' tastes genuinely differ because they value income and leisure differently.","The company's offer is technically inferior to both current positions.","Diminishing marginal utility makes the twins indifferent to any change."],"explanation":"Each twin's current position becomes the reference point, so any move is coded as a package containing a loss, the $10,000 cut for Albert or lost leisure for Ben. Because losses loom larger than gains, the disadvantage of moving outweighs the advantage even though the twins were originally indifferent. This is a direct prediction of a loss-averse value function and contradicts the indifference-map assumption of fixed tastes and a unique utility per state."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Detecting a High Reference Point Behind a Stubborn Listing","visual_frame_description":"Elena, 41, in a bright condo living room, argues with her listing agent over a tablet showing comparable sales around $505,000. A price line on the wall stretches from her 2007 purchase at $620,000 to today's market, and her rejected offers flash across the bottom of the screen.","player_mission":"Review Elena's baseline, the timed observation log, and the transcript, then diagnose the mechanism driving her stubborn asking price. Identify the reference point and how loss aversion amplifies it.","key_concepts":["For a rational agent, the buying price is irrelevant history; only current market value matters.","Owners with a high reference point face higher losses and set a higher price on their dwelling.","High-reference owners spend a longer time trying to sell their home.","High-reference owners eventually receive more money for their homes.","Loss aversion makes selling below the reference point feel like a real loss.","The selling-buying gap is a signature of the endowment effect.","Reference points can be the status quo or the price originally paid.","Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","Reference points are labile, but here the purchase price anchors the owner's valuation.","The chapter reports this pattern from a study of Boston condos during a downturn."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests reference-point-dependent pricing: in a down market, owners who bought at a higher price face larger perceived losses, set higher asking prices, wait longer, and eventually receive more. Kahneman cites a Boston condominium study showing this pattern, an illustration of the power of reference points and loss aversion outside the lab.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Elena lists her Boston condo during a housing downturn. She insists her asking price is just what it is worth. Her behavior suggests a reference point, her purchase price, is driving the listing. Diagnose the mechanism.","subject_baseline":"Elena, 41, bought her condo at the market peak in 2007 for $620,000. She describes herself as a practical engineer and says she prices things by current value, not by what she paid. Neighboring identical units are now selling near $505,000.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:00 - Asked what the unit is worth today, she answers instantly that it has to be six-twenty. She has not updated comparable sales in four months.","10:07 - Told identical units close near $505,000, she stares at the number for nine seconds, then waves her hand and says she is not selling hers at a loss.","10:12 - Her listing agent's phone log shows she has rejected three offers between $495,000 and $510,000 in the last month.","10:18 - Asked what she would pay for an identical unit today, she pauses and says she would perhaps pay five-oh-five, but this one is hers.","10:25 - She keeps repeating that she bought it in 2007, and her speech quickens whenever the purchase price is mentioned."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You say you price by current value. An identical unit is worth about $505,000.","Elena: Then let someone else sell at that. I refuse to give it away.","Auditor: What would you pay for this exact unit if you did not own it?","Elena: I would look at comps and probably land near five-oh-five.","Auditor: So your buying price is $505,000 and your selling price is $620,000. What changed?","Elena: When it is your own, losing money feels different."],"correct_diagnosis":"Elena's behavior is the Boston-condo pattern: owners with a high reference point face larger perceived losses, set higher prices, wait longer to sell, and eventually receive more money. Her purchase price acts as her reference point, so selling below it is coded as a loss that loss aversion amplifies.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her stated policy of current value predicts she should accept about $505,000, yet she rejected offers above that and demands $620,000. Her own buying-price estimate of about $505,000 matches the market, exposing a selling-buying gap consistent with the endowment effect. The instant answer that it has to be six-twenty and the agitation at the purchase price betray an automatic, emotional valuation; the chapter reports exactly this pattern in the Boston condo market, where higher-reference owners hold out longer and net more.","distractors":["Elena is strategically overpricing to anchor future buyers into paying more.","Elena's unit is genuinely worth $620,000 because of hidden renovations.","Elena is simply uninformed and has never seen the comparable sales data."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Endowment Effect Behind a Claim of Indifference","visual_frame_description":"A card convention table shows Marcus, a 19-year-old first-timer, with a university-branded mug resting beside his elbow. A researcher slides an equal-value chocolate bar toward him. A side camera captures his hand staying on the mug and his gaze flicking between the two gifts as the researcher offers the trade again.","player_mission":"Analyze Marcus's claim of indifference against his observed behavior and transcript, then diagnose the mechanism behind his refusal to trade. Weigh the evidence before rendering the verdict.","key_concepts":["The endowment effect appears when the owner has physical possession of the good before trading is mentioned.","List replicated Knetsch's experiment and found only 18% of inexperienced traders would exchange their gift.","48% of experienced traders showed no trace of an endowment effect.","Ownership makes giving a good up feel like a loss.","Stated indifference can coexist with loss-averse behavior.","Loss aversion is built into the automatic evaluations of System 1.","Veteran traders ask how much they want the good compared with alternatives, eliminating the effect.","Knetsch's pen-and-chocolate study found only about 10% of participants exchanged their gift.","The long-term consequences of keeping or trading are identical for the two groups.","The only difference between the choices is the emotion of the moment."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the endowment effect and its dependence on physical possession: ownership changes the reference point so giving the good up is a loss. Knetsch found the effect only when participants held the good before trading was offered, and List replicated it at a card convention, where only 18% of novices traded versus 48% of experienced traders.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus, a first-time attendee at a sports card convention, is given a coffee mug as a survey reward and told he may trade it for a chocolate bar of equal value. He claims to be indifferent, then refuses to trade. Diagnose the mechanism.","subject_baseline":"Marcus, 19, is at his first card convention and has never bought or sold a collectible in his life. He fills out List's survey expecting a small gift and says he could not care less which reward he gets.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02 - Handed a university-branded mug, he turns it over, sets it beside his elbow, and says that free stuff is fine either way.","14:11 - The researcher says he may trade the mug for a chocolate bar instead. Marcus glances at the chocolate, looks at the mug, and says he will keep this, without picking up the chocolate.","14:14 - Asked if the two gifts are equally valuable, he says yes, while his hand stays resting on the mug.","14:20 - When the researcher mentions the trade again, Marcus pulls the mug closer to his side and shakes his head.","14:26 - Asked to imagine the chocolate bar was his gift instead, he says he would probably keep the chocolate too."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You said the gifts were equal. Why keep the mug?","Marcus: I do not know. It is mine now. Feels like I would be losing it.","Auditor: Losing a free mug?","Marcus: Sounds dumb, I know. But the bar is just something I could have. The mug is something I have.","Auditor: Would a more experienced trader feel the same?","Marcus: Probably not. They are used to swapping."],"correct_diagnosis":"Marcus shows the endowment effect of a novice: because he physically possesses the mug, giving it up feels like a loss, so he refuses a trade he himself calls equal. His behavior mirrors List's finding that only 18% of inexperienced traders exchanged their gift.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His stated indifference is contradicted by his behavior, the hand resting on the mug, pulling it closer, and refusing an equal-value trade. Knetsch's manipulation shows the effect appears only when the person has physical possession before trading is mentioned, and Marcus's instant ownership does exactly that. His own explanation that the bar is something he could have while the mug is something he has captures the reference-point structure of loss aversion, and his guess about experienced traders matches List's data showing 48% traded.","distractors":["Marcus secretly prefers mugs over chocolate and is hiding his true taste.","Marcus believes the mug is worth more money than the chocolate bar.","Marcus is being polite and would trade if asked a third time."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Spending as Loss: Living on the Steep Limb","visual_frame_description":"Omar, a 38-year-old engineer in a tidy home office, faces a spending exercise on a tablet. On one side a $6 branded mug and $6 in cash sit on the desk; on the other, a gift card with unused balance. A behavioral log streams his decision times and a hand that tightens around the mug.","player_mission":"Review Omar's baseline and observation log, then diagnose why he treats spending as a loss. Distinguish his behavior from the trader pattern and identify the reference-relative mental accounting at work.","key_concepts":["For the poor, all choices are between losses, and costs are losses.","Being poor, in prospect theory, is living below one's reference point.","Small amounts of money the poor receive are perceived as a reduced loss, not a gain.","The poor always remain on the steep limb of the value function.","Money spent on one good is the loss of another good that could have been purchased instead.","We all know people for whom spending is painful, although they are objectively quite well-off.","The poor think like traders, but unlike traders they are not indifferent between gaining and giving up.","Loss aversion makes both buying and selling feel costly when money and goods are held for use.","The endowment effect means giving up the free mug feels like a loss.","Reference points can be the status quo, expectations, or outcomes to which a person feels entitled."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests loss aversion applied to spending: for people who treat money as a reference-relative resource, every purchase is a loss. Kahneman describes the poor as living in the losses so small amounts are reduced losses rather than gains, and notes that some well-off people experience the same painful spending.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Omar, a well-off engineer, describes himself as just careful with money. His behavior during a spending exercise suggests he experiences every purchase as a loss. Diagnose the mechanism.","subject_baseline":"Omar, 38, earns $140,000 a year and has six months of expenses saved. He describes money as the only thing you cannot get back and says he cannot enjoy a purchase after the fact. Colleagues call him the human coupon.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:00 - Given a $50 gift card for a bookstore, he spends 11 minutes comparing prices and leaves with a single paperback and unused balance intact.","15:09 - Asked to choose between a $6 branded mug and $6 cash, he picks the cash instantly and says money is money.","15:14 - When told the mug would be free, he takes it immediately and later resists exchanging it for $6 in cash, saying it is his mug now.","15:20 - Asked why he treats his salary so differently from windfalls, he says salary is already spoken for while a windfall he can actually spend.","15:25 - His hand tightens around the mug when a colleague jokingly offers to trade a nicer one for it."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: You picked $6 cash over a mug worth $6, but then refused to sell the free mug for $6.","Omar: Buying it means giving up the cash. That is a cost. Selling it means giving up the mug. Also a cost. Costs are losses, so I avoid both.","Auditor: But you have plenty of money.","Omar: Irrelevant. The loss is the loss.","Auditor: Your salary is a gain when it lands. Why treat it as already spent?","Omar: Because I earned it. It has a job to do."],"correct_diagnosis":"Omar treats spending as a loss, the mental accounting of someone for whom costs are losses. The chapter predicts this pattern for the poor, who live below their reference point, but notes that we all know well-off people for whom spending is painful.","diagnostic_breakdown":"His behavior shows symmetric avoidance of giving anything up: he will not give cash for a mug because buying is a cost, and will not give the mug for cash because selling is a loss of possession. Both sides of the mug exercise show loss aversion. The chapter describes the same dynamic among the poor, who always face choices between losses and for whom money spent on one good is the loss of another good, and explicitly notes that some people who are objectively quite well-off still find spending painful. His earned-versus-windfall distinction shows a reference point tied to expectations and entitlement.","distractors":["Omar is concealing a genuine cash shortage or debt.","Omar's mug is secretly worth more than $6 to him.","Omar is playing a game with the auditor and lying about his real preferences."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Breaking a Negotiation Deadlock by Resetting the Reference Point","visual_frame_description":"A union representative and a management team face each other across a long conference table. The current contract glows in the center as the reference point, and a negotiation ticker shows that neither side has moved in six rounds. A mediator's badge sits empty in the middle of the table.","player_mission":"Identify the mechanism that freezes both sides, then propose the reference-point intervention that could break the deadlock. Advance through both stages by naming the principle from the chapter.","key_concepts":["In labor negotiations the reference point is the existing contract.","Negotiations focus on mutual demands for concessions relative to that reference point.","Making concessions hurts because losses loom larger than gains.","The disadvantages of a change loom larger than its advantages.","Loss aversion induces a bias that favors the status quo.","The hedonic twins would both prefer to remain as they are after being endowed with a position.","Tastes are not fixed; they vary with the reference point.","Reference points are labile and the endowment effect can be eliminated by changing them.","Loss aversion biases choices to favor small rather than large changes.","The minimal wage unemployed workers accept averages 90% of their previous wage and falls slowly over a year."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests status quo bias and concession aversion in negotiation: the current contract is the reference point, and loss aversion makes any concession feel like a real loss. Kahneman notes that both sides in labor negotiations anchor on the existing contract and that making concessions hurts.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests status quo bias and concession aversion in negotiation: each side anchors on its current contract, and because losses loom larger than gains, making concessions hurts. The boss is a stalled labor negotiation.","stage_1_observation":"On screen, a union and a management team sit across a table with the current contract highlighted as the reference point. A line under it reads that the role of loss aversion in bargaining is well understood and that making concessions hurts. A ticker shows the two sides' offer history, and neither side has moved in six rounds.","stage_1_question":"Both sides refuse to move even when the other side offers something in return. What psychological mechanism, named in the chapter, explains why concessions are so painful?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Loss aversion: each side evaluates the other side's demands as losses relative to the existing contract, and losses loom larger than gains, so the pain of a concession exceeds the pleasure of a gain of equal size.","stage_2_intervention":"A mediator steps in and proposes reframing the negotiation so both sides evaluate proposals against a new reference point, the projected contract in two years, instead of the current one. The escalation: explain why this might break the deadlock.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Because the reference point determines what counts as a gain or loss, shifting it to the projected future contract recodes some concessions as gains or smaller losses. The chapter shows that changing the reference point can eliminate the loss aversion that freezes choice, since reference points are labile and the disadvantages of a change loom larger only relative to the current position."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Thinking Like a Trader to Beat the Endowment Effect","visual_frame_description":"A card convention table shows two volunteers under List's survey banner. Novice Nathan clutches a mug while experienced dealer Priya weighs a chocolate bar against a row of other collectibles. A tally board displays the numbers 18% and 48% beside two arrows, and the survey line reads that each person may trade if they wish.","player_mission":"Predict the experienced traders' behavior in List's replication, then deploy the intervention that converts a novice owner into a trader. Name the question that eliminates the endowment effect to defeat the boss.","key_concepts":["Veteran traders ask how much they want to have a good compared with other things they could have instead.","This is the question Econs ask, and with it there is no endowment effect.","List found that only 18% of inexperienced traders were willing to exchange their gift.","48% of experienced traders traded, showing no trace of an endowment effect.","The endowment effect is not universal; it depends on whether goods are held for use or exchange.","Goods held for exchange do not trigger loss aversion.","Knetsch's pen-and-chocolate study found only about 10% of participants exchanged their gift.","Physical possession of the good before trading is mentioned is required for the effect.","Reference points are labile, especially in unusual laboratory situations.","Routine commerce shows no loss aversion on either side of the exchange."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the boundary conditions of the endowment effect: trading experience and the trader's question eliminate it. List's card-convention study found 48% of experienced traders exchanged their gift versus 18% of novices, showing the effect is not universal and depends on whether goods are held for use or for exchange.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the trader's question that eliminates the endowment effect: how much do I want to have this good, compared with other things I could have instead? The boss is the novice's automatic attachment to owned goods.","stage_1_observation":"A table at a card convention shows two volunteers. Novice Nathan received a mug, and experienced dealer Priya received a chocolate bar. List's survey script asks each of them that they were given one gift but can trade for the other if they wish. A live tally shows Nathan clutching his mug while Priya weighs the bar against a row of other items.","stage_1_question":"List's exact replication of Knetsch's experiment found that only 18% of inexperienced traders exchanged their gift. What proportion of experienced traders traded, and why the difference?","stage_1_correct_answer":"48% of experienced traders traded, showing no trace of an endowment effect, because trading experience and the market context made them treat the gift as a carrier of exchange value rather than a good held for use.","stage_2_intervention":"Nathan refuses to trade. The escalation: deploy the intervention that converts an owner into a trader so Nathan can compare his mug against the alternatives.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Prompt Nathan to ask the trader's question, how much he wants the mug compared with the chocolate bar and other things he could have instead, because this reframes the good as held for exchange, makes the pleasure of getting and the pain of giving up symmetric, and eliminates the endowment effect."}]},{"id":"ch33","title":"28. Bad Events","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Negativity Dominance: Bad Is Stronger Than Good","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen neuroscience console. On the left, a masked pair of terrified eyes flashes for less than a hundredth of a second and is instantly buried under a checkerboard of visual noise, while a brain-scan heatmap lights up the amygdala. On the right, a bowl of cherries with a single cockroach sits beside a bowl of cockroaches holding one cherry. Evidence cards slide across the bottom of the screen, each describing an asymmetry between good and bad events.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the column for the mechanism it demonstrates: negativity dominance, loss aversion, or reference-point dependence. Drag all seven cards into place, then confirm your classifications to unlock the diagnostic breakdown.","key_concepts":["Bad is stronger than good: bad emotions, bad parents, and bad feedback have more impact than good ones.","Bad information is processed more thoroughly than good information.","The amygdala has a primary role as the 'threat center' of the brain and responds intensely to a masked threatening picture the viewer never consciously recognizes.","Threat information travels via a superfast neural channel that bypasses the visual cortex that supports conscious seeing.","Schematic angry faces are processed faster and more efficiently than schematic happy faces.","An angry face pops out of a crowd of happy faces, but a single happy face does not stand out in an angry crowd.","A single cockroach will completely wreck the appeal of a bowl of cherries, but a cherry does nothing at all for a bowl of cockroaches.","Gottman estimated that a stable relationship requires good interactions to outnumber bad ones by at least 5 to 1.","Bad impressions and bad stereotypes are quicker to form and more resistant to disconfirmation than good ones.","Loss aversion is one of many manifestations of a broad negativity dominance in which the negative trumps the positive.","No comparably rapid mechanism for recognizing good news has been detected in the brain."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests negativity dominance, the broad asymmetry in which bad events, bad feedback, and bad impressions have more impact and are processed more thoroughly than good ones, from the amygdala's superfast threat response to social and consumer judgments. Kahneman presents it as an evolutionary foundation of which loss aversion is one manifestation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism explains each observed asymmetry: negativity dominance, loss aversion, or reference-point dependence?","column_a_label":"Negativity dominance (bad is stronger than good)","column_b_label":"Loss aversion (losses loom larger than gains)","column_c_label":"Reference-point dependence (the boundary between good and bad depends on a baseline)","rows":[{"label":"The amygdala responds intensely to a masked picture of terrified eyes the viewer never consciously recognized.","col":"a"},{"label":"An angry face pops out of a crowd of happy faces faster than a happy face stands out in an angry crowd.","col":"a"},{"label":"A single cockroach ruins a bowl of cherries, but a cherry does nothing for a bowl of cockroaches.","col":"a"},{"label":"Professional golfers putt more accurately for par than for a birdie.","col":"b"},{"label":"New York cabdrivers quit early on rainy days once their daily earnings target is reached.","col":"b"},{"label":"82% of survey respondents rate a store raising shovel prices from $15 to $20 after a snowstorm as unfair.","col":"c"},{"label":"A soaked hiker finds intense pleasure at a rock that blocks the wind, though the relief is brief.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The amygdala's response to the masked picture is best explained by loss aversion because the face signals a potential loss.","The golfer's extra accuracy on par putts is best explained by negativity dominance because missing par feels catastrophic.","The hiker's relief is best explained by negativity dominance because the wind was a strong negative event."],"explanation":"The amygdala, angry-face pop-out, and cockroach-cherries rows all demonstrate negativity dominance: bad events are detected faster, processed more thoroughly, and outweigh good ones. The golfer and cabdriver rows show loss aversion, the asymmetric strength of avoiding losses versus pursuing gains, since failing to reach a goal reads as a loss. The shovel-price and hiker rows show reference-point dependence, where the boundary between good and bad depends on a baseline such as the pre-storm price or the current misery of the hiker."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Fairness Rules and Dual Entitlements","visual_frame_description":"A 1980s telephone-survey call center under a Department of Fisheries and Oceans banner. Interviewers in headsets read fairness vignettes from four color-coded questionnaires, with a standing rule that every version must mention fish. On a monitor, employer scenarios scroll past: a hardware store raising shovel prices after a blizzard, a photocopy shop cutting a worker's wage, and a profitable firm keeping its cost savings. A tally bar shows how respondents rate each action from Completely Fair to Very Unfair.","player_mission":"For each employer action, classify it as unfair, acceptable because it protects the firm's own entitlement to profit, or acceptable because no one's entitlement is infringed. Use the fairness rules the survey respondents applied to sort every row correctly.","key_concepts":["The existing wage, price, or rent sets a reference point that has the nature of an entitlement that must not be infringed.","82% of respondents rated the snow-shovel price hike from $15 to $20 as Unfair or Very Unfair.","83% of respondents rated cutting a current worker's wage from $9 to $7 as unfair, while a large majority (73%) called paying a replacement $7 acceptable.","The employer does not have a moral obligation to pay $9 an hour; the entitlement is personal to the current worker.","The firm has its own entitlement, which is to retain its current profit.","It is not unfair for a firm to reduce wages when its profitability is falling, because it is protecting its own entitlement.","A firm that faced lower production costs was not required by the rules of fairness to share the bonanza with customers or workers.","The exploitation of market power to impose losses on others is unacceptable.","Respondents showed indignation only when a firm exploited its power to break informal contracts and impose a loss to increase its profit.","Fairness concerns are economically significant: unfair merchants lose sales and unfair employers face reduced productivity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests reference-dependent fairness and dual entitlements: the existing wage, price, or rent sets a reference point that operates as an entitlement, and the firm holds its own entitlement to current profit. Kahneman, Knetsch, and Thaler found 82% called the shovel hike unfair, 83% called a wage cut to a current worker unfair, but 73% accepted paying a replacement worker $7.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Under the fairness rules the survey respondents applied, how should each employer action be classified?","column_a_label":"Unfair (imposes a loss on customers or workers relative to the reference transaction)","column_b_label":"Acceptable because it protects the firm's own entitlement to profit","column_c_label":"Acceptable because no entitlement is infringed and no loss is imposed","rows":[{"label":"A hardware store raises shovel prices from $15 to $20 the morning after a large snowstorm.","col":"a"},{"label":"A photocopy shop cuts its current worker's wage from $9 to $7 while the business remains profitable.","col":"a"},{"label":"After the current employee leaves, the shop hires a replacement worker at $7 an hour.","col":"c"},{"label":"A firm reduces wages when its own profitability is falling, to protect its entitlement to profit.","col":"b"},{"label":"A firm with lower production costs keeps the savings instead of sharing them with customers.","col":"c"},{"label":"A firm exploits its power to break informal contracts and impose a loss on others in order to increase its profit.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The snow-shovel price hike is acceptable because the store is simply responding to increased demand, which is the standard economic model.","Cutting a wage to $7 is fair whenever other shops in the area hire reliable workers at $7.","A firm must share windfall savings from lower costs, or it is branded unfair by respondents."],"explanation":"The survey data show respondents judged the shovel hike (82% unfair) and the wage cut to a current worker (83% unfair) unacceptable because they impose losses relative to the reference transaction. A firm may reduce wages when its own profitability is threatened because it is defending its entitlement, but a replacement worker holds no entitlement to the prior wage, so paying $7 is acceptable. Not sharing cost savings is also acceptable, since the firm is entitled to its profit and no one suffers an imposed loss."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Losses, Foregone Gains, and the Reference Point in Law and Negotiation","visual_frame_description":"A law library reading room at dusk. On a wall terminal, a comparison grid sorts legal and commercial situations into two columns: ACTUAL LOSS and FOREGONE GAIN. A jurist's annotations appear beside each row: a merchant whose goods were lost in transit, a customer who overpaid relative to a new catalog price, and a handshake over a contract renewal. The cursor hovers over rows waiting to be classified.","player_mission":"Classify each situation by how loss aversion and the reference point treat it: as an actual loss that is heavily weighted and protected, a foregone gain that is lightly weighted, or a reference-point entitlement that defines the boundary between good and bad.","key_concepts":["A merchant whose goods were lost in transit may be compensated for costs he actually incurred, but is unlikely to be compensated for lost profits.","The law draws a sharp distinction between actual losses and foregone gains.","The rule that possession is nine-tenths of the law confirms the moral status of the reference point.","People who lose suffer more than people who merely fail to gain, and may therefore deserve more protection from the law.","Customers who learned from a new catalog that the merchant now charged less than they had paid reduced future purchases by 15%, an average loss of $90 per customer.","The existing terms of an agreement define reference points, and a proposed change is viewed as a concession one side makes to the other.","Renegotiation of an existing contract is the typical situation in labor negotiations and international discussions of trade or arms limitations.","The concessions you make to me are my gains but your losses; they cause you much more pain than they give me pleasure.","Loss aversion creates an asymmetry that makes agreements difficult to reach.","The boundary between good and bad is a reference point that changes over time and depends on the immediate circumstances."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how loss aversion and the reference point structure law and negotiation: actual losses relative to a reference point are heavily weighted and protected, while foregone gains receive little weight. Kahneman notes the law compensates out-of-pocket costs but not lost profits, and that renegotiations are hard because existing terms define entitlements.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"How does the reference point treat each situation: as an actual loss, a foregone gain, or a reference-point entitlement?","column_a_label":"Actual loss (heavily weighted, compensated or protected)","column_b_label":"Foregone gain (lightly weighted, not compensated)","column_c_label":"Reference-point entitlement (existing terms define the boundary between good and bad)","rows":[{"label":"A merchant whose goods are lost in transit is compensated for the shipping costs he actually incurred.","col":"a"},{"label":"The same merchant is unlikely to be compensated for the profits the lost goods would have earned.","col":"b"},{"label":"Possession is nine-tenths of the law: the current holder's position is protected.","col":"c"},{"label":"Customers who learn a new catalog charges less than they paid reduce their future purchases by 15%.","col":"a"},{"label":"Renegotiating an existing contract is harder than a fresh deal because current terms are the reference point.","col":"c"},{"label":"The concessions you make to me are my gains but your losses, so you value them more than I do.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The law compensates foregone profits just as readily as out-of-pocket costs, because both are economic losses.","Possession is protected only when the holder is the true owner, never on the basis of current position alone.","Renegotiation is hard mainly because negotiators are irrational, not because the reference point creates entitlements."],"explanation":"The law compensates actual out-of-pocket losses but not foregone profits, because people who lose suffer more than people who merely fail to gain. Reference-point entitlement explains why possession is protected and why renegotiation of existing terms is difficult, since current terms define the boundary between good and bad. The catalog customers and the negotiation concessions show actual losses relative to a reference point driving behavior, because a loss hurts more than a foregone gain pleases."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Bargaining-Chip Bluff in Arms Negotiation","visual_frame_description":"A Vienna conference room where two delegations face each other across a long walnut table. Chief negotiator Elena Marchetti of the Alto Republic is mid-sentence, fist pressed flat on the table, describing her country's medium-range missile system as a matter of sovereign pride. A CCTV-style overlay records her micro-expressions while a transcript scrolls on a side monitor, and an analyst's cursor highlights the moment her voice rises above her normal range.","player_mission":"Audit negotiator Elena Marchetti's insistence on keeping the missile system out of the deal. Decide whether her demonstrated attachment is genuine commitment or a staged bargaining-chip bluff designed to force an equally painful reciprocal concession from the other side.","key_concepts":["Many of the messages negotiators exchange are attempts to communicate a reference point and provide an anchor to the other side.","Negotiators often pretend intense attachment to some good, such as missiles of a particular type in arms reductions, although they actually view it as a bargaining chip.","Because negotiators are influenced by a norm of reciprocity, a concession presented as painful calls for an equally painful concession from the other side.","The existing terms of an agreement define reference points, and a proposed change is inevitably viewed as a concession one side makes to the other.","Loss aversion creates an asymmetry that makes agreements difficult to reach.","The concessions you make to me are my gains but your losses; they cause you much more pain than they give me pleasure.","Negotiations over a shrinking pie are especially difficult because they require an allocation of losses.","People tend to be much more easygoing when they bargain over an expanding pie.","Potential losers will be more active and determined than potential winners in any proposed change.","Loss aversion is a powerful conservative force that favors minimal changes from the status quo."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the strategic use of loss aversion in negotiation: negotiators communicate reference points as anchors and often pretend intense attachment to a good they privately view as a bargaining chip they intend to give away. Kahneman notes that reciprocity then makes a concession presented as painful call for an equally painful concession in return.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Chief Negotiator Elena Marchetti genuinely committed to preserving the medium-range missile system, or is she bluffing with a bargaining chip?","subject_baseline":"Marchetti is a 24-year veteran diplomat who has led six arms-control rounds. In past rounds she argued in a low, steady register, made few gestures, and used understated language about her government's true red lines. Analysts noted that when she genuinely could not concede a point, she never dramatized it; she simply repeated the position flatly and refused to elaborate.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:12 - Marchetti slams her palm on the table and raises her voice a full octave while calling the missile system 'the heart of our sovereignty'.","10:13 - Immediately after the outburst she looks down at her own notes and swallows, then glances at her junior aide as if checking the performance.","10:21 - When the other side floats a package that excludes the missile system, she repeats 'never' loudly, but her shoulders drop and she leans back into the chair.","10:28 - She exchanges a barely visible nod with her trade delegate when the topic shifts to tariff concessions.","10:34 - Asked whether the missile system could be revisited in a later round, she pauses three seconds and then says 'everything is possible in the end' in her normal low voice."],"interrogation_transcript":["Inspector: 'You say the missile system is non-negotiable. Is that a final position?'","Marchetti: 'I said it is the heart of our sovereignty. That is a position, not a price.'","Inspector: 'Your predecessor once described this same system as a chip that could be cashed.'","Marchetti: 'My predecessor was not at this table. I am. We will not trade away our defenses for a tariff.'","Inspector: 'And if we were to make a painful concession of our own, would you still refuse?'","Marchetti: 'A painful concession deserves a painful concession. That is how these agreements are made.'","Inspector: 'So you would reconsider the missile system for the right price.'","Marchetti: 'You are the one who said price. I said reciprocity. But in the end, everything is possible.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Marchetti is bluffing: her theatrical outrage, the post-outburst check-in with her aide, the relaxed shoulders when she repeated 'never', and her final admission that 'everything is possible' reveal that the missile system is a bargaining chip. She is establishing a reference point and invoking reciprocity to force an equally painful concession from the other side, exactly the behavior Kahneman describes.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The voice spike and table slam deviate sharply from her documented flat-tone baseline for genuine red lines, which is the first evidence of staging. The glance at her aide right after the outburst and the relaxed posture during 'never' indicate performance rather than tension. The decisive evidence is her own words: she converts 'non-negotiable' into a reciprocity equation ('a painful concession deserves a painful concession') and then admits 'everything is possible', confirming the attachment was manufactured to anchor the other side.","distractors":["Marchetti is genuinely committed, because her intense emotion and raised voice show authentic investment in the missile system.","Marchetti is lying about whether the missile system exists, which the opposing delegation will later discover.","Marchetti is being pressured by her own military to keep the system, and her anger is frustration at that pressure."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Post-Storm Price Hike and the Exploitation of Market Power","visual_frame_description":"A neighborhood hardware store the morning after a heavy snowstorm. Owner Mr. Delgado stands by a stack of shovels under a large hand-painted $20 sign, ringing up bundled-up customers. An interviewer's camera captures a customer pointing at a faded $15 sticker still visible on the shelf behind the register, while Delgado shrugs and gestures at the crowd waiting at the door.","player_mission":"Audit hardware store owner Mr. Delgado's decision to raise shovel prices from $15 to $20 after a blizzard. Determine whether his justification reflects a legitimate market response or an exploitation of market power that imposes a loss on customers relative to the reference price.","key_concepts":["The existing price sets a reference point that has the nature of an entitlement that must not be infringed.","82% of respondents rated raising shovel prices from $15 to $20 after a large snowstorm as Unfair or Very Unfair.","The standard economic model says the store responds to increased demand by raising its price, but survey participants did not agree.","Customers view the pre-blizzard price as a reference point and the raised price as a loss the store imposes because it can, not because it must.","A basic rule of fairness is that the exploitation of market power to impose losses on others is unacceptable.","Firms that follow unfair pricing policies can expect to lose sales.","Customers who learned from a new catalog that a merchant charged them more than the new reference price reduced future purchases by 15%.","Unfairly imposing losses on people can be risky if the victims are in a position to retaliate.","Fairness concerns impose constraints on profit seeking, which is the overarching question of the chapter's fairness research.","The moral rules by which the public evaluates firms draw a crucial distinction between losses and gains."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests reference-dependent fairness in pricing: customers treat the pre-blizzard $15 price as a reference point, so the hike is read as a loss the store imposes because it can, not because it must. Kahneman, Knetsch, and Thaler found 82% of respondents rated this exact action unfair, even though it matches the standard economic model.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Mr. Delgado's post-blizzard price hike from $15 to $20 a legitimate market response or an unfair exploitation of market power?","subject_baseline":"Mr. Delgado is a 30-year neighborhood hardware merchant who prides himself on knowing customers by name and keeping prices stable season to season. In normal weeks he is chatty and relaxed at the register, and he has never changed a sticker price mid-day. He stocks shovels at a steady wholesale cost that did not change this week.","observed_nonverbal_log":["08:02 - Delgado tapes the new $20 sign over the shovels before the store opens, then stands back and crosses his arms.","08:20 - A regular customer points at the $15 sticker on the shelf; Delgado's eyes dart to the crowd at the door and he shrugs without speaking.","08:24 - Asked whether his wholesale cost changed, he rubs his neck and looks at the floor before answering about 'demand'.","08:31 - He rings up a shovel and, when the customer hesitates, he says 'take it or leave it, there's a line' while gesturing at the door.","08:38 - A customer who paid then turns back and photographs the $15 sticker; Delgado watches but does not object."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'You sold these for $15 yesterday. Why $20 this morning?'","Delgado: 'Simple supply and demand. After a storm like this everybody needs a shovel.'","Interviewer: 'Did your own cost for the shovels go up?'","Delgado: 'Well, you know how it is. When demand goes up, price goes up. That's the market.'","Interviewer: 'So this isn't about your costs at all.'","Delgado: 'I'm not here to run a charity. If people are willing to pay $20, I'll take $20.'","Interviewer: 'Is that fair to your regulars?'","Delgado: 'Fair is what the market will bear. They don't have to buy it, but they will.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The price hike is an unfair exploitation of market power: Mr. Delgado's costs did not change, he raised the price purely because the storm created captive demand, and he thereby imposed a loss on customers relative to the $15 reference price. This is the exact scenario 82% of respondents branded unfair, and it risks retaliatory losses in future sales.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Delgado never cites a cost increase, and his nonverbal behavior at the cost question (neck rub, looking away) confirms there is none; his only justification is demand, which is the definition of exploiting market power. The reference price is anchored by the still-visible $15 sticker, so the hike reads as a loss the store imposes 'because it can, not because it must'. The chapter's survey data deliver the verdict: 82% rated this action unfair, and unfair merchants lose future sales, as the catalog customers' 15% purchase cutback shows.","distractors":["The price hike is fair because Delgado's wholesale costs genuinely rose overnight and he is merely passing through the increase.","The hike is fair because the standard economic model of supply and demand always determines what is fair.","The customer has no grounds to object because the $15 price was only a sticker, not a contractual promise."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why Large-Scale Reforms Fail: Loss Aversion and the Status Quo","visual_frame_description":"A city hall committee room the day a sweeping tax-simplification reform is introduced. The proposal promises to help 85% of taxpayers but eliminates 40 redundant clerk positions and shifts pension accrual for senior employees. In the lobby outside, the clerks and senior employees are organizing a petition, while the silent majority of beneficiaries is nowhere to be seen. A wall chart lists winners and losers under the plan.","player_mission":"You are the reform director for a city government trying to simplify the tax code and reduce costs. Predict how loss aversion will shape the reform's fate, then choose the intervention that gives the reform a realistic chance of surviving intact.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests loss aversion as a conservative force in institutional reform: plans for reform produce many winners and some losers, but potential losers mobilize more actively and determinedly than potential winners, biasing the outcome in their favor and padding the final package with grandfather clauses. The player must apply the asymmetric intensity of loss aversion to predict and steer a real reform's fate.","key_concepts":["As initially conceived, plans for reform almost always produce many winners and some losers while achieving an overall improvement.","If the affected parties have any political influence, potential losers will be more active and determined than potential winners.","The outcome of reform will be biased in favor of the losers and inevitably more expensive and less effective than initially planned.","Reforms commonly include grandfather clauses that protect current stakeholders.","Grandfather clauses can reduce the existing workforce by attrition rather than dismissals, or apply cuts in salaries and benefits only to future workers.","Loss aversion is a powerful conservative force that favors minimal changes from the status quo.","In the world of territorial animals, the owner of a territory almost always wins a challenge, and this principle explains the success of defenders in human affairs.","The existing terms of employment define reference points that have the nature of entitlements.","This conservatism helps keep us stable in our neighborhood, our marriage, and our job.","Loss aversion is the relative strength of two motives: we are driven more strongly to avoid losses than to achieve gains."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests loss aversion as a conservative force in institutional reform: potential losers mobilize more actively and determinedly than potential winners, so reforms get diluted, padded with grandfather clauses, and end up more expensive and less effective than planned. Kahneman links this to territorial defense, where the owner almost always wins a challenge.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"The city's tax-simplification reform would save $12 million a year and help 85% of taxpayers, but it eliminates 40 redundant clerk positions and reduces pension accrual for senior employees. A week into public consultation, the clerks and senior employees have formed a coalition and are petitioning the city council, while the diffuse majority of beneficiaries has organized nothing.","stage_1_question":"Given loss aversion, what is the most likely trajectory of this reform as it moves through the city council?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The reform will be diluted to protect the current stakeholders: potential losers will lobby more actively and determinedly than the silent majority of winners, the package will gain grandfather clauses and exemptions, headcount reductions will shift from dismissals to attrition, and the final result will be more expensive and less effective than the initial plan.","stage_2_intervention":"You must now design the version of the reform most likely to survive. Which approach is grounded in the psychology of reference points and entitlements?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Redesign the reform to grandfather the current stakeholders: reduce headcount through attrition rather than dismissals, and apply the pension accrual changes only to future hires. By protecting the existing employees' reference point and entitlement, you remove the intense loss-aversion motivation that drives the losers' resistance, allowing the overall improvement to pass in a diluted but workable form."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Amygdala's Superfast Threat Response","visual_frame_description":"A university brain-imaging suite at night. A participant lies in an MRI scanner staring at a fixation cross while a researcher monitors a display that flashes two pictures, each shown for less than a hundredth of a second and immediately masked by a random display of dark and bright squares. On the left is a pair of terrified eyes; on the right, the same eyes narrowed by the raised cheeks of a smile. A live trace beside the scanner plots amygdala activation.","player_mission":"You are the research assistant running the masked-threat experiment. Predict which picture will trigger the stronger amygdala response, then select the demonstration that best confirms the same mechanism operates for symbolic threats.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the brain's priority-for-bad-news mechanism: the amygdala, the threat center of the brain, responds intensely to a masked threatening picture the viewer never consciously recognizes, via a superfast neural channel that bypasses the visual cortex supporting conscious seeing. The player must see that this automatic System 1 operation has no comparably rapid counterpart for good news.","key_concepts":["The amygdala has a primary role as the 'threat center' of the brain, although it is also activated in other emotional states.","Images of the brain showed an intense response of the amygdala to a threatening picture that the viewer did not recognize.","The information about the threat probably traveled via a superfast neural channel that bypasses the visual cortex that supports conscious seeing.","None of the observers ever consciously knew that they had seen pictures of eyes.","Schematic angry faces are processed faster and more efficiently than schematic happy faces.","An angry face pops out of a crowd of happy faces, but a single happy face does not stand out in an angry crowd.","The brains of humans and other animals contain a mechanism designed to give priority to bad news.","The automatic operations of System 1 reflect an evolutionary history in which shaving time from predator detection improves the odds of reproducing.","No comparably rapid mechanism for recognizing good news has been detected.","Emotionally loaded bad words such as 'war' and 'crime' attract attention faster than happy words such as 'peace' and 'love'."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the amygdala's superfast threat response: a masked, unrecognized picture of terrified eyes triggers an intense amygdala reaction via a channel that bypasses the visual cortex supporting conscious seeing. Kahneman presents this as the automatic System 1 mechanism that gives priority to bad news, for which no comparable good-news mechanism exists.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"In the scanner bay, the participant reports seeing nothing but the visual-noise mask on every trial. The monitor shows the two eye pictures being flashed: the terrified eyes and the smiling eyes, each for less than one-hundredth of a second and each followed by the mask. The amygdala trace flickers after one of the two pictures.","stage_1_question":"Which picture produces the intense amygdala response, and what does the chapter say about how the information got there?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The terrified eyes produce the intense amygdala response even though the viewer never consciously recognizes seeing them. The threat information travels via a superfast neural channel that feeds directly into emotion-processing regions, bypassing the visual cortex that supports conscious seeing.","stage_2_intervention":"The lab director wants a second demonstration that the same priority-for-bad-news mechanism works for purely symbolic threats. Which demonstration is grounded in the chapter's evidence?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use a word-attention task: show that emotionally loaded bad words such as 'war' and 'crime' attract attention faster than happy words such as 'peace' and 'love'. The chapter states that the mere reminder of a bad event is treated by System 1 as threatening, producing in attenuated form the same reactions as the real thing."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Par Putts vs. Birdie Putts: Loss Aversion on the Green","visual_frame_description":"A professional golf course on the 17th green at dusk. The player stands over a putt, and an overlaid graphic splits the screen between two scenarios: putting for par to avoid a bogey, and putting for a birdie from the same distance. A data panel streams '2.5 million putts analyzed' and a leaderboard shows Tiger Woods at the top of the earnings list. A coach's notes beside the graphic read: failing to make par is a loss; missing a birdie is a foregone gain.","player_mission":"You are a sports-performance analyst advising a tour golfer. Use prospect theory to predict which putt the golfer makes more often, then choose the coaching intervention that best counteracts the loss-aversion gap documented by Pope and Schweitzer.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the loss-aversion prediction of prospect theory applied to professional golf: par is a reference point, so failing to make par is a loss while missing a birdie putt is a foregone gain, and golfers consequently try harder and succeed more often when putting for par. Pope and Schweitzer analyzed more than 2.5 million putts and found a 3.6% advantage for par putts at every distance.","key_concepts":["Par provides a reference point: a birdie is a gain and a bogey is a loss.","According to prospect theory, some strokes count more than others: failing to make par is a loss, while missing a birdie putt is a foregone gain, not a loss.","Pope and Schweitzer analyzed more than 2.5 million putts to test whether loss aversion makes players try harder for par.","Whether the putt was easy or hard, at every distance from the hole, players were more successful when putting for par than for a birdie.","The difference in success when going for par versus a birdie was 3.6%.","If Tiger Woods had putted as well for birdies as for par, his average tournament score would have improved by one stroke.","That one-stroke improvement would have raised Tiger Woods' earnings by almost $1 million per season.","These fierce competitors do not make a conscious decision to slack off on birdie putts; their intense aversion to a bogey contributes to extra concentration.","A reference point can be a goal in the future: not achieving a goal is a loss, exceeding it is a gain.","The aversion to the failure of not reaching a goal is much stronger than the desire to exceed it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests loss aversion applied to professional golf: par is a reference point, so failing to make par is a loss while missing a birdie is a foregone gain, and golfers therefore concentrate harder and succeed more often putting for par. Pope and Schweitzer's analysis of over 2.5 million putts found a 3.6% advantage for par putts at every distance.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"On the 17th green, your client faces two identical-length putts over two rounds: one for par to avoid a bogey, and one for a birdie. The wind, grain, and distance are the same, and the only difference is what each putt means. The data panel reminds you that par provides the baseline for good, but not outstanding, performance.","stage_1_question":"Based on prospect theory, which putt should your client make more often, and what did Pope and Schweitzer find in the real data?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The putt for par should be made more often, because failing to make par is a loss and the aversion to it generates extra concentration, while missing a birdie is only a foregone gain. Pope and Schweitzer found exactly this: golfers were more successful putting for par at every distance, by 3.6%.","stage_2_intervention":"Your client has been missing birdie putts while saving every par. Which coaching instruction is best grounded in the psychology of reference points?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Re-anchor the birdie putt as a loss to avoid rather than a gain to pursue: instruct your client to treat each birdie putt as protecting a reference point, since not achieving a goal is a loss and the aversion to that failure is stronger than the desire to exceed the goal. This counteracts the gap by applying the same loss-aversion concentration that powers the par saves."}]},{"id":"ch34","title":"29. The Fourfold Pattern","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Mapping Behavior onto the Fourfold Pattern","visual_frame_description":"A decision-theory lecture screen showing a 2x2 matrix titled THE FOURFOLD PATTERN. The four cells glow with labels: gains with high probability, gains with low probability, losses with high probability, and losses with low probability. A stack of prospect cards sits beside the matrix: a plaintiff's settlement offer, a lottery ticket, an insurance policy, and a failing business's long-shot bet. The player drags each card toward its cell.","player_mission":"Classify each prospect card into the correct cell of the fourfold pattern by identifying whether it involves gains or losses and whether the probability is high or low. Then confirm the risk attitude prospect theory predicts for each cell.","key_concepts":["In the fourfold pattern, people are risk averse in the top-left cell: gains with a substantial chance to achieve a large gain.","People are willing to accept less than the expected value of a gamble to lock in a sure gain when the gain is probable.","In the top-right cell, people are risk seeking when they face losses with a substantial probability of occurring.","The possibility effect in the bottom-left cell explains why lotteries are popular: gains with a very low probability are overweighted.","The bottom-right cell is where insurance is bought: people pay more than expected value to eliminate a small risk of a large loss.","The top-right cell initially surprised the researchers because they were accustomed to risk aversion except in the bottom-left cell.","Risk seeking with negative prospects was reported by at least two authors before, but the fourfold pattern framework made it easy to interpret.","A lottery ticket is the ultimate example of the possibility effect; people acquire the right to dream pleasantly of winning.","In the top-right cell, people facing very bad options accept a high probability of making things worse in exchange for a small hope of avoiding a large loss.","The fourfold pattern is considered one of the core achievements of prospect theory."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the fourfold pattern of risk attitudes: risk aversion for probable gains, risk seeking for probable losses, and the reverse for improbable outcomes driven by the possibility effect. Kahneman and Tversky considered the pattern one of the core achievements of prospect theory, with the top-right cell a new and unexpected finding.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Into which cell of the fourfold pattern does each prospect belong, and what risk attitude does prospect theory predict?","column_a_label":"Top-left: gains with substantial probability (risk averse)","column_b_label":"Bottom-left: gains with very low probability (risk seeking; possibility effect)","column_c_label":"Losses: probable losses drive risk seeking, improbable losses drive risk aversion","rows":[{"label":"A plaintiff with a strong case (95% chance to win) accepts a settlement of 90% of the claim rather than risk trial.","col":"a"},{"label":"A person buys a lottery ticket for a minuscule chance of a huge prize.","col":"b"},{"label":"A defendant with a weak case refuses an unfavorable settlement and gambles on trial.","col":"c"},{"label":"A homeowner pays more than expected value for insurance against an unlikely disaster.","col":"c"},{"label":"A person offered $9,500 for sure or a 95% chance of $10,000 chooses the sure amount.","col":"a"},{"label":"A failing business wastes its remaining assets on a long-shot bet to catch a superior technology.","col":"c"},{"label":"Ticket buyers are indifferent to whether their chance of winning is tiny or merely small.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["A plaintiff with a strong case is risk seeking because she has already almost won the case.","Insurance is a form of gambling, so it belongs in the risk-seeking cell for losses with low probability.","A defendant with a weak case is risk averse because he faces a large probable loss and must be careful."],"explanation":"The top-left rows show gains with substantial probability, where the certainty of the sure amount beats the gamble and the plaintiff becomes risk averse, settling below expected value. The bottom-left rows show low-probability gains, where the possibility effect overweights the chance and lottery tickets are bought. The loss rows show the surprise of the pattern: a probable loss drives the defendant and failing business to be risk seeking, while an improbable loss drives the homeowner to buy expensive insurance."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Certainty Effect, Possibility Effect, and the Expectation Principle","visual_frame_description":"A probability-scale control panel stretching from 0% to 100%. Two zones glow at the extremes: a red region near 0% labeled POSSIBILITY and a green region near 100% labeled CERTAINTY, with the middle band dimmed. A table of measured decision weights sits beside the scale, showing 2% mapped to 8.1 and a note that a 2% risk of not winning cuts utility from 100 to 87.1. Cards describing gambles, settlements, and insurance decisions wait to be sorted.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the weighting mechanism that explains it: the possibility effect, the certainty effect, or the expectation principle. Drag all seven cards into the correct column, then confirm your sorting.","key_concepts":["Decision weights are not identical to probabilities, contrary to the expectation principle.","Improbable outcomes are overweighted, which is the possibility effect.","Outcomes that are almost certain are underweighted relative to actual certainty, which is the certainty effect.","The decision weight corresponding to a 2% chance is 8.1, so the rare event is overweighted by a factor of about 4.","A 2% risk of not winning reduces the utility of a gamble by 13%, from 100 to 87.1.","Increasing the chances from 0% to 5% is a qualitative change that creates a hope of winning the prize.","The improvement from 95% to 100% is another qualitative change that has a large impact.","The expectation principle, by which values are weighted by their probability, is poor psychology.","Decision weights are identical to probabilities at the extremes: 0 when impossible and 100 when a sure thing.","The range of probabilities between 5% and 95% is associated with a much smaller range of decision weights, about two-thirds as much as rationally expected."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the two end-point distortions of decision weighting: the possibility effect overweights unlikely outcomes, while the certainty effect underweights near-certain outcomes relative to true certainty. Kahneman and Tversky measured decision weights showing a 2% chance weighted at 8.1 and a 2% risk of failure reducing utility by 13%.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which weighting mechanism explains each observation: the possibility effect, the certainty effect, or the expectation principle?","column_a_label":"Possibility effect (unlikely outcomes overweighted)","column_b_label":"Certainty effect (near-certain outcomes underweighted relative to certainty)","column_c_label":"Expectation principle (weight proportional to probability)","rows":[{"label":"A structured-settlement firm offers $910,000 outright for a claim with a 95% chance of a $1 million verdict, and many clients accept.","col":"b"},{"label":"Improving chances from 0% to 5% feels like a qualitative change because it creates a hope of winning.","col":"a"},{"label":"A 2% chance of winning a prize is weighted at 8.1 in people's decisions, about four times its probability.","col":"a"},{"label":"A 2% risk of not winning reduces a sure prize's utility by 13%, from 100 to 87.1.","col":"b"},{"label":"The utility of a gamble is the average of the utilities of its outcomes, each weighted by its probability.","col":"c"},{"label":"People buy lottery tickets in vast amounts, paying far more than expected value for very small chances.","col":"a"},{"label":"The improvement from a 95% to a 100% chance feels more impressive than the same 5% improvement from 60% to 65%.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The structured-settlement offer reflects the possibility effect because the chance of winning is small.","A 2% chance weighted at 8.1 shows that people underweight rare events relative to their probability.","The expectation principle correctly describes how people think about probabilities in risky prospects."],"explanation":"The certainty-effect rows share the same signature: people treat the gap between almost-sure and sure as enormous, so they accept a $910,000 offer against a $950,000 expectation and feel a 2% risk of failure as a 13% loss of value. The possibility-effect rows show rare outcomes being overweighted, from the 8.1 weight on a 2% chance to the qualitative hope created by moving from 0% to 5%. The expectation-principle row states the rational benchmark that the measured decision weights violate."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Allais's Paradox and the Axioms of Rational Choice","visual_frame_description":"A 1952 Paris conference room where a blackboard displays two choice problems. Problem A offers a 61% chance of $520,000 versus a 63% chance of $500,000; Problem B offers a 98% chance of $520,000 versus a 100% chance of $500,000. Seated economists including Samuelson, Arrow, Friedman, and Savage are shown making their selections, and an urn-diagram beside the board explains how the two problems are related.","player_mission":"Classify each element of the Allais paradox into the principle that explains it: the certainty effect, the expectation principle, or the possibility effect. Sort all seven cards, then confirm your reading of the paradox.","key_concepts":["In problem A, most people prefer a 61% chance to win $520,000 over a 63% chance to win $500,000.","In problem B, a large majority prefers the 100% chance of $500,000 over the 98% chance of $520,000.","The two urns of problem B are more favorable versions of the urns of problem A, with 37 white marbles replaced by red winning marbles in each.","The pattern of choices does not make logical sense, but the certainty effect provides a psychological explanation.","The 2% difference between a 100% and a 98% chance in problem B is vastly more impressive than the same 2% difference between 63% and 61% in problem A.","Von Neumann and Morgenstern proved that any weighting of uncertain outcomes that is not strictly proportional to probability leads to inconsistencies.","Allais intended to show that his distinguished audience was susceptible to a certainty effect and therefore violated expected utility theory.","The sophisticated participants at the meeting did not notice that their preferences violated utility theory until Allais drew their attention to it.","Amos called theorists who tried to rationalize violations of utility theory 'lawyers for the misguided'.","Kahneman and Tversky retained utility theory as a logic of rational choice but abandoned the idea that people are perfectly rational choosers."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the certainty effect through Allais's paradox: people prefer a 61% chance of $520,000 over a 63% chance of $500,000, but then prefer a guaranteed $500,000 over a 98% chance of $520,000, reversing preferences that are logically equivalent. Kahneman describes how this reversal violates the axioms of expected utility theory established by von Neumann and Morgenstern.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which principle explains each element of the Allais paradox: the certainty effect, the expectation principle, or the possibility effect?","column_a_label":"Certainty effect (the 2% gap between 98% and 100% is treated as vastly more impressive than the same gap between 61% and 63%)","column_b_label":"Expectation principle (utility is the probability-weighted average of outcome utilities)","column_c_label":"Possibility effect (a change from 0% to 5% creates hope and is a qualitative transformation)","rows":[{"label":"In problem A most people prefer a 61% chance of $520,000 over a 63% chance of $500,000, yet in problem B they prefer the guaranteed $500,000 over a 98% chance of $520,000.","col":"a"},{"label":"The urns of problem B are more favorable versions of the urns of problem A, with 37 white marbles replaced by red, yet preferences flip.","col":"a"},{"label":"A rational chooser who prefers the left urn in problem A must also prefer the improved left urn in problem B.","col":"b"},{"label":"The utility of a gamble is the average of the utilities of its outcomes, each weighted by its probability.","col":"b"},{"label":"The large impact of improving chances from 0% to 5% illustrates the possibility effect.","col":"c"},{"label":"Buyers of lottery tickets pay much more than expected value for very small chances to win a large prize.","col":"c"},{"label":"The economists at the 1952 Paris meeting did not notice their preferences violated utility theory until Allais drew their attention to it.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The preference reversal in the Allais problems follows directly from the expectation principle.","The two problems are unrelated, so there is no inconsistency in choosing differently.","The reversal is explained by the possibility effect because the chances of winning are low."],"explanation":"The reversal rows are explained by the certainty effect: the 2% difference between 100% and 98% in problem B feels vastly more impressive than the same 2% difference between 63% and 61% in problem A, so people trade the slightly better prize for the guarantee. The expectation-principle rows state the rational benchmark the reversal violates, and the possibility-effect rows describe separate lottery behavior at the low end of the probability scale."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Structured-Settlement Pitch and the Certainty Effect","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled office of a risk-adjustment company. Across a polished table, an advisor slides a single-page offer toward Marta, a woman in her forties: $910,000 outright for her contested $1 million inheritance claim. Her lawyer's memo beside her says she has a 95% chance to win, and the ruling is expected tomorrow. The advisor leans back with a practiced smile as the camera catches Marta glancing repeatedly between the offer and the memo.","player_mission":"Audit the risk-adjustment company's offer to Marta, who faces a 95% chance of winning her case tomorrow. Determine whether the offer of $910,000 is a fair deal or a deliberate exploitation of the certainty effect that charges her for removing a 5% risk.","key_concepts":["A 95% chance to win is not the same as certainty, and the psychological difference between 95% and 100% is the certainty effect.","The risk-adjustment company offers to buy the case for $910,000 outright, which is $40,000 below the expected value of $950,000.","Outcomes that are almost certain are given less weight than their probability justifies.","A large industry of structured settlements exists to provide certainty at a hefty price by taking advantage of the certainty effect.","Two emotions drive the plaintiff: the attraction of a sure and substantial gain, and the fear of intense disappointment and regret if she rejects a settlement and loses in court.","The plaintiff with a strong case is likely to be risk averse.","People are willing to pay far more than expected value to eliminate risk altogether.","The anxiety of a 1% risk of not winning appears to be more salient than the hope in a 1% chance to win.","Insurance companies cover their costs and make profits because people buy more than protection; they purchase peace of mind.","Systematic deviations from expected value are likely to be costly in the long run."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the certainty effect in commerce: the structured-settlement firm offers $910,000 for a claim with a 95% chance of a $1 million verdict, $40,000 below expected value, and profits because eliminating the residual 5% risk feels enormously valuable. Kahneman notes a large industry exists to provide certainty at a hefty price by taking advantage of the certainty effect.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is the risk-adjustment company's offer of $910,000 a fair deal for Marta's claim, or does it exploit the certainty effect by charging a premium for removing a 5% risk?","subject_baseline":"Marta is a cautious, financially conservative school principal who has never litigated before and has a strong case: her lawyer cites expert opinion that she has a 95% chance to win outright, while reminding her that judicial decisions are never perfectly predictable. The ruling is expected tomorrow. She is risk-averse in her investments and carries comprehensive insurance on everything she owns.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02 - Marta reads the memo twice, then the offer once, and her lips press together at the $910,000 figure.","14:05 - When the advisor says 'the judge could go the other way tomorrow,' Marta's eyes widen and she grips the memo.","14:07 - She asks twice what happens if she waits, and each time looks at the door as if weighing escape.","14:09 - The advisor emphasizes 'you could walk out with nothing'; Marta exhales slowly and her shoulders drop.","14:12 - She signs the offer, then immediately asks whether she can change her mind, and is told no."],"interrogation_transcript":["Advisor: 'You have a 95% chance to win tomorrow. Take the money today and none of that matters.'","Marta: 'Ninety-five percent is almost certain. Why would I give up $40,000?'","Advisor: 'Because almost certain is not certain. There is a real chance you walk out with nothing.'","Marta: 'The expected value of waiting is $950,000. You are offering less.'","Advisor: 'Expected value is what a statistician says. You get one ruling, not a thousand repetitions.'","Marta: 'But the chance of losing is only 5%.'","Advisor: 'Five percent is the difference between certainty and doubt. You are paying to stop worrying.'","Marta: 'So you profit from my fear of the 5%.'","Advisor: 'We profit from selling certainty. You profit from sleeping tonight.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The offer deliberately exploits the certainty effect: at $910,000 it sits $40,000 below the $950,000 expected value, and the entire pitch is engineered to amplify the residual 5% risk so that Marta pays a premium to eliminate it. Accepting reflects the risk aversion of a plaintiff with a strong case, exactly the behavior the fourfold pattern predicts.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The advisor's own words name the mechanism: 'you are paying to stop worrying' and 'we profit from selling certainty'. The $40,000 gap between the offer and expected value is the certainty premium, charged because the jump from 95% to 100% feels like a qualitative transformation. Marta's visible distress at the 'walk out with nothing' framing, her repeated glance at the door, and her immediate regret after signing all confirm that the certainty effect, not fair valuation, drove her decision.","distractors":["The offer is generous because it is only slightly below the expected value and saves Marta from trial costs.","Marta is being irrational to worry, because a 95% chance to win is effectively certain and the offer should be rejected without emotion.","The advisor is lying about the 95% probability, which is actually much lower than stated."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Willingness to Pay to Eliminate Risk and the Psychology of Worry","visual_frame_description":"A consumer-safety focus group room where parents of small children review a household insect-spray purchase. A moderator shows them a panel: the current spray causes 15 inhalation poisonings and 15 child poisonings per 10,000 bottles; a more expensive alternative cuts each risk to 5. A salesman for the premium spray stands to the side, urging the parents to eliminate the risk completely, and a price-tag graphic illustrates their average willingness to pay.","player_mission":"Audit the premium-insecticide sales pitch against the parents' actual valuation data. Determine whether the large premium paid to eliminate risk entirely reflects rational risk valuation or the overweighting of small probabilities driven by the psychology of worry.","key_concepts":["Parents were willing to pay an additional $2.38, on average, to reduce the risks from 15 per 10,000 bottles to 5.","They were willing to pay $8.09, more than three times as much, to eliminate the risk completely.","The parents treated the two risks (inhalation and child poisoning) as separate worries.","They were willing to pay a certainty premium for the complete elimination of either risk.","This premium is compatible with the psychology of worry but not with the rational model.","Because of the possibility effect, the worry is not proportional to the probability of the threat.","Reducing or mitigating the risk is not adequate; to eliminate the worry the probability must be brought down to zero.","When you pay attention to a threat, you worry, and the decision weights reflect how much you worry.","Overweighting of small probabilities increases the attractiveness of both gambles and insurance policies.","People are willing to pay far more than expected value to eliminate risk altogether."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the possibility effect and the psychology of worry in consumer risk valuation: parents paid $2.38 to reduce poison risks by two-thirds but $8.09, more than three times as much, to eliminate them completely. Kahneman notes this certainty premium is compatible with the psychology of worry but not with the rational model.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Does the parents' willingness to pay $8.09 to eliminate a risk they would pay only $2.38 to reduce reflect rational valuation, or the overweighting of small probabilities driven by worry?","subject_baseline":"The parents are typically budget-conscious shoppers who compare unit prices and clip coupons. They currently buy the $10-per-bottle spray and have never purchased the premium brand. In the study, each parent was asked how much they would be willing to pay for the safer product, after being told their household uses the spray regularly around small children.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:02 - When the moderator reads the phrase '15 child poisonings', several parents sit forward and stop taking notes.","10:06 - The salesman says 'even 5 poisonings is too many'; a mother nods and writes a figure without re-checking her budget notes.","10:11 - Asked how they would value a two-thirds reduction, parents consult the price list; the average they state clusters near $2.38.","10:14 - Asked to value complete elimination, parents give figures far above their reduction valuations without recalculating the odds.","10:18 - One father states he would 'pay anything to get it to zero', though he earlier declined a $3 price increase for a different product."],"interrogation_transcript":["Moderator: 'You would pay $2.38 to cut the risk from 15 to 5 per 10,000 bottles. What would you pay to eliminate it entirely?'","Parent: 'Maybe eight or nine dollars. Honestly, I'd pay whatever it takes to make it zero.'","Moderator: 'That is more than three times the amount you would pay to cut the risk by two-thirds.'","Parent: 'I know. But cutting it isn't the same as it being gone. If there is any chance my child gets poisoned, I can't relax.'","Moderator: 'The difference in risk is small relative to the difference in price.'","Parent: 'It's not about the odds. It's about knowing it can't happen at all.'","Moderator: 'Is that a rational price?'","Parent: 'It's what I would pay. Worry isn't rational, but it's real.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The $8.09 willingness to pay reflects the overweighting of a small probability driven by the psychology of worry, not rational risk valuation: the parent admits 'worry isn't rational, but it's real'. Because of the possibility effect, eliminating the risk entirely (bringing it to zero) is the only thing that removes the worry, which is why it commands more than three times the premium of a two-thirds reduction.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The decisive evidence is the stated logic of the parent: 'cutting it isn't the same as it being gone' and 'knowing it can't happen at all'. This matches the chapter's mechanism exactly: to eliminate the worry the probability must be brought down to zero, and the possibility effect makes worry disproportionate to the probability. The contrast between the $2.38 reduction valuation and the $8.09 elimination valuation is the certainty premium, compatible with the psychology of worry but not with the rational model of proportional risk pricing.","distractors":["The parents are paying rationally because a two-thirds reduction and complete elimination are equally valuable relative to the risk removed.","The salesman has provided independent laboratory evidence that the premium spray eliminates all poisonings, justifying the price.","The $8.09 figure is a statistical artifact of the survey format, not evidence about how parents actually value risk."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Desperate Gamble in the Top-Right Cell","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom at a struggling electronics firm whose product has been outclassed by a superior technology. The CEO stands before a decision slide: invest the company's remaining assets in a long-shot redesign, or accept a sale that guarantees a modest but certain return. An analyst's overlay labels the situation: probable loss, small hope of complete relief. The lights are dim and the mood is tense as the board waits.","player_mission":"You are the board's outside advisor watching the leadership consider a desperate gamble. Use the fourfold pattern to explain why the gamble feels so attractive, then choose the recommendation that best reflects the chapter's warning about risk taking in the domain of losses.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests risk seeking in the domain of losses, the top-right cell of the fourfold pattern: when people face very bad options, they take desperate gambles, accepting a high probability of making things worse in exchange for a small hope of avoiding a large loss. The player must see why this gamble feels attractive and why risk taking of this kind often turns manageable failures into disasters.","key_concepts":["The top-right cell of the fourfold pattern is where people who face very bad options take desperate gambles.","Risk seeking in the domain of losses means accepting a high probability of making things worse in exchange for a small hope of avoiding a large loss.","Diminishing sensitivity makes the sure loss very aversive because the reaction to a loss of $900 is more than 90% as intense as the reaction to a loss of $1,000.","The decision weight that corresponds to a probability of 90% is only about 71, much lower than the probability.","The certainty effect reduces the aversiveness of the gamble, making the sure loss look worse by comparison.","Risk taking of this kind often turns manageable failures into disasters.","The thought of accepting the large sure loss is too painful, and the hope of complete relief too enticing, to make the sensible decision to cut one's losses.","This is where businesses that are losing ground to a superior technology waste their remaining assets in futile attempts to catch up.","Because defeat is so difficult to accept, the losing side in wars often fights long past the point at which victory of the other side is certain.","The top-right cell of the fourfold pattern was a new and unexpected finding."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests risk seeking in the domain of losses: when people face a probable loss, the sure loss is repugnant and the small hope of complete relief is enticing, so they accept a high probability of making things worse. Kahneman shows this top-right cell of the fourfold pattern is where failing businesses waste their remaining assets and wars are fought long past the point of certain defeat.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"The electronics firm has two options: accept a $20 million buyout that guarantees survival at a smaller scale, or spend its remaining $30 million on a redesign that analysts give a 15% chance of beating the superior technology. The CEO argues that the buyout is 'the same as failing', while the redesign offers a real chance to win the market back. You watch the board weigh a sure loss against a long-shot hope.","stage_1_question":"According to the fourfold pattern, which option will the leadership be strongly drawn to, and what are the two psychological forces making it attractive?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The leadership will be strongly drawn to the desperate redesign gamble, because they are in the top-right cell facing a probable loss. The gamble feels attractive for two reasons: diminishing sensitivity makes the sure $20 million loss very aversive (a $900 loss feels more than 90% as intense as a $1,000 loss), and the certainty effect reduces the aversiveness of the gamble since the decision weight for a 90% probability is only about 71.","stage_2_intervention":"The board asks you to recommend a course of action. Which recommendation is best grounded in the chapter's analysis of the top-right cell?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recommend accepting the sure loss and cutting losses now, explicitly naming the trap: the painful sure loss and the enticing hope of complete relief are exactly what drive businesses in this cell to turn manageable failures into disasters. Evaluate the gamble in expected-value terms rather than by its emotional relief, because risk taking in the top-right cell typically makes things worse."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The City of New York's Frivolous-Suits Policy and the Long-Run Cost of Overweighting","visual_frame_description":"A legal-policy office at New York City Hall. A wall screen displays the city's litigation portfolio: 200 'frivolous' suits this year, each with a 5% chance to cost the city $1 million, and each settleable now for $100,000. Two policy columns glow on the screen: SETTLE ALL and GO TO TRIAL, with projected totals beneath them. A deputy counsel's calculator sits beside the numbers.","player_mission":"You are a deputy counsel for the City of New York deciding between two blanket policies for handling 200 frivolous suits. Compute the expected total loss under each policy and select the option that minimizes the city's long-run cost, then explain why the intuitive choice is the costly one.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the long-run cost of systematic deviations from expected value: consistent overweighting of improbable outcomes, a feature of intuitive decision making, eventually leads to inferior outcomes. The player must compute the city's arithmetic and resist the case-by-case urge to buy insurance against small risks of large losses.","key_concepts":["The City of New York faces 200 frivolous suits each year, each with a 5% chance to cost $1 million.","In each case the city could settle the lawsuit for a payment of $100,000.","If the city litigates all 200 cases, it will lose 10, for a total loss of $10 million.","If the city settles every case for $100,000, its total loss will be $20 million.","Paying a premium to avoid a small risk of a large loss is costly when the decision is repeated many times.","Consistent overweighting of improbable outcomes, a feature of intuitive decision making, eventually leads to inferior outcomes.","Systematic deviations from expected value are costly in the long run.","The rule applies to both risk aversion and risk seeking.","In a frivolous suit, the defendant's settlement is equivalent to purchasing insurance against the unlikely event of a bad verdict.","The decisions described by the fourfold pattern are not obviously unreasonable, but in the long run they are costly."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the long-run cost of systematic deviations from expected value: consistent overweighting of improbable outcomes eventually leads to inferior outcomes. Kahneman's arithmetic shows the City of New York loses $10 million if it litigates all 200 frivolous suits but $20 million if it settles every one, because settling is a premium paid to avoid small risks of large losses.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"The city faces 200 frivolous suits this year. Each has a 5% chance to cost the city $1 million if it goes to trial, and each could be settled today for $100,000. Two policies are on the table: settle every case, or litigate every case. Legal costs are ignored for simplicity, and a deputy counsel notes that the defendant in any single suit is inclined to settle to buy insurance against an unlikely bad verdict.","stage_1_question":"Which policy minimizes the city's total expected loss, and by how much?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Litigating every case minimizes the loss: the city will lose about 10 of the 200 cases for a total of $10 million, whereas settling every case for $100,000 costs $20 million. The settlement policy spends $10 million more because it is a premium paid in every case to avoid a small risk of a large loss.","stage_2_intervention":"A senior official proposes settling every suit because 'one freak verdict could bankrupt us'. Which response is best grounded in the chapter's lesson about long-run expected value?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reject the blanket-settlement policy and explain that settling every case is costly precisely because the city faces many similar decisions: consistent overweighting of improbable outcomes leads to inferior outcomes in the long run, and the rule applies to both risk aversion and risk seeking. The single-case fear of a freak verdict is the overweighting the arithmetic shows the city cannot afford."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Gambling in the Shadow of the Law: Plaintiff vs. Defendant","visual_frame_description":"A civil-court conference room where a plaintiff and a defendant discuss a settlement before trial. On one wall, a graphic splits the fourfold pattern into the two cells at play: the plaintiff's strong case sits in the top-left risk-averse cell, while the defendant's weak case sits in the top-right risk-seeking cell. A second panel shows a frivolous suit, where the plaintiff's flimsy case drops into the bottom-left lottery cell and the defendant moves to the bottom-right insurance cell.","player_mission":"You are a legal-practice analyst applying Chris Guthrie's fourfold-pattern analysis to settlement negotiations. Determine which party holds the stronger hand in each scenario, and identify which cell of the pattern each party occupies.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the fourfold pattern in litigation, applying Chris Guthrie's analysis: a plaintiff with a strong case is risk averse and settles low, while a defendant with a weak case is risk seeking and holds the stronger hand, and these postures reverse for frivolous claims. The player must map each party onto the correct cell and predict how the settlement will deviate from the expected trial outcome.","key_concepts":["A plaintiff with a strong case is likely to be risk averse and settle below the expected value of the trial.","Two emotions drive the strong-case plaintiff: the attraction of a sure gain and the fear of intense disappointment and regret if she rejects a settlement and loses.","A defendant with a weak case is likely to be risk seeking, prepared to gamble rather than accept a very unfavorable settlement.","In the face-off between a risk-averse plaintiff and a risk-seeking defendant, the defendant holds the stronger hand.","The superior bargaining position of the defendant should be reflected in settlements below the statistically expected outcome of the trial.","This prediction was confirmed by experiments with law students and practicing judges, and by analyses of actual negotiations.","For a plaintiff with a flimsy case, the claim is a lottery ticket for a large prize, and overweighting the small chance of success leads to bold and aggressive negotiation.","For the defendant in a frivolous suit, settling for a modest amount is equivalent to purchasing insurance against an unlikely bad verdict.","Plaintiffs with frivolous claims are likely to obtain a more generous settlement than the statistics of the situation justify.","The plaintiff is willing to gamble and the defendant wants to be safe in a frivolous suit, the reverse of a strong case."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the fourfold pattern in litigation, applying the pattern Chris Guthrie drew for plaintiffs and defendants: a strong-case plaintiff is risk averse and settles low, while a weak-case defendant is risk seeking and holds the stronger hand, and these postures reverse for frivolous claims. Kahneman reports the predictions were confirmed with law students, judges, and actual negotiations.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"stage_1_observation":"In the strong-case scenario, the plaintiff's lawyer cites expert opinion of a 95% chance to win and urges accepting a settlement of 90% of the claim. The plaintiff hesitates, imagining the regret of rejecting a settlement and losing in court. Across the table, the defendant, who is likely to lose, refuses to settle and insists on taking the case to a jury. You observe the defendant's counsel repeating that the verdict is never certain.","stage_1_question":"Which party holds the stronger hand in this face-off, and which cell of the fourfold pattern does each occupy?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The defendant holds the stronger hand. The plaintiff occupies the top-left cell and is risk averse, anxious about the small chance of getting nothing and the regret of losing after rejecting a settlement, so she will accept less than expected value. The defendant occupies the top-right cell and is risk seeking, preferring the gamble of trial to the repugnant sure loss, which is why the plaintiff settles below the statistically expected outcome.","stage_2_intervention":"The next file on your desk is a frivolous suit: the plaintiff has a flimsy case that is most likely to fail, yet the plaintiff's lawyers are negotiating aggressively, while the defendant's counsel wants to settle quietly for a modest amount. Which parties hold which postures, and what settlement should you expect?","stage_2_correct_answer":"The postures reverse: the flimsy-case plaintiff occupies the bottom-left lottery cell and is bold and aggressive, overweighting the small chance of a large verdict, while the defendant occupies the bottom-right insurance cell and is risk averse, settling to buy protection against an unlikely bad verdict. The expected result is that the plaintiff with a frivolous claim obtains a more generous settlement than the statistics of the situation justify."}]},{"id":"ch35","title":"30. Rare Events","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Overestimation vs Overweighting of Rare Events","visual_frame_description":"Priya, a behavioral risk analyst, sits in a seminar room where a projection shows the results of Craig Fox's NBA playoffs study. Eight team logos line the whiteboard with probability estimates written beneath them that sum to 240 percent, and a cash-equivalent ledger shows bets totaling $287 against a maximum payoff of $160. Her colleague Marcus points at the board and asks her to separate judgments of probability from decisions that put money on the line.","player_mission":"Study the evidence rows and classify each as an example of overestimation, overweighting, or neglect. Then identify the single bias that best explains why basketball fans were willing to bet far more than the tournament could ever pay out.","key_concepts":["People overestimate the probabilities of unlikely events.","People overweight unlikely events in their decisions.","Overestimation and overweighting are distinct phenomena driven by the same mechanisms: focused attention, confirmation bias, and cognitive ease.","In Fox's study, fans' successive probability estimates for the eight NBA playoff teams summed to 240 percent.","The absurdity disappeared when the same judges assessed whether the winner would come from the Eastern or Western conference, because the focal event and its alternative were equally specific and their judgments summed to 100 percent.","The probability of a rare event is most likely to be overestimated when its alternative is not fully specified.","Fans assigned cash equivalents to bets on the eight teams that summed to $287, while a winning bet would pay at most $160.","An average participant who took all eight bets would be guaranteed a loss of $127.","The pattern sheds new light on the planning fallacy and other manifestations of optimism, because successful execution of a plan is specific and easy to imagine while failure is diffuse.","Entrepreneurs and the investors who evaluate their prospects are prone both to overestimate their chances and to overweight their estimates.","Rare events are overweighted when they specifically attract attention, as when prospects are described explicitly.","Decision weight drives choices, whereas probability judgment is an assessment of likelihood, and the two can diverge."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between overestimation of a rare event's probability and overweighting of the rare event in decisions: both spring from focused attention, confirmation bias, and cognitive ease, but only overweighting moves actual choices. Kahneman shows that Fox's basketball fans overestimated the eight teams' chances (estimates summing to 240%) and simultaneously overweighted them in bets whose cash equivalents guaranteed an average loss of $127.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Basketball fans in Fox's study assigned cash equivalents to bets on each of the eight NBA playoff teams; the values summed to $287, while a winning bet could pay at most $160. Which bias does this betting behavior best demonstrate?","column_a_label":"Overestimation of a rare event's probability","column_b_label":"Overweighting of a rare event in decisions","column_c_label":"Neglect of a rare event (underweighting)","rows":[{"label":"Fans' successive probability estimates for the eight teams summed to 240 percent.","col":"a"},{"label":"Fans assigned cash equivalents totaling $287 for bets whose payoff could not exceed $160.","col":"b"},{"label":"Most Californians, having never felt a major earthquake, remain underprepared for quake risk.","col":"c"},{"label":"Israeli travelers avoided buses during the bombing period despite a negligible objective risk.","col":"b"},{"label":"Judging the chance a newborn leaves the hospital within three days by imagining medical complications that could delay discharge.","col":"a"},{"label":"In choice from experience, with outcomes sampled across repeated trials, rare events are underweighted.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The fans were ignoring the number of nonwinning teams, a denominator neglect error.","The fans treated each bet as if it were a sure thing, a certainty effect.","The fans had learned from repeated betting experience and so underweighted the rare win."],"explanation":"The betting cash equivalents reflect decision weight, not judged probability: fans put money on the events they focused on even though the combined stakes guaranteed a loss, which is overweighting. The summed 240 percent estimates illustrate overestimation, the Israeli bus avoidance illustrates overweighting of a salient threat, and the California example illustrates neglect."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Possibility Effect, Certainty Effect, and Denominator Neglect","visual_frame_description":"In a campus psychology lab, a teaching assistant lays out two urns on a table: a small urn holding ten marbles with a single red one and a large urn holding one hundred marbles with eight red ones. A student, Tomas, stares at the eight red marbles in the large urn and says they look more hopeful. Beside him a poster shows the phrase '1,286 out of every 10,000' next to the phrase '24.14 percent,' and a note in the margin asks which formulation feels more dangerous.","player_mission":"Classify each evidence item into the mechanism it demonstrates: the possibility effect, the certainty effect, or denominator neglect. Then identify the mechanism that best explains why roughly 30 to 40 percent of students pick the 100-marble urn despite its worse odds.","key_concepts":["About 30 to 40 percent of students choose the urn with the larger number of winning marbles rather than the urn that provides a better chance of winning.","The tendency to focus on the winning marbles and neglect the number of nonwinning marbles is called denominator neglect.","Denominator neglect helps explain why different ways of communicating risk vary so much in their effects.","A risk stated as 'a 0.001 percent risk of permanent disability' appears small, while 'one of 100,000 vaccinated children will be permanently disabled' summons the image of an individual child.","Low-probability events are much more heavily weighted when described as relative frequencies than when described as chances, risk, or probability.","A disease that kills 1,286 people out of every 10,000 is judged more dangerous than one that kills 24.14 percent of the population, though the first risk is only half as large.","The possibility effect refers to the overweighting of highly unlikely outcomes, where only possibility matters.","The certainty effect refers to the overweighting of sure and nearly sure outcomes, which are also represented vividly.","When you hold a vivid image of an event, the possibility of its not occurring is also represented vividly and overweighted.","The enhanced possibility effect combined with the enhanced certainty effect leaves little room for decision weights to change between chances of 21 percent and 84 percent.","For the sight of a bus, the vivid image of a bomb creates an impulse for protective action even though the probability is tiny."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the mechanisms behind overweighting of rare events: the possibility effect, which makes mere possibility outweigh tiny probabilities, and denominator neglect, which makes people count winning marbles while ignoring nonwinners. Kahneman argues these mechanisms, plus the certainty effect, explain why a 10-percent chance can lose to an 8-percent chance when the winning image is more vivid.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Students choose between Urn A (1 red marble out of 10) and Urn B (8 red marbles out of 100), and about 30 to 40 percent pick Urn B even though its 8 percent chance is worse than the 10 percent chance of Urn A. Which mechanism best explains the attraction to the larger urn?","column_a_label":"Possibility effect","column_b_label":"Certainty effect","column_c_label":"Denominator neglect","rows":[{"label":"Students choose the 100-marble urn with 8 red winning marbles over the 10-marble urn with 1 red marble, despite worse odds.","col":"c"},{"label":"A lottery ticket is worth buying because the thrilling possibility of the big prize dominates, though the odds are minuscule.","col":"a"},{"label":"A 90 percent chance framed as '10 of 100 marbles are losers' makes the loss event more salient than '1 of 10 marbles.'","col":"b"},{"label":"People judge 'a disease that kills 1,286 of every 10,000' as more dangerous than one that kills 24.14 percent of the population.","col":"c"},{"label":"Holding a vivid image of winning roses also represents the possibility of not winning vividly, shrinking the decision-weight gap between 21 percent and 84 percent.","col":"b"},{"label":"The bus-side image of a bomb creates an impulse for protective action even though the probability is extremely small.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["The students were weighting the possibilities of each urn equally because only possibility matters.","The students treated the 8 percent chance as a near-certainty because it was described with a round number.","The students were neglecting the probability entirely and choosing based on the total number of marbles."],"explanation":"The larger urn wins because attention is drawn to the eight winning red marbles while the 92 nonwinning marbles fade into an indistinct background, so the count of nonwinners is not assessed with equal care. This is denominator neglect, the same mechanism that makes '1,286 of every 10,000' feel more dangerous than '24.14 percent.'"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Choice from Description vs Choice from Experience","visual_frame_description":"In a research cubicle, a participant faces two buttons on a console. On one trial the buttons are described in writing ('5% to win $12' versus '95% chance to win $1'); on another trial the participant presses the buttons repeatedly and watches the payoffs accumulate across trials. A wall chart in the lab contrasts the two conditions and notes that the results diverge sharply. In a corner of the screen, two colleagues, Adele and Brian, are pictured next to a question about whom to ask for help.","player_mission":"Sort each item into choice from description, choice from experience, or judgment from global impressions. Then identify which mechanism best explains why a person would pick Adele over Brian despite Brian's rare flashes of extraordinary generosity.","key_concepts":["Choice from description is implemented by showing the subject the verbal description of the risky prospect and asking for a choice.","Choice from experience is implemented by exposing the participant to many trials in which she observes the consequences of pressing one button or another.","As expected from prospect theory, choice from description yields a possibility effect: rare outcomes are overweighted relative to their probability.","In sharp contrast, overweighting is never observed in choice from experience, and underweighting is common.","Many participants never experience the rare event at all, which is a major cause of underweighting in the real world.","Most Californians have never experienced a major earthquake, and in 2007 no banker had personally experienced a devastating financial crisis.","Hertwig and Erev note that chances of rare events such as the burst of housing bubbles receive less impact than they deserve according to their objective probabilities.","When choosing between Adele and Brian, System 1 generates global representations that include an emotional attitude and a tendency to approach or avoid.","Unless a rare event is so extreme that it comes to mind separately, the norm will be biased toward typical and recent instances.","Adele is closer to a sure thing, while Brian's prospect is a low probability of a very good outcome plus a higher probability of a slightly inferior one.","Rare events are overweighted when they specifically attract attention, as when prospects are described explicitly in choice from description."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the divergence between choices made from descriptions and choices made from experience: description yields a possibility effect that overweights rare outcomes, while experience produces underweighting, largely because many people never experience the rare event. It also tests the global-impressions account, in which rare events enter the picture only if they are extreme enough to come to mind separately, as with Adele and Brian.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Adele is generally helpful and consistent; Brian is usually slightly less helpful but has occasionally been extremely generous with his time and advice. A person must choose whom to ask for help. Which mechanism best explains why, in a two-system mind, the choice favors Adele unless the rare event comes to mind explicitly?","column_a_label":"Choice from description (rare events overweighted)","column_b_label":"Choice from experience (rare events underweighted)","column_c_label":"Global impressions of a person or source","rows":[{"label":"Participants see each button described as '5% to win $12' and choose; rare outcomes are overweighted.","col":"a"},{"label":"Participants press two buttons across many trials and observe the outcomes; overweighting of rare events is never observed.","col":"b"},{"label":"Choosing whether to ask Adele or Brian for help by comparing pooled global impressions of their behavior.","col":"c"},{"label":"A restaurant that is usually good occasionally serves a brilliant or an awful meal; diners choose based on accumulated experience.","col":"b"},{"label":"Prospect theory's choices from description yield a possibility effect.","col":"a"},{"label":"Unless a rare event is so extreme that it comes to mind separately, it will not be overweighted in a global impression.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["The decision is a pure choice from description, so Brian's rare generosity is overweighted by a possibility effect.","The decision follows choice from experience, in which rare events are always underweighted regardless of how vivid they are.","The decision is an example of denominator neglect, in which the number of helpful occasions is ignored."],"explanation":"In a two-system mind, System 1 generates global impressions of Adele and Brian that pool good and bad experiences into a norm; unless Brian's rare generosity is extreme enough to come to mind separately, the typical and recent instances dominate and the choice favors Adele. The possibility-effect reading would only apply if the rare event were made focal, as in choice from description."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Denominator Neglect in Vaccine Risk Communication","visual_frame_description":"In a brightly lit community-health center, Dr. Miriam Okonkwo stands before a parents' forum. A slide behind her reads 'Vaccine Risk: 0.001 percent' and is replaced a moment later by a slide that reads 'One of 100,000 vaccinated children will be permanently disabled.' Parents shift in their seats as the second slide stays on screen, and you are the auditor assigned to review her presentation for statistical accuracy and framing.","player_mission":"Review the observation log, the transcript, and the two slide formulations, then decide whether Miriam is misstating the statistics or exploiting a cognitive mechanism. Render a diagnosis with the evidence that supports it.","key_concepts":["A risk stated as 'a 0.001 percent risk of permanent disability' appears small.","The frequency statement 'One of 100,000 vaccinated children will be permanently disabled' calls up the image of an individual child.","The 99,999 safely vaccinated children fade into the background in the frequency frame.","Low-probability events are much more heavily weighted when described in terms of relative frequencies than when stated as chances, risk, or probability.","Denominator neglect is the tendency to focus on the winning cases while not assessing the number of nonwinning cases with the same care.","The power of format creates opportunities for manipulation, which people with an axe to grind know how to exploit.","System 1 is much better at dealing with individuals than categories.","The same statistics can produce different decision weights depending on how they are framed.","Focused attention, confirmation bias, and cognitive ease underlie the overestimation and overweighting of unlikely events.","Emotion and vividness influence fluency, availability, and judgments of probability."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests denominator neglect in risk communication: expressing an identical probability as a frequency ('one of 100,000') summons a vivid individual image that raises decision weight, whereas the abstract percentage form leaves the risk feeling small. Kahneman reports that low-probability events are weighted much more heavily when described as relative frequencies, and that the effect appears even in experienced professionals.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An audit of a public-health advocacy meeting: is Dr. Miriam Okonkwo misstating the statistics, and what psychological mechanism is doing the work in her presentation?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Miriam Okonkwo, a pediatric epidemiologist and vaccination advocate, normally states figures as decimal percentages in her written reports and pauses to let numbers land. Colleagues describe her as precise, calm, and wary of alarmism in committee memos.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-0:30: Miriam opens by quoting a '0.001 percent risk of permanent disability,' speaking evenly and reading from her notes.","0:31-1:10: As she switches to the frequency form, she leans toward the audience, raises her index finger, and slows her delivery.","1:11-1:45: She states 'One of 100,000 vaccinated children will be permanently disabled,' holds eye contact, and lets the sentence hang in silence for three seconds.","1:46-2:30: She returns to a relaxed posture and a quicker pace when summarizing the benefits of vaccination.","2:31-3:00: Asked whether 0.001 percent and 1 in 100,000 are the same risk, she nods and says 'mathematically identical,' then immediately pivots to the emotional toll on families."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Are you aware that 0.001 percent and one in one hundred thousand describe exactly the same risk?'","Miriam: 'Yes, mathematically identical.'","Auditor: 'Then why present it as one child in a hundred thousand?'","Miriam: 'Because parents need to feel what is at stake. A number like 0.001 percent is meaningless to them.'","Auditor: 'Do you consider that manipulating their judgment?'","Miriam: 'I consider it communicating the risk in a way that registers. The statistics are honest.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Miriam is not falsifying the statistics; she is deliberately exploiting denominator neglect. The frequency frame 'one of 100,000 vaccinated children will be permanently disabled' summons a vivid image of a single disabled child, which raises the decision weight of the event far above the abstract '0.001 percent risk,' producing a higher judged danger for the same objective probability.","diagnostic_breakdown":["Her baseline is technical precision with decimal formats, so a pure factual error is unlikely.","The timing of the nonverbal shift (leaning in, raised finger, slow delivery) aligns exactly with the switch from probability format to frequency format, indicating deliberate staging rather than habit.","Her admission that the two formulations are mathematically identical, combined with her stated goal of making parents feel what is at stake, confirms strategic use of the frame.","The chapter's evidence predicts the outcome: low-probability events are more heavily weighted when described as frequencies, because the image of the one disabled child overwhelms the 99,999 safe children who fade into the background."],"distractors":["Miriam is lying about the vaccine's risk in order to frighten parents into vaccinating.","Miriam is committing a certainty effect, treating vaccination as completely safe because the risk is 0.001 percent.","Miriam is falling for the planning fallacy and genuinely believes the risk is higher than it is."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Frequency Framing of DNA Evidence in the Courtroom","visual_frame_description":"In a hushed capital courtroom, the prosecutor finishes restating a DNA match figure as 'a 0.1 percent chance of a false match' while gesturing at the decimals. Defense attorney Greta Vasquez then walks slowly to the jury rail, and the camera holds on her face as she says 'one of one thousand.' You are the trial-practice auditor reviewing how the same statistic was framed for the jury.","player_mission":"Study the observation log and transcript, then determine whether Greta is misrepresenting the DNA statistics or using a legitimate cognitive mechanism to shift decision weight, and name that mechanism with the evidence.","key_concepts":["A good attorney who wishes to cast doubt on DNA evidence will not tell the jury that the chance of a false match is 0.1 percent.","The statement that a false match occurs in 1 of 1,000 capital cases is far more likely to pass the threshold of reasonable doubt.","The frequency statement invites jurors to generate the image of the man in the courtroom being wrongly convicted because of flawed DNA evidence.","The prosecutor will favor the more abstract frame, hoping to fill the jurors' minds with decimal points.","Denominator neglect causes people to overweight the salient case and underweight the background count.","Different ways of communicating the same risk vary widely in their effects.","Low-probability events are more heavily weighted when described in terms of relative frequencies than as abstract probabilities.","The effect of format would be reduced or eliminated by a direct comparison of the two formulations, a task that explicitly calls for System 2.","Life is usually a between-subjects experiment, in which you see only one formulation at a time.","It takes an exceptionally active System 2 to generate alternative formulations and discover that they evoke a different response."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the courtroom manipulation of decision weight through frequency framing: a false-match probability expressed as 'one of 1,000 capital cases' invites a vivid image of the wrongly convicted defendant, raising the decision weight above reasonable doubt, while the abstract '0.1 percent' frame leaves the mind filled with decimal points. Kahneman describes this as the power of format creating opportunities for manipulation, grounded in denominator neglect.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An audit of a capital-murder trial: is defense attorney Greta Vasquez accurately presenting the DNA statistics, and what mechanism is influencing the jury's decision weight?","subject_baseline":"Greta Vasquez, a veteran public defender, is known for measured, low-key argument and for ending her opening statements with a single slow sentence. Her voice stays at a monotone pitch during technical testimony.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-0:40: The prosecutor finishes by stating that the chance of a false match is 0.1 percent, moving his hands rapidly through the decimals.","0:41-1:10: Greta rises, walks toward the jury box, and lays both hands flat on the railing.","1:11-1:50: She says 'A false match occurs in one of one thousand capital cases,' dropping her voice and pausing between each word.","1:51-2:20: She glances at the defendant, then back to the jury, and repeats the phrase 'one of one thousand' while the courtroom is silent.","2:21-2:40: She returns to her seat and resumes a neutral tone, as if the point has been settled."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'The two numbers are statistically equivalent, are they not?'","Greta: 'They are. A 0.1 percent error rate and one false match per one thousand cases describe the same fact.'","Auditor: 'Then why did you choose the frequency version?'","Greta: 'Because a jury does not convict or acquit on decimals. It convicts or acquits on the man sitting in that chair.'","Auditor: 'Is that fair?'","Greta: 'It is the job of an advocate. The state can use the same facts to argue the other way.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Greta is exploiting denominator neglect through a concrete frequency frame. 'One of 1,000 capital cases' invites the jurors to generate the image of a single man wrongly convicted on flawed DNA evidence, raising the decision weight of a false match above the threshold of reasonable doubt, whereas the abstract '0.1 percent' frame leaves no such image. As the chapter notes, the prosecutor will favor the abstract frame in the hope of filling the jurors' minds with decimal points.","diagnostic_breakdown":["Her deliberate body staging (moving to the railing, slowed cadence, glance at the defendant) occurred only at the frequency sentence, marking it as a strategic choice.","Her admission that the figures are equivalent while insisting the frequency version is the advocate's job confirms intentional framing, not misunderstanding.","The chapter reports that the frequency format invites the listener to generate the image of the individual, which is exactly what she engineered by looking at the defendant.","Both numbers are true, so this is a manipulation of decision weight through format, not a misstatement of fact."],"distractors":["Greta is presenting a fabricated DNA statistic in order to create reasonable doubt.","Greta is underweighting the DNA evidence because she trusts the prosecutor's decimal framing.","Greta is using the certainty effect by treating a 99.9 percent match as an absolute certainty."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Planning Fallacy and Overweighted Success Scenarios","visual_frame_description":"In a glass-walled startup office, founder Nolan Reyes faces three investors across a conference table. A roadmap slide glows on the wall behind him, and a handwritten '70% break-even' figure sits on a flip chart beside the table. His voice rises as he narrates the successful first month of launch, then drops when an investor asks about comparable failures.","player_mission":"Audit the pitch: determine whether Nolan is deceiving the investors or displaying a documented cognitive bias, and identify the mechanism that inflated his 70 percent break-even estimate.","key_concepts":["The successful execution of a plan is specific and easy to imagine when one tries to forecast the outcome of a project.","The alternative of failure is diffuse, because there are innumerable ways for things to go wrong.","Entrepreneurs and the investors who evaluate their prospects are prone both to overestimate their chances and to overweight their estimates.","Specific descriptions trigger the associative machinery of System 1 in its usual confirmatory mode.","People overestimate the probabilities of unlikely events.","The probability of a rare event is most likely to be overestimated when the alternative is not fully specified.","The probability judgments in Fox's NBA study summed to 240 percent because each focal event was assessed in isolation.","These findings shed new light on the planning fallacy and other manifestations of optimism.","Confirmatory imagination selectively retrieves evidence, instances, and images that would make the statement true.","A judgment of probability is ultimately determined by the cognitive ease with which a plausible scenario comes to mind."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the planning fallacy and the overestimation of focal scenarios: a founder's success path is specific and fluent to imagine while failure is diffuse, so his probability estimate is inflated and unanchored to base rates. Kahneman argues that the same confirmatory mechanisms that overestimate rare events shed new light on the planning fallacy and the optimism of entrepreneurs.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An audit of a pitch meeting: is founder Nolan Reyes deceiving investors, or is his optimism a cognitive phenomenon the chapter calls the planning fallacy?","subject_baseline":"Nolan Reyes, 34, a first-time founder of a food-delivery logistics startup, is normally terse and data-driven in one-on-ones. He keeps a spreadsheet of every pilot test and shares failure logs with his engineering team without prompting.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-0:50: Nolan walks investors through the roadmap slide, standing still and speaking in short declarative sentences.","0:51-1:40: He describes the launch scenario with his hands miming the delivery flow; his voice rises with enthusiasm as he narrates the successful first month.","1:41-2:30: Asked about risks, he gives three quick generic hazards (supplier delays, regulation, weather) and immediately returns to the upbeat narrative, drumming his fingers on the podium.","2:31-3:00: When an investor asks how many similar startups succeeded, Nolan has no number, shrugs, and says his team's execution is different.","3:01-3:40: He ends by projecting a 70 percent chance of hitting break-even in year one, a figure he concedes he chose himself."],"interrogation_transcript":["Investor: 'You gave a 70 percent chance of breaking even. Where does that number come from?'","Nolan: 'It feels right. The plan is very specific.'","Investor: 'How many comparable ventures have hit break-even in year one?'","Nolan: 'I have not run that comparison. Our case is different.'","Investor: 'Are you hiding problems from us?'","Nolan: 'No. I just do not think about them as often.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Nolan is not deliberately lying; he is exhibiting the planning fallacy and the overestimation of a focal scenario. The successful execution of his plan is specific and easy to imagine, so confirmatory imagination builds a fluent success scenario, while failure is diffuse because there are innumerable ways for things to go wrong. His 70 percent estimate reflects overestimation: he focused on a single vivid success path and never enumerated alternatives, so his probability was not anchored to any base rate of comparable outcomes.","diagnostic_breakdown":["His nonverbal surge occurred precisely while narrating the success scenario, evidence of the fluency and cognitive ease that drive overestimation.","He could not supply base rates for comparable ventures, the missing alternative that allows a focal scenario's probability to be inflated without a check.","His estimate is self-generated and unanchored to any statistical comparison, matching the chapter's account of entrepreneurs overestimating their chances and overweighting their estimates.","The absence of enumerated failure modes is the signature of the planning fallacy: the successful execution of a plan is specific and easy to imagine, while the alternative of failure is diffuse."],"distractors":["Nolan is hiding a known technical defect in the product in order to secure funding.","Nolan is underweighting his chances because he is excessively loss averse.","Nolan is showing denominator neglect by ignoring the number of competing startups."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Availability Cascade of Terror and Fear","visual_frame_description":"You are the transportation-security director of a city whose morning paper has run a week of front-page stories about bus bombings on another continent. A wall screen in your office loops a map of the local bus network with past bombing incidents marked in red, and a placard on your desk notes that the city's risk of a bus bombing is statistically negligible. Your deputy Lena watches commuters at a nearby station: riders edge away from buses, scan packages, and stand as far from the doors as the platform allows.","player_mission":"Determine why the commuters' fear persists despite their knowledge of the low probability. Then choose the decision rule that best reflects how the chapter says terrorism works and how a rational authority should respond.","key_concepts":["Terrorism works by inducing an availability cascade: an extremely vivid image of death and damage becomes highly accessible.","The image is constantly reinforced by media attention and frequent conversations.","The emotional arousal is associative, automatic, and uncontrolled, and it produces an impulse for protective action.","System 2 may know that the probability is low, but this knowledge does not eliminate the self-generated discomfort and the wish to avoid it.","System 1 cannot be turned off.","The emotion is disproportionate to the probability and insensitive to its exact level.","Residents told about two bombers versus one bomber would not feel twice as safe, because the emotion does not scale with probability.","Avoiding buses was immediately rewarded by relief from fear, just as buying a lottery ticket is rewarded by pleasant fantasies.","In both cases the actual probability is inconsequential; only possibility matters.","The original formulation of prospect theory argued that highly unlikely events are either ignored or overweighted."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the availability cascade behind terrorism: a vivid, repeatedly reinforced image of a bombed bus stays highly accessible and drives System 1's emotional impulse for protective action even when System 2 knows the probability is tiny. Kahneman reports that the emotion is both disproportionate to the probability and insensitive to its exact level, and that even he found his own behavior affected despite knowing better.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the availability cascade behind terrorism: an extremely vivid image of death and damage, constantly reinforced by media attention and frequent conversation, becomes highly accessible and is associated with a specific situation such as the sight of a bus. The emotional arousal is associative, automatic, and uncontrolled, producing an impulse for protective action, and System 2's knowledge that the probability is low does not eliminate the self-generated discomfort.","stage_1_observation":"Riders at the station visibly avoid standing next to buses, scan packages, and drive away more quickly than usual when a light changes. Everyone, including your deputy, knows the objective risk is negligible, yet the behavior is widespread. The chapter records that even its author, who knew better, found himself disliking stopping next to a bus at a red light and driving away more quickly when the light changed.","stage_1_question":"Why does the commuters' fear persist even though everyone in the room knows the probability of a bombing is minuscule?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the vivid image of a bombed bus is highly accessible and emotionally arousing, and System 1 produces an automatic impulse for protective action; System 2's knowledge of the low probability cannot turn System 1 off or remove the self-generated discomfort. The emotion is disproportionate to the probability and insensitive to its exact level, which is why residents told that the risk is half of what they feared would not feel twice as safe.","stage_2_intervention":"The mayor proposes spending $40 million on a bus-security program that will have a negligible effect on the statistical risk but will visibly reassure riders. Your deputy argues the money should instead go to road-safety measures that prevent a far larger number of deaths. The 23-bombing statistic from the news is on the table. Choose the decision rule that best reflects the chapter's account of how terrorism works and how to respond.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recognize that the security spending is being driven by an availability cascade and by the decision weight of a vivid, salient image, not by objective probability. Present the neglected alternative in concrete terms, such as the road-safety deaths the money could prevent expressed as real people in a frequency format, so the alternative becomes equally vivid, and require the two proposals to be compared on the same terms. This forces the alternatives to compete as specified scenarios rather than leaving the rare event as the sole focal image."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Vivid Outcomes and Probability Insensitivity","visual_frame_description":"You run the consumer-insights desk at a national retail bank. A customer study on a rewards program shows members paying almost no attention to probability differences when the prize is described as a dozen red roses in a glass vase, while responding sharply to probabilities when the prize is cash. A colleague, Sanjay, proposes to launch the roses promotion precisely because it suppresses price sensitivity, and the compliance officer objects that this exploits customers.","player_mission":"Explain the mechanism that makes valuations of a vivid prize insensitive to probability. Then choose the disclosure design that will most reliably restore sensitivity to probability, based on the chapter's evidence.","key_concepts":["A rich and vivid representation of the outcome reduces the role of probability in the evaluation of an uncertain prospect.","The hypothesis predicts that adding irrelevant but vivid details to a monetary outcome also disrupts calculation.","In 'Money, Kisses, and Electric Shocks,' valuations of gambles were much less sensitive to probability when the outcomes were emotional.","The fear of an impending electric shock was essentially uncorrelated with the probability of receiving the shock.","The Princeton team argued that low sensitivity to probability for emotional outcomes is normal and that gambles on money are the exception.","Money gambles have a definite expected value, which provides a helpful anchor as the search for an equally attractive cash gift proceeds.","Cognitive ease contributes to the certainty effect: when you hold a vivid image of an event, the possibility of its not occurring is also represented vividly and overweighted.","The enhanced possibility effect combined with the enhanced certainty effect leaves little room for decision weights to change between 21% and 84%.","Adding explicit price information along with the description of the prize did not alter the results.","The decision weight for a 90% chance was 71.2 and for a 10% chance 18.6, a ratio of only 3.83 despite a probability ratio of 9."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the vivid-outcomes hypothesis: a rich, fluent representation of an outcome reduces the role of probability in evaluating a gamble, because the enhanced possibility and certainty effects compress decision weights between 21% and 84%. Kahneman expresses reasonably high confidence in the prediction that adding irrelevant but vivid details to a monetary outcome disrupts calculation, and he reports that adding price information did not restore sensitivity.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the hypothesis, for which Kahneman expresses reasonably high confidence, that a rich and vivid representation of an outcome reduces the role of probability in evaluating an uncertain prospect. Vivid imagery, whether or not it is emotional, shrinks the gap between decision weights for a 21 percent and an 84 percent chance, because the enhanced possibility effect and the enhanced certainty effect leave little room for probability to matter.","stage_1_observation":"The customer study shows that the cash equivalent of a roses gamble barely changes whether the chance of winning is 21 percent or 84 percent, while a money gamble is evaluated with clear sensitivity to probability because it has a definite expected value. The research record in the chapter includes the finding that adding explicit price information such as 'Value $59' did not alter the results.","stage_1_question":"Why do customers' valuations of a roses prize barely change between a 21 percent and an 84 percent chance of winning, while cash prizes are evaluated with clear sensitivity to probability?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the roses create a rich, fluent, vivid representation of the outcome, which reduces the role of probability in the evaluation of the prospect, whereas money has a definite expected value that provides a calculable anchor. The enhanced possibility effect for the low chance and the enhanced certainty effect for the high chance compress the decision weights, so 21 percent and 84 percent are evaluated almost alike, and adding price information does not restore sensitivity.","stage_2_intervention":"The compliance officer wants a disclosure that restores sensitivity to probability for customers making an informed decision about a financial product. Option A adds a line stating the dollar value of the prize next to the probability. Option B requires customers to compare two probabilities side by side in abstract percentage form and state which they prefer, without the vivid prize description. Based on the chapter's evidence, which option will most reliably restore sensitivity to probability?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose Option B: forcing an explicit, side-by-side comparison of abstract probabilities engages System 2 and reduces the influence of the vivid image. Option A is predicted to fail, because the price-information experiment in the chapter showed that adding a specific monetary value to a vivid outcome did not restore sensitivity to probability."}]},{"id":"ch36","title":"31. Risk Policies","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Narrow Framing vs Broad Framing in Concurrent Decisions","visual_frame_description":"On a conference-room monitor, a choice pair from prospect theory is displayed: Decision (i) offers a sure gain of $240 or a 25 percent chance to gain $1,000, and Decision (ii) offers a sure loss of $750 or a 75 percent chance to lose $1,000. Below the pair, a third problem shows that the combination of the two rejected options dominates the combination of the two preferred ones. Your colleague Dana asks why 73 percent of respondents chose A and D while only 3 percent favored B and C.","player_mission":"Sort each item into narrow framing, loss aversion, or broad framing. Then identify the mechanism that best explains why respondents preferred the dominated combination A plus D over the dominant combination B plus C.","key_concepts":["In the original experiment, 73 percent of respondents chose the sure gain in Decision (i) and the risky loss in Decision (ii), while only 3 percent favored the combination of B and C.","People tend to be risk averse in the domain of gains and risk seeking in the domain of losses for moderate or high probabilities.","The emotional evaluation of a sure gain and a sure loss is an automatic reaction of System 1 that occurs before the computation of expected values.","Narrow framing treats a sequence of two simple decisions as separate; broad framing treats them as a single comprehensive decision with four options.","The combined option B plus C dominates option A plus D, because a 25 percent chance to win $250 and lose $750 is unequivocally better than a 25 percent chance to win $240 and lose $760.","Broad framing is superior, or at least not inferior, in every case in which several decisions are to be contemplated together.","A rational agent will engage in broad framing, but Humans are by nature narrow framers.","Because we are susceptible to WYSIATI and averse to mental effort, we tend to make decisions as problems arise even when instructed to consider them jointly.","It is costly to be risk averse for gains and risk seeking for losses, because both attitudes make you willing to pay a premium out of the same pocket.","Every simple choice formulated in terms of gains and losses can be deconstructed into a combination of choices that yields inconsistent preferences."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests narrow framing: evaluating concurrent risky decisions one at a time rather than as a single comprehensive decision, which leads people to prefer a dominated combination. Kahneman and Tversky showed that 73 percent of respondents preferred A and D while only 3 percent chose the dominant B plus C, demonstrating that logical consistency is not achievable by our limited mind.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Respondents preferred a sure gain of $240 (A) and a 75 percent chance to lose $1,000 (D), even though combining the other two options, B plus C, dominates it. Which mechanism best explains this preference pattern?","column_a_label":"Narrow framing","column_b_label":"Loss aversion","column_c_label":"Broad framing","rows":[{"label":"Sam rejects the single coin toss that would lose $100 or win $200 because the $100 loss would feel worse than the $200 gain.","col":"b"},{"label":"Respondents evaluate Decisions (i) and (ii) separately and never combine them to see that B plus C dominates.","col":"a"},{"label":"A CEO tells her 25 division heads to all accept their risky gambles because the portfolio will aggregate the outcomes.","col":"c"},{"label":"Investors who check their portfolio daily feel more pain from frequent small losses than pleasure from frequent small gains.","col":"b"},{"label":"Students told to make each decision as if it were the only one show stronger loss aversion.","col":"a"},{"label":"Students told to imagine themselves as a trader who does this all the time take more risks and show blunted emotional reactions to loss.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The respondents were underweighting the rare 25 percent chance in both gambles.","The respondents were overweighting the certainty of the sure gain through a possibility effect.","The respondents combined the two decisions and chose the option with the higher expected value."],"explanation":"The A plus D preference is the classic narrow-framing trap: each decision is attractive on its own (risk averse for the sure gain, risk seeking for the sure loss), but the pair is inferior to B plus C, the combined option that only 3 percent of respondents chose. The mechanism is evaluating each problem separately rather than as one comprehensive decision."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk Policies and the Outside View as Broad Frames","visual_frame_description":"In a financial-literacy classroom, an instructor named Rosa draws a two-column table on the board. The left column lists routine decision rules such as 'always take the highest possible deductible' and 'never buy extended warranties.' The right column shows a chart of outcomes from many similar projects, labeled as the outside view. She explains that both columns are ways of widening the frame around individual risky choices.","player_mission":"Sort each item into a risk policy, narrow framing, or the outside view of planning. Then identify the category that best describes a rule that is routinely applied whenever a relevant problem arises.","key_concepts":["A risk policy is a broad frame that embeds a particular risky choice in a set of similar choices.","Familiar examples of risk policies are always taking the highest possible deductible when purchasing insurance and never buying extended warranties.","Decision makers who are prone to narrow framing construct a preference every time they face a risky choice; a risk policy avoids this by being applied routinely.","With a risk policy, you expect the occasional loss of the entire deductible or the occasional failure of an uninsured product.","The relevant issue is your ability to reduce the pain of the occasional loss by the thought that the policy will almost certainly be advantageous over the long run.","The outside view shifts the focus from the specifics of the current situation to the statistics of outcomes in similar situations.","The outside view is a broad frame for thinking about plans, and a risk policy is a broad frame for a particular risky choice.","The outside view and the risk policy are remedies against the exaggerated optimism of the planning fallacy and the exaggerated caution induced by loss aversion.","The two biases oppose each other: exaggerated optimism protects organizations from the paralyzing effects of loss aversion, and loss aversion protects them from the follies of overconfident optimism.","Because we are susceptible to WYSIATI and averse to mental effort, we tend to make decisions as problems arise rather than applying broad frames.","The combination of the outside view with a risk policy should be the goal for an organization."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the idea that a risk policy is a broad frame: it embeds a particular risky choice in a set of similar choices so that occasional losses are absorbed emotionally. Kahneman compares it to the outside view of planning, and argues that combining the two remedies counters both the planning fallacy's optimism and loss aversion's exaggerated caution.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A shopper routinely applies the rule 'never buy extended warranties,' accepting the occasional failure of an uninsured product as part of the deal. Which category best describes this rule?","column_a_label":"Risk policy (a broad frame for repeated choices)","column_b_label":"Narrow framing","column_c_label":"Outside view of planning","rows":[{"label":"Always take the highest possible deductible when purchasing insurance.","col":"a"},{"label":"Forecasting a project by looking at the base-rate outcomes of similar projects.","col":"c"},{"label":"Treating each small risky decision as a one-off judgment made on the spot.","col":"b"},{"label":"Bundling 100 small favorable coin tosses into a single package.","col":"a"},{"label":"A division head refusing a risky bet because the possible loss of her own capital is painful.","col":"b"},{"label":"Shifting focus from the specifics of the current situation to the statistics of similar situations.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The rule is a form of narrow framing because it evaluates each warranty purchase in isolation.","The rule is an outside view because it looks at the statistics of the specific product being purchased.","The rule is an example of loss aversion being intensified by daily monitoring."],"explanation":"The extended-warranty rule is a risk policy, a broad frame applied routinely whenever the relevant problem arises, so the occasional loss is absorbed by the expectation that the policy is advantageous over the long run. The outside view, by contrast, is the broad frame applied to planning problems, shifting focus to the statistics of similar situations."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Loss Aversion in the Investment Audit","visual_frame_description":"In a quiet audit room, investor Dev Patel faces an examiner across a table covered with his daily portfolio logs. A monitor displays a skin-conductance trace that spikes sharply on down days and stays flat on up days. Dev crosses his arms as he recounts declining a 50-50 bet that would lose $100 or win $200.","player_mission":"Review Dev's baseline, the physiological trace, and the transcript, then decide whether his behavior reflects loss aversion under narrow framing, and state what would reduce it.","key_concepts":["Samuelson's friend rejected a coin toss in which he could lose $100 or win $200, explaining that he would feel the $100 loss more than the $200 gain.","Loss aversion is the tendency to weight losses more heavily than gains, roughly twice as intensely.","A single favorable gamble can be worth nothing to a loss-averse person once each loss is multiplied by 2.","Aggregating gambles reduces the probability of losing and shrinks the impact of loss aversion.","Two favorable gambles that are each worthless on their own can be jointly worth $50 when bundled.","The mantra 'you win a few, you lose a few' exists to control the emotional response when you do lose.","Broad framing blunts the emotional reaction to losses and increases the willingness to take risks.","Broad-framing instructions reduced skin-conductance responses to losses in the 'think like a trader' experiment.","Narrow framing makes a small loss feel like significant bad news about the whole portfolio.","The aggregation of small gambles works only when the gambles are independent and small relative to total wealth."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests loss aversion under narrow framing: Dev feels a $100 loss more intensely than a $200 gain, so a single favorable gamble is worth nothing to him, while the aggregated package becomes attractive. Kahneman shows that broad framing blunts the physiological emotional response to losses and increases risk taking, as demonstrated by the 'think like a trader' experiment.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"An audit of investor Dev Patel's trading decisions: why does he keep rejecting attractive small gambles, and is his stated reason consistent with the evidence?","subject_baseline":"Dev Patel, a 41-year-old software engineer and part-time investor, is described by his family as careful and deliberate. He keeps a daily log of his portfolio value, screenshots every trade, and says in interviews that he is not a gambler and that losing money ruins his day.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00-0:40: Dev recounts declining a colleague's offer of a 50-50 bet that would lose $100 or win $200. He shakes his head and crosses his arms.","0:41-1:20: He explains 'I would feel the $100 loss more than the $200 gain,' then adds that he would accept if allowed to make 100 such bets, leaning forward as he says it.","1:21-2:00: Asked to imagine the aggregated 100-bet package, he relaxes, uncrosses his arms, and nods, estimating that the package sounds like a good deal.","2:01-2:40: On a screen, the audit shows skin-conductance spikes on days his portfolio fell and near-flat readings on gain days.","2:41-3:10: When asked why he checks his portfolio daily, he says to catch problems early, but the physiological trace shows each check resets his emotional ledger."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Why did you turn down a bet that offers twice as much gain as loss?'","Dev: 'Because losing $100 feels twice as bad as winning $200 feels good.'","Auditor: 'But you would accept 100 of them?'","Dev: 'Yes, because then I would win a few and lose a few. The package is different.'","Auditor: 'What would change if you only looked at your investments once a quarter?'","Dev: 'I would worry less about the dips, probably. But I would not know what was happening.'","Auditor: 'Do you think the daily checking changes what you do?'","Dev: 'It makes me sell more when things drop. I know that is not rational.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Dev is exhibiting classic loss aversion under narrow framing: he feels the $100 loss more than twice as intensely as the $200 gain, so a single favorable gamble is worth nothing to him, while bundling gambles reduces the chance of losing and the impact of loss aversion. His daily portfolio monitoring is a narrow frame that repeatedly exposes him to small losses, and the skin-conductance record shows the emotional asymmetry his own words confirm.","diagnostic_breakdown":["His explicit statement that the $100 loss feels worse than the $200 gain matches the value function in which losses are weighted roughly twice as much as gains.","His acceptance of the 100-bet package shows the aggregation mechanism: as gambles are bundled, the probability of losing falls and loss aversion matters less.","The skin-conductance asymmetry on loss days versus gain days is the physiological evidence the chapter cites for broad framing blunting the emotional reaction to losses.","His admission that he sells more when prices drop confirms that narrow framing amplifies loss aversion in real decisions."],"distractors":["Dev is underweighting rare events because he has never personally experienced a large loss.","Dev is showing the planning fallacy, overestimating his future investment returns.","Dev is exhibiting an endowment effect, valuing his portfolio more because he owns it."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Samuelson's Problem and the Aggregation of Gambles","visual_frame_description":"You are a financial adviser in a city office, and your client Sam, a retired engineer, has just declined a one-time 50-50 offer to lose $100 or win $200. On the whiteboard behind him you draw his value function: the pain of the loss is drawn twice as tall as the pleasure of the gain, so the single bet computes to zero for him. Sam looks at the board and says that if he could make a hundred of these he would take them.","player_mission":"Explain why the single toss is worth nothing to Sam despite its positive expected value. Then guide Sam to the smallest bundle of tosses that turns the package positive, and state the caution about independence that the chapter adds.","key_concepts":["Samuelson's friend rejected a single coin toss that could lose $100 or win $200, saying he would feel the $100 loss more than the $200 gain.","The friend said he would accept if allowed to make 100 such bets.","Samuelson proved that under very specific conditions a utility maximizer who rejects a single gamble should also reject the offer of many.","Rabin and Thaler noted that the aggregated gamble of 100 bets has an expected return of $5,000 with only a 1/2,300 chance of losing any money.","One toss is worth nothing to Sam because the pain of losing a dollar is twice as intense as the pleasure of winning a dollar.","Two tosses reduce the chances of losing to 25 percent, and the intermediate outcome (one loss, one gain) is positive.","Two favorable gambles that are individually worth nothing to Sam are jointly worth $50 when bundled.","A third toss, although worthless on its own, adds $62.50 to the total value of the package.","By five tosses, the expected value is $250, the probability of losing anything is 18.75 percent, and the cash equivalent is $203.125.","The mantra 'you win a few, you lose a few' works only when gambles are genuinely independent and the possible loss is small relative to total wealth.","The mantra should not be applied to long shots, where the probability of winning is very small for each bet.","Aggregating favorable gambles rapidly reduces the probability of losing and diminishes the impact of loss aversion."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the aggregation of gambles against loss aversion: a single favorable coin toss is worth zero to Sam because he weights losses twice as much as gains, but bundling gambles reduces the probability of losing and shrinks the impact of loss aversion, making two tosses jointly worth $50. Kahneman presents Rabin and Thaler's demonstration that the package of 100 bets is so attractive that any utility theory that rejects it under some circumstances is wrong as a model of rational choice.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests Samuelson's problem: Samuelson's friend rejected a single coin toss with a 50 percent chance to lose $100 and a 50 percent chance to win $200 because loss aversion makes the $100 loss feel twice as intense as the $200 gain, leaving the single gamble worth zero to him. But aggregating gambles reduces the probability of losing and shrinks the impact of loss aversion: two tosses are jointly worth $50, three tosses add another $62.50, and five tosses have an expected value of $250 with only an 18.75 percent chance of losing anything.","stage_1_observation":"The whiteboard shows Sam's rewritten bet: he multiplies each possible loss by a factor of 2 to express his aversion, then computes expected value. One toss is worth nothing to him, though its unadjusted expected value is a gain of $50. Sam rejects the single offer and says he would accept 100 of them.","stage_1_question":"Why is the single toss worth nothing to Sam, even though its expected value is positive at $50?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because Sam multiplies the possible loss by 2 to express his loss aversion before computing expected value; rewriting the gamble that way makes one toss worth zero. He feels the pain of losing a dollar as twice the pleasure of winning a dollar, so the favorable expected value is not enough to overcome the aversion when the gamble is considered alone.","stage_2_intervention":"Sam now stands in front of a decision table with two, three, and five bundled tosses laid out as separate packages. Two tosses leave a 25 percent chance of losing, with the two extreme outcomes canceling out and the intermediate outcome (one loss, one gain) positive; three tosses add $62.50 to the package; five tosses have an expected value of $250 and an 18.75 percent chance of losing anything. Which recommendation follows from the chapter's arithmetic, and which caution about the mantra must you add?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recommend that Sam bundle at least two tosses, because two favorable gambles that are each worthless on their own are jointly worth $50 when aggregated, and the probability of losing drops to 25 percent. Add the chapter's qualifications: the gambles must be genuinely independent, the possible loss must be small relative to Sam's total wealth, and the mantra should not be applied to long shots or to multiple investments in the same industry, which would all go bad together."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The CEO's Broad Frame and the Think Like a Trader Mantra","visual_frame_description":"You sit in the boardroom of a large company where Richard Thaler has posed a gamble to the 25 division heads: with equal probability they lose a large amount of the capital they control or earn double that amount. One by one, all 25 executives decline, each explaining that the pain of losing their division's capital is unbearable even though the expected value is positive. On the screen behind them, the numbers show that the portfolio of all 25 bets has a very high probability of profit.","player_mission":"Explain why every division head refuses the gamble. Then, as the new chief executive, choose the response that best follows the chapter's evidence and state the role of the mantra 'you win a few, you lose a few.'","key_concepts":["Thaler's CEO wanted all 25 division heads to accept their risky gambles even though none of them was willing on his own.","In that conversation it was natural for the CEO to adopt a broad frame encompassing all 25 bets.","Like Sam facing 100 coin tosses, the CEO could count on statistical aggregation to mitigate the overall risk.","Each executive is loss averse in his or her own domain, which is perfectly natural, but the result is that the organization is not taking enough risk.","Experienced traders in financial markets live by broad framing, shielding themselves from the pain of losses.","Students told to think like a trader showed blunted physiological reactions to losses and increased willingness to take risks.","The broad-framing instructions used the phrases 'imagine yourself as a trader,' 'you do this all the time,' and 'treat it as one of many monetary decisions.'","The mantra 'you win a few, you lose a few' exists to control the emotional response when you do lose.","A risk policy is a broad frame that embeds a particular risky choice in a set of similar choices.","The outside view is the broad frame for planning, and a risk policy is the broad frame for risky choices.","Checking investments only once a quarter reduces the pain of frequent small losses that exceeds the pleasure of small gains.","Investors who get aggregated feedback are likely to be less risk averse and to end up richer."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the broad frame of a risk policy: a CEO aggregates 25 division-level risky bets into one portfolio so that statistical aggregation blunts the loss aversion each executive feels in his or her own domain. Kahneman reports that inducing people to think like traders blunted physiological responses to loss and increased willingness to take risk, and that the combination of the outside view with a risk policy is the goal.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the risk-policy idea: a broad frame embeds a particular risky choice in a set of similar choices, letting statistical aggregation blunt loss aversion. Thaler's CEO wanted all 25 division heads to accept the risky gambles even though each head, loss averse in his or her own domain, refused, and the chapter notes that the result is an organization that is not taking enough risk. The outside view of planning is the parallel broad frame, and combining a risk policy with the outside view counters both the planning fallacy's optimism and loss aversion's excessive caution.","stage_1_observation":"All 25 division heads refuse the gamble with equal probabilities of losing a large amount of their controlled capital or earning double that amount. Each explains the refusal in personal terms: the pain of losing their own division's capital is unbearable. The CEO, who is present, says without hesitation that he would like all of them to accept their risks.","stage_1_question":"Why does every division head refuse the gamble, even though accepting it across all 25 divisions would almost certainly be profitable for the firm?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because each division head is evaluating the gamble in a narrow frame and is loss averse in his or her own domain: the possible loss of their own controlled capital feels unbearable. The chapter notes that this is perfectly natural, but the result is an organization not taking enough risk, and the CEO's broad frame, which aggregates all 25 bets, is the antidote because statistical aggregation mitigates the overall risk.","stage_2_intervention":"As the newly appointed chief executive, you must respond to the division heads' collective refusal. Option A: order every division to accept the bet and take the losses personally. Option B: adopt a risk policy that treats the 25 bets as one portfolio, instruct heads to think like a trader and see each decision as one of many that will sum into a portfolio, while monitoring results quarterly rather than daily. Option C: forbid any risky decision above a fixed dollar threshold for a year. Choose the response that best follows the chapter's evidence and state the role of the mantra.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose Option B: adopt a risk policy that broadens the frame, telling each head to think like a trader who makes this kind of decision all the time and treating the bets as one portfolio. The mantra 'you win a few, you lose a few' exists to control the emotional response when a loss occurs, and the chapter's experiment showed that broad-framing instructions blunted skin-conductance responses to loss and increased willingness to take risk. Option A ignores that aggregation requires independence and emotional acceptance, and Option C is a blanket restriction rather than a change of frame."}]},{"id":"ch37","title":"32. Keeping Score","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Mental Accounting vs. Econ Framing vs. Sunk-Cost Escalation","visual_frame_description":"Nina, a retail investor, sits at a kitchen table covered with color-coded folders, one for every stock she owns. A ledger titled 'Blueberry Tiles +$5,000' is open next to 'Tiffany Motors -$5,000.' Floating above the table are three sorting bins labeled Econ, Mental Accountant, and Escalator, and a stack of decision cards drifts toward the player.","player_mission":"Sort each decision card into the bin that best explains the thinking behind it. Distinguish the comprehensive Econ view from Humans' mental accounting and from escalation into sunk costs.","key_concepts":["Money is often a proxy for points on a scale of self-regard and achievement, so people carefully keep score of wins and losses in their heads.","Mental accounts are a form of narrow framing: they keep things under control and manageable by a finite mind.","Econs of the rational-agent model do not resort to mental accounting; they take a comprehensive view of outcomes and are driven by external incentives.","The disposition effect is the documented preference for selling winners rather than losers, driven by a wish to close each mental account as a gain.","A rational agent with a comprehensive view sells the stock least likely to do well in the future, regardless of whether it is a winner or a loser.","In the United States, realizing losses reduces taxes while selling winners exposes them to taxes, so investors sell more losers in December.","Driving into a blizzard because a ticket was already paid for is a sunk-cost error.","The sunk-cost fallacy keeps people too long in poor jobs, unhappy marriages, and unpromising research projects.","The extra after-tax return expected from selling a loser rather than a winner is about 3.4% over the next year.","Experienced investors, who use System 2, are less susceptible to the disposition effect than novices are."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests narrow framing: mental accounting closes each decision in its own box, so a $5,000 win and a $5,000 loss feel like separate events instead of a $0 portfolio. Kahneman shows this produces the disposition effect and the sunk-cost fallacy, mistakes Econs never make.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Nina keeps every stock in its own mental ledger. Sort each decision into the thinking style that best explains it: Econ/comprehensive framing, mental accounting, or sunk-cost escalation.","column_a_label":"Econ / comprehensive framing","column_b_label":"Mental accounting (narrow framing)","column_c_label":"Sunk-cost escalation","rows":[{"label":"Selling the winning stock Blueberry Tiles to close its account at a gain while holding the losing Tiffany Motors","col":"b"},{"label":"Holding separate pots for spending, general savings, and the children's education, and refusing to mix them for a car repair","col":"b"},{"label":"A rational investor who weighs only expected future returns and ignores the price she paid for each share","col":"a"},{"label":"A company pours an extra $60 million into a project already $50 million in the red because the earlier money must be recouped","col":"c"},{"label":"Driving 40 miles into a blizzard because the basketball ticket was already paid for","col":"c"},{"label":"Keeping credit-card debt while money sits in a savings account, paying interest just to keep the accounts separate","col":"b"},{"label":"Selling the loser stock in December because realized losses reduce the tax bill","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Selling the winner reflects a comprehensive portfolio view and is tax-optimal.","Driving into a blizzard for a prepaid ticket is best explained by the endowment effect.","Maintaining debt beside savings is what Econs do because money is fungible."],"explanation":"The first row is the disposition effect: selling the winner lets Nina close the Blueberry Tiles account at a gain and score success, so it belongs to mental accounting (b). The Econ rows (a) are comprehensive: taxes and future returns, not purchase price, drive the decision. The sunk-cost rows (c) throw good money after bad to avoid closing an account in the red."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Disposition Effect in Portfolio Decisions","visual_frame_description":"A cold-brew coffee sits on Maya's desk at Northgate Advisors as she stares at a split screen of her personal portfolio. The left pane glows green for her winners; the right pane flickers red. A timer logs her eye movements and hesitations while an interviewer asks her to raise cash for her sister's wedding.","player_mission":"Watch Maya's reactions during the interview, read her nonverbal log and transcript, and diagnose the bias that is steering her stock-selling decisions.","key_concepts":["People keep a separate mental account for each share they buy and want to close every account as a gain.","The disposition effect is a documented massive preference for selling winners rather than losers.","Closing a mental account with a gain is a pleasure, but it is a pleasure you pay for.","A rational decision maker is interested only in the future consequences of current investments.","Selling winners in the United States exposes the gain to taxes, while realizing losses reduces the tax bill.","Stocks that recently gained in value tend to keep gaining, at least for a short while.","The expected after-tax extra return of selling a loser rather than a winner is about 3.4% over the next year.","Mental accounting is a form of narrow framing that keeps things manageable for a finite mind.","Experienced investors who engage System 2 are less susceptible to the disposition effect than novices are.","Finance research documents that investors sell more losers in December, when taxes are on their mind."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the disposition effect, in which investors sell winners to lock in a scored gain and ride losers to avoid closing accounts at a loss. The book's evidence is that this narrow-framing bias is costly, because tax rules and momentum favor selling losers instead.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which bias is driving Maya's decision to sell her winners rather than her losers?","subject_baseline":"Maya is a disciplined 41-year-old investment manager who prepares written rationales for every trade she makes in her own account. Colleagues describe her as calm and methodical, and she rarely makes impulsive moves.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:02:41 - Asked to name the money she could free up quickly, Maya's gaze drops instantly to the green 'gains' column of her screen.","14:03:05 - When the interviewer says 'either stock could go,' Maya's fingers hover over the Blueberry Tiles row; her shoulders relax and she smiles faintly.","14:03:28 - Mentioning Tiffany Motors, her jaw tightens, she exhales sharply, and she looks away from the red pane.","14:04:12 - Asked about taxes, she frowns, shakes her head, and says 'I never think about it that way,' then picks up her coffee and drinks slowly.","14:04:49 - On the word 'wedding,' her posture becomes eager and she starts tapping the screen next to her winning stock."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Which holding could you part with to cover the wedding?'","Maya: 'Blueberry Tiles, no question. I've had a great run on it. I want to take that win home.'","Interviewer: 'And Tiffany Motors?'","Maya: 'No. That one I'm holding. If I sell it now, it's like admitting I bought it wrong.'","Interviewer: 'Which is more likely to do well in the coming year?'","Maya: 'Honestly? Nobody knows. But Blueberry has been climbing all quarter.'","Interviewer: 'What if I told you selling Tiffany saves you a few hundred dollars in taxes?'","Maya: 'Taxes? I just... I don't think about my portfolio that way.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The disposition effect: Maya wants to close the Blueberry Tiles account at a gain and keep Tiffany Motors open, sacrificing wealth to score a success in her mental ledger.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her gaze locks onto the gains column, she brightens when discussing the winner, and she stiffens at the loser, matching a wish to close accounts in the black. She explicitly frames selling Tiffany as 'admitting I bought it wrong' - pure scorekeeping, not expected returns. She dismisses the tax argument, which is exactly the month-of-the-year effect the text describes: mental accounting prevails over financial common sense for 11 months of the year.","distractors":["The sunk-cost fallacy, because she is pouring more money into the losing stock to recoup her purchase price.","Anchoring, because the IPO price of Tiffany Motors is distorting her estimate of its future value.","Loss aversion in risk-taking, because she avoids selling winners to reduce the variance of her portfolio."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Sunk-Cost CEO and Escalation of Commitment","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled boardroom at HelioGrid overlooks a rainy skyline. CEO Victor Okada stands before a wall chart of Project Aegis showing $50 million spent, a red 'behind schedule' flag, and a falling forecast line. A rival proposal, OrbitReach, glows on the table with a brighter projected return. Directors watch Victor, who keeps stroking the Aegis chart.","player_mission":"First predict how a sunk-cost-afflicted company will act, then intervene as the board to break the escalation and protect shareholder value.","key_concepts":["The decision to invest additional resources in a losing account when better investments are available is the sunk-cost fallacy.","All too often a company afflicted by sunk costs drives into the blizzard, throwing good money after bad.","The sunk-cost situation sits in the top-right cell of the fourfold pattern: a choice between a sure loss and an unfavorable gamble.","In the presence of sunk costs, the manager's incentives are misaligned with the objectives of the firm, a version of the agency problem.","Canceling a floundering project leaves a permanent stain on the executive's record, so he may gamble further to postpone the day of reckoning.","Boards often replace a CEO who is encumbered by prior decisions and reluctant to cut losses.","The board does not necessarily believe the new CEO is more competent; they know she does not carry the same mental accounts.","A rational decision maker is interested only in the future consequences of current investments.","Graduate students in economics and business are more willing than others to walk away from a failing project.","The escalation of commitment is a mistake from the perspective of the firm but not necessarily from the perspective of the executive who owns the floundering project."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests escalation of commitment driven by sunk costs: the company keeps investing because canceling would close the mental account at a loss, even though better options exist. The book places this in the fourfold pattern's top-right cell, where an unfavorable gamble is often unwisely preferred to a sure loss.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the sunk-cost fallacy and its escalation: past, unrecoverable investment continues to justify new spending because closing the mental account at a loss feels like failure.","stage_1_observation":"Project Aegis has already consumed $50 million, is behind schedule, and its forecast returns now look worse than at planning. It needs a further $60 million to have a chance, while the new OrbitReach proposal offers higher expected returns for the same money. Watch Victor's reasoning as he stands in front of the Aegis chart.","stage_1_question":"What is the sunk-cost-afflicted company most likely to do, and which frame describes its situation?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It escalates: it throws good money after bad into Aegis rather than accept the humiliation of closing the account of a costly failure. This choice sits in the top-right cell of the fourfold pattern, a sure loss versus an unfavorable gamble, where the gamble is often unwisely preferred.","stage_2_intervention":"Victor insists the $50 million 'must be protected' and pushes the board to fund Aegis. As the board, your move: keep Victor and fund Aegis, keep Victor and fund OrbitReach, or replace Victor and let the new CEO choose freely. Choose the intervention that breaks the sunk-cost trap.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Replace the CEO who is encumbered by prior decisions, and reframe the decision around future costs and returns only. Boards do this not because the successor is more competent but because she does not carry the same mental accounts, so she can ignore sunk costs in evaluating current opportunities."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Three Shades of Loss Aversion: Standard, Enhanced, and Taboo","visual_frame_description":"A regulator's briefing table in Geneva displays three trays: one labeled '2:1 standard loss aversion,' one 'enhanced by responsibility,' and one 'taboo tradeoffs.' Photographs float in: a coffee mug, a vaccine vial, an insecticide bottle, and a list of inventions that would be blocked by the precautionary principle. The player drags each image into a tray.","player_mission":"Classify each loss-related response into the level of loss aversion that produces it: the standard 2:1 weighting, the enhanced version for important endowments and responsibility, or the taboo tradeoff that refuses to price safety at all.","key_concepts":["Losses are weighted about twice as much as gains in several contexts: choice between gambles, the endowment effect, and reactions to price changes.","The loss-aversion coefficient is much higher when the endowment is more important than money, such as health.","Reluctance to sell an important endowment increases dramatically when selling might make you responsible for an awful outcome.","People set a far higher price to accept a 1/1,000 disease risk as a volunteer than they would pay to remove the same risk with a vaccine.","Thaler reported informally that the volunteer fee versus vaccine price ratio is typically about 50 to 1.","More than two-thirds of parents said they would not buy a cheaper, slightly less safe insecticide at any price.","Parents who accept a discount for added child risk demand significantly more than they would pay for a far larger safety improvement.","The precautionary principle prohibits any action that might cause harm and would have blocked airplanes, X-rays, chlorine, and the measles vaccine.","The strong version of the precautionary principle is untenable, but enhanced loss aversion is embedded in a strong moral intuition that originates in System 1.","The taboo tradeoff against accepting any increase in risk may be motivated by a selfish fear of regret more than by optimizing the child's safety."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario discriminates among levels of loss aversion: a baseline coefficient near 2 in gambles and endowments, a steeper version when the endowment is health or when responsibility for a bad outcome looms, and taboo tradeoffs that refuse to price safety at all. The book shows the last two originate in System 1 moral intuition and can be incoherent, since even loving parents have a finite safety budget.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each reaction by the strength of loss aversion behind it: standard 2:1, enhanced by responsibility for important endowments, or taboo tradeoff.","column_a_label":"Standard loss aversion (~2:1)","column_b_label":"Enhanced loss aversion (important endowment, responsibility)","column_c_label":"Taboo tradeoff / precautionary principle","rows":[{"label":"Losing $100 feels roughly twice as bad as gaining $100 feels good, across gambles, endowments, and price changes","col":"a"},{"label":"A volunteer demands about 50 times more to accept a 1/1,000 disease risk than the same person would pay for a vaccine against it","col":"b"},{"label":"Parents refuse to switch to a cheaper insecticide at any discount because it raises the risk to their child","col":"c"},{"label":"A strict precautionary principle would have blocked airplanes, air conditioning, chlorine, X-rays, and the measles vaccine","col":"c"},{"label":"People value a mug they own at about twice what they would pay to buy the same mug","col":"a"},{"label":"Refusing to change insurers because you would feel responsible if the new plan ever failed you","col":"b"},{"label":"Demanding a much higher price to give up health than to give up money, because health cannot legitimately be sold","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The 50:1 volunteer fee is an ordinary 2:1 loss-aversion coefficient applied to a small probability.","The precautionary principle is a purely rational risk-management doctrine with no emotional component.","Parents' refusal to switch insecticide is best explained by the disposition effect on their past purchases."],"explanation":"The first row is the book's baseline claim that losses are weighted about twice as much as gains, so it belongs to column a. The 50:1 vaccine-versus-volunteer gap and the insurer reluctance involve responsibility for an awful outcome on an endowment more important than money, so they sort to b. The insecticide refusal and the precautionary principle refuse to price safety at all, sorting to c."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Sunk-Cost Fallacy and the Agency Problem","visual_frame_description":"Inside Mosaic Software's open-plan office, product lead Dev Mahlangu stands in front of a wall of Post-it notes for Project Phoenix, an app now 14 months overdue. A large burn chart shows the budget consumed. His manager, Priya, holds a forecast report with a declining line. Dev keeps pointing at the completed features rather than the red budget line.","player_mission":"Watch Dev justify continuing Project Phoenix, review his body language and words, and diagnose the bias and the incentive problem behind his escalation.","key_concepts":["The decision to invest additional resources in a losing account when better investments are available is the sunk-cost fallacy.","The sunk-cost fallacy keeps people for too long in poor jobs, unhappy marriages, and unpromising research projects.","Driving into a blizzard because one paid for the ticket is a sunk-cost error.","All too often a company afflicted by sunk costs throws good money after bad rather than accepting the humiliation of closing a costly account.","Canceling a floundering project leaves a permanent stain on the executive's record.","In the presence of sunk costs, the manager's incentives are misaligned with the objectives of the firm and its shareholders - the agency problem.","Boards replace CEOs encumbered by prior decisions because the successor carries no mental accounts and can ignore sunk costs.","The sunk-cost situation is the top-right cell of the fourfold pattern: a sure loss versus an unfavorable gamble.","Graduate students in economics and business are more willing to walk away from a failing project.","A rational decision maker is interested only in the future consequences of current investments."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the sunk-cost fallacy and the agency problem: Dev keeps funding Phoenix because closing the account admits failure, and his personal record is served by gambling further with the firm's money. The book notes this is rational for the executive even when it is a mistake for the firm.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What is driving Dev to keep spending on Project Phoenix, and whose interests is it serving?","subject_baseline":"Dev is a normally pragmatic product lead who praises 'quick kill' culture and has killed two of his own features in the past year. He keeps meticulous burndown charts and is known for data-driven planning meetings.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:02:14 - Asked how Phoenix compares to the new Solar app, Dev's eyes dart to the wall of completed feature cards and he straightens his posture.","10:02:58 - When Priya mentions 'sunk cost,' Dev's face flushes; he crosses his arms and stops making eye contact.","10:03:31 - He taps the burndown chart with his pen and says 'we are past the point of no return' while his voice rises half an octave.","10:04:02 - Presented with the Solar forecast, he pushes the report away and turns back to his Post-it wall.","10:04:40 - He begins recounting every feature 'we already built' as if that were a reason to build more."],"interrogation_transcript":["Priya: 'The forecast says Phoenix returns less than starting Solar fresh. What is your recommendation?'","Dev: 'We are $4 million deep. Walking away now would waste all of it.'","Priya: 'That money is already spent. It does not come back either way.'","Dev: 'Look at what we have shipped. Killing Phoenix is admitting I sold you all a broken roadmap.'","Priya: 'So we should put another $6 million into it?'","Dev: 'If we stop now, my whole two-year record at this company is a failure. Give it one more cycle.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The sunk-cost fallacy compounded by the agency problem: Dev's 'already $4 million deep' reasoning ignores that the money is gone, while his fear of a tainted record makes gambling more of the firm's money serve his own interests.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Dev anchors on the spent $4 million and treats completed work as a reason to continue, which is exactly throwing good money after bad. His explicit line 'killing Phoenix is admitting I sold you a broken roadmap' reveals the mental account he refuses to close at a loss. Priya's counterfactual, that the money does not come back, is the rational Econ move he cannot adopt. The agency element is his motive: a cancelled project stains his record, so his incentives are misaligned with the firm's.","distractors":["Loss aversion in gambles, because he is refusing a 50/50 bet that could break even.","Anchoring, because the Solar forecast's $6 million figure is anchoring his estimate of Phoenix's value.","The endowment effect, because he overvalues features the team already owns."]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Regret, Blame, and the Default Option","visual_frame_description":"A split courtroom sketch shows two witnesses: Mr. Brown, a man who almost never hitchhikes, and Mr. Smith, a habitual rider, both robbed after giving a ride yesterday. Above them float two counters - 'felt regret' and 'others blame' - plus a blackjack table where players answer 'Do you wish to hit?' and a stock chart where George sold and Paul did not.","player_mission":"Sort each regret or blame reaction into the mechanism that produces it: departure from the default, abnormal events that are easy to undo in imagination, or violation of conventional norms as judged by others.","key_concepts":["Regret is one of the counterfactual emotions triggered by the availability of alternatives to reality.","Unusual events are easier than normal events to undo in imagination, and abnormal events activate the idea of the event that would have been normal.","88% of respondents said Mr. Brown, who almost never picks up hitchhikers, would feel greater regret after being robbed than the habitual rider Mr. Smith.","Regret and blame are both evoked by comparison to a norm, but the relevant norms are different.","Observers compare both men to conventional norms and blame Mr. Smith for habitually taking unreasonable risks.","92% of respondents said George, who switched stocks and lost $1,200, feels more regret than Paul, who merely failed to switch.","People expect stronger emotional reactions to an outcome produced by action than to the same outcome produced by inaction.","The key is not commission versus omission but the distinction between default options and actions that deviate from the default.","In a blackjack simulation, saying yes to 'Do you wish to hit?' was associated with much more regret than saying no if the outcome was bad.","The physician who chooses an unconventional treatment faces a substantial risk of regret, blame, and litigation because the normal choice is easier to imagine."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario separates the counterfactual emotions that drive regret and blame: regret intensifies when an event departs from the default or is abnormal, while blame tracks deviation from conventional norms. Kahneman shows the departure-from-default account explains why George's action stings more than Paul's inaction and why a 'yes' in the blackjack simulation produces more regret than a 'no.'","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each reaction by the mechanism that produces it: departure from the default, abnormal-event counterfactuals, or observer blame for violating conventional norms.","column_a_label":"Regret from departing from the default","column_b_label":"Regret from abnormal events (easy to undo)","column_c_label":"Blame for departing from conventional norms","rows":[{"label":"George, who switched stocks and lost $1,200, feels more regret than Paul, who merely failed to switch","col":"a"},{"label":"Mr. Brown, who almost never hitchhikes, feels far more regret after being robbed on his rare ride than habitual rider Mr. Smith","col":"b"},{"label":"An observer criticizes Mr. Smith, the habitual risk-taker, most severely for the same robbery","col":"c"},{"label":"A blackjack player asked 'Do you wish to hit?' regrets a bad outcome more if the answer was yes","col":"a"},{"label":"A coach who makes no changes after a bad loss is blamed for the failure to act, because action was the default","col":"a"},{"label":"Passengers who 'should not' have been on a crashed plane - the last-minute seat, the bumped flight - are singled out in memorial stories","col":"b"},{"label":"A physician who prescribes an unusual treatment and gets a bad outcome fears regret, blame, and litigation","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Regret and blame are identical judgments made against the same norm.","George's regret is larger because his financial loss was objectively larger than Paul's.","Mr. Smith feels more regret because he took the risk knowingly and should have known better."],"explanation":"The first row is the classic action-versus-inaction result: George acted, and departing from the default of holding his stock makes the counterfactual vivid. The abnormal-event rows (b) work by undoing: rare events are easy to reverse in imagination. The blame rows (c) are what an observer compares to conventional norms of reasonable behavior."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Anticipated Regret and the Conventional-Choice Bias","visual_frame_description":"A hospital ward at dusk. Dr. Imani Okonkwo, an oncologist, stands at a patient's bedside chart, her pen hovering between two protocol boxes: 'standard of care' and 'experimental trial.' A colleague has slid an inconclusive evidence note under her door. Her shoulders are tight and she keeps looking at the clock, rehearsing the future she fears.","player_mission":"Analyze Dr. Okonkwo's behavior as she weighs a conventional treatment against an unconventional one and diagnose the emotion steering her toward the safe option.","key_concepts":["Regret is an emotion and also a punishment we administer to ourselves, triggered by the availability of alternatives to reality.","The asymmetry in the risk of regret favors conventional and risk-averse choices.","The physician who prescribes an unusual treatment faces a substantial risk of regret, blame, and perhaps litigation.","In hindsight it will be easier to imagine the normal choice; the abnormal choice will be easy to undo.","A good outcome contributes to the reputation of the physician who dared, but the potential benefit is smaller than the potential cost.","Departure from the default option is a natural candidate for regret or blame.","Consumers who are reminded they may feel regret show an increased preference for conventional options, favoring brand names over generics.","Financial fund managers clean their portfolios of unconventional stocks as the year approaches its end, an effect of anticipated evaluation.","If you can remember when things go badly that you considered the possibility of regret carefully, you are likely to experience less of it.","Regret and hindsight bias come together, so anything that precludes hindsight is likely to be helpful."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests anticipated regret: the physician avoids the unconventional treatment because the normal choice is easier to imagine and undo if it fails, making its potential cost loom larger than its potential benefit. Kahneman shows this asymmetry in regret risk reliably favors conventional, risk-averse choices.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which psychological force is most strongly shaping Dr. Okonkwo's lean toward the standard treatment?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Okonkwo is a 52-year-old oncologist with a reputation for reading trial literature and for being the first on her floor to adopt new protocols. She has given grand rounds on evidence-based medicine and has no history of lawsuit avoidance.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:12:03 - Reading the inconclusive trial note, she taps the pen against her teeth and her gaze drifts to the hospital's legal-risk poster on the wall.","15:13:20 - When her colleague says the trial 'could help,' she nods but her lips compress and she looks at the ceiling.","15:14:41 - She begins to recite the standard protocol's success figures, her voice steadier than at any point in the session.","15:16:05 - Asked what she would tell the family if the trial failed, she closes her eyes briefly and says 'I would replay that conversation every night.'","15:17:30 - She picks up the standard-of-care chart and starts writing orders, visibly relieved."],"interrogation_transcript":["Colleague: 'The trial data is promising but inconclusive. What are you leaning toward?'","Dr. Okonkwo: 'Standard of care. I keep imagining the family's face if the trial went wrong.'","Colleague: 'And if standard care goes wrong?'","Dr. Okonkwo: 'That is different. Everyone would understand. Nobody would ask why I did it.'","Colleague: 'The trial could raise her chances.'","Dr. Okonkwo: 'Maybe. But if it fails, I chose it. I can still see myself being cross-examined about it in five years.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Anticipated regret driving a preference for the conventional option: Dr. Okonkwo is pricing the future counterfactual of a failed unusual choice more heavily than the potential benefit, because the abnormal choice is easy to undo and hard to defend.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her self-reported 'I keep imagining the family's face' and 'if it fails, I chose it' are textbook anticipated regret: the outcome produced by action produces stronger emotion than the same outcome by inaction. Her claim that standard care 'would be different' captures the default-option logic - departing from the default is a natural candidate for regret and blame. The potential benefit of daring is real but smaller than the potential cost because success is generally a more normal outcome than failure.","distractors":["Anchoring, because the trial's success statistics anchor her estimate of the standard treatment's value.","The halo effect, because she overgeneralizes the colleague's enthusiasm to the evidence quality.","Availability, because a recent malpractice news story about trials is dominating her risk estimate."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Winning-Ledger Investor vs. the Disposition Effect","visual_frame_description":"Priyanka's dining table is strewn with wedding invitation samples and two stock certificates: Blueberry Tiles, glowing green with a +$5,000 tag, and Tiffany Motors, glowing red with a -$5,000 tag. A tax-form calculator sits folded at the edge of the table. She reaches for the green certificate and you see the words 'close as a win' floating over it.","player_mission":"First determine which stock Priyanka should sell to protect her wealth, then coach her through the emotional pull to keep the loser, countering the disposition effect.","key_concepts":["The disposition effect is a massive, documented preference for selling winners rather than losers.","The investor sets up an account for each share and wants to close every account as a gain.","A rational agent sells the stock least likely to do well in the future, without considering whether it is a winner or a loser.","In the United States, realizing losses reduces your taxes while selling winners exposes you to taxes.","Investors sell more losers in December, when taxes are on their mind, but mental accounting prevails for the other eleven months.","Stocks that recently gained in value are likely to go on gaining, at least for a short while.","The expected after-tax extra return of selling the loser rather than the winner is 3.4% over the next year.","Closing a mental account with a gain is a pleasure, but it is a pleasure you pay for.","Experienced investors, using System 2, are less susceptible to the disposition effect than novices.","An Econ would treat the purchase price as sunk and ignore it in deciding what to sell."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the disposition effect: Priyanka wants to close the Blueberry Tiles account at a gain, but wealth-maximizing choice favors selling the loser Tiffany Motors for tax and momentum reasons. Kahneman reports the expected after-tax extra return of selling the loser is about 3.4% over the next year.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the disposition effect: investors treat each holding as a separate mental account and sell winners to lock in scored gains, even though taxes and momentum make selling losers the wealth-optimizing move.","stage_1_observation":"Priyanka needs cash for her daughter's wedding and owns two equal positions: Blueberry Tiles, worth $5,000 more than she paid, and Tiffany Motors, worth $5,000 less. Both have been stable for weeks. She is reaching for Blueberry Tiles 'to feel like a successful investor.'","stage_1_question":"Which stock should Priyanka sell to serve her wealth rather than her emotions?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Sell Tiffany Motors, the loser. Realized losses reduce taxes while selling winners exposes the gain to taxes, recent winners tend to keep gaining, and the expected after-tax extra return of selling the loser is about 3.4% over the next year. The buying price matters only through taxes, not as a score to settle.","stage_2_intervention":"Priyanka hesitates: 'Selling Tiffany is admitting I was wrong to buy it. Blueberry makes me feel like I won.' How do you respond to break the disposition effect?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reframe her as a rational agent: the money is already spent on both stocks, so only future returns and taxes should decide; explicitly separate her 'investor score' from the portfolio's wealth. Point to the tax deduction and momentum, and note that experienced investors who engage System 2 resist exactly this bias."}]},{"id":"ch38","title":"33. Reversals","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Single vs. Joint Evaluation and the Burglary Compensation Reversal","visual_frame_description":"A crime-victim compensation office displays two victim cards side by side: a man shot in his regular convenience store and the same man shot in the other store while his regular one was closed for a funeral. A toggle on the screen lets the player view the cases 'alone' or 'together,' and dollar amounts shift as the toggle flips.","player_mission":"Classify each judgment by the evaluation mode that produced it: single evaluation, joint evaluation, or a within-category judgment that shows no reversal.","key_concepts":["In joint evaluation, where both scenarios are seen together, there is almost universal agreement that compensation should be the same in both.","In single evaluation, the victim was awarded a much larger sum if he was shot in the store he rarely visited.","Poignancy is a counterfactual feeling evoked because 'if only he had shopped at his regular store' comes readily to mind.","Substitution and intensity matching translate the strength of the emotional reaction to the story onto a monetary scale.","We normally experience life in the between-subjects mode, where contrasting alternatives are absent, and of course WYSIATI.","The beliefs you endorse when you reflect about morality do not necessarily govern your emotional reactions.","Joint evaluation always involves a more careful and effortful assessment, which calls for System 2.","The emotional reactions of System 1 are much more likely to determine single evaluation.","Poignancy is a close cousin of regret.","Judgments are coherent within categories but potentially incoherent when the objects belong to different categories."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the single-versus-joint evaluation reversal: alone, the rare-store victim draws a larger award driven by poignancy and System 1 intensity matching, but seen together, almost everyone endorses equal compensation. Kahneman shows the moral principle becomes relevant only when both scenarios are viewed together, which is not how life usually works.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each judgment by the evaluation mode that produced it: single evaluation, joint evaluation, or a within-category judgment that does not reverse.","column_a_label":"Single evaluation (System 1 intensity)","column_b_label":"Joint evaluation (System 2 comparison)","column_c_label":"Coherent within a category (no reversal)","rows":[{"label":"The victim shot in the store he rarely visits is awarded a much larger sum when each scenario is seen alone","col":"a"},{"label":"Anyone who sees both burglary scenarios together agrees compensation should be identical","col":"b"},{"label":"John, 5'0 at age 6, is judged 'very tall,' while Jim, 5'1 at age 16, is judged 'not tall,' each against his own norm","col":"c"},{"label":"Dictionary A (10,000 entries, like new) is valued more highly than dictionary B (20,000 entries, torn cover) when shown alone","col":"a"},{"label":"Shown together, dictionary B wins because the entry count is obviously more important than the cover","col":"b"},{"label":"Apples and peaches keep the same ranking whether rated singly or jointly","col":"c"},{"label":"Bet B is chosen over bet A when the two are compared head-to-head","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Poignancy is a legitimate factor that compensation rules actually endorse.","Single evaluation engages System 2 and therefore produces more stable judgments.","The regular-store victim deserves a larger award because the shooting happened where he felt safe."],"explanation":"The first row is the Miller and McFarland between-subjects finding: poignancy drives a larger single-evaluation award. Joint-evaluation rows (b) are cases where explicit comparison engages System 2 and stabilizes the judgment. The category rows (c) use one norm for both items, so single and joint evaluations agree."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Poignancy and the Compensation Award","visual_frame_description":"A workers' compensation office. Claims adjuster Tanya Reyes reads a file with two claims from the same week: a machinist injured in the factory he has worked in for twelve years, and a courier who was substituting in an unfamiliar depot. Her pen hovers over the second claim's award field, and a sticky note reads 'if only he had been at his regular site.'","player_mission":"Read Tanya's behavior as she awards compensation to each claimant and diagnose why the two awards, judged one after the other, diverge so sharply.","key_concepts":["Poignancy is a counterfactual feeling, a close cousin of regret, evoked when 'if only' thoughts come readily to mind.","Unusual events are easier than normal events to undo in imagination.","Substitution and intensity matching translate the strength of an emotional reaction onto a monetary scale.","Single evaluation is much more likely to be determined by the emotional reactions of System 1.","Joint evaluation involves a more careful and effortful assessment, which calls for System 2.","We normally experience life in the between-subjects mode, in which contrasting alternatives are absent, and of course WYSIATI.","In single evaluation, the victim was awarded a much larger sum when the event was abnormal.","Almost everyone who sees both scenarios together endorses the principle that poignancy is not a legitimate consideration.","Moral intuitions that come to mind in different situations are not internally consistent.","The principle that poignancy is irrelevant becomes applicable only when the scenarios are seen together."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests poignancy and intensity matching in single evaluation: Tanya awards more to the abnormal-site injury because the counterfactual 'if only' is easy to construct, and she maps the strength of that emotion straight onto dollars. Kahneman shows this System 1 reaction reverses once the two cases are judged jointly.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Why does Tanya award the substituting courier substantially more than the machinist injured at his regular workplace?","subject_baseline":"Tanya Reyes is a 34-year-old claims adjuster with five years of experience who follows the published compensation schedule line by line and prides herself on never improvising amounts. Her supervisors rate her as consistent.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:31:05 - Reading the machinist's file, she flips to the schedule, finds the standard figure, and enters it without hesitation.","09:33:18 - Opening the courier's file, her eyebrows rise, and she reads the 'was covering a shift at an unfamiliar depot' line twice.","09:34:02 - She murmurs the words 'if only he had been at his regular site' and sets her pen down.","09:34:41 - Rather than reaching for the schedule, she writes a number from memory, then stares at it and nods slowly.","09:35:20 - Asked to justify it, she hesitates and says 'the story just hits differently' before finding her usual schedule reference."],"interrogation_transcript":["Manager: 'The machinist was injured after twelve years at that plant. Why does he get the base award?'","Tanya: 'Because that is exactly the schedule for his injury class.'","Manager: 'And the courier who was substituting?'","Tanya: 'I gave him more. He should not have been there at all.'","Manager: 'The injury is identical. Same body part, same treatment cost.'","Tanya: 'I know. But when I picture him, I keep thinking - if he had just been at his own depot...'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Poignancy-driven single evaluation: the courier's abnormal location makes the counterfactual 'if only' vivid, and Tanya substitutes that emotional intensity for the schedule number, inflating the award.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her automatic murmur 'if only he had been at his regular site' is the exact poignancy trigger the book describes, and her 'the story just hits differently' is substitution of an emotional reaction for a dollar value. She bypasses her own schedule, the behavior she shows on the machinist's normal case, which is evidence of System 1 intensity matching rather than calculation. If she saw both cases together, the poignancy would be ruled illegitimate.","distractors":["The halo effect, because the courier's clean uniform makes her rate his whole file more favorably.","Anchoring, because the machinist's base award anchors her downward estimate of the courier's case.","Availability, because a recent news report about substitute-worker accidents is inflating her award."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Preference Reversal Bazaar: Choice vs. Pricing","visual_frame_description":"A neon gambling arcade lit in red and blue. A roulette wheel with 36 sectors spins behind a counter where two bet cards float: Bet A, '11/36 win $160, 25/36 lose $15,' and Bet B, '35/36 win $40, 1/36 lose $10.' Traders crowd the choice board on the left and the pricing booth on the right, and the two lists are visibly inconsistent.","player_mission":"First explain why the same people choose Bet B but price Bet A higher, then defend the finding against economists determined to discredit it.","key_concepts":["Between a safe bet and a riskier one, safety prevails and Bet B is clearly the more popular choice.","When asked the lowest price at which they would sell each bet, people set a higher selling price on Bet A than on Bet B.","The preference reversal occurs because joint evaluation focuses attention on how much less safe Bet A is.","In single evaluation the selling price is anchored on the salient prize of $160.","The emotional reactions of System 1 determine single evaluation; comparison in joint evaluation calls for System 2.","Sarah Lichtenstein and Paul Slovic reported the original preference reversals between bets.","Grether and Plott published a study in the American Economic Review designed to discredit the psychologists' findings.","Of thirteen theories, only one hypothesis was left standing: the psychologists were right.","That hypothesis was least satisfactory to standard preference theory because it allows individual choice to depend on context.","In a within-subject experiment, participants are unaware of the inconsistency and react with bewilderment when confronted with it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the preference reversal: joint evaluation makes Bet A's riskiness salient so people choose B, while single evaluation anchors the selling price on the $160 prize so they price A higher. Kahneman recounts how Grether and Plott tried to discredit the finding and confirmed it instead, a landmark challenge to the coherence of preference theory.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the Lichtenstein and Slovic preference reversal: a choice between bets and a selling price for the same bets produce contradictory orderings because different features are salient in single versus joint evaluation.","stage_1_observation":"At the arcade counter, Bet A offers 11/36 to win $160 with 25/36 to lose $15, and Bet B offers 35/36 to win $40 with 1/36 to lose $10. Watching the traders: they pick B when choosing between the two, but when each trader is asked alone for the lowest price at which they would sell one of the bets, the prices on A run higher.","stage_1_question":"Why does the reversal happen?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Choosing between the bets jointly highlights that A is much less safe, so safety prevails and B wins; pricing each bet alone anchors the valuation on the salient $160 prize, so A gets the higher price. System 1's emotional reactions dominate single evaluation, while the comparison in joint evaluation calls in System 2.","stage_2_intervention":"Two skeptical economists, modeling Grether and Plott, demand proof that the reversal is real and list rival explanations. How do you demonstrate that the reversal is genuine?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Run a within-subject design: have the same people price both bets on one long list and then choose between them, and confront them with the inconsistency. Like Lichtenstein's 1968 interview, a participant will choose one bet and then be willing to pay to exchange it for the one he just rejected, cycling repeatedly. This is exactly the test that left only the psychologists' hypothesis standing."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Anchored Verdict: Mock Jurors and Punitive Damages","visual_frame_description":"A Texas jury deliberation room. Mock juror Marcus Webb studies two civil cases on a tablet: Case 1, a child burned when his pajamas caught fire while playing with matches, and Case 2, a bank defrauded by another bank's unscrupulous dealings for $10 million. Marcus jots numbers first beside the bank, then, after rereading the child's case, he crosses the number out and writes a larger one.","player_mission":"Trace Marcus's punitive-damage awards across single and joint evaluation and diagnose what shifts his verdict when the second case appears.","key_concepts":["In single evaluation, mock jurors awarded higher punitive damages to the defrauded bank than to the burned child.","The size of the financial loss, $10 million, provided a high anchor for the bank's award.","When the cases were considered together, sympathy for the individual victim prevailed over the anchoring effect.","In joint evaluation, the jurors increased the award to the child to surpass the award to the bank.","Awards to victims of personal injury were more than twice as large in joint as in single evaluation.","The jurors who saw the burned-child case alone matched the award to the intensity of their feelings.","The award to the bank remained anchored on its loss even in joint evaluation.","The punitive award to the child increased, reflecting outrage evoked by negligence that causes injury to a child.","Single evaluations often reflect the intensity of emotional responses of System 1.","The legal system favors single evaluation, contrary to psychological common sense."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests anchoring and the reversal that joint evaluation produces: alone, the bank's $10 million loss anchors a high award, but when the child's case is seen alongside it, sympathy and outrage override the anchor and push the child's award past the bank's. Kahneman notes personal-injury awards more than double in joint evaluation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What explains Marcus giving the bank the higher punitive award first, then raising the child's award when he compares the cases?","subject_baseline":"Marcus Webb, 58, is a retired high-school civics teacher called for jury duty. In everyday life he is deliberative, reads both sides of an issue, and has never been party to a lawsuit.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:02:12 - Skimming the bank case first, Marcus writes '6,500,000' quickly, then underlines the $10 million loss figure.","10:03:40 - Reading the burned child's case alone, he pauses, looks at his number, and leaves it unchanged.","10:05:07 - When the second scenario card for the child is placed beside the bank case, his eyes widen and he leans forward.","10:06:33 - He crosses out the child's number and writes a figure higher than the bank's, then rechecks the bank's line.","10:07:51 - He mutters 'a kid, versus a bank's lost quarter... that cannot be right' and revises the bank's award down slightly."],"interrogation_transcript":["Researcher: 'What did you award the bank in the first case?'","Marcus: 'Six and a half million. Their loss was ten million, so it felt like a fair fraction.'","Researcher: 'And the burned child, when you first saw only that case?'","Marcus: 'A smaller number. I thought, this is a small claim compared to the bank.'","Researcher: 'Now that you see both side by side?'","Marcus: 'Then it is obvious the child deserves more. A pajama maker that leaves a kid burned? That is worse than a bank losing money.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Anchoring in single evaluation followed by a joint-evaluation reversal: the $10 million loss anchors the bank's award until the child's case is seen together with it, at which point sympathy and outrage override the anchor and push the child's award past the bank's.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Marcus's initial award scales off the $10 million figure ('fair fraction'), which is anchoring on the size of the financial loss. His single-evaluation number for the child matches the intensity of his feelings, exactly as the mock-jury experiment found. When the cases are compared, the 'human versus money' comparison becomes salient, his outrage at negligence to a child takes over, and he revises both figures - the personal-injury award rising to surpass the bank's, consistent with awards more than doubling in joint evaluation.","distractors":["The availability heuristic, because recent news about child burns is inflating his sympathy.","The affect heuristic, because the child's case triggers a like/dislike reaction that replaces all reasoning.","Loss aversion, because he frames giving the child less as a loss of fairness he wants to avoid."]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Evaluability, Category Norms, and the Dolphins vs. Farmworkers Reversal","visual_frame_description":"A donation screen shows two appeal letters: a dolphin rescue fund and a farmworker skin-cancer screening fund. In single view, the dolphin letter attracts a larger pledge and a charm meter fills. In joint view, the scales visibly tip to the farmworkers as the words 'human vs. animal' light up. Beside them float a 1993 music dictionary and two height charts for six-year-old John and sixteen-year-old Jim.","player_mission":"Classify each reversal by its mechanism: evaluability, category-based norms, or the narrow framing of single evaluation (WYSIATI).","key_concepts":["Dolphins attracted somewhat larger contributions than farmworkers in single evaluation.","The dolphin has a highly favorable rank among the species to which it is spontaneously compared.","The farmworkers' problem did not rank high among public-health issues, which was the category it evoked as a norm.","In joint evaluation, people show a solid preference for the farmworkers and contribute substantially more to their welfare.","The 'human vs. animal' feature becomes salient only when the two causes are seen together.","The narrow framing of single evaluation allowed dolphins to have a higher intensity score.","The fact that farmworkers are human did not come to mind because all public-health issues involve humans.","Hsee's evaluability hypothesis holds that the number of entries in a dictionary is given no weight in single evaluation because the numbers are not evaluable on their own.","In joint evaluation it is immediately obvious that the 20,000-entry dictionary is superior and that entries matter more than the condition of the cover.","Judgments and preferences are coherent within categories but potentially incoherent when the objects belong to different categories."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests why a charming species outearns a human cause in single evaluation: WYSIATI makes the comparison category invisible, so intensity matching favors dolphins, while joint evaluation makes the 'human vs. animal' feature salient and reverses the choice. It also tests Hsee's evaluability hypothesis, where an attribute is ignored unless it can be compared.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each judgment by the mechanism behind it: evaluability, category-based norms, or the narrow framing of single evaluation (WYSIATI).","column_a_label":"Evaluability hypothesis","column_b_label":"Category-based norms","column_c_label":"Narrow framing of single evaluation (WYSIATI)","rows":[{"label":"The number of entries in a dictionary is given no weight in single evaluation because 10,000 versus 20,000 is not evaluable on its own","col":"a"},{"label":"In joint evaluation the 20,000-entry dictionary wins because entries are obviously more important than the torn cover","col":"a"},{"label":"A six-year-old who is 5'0 is very tall, while a sixteen-year-old at 5'1 is not, because each is matched to the norm for his age","col":"b"},{"label":"Dolphins attract larger donations than farmworkers in single evaluation because only similar species come to mind","col":"c"},{"label":"The farmworkers' human-ness never surfaces in single evaluation because every public-health issue involves humans","col":"c"},{"label":"Apples and peaches, both fruit, rank identically whether rated singly or jointly","col":"b"},{"label":"A restaurant meal 'matching' John's height costs much less if John is 6 than if he is 16","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Dolphins outearn farmworkers even in joint evaluation because of their charisma.","The torn cover is more important than the entry count in any presentation.","Within a category, single and joint judgments usually disagree."],"explanation":"The first row is Hsee's evaluability hypothesis: a standalone number like 10,000 has no reference, so it is ignored until comparison makes it matter (column a). The height and apple rows are within-category norms, coherent in both modes (b). The dolphin and farmworker rows are narrow-framing effects where the relevant comparison category is invisible in single evaluation (c)."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Context Artist: Manipulating Comparison","visual_frame_description":"A showroom floor of a high-end furniture store. Salesperson Dario Menendez walks a couple, Leah and Owen, past a $2,900 armchair displayed alone beside a pricey designer sofa, then waits while they circle back. He watches their hands touch the cheaper chair and adjusts which piece is lit by the spotlight before they decide.","player_mission":"Watch how Dario controls what the customers see and when, then diagnose the psychological lever he is pulling on their preferences.","key_concepts":["Salespeople quickly learn that manipulation of the context in which customers see a good can profoundly influence preferences.","Joint evaluation focuses attention on features that are not salient in single evaluation.","Single evaluations often reflect the intensity of emotional responses of System 1.","The comparative judgment, which necessarily involves System 2, is more likely to be stable than single evaluations.","WYSIATI means that what you see is all there is, so absent contrasting alternatives do not enter the judgment.","When one option is presented alone, its evaluation is anchored on whatever attribute is salient in that context.","A broad frame leads to more reasonable decisions, and joint evaluation is obviously broader than single evaluation.","You should be wary of joint evaluation when someone who controls what you see has a vested interest in what you choose.","Except for deliberate manipulation, the comparative judgment is presumed more stable than single evaluation.","Manipulation of context can make a mid-priced option look like a bargain against a higher-priced neighbor."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests deliberate manipulation of evaluation context: Dario stages which goods appear together so that a joint evaluation makes the mid-priced chair look like a bargain. Kahneman warns that broad comparison is generally good but dangerous when someone who controls what you see has a vested interest in your choice.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"What psychological lever is Dario pulling to steer Leah and Owen toward the $2,900 armchair?","subject_baseline":"Dario Menendez, 39, has been a furniture salesperson for eleven years and is the store's top seller by commission. He keeps the sales floor meticulously arranged and is known among colleagues for 'curating' which pieces are displayed together.","observed_nonverbal_log":["13:02:11 - Dario positions the $2,900 armchair beside the $7,400 designer sofa, then steps back and studies how the couple's eyes move between them.","13:04:33 - When Leah stops in front of the armchair alone, he angles the spotlight to leave it in shadow and lights the sofa instead.","13:06:47 - He waits until the couple has seen the price tag of the sofa before he mentions the armchair's price, watching Owen's face as he reads it.","13:08:02 - As they hesitate, he drapes a display card over the armchair's price and slides the sofa's card into full view.","13:10:24 - When Leah says the armchair 'suddenly seems reasonable,' Dario's posture relaxes and he reaches for the order pad."],"interrogation_transcript":["Leah: 'We came in looking for one nice reading chair, around two thousand.'","Dario: 'Of course. Have a seat in this one. You will not believe the frame on it.'","Owen: 'This sofa behind it is beautiful too. Is it much more?'","Dario: 'That one is seven-four. But compared to it, this chair is an absolute steal.'","Leah: 'It really is, for what you get.'","Dario: 'Exactly. And honestly, you will not find this leather in a cheaper range anywhere.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Deliberate manipulation of comparison context: Dario stages the sofa next to the armchair so the joint evaluation anchors on the high price, making the $2,900 chair look like a bargain and shifting the customers' reference point.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Dario controls which items appear together and which price is visible, engineering a joint evaluation that favors the mid-priced chair. The sequence - spotlighting the sofa, hiding the chair's price, then revealing it after the $7,400 anchor - is exactly the context manipulation Kahneman warns about. The customers' feeling that the chair 'suddenly seems reasonable' is the comparative judgment being steered, which is why the book cautions that broad comparison is valuable only when the person controlling what you see has no vested interest.","distractors":["The endowment effect, because the couple has already sat in the chair and now owns it psychologically.","Loss aversion, because he frames missing the chair as a loss the couple would regret.","Priming, because the spotlight on the sofa primes the couple's brain toward the color of the chair."]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Why the Psychologists Won: Anatomy of a Reversal","visual_frame_description":"A research archive splits into three columns: 'Choice board' where Bet B wins, 'Pricing booth' where Bet A prices higher, and a chalkboard of Econ axioms labeled 'coherence doctrine.' A grainy 1968 interview tape shows a bewildered participant swapping his chosen bet back and forth while a researcher annotates his moves.","player_mission":"Classify each statement about the bets into the account it supports: joint-evaluation choice, single-evaluation pricing, or the rational-Econ coherence doctrine that the reversal violates.","key_concepts":["Bet B wins the head-to-head choice because joint evaluation makes its safety salient.","Bet A gets the higher selling price alone because the price is anchored on the salient $160 prize.","The preference reversal occurs because joint evaluation focuses attention on how much less safe Bet A is.","The emotional reactions of System 1 are much more likely to determine single evaluation.","The comparison that occurs in joint evaluation always involves a more careful and effortful assessment that calls for System 2.","Lichtenstein and Slovic reported the reversals; Grether and Plott tried to discredit them and could not.","Grether and Plott listed thirteen theories that could explain the findings, including that the experimenters were psychologists.","Only one hypothesis was left standing: the psychologists were right, which allows individual choice to depend on context.","In a within-subject experiment participants are unaware of the inconsistency and react with bewilderment when confronted with it.","The reversal is a violation of the coherence doctrine that standard preference theory relies on."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario maps the anatomy of the Lichtenstein-Slovic reversal: joint evaluation of the bets makes riskiness salient so safety wins, while single evaluation anchors prices on the prize. Kahneman recounts that Grether and Plott's attempt to discredit the finding left only the psychologists' hypothesis standing, confirming that context dependence is real.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Classify each statement by the account it supports: joint-evaluation choice, single-evaluation pricing, or the Econ coherence doctrine.","column_a_label":"Joint evaluation / safety salience","column_b_label":"Single evaluation / anchoring on the prize","column_c_label":"Econ coherence doctrine","rows":[{"label":"Bet B wins the head-to-head choice because joint evaluation makes its safety salient","col":"a"},{"label":"Bet A gets the higher selling price alone because the price is anchored on the $160 prize","col":"b"},{"label":"A rational Econ values each bet by expected value and shows no reversal","col":"c"},{"label":"In a within-subject study, participants price both bets and choose between them without noticing the contradiction","col":"b"},{"label":"Lichtenstein's 1968 participant chose one bet but paid to swap it for the one he had rejected","col":"b"},{"label":"Grether and Plott set out to discredit the psychologists but left only the psychologists' hypothesis standing","col":"c"},{"label":"Confronted with both bets, the riskiness of Bet A - not its prize - dominates the choice","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The reversal disappears when economists run the experiment themselves.","Bet A's higher selling price shows that people love risk in their valuations.","Preference reversals are explained by simple arithmetic mistakes by the participants."],"explanation":"The first row sorts to a because safety only becomes salient when the bets are compared. The pricing rows (b) capture anchoring on the $160 prize in single evaluation, including the within-subject inconsistency and the 1968 swap. The coherence rows (c) are the Econ doctrine the reversal challenges, including Grether and Plott's failed attempt to discredit it."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Seven-Thousand-Dollar Fine: Regulatory Incoherence","visual_frame_description":"Two enforcement desks side by side in a Washington hearing room. On the left, an OSHA badge pins a $7,000 cap on a serious worker-safety violation; on the right, a wildlife officer notes a $25,000 fine ceiling for a Wild Bird Conservation Act violation. A monitor plays stacked penalty lists for each agency, each list internally sensible and the two lists visibly absurd together.","player_mission":"First diagnose why the penalty system looks absurd only when the two agencies are compared, then design the reform that makes penalties coherent across agencies.","key_concepts":["The fine for a serious violation of worker-safety regulations is capped at $7,000, while a violation of the Wild Bird Conservation Act can be fined up to $25,000.","Within categories, penalties seem extremely sensible: more serious harms are punished more severely.","For occupational safety violations, the largest penalties are for repeated violations, then willful and serious ones, and least for failures in record-keeping.","The size of penalties varies greatly across agencies, reflecting politics and history more than global fairness.","The fines are sensible in the context of other penalties set by each agency but appear odd when compared to each other.","You can see the absurdity only when the two cases are viewed together in a broad frame.","The system of administrative penalties is coherent within agencies but incoherent globally.","Joint evaluation is obviously broader than single evaluation and generally serves rationality.","Any institution that wishes to elicit thoughtful judgments should provide judges a broad context for individual assessments.","The legal system favors single evaluation, contrary to psychological common sense."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests Sunstein's finding that administrative penalties are coherent within each agency but incoherent globally: each agency sets fines against its own internal category of penalties, so the $7,000 safety fine and the $25,000 bird fine coexist. The book shows the absurdity is visible only in a broad joint frame, which is why broad comparison improves decisions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is incoherence across evaluation contexts: penalties that are perfectly sensible within an agency's own category become absurd when different agencies' scales are compared, because no single global norm binds them.","stage_1_observation":"You are auditing the federal penalty book. The Occupational Safety and Health Administration caps a serious worker-safety violation at $7,000, while the Wild Bird Conservation Act allows fines up to $25,000. Inside each agency the penalties scale sensibly: repeated violations cost the most, then willful and serious ones, with record-keeping failures cheapest.","stage_1_question":"Why does the system look absurd when you compare the two agencies?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because each agency sets penalties relative to its own internal category of harms, so fines are coherent within agencies but globally incoherent. The absurdity of a bird violation costing more than a serious safety hazard becomes visible only when both scales are viewed together in a broad frame, exactly the joint evaluation the legal system avoids.","stage_2_intervention":"As a policy adviser, you are asked to fix the incoherence. Which approach produces more stable, thoughtful penalties?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Force cross-agency joint evaluation: place each agency's severity scale side by side and calibrate fines to a common metric of harm. Broad frames and explicit comparison engage System 2 and produce more stable judgments, which is why institutions that want thoughtful decisions should provide judges with a broad context for individual assessments."}]},{"id":"ch39","title":"34. Frames and Reality","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"KEEP and LOSE framing","visual_frame_description":"A split screen shows two versions of the same decision: a wheel-of-chance puzzle in which the sure outcome appears once as 'KEEP £20' and once as 'LOSE £30' out of an imaginary £50. A research console tracks the subject's choice beside a brain map that highlights the amygdala when the choice conforms to the frame. Three sorting bins labeled KEEP frame, LOSE frame, and reality-bound evaluation sit at the bottom of the screen for the player to drop each statement card into place.","player_mission":"Classify each decision formulation into the KEEP (gain) frame, the LOSE (loss) frame, or the reality-bound evaluation that ignores the frame entirely. Then identify which column the survival-rate description of surgery belongs to and confirm the framing effect the book predicts.","key_concepts":["Logically equivalent statements such as 'Italy won' and 'France lost' evoke different associations in System 1, so they 'mean' different things even though their truth conditions are identical.","Kahneman and Tversky called the unjustified influence of formulation on beliefs and preferences a framing effect.","In the University College London study, all 20 subjects showed a framing effect: they preferred the sure thing when it was labeled KEEP £20 and accepted the gamble when the same option was labeled LOSE £30.","The amygdala, a region linked to emotional arousal, was most likely to be active when a subject's choice conformed to the frame, as expected if the words KEEP and LOSE directly evoke approach or avoidance.","The anterior cingulate, associated with conflict and self-control, was more active when subjects resisted the frame, such as choosing the sure thing despite its being labeled LOSE.","The most rational subjects, least susceptible to framing, showed enhanced activity in a frontal area implicated in combining emotion and reasoning, and they chose with little neural evidence of conflict.","In the Harvard Medical School lung-cancer experiment, 84% of physicians chose surgery when the short-term risk was framed as a 'one-month survival rate of 90%' but 50% favored radiation when the identical statistic was framed as '10% mortality in the first month.'","Physicians were just as susceptible to the framing effect as hospital patients and graduate students, showing that medical training is no defense against framing.","Reframing is effortful and System 2 is normally lazy, so most people passively accept decision problems as they are framed and rarely discover how frame-bound their preferences are.","The emotion evoked by a word can leak into the final choice, which is why the framing study is a good illustration of emotion influencing decision."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the framing effect: System 1 reacts to emotionally loaded words, so logically identical outcomes produce different choices when described as gains versus losses. Kahneman and Tversky demonstrated that framing reverses preferences on the same objective outcomes, and neuroimaging showed the amygdala driving frame-conforming choices while the anterior cingulate signals the conflict of resisting the frame.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Physicians in the classic lung-cancer experiment chose surgery far more often when the short-term risk was described as a 'one-month survival rate of 90%' than when it was described as '10% mortality in the first month.' Into which column does the survival-rate description belong?","column_a_label":"KEEP / gain frame (approach the sure thing)","column_b_label":"LOSE / loss frame (avoid the sure thing, accept the gamble)","column_c_label":"Reality-bound evaluation (Econs' frame-neutral view)","rows":[{"label":"The sure outcome described as keeping £20 out of £50","col":"a"},{"label":"The same sure outcome described as losing £30 out of £50","col":"b"},{"label":"Physician reads that the one-month survival rate is 90%","col":"a"},{"label":"Physician reads that there is 10% mortality in the first month","col":"b"},{"label":"An alert subject translates the LOSE £30 into its KEEP £20 equivalent before choosing","col":"c"},{"label":"Asian disease Program A: 200 people will be saved","col":"a"},{"label":"Asian disease Program A': 400 people will die","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b: Survival is a negative phrasing that triggers avoidance, so the survival frame should be filed under LOSE.","c: Because the two descriptions are logically identical, physicians who saw either version must have made the same choice.","b: The survival-rate description emphasizes the risk of dying, which activates the amygdala the same way the LOSE frame does."],"explanation":"The survival-rate description belongs in the KEEP/gain column because '90% survival' evokes positive associations and an approach tendency, biasing the physician toward the safer sure thing (surgery). Its logical twin, '10% mortality,' evokes the aversive LOSE frame and pushes the same physicians toward the gamble (radiation). An Econs-style subject who translates one frame into the other, or ignores the wording entirely, belongs in the reality-bound column. The finding that 84% chose surgery under the survival frame but 50% favored radiation under the mortality frame is exactly the framing effect the chapter documents."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Opt-in versus opt-out defaults","visual_frame_description":"A map of Europe lights up donation rates: Austria near 100% and Sweden at 86% glow bright green, while Germany at 12% and Denmark at 4% sit in red. Beside the map, two blank driver-license forms show the critical difference: one requires a check box to join the donor registry and the other requires a check box to opt out. A sorting area offers three bins labeled opt-in form, opt-out form, and deliberate decision.","player_mission":"Sort each country and driver behavior into the correct column: opt-in form, opt-out form, or a deliberate System 2 decision. Then determine which form of the critical question produces the near-100% donation rate.","key_concepts":["Organ-donation rates in Europe differ dramatically between culturally similar neighbors: about 100% in Austria versus 12% in Germany, and 86% in Sweden versus 4% in Denmark.","The enormous differences are a framing effect caused by the format of the critical question on the driver's license.","In opt-out countries, individuals who do not wish to donate must check a box; in opt-in countries, you must check a box to become a donor.","The best single predictor of whether people will donate their organs is the designation of the default option that will be adopted without having to check a box.","Unlike other framing effects that have been traced to features of System 1, the organ-donation effect is best explained by the laziness of System 2.","People who have already decided what they wish to do will check the box regardless of the default.","Kahneman imagines an organ-donation form requiring people to solve '2 + 2 = ?' in one box and '13 × 37 = ?' in the other, arguing that the rate of donations would surely be swayed by the effort.","If you believe a large supply of donated organs is good for society, you cannot be neutral between a formulation that yields almost 100% donations and another that elicits donations from only 4% of drivers.","An important choice is often controlled by an utterly inconsequential feature of the situation, which is embarrassing but supported by the evidence for these cognitive illusions."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how the default option in a choice's framing governs behavior: organ-donation rates jump from roughly 4% to near 100% depending solely on whether the form is opt-in or opt-out. Kahneman attributes the effect to the laziness of System 2, which accepts the default rather than exerting the effort to check a box.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which form of the organ-donation question is associated with the near-100% donation rate in Austria and Sweden?","column_a_label":"Opt-in form (check a box to become a donor)","column_b_label":"Opt-out form (check a box to decline donation)","column_c_label":"Deliberate decision (the driver has already made up their mind)","rows":[{"label":"Austria, where roughly 100% of drivers end up as donors","col":"b"},{"label":"Germany, where only 12% of drivers become donors","col":"a"},{"label":"Sweden, with 86% of drivers donating","col":"b"},{"label":"Denmark, with only 4% of drivers donating","col":"a"},{"label":"A driver who has decided to donate and checks the box to register","col":"c"},{"label":"A driver who has decided against donation and checks the box to decline","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["a: High-donation countries must have used an opt-in form because donation is an active choice there.","c: The differences reflect each country's cultural attitude toward donation rather than the form's format.","a: Austria must be opt-in because voluntary giving is more consistent with its near-universal donation rate."],"explanation":"The opt-out form belongs to Austria and Sweden: because the default is consent, a driver who never checks a box automatically becomes a donor, and the lazy System 2 lets that default stand. The opt-in form belongs to Germany and Denmark, where donors must actively check the box, so the passive majority simply never registers. Drivers who check a box either way are demonstrating a deliberate decision, which is exactly why the default predicts donation rates so powerfully."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Mental accounting and sunk costs","visual_frame_description":"A theater lobby at night: the woman with the lost tickets stares at a locked door, while the woman who lost the cash pulls out a credit card. An open ledger on screen shows two accounts — one labeled 'Tonight's Show,' where the lost tickets are posted, and a 'General Revenue' account, where the lost cash lands. Three sorting bins labeled specific ticket account, general revenue, and costs-versus-losses await the player's classifications.","player_mission":"Sort each decision into the mental account or frame it belongs to: the narrow show-specific ticket account, the broad general-revenue account, or the costs-versus-losses framing Thaler identified. Then identify which frame produces the more reasonable decision.","key_concepts":["Most respondents believe the woman who lost the two $80 theater tickets will go home without seeing the play, while the woman who lost the $160 cash will charge the tickets.","The different judgments arise from mental accounting and the sunk-cost fallacy, with the lost tickets posted to the account associated with that particular show.","When the tickets are lost, the cost appears to have doubled and may exceed what the experience is worth, so the woman stays home.","A loss of cash is charged to a 'general revenue' account, making the woman only slightly poorer than she thought she was, so she still buys the tickets.","The cash-loss version leads to more reasonable decisions because the loss, even if it were tickets, is sunk, and sunk costs should be ignored.","Broader frames and inclusive accounts generally lead to more rational decisions than narrow accounts.","Thaler pinned a card on his board as a graduate student that read 'costs are not losses.'","People will more readily forgo a discount than pay a surcharge, so the credit-card lobby wanted any price difference labeled a cash discount rather than a credit surcharge.","The gas-station cash-discount and credit-surcharge descriptions are economically equivalent but not emotionally equivalent.","Kahneman's advice to the woman who lost tickets: 'Would you have bought tickets if you had lost the equivalent amount of cash? If yes, go ahead and buy new ones.'"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests mental accounting and the sunk-cost fallacy: the frame determines which mental account a loss is posted to, and that account decides whether the loss changes behavior. Kahneman shows that a broader, inclusive account (general revenue) ignores the sunk cost and produces more rational decisions than the narrow account tied to the specific show.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which frame produces the more reasonable decision for the woman who lost the tickets, and why?","column_a_label":"Narrow specific account (the show's ticket account)","column_b_label":"Broad inclusive account (general revenue)","column_c_label":"Costs-versus-losses framing (Thaler's 'costs are not losses')","rows":[{"label":"The lost theater tickets are posted to the account of tonight's show, doubling its apparent cost","col":"a"},{"label":"The lost cash is posted to general revenue, only slightly reducing disposable wealth","col":"b"},{"label":"Kahneman's advice to ask whether she would buy tickets if she had lost the equivalent cash","col":"b"},{"label":"Gas stations charging less for cash purchases, labeled a cash discount rather than a credit surcharge","col":"c"},{"label":"People more readily forgoing a discount than paying a surcharge","col":"c"},{"label":"The woman deciding that the doubled ticket cost exceeds what the play is worth","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["a: The narrow ticket account is more rational because it captures what the show truly costs the woman.","c: The costs-versus-losses label is the correct frame because it reminds her the tickets are sunk.","a: Posting the loss to the show account is the best frame because history should influence the decision."],"explanation":"The general-revenue frame (b) is the better one: whether the loss was tickets or cash, the money is gone, so only the current options and their likely consequences matter. Posting the lost tickets to the specific show account (a) doubles the apparent cost and triggers the sunk-cost fallacy, making her overvalue the loss and skip the play. The discount-versus-surcharge items illustrate the costs-are-not-losses insight, which is an emotional equivalence problem rather than a mental-accounting one."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Asian disease flip-flop in public health","visual_frame_description":"A conference room in a state health department. Dr. Mara Velez sits across from an auditor, two printed versions of the same outbreak problem on the table between them. A small counter on screen logs her reaction times and facial expressions. One sheet reads '200 people will be saved,' the other '400 people will die,' and the auditor watches as she gives opposite answers to identical outcomes.","player_mission":"Review Dr. Velez's baseline, her timed nonverbal reactions, and the interrogation transcript. Decide whether her two contradictory choices reveal deliberate reversal, a stable preference, or the framing effect the book documents, and record the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["In the Asian disease problem, a substantial majority of respondents choose the certain option when told '200 people will be saved,' but a large majority choose the gamble when told '400 people will die.'","The consequences of programs A and A' are identical, and so are the consequences of B and B', so the reversal is produced by the frame alone.","The different choices fit prospect theory: people are risk averse when outcomes are good and risk seeking when both outcomes are negative.","The disease problem shows that the risk-aversion and risk-seeking rules apply when outcomes are measured in lives saved or lost, not only in money.","Amos presented the Asian disease problem to public-health professionals who make decisions about vaccines, and they were as susceptible to the framing effects as anyone else.","It is worrying that officials who make decisions affecting everyone's health can be swayed by such a superficial manipulation.","When people are confronted with their inconsistency, the answer is usually embarrassed silence, because the intuitions came from System 1.","System 2 has no moral intuitions of its own to answer the question of how to decide between the framed versions."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the framing effect on important real decisions: presenting identical outbreak outcomes as 'people saved' versus 'people dying' reverses risk preference even among public-health professionals who make decisions affecting everyone's health. When the inconsistency is exposed, the book records that the answer is usually embarrassed silence, because System 1 supplied the intuitions and System 2 has no independent moral verdict.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Was Dr. Mara Velez, a state epidemiologist, deliberately reversing her position on a vaccination program, or is something else governing her two contradictory decisions?","subject_baseline":"Dr. Mara Velez is the deputy director of disease control for a state health department. In her eighteen months in the role she has built a reputation for methodical, evidence-based reviews: she reads the primary literature, asks junior analysts to re-run models, and is rarely accused of inconsistency. Her colleagues describe her as calm, slow to commit, and unusually careful about risk data.","observed_nonverbal_log":["T-30s: She reads the first version of the outbreak problem, nodding with her lips pressed together; her eyes rest on the words '200 people will be saved.'","T-0s: Asked for her choice, she answers 'Program A, the sure thing' without hesitation, leaning back in her chair as if satisfied.","T+2min: The auditor hands her the second sheet, which shows the same programs described as '400 people will die.' Her pupils widen; she frowns and rubs her temple.","T+2min45s: She reads the gamble option twice and mutters 'nobody will die' under her breath; her posture tightens.","T+3min: She announces she would gamble on Program B'. Her voice is firmer than it was for the first answer, and she nods once, as if the choice were obvious.","T+5min: Told that the two sheets describe identical outcomes, she stares at the desk for several seconds; her face goes blank and she laughs softly, shaking her head."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Dr. Velez, in the first version you chose to save 200 lives for sure. In the second, you rejected the sure thing of 400 deaths and chose the gamble. The outcomes are the same. Which is your real position?'","Dr. Velez: 'They are not the same. One saves people. The other lets them die. Nobody wants to preside over a number of deaths.'","Auditor: 'But the second version's sure option simply states the same consequence as a death count. Is there a principle that separates them?'","Dr. Velez: 'I am not sure there is. I feel differently about them, but I cannot tell you what the difference is worth. This is embarrassing.'","Auditor: 'So how do you decide, then?'","Dr. Velez: 'Honestly? Silence. I do not have an answer. I only know that my first instinct felt right at the time.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Framing effect on an otherwise rational official: Dr. Velez's contradictory choices are driven by System 1's response to the emotional words 'saved' versus 'die,' not by any underlying, stable preference. Confronted with the inconsistency, she produces exactly the 'embarrassed silence' the book predicts.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her baseline is careful and consistent, which rules out casual carelessness. The decisive evidence is the reversal itself: the two versions are logically identical, and a reality-bound decision maker would give the same answer twice. Her nonverbal reactions — the frown and widened pupils when the death framing appears, the firmer voice when endorsing the gamble — show the emotional 'leak' of the words into the choice. Prospect theory explains the direction: risk-averse for sure gains ('saved') and risk-seeking when outcomes are framed as losses ('die'). When the auditor exposes the contradiction, her blank stare and admission of 'silence' match the book's finding that System 2 has no moral intuitions of its own to arbitrate the frame-bound choice.","distractors":["Deliberate duplicity: she reversed positions for political advantage or to please the auditor, and the blank stare was a cover.","Simple risk preference: she is genuinely risk-seeking, and the first answer was a mistake she now corrects.","Decision fatigue: by the second sheet she was mentally depleted and answered impulsively, unrelated to the wording."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The MPG illusion in a fleet budget","visual_frame_description":"A fleet-management office. Priya Raman stands at a whiteboard with two lines written in marker: '12 → 14 mpg' and '30 → 40 mpg.' A calculator and a fuel-cost ledger sit on the desk. The screen displays a counter of her reaction times as she first insists Beth's switch is the bigger saving, then recomputes the gallon figures for 10,000 miles.","player_mission":"Audit Priya's fuel-saving analysis: review her arithmetic, her confident first judgment, and her correction during questioning. Decide whether her initial verdict was sound or was produced by the misleading miles-per-gallon frame.","key_concepts":["Most people share the widespread intuition that Beth's switch from 30 to 40 mpg saves more gas than Adam's switch from 12 to 14 mpg, because she gained 10 mpg rather than 2.","The intuition is wrong: over 10,000 miles, Adam drops from 833 gallons to 714 (a saving of 119 gallons) while Beth drops from 333 to 250 (a saving of only 83 gallons).","The miles-per-gallon frame exaggerates the value of improvements at the high end and hides them at the low end of the scale.","Larrick and Soll, writing in Science in 2008, called this the MPG illusion and argued that the mpg frame provides very poor guidance to both individuals and policy makers.","The correct frame is gallons-per-mile (or liters per 100 kilometers, which is used in most other countries).","The misleading intuitions fostered by the mpg frame are likely to mislead policy makers as well as car buyers.","The 2013 fuel-economy sticker in the United States added the gallons-per-mile information for the first time, though in small print next to the familiar mpg figure.","The mpg frame is a case in which passive acceptance of a misleading frame has substantial costs and serious policy consequences."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the MPG illusion, a framing effect in which the miles-per-gallon metric distorts intuitions about fuel savings: switching from 12 to 14 mpg saves more gas than switching from 30 to 40 mpg, yet almost everyone judges the opposite. Kahneman, citing Larrick and Soll in Science, argues that gallons-per-mile is the correct frame and that the misleading mpg frame has substantial real-world and policy costs.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya Raman, a fleet manager, argues that Beth's switch from a 30 mpg to a 40 mpg car is the bigger fuel saving. Is her analysis correct, or is she being misled by the frame?","subject_baseline":"Priya Raman manages the 180-vehicle delivery fleet of a regional grocery chain. She is numerically sharp, keeps meticulous cost ledgers, and won a company award last year for a maintenance-cost overhaul. She is confident in her arithmetic and rarely defers to the two mechanics who report to her.","observed_nonverbal_log":["T-1min: She writes '12 → 14 mpg' and '30 → 40 mpg' on the whiteboard, underlining the mpg gains.","T-0s: Asked who saves more gas, she points at the 30-to-40 line without pausing and says 'Clearly Beth.' She taps the board for emphasis.","T+45s: When asked to compute gallons over 10,000 miles, she picks up a calculator but keeps glancing at the mpg numbers, as if the answer were obvious.","T+2min: She completes the division and goes quiet; she re-checks the keystrokes, then re-reads the problem statement.","T+3min: She looks up and says the mpg number 'made me think the wrong thing,' then laughs at her own stubbornness."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Priya, why did you call Beth's switch the bigger saving?'","Priya: 'Because 10 miles per gallon is ten miles per gallon. She gains a third of her mileage; Adam gains a sixth. That is bigger.'","Auditor: 'Now assume both drivers cover 10,000 miles a year. What are the gallon figures?'","Priya: 'Adam drops from 833 gallons to 714, so 119 saved. Beth drops from 333 to 250, so 83 saved. Adam wins. That cannot be right.'","Auditor: 'What made the first judgment feel so certain?'","Priya: 'The mpg number itself. Ten is bigger than two, so my gut said ten. I did not invert it.'","Auditor: 'What would you do if this were a real fuel policy?'","Priya: 'Change the sticker. Show gallons per mile, not miles per gallon, before I approve any rebate program.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The MPG illusion: Priya's intuition was driven by the miles-per-gallon frame, which exaggerates the value of improvements at the high end and hides them at the low end. The correct gallons-per-mile frame shows Adam's switch from 12 to 14 mpg saves 119 gallons versus Beth's 83, the reverse of her intuition.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Priya's first judgment followed the classic pattern of the MPG illusion documented by Larrick and Soll: comparing mpg differences (10 versus 2) and ratios (a third versus a sixth) makes Beth look better, because mpg is a ratio whose inverse governs consumption. The moment she converted to gallons per 10,000 miles, the evidence forced a reversal — Adam saves 119 gallons, Beth only 83. Her surprise and self-correction confirm the diagnosis: the intuition was an artifact of the misleading frame, not a real preference or an arithmetic error, and the book notes that the same illusion misleads policy makers as well as car buyers.","distractors":["Rounding error: she simply misdivided the numbers and a correct calculation would vindicate Beth.","Anchoring: she anchored on the 30 mpg figure and never adjusted, so the mpg frame is incidental.","Loss aversion: she undervalued Adam's improvement because upgrading from a guzzler feels like repairing past waste."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Schelling's child-exemption contradiction","visual_frame_description":"A policy think-tank office. Tomas Berg sits across a table from an auditor, two index cards in front of him. The first reads 'Should the child exemption be larger for the rich than for the poor?' and the second 'Should the childless poor pay as large a surcharge as the childless rich?' A tax table showing the same redistribution from two different defaults is projected on the wall behind them.","player_mission":"Audit Tomas's two moral verdicts: review his fast emotional rejections, his slower second answer, and the logical demonstration that the two propositions are equivalent. Decide whether his morality rests on a coherent principle or is bound to the frame.","key_concepts":["Schelling's students found the idea of a larger child exemption for the rich completely unacceptable.","The tax law is arbitrary: it assumes a childless family as the default and reduces tax by the exemption for each child.","The law could be rewritten with a two-child default, in which case families with fewer than the default number of children would pay a surcharge.","Schelling's students rejected the proposition that the childless poor should pay as large a surcharge as the childless rich with as much vehemence as the first.","The two formulations describe the same tax difference as a reduction in one version and an increase in the other, so a person who rejects both is logically committed to contradictions.","If you want the poor to receive the same benefit as the rich for having children, you must want the poor to pay at least the same penalty as the rich for being childless.","System 1 delivers an immediate response to any question about rich and poor: when in doubt, favor the poor.","The apparently simple moral rule does not work reliably, because it generates contradictory answers to the same problem depending on how it is framed.","Moral feelings are attached to frames, to descriptions of reality rather than to reality itself, and there is no underlying preference that is masked or distorted by the frame."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the claim that moral intuitions are attached to frames rather than to reality: Schelling's tax problem shows the same distribution rule accepted or rejected depending on whether it is framed as an exemption for the rich or a surcharge for the childless poor. Kahneman concludes that there is no underlying preference being masked — System 1 delivers the reflexive 'favor the poor' response to whichever description is on the page, and System 2 has no moral intuitions of its own to reconcile the contradiction.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Policy analyst Tomas Berg simultaneously rejects 'a larger child exemption for the rich' and 'an equal surcharge for the childless poor.' Is there a coherent moral principle behind both verdicts, or is his morality frame-bound?","subject_baseline":"Tomas Berg is a senior policy analyst at a nonprofit tax-reform institute with a law degree and a decade of experience drafting equity arguments. He is articulate, enjoys Socratic debate, and prides himself on reasoning from principle. He has never worked on family-tax design before today.","observed_nonverbal_log":["T-1min: He reads proposition one — 'Should the child exemption be larger for the rich than for the poor?' — and scoffs, shaking his head immediately.","T-0s: He answers 'Absolutely not. Unacceptable,' crossing his arms.","T+2min: He is handed proposition two — 'Should the childless poor pay as large a surcharge as the childless rich?' — and his brow furrows; he re-reads it twice.","T+2min30s: He answers 'No,' more slowly this time, then adds 'That would punish poor families without children.'","T+4min: When shown that the two propositions are algebraically the same family-tax rule viewed from different defaults, he stops mid-sentence and rubs his eyes.","T+5min: He concedes he cannot reject both, then adds quietly that his feelings 'still want the poor to come out ahead, in whichever sentence the question is written.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Tomas, you rejected the larger exemption for the rich. Do you also reject the surcharge for the childless poor?'","Tomas: 'Of course. The poor should not be penalized for not having children.'","Auditor: 'But set the two rules next to each other. The tax difference between a childless family and a two-child family is a reduction in the first framing and an increase in the second. If you want the poor to receive the same benefit as the rich for having children, you must want the poor to pay the same penalty as the rich for being childless.'","Tomas: 'That is logically airtight, and I have no answer. My gut rejected both sentences, and they cannot both be wrong.'","Auditor: 'So what is your actual position on how the tax code should treat the children of rich and poor?'","Tomas: 'I am dumbfounded. I have feelings about rich and poor, not about filling in the cells of the tax matrix.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Frame-bound moral intuition: Tomas's two verdicts are generated by System 1's simple rule — when in doubt, favor the poor — applied to whichever description of reality is presented. There is no underlying preference being masked; his moral feelings are attached to frames, and once the contradictory answers are exposed he cannot ground either in a stable principle.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The decisive evidence is the logical demonstration: the exemption and the surcharge are the same redistribution described from different default assumptions, so a coherent moral position could not reject both. Tomas's fast, emotional rejections ('scoffs,' 'unacceptable') show System 1 delivering its reflexive favor-the-poor response, while his slower, troubled 'no' to the surcharge and his eventual admission of being dumbfounded reveal that System 2 has no independent moral verdict. This matches Schelling's demonstration at the Kennedy School and Kahneman's conclusion that moral intuitions are about descriptions of reality, not about substance — there is no stable preference underneath for the frame to distort.","distractors":["Hypocrisy or bias against the rich: he secretly favors the rich and used the surcharge framing to disguise it.","A principled welfarist position that genuinely values childless-poor households differently from rich households.","Fatigue or inattention: he would reject neither proposition if he had more time to compute the tax tables."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The organ-donation default boss","visual_frame_description":"A European policy conference room. A wall map shows donation rates glowing next to each country: Austria near 100%, Germany 12%, Sweden 86%, Denmark 4%. On the table lie two blank driver-license forms, identical except that one requires a check box to join the donor registry and the other requires a check box to decline. Delegates argue that the differences must reflect national generosity or religion.","player_mission":"Defeat the Default Dictator by identifying which feature of the forms governs donation rates, then redesign the form so donations rise while the right to decline is preserved.","key_concepts":["Organ-donation rates are close to 100% in Austria but only 12% in Germany, and 86% in Sweden but only 4% in Denmark.","The differences are a framing effect caused by the format of the critical question: opt-out versus opt-in.","The best single predictor of donation is the designation of the default option that will be adopted without having to check a box.","The organ-donation effect is best explained by the laziness of System 2 rather than by the emotional features that drive other framing effects.","People will check the box if they have already decided what they wish to do; unprepared people must make the effort of thinking whether to check it.","Kahneman imagines a form requiring people to solve '2 + 2 = ?' in one box and '13 × 37 = ?' in the other, and argues the donation rate would surely be swayed.","If a large supply of donated organs is good for society, you cannot be neutral between a formulation yielding almost 100% donations and one eliciting donations from 4% of drivers.","An important choice is controlled by an utterly inconsequential feature of the situation, and the evidence for these cognitive illusions is undeniable."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the power of default options in framing: organ-donation rates swing from roughly 4% to near 100% depending solely on whether the form is opt-in or opt-out. Kahneman attributes the effect to the laziness of System 2, which accepts whichever option requires no active step.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The 'Default Dictator' boss tests whether the player understands that the default option in a framed choice, not people's attitudes, governs organ-donation rates. The book shows that donation swings from roughly 4% to near 100% depending solely on whether the form is opt-in or opt-out, an effect best explained by the laziness of System 2.","stage_1_observation":"A European policy conference room. A wall map shows donation rates glowing next to each country: Austria near 100%, Germany 12%, Sweden 86%, Denmark 4%. On the table lie two blank driver-license forms, identical except that one requires a check box to join the donor registry (opt-in) and the other requires a check box to decline (opt-out). Delegates argue that the differences must reflect national generosity or religion.","stage_1_question":"Which single feature of the forms best predicts whether a country's drivers end up as organ donors?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The designation of the default option — in opt-out countries the passive majority becomes donors without checking anything, while in opt-in countries the passive majority never registers. The 100%-versus-4% gap is a framing effect, not a difference in national values.","stage_2_intervention":"The minister asks the player to redesign the form for a country with 4% donation while respecting the right to decline. A draft form offers two decision boxes: one containing '2 + 2 = ?' and the other '13 × 37 = ?'. The player must choose how to set the default and whether to adopt Kahneman's effort-laden box idea.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Adopt an opt-out design (donors by default, with an easy box to decline) rather than opt-in, because the lazy System 2 will accept whichever option requires no effort. The '2 + 2' versus '13 × 37' demonstration is a thought experiment showing that donation rates would swing if declining required real effort, reinforcing that the default — not reasoned conviction — is what moves the rate."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The MPG-illusion boss: gallons per mile","visual_frame_description":"A city transportation office. On the whiteboard, two fleet proposals: Adam's department truck will move from 12 to 14 mpg, Beth's sedan from 30 to 40 mpg. The director has written 'BETH WINS' in marker beneath a ratio calculation, and the budget analyst nods along. A fuel-pump calculator sits beside the board for the player to use.","player_mission":"Defeat the MPG Mirage by computing the true gallon savings for 10,000 miles, then choose the fuel-economy measure that will guide buyers and policy without distortion.","key_concepts":["Over 10,000 miles, Adam's switch from 12 to 14 mpg cuts consumption from 833 gallons to 714, a saving of 119 gallons.","Beth's switch from 30 to 40 mpg cuts consumption from 333 gallons to 250, a saving of only 83 gallons.","Almost everyone shares the intuition that Beth's action is more significant because she reduced mpg by 10 rather than 2 and by a third rather than a sixth.","The miles-per-gallon frame provides very poor guidance to the decisions of both individuals and policy makers.","The correct frame is gallons-per-mile, or liters per 100 kilometers as used in most other countries.","Larrick and Soll called the misleading intuition the MPG illusion in a Science article published in 2008.","The misleading intuitions fostered by the mpg frame are likely to mislead policy makers as well as car buyers.","The 2013 fuel-economy sticker added gallons-per-mile information for the first time, though in small print beside the familiar mpg figure in large print."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the MPG illusion, in which the miles-per-gallon frame hides the true value of efficiency gains at the low end of the scale: switching 12 to 14 mpg saves more gas than switching 30 to 40 mpg, opposite to intuition. Kahneman, citing Larrick and Soll, argues that gallons-per-mile is the correct frame with substantial costs and serious policy consequences riding on the choice.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The 'MPG Mirage' boss tests whether the player can defeat a misleading frame with an inverse measure. Larrick and Soll's MPG illusion shows that miles-per-gallon exaggerates high-end improvements and hides low-end ones, so switching 12 to 14 mpg saves more gas than switching 30 to 40 mpg, opposite to intuition.","stage_1_observation":"A city transportation office. On the whiteboard, two fleet proposals: Adam's department truck will move from 12 to 14 mpg, Beth's sedan from 30 to 40 mpg. The director has written 'BETH WINS' in marker beneath a ratio calculation, and the budget analyst nods along. A fuel-pump calculator sits beside the board for the player to use.","stage_1_question":"Both drivers cover 10,000 miles a year. Which switch actually saves more gallons, and what does the mpg frame hide?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Adam saves more: 10,000 / 12 = 833 gallons and 10,000 / 14 = 714, a saving of 119 gallons, versus Beth's 333 to 250, a saving of only 83. The mpg frame hides this because mpg is a ratio whose inverse, gallons per mile, governs consumption, and intuition compares the 10-versus-2 difference instead of the inverted gallons.","stage_2_intervention":"The director must issue a city fuel-efficiency rebate but cannot tell which vehicles deserve the bonus from the current sticker. She asks the player to pick the measure that will guide both buyers and policy correctly: keep the mpg figure in large print, replace it with gallons per 100 miles, or add the gallons-per-mile figure in small print alongside mpg.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Replace or feature the gallons-per-mile (inverse) measure — for example gallons per 100 miles, as used in most other countries — because it linearizes fuel cost and prevents the MPG illusion. Merely adding the correct number in small print beside the familiar mpg figure is the partial fix the book records, and keeping mpg prominent preserves the misleading frame."}]},{"id":"ch40","title":"35. Two Selves","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Experiencing self, remembering self, and decision utility","visual_frame_description":"A hospital monitoring station shows two pain curves from a colonoscopy study: patient A over 8 minutes and patient B over 24 minutes, with a highlighted 'area under the curve' beneath each. Three columns on screen are labeled Experiencing Self, Remembering Self, and Decision Utility, with statement cards floating between them for the player to sort.","player_mission":"Sort each observation into the correct perspective: the experiencing self's moment-by-moment reports, the remembering self's global retrospectives, or the decision utility that drives choices. Then identify which perspective produced the hedonimeter total in the colonoscopy study.","key_concepts":["The experiencing self answers the question 'Does it hurt now?' and the remembering self answers 'How was it, on the whole?'","Bentham's interpretation of utility is called experienced utility, while economists use the same word to mean 'wantability,' which Kahneman calls decision utility.","Edgeworth proposed a hedonimeter, an imaginary instrument that would measure the level of pleasure or pain at any moment, with the total shown as the area under the curve.","The hedonimeter totals from the colonoscopy study were computed by an observer from moment-by-moment reports and are duration-weighted.","The retrospective assessments are insensitive to duration and weight the peak and the end much more than other moments.","In the cold-hand experiment, fully 80% of participants whose pain diminished in the final phase chose to repeat the longer 90-second episode.","The cold-hand choice revealed a discrepancy between decision utility and experienced utility.","The remembering self keeps score and governs what we learn from living, and it is the one that makes decisions.","In the injections puzzle, people pay more to reduce injections from 6 to 4 than from 20 to 18, even though the same amount of pain is avoided."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between the two selves and decision utility: the experiencing self tallies moment-by-moment pain, the remembering self issues global ratings that follow the peak and the end, and decision utility governs actual choices. Kahneman's cold-hand experiment shows the two selves in conflict, with 80% of participants choosing the longer, more painful episode because its memory was less aversive.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which perspective produced the 'hedonimeter total' in Redelmeier's colonoscopy study — the observer's moment-by-moment tally or the patient's global memory?","column_a_label":"Experiencing self (moment-by-moment, duration-weighted reports)","column_b_label":"Remembering self (global retrospective, peak and end)","column_c_label":"Decision utility (wantability, the utility behind choices)","rows":[{"label":"Patient B's pain profile summed over 24 minutes, the larger 'area under the curve'","col":"a"},{"label":"Patient A's global rating of total pain despite a shorter 8-minute procedure","col":"b"},{"label":"Edgeworth's imaginary hedonimeter recording pain level at every moment","col":"a"},{"label":"80% of cold-hand participants choosing to repeat the 90-second episode","col":"c"},{"label":"The remembering self's answer to 'How was it, on the whole?'","col":"b"},{"label":"The experiencing self's answer to 'Does it hurt now?'","col":"a"},{"label":"Paying more to reduce injections from 6 to 4 than from 20 to 18","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b: The hedonimeter total comes from the patient's memory of the whole procedure.","c: The hedonimeter total measures what people want, not what they feel.","a: The hedonimeter total is the rating the patient gives after the procedure ends."],"explanation":"The hedonimeter total is duration-weighted and computed from moment-by-moment reports, the domain of the experiencing self, which is why Edgeworth's weather-station analogy and the 'area under the curve' belong in column a. Global retrospective ratings follow the peak-end rule and ignore duration, the signature of the remembering self (column b). Choices such as repeating the longer cold-hand trial or paying more for the 6-to-4 injection reduction reflect decision utility, the 'wantability' that drives decisions even when it misaligns with what is actually experienced."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Peak-end rule, duration neglect, and the hedonimeter total","visual_frame_description":"Two patient pain profiles scroll side by side on a monitor: patient A's 8-minute colonoscopy spiking to 8 and ending at 7, and patient B's 24-minute procedure peaking at 8 early and ending at 1. A line under each curve shows the computed peak-end average, while a separate tally displays the duration-weighted 'area under the curve.'","player_mission":"Classify each statement into the rule it illustrates: the peak-end rule, duration neglect, or the observer's duration-weighted hedonimeter total. Then explain why the shorter procedure was remembered as the more painful one.","key_concepts":["The peak-end rule states that the global retrospective rating is well predicted by the average of the level of pain at the worst moment and at the end.","Duration neglect means the duration of the procedure had no effect whatsoever on the ratings of total pain.","In the colonoscopy study, patient A's procedure lasted 8 minutes and patient B's lasted 24 minutes, yet A retained a much worse memory of the episode.","The peak-end average was 7.5 for patient A and only 4.5 for patient B, because A ended at 7 and B ended at 1 while both peaked at 8.","It was the bad luck of patient A that the procedure ended at a bad moment, leaving an unpleasant memory.","Hedonimeter totals are duration-weighted because the computation assigns equal weights to all moments: two minutes of pain at level 9 is twice as bad as one minute.","A total of 154 patients participated in the colonoscopy experiment, with the shortest procedure lasting 4 minutes and the longest 69 minutes.","If the objective is to reduce patients' memory of pain, lowering the peak intensity could be more important than minimizing the duration of the procedure."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the two rules that govern retrospective ratings: the peak-end rule, which weights the worst and final moments, and duration neglect, which removes total length from the equation. Redelmeier and Kahneman's 154-patient colonoscopy study showed the 24-minute procedure was remembered as milder than the 8-minute one because of its gentle ending.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Patient A's colonoscopy lasted 8 minutes and patient B's lasted 24 minutes, yet A remembered far more pain. Which rule explains that reversal of the 'area under the curve'?","column_a_label":"Peak-end rule (global rating is the average of the worst moment and the end)","column_b_label":"Duration neglect (total duration has no effect on the global rating)","column_c_label":"Hedonimeter total (duration-weighted sum computed by an observer)","rows":[{"label":"Global retrospective rating predicted by the average of the worst pain and the final pain","col":"a"},{"label":"Procedure length having no effect on ratings of total pain","col":"b"},{"label":"Two minutes at pain level 9 being twice as bad as one minute at the same level","col":"c"},{"label":"Patient A's peak of 8 and end of 7 averaging to a 7.5 memory","col":"a"},{"label":"Patient B's peak of 8 and end of 1 averaging to a 4.5 memory","col":"a"},{"label":"The observer's integration of pain over the whole 24-minute procedure","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b: The shorter procedure must be remembered as worse because duration is neglected, not because of its ending.","c: The hedonimeter total is what patients report when they are asked for their total pain.","b: Duration neglect predicts that longer procedures are remembered as less painful in general."],"explanation":"Patient A's worse memory is exactly the peak-end rule: both patients peaked at 8, but A's procedure ended at 7 while B's ended at 1, giving peak-end averages of 7.5 and 4.5 — A's bad luck was that the procedure ended at a bad moment. Duration neglect would predict no effect of the 16-minute difference, which is why the total duration does not rescue patient B's rating. The hedonimeter total (column c) is the observer's duration-weighted integration and is what the retrospective ratings conspicuously fail to reproduce."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"System 1 stores averages and prototypes, not sums","visual_frame_description":"A memory-laboratory screen shows three visualizations side by side: a set of 24 dishes with two broken plates added and its value bar dropping, a profile card for 'Linda' next to her bank-teller description, and a cold-water pain trace collapsing into a single prototypical moment. Three sorting columns labeled averages and prototypes, duration-weighted sums, and decision utility frame the puzzle.","player_mission":"Sort each finding into the mechanism it demonstrates: System 1's representation of sets by averages, norms, and prototypes; the duration-weighted integration of an observer; or the wantability that drives choices. Then identify why the cold-hand episode is remembered as a single representative moment.","key_concepts":["System 1 represents sets by averages, norms, and prototypes, not by sums.","Christopher Hsee's study showed that adding dishes to a set of 24 dishes lowered its total value because some of the added dishes were broken.","Linda, the activist woman, is judged more likely to be a feminist bank teller than a bank teller, a conjunction that a sum-based logic should forbid.","The less-is-more effect covers the broken-dish set, the Linda problem, and the cold-hand experiment alike.","Each cold-hand episode is a set of moments, which the remembering self stores as a prototypical moment strongly influenced by the peak and the end.","For an objective observer, what counts is the area under the curve that integrates pain over time, which has the nature of a sum.","It is important for a squirrel to know the total amount of food it has stored, and a representation of the average size of the nuts would not be a good substitute.","Rats show duration neglect for both pleasure and pain, with the duration of a shock having little or no effect on fear.","Electrical stimulation of specific brain areas can produce intense pleasure, and only the intensity of the stimulation matters, not its duration."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests why the remembering self keeps prototypes rather than sums: System 1 represents sets by averages, norms, and prototypes, which produces the less-is-more effect seen in Hsee's broken dishes, the Linda problem, and the cold-hand experiment. Kahneman argues this operating feature of System 1 explains why the peak and end dominate memory while duration is neglected.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Why does the remembering self treat the 90-second cold-hand episode as a single prototypical moment rather than a sum of painful moments?","column_a_label":"Representation by averages, norms, and prototypes (System 1)","column_b_label":"Duration-weighted integration ('area under the curve')","column_c_label":"Decision utility (wantability that guides choices)","rows":[{"label":"Linda the activist judged more likely to be a feminist bank teller than a bank teller","col":"a"},{"label":"Hsee's set of 24 dishes losing value when broken dishes are added","col":"a"},{"label":"Each cold-hand episode stored as a representative moment weighted by peak and end","col":"a"},{"label":"The observer's integration of pain over time for a 90-second episode","col":"b"},{"label":"Paying more to reduce injections from 6 to 4 than from 20 to 18","col":"c"},{"label":"A squirrel needing to know the total amount of food stored, not the average nut","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b: The cold-hand memory is stored as an integral of pain over time.","c: Decision utility explains why memories keep an average, since wantability follows sums.","b: The less-is-more effect comes from duration-weighted integration, not from prototypes."],"explanation":"System 1 represents sets by averages, norms, and prototypes rather than by sums, which is why adding broken dishes to Hsee's set lowered its value and why the remembering self keeps a representative moment from each cold-hand episode instead of a pain total. Column b is the objective observer's integration, the 'area under the curve' that sums pain — evolution may store food totals for a squirrel, but the integral of pain is less biologically significant. Decision utility (column c) is the wantability that drives choices like paying more for the 6-to-4 injection cut, which is why the choice misaligns with the experienced sum."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The scratched symphony: was the experience ruined?","visual_frame_description":"A lecture hall foyer after a talk. Marcus, an investment analyst in his forties, stands with an auditor, gesturing as he describes a long symphony recording that was scratched near the end. A timeline of the evening renders behind them: 40 minutes of bright, rising segments collapsing into a single dark screech at the final second.","player_mission":"Audit Marcus's verdict that the scratched ending 'ruined the whole experience.' Review his moment-by-moment description against his global rating, then diagnose whether the experience itself was altered or only its memory.","key_concepts":["A listener told Kahneman that a scratched disc near the end of a long symphony 'ruined the whole experience,' but the experience was not actually ruined, only the memory of it.","The experiencing self had an experience that was almost entirely good, and the bad end could not undo it because it had already happened.","The peak-end rule predicts that the global retrospective rating is the average of the worst moment and the end, making the screech dominate the verdict.","Duration neglect means the 40 minutes of musical bliss barely count in the retrospective rating.","Confusing experience with the memory of it is a compelling cognitive illusion, and it is this substitution that makes a past experience seem ruinable.","The experiencing self does not have a voice; the remembering self is the one that keeps score and governs what we learn from living.","The remembering self is sometimes wrong, but it is the one that makes decisions, which Kahneman calls the tyranny of the remembering self.","What we learn from the past is to maximize the qualities of our future memories, not necessarily of our future experience."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the substitution of memory for experience: a bad ending led Marcus to grade a nearly all-good evening as ruined, although the 40 minutes of musical bliss had already happened and could not be undone. Kahneman's lecture anecdote makes the same point, and the peak-end rule plus duration neglect explain why the memory overrides the actual experience in the final verdict.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus, an investment analyst, insists the scratch on the last movement of a symphony recording 'ruined the whole experience.' Is his verdict a faithful report of the experience itself?","subject_baseline":"Marcus is a 42-year-old investment analyst and an experienced classical-music listener who attends concerts monthly and owns a large record collection. He is articulate and introspective, and friends value his taste. He has never been in a psychology study before this lecture-hall exchange.","observed_nonverbal_log":["T-1min: Recounting the evening, Marcus's face brightens as he describes the opening movements; he gestures broadly, humming a phrase.","T-0s: He reaches the final movement, his expression clouds, and he winces, saying the record 'screeched.'","T+20s: Asked what grade he gives the whole evening, he answers without hesitation: a failing one, 'because of the ending.'","T+1min: Asked what the first 40 minutes were like, he pauses and says 'sublime, honestly' before insisting the ending still counts for everything.","T+2min: When the interviewer notes that the experience itself is already over, Marcus is quiet for a long moment, then says the memory 'is all I have left of it.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Marcus, you graded the whole symphony evening a failure because the last seconds screeched. How would you grade the first 40 minutes alone?'","Marcus: 'Sublime. I was lost in it. But the scratch made the ending unbearable, and the ending colors everything.'","Auditor: 'The ending happened after the bliss. Can a moment that comes last change what you already felt during the earlier 40 minutes?'","Marcus: 'Logically it cannot. It was already a good experience. But it feels as if the whole thing is ruined.'","Auditor: 'Which is the better description of what happened — the experience was ruined, or the memory of it was?'","Marcus: 'The memory. I see it now. I let the ending rewrite forty minutes of music that had already happened.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Substitution of memory for experience, governed by the peak-end rule: the screeching finale dominates Marcus's global rating so thoroughly that he downgrades the entire episode. The experiencing self's 40 minutes of musical bliss already occurred and cannot be undone; only the memory — and therefore his verdict — was ruined.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The key evidence is the split between his moment-by-moment account ('sublime' for 40 minutes) and his global verdict (a failure). The ending was the most salient moment and, being last, anchors the retrospective rating under the peak-end rule, while duration neglect means the 40 good minutes barely count in the memory. The book's lecture anecdote — a disc scratched near the end 'ruining the whole experience' — is precisely this case, and Kahneman's conclusion is that confusing experience with its memory is a compelling cognitive illusion: the substitution is what makes a past experience seem ruinable. Marcus's own concession that the experience 'cannot' be changed confirms the diagnosis of the remembering self's tyranny over evaluation.","distractors":["A genuine hedonic reversal: the screech retroactively changed the pleasure Marcus felt during the earlier movements.","Dislike of the composer: the negative verdict reflects longstanding musical taste, not the ending.","Mood contagion: Marcus was irritable all evening and would have rated the symphony poorly regardless of the scratch."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Colonoscopy pain ratings and memory","visual_frame_description":"A clinic review room. Two patients' pain profiles are projected side by side: Elena's 8-minute curve peaking at 8 and ending at 7, Rosa's 24-minute curve peaking at 8 early and ending at 1. The screen labels each curve with its computed peak-end average while a nurse's note reads 'patient A retained a much worse memory.'","player_mission":"Audit Elena's high 'total pain' rating against the monitoring records. Review the timed pain reports and the interview, then decide whether Elena is exaggerating or whether her memory is following the rules the chapter documents.","key_concepts":["The colonoscopy study, designed by Redelmeier and Kahneman in the early 1990s at the University of Toronto, included 154 patients with procedures from 4 to 69 minutes long.","The peak-end rule states that the global retrospective rating is well predicted by the average of the level of pain at the worst moment and at its end.","Duration neglect means the duration of the procedure had no effect whatsoever on the ratings of total pain.","Patient A's procedure lasted 8 minutes with a peak of 8 and an end of 7, giving a peak-end average of 7.5.","Patient B's procedure lasted 24 minutes with a peak of 8 and an end of 1, giving a peak-end average of only 4.5.","As expected, patient A retained a much worse memory of the episode than patient B, despite the shorter procedure.","If the objective is to reduce patients' memory of pain, lowering the peak intensity could be more important than minimizing the duration of the procedure.","Gradual relief may be preferable to abrupt relief if patients retain a better memory when the pain at the end of the procedure is relatively mild."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the peak-end rule and duration neglect in clinical pain: retrospective ratings of a colonoscopy track the average of the worst and final moments and ignore total duration, so the shorter procedure that ended badly is remembered as more painful. Redelmeier and Kahneman's 154-patient study showed that duration had no effect on ratings of total pain, while the peak-end average predicted them well.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Elena rated her colonoscopy as far more painful 'on the whole' than Rosa rated hers, yet the monitoring records show Elena's procedure was far shorter. Is Elena exaggerating her pain?","subject_baseline":"Elena, 58, and Rosa, 61, are two patients in Don Redelmeier's early-1990s colonoscopy study at the University of Toronto. Both rated their pain every 60 seconds on a zero-to-ten scale, and neither received the amnesic drugs that are now routine. Both patients used the scale in similar ways, per the study's assumption.","observed_nonverbal_log":["During procedure (Elena, 8 minutes): her ratings climb to a peak of 8, then remain high; the final reading before the end is 7.","During procedure (Rosa, 24 minutes): her ratings also peak at 8 early on but gradually fall; the final reading before the end is 1.","T+0s after procedure (Elena): she rates 'total pain' near the peak-end average of 7.5 and says the worst moment and the ending were 'both awful.'","T+0s after procedure (Rosa): she rates total pain near the peak-end average of 4.5 and says 'the worst part was early, then it eased.'","T+5min: Elena is surprised that her procedure was shorter than Rosa's and insists it 'felt longer.'","T+10min: Both patients confirm the pain scale and their reports; neither shows signs of confusion or exaggeration."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: 'Elena, your procedure lasted 8 minutes; Rosa's lasted 24. Your pain at any moment was never higher than hers, and you spent less time at high pain. Yet you rated total pain far higher. How do you explain that?'","Elena: 'I am as surprised as you are. I only know that when it ended I was still hurting badly. The ending is what I remember.'","Auditor: 'Rosa, your procedure was three times as long. Why did you rate it more mildly?'","Rosa: 'The worst was at the start. By the end the pain had come down a lot, so it did not feel so bad overall.'","Auditor: 'So the global rating tracks the worst moment and the ending, not the minutes on the clock?'","Both: 'Apparently. The minutes themselves seemed to count for nothing.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Not exaggeration but the peak-end rule and duration neglect: Elena's memory of total pain follows the average of her peak (8) and final rating (7), yielding a 7.5 retrospective rating, while Rosa's peak-end average of (8 + 1) / 2 = 4.5 gives her a milder memory despite a procedure three times longer. Elena's rating is a faithful report of her remembering self, not an inflated claim.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The monitoring data rule out exaggeration: both patients used the same scale and their moment-by-moment reports match the study's records. The reversal is predicted exactly by the two memory rules — the peak-end rule (global rating equals the average of the worst and the final moment) and duration neglect (the 16-minute difference had no effect on the global rating). Patient A's profile in the chapter is Elena's: peak 8, end 7, peak-end average 7.5, and a worse memory; patient B is Rosa: peak 8, end 1, average 4.5, and a milder memory. The book's 154-patient study found precisely that the procedure's duration had no effect whatsoever on the ratings of total pain.","distractors":["Exaggeration or catastrophizing: Elena overstates her pain to gain sympathy or compensation.","Confusion between procedures: Elena misremembers Rosa's longer procedure as her own.","A higher pain threshold for Rosa: Rosa genuinely felt less pain, so the comparison is apples to oranges."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The cold-hand choice","visual_frame_description":"A psychophysics lab. Priya sits with her forearm immersed in 14-degree-Celsius water while a cursor traces her continuous pain ratings on a screen. She completes a 60-second trial, then a 90-second trial whose final 30 seconds are about 1 degree warmer and mildly less painful. A clock shows the 7-minute gap before she must choose which episode to repeat for trial three.","player_mission":"Defeat the Cold-Hand Paradox: predict which trial the remembering self will rate as less aversive, then guide Priya's repeat choice so the experiencing self wins once the memory illusion is made visible.","key_concepts":["In the cold-hand experiment, each participant endured a 60-second trial at 14 degrees Celsius and a 90-second trial whose final 30 seconds rose about 1 degree, just enough to detect a slight decrease in pain.","Participants were told they would have three cold-hand trials but in fact experienced only the short and the long episodes, separated by seven minutes.","Fully 80% of participants who reported that their pain diminished during the final phase of the longer episode opted to repeat it.","The participants thereby declared themselves willing to suffer 30 seconds of needless pain in the anticipated third trial.","The peak-end rule predicts a worse memory for the short than for the long trial, and duration neglect predicts the extra 30 seconds will be ignored.","The subjects knew quite well which exposure was longer, but they did not use that knowledge in their choice.","Their decision was governed by a simple rule of intuitive choice: pick the option you like the most, or dislike the least.","The cold-hand experiment revealed a discrepancy between decision utility and experienced utility.","If asked 'Would you prefer a 90-second immersion or only the first part of it?' the subjects would certainly have selected the short option."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the conflict between the experiencing and remembering selves: 80% of participants who felt their pain diminish in the final phase of the longer trial chose to repeat the 90-second episode over the 60-second one. The choice maximizes remembered relief while inflicting 30 seconds of needless pain, a decision utility that betrays experienced utility.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The 'Cold-Hand Paradox' boss tests the conflict between the experiencing and remembering selves: 80% of participants who felt their pain diminish in the final phase of the longer trial chose to repeat the 90-second episode over the 60-second one. The choice maximizes remembered pain relief while inflicting 30 seconds of needless pain — a decision utility that betrays experienced utility.","stage_1_observation":"A psychophysics lab. Priya sits with her forearm immersed in 14-degree-Celsius water; a cursor traces her continuous pain ratings on a screen. She completes a 60-second trial, then a 90-second trial whose final 30 seconds are about 1 degree warmer and mildly less painful. The clock shows the 7-minute gap before she must choose which episode to repeat for trial three.","stage_1_question":"From the perspective of the experiencing self, which trial is objectively worse, and which trial will the remembering self rate as less aversive?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The experiencing self should prefer the short trial, because the long trial contains everything in the short one plus 30 extra seconds of pain — a larger 'area under the curve.' But the remembering self will rate the long trial less aversively, because its final 30 seconds were milder, lowering the peak-end average, and duration neglect ignores the extra seconds.","stage_2_intervention":"Priya must now pick which trial to repeat in trial three. The experimenter also offers her a clearer choice: 'Would you prefer the 90-second immersion or only the first 60 seconds of it?' The player must decide which framing Priya should use to protect the experiencing self.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose the short 60-second trial and expose the illusion with the reframe. When the choice is stated as '90 seconds versus only the first 60,' Priya immediately recognizes that the long trial is worse and selects the short one. The book shows subjects chose the long episode only because they were picking the option they disliked least in memory; making the duration explicit lets the experiencing self win, because its preferences are clear once the memory illusion is stripped away."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The colonoscopy treatment design","visual_frame_description":"An endoscopy suite. Two pain curves on a monitor show patient A (8 minutes, peak 8, ending at 7) and patient B (24 minutes, peak 8, ending at 1). A nurse's note reads 'patient A retained a much worse memory despite the shorter procedure.' Dr. Omar Reyes reviews scheduling options for future procedures while a clinical-objectives board lists two goals: reduce the memory of pain, or reduce the pain actually experienced.","player_mission":"Defeat the Two-Selves Clinic boss by identifying which patient has the worse memory and why, then choose a procedure design matched to an explicit objective and explain the conflict between the two selves.","key_concepts":["If the objective is to reduce patients' memory of pain, lowering the peak intensity of pain could be more important than minimizing the duration of the procedure.","By the same reasoning, gradual relief may be preferable to abrupt relief if patients retain a better memory when the pain at the end is relatively mild.","If the objective is to reduce the amount of pain actually experienced, conducting the procedure swiftly may be appropriate even if it increases the peak pain intensity.","Kahneman's impression is that a strong majority would favor reducing the memory of pain over reducing the pain actually experienced.","The dilemma is a conflict of interests between the experiencing self and the remembering self, which do not correspond to the two familiar systems.","Memories are all we get to keep from our experience of living, and the only perspective we can adopt when thinking about our lives is that of the remembering self.","The peak-end average was 7.5 for patient A and 4.5 for patient B, because A ended at 7 and B at 1 while both peaked at 8.","The remembering self is sometimes wrong, but it is the one that keeps score and governs what we learn from living, and it is the one that makes decisions."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the clinical dilemma at the heart of the chapter: reducing the memory of pain requires lowering the peak and ending gently, while reducing pain actually experienced may require a swift procedure even at the cost of an awful memory. Kahneman frames the choice as a conflict of interests between the experiencing self and the remembering self.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The 'Two-Selves Clinic' boss tests the clinical dilemma at the heart of the chapter: if the goal is to reduce the memory of pain, lower the peak and end the procedure gently; if the goal is to reduce pain actually experienced, finish swiftly even if that raises the peak. The physician's choice is a conflict of interests between the two selves.","stage_1_observation":"An endoscopy suite. Two pain curves on a monitor show patient A (8 minutes, peak 8, ends at 7) and patient B (24 minutes, peak 8, ends at 1). A nurse notes that patient A retained a far worse memory despite the shorter procedure. Dr. Omar Reyes must decide how to schedule and conduct future procedures.","stage_1_question":"Which patient has the worse memory of the procedure, and which of the chapter's rules explains it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Patient A has the worse memory, despite the shorter procedure, because the peak-end rule gives A a peak-end average of (8 + 7) / 2 = 7.5 versus B's (8 + 1) / 2 = 4.5, and duration neglect removes the 16-minute difference. A's bad luck was that the procedure ended at a painful moment.","stage_2_intervention":"Dr. Reyes must choose a redesign with a clear objective in mind. Option 1 lowers peak pain but prolongs the procedure; option 2 shortens the procedure but spikes the peak intensity near the end; option 3 adds a long, gradual relief phase that is painless but extends duration. The player must state which objective the redesign serves and pick the design that matches it.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Pick the design for the stated objective and name the conflict. If the objective is to reduce the memory of pain, choose gradual relief that ends mildly (the peak-end average drops even if duration grows), because duration does not count in memory. If the objective is to reduce pain actually experienced, choose the swift option regardless of the memory. The key lesson is that the two objectives conflict — the chapter's majority intuition favors reducing the memory of pain, and the tension between the two selves is what makes the choice a genuine dilemma."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The tyranny of the remembering self","visual_frame_description":"A therapist's office. Daniel, 47, describes his 15-year marriage, which ended in a bitter divorce over the final 18 months. He keeps returning to the last fight, the messages, and the abrupt separation, adding 'there were good years, sure, but look how it ended.' A timeline on the wall shows 15 years of mostly warm months with a single dark segment at the end.","player_mission":"Defeat the Tyranny of the Remembering Self: identify which self is issuing Daniel's verdict, then choose the intervention that lets him recover the duration-weighted view of the actual experience.","key_concepts":["The remembering self is the one that answers 'How was it, on the whole?' and keeps score, and it is the one that makes decisions.","A divorce, like a symphony with a screeching sound at the end, is an episode whose bad ending can dominate the global verdict even when most of it was good.","The fact that something ended badly does not mean it was all bad.","Confusing experience with the memory of it is a compelling cognitive illusion, and it is the substitution that makes us believe a past experience can be ruined.","The experiencing self does not have a voice; the remembering self is sometimes wrong but is the one that keeps score and governs what we learn from living.","This is the tyranny of the remembering self: what we learn from the past is to maximize the qualities of our future memories, not necessarily of our future experience.","Duration neglect means the long good stretch of the episode counts for little in the global rating.","The peak-end rule means the final bitter segment is weighted heavily in the retrospective verdict."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the tyranny of the remembering self: a bad ending recolors an entire episode, so Daniel grades 15 years of mostly good marriage as a failure because of its final 18 months. Kahneman's lesson is that the bad end cannot undo experience that has already happened, and the fix is to separate the ending from the episode and restore the duration-weighted view.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The 'Tyranny of the Remembering Self' boss tests how a bad ending can recolor an entire life episode: a divorce judged only by its final year, like the symphony with a screeching end, leads the remembering self to grade the whole experience a failure even when most of it was good. The fix is to separate the ending from the episode and restore the duration-weighted view.","stage_1_observation":"A therapist's office. Daniel, 47, is describing his 15-year marriage, which ended in a bitter divorce over the last 18 months. He keeps returning to the final fight, the messages, and the abrupt separation. When the therapist asks about the first 13 years, he says 'there were good years, sure,' then immediately adds 'but look how it ended.' A timeline on the wall shows 15 years of mostly warm months with a dark final segment.","stage_1_question":"Which self is issuing Daniel's verdict on the marriage, and what pattern of attention does it exhibit?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The remembering self governs his verdict: it weights the peak and the end of the episode, so the bitter final segment dominates the global evaluation, while duration neglect makes the 13 good years nearly invisible. This is the same peak-end substitution that made the symphony listener call a scratched ending 'ruined,' with the bad ending rewriting the memory of the whole.","stage_2_intervention":"The therapist asks Daniel to grade the marriage overall, and he starts to say 'a failure.' The player must choose the intervention that lets Daniel recover the actual experience: (a) accept the final-year verdict as the honest total, (b) list and duration-weight the good years against the bad ending, or (c) decide that no part of the past can be evaluated once the ending was bad.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose (b): reframe Daniel's evaluation to separate the ending from the episode and weight the episode by its actual duration. The book's speaking-of-frames line is direct — a divorce is like a symphony with a screeching sound at the end, and the fact that it ended badly does not mean it was all bad. The remembering self keeps score and governs decisions, so the intervention must deliberately restore the experiencing self's 13 good years; accepting the final-year verdict (a) or declaring the whole un-evaluable (c) both cede the episode to the peak-end illusion."}]},{"id":"ch41","title":"36. Life as a Story","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Duration Neglect, Peak-End Rule, and the Less-Is-More Effect in Judgments of a Whole Life","visual_frame_description":"A research console displays two biographies of a fictional woman named Jen side by side, one with a 30-year lifespan and one with a 60-year lifespan, both marked as extremely happy. Below them a third card appends five extra slightly happy years, and a bar chart shows desirability ratings. A cursor hovers over the words less-is-more as the player sorts each finding into one of three labeled columns.","player_mission":"Read each piece of evidence from Ed Diener's studies of Jen's life and classify it into the correct column: duration neglect, peak-end rule, or less-is-more prototype substitution. Place the final verdict for the key finding about the extra five years to complete the matrix.","key_concepts":["In Diener's studies, doubling the duration of Jen's life from 30 to 60 years had no effect on judgments of how desirable her life was.","Doubling the duration also left judgments of the total happiness Jen experienced unchanged.","Jen's total happiness was judged as the happiness of a typical period in her life, not as the sum of happiness over its duration.","Adding five slightly happy years to a very happy life caused a substantial drop in evaluations of the total happiness of that life.","This less-is-more effect indicates that an average, or prototype, has been substituted for a sum when people evaluate a whole life.","The pattern persisted when parents and older friends of the students answered the same questions.","In a within-subject experiment, participants still judged the extra five slightly happy years as making the whole life worse.","In intuitive evaluation of entire lives as well as brief episodes, peaks and ends matter but duration does not.","Kahneman realized he cared intensely about the final ten minutes of Violetta's life but not about whether she lived to 27 or 28.","Duration appears to matter for labor and vacations only because the quality of the end changes with the length of the episode."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the less-is-more prototype-substitution mechanism: when judging Jen's total happiness, System 1 substitutes the average happiness of a typical period for the sum over her whole life, so diluting that average with disappointing years makes the entire life look worse. Kahneman and Diener showed the same peak-end and duration-neglect pattern governs brief episodes and whole lives alike.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism best explains why adding five slightly happy years to Jen's very happy life made the whole life judged worse?","column_a_label":"Duration neglect","column_b_label":"Peak-end rule","column_c_label":"Less-is-more prototype substitution","rows":[{"label":"Doubling Jen's lifespan from 30 to 60 years leaves desirability ratings unchanged","col":"a"},{"label":"Jen's total happiness is judged as the happiness of a typical period, not a summed total","col":"c"},{"label":"Adding five pleasant-but-less-happy years drops the judged desirability of the whole life","col":"c"},{"label":"The audience's verdict on Violetta's life turns on whether her lover arrives before she dies","col":"b"},{"label":"In a within-subject comparison, participants still judged the extra years as making the life worse","col":"c"},{"label":"A mother is judged by how she feels at the end of a 24-hour labor, not by the sum of her hours","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Duration neglect, because the added years are simply ignored along with everything else","Peak-end rule, because the added years change the ending into a happier one","WYSIATI, because the researcher only showed one version of the biography"],"explanation":"The drop caused by five slightly happy extra years is the less-is-more effect: people substitute the prototype (an average of a typical period) for a sum, so diluting that average drags the whole judgment down. The unchanged ratings when lifespan doubled are duration neglect, and the weight of the ending in Violetta and labor examples is the peak-end rule."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Remembering Self Versus the Experiencing Self in Vacation Choice","visual_frame_description":"A travel agency desk with a family debating next summer's destination while a photo album, a video camera, and a travel diary lie between them. Two silhouettes, one labeled remembering self and one labeled experiencing self, face the family as the player drags each decision onto the agent who really made it.","player_mission":"For each vacation-related decision, decide whether it was driven by the remembering self, the experiencing self, or by the peak-end rule shaping memory. Sort all six evidence cards into the correct column.","key_concepts":["Diener's team showed that intentions to repeat a vacation were entirely determined by its final global evaluation, not by the daily diary ratings.","Right or wrong, people choose by memory when they decide whether to repeat an experience.","Tourists who photograph every scene are designing a future memory rather than savoring the present view.","In the amnesic-vacation thought experiment, some people say they would not bother to go at all, revealing that they care only about their remembering self.","Some people treat themselves as they would treat another amnesic, maximizing overall pleasure by returning to a place where they were happy before.","A self-consciously memorable moment gains a weight and significance it would not otherwise have.","The word memorable in describing vacation highlights explicitly reveals that the goal of the experience is to store memories.","The remembering self composes stories and keeps them for future reference, in memories of colonoscopies, vacations, and films alike.","Kahneman's informal impression is that eliminating memories greatly reduces the value of an experience for most people.","Picture taking is not necessarily the best way for the tourist's experiencing self to enjoy a view."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the split between the remembering self and the experiencing self: vacation choices and repeat intentions are driven by the stored story and its final evaluation rather than by the pleasure actually experienced moment by moment. Kahneman's evidence shows that the remembering self chooses vacations, even when its verdict misrepresents the diary record.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which agent really makes each vacation decision: the remembering self that stores the story, the experiencing self that lives the moments, or the peak-end rule that reshapes the memory?","column_a_label":"Remembering self decides","column_b_label":"Experiencing self decides","column_c_label":"Peak-end rule reshapes memory","rows":[{"label":"A student plans to repeat spring break based only on her final global rating, not her daily diary scores","col":"a"},{"label":"A tourist photographs every scene as a future memory to be designed rather than savoring the view","col":"a"},{"label":"Violetta's lover makes it just in time, transforming the audience's verdict on her whole life","col":"c"},{"label":"A person offered a memory-erasing potion says they would not bother to take the trip at all","col":"a"},{"label":"A traveler returns to the familiar beach where she was happy before, maximizing pleasure for her own amnesic experiencing self","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Duration neglect, because the vacations differ only in length","WYSIATI, because the traveler attends only to the departure terminal","Affective forecasting, because the family predicts its future mood"],"explanation":"The remembering self dominates: repeat decisions follow the final global evaluation even when it misrepresents the diary, and many people value a trip by the story they expect to store. Only the traveler who maximizes the amnesic self's future pleasure is choosing as the experiencing self would."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Peak-End Evaluation Masking the Experiencing Self in a Vacation Retrospective","visual_frame_description":"A bright living room where Nina, a project manager in her thirties, sits across from an interviewer, a tablet showing her mood journal on the table between them. A timeline of her Lisbon trip scrolls along the wall, the middle days faded to gray while the final sunset glows in color.","player_mission":"Review Nina's baseline journal, the timed nonverbal log of her interview, and her transcript. Determine whether her glowing account of the trip is an honest record of experience or the product of a specific judgment mechanism, and file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Duration neglect is normal in a story, and the ending often defines its character.","The remembering self evaluates episodes through critical moments, especially the beginning, the peak, and the end.","In the cold-hand experiments, people's memory-based choices did not match their moment-by-moment experience.","Vacation repeat intentions are determined by the final global evaluation, not by the diary record.","Adding five slightly happy years to a very happy life made the whole life judged worse, a less-is-more effect.","The peak-end rule governs evaluations of episodes, vacations, and entire lives.","Peaks and ends matter in intuitive evaluation but duration does not.","Kahneman reports that many people care more about their remembering self than about their amnesic experiencing self.","Caring for people often takes the form of concern for the quality of their stories, not for their feelings.","A self-consciously memorable moment gains a weight and significance it would not otherwise have."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the peak-end rule and duration neglect in memory: Nina's retrospective verdict is built from the peak and ending of the trip while the flat middle days are neglected, so her report reflects her remembering self rather than her experienced self. Kahneman's vacation-diary evidence shows such final evaluations, not actual experience, drive repeat decisions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Nina's account of her week in Lisbon an honest report of how she experienced it, or is it distorted by a specific judgment mechanism?","subject_baseline":"Nina, a 34-year-old project manager, is normally precise and self-critical in meetings and keeps a meticulous mood journal. She rates her workdays candidly and complains freely about long commutes and rainy afternoons.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:02 Nina leans forward, eyes bright, recounting the sunset at Belem on the final evening; her voice rises with excitement.","10:09 Asked how she felt on Tuesday, she pauses, shrugs, and says she does not really remember the middle days.","10:15 When the interviewer mentions rain on Thursday, Nina's smile fades and she rubs her forehead, then quickly returns to describing the last dinner.","10:21 She gestures with both hands while describing the perfect ending at the airport when the gate agent upgraded her to a window seat.","10:28 Asked if she would go again, she answers instantly, absolutely, before the interviewer has finished the question."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: Tell me about the middle of the trip, say Tuesday afternoon.","Nina: Hmm, honestly it is a blur. A long walk, some rain I think. I mostly remember the beginning and the end.","Interviewer: Would you say it was a happy week overall?","Nina: Very. The ending sealed it, that last evening was perfect.","Interviewer: If you found out the final night had actually been cancelled, would that change your verdict?","Nina: Completely. It would be a different trip."],"correct_diagnosis":"Nina is not fabricating anything. Her verdict is produced by the remembering self, which composes the trip as a story weighted by its peak and ending, neglecting the duration and the flat middle days.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Her baseline journal shows rain and fatigue midweek, yet she instantly substitutes the ending for the total-happiness question, which is the peak-end rule. The blurring of Tuesday, the eager return to the final dinner, and the reflexive yes all indicate duration neglect and prototype substitution: the week is represented by a few critical moments rather than a sequence. Her report is honest to her remembering self but is not a faithful record of her experiencing self.","distractors":["Nina is lying to appear adventurous and impress the interviewer","Nina is letting a likeable mood, the affect heuristic, color everything","Nina is suffering from an anchoring effect set by the airport upgrade"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Ending Defines the Story: Duration Neglect in a Life and an Episode","visual_frame_description":"The final act of La Traviata fills the screen: Violetta on a chaise, the young lover arriving at the door, a glowing duet behind them. A caption asks whether the lover arrives in time, while a small timer shows 10 minutes remaining in the opera.","player_mission":"First explain why the last ten minutes matter more to the audience than a full year of Violetta's life. Then apply the same principle to advise a physician which of two patients will remember their painful procedure as milder.","key_concepts":["Duration neglect is normal in a story, and the ending often defines its character.","Kahneman cared about the possibility of missing Violetta's final ten minutes but not about missing a full year of her life.","The peak-end rule means evaluations are governed by the most intense moment and the ending.","Duration appears to matter for labor and vacations only because the end changes with the length of the episode.","A mother is more depleted after 24 hours of labor than after 6, which changes how she feels at the end.","In intuitive evaluation of entire lives as well as brief episodes, peaks and ends matter but duration does not.","The remembering self composes stories and keeps them for future reference.","We can be deeply moved even by events that change the stories of people who are already dead.","Diener's Jen study showed that doubling the lifespan had no effect on judged desirability.","We all care intensely for the narrative of our own life and want it to be a good story with a decent hero."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the peak-end rule and duration neglect: evaluations of an episode or a life are set by its memorable moments and its ending, not by the total time that passed. Kahneman's own response to La Traviata shows that the ending defines the character of the entire story, which is why retrospective judgments so often ignore duration.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Duration neglect and the peak-end rule: a story, and an evaluation of a life, is about significant events and memorable moments rather than time passing, so how an episode ends defines its character.","stage_1_observation":"In the final act of La Traviata, Violetta is dying when word arrives that her lover is rushing to Paris. She rallies with hope and joy, they sing a duet, and ten minutes later she dies. The audience is gripped by whether the lover arrives in time yet is utterly unmoved by whether Violetta lived to 27 or 28.","stage_1_question":"Why does the last ten minutes matter more to the audience than a full year of Violetta's life?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the audience's remembering self evaluates the whole story through its peak and its ending: duration is neglected, and the ending, reunion before death, defines the character of her entire life. A missed year of happy life would not move them, but a missed reunion would make La Traviata an altogether different story.","stage_2_intervention":"Two patients both underwent the same painful procedure this morning. Patient A endured 10 minutes of pain that ended with a slow taper to mild discomfort. Patient B endured 6 minutes that ended in a sharp, sudden spike. The physician asks you which patient's procedure patients will retrospectively rate as milder.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Patient A's 10-minute taper-ending will be remembered as milder, even though the actual experienced pain lasted longer, because retrospective evaluation is governed by the ending (the peak-end rule) rather than total duration. Duration appears to matter only when the ending changes with length, as when a mother is more depleted after 24 hours of labor than after 6."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Choosing by Memory: The Final Evaluation Decides the Repeat Vacation","visual_frame_description":"A dorm room at the end of spring break: a student's daily diary lies open with mostly high ratings, beside a questionnaire asking for a single global rating and a repeat-or-not checkbox. A calendar counts down to booking season.","player_mission":"Predict how the student's repeat-vacation decision will be made from the research evidence, then advise a friend who says he would not travel at all if all memories would be erased, who is about to book a painful jungle trek.","key_concepts":["Diener's team showed that intentions for future vacations were entirely determined by the final evaluation, even when that score misrepresented the diary quality.","Right or wrong, people choose by memory when they decide whether to repeat an experience.","The remembering self chooses vacations, not the experiencing self.","In the amnesic-vacation thought experiment, many people would value the trip far less if all pictures and memories were destroyed.","Some people treat themselves as they would treat another amnesic, returning to a place where they were happy before.","Some people say they would not bother to go at all, revealing that they care only about their remembering self.","Most people are remarkably indifferent to the pains of their experiencing self.","Kahneman reports feeling pity for his suffering self but not more than he would feel for a stranger in pain.","The experiencing self, who does the living, is like a stranger to me, Kahneman writes.","Touristic vacations are often evaluated by the story and the memories expected to be stored."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the remembering self's control over choice: repeat decisions follow the final global evaluation of an experience, not the diary of moments that made it up. Kahneman's vacation-diary study shows that people choose by memory, right or wrong, and that many people value their remembered story above their experienced pleasure.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"People decide whether to repeat an experience based on their memory of it, specifically the final global evaluation, rather than on the experienced quality captured moment by moment, even when the memory misrepresents that experience.","stage_1_observation":"Students keep daily diaries during spring break and rate each day. At the end they give one global rating of the vacation and say whether they would repeat it. One student's diary shows a long, pleasant stretch followed by a disappointing final day in the rain.","stage_1_question":"What does the research by Ed Diener and his team predict this student will do with her repeat decision?","stage_1_correct_answer":"She will decide based entirely on the final global evaluation, so the rainy ending will sink the repeat decision even though the diary showed most days were excellent. Statistical analysis showed intentions for future vacations are entirely determined by the final evaluation, right or wrong, because the remembering self chooses by memory.","stage_2_intervention":"A friend says that in the amnesic-vacation thought experiment he would not bother to go at all if all pictures and memories would be destroyed after the trip. He is about to book an arduous jungle trek that is mostly painful in real time. Do you stop him?","stage_2_correct_answer":"No, his preference is consistent with what Kahneman observes informally: many people reveal they care mainly about their remembering self. But do point out that the trek gains value only from the expectation that the pain and the joy of reaching the goal will be memorable; if he truly wanted to maximize his experiencing self, he would book the comfortable beach where he was happy before, as one would for another amnesic."}]},{"id":"ch42","title":"37. Experienced Well-Being","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Measuring the Two Selves: Experience Sampling, the Day Reconstruction Method, and Global Surveys","visual_frame_description":"A methodology lab shows three instruments: a cell phone beeping at random moments, a journal broken into scenes like a film, and a survey form asking about satisfaction with life as a whole. A diagram of the U-index charts time in an unpleasant state.","player_mission":"Match each measurement technique to what it actually captures, sorting every evidence card into the column for experience sampling, the Day Reconstruction Method, or global life-satisfaction surveys. Confirm which method approximates experience sampling for large-scale studies.","key_concepts":["Experience sampling interrupts people at random moments to ask what they are doing and how they feel right now.","The Day Reconstruction Method (DRM) has participants relive the previous day in detail, breaking it into episodes like scenes in a film.","The DRM produces a duration-weighted measure of daily affect in which longer episodes count more than short ones.","Validations against experience sampling confirmed the DRM recovers the feeling of a prototypical moment of an episode.","The global question about satisfaction with life as a whole is clearly addressed to the remembering self.","The Cantril Self-Anchoring Striving Scale asks people to place their life on a ladder from worst to best possible life.","The U-index is the percentage of waking time a person spends in an unpleasant state, based on an objective measurement of time.","The DRM questionnaires also included measures of life satisfaction, interpreted as the satisfaction of the remembering self.","Experience sampling was invented by Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi.","Gallup's life evaluation and experienced well-being are two distinct aspects of well-being."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between measuring the experiencing self and the remembering self: experience sampling and the Day Reconstruction Method capture felt moments, while global satisfaction surveys and the Cantril ladder capture the remembering self's judgment. Kahneman and his dream team built the DRM because it approximates experience sampling at far lower cost.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which technique captures each kind of data: moment-by-moment experience, a structured reconstruction of yesterday, or the remembering self's global judgment?","column_a_label":"Experience sampling","column_b_label":"Day Reconstruction Method (DRM)","column_c_label":"Life-satisfaction survey","rows":[{"label":"A cell phone beeps at random moments and asks what the person is doing and how they feel right now","col":"a"},{"label":"Participants relive the previous day as a sequence of scenes like a film, then rate each episode","col":"b"},{"label":"A duration-weighted daily affect score gives longer episodes more weight","col":"b"},{"label":"All things considered, how satisfied are you with your life as a whole these days?","col":"c"},{"label":"The U-index, the share of waking time spent in an unpleasant state","col":"b"},{"label":"The Cantril ladder asks where you stand from the worst to the best possible life","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Experience sampling, because the DRM was also invented by Csikszentmihalyi","The U-index, because it measures only positive moods","Life satisfaction, because both selves answer the same question"],"explanation":"The Day Reconstruction Method approximates experience sampling by having people reconstruct yesterday into episodes and rate feelings, yielding duration-weighted measures like the U-index. Life-satisfaction surveys and the Cantril ladder instead ask the remembering self for a global judgment, which is a different thing from experienced well-being."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"What Moves Experienced Well-Being Versus Life Evaluation","visual_frame_description":"A dashboard splits a person's day in two: on the left, a live mood trace that dips during the commute, under the boss, and during a headache; on the right, a global ladder score that rises with education and income. The player sorts findings between the two panels and a neutral middle zone.","player_mission":"Sort each research finding by what it chiefly affects: the emotions experienced during the day, the global evaluation of life, or neither because the effect is balanced or satiated. Finish by identifying the column that best characterizes experienced well-being as a whole.","key_concepts":["A headache raises the proportion reporting sadness and worry from 19% to 38% for the top two-thirds of the income distribution.","For the poorest tenth, a headache raises that proportion from 38% to 70%, a higher baseline and a much larger increase.","Educational attainment is associated with higher evaluation of one's life but not with greater experienced well-being.","More educated Americans tend to report higher stress.","Ill health has a much stronger adverse effect on experienced well-being than on life evaluation.","Living with children raises daily stress and anger, but the adverse effects on life evaluation are smaller.","Income beyond the satiation level of about $75,000 adds precisely zero to average experienced well-being.","Time pressure is a significant source of negative affect at work.","In the first study, the immediate presence of a boss was the only thing that was worse than being alone.","Happiness is, with only slight exaggeration, the experience of spending time with people you love and who love you."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the dissociation between experienced well-being and life evaluation: daily emotions track situational factors such as health, time pressure, and social contact, while the global judgment tracks education, income, and status. Kahneman's Gallup analyses show these two aspects of well-being are related but different, and life satisfaction is not a flawed measure of experience but something else entirely.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each finding by what it chiefly affects: the emotions of the day, the global judgment of life, or neither because the effect is balanced or satiated.","column_a_label":"Affects experienced well-being","column_b_label":"Affects life evaluation","column_c_label":"Affects neither (balanced or satiated)","rows":[{"label":"A headache raises sadness and worry more sharply for the poorest tenth than for the better-off","col":"a"},{"label":"Educational attainment raises life evaluation but is associated with higher stress in daily experience","col":"b"},{"label":"Income above roughly $75,000 adds zero to average experienced well-being","col":"c"},{"label":"Time pressure and the immediate presence of a boss sour mood at work","col":"a"},{"label":"Marriage changes some aspects of life for the better and others for the worse, with no average effect on experienced well-being","col":"c"},{"label":"Living with children raises daily stress and anger but has smaller effects on life evaluation","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Life evaluation, because all of these are global judgments people make in surveys","Temperament, because heritability determines every outcome here","The U-index, because it counts only negative moments"],"explanation":"Experienced well-being is driven largely by situational factors, including health, time pressure, the boss in the room, and social contact, while life evaluation responds more to education and income. Marriage and income above the satiation point are balanced or satiated, but the general rule is that the emotions of the day track situations, not the variables that raise global satisfaction."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"High Global Satisfaction Masking a Miserable Workday","visual_frame_description":"A bright office where Marcus, a bank analyst, gives a 9 on a ladder diagram while a companion mood-trace graph beside him shows deep dips on the morning train and at his manager's door. The tension between the two displays is visible on the screen.","player_mission":"Compare Marcus's global life-satisfaction score with his experience-sampling log and interview. Diagnose whether he is lying, deluded, or answering a different question entirely, then file the correct verdict.","key_concepts":["Global satisfaction with life is addressed to the remembering self, which has not proved to be a good witness.","The mood of the moment depends primarily on the current situation, not on global life satisfaction.","Time pressure and the immediate presence of a boss are significant sources of negative affect at work.","The morning commute had a U-index of 29% among 1,000 American women.","Life satisfaction and experienced well-being are related but different; life satisfaction is not a flawed measure of experience.","People's evaluations of their lives and their actual experience may be related, but they are also different.","Our emotional state is largely determined by what we attend to, and we are normally focused on our current activity and environment.","The U-index is higher by about 6% on weekdays than on weekends, mostly because of work-related stress and disliked activities.","Situational factors such as the opportunity to socialize with coworkers affect mood at work.","The presence of a boss was the only thing in the first study that was worse than being alone."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the gap between the remembering self's global evaluation and the experiencing self's record: Marcus's life-satisfaction score tracks his status and self-image while his daily mood is dominated by situational misery such as the commute and the boss. Kahneman's DRM and Gallup analyses show these two measures are different things rather than one being a flawed version of the other.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Marcus gives his life satisfaction a 9 on the Cantril ladder, but his experience-sampling log tells a different story. Is he lying, deluded, or measuring something else?","subject_baseline":"Marcus, 41, a bank analyst, describes himself as a satisfied man. In interviews he is cheerful, stable, and articulate. His DRM log, however, shows a 90-minute commute each way, about 28% of his waking day rated in unpleasant states, with his worst moments clustered at work and under his manager's oversight.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:31 Asked how satisfied he is with his life as a whole, Marcus answers fast, with a relaxed posture and a confident nod.","09:36 Asked to describe a typical workday, he leans back and says fine, standard, shoulders sagging slightly as he mentions his manager's office.","09:44 Recalling yesterday's 6:20 a.m. train, he checks his phone, sighs, and rubs the back of his neck.","09:52 Asked about the weekend with his sister, his face visibly relaxes; he smiles and talks for four uninterrupted minutes.","10:01 Asked to rate his mood right now, at this desk, he hesitates and gives a 6, then immediately corrects to 7, adding that he is basically a happy person."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: Overall, how satisfied are you with your life these days?","Marcus: Very. A solid 9. I have a good job, a family, a plan.","Interviewer: And during an average workday, how do you actually feel?","Marcus: You know, fine. You just do it. I do not really think about it.","Interviewer: Your morning commute yesterday?","Marcus: Rotten, honestly. But that is not my life. That is just Tuesday morning.","Interviewer: If your commute were suddenly shorter, would your life satisfaction go up?","Marcus: My satisfaction with my life? No. My Tuesday mornings, definitely."],"correct_diagnosis":"Marcus is not deceiving you. He is answering a global life-evaluation question with the remembering self, which is largely disconnected from the emotional record of his day; the situational misery of the commute and the boss shows up in experienced well-being but not in his life evaluation.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The fast, confident global answer and the dismissal of Tuesday as not my life reveal substitution: Marcus answers the easier question of how his life looks as a whole rather than summing his felt moments. His nonverbal relief on the weekend topic and his hesitation rating the in-the-moment mood confirm that situational factors dominate his day while leaving his life judgment untouched, exactly the measured gap between experienced well-being and life evaluation.","distractors":["Marcus is suppressing anger and scoring high to impress his employer","Marcus is anchored on his own 9-point self-rating","Marcus has a high U-index because he overestimates his pleasant activities"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Income Above Satiation and the Fading Pleasure of Small Joys","visual_frame_description":"A tasting room where Elena, a private-equity partner, takes a bite of dark chocolate while an expression-analysis overlay tracks her flat, brief smile. A chart beside her shows the Gallup satiation curve flattening near $75,000 while her forecast line keeps climbing.","player_mission":"Analyze Elena's baseline, her expression during a chocolate tasting, and her interview. Decide whether her claim that money buys happiness survives contact with the evidence, and file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Being poor makes one miserable, but being rich may enhance life satisfaction without improving experienced well-being.","Income beyond about $75,000 in high-cost areas adds zero to average experienced well-being.","Higher income is associated with a reduced ability to enjoy the small pleasures of life.","Priming students with the idea of wealth reduced the pleasure their faces expressed as they ate a bar of chocolate.","Life satisfaction keeps rising with income well beyond the point where experience stops improving.","Higher income permits many pleasures, but they do not show up in reports of emotional experience.","There is a clear contrast between the effects of income on experienced well-being and on life satisfaction.","An analysis of more than 450,000 Gallup-Healthways responses provided a definite answer about money and happiness.","The satiation level can be lower in areas where the cost of living is lower.","The average increase of experienced well-being associated with incomes beyond the satiation level was precisely zero."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the satiation of experienced well-being: beyond about $75,000, extra income adds nothing to daily emotional experience even though it keeps raising life satisfaction, and wealth priming appears to dull the enjoyment of small pleasures. The Gallup-Healthways analysis of over 450,000 responses supports this dissociation between experienced well-being and life evaluation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Elena, who earns $310,000 a year, insists money buys happiness. Her facial-expression data during a chocolate-tasting session suggest otherwise. What is happening?","subject_baseline":"Elena, 45, a private-equity partner, describes wealth as the key to well-being and says the more the better. She is animated and expressive at work and describes herself as someone who appreciates the finer things.","observed_nonverbal_log":["11:02 Elena is told she is tasting a premium bar of dark chocolate; she glances at the brand and price on the wrapper, then shrugs.","11:04 First bite: a minimal smile, brief, and she immediately checks her watch.","11:08 Asked whether the chocolate was enjoyable, she says nice, I guess, with a flat intonation and no change in expression.","11:13 A colleague mentions a bonus; Elena brightens and leans in, clearly more engaged by the money talk than by the chocolate.","11:21 Asked how happy a newly awarded $50,000 bonus will make her, she answers enormously, with a wide, fast smile."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: So you think income is the surest route to happiness?","Elena: Absolutely. Give me more money and you give me more pleasure.","Interviewer: We measured your expression while you ate that chocolate a few minutes ago.","Elena: It was fine. What does that prove?","Interviewer: The Gallup data suggest that beyond about $75,000, more income does not raise experienced well-being at all.","Elena: That cannot apply to me. I know I would enjoy my life more with another million."],"correct_diagnosis":"Elena's forecast that income reliably buys emotional pleasure is contradicted by both the data and her own behavior: beyond the satiation point, extra income does not raise experienced well-being, and the suggestive priming finding, that thinking of wealth reduces the pleasure a face expresses while eating chocolate, shows up in her own tasting session.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Elena's flat response to the chocolate despite high income matches the chapter's observation that higher income is associated with a reduced ability to enjoy small pleasures, and the finding that priming students with wealth reduced the pleasure expressed while eating a bar of chocolate. Her excitement about a bonus, a future transition, versus her indifference to the present bite is an error of affective forecasting about her experiencing self. The verdict: not lying, but systematically misforecasting where pleasure comes from.","distractors":["Elena has an unusually high U-index caused by poor sleep","Elena is anchoring her happiness on her salary negotiation","Elena is experiencing a focusing illusion about her commute"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reducing the U-Index: Where Policy Can Cut Experienced Suffering","visual_frame_description":"A policy war room with the U-index activity chart glowing on a wall screen: commute at 29%, work at 27%, child care at 24%, housework at 18%, socializing 12%, TV 12%, sex 5%. Three policy folders lie on the table for the player to pick from.","player_mission":"First identify which activity carries the highest U-index and explain why the weekend is 6% better than weekdays. Then choose the policy recommendation best supported by the chapter's evidence about reducing society's experienced suffering.","key_concepts":["The U-index is the percentage of waking time a person spends in an unpleasant state, based on an objective measurement of time.","For 1,000 American women, the U-index was 29% for the morning commute, 27% for work, 24% for child care, and 5% for sex.","Time spent with one's children was slightly less enjoyable than housework for American women.","The U-index was higher by about 6% on weekdays than on weekends, mostly because of work tension and disliked activities.","About half the participants reported going through an entire day without an unpleasant episode, while a small minority did most of the suffering.","Improved transportation, child care for working women, and socializing opportunities for the elderly are relatively efficient ways to reduce the U-index.","Even a 1% reduction in the U-index would be a significant achievement, amounting to millions of hours of avoided suffering.","The use of time is one of the areas of life over which people have some control.","Switching time from passive leisure such as TV watching to socializing and exercise improves experience.","Economist Alan Krueger took the lead in introducing elements of the method into national statistics."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the U-index as a policy instrument: experienced suffering is concentrated in specific blocks of time such as commuting and work, and it is unequally distributed, with a small minority absorbing most of the day's emotional pain. Kahneman argues that time-use choices and social interventions can reduce the U-index even when a 1% drop represents millions of hours of avoided suffering.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The U-index measures the share of waking time spent in an unpleasant state, and it is not distributed equally: about half of people report no unpleasant episodes on a given day while a small fraction does most of the suffering. Because time use is one area people can control, policy can reduce suffering by shifting how time is spent.","stage_1_observation":"You are a policy analyst reviewing the U-index data from 1,000 American women in a Midwestern city: 29% for the morning commute, 27% for work, 24% for child care, 18% for housework, 12% for socializing, 12% for TV watching, and 5% for sex. The team needs to choose where to intervene.","stage_1_question":"According to the chapter's data, which activity carries the highest U-index, and why is the weekend about 6% better than weekdays?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The morning commute carries the highest U-index at 29%. The weekday-weekend gap is mostly explained by the tension and stress of work and the time spent on disliked activities; the weekend gap closes because people spend less time in activities they dislike. Time pressure is a significant source of negative affect, and the immediate presence of a boss was the only thing worse than being alone in the first study.","stage_2_intervention":"You must recommend one of three policies: improved public transit and child care, a program encouraging workers to switch from passive leisure such as TV to socializing and exercise, or a tax cut aimed at households earning above $150,000. Which best matches the chapter's evidence about reducing the U-index?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Combine the first two policies. The chapter argues that improved transportation, child care for working women, and improved socializing opportunities for the elderly are relatively efficient ways to reduce the U-index, and that switching time from passive leisure such as TV watching to more active leisure including socializing and exercise improves experience. The tax cut fails because income beyond the satiation level adds zero to experienced well-being."}]},{"id":"ch43","title":"38. Thinking About Life","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Reading the Marriage Graph: Adaptation Versus a Salience Heuristic","visual_frame_description":"A conference slide shows Figure 16 from the German Socio-Economic Panel: life satisfaction rising sharply for two or three years around a wedding and then declining. Three labeled thought bubbles hover over the graph, one about adaptation, one about salience, and one about forecasting errors.","player_mission":"Sort each claim about the marriage graph into the column that matches Kahneman's interpretation: adaptation, the salience heuristic, or affective forecasting error. Finish by selecting the column that Kahneman argues the graph itself actually traces.","key_concepts":["Figure 16 shows life satisfaction spiking for two or three years around marriage and then declining.","The graph is commonly interpreted as tracing adaptation, in which the early joys of marriage disappear as experiences become routine.","Kahneman instead reads the graph as the time course of a heuristic for answering the life-satisfaction question.","Attention is the key: the spike mirrors the likelihood that people will think of their recent or forthcoming marriage when asked about their life.","The salience of the marriage thought is bound to diminish with the passage of time as its novelty wanes.","People who are happy to be reminded of their marriage are not necessarily happier the rest of the time.","Newlyweds' experienced well-being will eventually depend, as it does for the rest of us, on the environment and activities of the present moment.","The decision to get married reflects, for many people, a massive error of affective forecasting.","In the DRM studies, there was no overall difference in experienced well-being between women who lived with a mate and women who did not.","Survey participants manage to answer global life questions in a few seconds, mostly by substituting an easier question; System 1 is at work."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between an adaptation explanation and a heuristic explanation of the marriage graph: Kahneman argues the spike and decline trace how salient the marriage thought is when people answer a life-satisfaction question, not a surge in daily happiness that recedes. The graph can therefore tell us little about happiness or adaptation, only about how people answer the question.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which explanation best matches Kahneman's own reading of the spike and decline in life satisfaction around marriage?","column_a_label":"Adaptation explanation","column_b_label":"Salience heuristic explanation","column_c_label":"Affective forecasting error","rows":[{"label":"The graph is commonly read as tracing adaptation, with early joys dissolving into routine","col":"a"},{"label":"Attention is the key: the spike mirrors the likelihood of being reminded of a recent or forthcoming marriage when asked about life","col":"b"},{"label":"The salience of the marriage thought diminishes as its novelty wanes","col":"b"},{"label":"Couples know divorce is common but do not believe the statistics apply to them","col":"c"},{"label":"Survey participants answer global life questions in seconds by substituting an easier question","col":"b"},{"label":"People who are happy to be reminded of their marriage are not necessarily happier the rest of the day","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Adaptation, because the graph literally shows a decline after the wedding","Duration neglect, because married years simply count the same as single years","Temperament, because heritability explains the whole spike"],"explanation":"Kahneman argues the spike and decline in Figure 16 can be read as the time course of a heuristic: newlyweds are likely to retrieve the marriage when asked to evaluate their life, and that salience fades with novelty. The adaptation story is the folk reading and the divorce statistics are a separate affective-forecasting error, but the graph itself, Kahneman argues, mostly tells us about the question-answering heuristic."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"What Actually Drives Answers to Life-Satisfaction Questions","visual_frame_description":"A survey kiosk in a campus hallway where students answer a happiness question while floating reminders appear above them: a date card, a dime on a copying machine, a wedding photo, and a twins study pamphlet. Each reminder is tagged with the mechanism it activates.","player_mission":"Sort each finding by the mechanism that best explains the survey answers people give about their life as a whole: salience and substitution, temperament, or moment-by-moment experience. Confirm the column that dominates quick global judgments.","key_concepts":["Students who had just been asked how many dates they had reported their happiness these days as if dating was the only significant fact in their life.","Half the respondents who found a dime planted on the copying machine reported a marked improvement in satisfaction with their life as a whole.","A mood heuristic is one way to answer life-satisfaction questions.","The score quickly assigned to one's life is determined by a small sample of highly available ideas, not by a careful weighting of the domains of life.","A disposition for well-being is as heritable as height or intelligence, as demonstrated by studies of twins separated at birth.","Both experienced happiness and life satisfaction are largely determined by the genetics of temperament.","People who appear equally fortunate vary greatly in how happy they are.","The heuristics used to answer life questions are examples of both substitution and WYSIATI.","Any aspect of life to which attention is directed will loom large in a global evaluation.","The concept of happiness is not suddenly changed by finding a dime, but System 1 readily substitutes a small part of it for the whole."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests substitution and WYSIATI in global well-being judgments: quick answers to life-satisfaction questions are determined by a small sample of highly available ideas, such as a recent date or a found dime, rather than by careful weighting. Kahneman distinguishes these heuristic effects from the heritable temperament that explains stable between-person differences.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each finding by the mechanism that best explains the answers people give about their life as a whole.","column_a_label":"Salience and substitution","column_b_label":"Temperament","column_c_label":"Experience of the moment","rows":[{"label":"Students asked about dating before the happiness question report happiness as if dating were everything","col":"a"},{"label":"Finding a dime on the copying machine measurably raises reported life satisfaction","col":"a"},{"label":"Studies of twins separated at birth show a disposition for well-being is as heritable as height or intelligence","col":"b"},{"label":"People who recently married retrieve that fact when asked a general question about their life","col":"a"},{"label":"Paraplegics show near-normal experienced well-being much of the time once attention is withdrawn from the condition","col":"c"},{"label":"Newlyweds' experienced happiness still depends on the environment and activities of the present moment","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Experience sampling, because all survey answers reflect the U-index","The peak-end rule, because only endings are retrieved","Goal-setting, because life satisfaction depends on your ambitions"],"explanation":"Kahneman's core claim is that global life-satisfaction answers are produced by substitution and WYSIATI: a small sample of highly available ideas, a recent date, a found dime, a wedding, dominates the score. Temperament explains part of the stable between-person variance, and momentary experience explains felt states, but the quick survey answer is best predicted by what is salient at the moment of the question."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Time and the Two Selves: Adaptation, Duration Neglect, and the Transition Focus","visual_frame_description":"A time-themed diagram shows a long horizontal line of moments, but the mind's eye paints only three dots: a beginning, a peak, and an end. Off to the side, a lottery ticket, a paraplegic's day, and a colostomy patient's chart each route to one of three labeled mechanisms.","player_mission":"Sort each finding from the chapter's final reflections on time into the correct column: attention withdrawal and adaptation, duration neglect, or the transition focus of prospect theory. Finish by confirming the column that best captures adaptation to long-term circumstances.","key_concepts":["Adaptation to a new situation consists in large part of thinking less and less about it.","Most long-term circumstances of life, including paraplegia and marriage, are part-time states that one inhabits only when one attends to them.","Paraplegics are in a fairly good mood more than half of the time as early as one month following their accident.","Those who knew a paraplegic estimated 41% bad-mood time a year after the accident versus 68% for those who did not.","The main exceptions to adaptation are chronic pain, constant exposure to loud noise, and severe depression.","Colostomy patients show no difference in experienced happiness from healthy people but would trade away years of life to avoid the condition.","Duration is neglected in storytelling, where an episode is represented by a few critical moments, especially the beginning, the peak, and the end.","In prospect theory, a new state of wealth is represented by the transition to it, neglecting adaptation and time.","The mind is good with stories, but it does not appear to be well designed for the processing of time.","Winning a lottery is evaluated by the anticipated intensity of the reaction to the news that one has won."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests three distinct ways the mind mishandles time: attention withdrawal and adaptation for long-term circumstances, duration neglect in storytelling, and the transition focus of prospect theory where a state is judged by its onset. Kahneman concludes that the remembering self represents episodes by a few critical moments and that the mind is not well designed for the processing of time.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Sort each finding by the mechanism through which the mind handles time.","column_a_label":"Attention withdrawal and adaptation","column_b_label":"Duration neglect","column_c_label":"Transition focus","rows":[{"label":"Paraplegics are in a fairly good mood more than half the time as early as a month after the accident","col":"a"},{"label":"People who know a paraplegic estimate far less bad-mood time a year on (41%) than people who do not (68%)","col":"a"},{"label":"Adding five slightly happy years to Jen's life drops judged desirability because duration is neglected","col":"b"},{"label":"Colostomy patients would trade years of life to be without the condition though their experienced happiness matches healthy people","col":"b"},{"label":"Winning a lottery is evaluated by the anticipated reaction to the news, not by the years of the new state","col":"c"},{"label":"In prospect theory, a new state of wealth is represented by the transition to it","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Duration neglect, because adaptation ignores how long episodes last","The peak-end rule, because all of these involve endings","Temperament, because heritability governs adaptation speed"],"explanation":"The chapter argues that most long-term circumstances are part-time states inhabited only when attended to: adaptation consists of thinking less and less about a situation, which is why paraplegics' experienced mood returns toward normal. Duration neglect (the Jen results and the colostomy years traded away) and the transition focus of prospect theory (a lottery win judged by its announcement) are distinct errors of the same story-telling mind."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The California Transplant and the Focusing Illusion About Climate","visual_frame_description":"An engineer's kitchen in San Diego, Dario gesturing at a sunny window while a map of Ohio glows faintly on a tablet behind him. A mood log on the counter shows flat, ordinary weekdays punctuated by the word January in gray.","player_mission":"Evaluate whether Dario's report that the California climate made him happier is trustworthy. Weigh his baseline, the timed nonverbal log, and the transcript against the Schkade and Kahneman findings, then file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Students in California and the Midwest showed no difference whatsoever in life satisfaction.","Californians and Midwesterners shared the same mistaken view that Californians enjoy greater well-being.","The error was traced to an exaggerated belief in the importance of climate, which the researchers described as a focusing illusion.","The essence of the focusing illusion is WYSIATI: giving too much weight to the climate, too little to all the other determinants of well-being.","Long-term Californians are unlikely to be reminded of the climate when asked for a global evaluation of their life.","Thoughts of any aspect of life are more likely to be salient if a contrasting alternative is highly available.","The focusing illusion can cause people to be wrong about their present state of well-being, about the happiness of others, and about their own future happiness.","A person who recently moved will report himself happier because thoughts of the climate will make him believe that he is.","Nothing in life is as important as you think it is when you are thinking about it.","Climate was not an important determinant of well-being in the students studied."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the focusing illusion: when a salient thought such as the move from Ohio to California dominates a life-satisfaction question, the true weight of climate in experience is exaggerated. Kahneman and Schkade showed Californians and Midwesterners had identical life satisfaction despite both believing the opposite, because attention to a contrasting, salient feature distorts the global evaluation.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dario moved from Cleveland to San Diego three years ago, convinced the climate made him happier. Is his reported well-being trustworthy?","subject_baseline":"Dario, 38, an engineer, is energetic, decisive, and good-humored. He talks about the move constantly, keeps a weather app on his home screen, and tells colleagues the relocation was the best decision of his life.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:00 Asked about his life since moving, Dario's eyes light up; he gestures toward the window and says, look at that sky.","14:06 Asked how a typical Tuesday actually goes, he pauses and says normal, his enthusiasm visibly dropping.","14:12 When the interviewer asks about his commute, he grimaces; the 70-minute drive in traffic is clearly not sunny.","14:18 Asked whether he would move back to Ohio for a 40% raise, he hesitates, looks at the window again, and says he would miss the weather.","14:25 Asked how often he thinks about the climate during an ordinary week, he laughs and says he does not really think about it."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: Are you happier in San Diego than you were in Cleveland?","Dario: Definitely. Just look outside.","Interviewer: And on an ordinary Tuesday, how is your actual mood?","Dario: Same as anywhere, I suppose. Traffic is bad, work is work.","Interviewer: If you moved back, how much would your day-to-day happiness fall?","Dario: A lot. I would be miserable in that gray winter.","Interviewer: But in Cleveland, were you miserable on ordinary days?","Dario: No... no, I guess not. I just hated January."],"correct_diagnosis":"Dario's belief that the climate raises his well-being is a focusing illusion: the salient thought of the move and its contrast with Ohio distorts the weight of climate, while his actual daily experience of the commute and work is roughly unchanged.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The Schkade and Kahneman study found Californians and Midwesterners had identical life satisfaction even though both regions believed Californians were happier, an exaggerated belief in the importance of climate. Dario's own log shows the salience: he recalls the sky instantly but admits he rarely thinks about the climate on a normal week, and his Tuesday mood is unchanged. The verdict: he reports himself happier because the thought of the climate makes him believe he is, a focusing illusion, not a lie.","distractors":["Dario is deliberately exaggerating to justify a costly move","Dario's happiness is real and driven entirely by temperature effects","Dario is anchored on his old Ohio salary when judging the move"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Newlywed's Perfect Score and the Salience of the Wedding","visual_frame_description":"A kitchen in a new apartment where Nadia, a nurse, answers a survey while a wedding photo glows on the fridge and a mood-tracking phone shows ordinary, flat hospital shifts. The wedding photo and the phone are in visible tension.","player_mission":"Compare Nadia's perfect life-satisfaction score with her experience-sampling record and interview. Determine whether the score reflects daily happiness, the salience of the wedding thought, or something else, and file the correct diagnosis.","key_concepts":["The marriage graph spike reflects the likelihood that people will think of their recent or forthcoming marriage when asked about their life.","People who recently married, or expect to marry soon, are likely to retrieve that fact when asked a general question about their life.","Because marriage is almost always voluntary in the United States, almost everyone reminded of it will be happy with the idea.","Even newlyweds who enjoy a state of happy preoccupation with their love will eventually return to earth.","Unless people think happy thoughts about their marriage during much of their day, it will not directly influence their happiness.","Experienced well-being is on average unaffected by marriage because it changes some aspects of life for the better and others for the worse.","In the DRM studies, there was no overall difference in experienced well-being between women who lived with a mate and women who did not.","The decision to get married reflects, for many people, a massive error of affective forecasting.","The salience of the marriage thought is bound to diminish with the passage of time as its novelty wanes.","If the apparent surge in Figure 16 reflects a heuristic, there is little to learn from it about either happiness or adaptation."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the salience heuristic behind life-satisfaction answers: Nadia's perfect score reflects how readily the wedding comes to mind when she evaluates her life, not a surge in her daily experienced well-being. Kahneman reads the marriage graph as the time course of this heuristic, whose salience fades as the wedding's novelty wanes.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Nadia married three months ago and gives her life satisfaction a perfect 10, yet her experience sampling shows an ordinary, even stressful, daily life. What explains the gap?","subject_baseline":"Nadia, 29, a nurse, is warm and talkative. She planned her wedding for two years, posts about it constantly, and says being married is everything she wanted. Her DRM log shows average moods across hospital shifts, cooking, and some friction with her husband over chores.","observed_nonverbal_log":["16:02 Asked how satisfied she is with her life, Nadia answers, a 10, no question, with a bright, fast smile.","16:08 Describing a typical Tuesday, she hesitates; her tone flattens while mentioning the hospital and the dishes.","16:15 When the word wedding is mentioned, her face animates again immediately.","16:22 Asked how her mood was during yesterday's 11 p.m. shift change, she says fine with a shrug.","16:30 Asked whether marriage has made her daily moments happier, she says the moment? I do not know. The fact of being married, yes."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: Overall, how happy are you with your life right now?","Nadia: A 10. I am a married woman. It is everything.","Interviewer: And yesterday afternoon, during your shift?","Nadia: Oh, standard. Busy, a bit stressful. That is work.","Interviewer: So the marriage does not change your mood at work?","Nadia: Not the mood, no. But my life is better. Those are different questions.","Interviewer: In five years, when the wedding is old news, how will you answer?","Nadia: I hope the same. But I would probably think about something else."],"correct_diagnosis":"Nadia's perfect score reflects the salience of the marriage thought when she evaluates her life, a substitution heuristic, not a surge in her experienced well-being, which the DRM shows is largely unchanged.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Figure 16's spike is best read as the likelihood of being reminded of a recent wedding when asked about life, and that salience fades as novelty wanes. Nadia's own testimony confirms it: the word wedding animates her face, while describing her Tuesday or her shift flattens it, and she admits the marriage does not change her in-the-moment mood. The verdict: not deception, but a heuristic answer whose underlying daily happiness is separate from the remembered event.","distractors":["Nadia is hiding marital conflict behind a perfect score","Nadia is experiencing the halo effect of her wedding dress","Nadia's temperament guarantees the 10 is a stable, accurate judgment"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Luxury Car and Miswanting Through the Focusing Illusion","visual_frame_description":"A driveway where Owen, a sales director, stands beside a $72,000 sedan, grinning. A thought bubble over his head shows the car only appearing twice a week, while a weekly poker night with friends glows steadily in the corner of the scene.","player_mission":"Diagnose why Owen still insists the car transformed his daily happiness six months on. Review his baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then apply the focusing illusion and miswanting to file the correct verdict.","key_concepts":["You get pleasure from your car when you think about your car, which is probably not very often.","When asked how much you enjoy your car, you actually answer how much you enjoy it when you think about it.","The substitution caused you to ignore the fact that you rarely think about your car, a form of duration neglect.","The upshot is a focusing illusion: if you like your car, you are likely to exaggerate the pleasure you derive from it.","The focusing illusion misleads you when you think of your current vehicle and when you contemplate buying a new one.","Gilbert and Wilson introduced the word miswanting for bad choices that arise from errors of affective forecasting.","You are likely to exaggerate the long-term benefits of the car but not of a weekly social gathering that always demands attention.","The focusing illusion creates a bias in favor of goods that are initially exciting even if they will eventually lose their appeal.","Time is neglected, causing experiences that retain their attention value to be appreciated less than they deserve.","Nothing in life is as important as you think it is when you are thinking about it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the focusing illusion as a source of miswanting: when Owen rates his car, he answers the narrower question of how much he enjoys it when he thinks about it, substituting that for the broader question and neglecting how rarely he actually thinks about the car. Kahneman argues this biases people toward initially exciting goods whose appeal fades, while attention-demanding activities like a weekly club retain their value.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Owen bought a $72,000 luxury sedan expecting it to transform his daily happiness. Six months later he still rates his life much better thanks to the car. His behavior suggests otherwise. Diagnose.","subject_baseline":"Owen, 44, a sales director, is decisive and image-conscious. He traded his compact for a flagship sedan, says the purchase was the smartest money he ever spent, and privately admits he could not stop thinking about it for the first month.","observed_nonverbal_log":["15:02 Asked how much pleasure the car gives him, Owen leans back, grins, and says huge, every single day.","15:09 Asked when he actually thinks about the car during a normal day, he stops; his eyes shift; he says during the drive, I guess. Or not really.","15:15 Describing his actual commute, he mimics staring at traffic, shoulders slumped, and says it is just the road, honestly.","15:21 When the interviewer mentions the car's acceleration, Owen brightens and recounts a single highway moment from last month.","15:28 Asked to compare the car to his weekly poker night, he pauses, then says the poker night probably matters more, now that you ask."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: How much pleasure does the car give you?","Owen: Massive. Best money I have ever spent.","Interviewer: And when do you get that pleasure?","Owen: When I am driving it... well, I am not thinking about the car when I am driving. I am thinking about the call I have to make. So I guess, when I am thinking about it?","Interviewer: So when you rated the car, what were you really rating?","Owen: Huh. How much I enjoy it when I think about it. Which is maybe twice a week.","Interviewer: Would you trade the car for a weekly dinner with your old team?","Owen: Now that you make me think about it, yes, probably."],"correct_diagnosis":"Owen is rating how much pleasure he gets from the car when he thinks about it, a substitution that ignores how rarely he actually thinks about it. This is the focusing illusion, producing miswanting about the car's long-term payoff.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The chapter's car exercise shows the trick: you know instantly how much you like your car, but asking when you get pleasure from it reveals you only feel it when you think about it, which is seldom. Owen's log confirms it: a single vivid highway memory stands in for the whole month, and he admits the weekly poker night would be worth more. The verdict: an error of affective forecasting, miswanting, driven by the focusing illusion, which biases people toward initially exciting goods whose appeal fades while time is neglected.","distractors":["Owen is under an anchoring effect from the car's sticker price","Owen is accurately reporting a genuine transformation of his daily happiness","Owen is suffering from ego depletion after the negotiation"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Forecasting the Paraplegic's Mood: Attention Withdrawal and Adaptation","visual_frame_description":"A rehabilitation center office with two graphs side by side: one month and one year after a spinal-cord injury. Two estimation curves split dramatically at the one-year mark, while a patient in a wheelchair laughs with friends in the background of the scene.","player_mission":"First, read Beruria Cohn's survey results and state the two divergent estimates of bad-mood time one year after a paraplegic accident, explaining why they diverge. Then counsel two patients whose cases hinge on whether attention is withdrawn from a condition.","key_concepts":["Adaptation to a new situation consists in large part of thinking less and less about it.","Paraplegics are in a fairly good mood more than half of the time as early as one month following their accident.","People who knew a paraplegic estimated 41% bad-mood time one year after the accident; those who did not averaged 68%.","Those who did not know a paraplegic failed to forecast that attention would be withdrawn from the condition.","Chronic pain, constant exposure to loud noise, and severe depression are exceptions with no adaptation.","Pain and noise are biologically set to be signals that attract attention, and depression involves a self-reinforcing cycle of miserable thoughts.","Colostomy patients show no difference in experienced happiness from healthy populations yet would trade away years of life to avoid the condition.","The remembering self of colostomy patients is subject to a massive focusing illusion about the life the experiencing self endures quite comfortably.","Judgments about the mood of lottery winners one month and one year after the event showed exactly the same pattern.","Most long-term circumstances of life, including paraplegia and marriage, are part-time states that one inhabits only when one attends to them."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests adaptation as attention withdrawal: people who know a paraplegic personally observe the gradual withdrawal of attention and forecast far less bad-mood time a year on, while people who do not know one anchor on the salient early state. Kahneman's data show paraplegics return toward normal experienced mood quickly, with chronic pain, loud noise, and severe depression as the exceptions.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Adaptation consists largely of attention withdrawal: most long-term circumstances are part-time states inhabited only when attended to, so people who have never experienced a condition overestimate its hold on daily mood, while those who know someone affected see the withdrawal.","stage_1_observation":"You are a rehabilitation counselor reviewing Beruria Cohn's survey. Respondents estimated what proportion of time paraplegics spend in a bad mood. One group was told the accident happened a month earlier, another a year earlier. Both were split into people who know a paraplegic personally and people who do not. The estimates for the recent accident are nearly identical, about 75% versus 70%, but the one-year estimates diverge sharply.","stage_1_question":"What are the two estimates for the proportion of bad-mood time one year after the accident, and why do they differ?","stage_1_correct_answer":"People who know a paraplegic estimated 41%, while people who did not averaged 68%. Those who know someone observed the gradual withdrawal of attention from the condition; those who do not failed to forecast that adaptation would occur, anchoring their judgment on the salient early state. Judgments about lottery winners one month and one year out showed exactly the same pattern.","stage_2_intervention":"Your hospital is counseling two patients. Patient A is newly injured and is about to read an article claiming that paraplegics are miserable most of the time. Patient B is a colostomy patient who, on experience sampling, shows normal happiness but would trade years of life to avoid the condition. Both need a prognosis. Which guidance best matches the chapter's findings?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Tell Patient A that adaptation is real and rapid: paraplegics are in a fairly good mood more than half of the time as early as one month after the accident, though chronic pain, constant loud noise, and severe depression never adapt. For Patient B, explain that the experienced happiness of the condition is close to normal, but the remembering self's evaluation is dominated by a massive focusing illusion, hence the willingness to trade years of life despite normal experienced happiness."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Goals Set at Eighteen Predicting Satisfaction at Thirty-Eight","visual_frame_description":"A guidance counselor's office where an archival spreadsheet of 12,000 students from 1976 glows on a monitor, each row connecting a money-importance rating at age 18 to income and life-satisfaction scores twenty years later. A teenager in the foreground weighs three ambition cards.","player_mission":"First, extract the cohort findings on how the importance of money at age 18 predicted income and life satisfaction by age 38. Then advise a teenager choosing among three ambitions, identifying the goal that most risks a dissatisfied adulthood.","key_concepts":["Among 597 physicians and other medical professionals, each additional point on the money-importance scale was associated with an increment of over $14,000 of job income in 1995 dollars.","For nonworking married women, each point on the scale translated into more than $12,000 of added household income.","The importance people attached to income at age 18 anticipated their satisfaction with their income as adults.","For those who rated money as essential, the effect of income on life satisfaction was .57 point; for those who said money was not important, it was .12.","People who wanted money and got it were significantly more satisfied than average.","People who wanted money and did not get it were significantly more dissatisfied.","One recipe for a dissatisfied adulthood is setting goals that are especially difficult to attain.","The least promising goal, measured by life satisfaction twenty years later, was becoming accomplished in a performing art.","Teenagers' goals influence what happens to them, where they end up, and how satisfied they are.","Goals matter so much that an exclusive focus on experienced well-being is untenable; a hybrid view considering both selves is needed."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the lifelong effects of goals: ambitions rated at age 17 to 18 in a cohort of nearly 12,000 elite-school students predicted both income and satisfaction with income twenty years later, and hard-to-attain goals predicted dissatisfied adulthood. Kahneman concludes that well-being requires a hybrid view because goals are so central to what people do and how they feel about it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The goals people set for themselves matter to what they do and how satisfied they are with the result: a large cohort study showed that financial ambitions rated at age 17 to 18 predicted income and life satisfaction twenty years later, and that especially difficult-to-attain goals predict a dissatisfied adulthood.","stage_1_observation":"You are advising a guidance counselor reviewing the 1976 elite-school cohort data. Nearly 12,000 students rated how important being very well-off financially was on a 4-point scale; twenty years later their income and life satisfaction were measured. Among the 597 physicians and other medical professionals, each additional point on the money-importance scale meant over $14,000 more job income in 1995 dollars, and for nonworking married women over $12,000 more household income per point.","stage_1_question":"How did the importance of money at age 18 predict satisfaction with income at age 38, according to the chapter's comparison?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The effect of income on life satisfaction was far larger for those who called money essential, a difference of .57 point on a 5-point scale between the high-income group, above $200,000, and the low-to-moderate group, below $50,000, than for those who said money was not important, where the difference was only .12. People who wanted money and got it were significantly more satisfied than average, and people who wanted money and did not get it were significantly more dissatisfied.","stage_2_intervention":"A teenager in your office is choosing between three ambitions: becoming accomplished in a performing art, becoming a well-paid physician, and joining a weekly community orchestra for fun. Based on the chapter's findings, which ambition carries the greatest risk of a dissatisfied adulthood, and what does the counselor conclude about measuring well-being by experience alone?","stage_2_correct_answer":"The performing-art goal is the riskiest: the chapter reports that becoming accomplished in a performing art was the least promising goal measured by life satisfaction twenty years later, because setting goals that are especially difficult to attain is a recipe for a dissatisfied adulthood. The counselor should also accept a hybrid view: goals matter so much to what people do and how they feel that an exclusive focus on experienced well-being is not tenable, and the well-being of both selves must be considered."}]},{"id":"ch44","title":"Conclusions","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Duration Neglect and the Peak-End Rule","visual_frame_description":"A vacation-retrospective board at a travel agency shows five postcard-sized memory bubbles above an employee's desk. Each bubble is a different person's snapshot of the same week: a firework finale, a lost tent, a violin lesson, a hand pressed into ice water. The player sorts these memory snapshots into three labeled columns while a clock icon ticks away the forgotten middle days.","player_mission":"Classify each remembered episode under the evaluation rule that governs it: duration neglect, the peak-end rule, or duration-weighted well-being. Read the evidence rows, assign each to the correct column, and answer which rule explains a person rating a whole festival by its final concert alone.","key_concepts":["The remembering self keeps score and makes the choices, while the experiencing self does the living.","Duration neglect means the remembering self gives no weight to how long an episode lasted.","The peak-end rule weights an episode by its most intense moment and by how it ended.","In the cold-hand study, duration neglect plus the peak-end rule made people willingly choose more total pain in order to repeat the trial with the better ending.","An objective observer choosing for someone else would favor the shorter exposure and the interests of the experiencing self.","Duration neglect and the peak-end rule are characteristics of memory and originate in System 1, not in the values of System 2.","Duration-weighted well-being treats all moments of life alike, whether they are memorable or not.","A moment can gain weight by altering later experience, as an hour of violin practice enhances many later hours of playing and listening to music.","The rules that govern the evaluation of the past are poor guides for decision making, because time does matter.","Time is the ultimate finite resource, but the remembering self ignores that reality when it evaluates episodes and lives.","The remembering self's neglect of duration, its emphasis on peaks and ends, and its susceptibility to hindsight combine to distort our reflections of actual experience."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how the remembering self evaluates episodes: duration neglect strips away how long an experience lasted, while the peak-end rule compresses the whole episode into its most intense moment and its ending. In the cold-hand study Kahneman showed that these System 1 memory rules made people choose more total pain for a better-remembered ending, choices an objective observer would call mistakes.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Priya rates an entire weeklong festival almost solely by its spectacular closing concert, as if the two forgettable middle days never happened. Which evaluation mechanism best explains her rating?","column_a_label":"Duration neglect: the remembering self gives no weight to how long the episode lasted","column_b_label":"Peak-end rule: the remembering self weights the most intense moment and the ending","column_c_label":"Duration-weighted well-being: the experiencing self counts every moment equally","rows":[{"label":"Helena rates a 20-minute cold-water hand soak as just as bad as a 10-minute one, because how long it lasted never enters her score.","col":"a"},{"label":"Marcus recalls his entire backpacking trip as the day they lost the tent and dismisses twelve pleasant days.","col":"b"},{"label":"An objective observer choosing for someone else would always pick the shorter, less painful exposure.","col":"c"},{"label":"A patient remembers a procedure by its agonizing peak and the soothing final minute a nurse spent holding her hand.","col":"b"},{"label":"Ahmed prefers one vivid week of intense joy over a full year of moderate contentment because the year has no memorable peaks.","col":"b"},{"label":"An hour of violin practice is weighted heavily because it improves hundreds of later hours of playing and listening to music.","col":"c"},{"label":"Sofia fears a short, intense but tolerable pain more than a much longer stretch of moderate pain.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Anchoring: the closing concert sets a reference value that pulls all later estimates toward it","Availability cascade: media coverage of the festival amplified fear beyond the actual risk","Loss aversion: the remembered trip is treated as a loss that must be recovered"],"explanation":"Priya's rating collapses onto the last moment, which is exactly the peak-end rule: the remembering self weights the most intense and the final moments of an episode and ignores its duration. Duration neglect and the peak-end rule work together, and in the cold-hand study they produced choices that an objective observer, favoring the experiencing self's duration-weighted view, would describe as mistakes."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Econs, Humans, and Libertarian Paternalism","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen policy design studio. On the left, a man in a pinstripe suit reads a contract under a magnifying glass labeled Econ. On the right, a woman signs the same contract without reading while a small print disclaimer scrolls beneath. In the center, a retirement-plan enrollment form sits with the opt-in box already checked, stamped Default.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence row under the category it illustrates: Econ behavior under the rational-agent model, Human behavior under System 1 quirks, or a libertarian-paternalist nudge. Then judge which category best explains an employee who stays in a pension plan purely because enrollment was the automatic default.","key_concepts":["For economists, the only test of rationality is internal consistency, not whether beliefs and preferences are reasonable.","An Econ is by definition not susceptible to priming, WYSIATI, narrow framing, the inside view, or preference reversals, which Humans cannot consistently avoid.","Reasonable people cannot be rational by the coherence definition, but they should not be branded irrational for that reason.","Thaler and Sunstein's libertarian paternalism lets institutions nudge people toward decisions that serve their own long-term interests.","The default option is naturally perceived as the normal choice, so deviating from it requires more effortful deliberation and risks regret.","Choice architecture, the framing of an individual's decision, has a huge effect on the outcome.","Save More Tomorrow ties increased saving to pay raises, turning losses into foregone gains that are easier to bear.","Save More Tomorrow's automaticity aligns the laziness of System 2 with the long-term interests of workers.","A rational agent is assumed to read and understand the fine print of a contract, but a Human usually does not.","The Chicago school view that rational people should be free to choose provides the foundation for the libertarian approach to public policy.","A nation of Econs needs no government protection because rational agents make no mistakes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests libertarian paternalism and the default option effect: because the default is naturally perceived as the normal choice, deviating requires effortful System 2 deliberation and invites regret, so people passively stay enrolled. Thaler and Sunstein's Nudge shows that choice architecture can steer Humans toward their long-term interests without curtailing anyone's freedom, something the rational-agent model assumes is unnecessary.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dana stays enrolled in her employer's pension plan simply because joining was the automatic default; she never even considers the opt-out checkbox. Which account best explains her behavior?","column_a_label":"Econ behavior under the rational-agent model (coherence)","column_b_label":"Human behavior under System 1 quirks (WYSIATI, framing, priming)","column_c_label":"Libertarian paternalism: a nudge through choice architecture","rows":[{"label":"An Econ reads and understands the fine print of a contract before signing it.","col":"a"},{"label":"A Human routinely signs contracts in which important information is hidden in plain sight.","col":"b"},{"label":"The default option is perceived as the normal choice, and deviating is an act of commission that takes more effort and risks regret.","col":"c"},{"label":"A rational agent is by definition never susceptible to priming, WYSIATI, or narrow framing.","col":"a"},{"label":"Save More Tomorrow ties increased saving to pay raises, turning losses into easier-to-bear foregone gains.","col":"c"},{"label":"A shopper buys a product labeled 90 percent fat-free and rejects the identical one labeled 10 percent fat.","col":"b"},{"label":"Extreme conservatives and liberals jointly sponsor the Save More Tomorrow bill in Congress.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Prospect theory: Dana frames the pension contribution as a gain she is unwilling to risk","Affect heuristic: Dana likes the pension brand, so she judges enrollment by feeling alone","Hindsight bias: once the outcome is known, Dana believes she always intended to save"],"explanation":"Dana never actively chose; she stayed with the default, which is the essence of a nudge. Libertarian paternalism relies on choice architecture, and the default is perceived as the normal choice, so deviating requires effortful deliberation and risks regret. An Econ would be immune because the rational-agent model assumes careful reading of all information, but a Human needs the default arranged in her own long-term interest."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"System 1, System 2, and Skilled Responses","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory bench shows the classic Mueller-Lyer illusion, two equal lines flanked by fins pointing in and out. Three gauges labeled System 1, System 2, and Skill hang above it. Below, a queue of mini-scenarios scrolls past: a math problem, a passenger outburst, a stone in the path, a chessboard.","player_mission":"Assign each scenario to the process that produces it: automatic System 1, effortful System 2, or a skilled response drawn from the repertory of practice. Then determine which process must be deliberately recruited to overcome a perceptual illusion that System 1 will never unsee.","key_concepts":["System 1 operates automatically and quickly, with little or no effort and no sense of voluntary control.","System 2 is the effortful, slower system that monitors System 1 and maintains control as best it can within its limited resources.","System 2 articulates judgments and makes choices, but it often endorses or rationalizes ideas and feelings generated by System 1.","When a challenge is encountered to which a skilled response is available, that response is evoked automatically.","Memory holds a vast repertory of skills acquired through practice, from walking around a large stone to averting an incipient customer outburst.","Skill develops when there is a regular environment, adequate opportunity to practice, and rapid, unequivocal feedback.","System 1 is not constrained by capacity limits and may substitute a response that more easily comes to mind for the one requested.","System 1 registers the cognitive ease with which it processes information but does not generate a warning signal when it becomes unreliable.","System 2's only recourse against an unreliable intuition is to slow down and construct an answer on its own, which it is reluctant to do because it is indolent.","The way to block errors originating in System 1 is to recognize the signs of a cognitive minefield, slow down, and ask for reinforcement from System 2.","Cognitive illusions are generally more difficult to recognize than perceptual illusions such as the Mueller-Lyer illusion."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the two-systems account of error correction: System 1 produces fast, confident impressions that cannot be unlearned on the spot, so the way to block its errors is to recognize the situation and slow down to recruit System 2. Kahneman warns that this is hardest exactly when it is needed most, because more doubt is the last thing you want when you are in trouble and cognitive illusions are harder to recognize than perceptual ones.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Facing a Mueller-Lyer illusion, you can verify that the two lines are equal but still cannot unsee the length difference. Which process must be deliberately recruited to avoid the error?","column_a_label":"System 1: automatic, fast, with no sense of voluntary control","column_b_label":"System 2: effortful, slow, monitors System 1 within its limited resources","column_c_label":"Skilled response: automatic yet accurate, refined by practice and feedback","rows":[{"label":"The answer 10 cents comes to mind immediately in the bat-and-ball problem.","col":"a"},{"label":"You recognize that 17 times 24 calls for deliberate calculation and effort.","col":"b"},{"label":"A veteran flight attendant averts an incipient passenger outburst almost without thinking.","col":"c"},{"label":"You register cognitive ease and accept a familiar-sounding claim as true without checking it.","col":"a"},{"label":"An auditor slows down and recomputes a forecast line by line before signing off.","col":"b"},{"label":"Walking around a large stone in the path is executed automatically and correctly.","col":"c"},{"label":"A chess master's opening response to a known position comes to mind instantly and accurately.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Skilled response: enough chess-style practice makes the illusion itself disappear","Cognitive ease: the correct answer simply feels more fluent after repetition","Anchoring: you can adjust away from the illusory length by estimating a correction"],"explanation":"The illusion persists at the perceptual level, so System 1 keeps insisting on the wrong length, and the only reliable correction is to slow down and let System 2 override the impression. Kahneman's closing point is that System 2's only recourse is to slow down and construct an answer on its own, which it is reluctant to do because it is indolent."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing the Remembering Self's Evaluation","visual_frame_description":"A conference-room audit plays back as a split screen: on the left, Mara, a product manager in a gray blazer, answers questions; on the right, a live log scrolls with timestamps and arousal notes. A 9-month project timeline runs along the bottom, with the middle eight months dimmed and the final demo glowing brightly.","player_mission":"Audit whether Mara's rating of her nine-month project reflects the duration-weighted experience or a memory distortion. Weigh the behavioral log and the interview transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis of the mechanism shaping her verdict.","key_concepts":["The remembering self keeps score and makes the choices, while the experiencing self does the living.","Duration neglect means the remembering self gives no weight to how long an episode lasted.","The peak-end rule weights an episode by its most intense moment and by how it ended.","The remembering self is a construction of System 2, but duration neglect and the peak-end rule originate in System 1.","The rules that govern the evaluation of the past are poor guides for decision making, because time does matter.","An objective observer of the hedonimeter profile might well offer different advice than the remembering self.","The central fact of our existence is that time is the ultimate finite resource, but the remembering self ignores that reality.","The remembering self's neglect of duration, emphasis on peaks and ends, and susceptibility to hindsight distort our reflections of actual experience.","A duration-weighted conception of well-being treats all moments of life alike, memorable or not.","In the cold-hand study, duration neglect and the peak-end rule made people choose more total pain to get a better-remembered ending."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests whether an evaluation is driven by the remembering self's memory rules rather than by the duration-weighted experience. The arousal spike at the remembered peak and the flat dismissal of the long middle are the signature of duration neglect plus the peak-end rule, which Kahneman showed in the cold-hand study can make people choose outcomes that are worse for the experiencing self.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Is Mara, a product manager, evaluating her nine-month project by the remembered whole, or is a memory distortion inflating her report?","subject_baseline":"Mara normally reviews projects with a detailed scorecard: budget variance, milestone timing, customer interviews, and team retrospective notes. Colleagues describe her as meticulous and numbers-driven; she keeps dated logs and can quote figures from months past.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:12 - Asked for an overall rating of the project, Mara's eyes brighten, she leans forward, and she smiles broadly.","09:17 - Asked how the middle months went, Mara glances away, shrugs, and says 'fine, normal,' with flat, low-energy delivery.","09:24 - Asked to recall the final demo, Mara's posture snaps upright, her voice rises, and she gestures vividly while recounting the standing ovation.","09:31 - Told that the project actually ran two months late, Mara's mouth tightens; she quickly adds that the ending made it worth it.","09:38 - Asked to compare total effort across phases, she waves a hand dismissively and gives only the last-week numbers."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Overall, how would you rate the nine months of the project?'","Mara: 'Honestly? Ten out of ten. The demo we gave in the final week was incredible - everyone in the room was on their feet.'","Interviewer: 'What about the stretch from March to August?'","Mara: 'Hmm, that part was fine. Standard grind. Nothing much to say.'","Interviewer: 'The retrospective flagged that you delivered two months late. Does that change the rating?'","Mara: 'It is a wrinkle. But the ending - that is what the project is, really.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Duration neglect combined with the peak-end rule: Mara's remembering self compressed nine months into its final peak, gave the long middle no weight, and her perfect rating reflects the story of the ending rather than the duration-weighted experience.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The arousal spikes at 09:12 and 09:24 track with vivid peak-and-end material, while the middle months produce flat, dismissive responses, exactly the signature of an episode rated by its most intense and final moments. Her willingness to discount the two-month delay with the ending line is the remembering self's neglect of duration: time, the ultimate finite resource, drops out of the score. An objective observer of the whole profile would weigh all nine months, but Mara's memory does not.","distractors":["Anchoring: Mara anchored her rating on the standing ovation's intensity as a reference value","Availability cascade: media buzz about the demo amplified her fear of a bad ending","Loss aversion: Mara treats the late delivery as a loss she must immediately recover"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing the Default Option Effect","visual_frame_description":"A benefits office cubicle, Dmitri a systems engineer in a hoodie, sits in front of a retirement enrollment portal where the 6 percent contribution box is already checked. A red tracer line highlights the single opt-out checkbox, tiny and far down the page, while a live emotion log records his hesitations.","player_mission":"Audit whether Dmitri's pension behavior is deliberate saving or passive acceptance of a nudge. Examine the timed behavioral log and the interview transcript, then diagnose the mechanism driving his 'choice' to keep the default rate.","key_concepts":["The default option is naturally perceived as the normal choice.","Deviating from the default is an act of commission that requires more effortful deliberation and takes on more responsibility.","Deviating from the default is more likely to evoke regret than doing nothing.","Choice architecture, the framing of the individual's decision, has a huge effect on the outcome.","Libertarian paternalism allows institutions to nudge people toward decisions that serve their own long-term interests.","It is difficult to argue that anyone's freedom is diminished by automatic enrollment when they merely have to check a box to opt out.","The nudge is based on sound psychology grounded in the workings of System 1 and System 2.","Save More Tomorrow avoids resistance to an immediate loss by tying increased saving to pay raises.","The automaticity of Save More Tomorrow aligns the laziness of System 2 with the long-term interests of workers.","Humans, unlike Econs, need help to make good decisions, and there are informed and unintrusive ways to provide that help."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the default option effect within libertarian paternalism: a default is naturally perceived as the normal choice, so deviating feels like an effortful act of commission that risks regret. Kahneman's chapter concludes that such choice architecture can steer Humans toward their long-term interests, because an Econ would need no such help and a Human receives it without losing freedom.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Dmitri, a financially literate engineer, never opted out of his default pension plan despite earning enough to save far more on his own. Is his behavior deliberate saving, or is a nudge doing the work?","subject_baseline":"Dmitri is a sharp systems engineer who reads contracts carefully at work, debates fees with his bank, and complains loudly about inertia in others. He has never once reviewed his own benefits enrollment package and files those emails unread.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:05 - Asked how he chose his contribution rate, Dmitri frowns and looks at the ceiling; his hand hovers motionless over the table.","10:11 - When the default rate of 6 percent is mentioned, he nods rapidly and says 'right, the 6 percent,' with no change in arousal.","10:18 - Asked why he never raised it to 10 percent, he shrugs, tilts his head, and delivers a memorized line about a sensible balance.","10:26 - Asked to name his actual rate, he hesitates for four seconds before answering, and his fingers tap the table.","10:33 - Told that opting out requires only one checkbox, his eyebrows rise and he laughs once, then goes silent."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'How did you decide on your retirement contribution?'","Dmitri: 'I kept it where it was. It is the sensible, normal thing - that is what the plan sets you up with.'","Interviewer: 'Why not bump it up to the 10 percent you could easily afford?'","Dmitri: 'Changing it feels like a whole process - you would own the responsibility for it, and if it backfired you would kick yourself.'","Interviewer: 'It is literally one checkbox.'","Dmitri: 'Yeah, sure. Still. Why poke it.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Default option effect: Dmitri's 'choice' is passive acceptance of the default, which feels like the normal choice, while deviating would be an act of commission requiring effortful deliberation and risking regret.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The hesitation when naming his own rate and the memorized rationalization about the sensible, normal thing reveal that he never made a reasoned contribution decision; the 6 percent default was perceived as normal, and switching felt costly. His laugh at the single-checkbox fact is the recognition that inertia, not analysis, runs the account. Kahneman's summary: the default is naturally perceived as the normal choice, and deviations are more effortful and more likely to evoke regret.","distractors":["Loss aversion: Dmitri treats any contribution increase as a loss to his take-home pay","Anchoring: Dmitri anchored on the 6 percent number shown in the enrollment form","Availability cascade: retirement-fear media made Dmitri over-save"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Auditing a Rationalized System 1 Decision","visual_frame_description":"A corner-office audit plays with a live fact-check pane: a VP's fluent pitch about a product launch is cross-cut with her own March memo warning of the same market risks. A trail of micro-expressions - a blink, a collar touch - flickers at the moment a question about the project's leader lands.","player_mission":"Determine whether the VP's green light was reasoned analysis or a System 1 feeling that System 2 dressed up afterward. Read the nonverbal log and the interview transcript, then issue the correct diagnosis of the mechanism behind her fluent rationale.","key_concepts":["System 2 articulates judgments and makes choices, but it often endorses or rationalizes ideas and feelings generated by System 1.","You may not know that you are optimistic about a project because something about its leader reminds you of your beloved sister.","If asked for an explanation, you will search your memory for presentable reasons and will certainly find some, and you will believe the story you make up.","System 2 is not merely an apologist for System 1; it also prevents many foolish thoughts and inappropriate impulses from overt expression.","System 1 registers cognitive ease with which it processes information but does not generate a warning signal when it becomes unreliable.","Intuitive answers come to mind quickly and confidently, whether they originate from skills or from heuristics.","Many suggestions of System 1 are casually endorsed with minimal checking, as in the bat-and-ball problem.","The attentive System 2 is who we think we are, but its abilities and knowledge are limited.","We do not always think straight when we reason, and the errors are not always due to intrusive and incorrect intuitions.","A richer language of bias labels helps us recognize situations in which errors are likely."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests System 2's role as an apologist for System 1: a decision can be generated by an automatic feeling, and System 2 then searches memory for presentable reasons and sincerely believes the story it constructs. Kahneman's chapter stresses that System 2 often endorses or rationalizes System 1's output rather than independently analyzing it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Renee, a VP, greenlit a risky product because she felt unusually optimistic about its leader. Was the decision reasoned analysis, or a System 1 feeling that System 2 dressed up afterward?","subject_baseline":"Renee is an experienced operations VP famous for demanding spreadsheets, market data, and pre-mortems before any launch. In one documented case she killed a project she privately admitted she liked too much. She gives polished, fluent rationales for every decision.","observed_nonverbal_log":["11:02 - Asked why she approved the launch, Renee answers instantly and fluently, eyes steady, with confident upward gestures.","11:09 - Asked to name the three weakest market assumptions, she stops, looks down, and lists generic competitive pressure items; her voice drops.","11:15 - Asked whether anything about the product's leader, Tom, influenced her, Renee blinks twice, touches her collar, and replies that this is not how she works.","11:22 - Asked again, she says Tom reminds her of someone dependable, then immediately adds that it is irrelevant to the numbers.","11:28 - Shown her own prior memo that flagged the same market risks, she squints and says those were resolved."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'Walk me through the decision to approve the launch.'","Renee: 'The market timing is right, the unit economics hold at scale, and Tom's team is exactly the experienced group this needs.'","Interviewer: 'What are the two weakest assumptions in that case?'","Renee: 'Ah - competitive response, and maybe adoption speed. We have modeled both. We are comfortable.'","Interviewer: 'Could anything about Tom personally have shaped your optimism?'","Renee: 'No. I evaluate on the data. Tom just happens to be a very capable leader.'","Interviewer: 'Your March memo flagged the same adoption risk as a blocker.'","Renee: 'That was a different context. This is resolved.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"System 2 endorsing and rationalizing a System 1-generated feeling: Renee's optimism was primed by Tom's resemblance to someone dependable, and her fluent answers are presentable reasons invented after the fact, which she sincerely believes.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The instant, fluent rationale at 11:02 with only generic risk items at 11:09, followed by the defensive collar-touch when the Tom question lands, matches Kahneman's description: you may not know you are optimistic because something about a leader reminds you of a beloved person, but asked for an explanation you search for presentable reasons and believe the story. Her denial is the hallmark of System 2 as apologist, articulating and justifying a choice actually generated by System 1, while her own March memo shows the analysis did not change the outcome.","distractors":["Anchoring: Renee anchored on the launch date and adjusted insufficiently","Duration neglect: Renee ignored the multi-year cost of a failed launch","Planning fallacy: Renee underestimated effort because of the inside view"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Two Selves Face-Off","visual_frame_description":"A pain-research lab with two cold-water baths under crimson lights. A volunteer, Iris, stands between Trial A, a shorter flat 60-second immersion, and Trial B, a longer 90-second immersion that ends in pleasantly warming water. Above her, two crowned figures argue: the Remembering Self waves a highlight reel of the ending while the Experiencing Self points at a clock counting total time under the water.","player_mission":"Defeat the Remembering Self, the boss who keeps people choosing by the quality of their memories. Observe which trial Iris picks and identify the self that drives her choice, then design a nudge-style decision aid that lets patients choose in line with the experiencing self's interests.","key_concepts":["The remembering self keeps score and makes the choices, while the experiencing self does the living.","Duration neglect means the remembering self gives no weight to how long an episode lasted.","The peak-end rule weights an episode by its most intense moment and by how it ended.","In the cold-hand study, subjects preferred to repeat the trial that left the better memory even though it involved more pain.","An objective observer making the choice for someone else would undoubtedly choose the short exposure, favoring the sufferer's experiencing self.","The choices people made on their own behalf in the cold-hand study are fairly described as mistakes.","Duration neglect and the peak-end rule originate in System 1 and do not necessarily correspond to the values of System 2.","The central fact of our existence is that time is the ultimate finite resource, but the remembering self ignores that reality.","An objective observer of the hedonimeter profile might well offer different advice than the remembering self.","The remembering self's neglect of duration, emphasis on peaks and ends, and susceptibility to hindsight distort our reflections of actual experience."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the conflict between the remembering self, which keeps score using duration neglect and the peak-end rule, and the interests of the experiencing self, which endures every moment. Kahneman's cold-hand study shows people will choose more total pain for a better-remembered ending, choices an objective observer would call mistakes, because the remembering self ignores that time is the ultimate finite resource.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The final conflict pits the remembering self, which keeps score and makes choices using duration neglect and the peak-end rule, against the interests of the experiencing self, which endures every moment. Kahneman's cold-hand study showed that people willingly repeated a longer, more painful trial because it ended better; the remembering self won, and by an objective standard the choice was a mistake.","stage_1_observation":"In a hospital pain-research lab, the experimenter offers each participant two versions of the same cold-water immersion. Trial A is 60 seconds of 14-degree water, flat the whole time. Trial B is 90 seconds of 14-degree water followed by 30 extra seconds of gradually warming water, so the ending is milder. Every participant chooses which trial to repeat for a small fee. You watch a volunteer, Iris, study the two options with a neutral expression and pick Trial B without hesitation.","stage_1_question":"Iris chose Trial B, which involves more total pain, because it ends more pleasantly. Which self is making the choice, and what rule drives it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The remembering self, driven by the peak-end rule and duration neglect: Iris will remember the milder ending more than the longer exposure, so the memory of Trial B wins the choice, even though an objective observer favoring the experiencing self would pick the shorter Trial A.","stage_2_intervention":"The lab director asks you to design a nudge-style decision aid so future volunteers, and patients facing real procedures, choose in line with the experiencing self's interest. You can redesign the consent screen. Choose the intervention that research in this chapter supports as most effective.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Present a duration-weighted hedonimeter profile, a graph of the pain over time for each option so the total duration and the flat middle are visible, and ask the patient to choose for the person who actually endures the procedure, making the experiencing self's interest the explicit frame. Kahneman notes that an objective observer of the hedonimeter profile would choose the short exposure, and this framing recruits System 2 to counteract the peak-end shortcut of the remembering self."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Cognitive Minefield and Organizational Remedies","visual_frame_description":"A cavernous boardroom table holds a single optimistic project estimate: 18 months and $210 million, stamped High Confidence. Beneath it, a hidden comparison chart lists ten comparable tunnels averaging 34 months and $380 million. A small red wire, labeled Premortem, hangs unused beside the projector while the board members nod along.","player_mission":"Defeat the Cognitive Minefield, the boss that makes System 1 errors look like certainty. Observe why the team's plan is so far from comparable projects, then choose the organizational procedure that best corrects overconfident, inside-view predictions before the board votes.","key_concepts":["System 1 registers the cognitive ease with which it processes information but does not generate a warning signal when it becomes unreliable.","Kahneman's own intuitive thinking remains prone to overconfidence, extreme predictions, and the planning fallacy despite years of study.","The way to block errors originating in System 1 is to recognize the signs of a cognitive minefield, slow down, and ask for reinforcement from System 2.","It is much easier to identify a minefield when you observe others wandering into it than when you are about to do so.","Organizations are better than individuals at avoiding errors because they naturally think more slowly and have the power to impose orderly procedures.","Organizations can institute and enforce the application of useful checklists and more elaborate exercises such as reference-class forecasting and the premortem.","An organization is a factory that manufactures judgments and decisions and must ensure the quality of its products at framing, information collection, and review.","A richer language is essential to the skill of constructive criticism, because labels such as anchoring effects and narrow framing bring together what we know about a bias.","The operative concept for improving decisions is routine; constant quality control is an alternative to wholesale reviews after disasters.","Decision makers make better choices when they expect their decisions to be judged by how they were made, not only by how they turned out."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests why System 1 errors such as overconfidence, extreme predictions, and the planning fallacy are so hard to correct in oneself and why organizations, which think more slowly and can impose orderly procedures, are better positioned to avoid them. Kahneman's closing remedy is to recognize the minefield, slow down, recruit System 2, and let organizations institutionalize checklists, reference-class forecasting, and the premortem.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests why System 1 errors, overconfidence, extreme predictions, and the planning fallacy, are so hard to correct in oneself and why organizations, which think more slowly and can impose orderly procedures, are better positioned to avoid them. Kahneman's remedy in the closing chapter is to recognize the minefield, slow down, recruit System 2, and let organizations institutionalize checklists, reference-class forecasting, and the premortem.","stage_1_observation":"The Brightline construction board reviews a new transit tunnel. The project lead, Naomi, presents a single, detailed estimate: 18 months and $210 million, with high confidence. The board members nod along. On your screen you can pull up a summary of ten comparable tunnels across the country: an average of 34 months and $380 million. Naomi's plan has no reference class, no premortem, and no buffer line item. A junior analyst, Tomas, quietly slides a comparison chart across the table.","stage_1_question":"Naomi's team expresses high confidence in 18 months and $210 million. Which combination of System 1 processes explains why the plan is so far from the comparable projects?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The inside view and the planning fallacy: Naomi's team is anchored on its own constructed story, makes overconfident, non-regressive predictions, and ignores the reference class of comparable tunnels. Kahneman notes that his own intuitive thinking remains just as prone to overconfidence, extreme predictions, and the planning fallacy despite years of studying these issues.","stage_2_intervention":"The board is minutes from voting. You must choose the intervention most consistent with the chapter's argument about how organizations improve judgment.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Institute reference-class forecasting before approval and run a premortem: take the outside view using the distribution of the ten comparable tunnels, and imagine the project has failed a year from now and list the causes. Organizations are better than individuals because they think more slowly and can impose orderly procedures, and the premortem together with the outside view directly counters the inside view, overconfidence, and the planning fallacy."}]},{"id":"ch45","title":"Appendix A: Judgment Under Uncertainty","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Representativeness vs. Base-Rate Neglect vs. Availability","visual_frame_description":"A bright audit workstation in Meridian Insurance's analytics suite shows a sorting board with three bins labeled 'Representativeness,' 'Base-Rate Neglect,' and 'Availability.' Marina, a data analyst, is dragging evidence cards from the classic engineers-and-lawyers study into the bins, and a verdict stamp glows green when a card lands correctly.","player_mission":"Sort each evidence card into the bin naming the mechanism that drives the judgment it describes. Then read the engineers-and-lawyers study summary, identify which mechanism explains the headline result, and justify your diagnosis in the on-screen panel.","key_concepts":["The representativeness heuristic evaluates probability by the degree to which A resembles B, not by objective frequency.","People rank occupations for a person like Steve by similarity to stereotypes, in exactly the same order as by judged probability.","Prior probability (base rate) frequency does not affect similarity, so judging by representativeness leads to neglect of base rates.","Bayes' rule implies that, for each description, the odds of being an engineer rather than a lawyer should be 5.44 times higher in the 70-engineer condition than in the 30-engineer condition.","Subjects produced essentially identical engineer-or-lawyer judgments in the 70/30 and 30/70 conditions, sharply violating Bayes' rule.","When no personality description was given, subjects used the prior correctly, judging the probability of an engineer at .7 and .3 in the two conditions.","A worthless description such as Dick's, 'high ability and high motivation,' drove judgments to .5 regardless of the stated proportion of engineers.","People respond differently to no evidence, where priors are used, than to worthless evidence, where priors are ignored.","Availability, the ease with which instances come to mind, is a distinct mechanism not invoked in the engineers-and-lawyers task.","The biases described are not attributable to motivational effects such as wishful thinking or distortion by payoffs and penalties."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests base-rate neglect produced by the representativeness heuristic: once a description resembles a stereotype, people judge probability by similarity and abandon prior probabilities. Kahneman and Tversky showed that engineers-and-lawyers subjects produced the same judgments under opposite base rates, and even Dick's uninformative sketch reset answers to .5.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"The engineers-and-lawyers study found that subjects given base rates of 70/30 versus 30/70 produced essentially identical probability judgments once a personality sketch was shown. Which mechanism best explains this result?","column_a_label":"Representativeness heuristic","column_b_label":"Base-rate neglect","column_c_label":"Availability heuristic","rows":[{"label":"Steve, described as 'shy and withdrawn... a passion for detail,' judged likely to be a librarian","col":"a"},{"label":"Occupation rankings mirror similarity to stereotypes, not population frequencies","col":"a"},{"label":"Subjects given 70% engineers still judged a blank description at the prior probability","col":"b"},{"label":"Without any description, subjects used the base rates correctly (.7 and .3)","col":"b"},{"label":"Dick, 'high ability and high motivation,' judged .5 regardless of the stated base rate","col":"b"},{"label":"Judging the risk of a business venture by imagining the difficulties it could hit","col":"c"},{"label":"Word frequency judged by how easily words starting with a letter come to mind","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Representativeness, because the sketch resembles a stereotype, but the subjects used prior probabilities correctly when no sketch was shown, which isolates the base rate as the neglected factor.","Availability, but the study did not involve retrieval ease or memory search, so no availability effect is at work in this result.","Motivational bias, but subjects were not wishfully distorting; they were encouraged to be accurate, so a motivated error cannot explain the shift."],"explanation":"Subjects used prior probabilities (.7 and .3) correctly when no description existed, but ignored them the moment a description appeared, even an uninformative one like Dick's. The description activated stereotype similarity (representativeness), and because similarity does not reflect base rates, the prior probability was dropped. Availability plays no role because nothing about retrieval ease changes between the conditions."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Sample-Size Neglect, Misconception of Chance, and Regression","visual_frame_description":"A hospital operations classroom at St. Anne's Medical Center shows a whiteboard split into three zones labeled 'Insensitivity to Sample Size,' 'Misconception of Chance,' and 'Regression toward the Mean.' Dr. Okonkwo, a resident in evidence-based medicine, is sorting flashcards of classic statistical puzzles while a cohort of students watches.","player_mission":"Classify each statistical puzzle onto the whiteboard zone that names the mechanism producing the error. Then diagnose the hospital-births problem, pick the mechanism behind the 53-of-95 'about the same' verdict, and confirm your classification.","key_concepts":["People assess the probability of a sample result by its similarity to the population parameter, a similarity that does not depend on sample size.","Subjects assigned identical distributions for the average height of samples of 10, 100, and 1,000 men.","In the hospital problem, 53 of 95 undergraduates judged 60-percent-boys days equally likely in the large and small hospital.","Sampling theory implies the smaller hospital records many more such days, because a large sample is less likely to stray from 50%.","In the urn problem, correct posterior odds are 8 to 1 for a 4-red-1-white sample and 16 to 1 for a 12-red-8-white sample, assuming equal priors.","Most people judged the 4:1 sample (proportion .80) as stronger evidence than the 12:8 sample (proportion .60), reversing the statistical order.","Intuitive posterior odds are far less extreme than the correct values, a pattern labeled conservatism.","People expect a random process to be represented locally as well as globally, producing the belief in short-run alternation behind the gambler's fallacy.","Chance is commonly viewed as a self-correcting process, but deviations are not corrected; they are merely diluted.","Experienced research psychologists displayed a lingering 'law of small numbers,' overestimating the replicability of results from small samples."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests insensitivity to sample size: people judge the likelihood of a sample statistic by representativeness rather than by the sample's size, so they expect large and small samples to behave alike. Kahneman and Tversky showed that even trained researchers obey a 'law of small numbers,' trusting small samples more than statistics justify.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"A town has two hospitals: about 45 births a day and about 15 births a day. Over a year, which recorded more days with more than 60% boys? Fifty-three of 95 undergraduates answered 'about the same.' Which mechanism explains that verdict?","column_a_label":"Insensitivity to sample size","column_b_label":"Misconception of chance","column_c_label":"Regression toward the mean","rows":[{"label":"Expecting 60%-boys days to be equally likely in the 45-baby and 15-baby hospitals","col":"a"},{"label":"Judging the urn with 4 red and 1 white as stronger evidence than 12 red and 8 white","col":"a"},{"label":"Expecting H-T-H-T-T-H to be more likely than H-H-H-T-T-T","col":"b"},{"label":"Believing black is 'due' on the roulette wheel after a run of red","col":"b"},{"label":"Viewing chance as a self-correcting process that restores equilibrium","col":"b"},{"label":"Selecting the ten best scorers on one aptitude version and seeing them decline on the other","col":"c"},{"label":"Tall fathers having sons whose heights are closer to the population mean","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Misconception of chance, but this error stems from ignoring sample size, not from expecting local self-correction in a sequence.","Regression toward the mean, but no repeated-measures or retest comparison is involved in the hospital question.","Illusion of validity, but the hospital question involves no personality match and no prediction confidence."],"explanation":"The two events are described by the same statistic, so they look equally representative of the population. Sampling theory, however, says a large sample rarely strays from 50%, so the smaller hospital should record many more 60%-boys days. Ignoring sample size is the core mechanism; the gambler's fallacy concerns sequence self-correction and regression concerns retest shrinkage, neither of which operates here."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Availability: Retrieval, Imaginability, and Illusory Correlation","visual_frame_description":"A linguistics research lab in Portland displays three monitors labeled 'Retrievability,' 'Imaginability,' and 'Illusory Correlation.' Dr. Chen, a cognitive scientist, is sorting demonstration cards covering word lists, committee counts, and patient drawings, while a histogram of judged frequencies updates on the wall.","player_mission":"Sort each demonstration onto the monitor for the availability sub-mechanism it illustrates. Then diagnose the r-word frequency puzzle, identify the mechanism behind it, and justify why the other two monitors do not apply.","key_concepts":["Availability estimates the frequency of a class by the ease with which instances can be brought to mind.","When a list mixes famous men and famous women, subjects judge the sex with the more famous members as the more numerous, regardless of true counts.","Salience and recency inflate retrievability: seeing a house burn outweighs reading about a fire, and a freshly seen overturned car raises the subjective probability of accidents.","Because words are easier to search by first letter than by third letter, people think words beginning with r outnumber words with r in the third position.","Abstract words are judged more frequent and more varied in context than equally frequent concrete words, because contexts for them are easier to imagine.","When instances are constructed by rule, ease of construction substitutes for frequency: committees of 2 members (median estimate 70) seemed more numerous than committees of 8 (median 20), though the true count is 45 in both cases.","The risk of an adventurous expedition is evaluated by imagining contingencies it cannot handle, and vivid imagined disasters need not reflect actual likelihood.","Chapman and Chapman found that naive judges overestimated the co-occurrence of natural associates such as suspiciousness and peculiar eyes, an effect they labeled illusory correlation.","The illusory correlation effect persisted even when the true correlation was negative and prevented judges from detecting relationships that were in fact present.","Availability explains illusory correlation: a strong associative bond makes paired events seem to have occurred together frequently."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the availability heuristic, in which the frequency of a class is estimated by the ease of retrieving, searching for, or constructing instances. Kahneman and Tversky showed that ease of retrieval, not true frequency, drives estimates, producing biases in word-frequency, committee-counting, and illusory-correlation judgments.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"People were asked whether words sampled from an English text are more likely to start with r or to have r as the third letter. Most judged the first-letter case more common, even though consonants such as r are more frequent in the third position. Which mechanism produces this error?","column_a_label":"Availability: retrievability and search set","column_b_label":"Availability: imaginability","column_c_label":"Illusory correlation","rows":[{"label":"Judging that famous male names outnumber famous female names in a list of equal size","col":"a"},{"label":"Traffic-accident probability rising after seeing an overturned car by the road","col":"a"},{"label":"Abstract words judged more frequent than equally frequent concrete words","col":"a"},{"label":"Judging committees of 2 members more numerous than committees of 8 from the same group of 10","col":"b"},{"label":"An expedition made to appear exceedingly dangerous by vividly imagined contingencies","col":"b"},{"label":"Judges overestimating how often suspiciousness accompanies peculiar eyes in patients' drawings","col":"c"},{"label":"Strong associative bonds being read as frequent co-occurrence between two events","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Illusory correlation, but no diagnosis-drawing pairing or co-occurrence frequency is being judged here.","Imaginability, but the r-word task involves retrieving stored words, not constructing novel instances from a rule.","Insensitivity to sample size, but nothing about sample size or sampling variability is at stake in the word-frequency judgment."],"explanation":"Words are searched by their first letter far more easily than by their third letter, so instances of the first-letter class are easier to retrieve. Because frequency is judged by ease of retrieval, the easier-to-search class is judged more numerous. The task uses stored examples rather than constructed ones, so imaginability is not the operative mechanism, and no pair of events is being judged for co-occurrence."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Anchoring, Conjunctive-Disjunctive Bias, and Calibration","visual_frame_description":"A decision-analysis seminar room at Rift University shows a spinning wheel of fortune on the projector and three pillars labeled 'Anchoring,' 'Compound-Event Bias,' and 'Calibration.' Professor Weiss, a behavioral economist, is pinning result cards for UN percentages, multiplication products, and confidence intervals onto the pillars.","player_mission":"Pin each experimental result onto the pillar that names the mechanism it demonstrates. Then diagnose the wheel-of-fortune finding on African countries in the UN, select the governing mechanism, and defend the choice against the distractors.","key_concepts":["In many estimation tasks people start from an initial value and adjust, and adjustments are typically insufficient, a phenomenon called anchoring.","Median estimates of the percentage of African countries in the UN were 25 for anchor 10 and 45 for anchor 65.","Payoffs for accuracy did not reduce the anchoring effect.","High school students estimated the product 8x7x6x5x4x3x2x1 at a median of 2,250 and 1x2x3x4x5x6x7x8 at 512, versus the correct value of 40,320.","Bar-Hillel's subjects preferred betting on a conjunctive event (probability .48) over a simple .50 event, and preferred the simple event over a disjunctive .52 event.","People tend to overestimate the probability of conjunctive events and underestimate the probability of disjunctive events.","The success of a plan has a conjunctive chain structure, producing unwarranted optimism about completing a project on time.","A complex system such as a nuclear reactor fails if any component fails, a disjunctive structure whose overall failure probability is underestimated.","Decision analysts elicit beliefs by asking judges to select percentile values, and judges produce overly narrow confidence intervals.","Proper scoring rules that reward external calibration did not eliminate the overconfidence bias."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests anchoring with insufficient adjustment: estimates start from a given or partially computed value and remain biased toward it. Kahneman and Tversky showed the same insufficient adjustment produces overestimation of conjunctive events, underestimation of disjunctive events, and confidence intervals that are too narrow.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In a demonstration, subjects watched a wheel of fortune spin a number between 0 and 100, then estimated the percentage of African countries in the UN. Median estimates were 25 for a starting value of 10 and 45 for a starting value of 65. Which mechanism best explains the shift?","column_a_label":"Anchoring with insufficient adjustment","column_b_label":"Conjunctive-disjunctive evaluation","column_c_label":"Calibration","rows":[{"label":"Median estimate of 25 for anchor 10 and 45 for anchor 65 on African countries in the UN","col":"a"},{"label":"Payoffs for accuracy failing to reduce the anchoring effect","col":"a"},{"label":"The descending product 8x7x...x1 judged at 2,250 versus 512 for the ascending order (correct 40,320)","col":"a"},{"label":"Preferring a bet on drawing a red marble seven times (.48) over a simple .50 chance","col":"b"},{"label":"Planning that overestimates the likelihood a project will finish on time","col":"b"},{"label":"Underestimating the probability of failure in a many-component system","col":"b"},{"label":"True values falling outside the stated X01-X99 interval on about 30% of problems","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Conjunctive-disjunctive bias, but the UN question involves a single quantity, not a compound event.","Calibration, but the anchor shift changes point estimates, not the width of a confidence interval.","Availability, but the wheel number is not retrieved or searched for; it is a starting point supplied by the problem."],"explanation":"Estimates are formed by starting from the given number and adjusting, but adjustment is typically insufficient, so answers remain biased toward the starting value. The same mechanism explains why the descending product was judged larger than the ascending one. Calibration concerns the width of probability distributions, and conjunctive-disjunctive bias concerns compound events, neither of which the UN question involves."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Predictability Neglect, Illusion of Validity, and Non-Motivational Bias","visual_frame_description":"A psychometrics office at an education school displays a whiteboard with three columns labeled 'Insensitivity to Predictability,' 'Illusion of Validity,' and 'Motivational Distortion.' Dr. Herrera, a measurement specialist, is sorting findings from the student-teacher prediction study into the columns while a chart of percentile judgments updates beside her.","player_mission":"Sort each study finding into the correct column. Then diagnose the finding that five-year predictions equaled same-day evaluations, choose the governing mechanism, and explain why a motivational explanation fails.","key_concepts":["Predictions are often made by representativeness: a very favorable description produces a very favorable predicted profit or grade.","The favorableness of a description is unaffected by its reliability, so representative predictions are insensitive to the expected accuracy of prediction.","Normative statistical theory holds that when predictability is nil, the same prediction (such as the average) should be made in all cases.","Student-teacher predictions of success after five years were identical to evaluations of the single practice lesson.","Confidence in a prediction depends mainly on the quality of the match between outcome and input, the basis of the illusion of validity.","The illusion of validity persists even when the judge knows the factors that limit accuracy, as with repeatedly discredited selection interviews.","More confidence is placed in a consistent all-B first-year record than in a mixed A-and-C record.","Given input variables of stated validity, a prediction based on independent inputs achieves higher accuracy than one based on redundant, correlated inputs.","Redundancy among inputs decreases accuracy even as it increases confidence.","The severe judgment errors reported occurred despite subjects being encouraged to be accurate and rewarded for correct answers."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests insensitivity to predictability and the illusion of validity: people predict by choosing the outcome most representative of the input and feel confident when the fit is good. Kahneman and Tversky showed such predictions ignore reliability, that confidence tracks internal consistency rather than accuracy, and that the errors are not motivational.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"In a study, one group evaluated the quality of a practice lesson in percentile scores; another group predicted, in percentile scores, the standing of the same student teachers five years later. The two sets of judgments were identical. Which mechanism explains this?","column_a_label":"Insensitivity to predictability","column_b_label":"Illusion of validity","column_c_label":"Motivational distortion","rows":[{"label":"Predictions of a student teacher's standing after 5 years were identical to evaluations of the practice lesson","col":"a"},{"label":"Predictions that ignore the reliability of the evidence and the expected accuracy of prediction","col":"a"},{"label":"Confidence in a librarian prediction that comes from the fit between description and stereotype","col":"b"},{"label":"Selection interviews still trusted despite repeated demonstrations of their fallibility","col":"b"},{"label":"A consistent all-B record producing more confidence than a mixed A-and-C record","col":"b"},{"label":"Redundant, correlated inputs raising confidence while lowering predictive accuracy","col":"b"},{"label":"Severe judgment errors persisting even when subjects were rewarded for accuracy","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Illusion of validity, but that explains confidence in a prediction, not why a distant prediction equals an immediate evaluation.","Anchoring, but the two tasks were administered to separate groups with no shared external starting value.","Regression neglect, but the study concerns the range of predictions, not the retest shrinkage of extreme scores."],"explanation":"Predictions are made by representativeness: the outcome most representative of the description is produced, with no adjustment for how much the evidence actually predicts. Because predictability is not considered, the five-year prediction was as extreme as the immediate evaluation. Confidence driven by fit rather than validity is a separate illusion, and the errors persisted despite accuracy incentives, ruling out motivation."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Anchoring in Procurement Estimates","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled audit room at Brightline Construction shows Maya, a procurement lead, negotiating with a steel vendor whose opening offer of $180 per ton is displayed on a large screen. On a side table sits her notepad, where the figure 180 is circled at the top, and an auditor's dashboard tracks her estimate against historical averages.","player_mission":"Review Maya's estimate, her body language, and the interview transcript. Determine which judgment bias corrupted her $172-per-ton forecast, record the correct diagnosis, and explain the evidence trail to the audit committee.","key_concepts":["People estimate quantities by starting from an initial value and adjusting, and adjustments are typically insufficient.","The initial value may be suggested by the formulation of the problem or by a partial computation.","Arbitrary starting numbers had marked effects on estimates, such as medians of 25 and 45 for anchors of 10 and 65.","Payoffs for accuracy do not reduce the anchoring effect.","Anchoring operates even when the starting point is the result of an incomplete computation, as in the 8x7x...x1 versus 1x2x...x8 study.","A completely uninformative starting value can nonetheless pull a final estimate toward itself.","Being aware of an anchor does not reliably eliminate its influence on judgment.","An auditor can test for anchoring by asking whether the estimate would change with a different starting value.","Insufficient adjustment from an anchor produces estimates that cluster near the starting point.","Insufficient adjustment is demonstrated when the same quantity is estimated differently under different starting values."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests anchoring with insufficient adjustment: once a number enters an estimate, final values stay biased toward it. Kahneman and Tversky demonstrated the effect across percent-estimation and arithmetic-extrapolation tasks, and showed that payoffs for accuracy do not weaken it.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the bias behind Maya's cost estimate: what is driving her figure, and should the audit flag her numbers for adjustment?","subject_baseline":"Maya, 34, is a procurement lead at Brightline Construction who normally estimates steel and concrete costs within 5% of tender. Her colleagues describe her as careful and numbers-driven, and her past forecasts for framing materials have been her best in the department.","observed_nonverbal_log":["10:03 - The vendor opens with 'Most buyers are signing around $180 per ton these days.' Maya writes '$180' at the top of her notepad before any calculation.","10:11 - As she compares three quotes, Maya's cursor hovers longest over the quote near the $180 figure; she taps her pen and re-reads it twice.","10:19 - Asked to justify a lean $172 estimate, Maya's face flushes slightly and she glances at the $180 figure on her pad before speaking.","10:26 - After the meeting, Maya tells a teammate, 'Whatever I do, I keep landing back near 180; that number just feels right.'","10:31 - She never opens the supplier index data or the prior quarter's average price, despite having both available in another tab."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: How did you arrive at $172 per ton?","Maya: The supplier opened at 180, so I walked down from there. I know I should account for the last tender, but 180 is where my head is at.","Auditor: Did you review the historical average price in your analysis?","Maya: I have it in another tab, but honestly I did not open it. I kept adjusting off 180.","Auditor: Would your estimate change if the opening figure had been $210?","Maya: Probably. I would start from 210 and come down. Yeah, it would."],"correct_diagnosis":"Anchoring with insufficient adjustment: Maya anchored on the vendor's opening $180 figure and adjusted too little, so her estimate remained biased toward the anchor despite ample data for a fuller analysis.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Maya wrote the vendor's opening figure before computing, kept returning to it during comparison (prolonged hover, re-reading), and admitted 'I kept adjusting off 180' in the transcript. Her admission that a $210 opening would have changed her estimate confirms that the starting value, not the data, drove the answer. The research analog is the wheel-of-fortune study, where medians were 25 and 45 for anchors 10 and 65, and accuracy incentives did not remove the effect.","distractors":["Availability, because Maya is not estimating a frequency or class size from recalled instances; her error is a numeric starting point.","Representativeness, because no stereotype or category resemblance is involved in her cost forecast.","Illusion of validity, because Maya shows no special confidence from a good fit; she is simply biased toward a number."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Representativeness and Base-Rate Neglect in Hiring","visual_frame_description":"A startup hiring suite at Helix Robotics shows Andre, the engineering manager, flipping through resumes on a tablet. One candidate's file featuring chess championships and rocketry is enlarged on the wall display, while an unopened coding-sample tab blinks in the corner and an HR audit panel tracks his rubric scores.","player_mission":"Study Andre's screening behavior, the nonverbal log, and his interview answers. Diagnose the bias driving his 'fast yes' on Elias, then determine whether his reasoning ignores the applicant base rate and write up the audit.","key_concepts":["The representativeness heuristic evaluates the probability that A belongs to class B by how much A resembles B.","Occupations are ranked by probability and by similarity in exactly the same way, as in the Steve-librarian problem.","Prior probabilities have no effect on similarity and are therefore neglected under representativeness.","Bayes' rule implies the odds of being an engineer should be 5.44 times higher in the 70-engineer condition than in the 30-engineer condition.","Subjects given a personality sketch produced the same judgments in the 70/30 and 30/70 conditions.","When no evidence was given, subjects used base rates correctly, answering .7 and .3.","An uninformative description like Dick's drove judgments to .5 regardless of the stated base rate.","Confidence based on a good fit between outcome and input is called the illusion of validity.","Selection interviews are routinely relied on despite repeated demonstrations of their fallibility.","Even people with extensive statistical training make intuitive representativeness errors in less transparent problems."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests representativeness-driven base-rate neglect: people judge probability by resemblance to a stereotype and abandon base rates. Kahneman and Tversky's engineers-and-lawyers study showed opposite base rates yielded identical judgments once a description appeared, and the illusion of validity keeps confidence high despite the low predictive value of fit.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the bias driving Andre's hiring decision and decide whether his fit-based reasoning is sound.","subject_baseline":"Andre, 41, is the senior engineering manager at a robotics startup. He reviews dozens of resumes a month and is normally methodical, using a scored rubric. His prior hires have above-average retention, and HR audits show his rubric scores track performance well.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:48 - Andre flips past two strong applicants in under a minute each because their resumes do not 'feel like engineer material.'","09:57 - He stops on a third applicant, Elias, whose resume lists chess championships and model rocketry, and smiles, muttering 'this is what I am looking for.'","10:02 - Andre leans back and tells a colleague 'Elias is clearly the engineer type; you can just tell.'","10:12 - He does not open the portfolio link or the coding-sample repository, even though both load instantly.","10:18 - Asked about the ratio of strong to weak candidates in the pool, Andre shrugs and says 'that does not matter, I am picking the person, not the population.'","10:25 - He writes 'fast yes' on Elias's file and moves on without scoring the rubric."],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: What made Elias your top pick?","Andre: The chess and the rocketry. He is the engineer archetype, exactly the person I picture for this team.","Interviewer: You reviewed his coding sample?","Andre: I did not need to. When the fit is that obvious, the details are just noise.","Interviewer: What share of applicants who look like this turn out to be strong hires?","Andre: I do not know, but look at him. He is clearly cut from the right cloth.","Interviewer: If only 5% of engineer-stereotyped candidates are actually strong, would that change your call?","Andre: I would still take him. You cannot put a number on instinct like that."],"correct_diagnosis":"Representativeness with neglect of prior probability: Andre judged Elias by how well the resume matched the engineer stereotype and dismissed base rates about how often stereotype-fitting candidates actually succeed.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Andre announced the candidate 'is the engineer archetype' from chess and rocketry, skipped the objective coding sample, dismissed pool composition ('that does not matter'), and refused to revise his call when told stereotype-fitting candidates rarely succeed. This mirrors the engineers-and-lawyers result: a description that resembles a stereotype overrides base rates, and even worthless evidence such as Dick's sketch resets judgments to .5. His behavior also shows the illusion of validity, because confidence comes from fit rather than from predictive accuracy.","distractors":["Anchoring, because no initial numeric value anchors Andre's judgment; the driver is category resemblance.","Availability, because Andre is not estimating a frequency from the ease of recalling instances.","Confirmation bias, which is plausible but secondary; the primary mechanism is resemblance-driven probability judgment with prior neglect, which is the study's explicit finding."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Availability and Risk Perception","visual_frame_description":"A risk command center at Harbor Freight Lines shows Priya at her desk. On one monitor the monthly incident table lists causes of injury with crash fatality counts; on another a graphic truck-crash video is paused with its play icon glowing. A budget-allocation chart tilts heavily toward crash-response gear.","player_mission":"Audit Priya's budget decision using the incident table, her observed behavior, and the interview transcript. Diagnose the bias that shifted 60% of safety funds, and state whether the allocation matches the frequency data.","key_concepts":["Availability estimates the frequency of a class or event by the ease with which instances come to mind.","Instances of large classes are usually recalled better and faster, which makes availability a useful but biased clue.","A class whose instances are easily retrieved appears more numerous than an equal class that is harder to retrieve.","Salience affects retrievability: seeing a house burn has more impact on judged fire risk than reading about a fire.","Recent occurrences are more available: subjective accident probability rises temporarily after seeing an overturned car.","A dramatic clip can displace frequency data because its image is easy to bring to mind.","Different search sets produce different availability estimates, as with abstract versus concrete words.","Ease of imagining contingencies substitutes for their actual likelihood in risk evaluation.","Vivid imagined disasters can make an undertaking appear exceedingly dangerous regardless of true probability.","The reliance on availability is economical and usually effective but leads to systematic and predictable errors."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the availability heuristic: risk is judged by the ease with which vivid, recent instances come to mind rather than by their true frequency. Kahneman and Tversky showed that salience and recency inflate judged probabilities, as when a freshly witnessed overturned car temporarily raises the subjective risk of traffic accidents.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the bias driving Priya's risk ranking and decide whether her reallocation of the safety budget is justified by the data.","subject_baseline":"Priya, 38, is the head of operations risk at Harbor Freight Lines, a trucking firm. She reviews incident statistics monthly and has a record of evidence-based resource allocation. Her own father is a truck driver, and she drives the interstate to work daily.","observed_nonverbal_log":["14:05 - Priya opens the monthly incident report; the table lists injuries by cause, with highway crashes first and slips and falls last.","14:09 - She pauses the report and searches 'truck crash' videos, watching one graphic clip twice; her jaw tightens and she taps the screen.","14:16 - She tells a teammate 'I drive that highway every day; I swear the risk has doubled since last year.'","14:22 - When a colleague notes crashes per mile are flat, Priya frowns and changes the subject, reopening the video.","14:30 - She drafts a memo shifting 60% of the safety budget toward crash-response gear and cutting slip-and-fall training, which the stats show causes more injuries per mile of road.","14:41 - Asked for the data behind the shift, she replies 'the numbers are in the video; you can see it yourself.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: What drives the new budget split toward crash gear?","Priya: I see overturned trucks on my commute, and that video. That is the real risk.","Auditor: The incident table says slips and falls cause more injuries. Did you weigh that?","Priya: Nobody dies from a wet floor in a way you can picture. The crash image is what sticks with you.","Auditor: So the decision rests on how easily you can picture the danger?","Priya: It rests on the danger being real. I have seen it with my own eyes."],"correct_diagnosis":"Availability bias: Priya judged risk by the ease with which dramatic, salient instances came to mind, overriding the frequency data that showed a different hazard was more common.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Priya's own commentary is direct evidence: 'the crash image is what sticks with you' and 'I have seen it with my own eyes.' Her subjective probability rose after watching a vivid clip and after witnessing an overturned truck, exactly the mechanism the chapter describes in which recent, salient, and easily retrieved instances inflate judged frequency. She explicitly rejected the incident table ('nobody dies from a wet floor in a way you can picture'), confirming that retrievability, not frequency, drove the ranking. The chapter's examples include the temporary rise in subjective accident probability after seeing an overturned car.","distractors":["Representativeness, because Priya is not matching an event to a category stereotype; she is judging frequency from recall.","Anchoring, because no starting numeric value anchors her estimate.","Illusory correlation, because she is not overestimating the co-occurrence of two associated events."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Misconceptions of Regression and Feedback","visual_frame_description":"A flight-training review room at Ridgefield Aviation shows Marcus's desk covered with landing-grade logs, a highlighter resting beside a circled streak of excellent landings followed by declines. A second screen displays the week's grade distribution with the mean line, and an auditor's checklist floats beside the policy memo Marcus is drafting.","player_mission":"Review Marcus's before-and-after pattern, his logs, and the interview. Diagnose the statistical flaw in his conclusion that punishment outperforms reward, and decide what a proper control comparison would show.","key_concepts":["Regression toward the mean was first documented by Galton more than a century ago.","Among the ten children who did best on one aptitude test version, performance on the second version is usually somewhat disappointing.","If individuals deviate from the mean of X by k units, the average of their Y scores usually deviates by less than k units.","Regression occurs in many contexts: fathers and sons, husbands and wives, and performance on consecutive examinations.","People do not expect regression where it is bound to occur, and they invent spurious causal explanations when they see it.","Praise for an exceptionally smooth landing is typically followed by a poorer landing, and harsh criticism after a rough landing by an improvement.","Flight instructors concluded that rewards are detrimental to learning and punishments beneficial, contrary to accepted psychological doctrine.","Regression alone predicts improvement after poor performance and deterioration after outstanding performance, even without any feedback.","Because rewards follow good performance and punishments follow poor performance, behavior is most likely to improve after punishment and to deteriorate after reward.","By chance alone, one is most often rewarded for punishing others and most often punished for rewarding them."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests misconceptions of regression: because rewards follow good performance and punishments follow poor performance, behavior naturally improves after punishment and worsens after reward by regression alone. Kahneman and Tversky showed that experienced flight instructors misread this as evidence that punishment works, an error with real costs for how we train and manage.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Diagnose the flaw in Marcus's coaching strategy and decide whether the praise-and-punishment pattern he observed is evidence of causality.","subject_baseline":"Marcus, 45, is a flight-training instructor at Ridgefield Aviation with 20 years of experience. He is a respected, safety-obsessed teacher who logs every student flight and reviews performance data weekly. He believes strongly in direct, corrective feedback.","observed_nonverbal_log":["09:31 - Marcus reviews the week's landing grades and circles a string of excellent landings, then marks the following flights that scored lower.","09:40 - He highlights several rough landings that were followed by notably better attempts, nodding as he connects the dots.","09:48 - He tells a colleague 'It is consistent: praise the good ones and they slip; chew them out and they tighten up.'","09:55 - When a colleague suggests the pattern might just be luck, Marcus shakes his head and taps the logged grades.","10:05 - He drafts a policy memo recommending harsher criticism of poor landings and curtailing praise for smooth ones.","10:18 - He never compares grades against an untreated control group or checks the average performance across all flights."],"interrogation_transcript":["Auditor: What led you to cut praise and add criticism?","Marcus: The logs. Every good landing I praised was followed by a worse one, and every rough landing I corrected was followed by improvement. That is data.","Auditor: Could the improvement after criticism be a coincidence?","Marcus: Twenty years of it? No. Reward softens people; punishment sharpens them.","Auditor: Did you compare against students who got no feedback at all?","Marcus: Did not need to. The before-and-after pattern is right there."],"correct_diagnosis":"Misconceptions of regression: Marcus misattributed regression toward the mean to the causal power of punishment versus reward.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Marcus praised outstanding landings and criticized poor ones, so praise was typically followed by deterioration and criticism by improvement, which is exactly what regression predicts with no feedback at all. Extremely smooth landings are followed on average by somewhat poorer ones and rough landings by somewhat better ones, purely because performance fluctuates around a mean. The chapter's flight-training example is identical: instructors concluded rewards harm learning and punishments help, contrary to accepted psychological doctrine, because they missed the regression. Marcus's refusal to test a no-feedback control confirms the confound.","distractors":["Anchoring, because Marcus's estimates are not pulled toward an initial value; the error is causal misattribution.","Availability, because he is not judging a frequency from the ease of recall.","Illusion of validity, because his confidence comes from a mistaken causal reading, not from a stereotype fit."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Stereotype Sorter (Representativeness and Base Rates)","visual_frame_description":"A consultant's war room at Terra Analytics shows a split-screen study viewer: the 70-engineer and 30-engineer conditions side by side, with judgment sliders parked at identical probabilities. A recruiting-team leader in the corner insists the sketches make base rates irrelevant, while a Bayes' rule calculator updates on the center display.","player_mission":"Defeat the Stereotype Sorter by keeping base rates alive. First explain what identical scoring of neutral sketches reveals, then set the correct answer key for a new worthless description so the study reruns with proper calibration.","key_concepts":["The representativeness heuristic judges the probability that A belongs to class B by how much A resembles B.","Prior probability frequency has no effect on representativeness and is therefore neglected.","Bayes' rule implies the odds of being an engineer should be 5.44 times higher in the 70-engineer condition than in the 30-engineer condition.","Subjects in the two base-rate conditions produced essentially identical probability judgments.","When no description was provided, subjects used the priors correctly, answering .7 and .3.","An uninformative description like Dick's drove judgments to .5 in both conditions.","People respond differently to no evidence than to worthless evidence.","Even statistically sophisticated individuals make representativeness errors in less transparent problems.","The illusion of validity keeps confidence high when the fit is good, even if the description is scanty.","These errors occur despite incentives to be accurate, so they are not motivational."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the representativeness heuristic and base-rate neglect: probability is judged by resemblance to a stereotype, so prior probabilities drop out of the calculation. Kahneman and Tversky's engineers-and-lawyers study showed opposite base rates (70/30 versus 30/70) produced identical judgments, with even worthless descriptions resetting answers to .5.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central test is the representativeness heuristic and its partner, base-rate neglect: whether the player can keep prior probabilities in play when a personality description matches a stereotype. The engineers-and-lawyers study is the ground truth.","stage_1_observation":"You are a consulting statistician called into Terra Analytics, where the recruiting team is replaying the classic study: 100 engineer-and-lawyer resumes, 70 engineers in one condition and 70 lawyers in another. Each resume gets a personality sketch, and analysts are scoring the probability that the person is an engineer. The screen splits: the 70/30 and 30/70 conditions side by side, with judgment sliders showing identical probabilities for the same sketch in both conditions.","stage_1_question":"In the 70-engineer and 30-engineer conditions, identical personality sketches are being scored the same (around .5 for a neutral description). What does this identical scoring reveal about the analysts' use of base rates?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It reveals base-rate neglect: the description overrode the prior probability, so the 5.44-to-1 ratio of the odds implied by Bayes' rule between conditions was ignored. The same sketch produced identical judgments even though the base rates between the conditions differed by a factor of more than five.","stage_2_intervention":"Now the boss escalates: 'Fine, so we calibrate the sliders to the base rates.' Your team must set the correct answer key for a brand-new neutral description ('Dick is a 30-year-old man, married, with high ability and high motivation') before the study reruns. Choose the probability that Dick is an engineer in each condition, given that the description carries no diagnostic information.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Set the probability to the base rate: .7 in the 70-engineer condition and .3 in the 30-engineer condition. Because the description is worthless evidence, the rational answer is the prior, exactly what Kahneman and Tversky found subjects abandoned when they answered .5 in both conditions."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Vividness Demon (Availability and Risk)","visual_frame_description":"The Ashford municipal council chamber glows with a dual display: a news wall replaying a week of graphic factory-explosion coverage and a 20-year incident table showing flat, low counts. Council members point at bars showing spiked judged risk while a facility-safety engineer waits with the frequency breakdown.","player_mission":"Advise the council by defeating the Vividness Demon. Identify the mechanism behind the spiked risk perception and its driving cue, then select the budget decision that best reflects the frequency data rather than the vivid coverage.","key_concepts":["Availability estimates the frequency of a class or the probability of an event by the ease of bringing instances to mind.","Instances of large classes are usually recalled better and faster, making availability useful but fallible.","Salience affects retrievability: seeing a house burn has more impact on judged fire risk than reading about a fire.","Recent occurrences are more available than earlier ones, as with the temporary spike after seeing an overturned car.","The ease with which instances are retrieved need not reflect their actual frequency.","Vivid imagined contingencies can make an undertaking appear exceedingly dangerous without changing its real likelihood.","A class whose instances are easily retrieved appears more numerous than an equal class that is harder to retrieve.","Abstract words are judged more frequent than concrete words of equal frequency due to search-set ease.","The availability heuristic is highly economical and usually effective but leads to systematic, predictable errors.","A risk assessed by imaginability may be grossly underestimated when possible dangers are difficult to conceive."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests the availability heuristic: judged frequency follows the ease with which vivid, recent, salient instances come to mind. Kahneman and Tversky showed such retrievability biases, from the overturned car to imagined expedition dangers, produce systematic misperceptions of risk.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central test is the availability heuristic: whether the player can resist letting vivid, easily retrieved instances inflate the judged frequency of an event. The chapter's demonstrations, from famous-name lists to the overturned car and imagined expedition contingencies, are the ground truth.","stage_1_observation":"You are a member of the municipal risk council in Ashford. The media have run a week of graphic stories about a single rare factory explosion, and residents now demand an emergency ordinance. The council's risk board displays two bars: the judged frequency of industrial explosions, which is sky-high and driven by the news cycle, versus the 20-year incident table showing stable low counts. Your job is to advise the council on where the perception came from.","stage_1_question":"Judged risk of factory explosions spiked after the news coverage, while the incident table shows the frequency is unchanged. Which mechanism best explains the spike, and what specific cue is driving it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The availability heuristic: probability is being judged by the ease with which vivid instances come to mind. Salience and recency, driven by graphic coverage and the memorable single event, inflate retrievability, exactly as the chapter shows a freshly seen overturned car temporarily raises judged accident probability.","stage_2_intervention":"The council must vote on where to allocate the safety budget. An activist argues for banning all pressure-vessel plants based on the vivid explosion; an engineer presents the frequency data showing slips and falls in the facility cause far more injuries. Pick the decision that best counteracts the availability bias while respecting the data.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Allocate the budget according to the incident-frequency table rather than the vivid single event, and explicitly counter the coverage with base-rate data. The chapter shows availability is a useful clue but leads to systematic errors when salience and recency displace true frequency."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Conjunction Optimist (Anchoring and Compound Events)","visual_frame_description":"A program-review war room at Northwind Tech shows Daniela's Helios slide deck with a chain of twelve green 95% links and a glowing '95% overall success' banner. Across the table, Rajiv's screen displays the data center's 200-pump cooling network with red warning nodes. Two competing funding requests sit on the desk.","player_mission":"Defeat the Conjunction Optimist by correcting both compound-event estimates. First judge whether the 95% overall-success claim for the twelve-link project is biased and in which direction, then decide the correct reading of the cooling system's shutdown risk and the better funding choice.","key_concepts":["A conjunctive event requires all of a series of elementary events to occur, giving it a chain-like structure.","A disjunctive event occurs if any one of a set of elementary events fails, giving it a funnel-like structure.","The probability of a conjunctive event is lower than the probability of each of its elementary events.","The probability of a disjunctive event is higher than the probability of each of its elementary events.","Bar-Hillel's subjects preferred a conjunctive bet (probability .48) over a simple .50 event, and the simple event over a disjunctive .52 event.","People tend to overestimate conjunctive probabilities and underestimate disjunctive probabilities.","Anchoring explains both: the stated probability of the elementary event is the starting point, and adjustment is insufficient.","Planning has a conjunctive character, so the tendency produces unwarranted optimism about completing a project on time.","A complex system malfunctions if any essential component fails, so overall failure probability is underestimated.","The direction of the anchoring bias can be inferred from the structure of the event: chains lead to overestimation, funnels to underestimation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This HIGH-STAKES STRESS SIMULATION tests how anchoring biases compound events: people anchor on the elementary probability and adjust insufficiently, overestimating conjunctive success and underestimating disjunctive failure. Kahneman and Tversky cited Bar-Hillel's betting study and showed this drives unwarranted optimism in planning and misestimation of complex-system risk.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central test is how anchoring biases the evaluation of conjunctive and disjunctive events: chain-like conjunctions get overestimated and funnel-like disjunctions get underestimated, because people anchor on the probability of each elementary event and adjust insufficiently.","stage_1_observation":"You are a program-review advisor at Northwind Tech. The CEO, Daniela, is championing Project Helios: a hardware launch that requires twelve sequential milestones, each with a 95% chance of success. The slideshow shows a glowing chain of twelve green links, each labeled 95%, with an overall success banner at 95%. Separately, the CTO, Rajiv, is arguing about the data center's cooling system, a network where any one of 200 pumps failing can trigger shutdown, with each pump having a 0.5% annual failure rate.","stage_1_question":"For Project Helios, each of the twelve milestones has a 95% chance of success. Daniela's slides claim the overall probability of full success is about 95%. Is that estimate biased, and in which direction?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It is biased upward. Success is conjunctive: all twelve links must hold, so the true probability is 0.95 to the twelfth power, roughly 0.54, far below 95%. Because people anchor on the probability of each elementary event and adjust insufficiently, conjunctive events are systematically overestimated, which produces unwarranted optimism about plans.","stage_2_intervention":"Now resolve the cooling-system debate. Rajiv proposes a full redundant-pump overhaul because he estimates the chance of any single pump failure as tiny; the CEO, overoptimistic about Helios, wants to move the money to marketing. Given the disjunctive structure (any one of 200 pumps failing causes shutdown), choose the correct reading of the shutdown risk and the better decision.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recognize that the system is disjunctive: with 200 pumps at a 0.5% failure rate each, the probability that at least one fails is roughly 1 minus 0.995 to the 200th power, about 63% per year, much higher than intuition suggests. Because disjunctive events are underestimated via anchoring, the prudent decision is to fund the redundancy rather than the marketing push."}]},{"id":"ch46","title":"Appendix B: Choices, Values, and Frames","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The S-Shaped Value Function","visual_frame_description":"A financial-advising office on a Friday afternoon. Advisors Nina and Diego sit across from a tablet showing Figure 1, an S-shaped curve that passes through the origin and is steep in the negative quadrant. On the desk are stacks of client folders, each tagged with a behavior: 'sure $800 chosen,' 'gamble on loss chosen,' 'refuses coin bet.' A whiteboard behind them is split into three columns labeled with properties of the value function.","player_mission":"Read each client folder and classify the behavior it describes into one of three properties of the value function: concave in gains, convex in losses, or steeper for losses than for gains. Assign all six folders to the correct column before the advising session ends.","key_concepts":["Subjective value is a concave function of the size of a gain: the difference in value between $200 and $100 exceeds the difference between $1,200 and $1,100.","The same concavity applies to losses, so the difference in subjective value between a loss of $200 and a loss of $100 appears greater than between a loss of $1,200 and a loss of $1,100.","The value function is defined on gains and losses relative to a reference point rather than on total wealth.","The value function is concave in the domain of gains and convex in the domain of losses, producing an S-shaped curve.","The value function is considerably steeper for losses than for gains, a property labeled loss aversion.","Loss aversion expresses the intuition that a loss of $X is more aversive than a gain of $X is attractive.","Loss aversion explains the reluctance to bet on a fair coin for equal stakes, because the possible gain does not compensate for the possible loss.","Most undergraduates in the chapter's sample refused to stake $10 on the toss of a coin unless they could win at least $30.","Concavity of value in gains entails risk aversion for favorable gambles with equal expectation.","Convexity of value in losses entails risk seeking when losses are substantial.","The S-shaped value function has implications that are normatively unacceptable, as shown by violations of invariance and dominance.","These properties are part of prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky 1979)."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the shape of the prospect-theory value function: concave for gains, convex for losses, and steeper for losses than for gains. Kahneman and Tversky derive risk aversion in gains and risk seeking in losses directly from this S-shaped psychophysical function.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Nina and Diego must sort six client behaviors into the value-function property each behavior demonstrates. Which property does the behavior 'losing $100 hurts more than gaining $100 feels good' illustrate?","column_a_label":"Concave in gains (diminishing sensitivity to larger gains)","column_b_label":"Convex in losses (diminishing sensitivity to larger losses)","column_c_label":"Steeper for losses than for gains (loss aversion)","rows":[{"label":"Marta, given a sure $800 or an 85% chance to win $1,000, chooses the sure $800 even though the gamble has a higher expectation of $850.","col":"a"},{"label":"Diego, forced to choose between an 85% chance to lose $1,000 and a sure loss of $800, prefers the gamble despite its inferior expectation of -$850.","col":"b"},{"label":"Priya says losing $100 is more painful than gaining $100 is pleasant.","col":"c"},{"label":"For Ben, the difference in value between a gain of $200 and $100 seems greater than the difference between $1,200 and $1,100.","col":"a"},{"label":"For Lena, the difference in value between a loss of $200 and $100 appears greater than the difference between $1,200 and $1,100.","col":"b"},{"label":"Tomas refuses to stake $10 on the toss of a coin unless he can win at least $30.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Risk aversion in the domain of gains, because the person always chooses sure outcomes.","Risk seeking in the domain of gains, because gains are evaluated on total wealth rather than changes.","Overweighting of sure things, because a certain gain is preferred to a probabilistic gain."],"explanation":"Loss aversion is the asymmetry in the value function: the curve is considerably steeper for losses than for gains, so a loss of $X is more aversive than a gain of $X is attractive. Concavity in gains and convexity in losses are separate properties that generate risk aversion and risk seeking respectively, but the 'losses hurt more' intuition is specifically the steepness asymmetry labeled loss aversion."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Risk Attitudes in Gains versus Losses","visual_frame_description":"A trading desk at a small investment firm at 9:40 AM. Trader Omar has a split-screen monitor: on the left, green 'gain' decision problems with dollar figures; on the right, red 'loss' problems. His colleague Ana is building a decision tree on the shared whiteboard, drawing an S-shaped curve next to a list of six gamble choices. The phone rings with a client asking whether a guaranteed gain or a gamble is better.","player_mission":"Classify each of the six decision situations into the risk attitude or decision-weight mechanism it reveals: risk aversion in gains, risk seeking in losses, or a distortion produced by the psychophysics of chance. Fill in the matrix before Omar closes the morning positions.","key_concepts":["A preference for a sure outcome over a gamble with higher or equal expectation is called risk aversion.","The rejection of a sure thing in favor of a gamble of lower or equal expectation is called risk seeking.","In Bernoulli's analysis, risk aversion is explained by a concave utility function of money.","The concavity of the value of gains entails risk aversion in the domain of gains.","The convexity of the value of losses entails risk seeking in the domain of losses.","Risk seeking in losses is a robust effect, particularly when the probabilities of loss are substantial.","Risk seeking in losses has been observed with nonmonetary outcomes such as hours of pain and loss of human lives.","Decision weights are lower than stated probabilities over most of the range, which contributes to risk aversion in gains and risk seeking in losses.","Low probabilities are overweighted, and very low probabilities are either overweighted grossly or neglected altogether.","Overweighting of low probabilities enhances the value of long shots and amplifies the aversiveness of a small chance of a severe loss.","The characteristics of decision weights contribute to the attractiveness of both lottery tickets and insurance policies.","An 85% chance to win $1,000 versus a sure $800 is an illustrative choice in which most people show risk aversion despite the gamble's higher expectation."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix separates risk aversion (sure outcomes preferred in gains) from risk seeking (gambles preferred in losses), and from decision-weight distortions where the psychophysics of chance, rather than the value function, drives the choice. Kahneman and Tversky show that the value function produces the gain-loss asymmetry, while nonlinear decision weights explain why both long-shot lotteries and insurance are attractive.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"When respondents choose a gamble with a 75% chance to lose $1,000 over a sure loss of $750, which mechanism is at work?","column_a_label":"Risk aversion (sure gain preferred over equal-or-better gamble)","column_b_label":"Risk seeking (gamble preferred over equal-or-better sure loss)","column_c_label":"Decision-weight distortion (psychophysics of chance)","rows":[{"label":"A large majority prefers a sure $800 over an 85% chance to win $1,000, despite the gamble's higher expectation of $850.","col":"a"},{"label":"A large majority prefers an 85% chance to lose $1,000 over a sure loss of $800.","col":"b"},{"label":"People find the purchase of a lottery ticket attractive despite a tiny chance of winning.","col":"c"},{"label":"People find the purchase of an insurance policy attractive despite a small chance of a severe loss.","col":"c"},{"label":"In Problem 4, 84% of respondents choose a sure gain of $240 over a 25% chance to gain $1,000.","col":"a"},{"label":"In Problem 4, 87% of respondents choose a 75% chance to lose $1,000 over a sure loss of $750.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Risk aversion, because the respondent dislikes any gamble that involves a loss.","Overweighting of sure things, because the sure loss feels more certain than the gamble.","Concavity of value in gains, because losses are evaluated relative to current wealth."],"explanation":"Choosing a 75% chance to lose $1,000 over a sure loss of $750 is risk seeking: the gamble's expectation of -$750 is equal to the sure loss, yet respondents prefer the gamble because the convex value function in the domain of losses makes the sure loss feel worse than its actuarial value. This contrasts with risk aversion in the gain domain, and with lottery and insurance choices, which are driven by the overweighting of low probabilities in decision weights rather than by the shape of the value function."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Framing and the Failure of Invariance","visual_frame_description":"A public-health agency conference room. Policy analyst Sana stands before two flip charts that describe the same disease-outbreak programs, one headlined '200 people will be saved' and the other '400 people will die.' Eighteen survey respondents have written their votes on sticky notes clustered around the charts, and the room's digital clock shows the deadline for the final recommendation. A large banner behind Sana reads: 'Invariance: the preference order must not depend on how options are described.'","player_mission":"Assign each of the six observed choice patterns to the framing mechanism that produced it: a gain frame inducing risk aversion, a loss frame inducing risk seeking, or a formulation effect in which a change of wording shifts preferences. Then identify which mechanism explains why the same health policy is endorsed in one wording and rejected in another.","key_concepts":["Invariance requires that the preference order between prospects should not depend on the manner in which they are described.","Dominance demands that if prospect A is at least as good as prospect B in every respect and better in at least one, A should be preferred to B.","The same option can be framed as gains and losses relative to the status quo or as asset positions that incorporate initial wealth.","In the Asian disease problem, a majority of respondents prefer saving 200 lives for sure over a one-third chance of saving 600.","When the identical programs are described in terms of lives lost, a majority prefer the gamble to the sure loss of 400 lives.","Options C and D in the loss version are undistinguishable in real terms from options A and B in the gain version.","The failure of invariance is as common among sophisticated respondents as among naive ones.","Even when the same respondents answer both versions within minutes, they still wish to be risk averse in the lives-saved version and risk seeking in the lives-lost version.","Framing effects resemble perceptual illusions more than computational errors in their stubborn appeal.","Invariance is normatively essential, intuitively compelling, and psychologically unfeasible.","It is good practice to test the robustness of preferences by deliberately framing a decision problem in more than one way.","Formulation effects can be exploited deliberately, as when credit-card lobbyists insisted that price differences be labeled a cash discount rather than a credit card surcharge."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the violation of invariance produced by outcome framing: describing identical outcomes as gains induces risk aversion, while describing them as losses induces risk seeking. Kahneman and Tversky show the failure of invariance is pervasive, robust, and resembles a perceptual illusion, and that a physician or presidential advisor could influence a decision merely by framing, without distorting or suppressing information.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which framing mechanism explains why respondents favor 'saving 200 people' but reject the equivalent program described as '400 people will die'?","column_a_label":"Gain frame induces risk aversion","column_b_label":"Loss frame induces risk seeking","column_c_label":"Formulation effect (change of wording shifts preference)","rows":[{"label":"Program A, which saves 200 people for sure, is chosen by 72% of respondents (Problem 1).","col":"a"},{"label":"Program D, a one-third chance that nobody dies and a two-thirds chance that 600 die, is chosen by 78% of respondents (Problem 2).","col":"b"},{"label":"Physicians and patients shift preferences between lung-cancer therapies when outcomes are described as survival versus mortality.","col":"c"},{"label":"Consumers are less likely to accept a credit card surcharge than to forgo a cash discount for the same price difference.","col":"c"},{"label":"In the lives-saved version, the reference state is one in which the disease is allowed to take its toll of 600 lives.","col":"a"},{"label":"In the lives-lost version, the reference state is one in which no one dies of the disease.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Gain framing, because the respondent evaluates the program as a gain in both versions.","Dominance, because Program D dominates Program A in the loss version.","The certainty effect, because the sure option is overweighted in both versions."],"explanation":"The lives-lost version assumes a reference state in which no one dies, so all outcomes are losses; convexity of value in the loss domain produces risk seeking, and 78% choose the gamble. The lives-saved version adopts the 600-deaths state as its reference, making outcomes gains and producing risk aversion. The physician-survival and cash-discount cases are formulation effects, where changing the wording rather than the reference-point logic shifts preferences."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Levels of Mental Accounts","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit kitchen in a suburb. Priya is looking at a two-store comparison on her phone: a $125 jacket and a $15 calculator at the first store, and a $10 calculator at the other branch, 20 minutes away. Her partner Lena watches the traffic app while her mother-in-law sips coffee. A sticky note on the fridge lists three account types: minimal, topical, and comprehensive.","player_mission":"Classify each of the six mental-accounting observations into the level of account it reflects: minimal (only the differences between options), topical (the context of the decision), or comprehensive (all related expenses combined). Then determine which account level best explains the finding that people will travel to save $5 on a $15 item but not on a $125 item.","key_concepts":["To evaluate a multiattribute option, a person sets up a mental account that specifies the advantages and disadvantages of the option relative to a reference state.","The overall value of an option is given by the balance of its advantages and disadvantages in relation to the reference state.","The minimal account includes only the differences between the two options and disregards the features they share.","A topical account relates consequences to a reference level determined by the context within which the decision arises.","In the calculator problem, the topical account frames the benefit of the trip as a reduction of the calculator's price from $15 to $10.","In the topical account, the price of the jacket is not included because the potential saving is associated only with the calculator.","A comprehensive account would include the jacket price and other expenses, evaluating the saving in relation to monthly expenses.","68% of respondents were willing to drive 20 minutes to save $5 on a $15 calculator, but only 29% were willing to make the same trip to save $5 on a $125 calculator.","The two versions of the calculator problem are identical in terms of both a minimal and a comprehensive account.","Topical organization in decision making plays a role analogous to that of good forms in perception and basic-level categories in cognition.","Consumers hardly exert more effort to save $15 on a $150 purchase than to save $5 on a $50 purchase.","Mental accounting explains anomalies of consumer behavior, including why the acceptability of an option depends on how negative outcomes are organized."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the three levels of mental accounts, minimal, topical, and comprehensive, that Kahneman and Tversky, following Thaler, propose for evaluating transactions. The topical account uniquely explains the saving behavior because the minimal and comprehensive accounts cannot distinguish the calculator versions, and topical organization makes willingness to travel inversely related to the item's price and independent of the jacket's price.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which level of mental account best explains why respondents will drive 20 minutes to save $5 on a $15 calculator but not on a $125 calculator, holding the total saving constant?","column_a_label":"Minimal account (only differences between options)","column_b_label":"Topical account (reference level set by decision context)","column_c_label":"Comprehensive account (all related expenses combined)","rows":[{"label":"The advantage of driving to the other store is framed as a gain of $5, disregarding shared features.","col":"a"},{"label":"The benefit of the trip is framed as a reduction of the calculator's price from $15 to $10.","col":"b"},{"label":"The price of the jacket is excluded from the account because the saving is associated only with the calculator.","col":"b"},{"label":"The saving is evaluated in relation to monthly expenses.","col":"c"},{"label":"Willingness to travel is inversely related to the price of the calculator and independent of the price of the jacket.","col":"b"},{"label":"The standard deviation of prices quoted for the same product is roughly proportional to the average price of that product.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The minimal account, because it includes the $5 saving regardless of which item is discounted.","The comprehensive account, because it combines the jacket and calculator into a single transaction.","Loss aversion, because respondents dislike the effort of the trip more than they value the saving."],"explanation":"Only the topical account predicts the result: it ties the $5 saving to the calculator's price, making the same saving feel large on a $15 item and trivial on a $125 item. The minimal and comprehensive accounts are identical across the two versions, so they cannot explain the sharp difference in willingness to travel. The price-dispersion observation confirms that consumers evaluate savings relative to the price of the item, not in absolute terms."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Psychophysics of Chance and Decision Weights","visual_frame_description":"An insurer's analytics lab at 3 PM. Actuary Tom studies Figure 2, a curve plotting decision weight against stated probability: it rises steeply near zero, stays below the diagonal through the middle, and jumps near one. On his desk sit six cards describing probability changes and risk choices, and his colleague Maya is annotating the curve with the labels 'overweighting of sure things,' 'overweighting of low probabilities,' and 'regressive weights.'","player_mission":"Sort each of the six observations into the pattern of decision weights that produces it: overweighting of sure things, overweighting of low probabilities, or regressive underweighting of moderate and high probabilities. Then state which pattern drives the attractiveness of both lottery tickets and insurance policies.","key_concepts":["Setting the value of the status quo at zero, a $300 cash gift can be assigned a value of one, and the value of a lottery ticket must vary between zero and one.","The value of a ticket is not a linear function of the probability of winning, as the expectation rule would require.","An increase from 0% to 5% appears to have a larger effect than an increase from 30% to 35%.","An increase from 95% to 100% also appears larger than an increase from 30% to 35%.","These observations suggest a category-boundary effect: a change from impossibility to possibility or from possibility to certainty has a bigger impact than a comparable change in the middle of the scale.","Decision weights are regressive with respect to stated probabilities: except near the endpoints, an increase of .05 in probability raises prospect value by less than 5% of the prize.","Decision weights are lower than the corresponding probabilities over most of the range.","Underweighting of moderate and high probabilities relative to sure things contributes to risk aversion in gains.","Low probabilities are overweighted, and very low probabilities are either overweighted grossly or neglected altogether.","Overweighting of low probabilities enhances the value of long shots and amplifies the aversiveness of a small chance of a severe loss.","The characteristics of decision weights contribute to the attractiveness of both lottery tickets and insurance policies.","The pseudo-certainty effect makes an event that is actually uncertain weighted as if it were certain."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the nonlinearity of decision weights: sure things and low probabilities are overweighted while moderate and high probabilities are underweighted in a regressive pattern. Kahneman and Tversky show these psychophysical properties explain why both lottery tickets and insurance are attractive, and why the pseudo-certainty effect and probabilistic insurance aversion arise.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which pattern of decision weights explains why both long-shot lottery tickets and insurance against a small chance of a severe loss are attractive?","column_a_label":"Overweighting of sure things (possibility-to-certainty boundary)","column_b_label":"Overweighting of low probabilities (impossibility-to-possibility boundary)","column_c_label":"Regressive weights (underweighting of moderate and high probabilities)","rows":[{"label":"An increase in probability from 95% to 100% has a bigger impact than an increase from 30% to 35%.","col":"a"},{"label":"An increase in probability from 0% to 5% has a bigger impact than an increase from 30% to 35%.","col":"b"},{"label":"Except near the endpoints, a .05 increase in probability raises prospect value by less than 5% of the prize value.","col":"c"},{"label":"People are often risk seeking in dealing with improbable gains.","col":"b"},{"label":"People are often risk averse in dealing with unlikely losses.","col":"b"},{"label":"In the sequential two-stage game, an option with a sure gain in the second stage is weighted as if it were certain.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Overweighting of sure things, because both lottery and insurance outcomes are ultimately certain.","Regressive weights, because the middle of the probability range is the only region that matters for these choices.","The certainty effect, because both purchases eliminate risk entirely."],"explanation":"Both the long-shot lottery (improbable gain) and the insurance policy (small chance of a severe loss) depend on the overweighting of low probabilities near the impossibility-to-possibility boundary. Overweighting makes the tiny prize feel valuable and the tiny risk feel dreadful. The sure-thing boundary operates at the other end of the scale (95% to 100%), and regressive underweighting dominates the middle range where moderate probabilities are discounted."},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Cost-Loss Discrepancy","visual_frame_description":"A psychology laboratory with a respondent, Keiko, seated at a terminal running two decision problems. On the left screen, Problem 10 asks about accepting a gamble with a 10% chance to win $95 and a 90% chance to lose $5. On the right screen, Problem 11 asks whether to pay $5 for a lottery with a 10% chance to win $100. A researcher in a gray coat logs her responses while a one-way mirror reflects the observation room.","player_mission":"Audit Keiko's session and decide which psychological mechanism best explains why she rejects the objectively identical offer when it is framed as a gamble but accepts it when it is framed as a lottery payment. Read the baseline, the nonverbal log, and the interrogation transcript, then issue your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Problem 10 and Problem 11 offer objectively identical options: a 10% chance at roughly $95 to $100 with a 90% chance of losing $5.","Among 132 undergraduates, 55 expressed different preferences in the two versions of the problem.","Among the inconsistent respondents, 42 rejected the gamble in Problem 10 but accepted the equivalent lottery in Problem 11.","Thinking of the $5 as a payment makes the venture more acceptable than thinking of the same amount as a loss.","The effect illustrates both the cost-loss discrepancy and the power of framing.","Losses are more aversive than costs: an amount framed as an uncompensated loss is rejected where the same amount framed as a cost of protection is accepted.","An individual's subjective state can be improved by framing negative outcomes as costs rather than as losses.","In the insurance example, 80% of subjects chose a gamble over a sure loss of $50, but only 35% refused to pay $50 for insurance against a 25% risk of losing $200.","The same money framed as an uncompensated loss versus a cost of protection reversed the modal preference.","The cost-loss discrepancy can lead to failures of invariance.","The order of the two questions was reversed for half the respondents, and the result held.","The effectiveness of this seemingly inconsequential manipulation illustrates the power of framing in decision making."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This audit tests the cost-loss discrepancy: the same $5 is evaluated as an acceptable cost when framed as a lottery payment but as an aversive uncompensated loss when framed as a potential loss in a gamble. Kahneman and Tversky report that most inconsistent respondents rejected the gamble yet accepted the identical lottery, showing losses are more aversive than costs.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism best explains why Keiko rejects the gamble ('lose $5') but accepts the identical lottery ('pay $5')?","subject_baseline":"Keiko, a 27-year-old graduate student, generally treats small sums of money casually, buys a weekly lottery ticket, and does not dwell on sunk costs. In screening tasks, she answers quickly and shows no strong aversion to gambling in principle.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00 Problem 10 (gamble with 10% chance to win $95 and 90% chance to lose $5) appears; Keiko frowns, leans back, and takes 6 seconds to respond 'No.'","0:20 She explains the gamble 'feels like handing over five dollars for nothing.'","0:30 Problem 11 (pay $5 for a lottery with 10% chance to win $100) appears; Keiko's expression softens and she responds 'Yes' in under 2 seconds.","0:45 When asked to compare the two, she shrugs and says the lottery is 'just buying a ticket, everyone does that.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Researcher: 'You rejected the first offer but accepted the second. Can you tell me why?'","Keiko: 'The first one, I lose five dollars most of the time. That's a loss.'","Researcher: 'And the second?'","Keiko: 'The second is just paying five dollars to play a lottery. Paying for a ticket isn't losing money.'","Researcher: 'Mathematically, the two are the same risk.'","Keiko: 'I know they look the same, but one feels like giving money away and the other feels like buying a chance.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The cost-loss discrepancy: Keiko frames the $5 as an uncompensated loss in the gamble but as a cost of participation in the lottery, and because losses are more aversive than costs, her preference reverses despite the objectively identical options.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The only difference between the two problems is the framing of the $5: as a possible loss in a gamble versus as a payment for a lottery ticket. Kahneman and Tversky found 42 of 55 inconsistent respondents rejected the gamble yet accepted the identical lottery. Keiko's explanation ('paying for a ticket isn't losing money') directly matches the proposed mechanism that losses are more aversive than costs, and her nonverbal hesitation on the gamble reflects the aversive loss frame. No other mechanism differs between the two offers because the odds, stake, and expected value are equivalent.","distractors":["Loss aversion at the value-function level: Keiko is simply more sensitive to losses than to gains, which would apply equally to both offers.","The certainty effect: Keiko treats the 90% chance of losing $5 as if it were a certain loss in the first offer only.","The pseudo-certainty effect: Keiko behaves as if the lottery's 10% chance of winning were certain."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Mortality versus Survival Framing in Medical Choice","visual_frame_description":"A hospital consultation room. Oncologist Dr. Amara Osei shows Mr. Ito two summary sheets for the same lung-cancer therapy: one reports survival statistics, the other reports the identical outcomes as mortality statistics. Both list the risk of death during surgery. Dr. Osei watches Mr. Ito's expression shift as the framing changes, and a second physician in the doorway records the consultation for the shared-decision audit.","player_mission":"Audit Dr. Osei's consultation and determine which mechanism explains why the therapy choice shifts when outcomes are described as mortality rather than survival, even though the underlying statistics are identical. Read the baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["McNeil, Pauker, Sox, and Tversky (1982) found that preferences of physicians and patients between lung-cancer therapies varied markedly with whether outcomes were described as mortality or survival.","Surgery, unlike radiation therapy, entails a risk of death during treatment.","The surgery option was relatively less attractive when treatment outcomes were described in terms of mortality rather than survival.","Formulation effects can occur fortuitously, without anyone being aware of the impact of the frame on the decision.","Formulation effects can also be exploited deliberately to manipulate the relative attractiveness of options.","A physician, and perhaps a presidential advisor as well, could influence the decision made by the patient or by the President merely by the framing of outcomes and contingencies.","The evaluation of outcomes is susceptible to formulation effects because of the nonlinearity of the value function and the tendency to evaluate options in relation to a suggested reference point.","The framing of outcomes of therapies in terms of mortality or survival is unlikely to affect experience, although it can have a pronounced influence on choice.","The public health problem illustrates a formulation effect in which a change of wording from lives saved to lives lost induced a shift from risk aversion to risk seeking.","People adopt the descriptions of the outcomes as given in the question and evaluate the outcomes accordingly as gains or losses.","The mental machinery that silently recodes language is not adequate to recode the mortality-survival statistics into a common abstract form.","It is good practice to test the robustness of preferences by deliberately framing a decision problem in more than one way."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the formulation effect: changing the description of identical outcomes from survival to mortality shifts the relative attractiveness of surgery because the risk of death during treatment becomes salient. McNeil, Pauker, Sox, and Tversky demonstrated this shift in both physicians and patients, showing that framing alone can change medical choices without any change in the statistics.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism best explains why Mr. Ito's preference shifts when the same therapy statistics are presented as mortality rather than survival?","subject_baseline":"Mr. Ito, 61, is a retired accountant who makes decisions methodically and requests detailed information. In prior consultations he compared treatment options in writing and rarely changed his mind once a preference was stated. Dr. Osei is an experienced oncologist who believes she presents options neutrally.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00 Survival-frame sheet presented; Mr. Ito reads slowly, nods, and says surgery 'sounds like a reasonable option.'","1:30 Mortality-frame sheet presented with identical statistics; Mr. Ito pauses, furrows his brow at the phrase 'death during surgery,' and leans back.","2:10 He asks Dr. Osei to repeat the mortality figures and then says he would 'rather avoid the knife.'","2:40 When told the two sheets show the same numbers, Mr. Ito looks confused and says the mortality sheet 'made it feel like the operation was more dangerous.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Researcher: 'You changed your mind between the two sheets. What happened?'","Mr. Ito: 'On the survival sheet, surgery looked like the better chance. On the other one, the word mortality next to surgery made me picture dying on the table.'","Researcher: 'But the survival rate and the mortality rate are exact opposites of the same number.'","Mr. Ito: 'I understand that now, but when I was deciding, the mortality version made the risk during surgery feel bigger than the survival version did.'","Dr. Osei: 'I did not intend to push him either way, but I can see I presented the mortality sheet more cautiously.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"A formulation effect: describing identical outcomes as mortality rather than survival made the in-treatment death risk of surgery salient and reduced its relative attractiveness, shifting Mr. Ito's choice despite unchanged statistics.","diagnostic_breakdown":"The statistics were identical across the two sheets; only the attribute used to describe them changed. Because surgery carries a risk of death during treatment, mortality framing highlights that risk, while survival framing highlights the larger share of survivors. This matches the McNeil et al. finding that both physicians and patients show the shift, and it explains why Dr. Osei's caution also increased in the mortality frame: the frame changes the salience of the hazard, not the underlying facts. The effect is a formulation effect rather than a change in risk aversion, since no probability or outcome changed.","distractors":["Loss aversion: Mr. Ito weighs the loss of life more heavily than the gain of survival, which would apply equally to both frames.","The certainty effect: Mr. Ito treats death during surgery as certain in the mortality frame.","Anchoring: Mr. Ito anchors on the first number he reads and does not adjust when the survival frame appears."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"Mental Accounting and the Lost Ticket","visual_frame_description":"The lobby of the Civic Playhouse on a rainy Saturday. Elena stands at the box office after discovering her ticket is gone. In one version of the audit she lost the $10 ticket; in a matched version she lost a $10 bill. Her hands are in her pockets, and the cashier waits with a replacement ticket in hand. A research assistant off to the side logs her hesitation and final choice.","player_mission":"Audit Elena's behavior across the two matched situations and decide which mental-accounting mechanism explains why she refuses a second ticket after losing the ticket itself but readily buys one after losing the equivalent cash. Read the baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["Problem 8 asked respondents who had lost a paid $10 ticket whether they would pay $10 for another: only 46% said yes.","Problem 9 asked respondents who had lost a $10 bill whether they would pay $10 for a ticket: 88% said yes.","The difference is attributed to the topical organization of mental accounts.","Going to the theater is normally viewed as a transaction in which the cost of the ticket is exchanged for the experience of the play.","Buying a second ticket increases the cost of seeing the play to a level that many respondents find unacceptable.","The loss of the cash is not posted to the account of the play; it only makes the individual feel slightly less affluent.","When both versions were presented to the same subjects, the willingness to replace a lost ticket increased.","The juxtaposition of the two problems enabled subjects to realize it makes sense to think of the lost ticket as lost cash, but not vice versa.","The willingness to buy a ticket after losing cash was not affected by prior presentation of the other problem.","It may be more annoying to pay twice for the same ticket than to lose $10 in cash.","Regret, frustration, and self-satisfaction can be affected by framing.","Systematic examination of alternative framings is a useful reflective device for assessing the values attached to primary and secondary consequences."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This audit tests the topical organization of mental accounts: the theater outing is treated as one account, so buying a second ticket doubles the cost of that account, whereas lost cash is not posted to the play's account. Kahneman and Tversky report 46% versus 88% willingness across the two problems, and show that juxtaposing the versions increases replacement because subjects can recode the lost ticket as lost cash.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism best explains why Elena replaces a lost ticket less readily than she buys a ticket after losing equivalent cash?","subject_baseline":"Elena, 33, a product manager, attends the theater a few times a year and habitually buys her ticket online in advance. She does not normally agonize over small sums and considers herself financially pragmatic.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00 In the lost-ticket scenario, Elena discovers her ticket is missing, frowns, and stares at the box office for about 8 seconds before shaking her head.","0:20 She declines to buy a replacement and turns toward the exit.","1:00 In the separate lost-cash session, Elena discovers a $10 bill is missing, shrugs, and walks directly to the ticket window.","1:15 She purchases the $10 ticket without hesitation.","1:40 When asked about the difference, she says the ticket 'already counted as paying for the show.'"],"interrogation_transcript":["Interviewer: 'In the first scenario you lost a $10 ticket. In the second you lost a $10 bill. Why did you act differently?'","Elena: 'Because in the first one I had already paid for the play. Buying another ticket means the play costs me $20. That's too much for one show.'","Interviewer: 'And when you lost the cash?'","Elena: 'That's just bad luck. The play still costs $10, and I still want to see it.'","Interviewer: 'But in both cases you are out $10 either way.'","Elena: 'I know, but the lost bill doesn't belong to the play. The lost ticket belongs to the play account, and I already paid that account.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"Topical organization of mental accounts: the cost of the play is posted to a 'theater outing' account, so a second ticket raises that account to an unacceptable level, while the lost cash is not posted to the play's account and only makes Elena feel slightly less affluent.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Both scenarios reduce Elena's wealth by $10, so wealth-based accounts predict identical behavior. The topical account differs: buying a replacement ticket is booked against the same theater account, making the outing effectively cost $20; losing cash is not attributed to the play and leaves the $10 ticket price acceptable. The 46% versus 88% split in the original problems matches Elena's behavior, and the fact that presenting both problems together increased replacement confirms that the two situations are bookkept differently rather than being truly different in substance.","distractors":["Loss aversion: Elena values the lost ticket more than the lost cash because it is a possessed asset.","The certainty effect: Elena treats the second ticket purchase as a certain loss but the cash loss as uncertain.","The dead-loss effect: Elena refuses to buy a second ticket to avoid recognizing the first as a dead loss, which would be reversed by juxtaposition."]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Dead-Loss Effect in Sunk-Cost Behavior","visual_frame_description":"A tennis club court on a humid evening. Mateo, a 44-year-old member, serves with a visible wince and shakes out his right elbow between points. His coach stands at the net holding a rescheduling form, while a sports-club analyst observes from the bench and logs Mateo's expressions. A banner behind the court advertises the club's $850 annual membership.","player_mission":"Audit Mateo's behavior and decide which mechanism best explains why he continues to play in pain to avoid stopping, even though quitting would spare him the discomfort. Read the baseline, the nonverbal log, and the transcript, then issue your diagnosis.","key_concepts":["The dead-loss effect describes a paradoxical behavior: an individual continues an activity to avoid recognizing a paid fee as a dead loss.","Thaler (1980) discussed a man who develops tennis elbow soon after paying a club membership fee and continues to play in agony to avoid wasting his investment.","Playing in pain maintains the evaluation of the membership fee as a cost.","If the individual stopped playing, he would be forced to recognize the fee as a dead loss, which may be more aversive than playing in pain.","An individual's subjective state can be improved by framing negative outcomes as costs rather than as losses.","Losses are more aversive than costs.","The dead-loss effect is a consequence of the cost-loss discrepancy applied to sunk costs.","The acceptability of an option can depend on whether a negative outcome is evaluated as a cost or as an uncompensated loss.","The analysis assumes that the individual would not play if he had not paid the membership fee.","Money is naturally viewed as a proxy for the goods and services that it could buy in standard economic analysis.","Loss aversion favors stability over change.","Secondary consequences such as regret and frustration can be affected by framing."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This audit tests the dead-loss effect: Mateo continues to play in pain to keep the membership fee classified as a cost rather than reclassify it as a dead loss, which he finds more aversive. Kahneman and Tversky, following Thaler, present this as a case where framing a negative outcome as a cost rather than a loss improves the individual's subjective state despite objectively worsening physical discomfort.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"question":"Which mechanism best explains why Mateo continues to play in pain rather than stop and forfeit his paid membership?","subject_baseline":"Mateo, 44, joined the club three weeks ago and paid the full $850 annual fee. He enjoys tennis in principle but has complained of elbow pain since his second session. In the past he has canceled gym memberships without much thought.","observed_nonverbal_log":["0:00 Mateo serves and immediately winces, rotating his right shoulder and rubbing his elbow.","0:20 When the coach suggests resting for two weeks, Mateo's jaw tightens and he replies that he 'already paid for the year.'","0:45 He plays on for another 10 minutes, his strokes visibly cautious.","1:05 As he leaves the court, Mateo looks at the club's membership banner and mutters that at least he is 'getting his money's worth.'","1:20 He declines the rescheduling offer without reading it."],"interrogation_transcript":["Coach: 'Mateo, if you keep playing on that elbow you might make it worse. Why not take a break?'","Mateo: 'Because I paid $850 up front. If I stop now, that money is just gone.'","Coach: 'But you would be avoiding the pain and the risk of injury.'","Mateo: 'Quitting would mean admitting I wasted the whole fee. Playing through it keeps it a real membership, not money down the drain.'","Coach: 'So continuing in pain is easier than stopping?'","Mateo: 'Weirdly, yes. Stopping makes the loss final. This way I can tell myself I'm still using what I paid for.'"],"correct_diagnosis":"The dead-loss effect: Mateo continues to play in pain to keep the membership fee classified as a cost, because stopping would force him to recognize the fee as an uncompensated dead loss, which he experiences as more aversive than playing in agony.","diagnostic_breakdown":"Mateo's own words make the mechanism explicit: stopping 'makes the loss final,' while continuing 'keeps it a real membership.' The fee is framed as a cost of an active membership rather than as a sunk and unrecoverable loss. Because losses are more aversive than costs, Mateo prefers sustained physical pain to the psychological pain of booking a dead loss. The baseline note that he cancels memberships easily when he has not just paid confirms the effect is tied to the recent large payment and its framing, not to a general aversion to quitting.","distractors":["Loss aversion at the value-function level: Mateo is reluctant to give up the membership as an owned asset, independent of the fee's framing.","The certainty effect: Mateo treats the pain of stopping as certain and the injury risk as negligible.","The endowment effect: Mateo overvalues the membership because he paid a high price for it, so he refuses to waste it."]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Asian Disease Problem and the Framing Effect","visual_frame_description":"A windowless war-room at a public-health agency. A briefing screen shows the two versions of the Asian disease problem side by side: '200 people will be saved' next to '400 people will die.' Below, bar charts display the 72% versus 28% split and the 22% versus 78% split. The analyst, Dr. Yuki Tanaka, faces a countdown clock; the boss, the Framing Effect, appears as a mirrored pair of identical policy documents with different wordings.","player_mission":"Defeat the Framing Effect by reading the two versions of the disease program and identifying why preferences flip despite identical real-world outcomes. Then choose the intervention that restores invariance in the final policy recommendation.","key_concepts":["Problem 1 adopts as a reference point a state in which the disease is allowed to take its toll of 600 lives, so outcomes are gains measured in lives saved.","Problem 2 adopts a reference state in which no one dies, so outcomes are losses measured in lives lost.","Options C and D in the loss version are undistinguishable in real terms from options A and B in the gain version.","Risk aversion in the domain of gains produces a preference for saving 200 lives for sure.","Risk seeking in the domain of losses produces a preference for the gamble in the lives-lost version.","The failure of invariance is pervasive and robust, common among sophisticated and naive respondents alike.","Respondents confronted with their conflicting answers are typically puzzled and still wish to be risk averse in one version and risk seeking in the other.","Framing effects resemble perceptual illusions more than computational errors.","Invariance requires that the preference order between prospects not depend on the manner in which they are described.","Dominance demands that a prospect at least as good in every respect and better in one be preferred.","Frame invariance cannot be expected to hold, and a sense of confidence in a choice does not ensure the same choice would be made in another frame.","It is good practice to test the robustness of preferences by deliberately framing a decision in more than one way."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the framing effect and the failure of invariance: identical programs described as gains produce risk aversion while the same programs described as losses produce risk seeking. Kahneman and Tversky's Asian disease problem demonstrates that a physician or policy advisor can influence a decision merely by the frame, without distorting or suppressing information.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss, the Framing Effect, is the violation of invariance produced by describing identical outcomes as gains versus losses. Because the value function is concave for gains and convex for losses, respondents are risk averse when told '200 people will be saved' but risk seeking when told '400 people will die.' The player must see through the wording to the identical real-world outcomes.","stage_1_observation":"Dr. Tanaka reviews the briefing screen. Problem 1 asked 152 respondents to choose between Program A, which saves 200 people for sure, and Program B, which offers a one-third chance that 600 people will be saved and a two-thirds chance that no one will be saved. The data show 72% chose A and 28% chose B.","stage_1_question":"Why do 72% of respondents prefer the sure saving of 200 lives even though Program B has the same expected number of lives saved?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The outcomes are framed as gains relative to a reference state in which the disease is allowed to take its toll of 600 lives, and the value function is concave in the domain of gains, producing risk aversion: the sure saving of 200 lives is valued above its actuarial expectation, while the one-third chance of saving 600 lives is discounted.","stage_2_intervention":"The briefing then shows Problem 2, where the same programs are described as '400 people will die' versus a gamble with a one-third probability that nobody dies and a two-thirds probability that 600 people die. Dr. Tanaka must decide how to present the policy recommendation so that the choice does not flip with the frame. Choose the intervention that best defeats the Framing Effect.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Restore a single canonical representation by evaluating both programs on a common metric, such as expected lives saved, rather than accepting the frame each version suggests. Recognize that options C and D are real-world equivalents of A and B, that the loss frame induces risk seeking because the value function is convex for losses, and test the robustness of the final choice by deliberately framing the decision in more than one way before recommending it."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Dominance Violation in Concurrent Decisions","visual_frame_description":"A fintech product studio at 6 PM. Lead designer Jonah has two whiteboards: on one, the concurrent-decision problem showing a sure gain of $240 beside a 25% chance to gain $1,000, and a sure loss of $750 beside a 75% chance to lose $1,000. On the second whiteboard, the combination table reveals that the rejected conjunction B and C dominates the chosen conjunction A and D. The boss, Dominance Violation, looms as a pair of equal-expectation columns the players must reconcile.","player_mission":"Defeat the Dominance Violation by explaining why 73% of respondents chose the combination of a sure gain and a loss-gamble, and then intervene to redesign the interface so the client sees the dominated combination before choosing. Read the observation, answer the question, and pick the intervention that combines the concurrent decisions.","key_concepts":["Problem 4 presented two concurrent decisions: a sure gain of $240 versus a 25% chance to gain $1,000, and a sure loss of $750 versus a 75% chance to lose $1,000.","84% of respondents chose the sure gain of $240 in the first decision.","87% of respondents chose the gamble over the sure loss in the second decision.","73% of respondents chose A and D, and only 3% chose B and C.","The preferred conjunction A and D is dominated by the rejected conjunction B and C.","Adding the sure gain of $240 to option D yields a 25% chance to win $240 and a 75% chance to lose $760, which is option E in Problem 3.","Adding the sure loss of $750 to option B yields a 25% chance to win $250 and a 75% chance to lose $750, which is option F in Problem 3.","Problem 3 showed that all respondents chose F over E when the options were shown together because F dominates E.","The susceptibility to framing and the S-shaped value function produce a violation of dominance in a set of concurrent decisions.","Risk aversion in gains and risk seeking in losses combine to favor the dominated conjunction.","A canonical representation of risky prospects requires compounding all outcomes of concurrent decisions.","The standard admonition to consider each decision in terms of total assets rather than gains or losses is easier to give than to follow."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the dominance violation created when risk aversion in gains and risk seeking in losses are applied to concurrent decisions in isolation. Kahneman and Tversky show that the conjunction chosen by 73% of respondents is dominated by the rejected one, demonstrating that the S-shaped value function and framing combine to violate the dominance principle of rational choice.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss, Dominance Violation, arises when people evaluate concurrent decisions separately: they are risk averse on a sure gain and risk seeking on a sure loss, so they pick a combination that is dominated by the one they rejected. The player must combine the outcomes of the two decisions to expose the dominance.","stage_1_observation":"Jonah pulls up the decision from Problem 4: Decision (i) offers a sure gain of $240 or a 25% chance to gain $1,000 with a 75% chance to gain nothing; Decision (ii) offers a sure loss of $750 or a 75% chance to lose $1,000 with a 25% chance to lose nothing. The studio's test data show that 84% chose the sure gain, 87% chose the gamble on the loss, and 73% chose the combination A and D.","stage_1_question":"Why do 73% of respondents choose the conjunction of the sure gain (A) and the loss-gamble (D) when that conjunction is dominated by the rejected conjunction of the two gambles (B and C)?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because the two decisions are framed and evaluated separately: the value function's concavity in gains makes the sure $240 attractive, and its convexity in losses makes the gamble against the sure loss attractive. When the outcomes are combined, A plus D equals a 25% chance to win $240 and a 75% chance to lose $760, which is exactly option E of Problem 3, while B plus C equals a 25% chance to win $250 and a 75% chance to lose $750, option F, which dominates E. Evaluating the decisions in isolation hides this dominance.","stage_2_intervention":"Jonah must redesign the product's choice screen so users do not fall into the dominated conjunction. The data show that when options E and F are shown together, every respondent chooses F. Choose the intervention that most reliably defeats the Dominance Violation.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Combine the two concurrent decisions into a single joint display before the user chooses, so the four conjunctions are compared in a canonical representation. Presenting A plus D versus B plus C as combined outcomes lets the user see that the rejected conjunction dominates, mirroring the finding that all respondents chose F over E when they were shown together. This implements the standard admonition to consider the compounding of outcomes rather than separate gains and losses."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Dual-Process Theory | Heuristics & Statistical Biases","topic":"The Endowment Effect and Status-Quo Bias in Negotiation","visual_frame_description":"A mountain-lodge sales negotiation. Founder Amara is selling her ski lodge after years of ownership. Across the table, buyer Ben has offered $2.4 million, but Amara insists on $3.1 million, a figure shaped by the $2.2 million she paid a decade ago and by her reluctance to part with the lodge. Her advisor's tablet shows a bar chart comparing her buying price, her selling price, and Ben's offer, with the gap highlighted in red. The boss, the Endowment Effect, stands behind Amara as a mirrored scale weighing 'giving up' against 'acquiring.'","player_mission":"Defeat the Endowment Effect by explaining why selling prices systematically exceed buying prices and why Amara is biased toward retaining the status quo. Then choose the negotiation reframing that lets her set a price anchored in market value rather than in ownership.","key_concepts":["Thaler (1980) coined the term endowment effect to describe the reluctance of people to part from assets that belong to their endowment.","When it is more painful to give up an asset than it is pleasurable to obtain it, buying prices will be significantly lower than selling prices.","The highest price an individual will pay to acquire an asset is smaller than the minimal compensation that would induce the same individual to give it up once acquired.","Several studies have reported substantial discrepancies between buying and selling prices in both hypothetical and real transactions.","Loss aversion is the mechanism behind the endowment effect: losses loom larger than gains.","In the job study, most subjects assigned to one position did not wish to move to the other, and vice versa, because the same difference looms larger as a disadvantage than as an advantage.","Loss aversion favors stability over change.","Two hedonically identical twins placed in two equally attractive environments will both prefer to stay where they happen to be once they adopt their states as reference points.","The status quo defines the reference level for all attributes in a multiattribute decision.","The combination of adaptation and loss aversion reduces the attractiveness of foregone alternatives and of others' endowments.","Loss aversion and the consequent endowment effect are unlikely to play a significant role in routine economic exchanges where money is treated as a proxy for goods and services.","Money is naturally viewed as a proxy for the goods and services that it could buy, as when a person thinks 'I can either buy a new camera or a new tent.'"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the endowment effect and status-quo bias: because losses loom larger than gains, people demand more to give up an asset than they would pay to acquire it, and the status quo becomes the reference point for all attributes. Kahneman and Tversky, following Thaler, show this produces substantial buying-selling price discrepancies and a general preference for stability over change.","credibility":{"level":"High","consensus":"Broad","basis":"Empirical behavioral economics research (Kahneman & Tversky; Nobel 2002). Widely replicated judgment-and-decision effects."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss, the Endowment Effect, is the reluctance to part with owned assets caused by loss aversion: giving up an asset is more painful than acquiring it is pleasurable, so selling prices exceed buying prices and the status quo is favored. The player must recognize the effect and reframe the negotiation so value is evaluated in a common currency rather than as loss versus gain.","stage_1_observation":"Amara watches the negotiation replay. In round one, the advisor presented a market survey showing that sellers list their used equipment at prices roughly twice what buyers offer for identical items. In round two, the same advisor showed the lodge's purchase price of $2.2 million, Amara's asking price of $3.1 million, and Ben's offer of $2.4 million. Amara stated she would not accept less than $3.1 million because she 'already owns it and knows what it is worth to me.'","stage_1_question":"Why does Amara's minimum acceptable selling price ($3.1 million) exceed both what she paid ($2.2 million) and what a buyer like Ben would offer ($2.4 million)?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Amara treats the lodge as part of her endowment and her ownership as the reference point. Because it is more painful to give up an asset than it is pleasurable to acquire it, her selling price is inflated above what she would pay for an equivalent lodge she did not own. The buying-selling price gap is the hallmark of the endowment effect, and the same loss aversion biases her toward retaining the status quo rather than accepting the move to cash.","stage_2_intervention":"Ben's final offer is $2.8 million, still below Amara's floor of $3.1 million. The advisor must help Amara decide whether to accept, reframe the decision, or walk away. Choose the intervention that best defeats the Endowment Effect so her decision reflects value rather than ownership.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reframe the sale as a choice between alternative uses of money rather than as the loss of an owned asset, matching how routine exchanges treat money as a proxy for what it can buy. Specifically, ask Amara to compare the cash from selling against specific alternatives, such as reinvesting in her new venture or the financial security it buys, so the transaction is evaluated as a gain of purchasing power instead of a loss of the lodge. This cancels the gain-loss asymmetry at the point of evaluation and lets her price the lodge against market value rather than against her endowment."}]}]};
